window.DATA = {"exams":[{"id":"ccar-f","code":"CCAR-F","title":"Claude Certified Architect — Foundations","vendor":"Anthropic","exam":{"count":60,"minutes":90,"passPercent":80},"domains":{"architecture":"Architecture","claude-code":"Claude Code","context-reliability":"Context Reliability","tools":"Tools","prompt-output":"Prompt Output"},"qs":[{"id":"ccar-f-1","d":"architecture","x":"A developer asks the agent to investigate why a specific API endpoint intermittently returns 500 responses. The codebase has 200+ files and the developer doesn't know which components are involved. The agent must trace the error through routing, middleware, business logic, and database layers.\n\nWhat task decomposition approach would be most effective?","o":["Have the agent first create a comprehensive plan mapping all code paths through documented components before beginning any exploration or code reading.","Run parallel worker agents on the file system of four layers, then combine their findings to reconstruct the complete error path.","Define a fixed sequence of investigation steps upfront—grep for error handlers, then examine middleware, then check database queries, then examine middleware-woven code.","Have the agent dynamically generate investigation subtasks based on what it discovers at each step, adapting its exploration plan as new information about the error path emerges."],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Debugging is adaptive by nature — each file you read changes the most useful next step. Let the agent follow the evidence."],"e":"Debugging is adaptive by nature — each file you read changes the most useful next step. Let the agent follow the evidence.","t":"Unknown bug path = ADAPTIVE investigation, follow the evidence.","k":["dynamic decomposition","debugging","adaptive"]},{"id":"ccar-f-2","d":"claude-code","x":"Your agent needs to insert a new helper function into the middle of a 150-line utility module, between two existing functions. The Edit tool fails because its old_string parameter cannot find unique text to match – the file has repetitive docstrings, variable names, and structural patterns.\n\nWhat's the most reliable way to complete this insertion?","o":["Use Bash to append the function definition to the end of the file using heredoc syntax","Use Edit's replace_all parameter to target a common pattern and embed the new function in the replacement text","Use Edit with an extremely long old_string capturing 30+ lines of context to guarantee uniqueness","Use Read to load the file, add the function at the appropriate location, then Write the updated file"],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","When old_string cannot find unique text because the file has many repeating patterns, the most reliable way is to read the entire file (Read), edit"],"e":"When old_string cannot find unique text because the file has many repeating patterns, the most reliable way is to read the entire file (Read), edit","t":"Edit can't find unique anchor = READ + WRITE whole file.","k":["Read","Write","Edit fallback"]},{"id":"ccar-f-3","d":"architecture","x":"An engineer used Claude Code yesterday to investigate authentication flows in a legacy monolith, building up significant context over a 2-hour session. Today she wants to continue that specific investigation. She's worked on three other codebases since then and knows the session was named \"auth-deep-dive\".\n\nHow should she resume?","o":["Use --resume auth-deep-dive to load that specific session by name","Use --session-id with the UUID from yesterday's session transcript file","Use --continue to pick up where the most recent conversation left off","Start fresh and re-read the same files"],"a":[0],"w":["--resume allows resuming a specific session by name. --continue only continues the most recent session (not the session 2 days ago), and --session-id needs a specific UUID, not a name.","Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem."],"e":"--resume allows resuming a specific session by name. --continue only continues the most recent session (not the session 2 days ago), and --session-id needs a specific UUID, not a name.","t":"By NAME = --resume. Most recent = --continue.","k":["--resume","named session","--continue"]},{"id":"ccar-f-4","d":"architecture","x":"An engineer's exploration subagent spent 30 minutes analyzing a legacy payment system, reading 47 files and documenting data flows. The session was interrupted when the engineer's connection dropped. While away, a teammate merged a PR that renamed two utility functions. The engineer wants to continue the same exploration.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Resume the subagent from its previous transcript without mentioning the changes–the architecture understanding remains valid.","Launch a fresh subagent and include the prior transcript in the initial prompt for context.","Launch a fresh subagent with a summary of prior findings.","Resume the subagent from its previous transcript and inform it about the renamed functions."],"a":[3],"w":["Resuming without explicitly incorporating the new changes risks carrying stale assumptions forward into the next step.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Continuing from the old transcript with updated information about renamed functions is optimal: keep the accumulated 30 minutes of architectural context intact, while notifying agents about specific changes to avoid re-analyzing from scratch."],"e":"Continuing from the old transcript with updated information about renamed functions is optimal: keep the accumulated 30 minutes of architectural context intact, while notifying agents about specific changes to avoid re-analyzing from scratch.","t":"Resume + tell it WHAT CHANGED.","k":["resume","renamed functions","context"]},{"id":"ccar-f-5","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your agent has spent 25 minutes exploring a game engine's rendering subsystem–reading shader code, buffer management, and frame synchronization logic. An engineer now asks it to understand how the physics engine integrates with rendering for collision debug overlays. You notice recent responses reference \"typical rendering patterns\" rather than the specific VulkanPipeline and FrameGraph classes it discovered earlier.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Use /clear to reset context completely, then start fresh with physics exploration using file paths from the project's CLAUDE.md.","Continue in the current context with more targeted prompts referencing the specific classes by name.","Spawn a sub-agent to explore physics independently, then manually synthesize its findings with the rendering knowledge accumulated in the main conversation.","Summarize key rendering findings, then spawn a sub-agent for physics exploration with that summary in its initial context."],"a":[3],"w":["Resetting context can throw away useful information and does not by itself create a better investigation strategy.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The generic \"typical rendering patterns\" responses signal context degradation from a long, bloated session, so the fix is to compact the valuable rendering knowledge into a summary and hand it to a sub-agent with a fresh context for the physics task. This follows Anthropic's context-management best practice of summarization plus sub-agents with clean contexts, preserving the specific VulkanPipeline/FrameGraph findings that the integration task depends on, unlike A (loses knowledge), B (sub-agent lacks needed rendering context and synthesis happens in the degraded context), or D (continues in a degraded context)."],"e":"The generic \"typical rendering patterns\" responses signal context degradation from a long, bloated session, so the fix is to compact the valuable rendering knowledge into a summary and hand it to a sub-agent with a fresh context for the physics task. This follows Anthropic's context-management best practice of summarization plus sub-agents with clean contexts, preserving the specific VulkanPipeline/FrameGraph findings that the integration task depends on, unlike A (loses knowledge), B (sub-agent lacks needed rendering context and synthesis happens in the degraded context), or D (continues in a degraded context).","t":"Generic answers = bloated context. SUMMARIZE + fresh SUBAGENT.","k":["context degradation","summary","subagent"]},{"id":"ccar-f-6","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer asks the agent to find all callers of a function before removing it. The function is defined in a core library but is also exposed through wrapper modules that rename the function for domain-specific use (e.g., calculateTax in the library becomes computeOrderTax in the orders module).\n\nWhat exploration strategy will most reliably identify all callers?","o":["Search for the function name in project documentation to understand intended usage patterns and navigate to documented integration points.","Use Grep to search for the function's original name across the codebase.","Read the library and wrapper modules to identify all exposed names for the function, then Grep for each name across the codebase.","Use Grep to find all files that import from the library or wrapper modules, then read each file to check whether it uses the function."],"a":[2],"w":["Documentation may give useful background, but it is not a reliable way to discover every real caller or usage path in code.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Because the function is exposed through many wrapper modules with different names, it is necessary to determine ALL names (original + aliases in wrappers), then Grep each name. Only Grep the original name (B) will miss callers using wrappers.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"Because the function is exposed through many wrapper modules with different names, it is necessary to determine ALL names (original + aliases in wrappers), then Grep each name. Only Grep the original name (B) will miss callers using wrappers.","t":"Find ALL aliases first, then grep each.","k":["Grep","aliases","wrapper modules"]},{"id":"ccar-f-7","d":"context-reliability","x":"During testing, you observe that in extended exploration sessions (30+ minutes), the agent starts giving inconsistent answers about code structure it discussed earlier. Engineers report having to repeat context about modules they've already explored.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to address this?","o":["Switch to a higher-capacity model tier to provide more context window space for accumulated exploration data.","Have the agent maintain a scratchpad file that records key findings, referencing it for subsequent questions.","Create summaries of all source files before exploration begins, loading only these compressed representations into context.","Implement automatic context clearing every 15 minutes to ensure the agent starts with fresh, uncontaminated context."],"a":[1],"w":["Changing model size affects capacity or cost, but it does not directly fix the workflow mistake described in the question.","Context window is limited and after 30+ minutes, old information is pushed out. Maintain a scratchpad file to record key findings so the agent can refer back to them without depending on memory in the context.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Clearing context blindly removes useful information and treats the symptom rather than designing a better way to preserve what matters."],"e":"Context window is limited and after 30+ minutes, old information is pushed out. Maintain a scratchpad file to record key findings so the agent can refer back to them without depending on memory in the context.","t":"Long session forgetting? Keep a SCRATCHPAD file.","k":["scratchpad","context window","long sessions"]},{"id":"ccar-f-8","d":"tools","x":"After adding an MCP server with specialized code refactoring tools (extract_function, rename_variable, inline_function), you notice the agent still uses basic text manipulation via Write and Bash sed commands for refactoring tasks. The MCP server is connected and working. Examining the configuration, you find each MCP tool has a minimal description like \"extract_function: extracts a function from code.\"\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve adoption of the MCP refactoring tools?","o":["Enhance the MCP tool descriptions to explain when each tool is preferable to text manipulation and clarify expected inputs and outputs.","Remove the Write tool from the agent's configuration for refactoring sessions so it must use the MCP tools for code modifications.","Implement a request classifier that detects refactoring intent and automatically routes those requests to the MCP server before the agent processes them.","Accept this as expected behavior since simpler tools like sed are more predictable than specialized refactoring tools."],"a":[0],"w":["Tool selection is driven by the descriptions Claude sees. When the MCP tools say 'extracts a function from code' and Write/sed come with rich documentation, Claude picks Write/sed. Beef up the descriptions.","Forcing tool use by removing alternatives may change behavior, but it does not solve the real issue of helping the agent understand when the specialized tool is appropriate.","This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","Treating the behavior as acceptable leaves the adoption problem unsolved instead of improving the agent’s decision-making."],"e":"Tool selection is driven by the descriptions Claude sees. When the MCP tools say 'extracts a function from code' and Write/sed come with rich documentation, Claude picks Write/sed. Beef up the descriptions.","t":"Tools ignored? Improve their DESCRIPTIONS.","k":["tool descriptions","MCP","tool selection"]},{"id":"ccar-f-9","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer asks the agent to investigate error handling in a legacy payment processing module spanning 15 files. After reading the first 8 files, the agent's responses are becoming noticeably less accurate—it's forgetting previously discovered code patterns and hasn't yet located all test files or traced the complete data flow.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to complete this investigation?","o":["Close the current context with /clear and start fresh, re-reading only the most critical files.","Spawn a subagent to explore the remaining files, providing a summary of discovered patterns as its initial context.","Use Grep to search for specific function names across remaining files, reducing the content loaded into context.","Document a summary of findings so far in a file, then start fresh with a new context using that summary as reference."],"a":[1],"w":["Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem.","When the agent is losing context after reading too many files, spawning a new subagent with a summary of the current findings is the optimal way to continue the investigation. The main agent coordinates while the subagent explores with clean context.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem."],"e":"When the agent is losing context after reading too many files, spawning a new subagent with a summary of the current findings is the optimal way to continue the investigation. The main agent coordinates while the subagent explores with clean context.","t":"Context full mid-task = SUBAGENT with a summary.","k":["subagent","summary","context limits"]},{"id":"ccar-f-10","d":"prompt-output","x":"An engineer used the agent yesterday to analyze a legacy authentication module, identifying two distinct refactoring approaches: extracting a microservice versus refactoring in-place. Today, they want to explore both approaches in depth–having the agent propose specific code changes for each–before deciding which to implement.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to structure this exploration?","o":["Use fork_session to create two branches from yesterday's analysis, exploring one approach in each fork.","Resume yesterday's session and explore both approaches sequentially within the same conversation thread.","Start two fresh sessions, manually providing a summary of yesterday's analysis findings to establish context.","Resume yesterday's session to explore the first approach, then start a new session for the second, manually recreating the original context."],"a":[0],"w":["fork_session allows creating two branches from the same analysis point, each branch exploring a different approach without affecting each other, while keeping the entire context from the previous session intact.","Resuming without explicitly incorporating the new changes risks carrying stale assumptions forward into the next step.","Fresh sessions lose the advantage of the accumulated context you already have and create unnecessary rework.","Resuming without explicitly incorporating the new changes risks carrying stale assumptions forward into the next step."],"e":"fork_session allows creating two branches from the same analysis point, each branch exploring a different approach without affecting each other, while keeping the entire context from the previous session intact.","t":"Two paths from one point = FORK the session.","k":["fork_session","branching","parallel exploration"]},{"id":"ccar-f-11","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your agent has analyzed a complex service module–reading 23 source files, tracing request flows, and identifying error handling patterns. A developer wants to compare two testing strategies before committing to one: end-to-end tests with mocked external services vs. snapshot tests capturing expected outputs. They need to independently develop both approaches to evaluate trade-offs.\n\nHow should you manage the sessions?","o":["Continue in the original session, developing end-to-end tests first, then snapshot tests sequentially.","Resume the analysis session with fork_session enabled, creating a separate branch for each testing strategy.","Export the analysis session's key findings to a file, then create two new sessions that reference this file.","Start two fresh sessions, having each re-read the relevant source files before beginning."],"a":[1],"w":["Handling both paths sequentially misses the point of keeping the branches separate and makes comparison or independent exploration harder.","fork_session allows creating two branches from the same analysis point, each branch develops an independent testing strategy without affecting each other, while keeping the entire context from the analysis session intact.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem."],"e":"fork_session allows creating two branches from the same analysis point, each branch develops an independent testing strategy without affecting each other, while keeping the entire context from the analysis session intact.","t":"Compare two strategies independently = FORK.","k":["fork_session","testing strategies"]},{"id":"ccar-f-12","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your codebase exploration tool stores session IDs to allow engineers to continue investigations across work sessions. An engineer spent an hour yesterday analyzing a legacy authentication module, building context about its architecture and dependencies. They want to continue today. The session ID is valid, but version control shows 3 of the 12 files the agent previously read were modified overnight by a teammate's merge.\n\nWhat approach best balances efficiency and accuracy?","o":["Resume the session without informing the agent about the changed files","Resume the session and inform the agent which specific files changed for targeted re-analysis","Start a fresh session to ensure the agent works with current codebase state without stale assumptions","Resume the session and immediately have the agent re-read all 12 previously analyzed files"],"a":[1],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Resume session + notification of changed files is the most balanced way: keep the context of the 9 files unchanged (avoid costly re-reads), and update the agent about new changes to ensure accuracy.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem."],"e":"Resume session + notification of changed files is the most balanced way: keep the context of the 9 files unchanged (avoid costly re-reads), and update the agent about new changes to ensure accuracy.","t":"Resume + name the CHANGED files only.","k":["resume","changed files","targeted re-analysis"]},{"id":"ccar-f-13","d":"prompt-output","x":"An engineer asks the agent to understand how the caching layer works before adding a new cache invalidation trigger. After initial Grep searches, the agent has identified that caching logic spans 15 files including decorators, middleware, and service classes (~8,000 lines total).\n\nWhat's the most effective next step for building understanding while managing context constraints?","o":["Use the Read tool to sequentially load all 15 files, building complete understanding across the full caching implementation.","Analyze imports and class hierarchies to identify the base cache class, Read that file to understand the interface, then trace specific invalidation implementations.","Use Glob to find files matching common caching patterns (cache.py, caching/), prioritize the largest files by reading them first, then check smaller files for gaps.","Use Grep to search for \"invalidate\" and \"expire\" patterns across all files, then Read only those specific line ranges with minimal surrounding context."],"a":[1],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Starting from the class hierarchy to find the base cache class, reading the interface, then trace implementations is the most effective way. Avoid reading all 15 files (too much context) or just searching for patterns (lacking architectural context).","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"Starting from the class hierarchy to find the base cache class, reading the interface, then trace implementations is the most effective way. Avoid reading all 15 files (too much context) or just searching for patterns (lacking architectural context).","t":"Start from the BASE class, then trace down.","k":["class hierarchy","base class","context management"]},{"id":"ccar-f-14","d":"tools","x":"An engineer who just joined the team asks the agent to help them understand the authentication and authorization architecture before making security improvements. The codebase has 800+ files across multiple services.\n\nWhat exploration strategy will most effectively build understanding, given Claude built-in tools and context limits?","o":["Read all files containing \"auth\", \"login\", \"permission\", or \"token\" in their content or filename.","Read any CLAUDE.md and README files first, then ask the engineer to specify which 10-15 files are most important for understanding the auth system.","Launch parallel subagents to explore different services simultaneously, then synthesize their findings into an architectural overview.","Use Grep to find authentication entry points, read those files, then follow imports and function calls to map the auth flow incrementally."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","With a codebase of 800+ files, incremental exploration from entry points and by imports is the most effective way to build structured understanding without being overwhelmed by context. Reading all related files (B) would consume too much context."],"e":"With a codebase of 800+ files, incremental exploration from entry points and by imports is the most effective way to build structured understanding without being overwhelmed by context. Reading all related files (B) would consume too much context.","t":"Big codebase = ENTRY POINT + follow imports.","k":["entry points","incremental exploration","Grep"]},{"id":"ccar-f-15","d":"tools","x":"After integrating a local MCP server providing code analysis tools (analyze_dependencies, find_dead_code, calculate_complexity), you notice the agent continues to use Grep to inspect dependencies even when users explicitly ask about \"code dependencies\". Tool definitions reveal: analyze_dependencies returns a dependency graph by analyzing imports.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to improve the agent's selection of MCP tools?","o":["Expand MCP tool descriptions and outputs–e.g., \"Builds dependency graph with list_imports, direct_circular_deps\"–to clearly distinguish dependencies and cycle analysis from Grep.","Remove Grep from the available tools to eliminate functional overlap.","Split analyze_dependencies into granular tools: list_imports, detect_transitive_deps, identify_circular_deps to avoid overlap with Grep.","Add routing questions to the system prompt specifying that dependency-related questions should use MCP tools rather than Grep."],"a":[0],"w":["Agents do not use MCP tools because the tool descriptions are not detailed enough to distinguish when to use them versus Grep. Expand descriptions with specific output examples to help agents understand and choose the right tool.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"Agents do not use MCP tools because the tool descriptions are not detailed enough to distinguish when to use them versus Grep. Expand descriptions with specific output examples to help agents understand and choose the right tool.","t":"Agent ignores MCP tool? Make its DESCRIPTION clearer than Grep's.","k":["tool descriptions","MCP","Grep overlap"]},{"id":"ccar-f-16","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction pipeline processes contracts that frequently include amendments. When a contract contains both original terms and later amendments (e.g., original clause specifies \"30-day payment terms\" while Amendment 1 changes this to \"45 days\"), the model inconsistently extracts one value or the other with no indication of which applies.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to improve extraction accuracy for documents with amendments?","o":["Add prompt instructions to always extract the most recent amendment value and ignore superseded original terms.","Implement post-extraction validation using pattern matching to detect amendments and flag those extractions for manual review.","Preprocess documents with a classifier that identifies and removes superseded sections before the main extraction step.","Redesign the schema so amended fields capture multiple values, each with source location and effective date."],"a":[3],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This defers the problem rather than solving the system behavior that caused it.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","When a document has both original terms and amendments, the schema should capture both values ​​along with effective date and source location. This preserves complete information and allows downstream systems to decide for themselves which values ​​to apply."],"e":"When a document has both original terms and amendments, the schema should capture both values ​​along with effective date and source location. This preserves complete information and allows downstream systems to decide for themselves which values ​​to apply.","t":"Conflicting versions = capture ALL with DATE + SOURCE.","k":["schema design","amendments","effective date"]},{"id":"ccar-f-17","d":"prompt-output","x":"After deployment, you find that 12% of extractions contain semantic errors that pass JSON schema validation (e.g., a duration like \"30 minutes\" incorrectly placed in an ingredient quantity field). Human reviewers have capacity to check only 20% of extractions.\n\nWhich approach most effectively allocates reviewer attention?","o":["Review all extractions from documents with formatting anomalies such as unusual layouts or mixed content types.","Prioritize review of all extractions where required fields are empty or explicitly marked as not found.","Randomly sample 20% of extractions for review, using corrections to track accuracy and identify error patterns.","Have the model output field-level confidence scores, then calibrate review thresholds using a labeled validation set."],"a":[3],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Semantic errors are difficult to detect through schema validation. To optimize 20% reviewer capacity, model output field-level confidence scores and then use labeled validation set to find the optimal threshold, focusing review on extractions with low confidence."],"e":"Semantic errors are difficult to detect through schema validation. To optimize 20% reviewer capacity, model output field-level confidence scores and then use labeled validation set to find the optimal threshold, focusing review on extractions with low confidence.","t":"Limited reviewers = FIELD CONFIDENCE + calibrated threshold.","k":["confidence scores","calibration","human review"]},{"id":"ccar-f-18","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction pipeline processes invoices and extracts line items, tax amounts, and grand totals. You discover that 8% of documents have extracted line item amounts that don't sum to the extracted grand total—the downstream accounting system has been detecting mismatches.\n\nWhat's the most effective improvement?","o":["Add a calculated_total field where the model extracts line items independently, then validate totals alongside a is_total_consistent boolean flag for human review when values differ.","Implement post-processing that automatically adjusts line item amounts when the extracted total doesn't match the sum.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt where extracted line items correctly sum to demonstrate the desired extraction behavior.","Extract line items and totals independently, then use a separate validation model to reconcile discrepancies."],"a":[0],"w":["Add the calculated_total field so the model can self-validate by calculating the total from line items and comparing with the grand total, combined with a boolean flag to flag cases that need review. This method detects mismatches right in the extraction step.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Add the calculated_total field so the model can self-validate by calculating the total from line items and comparing with the grand total, combined with a boolean flag to flag cases that need review. This method detects mismatches right in the extraction step.","t":"Self-check math: CALCULATED_TOTAL + consistency flag.","k":["calculated_total","self-validation","consistency flag"]},{"id":"ccar-f-19","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your pipeline uses a tool called extract_metadata with a JSON schema for paper details. During testing, the agent sometimes fails to call extract_metadata because it wants to provide direct answers.\n\nWhat's the most reliable way to ensure metadata extraction always happens first?","o":["Set tool_choice to \"auto\" and reorder the tool definitions placing extract_metadata first since Claude prioritizes earlier-listed tools.","Set tool_choice to \"auto\" in the API call, combined with tools so that Claude always prioritizes metadata before any other tool calls.","Set tool_choice to type \"any\" so Claude must use a tool, combined with system prompt instructions to call extract_metadata first.","Set tool_choice to type \"tool\", name \"extract_metadata\" and process the enrichment requests in subsequent turns after receiving the extracted metadata."],"a":[3],"w":["This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Using tool_choice with type \"tool\" and name \"extract_metadata\" forces Claude to call the correct tool first. After receiving metadata, enrichment requests are processed in subsequent turns. This is the only way that is 100% guaranteed."],"e":"Using tool_choice with type \"tool\" and name \"extract_metadata\" forces Claude to call the correct tool first. After receiving metadata, enrichment requests are processed in subsequent turns. This is the only way that is 100% guaranteed.","t":"Force ONE specific tool = tool_choice {type: tool, name}.","k":["tool_choice","forced tool","extract_metadata"]},{"id":"ccar-f-20","d":"prompt-output","x":"After your daily batch of 10,000 documents completes, 300 documents (3%) failed with \"context_length_exceeded\" errors. The results file identifies each failure by custom_id.\n\nWhat's the most cost-effective approach to process these failures?","o":["Resubmit the entire 10,000 document batch using a model tier with a larger context window","Resubmit only the 300 failed documents after chunking them into smaller pieces, then combine the partial extractions","Reprocess the entire batch with prompt caching enabled to reduce the cost of retrying requests with identical system prompts","Increase the max_tokens parameter for the 300 failed documents and resubmit them in a new batch"],"a":[1],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","context_length_exceeded is an error because the input is too long, not the output. Just resubmit 300 failed documents after chunking them into smaller pieces. No need to reprocess all 10,000 documents or increase max_tokens.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"context_length_exceeded is an error because the input is too long, not the output. Just resubmit 300 failed documents after chunking them into smaller pieces. No need to reprocess all 10,000 documents or increase max_tokens.","t":"Input too long = CHUNK and retry only the failures.","k":["context_length_exceeded","chunking","custom_id"]},{"id":"ccar-f-21","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction system parses e-commerce product descriptions to extract specifications like dimensions, weight, and materials into JSON. Despite having a well-defined schema, the model inconsistently extracts the \"materials\" field–sometimes returning \"cotton blend\", other times \"Cotton/Polyester mix\", and occasionally omitting the field when material information is clearly present in the source.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve extraction consistency?","o":["Switch to a more capable model tier since inconsistent extraction indicates insufficient model capability","Make the \"materials\" field required instead of optional in the schema to force the model to always extract a value","Set temperature to 0 to eliminate randomness and ensure deterministic outputs","Add few-shot examples showing 2-3 complete input-output pairs with standardized material description formats"],"a":[3],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Inconsistency in extraction is often due to the lack of specific examples of standard formats. Few-shot examples with standardized material descriptions teach the model to format output properly, more effectively than setting temperature=0 or making field required."],"e":"Inconsistency in extraction is often due to the lack of specific examples of standard formats. Few-shot examples with standardized material descriptions teach the model to format output properly, more effectively than setting temperature=0 or making field required.","t":"Inconsistent FORMAT = FEW-SHOT examples.","k":["few-shot","format consistency"]},{"id":"ccar-f-22","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your system extracts event metadata (date, location, organizer, attendee_count) from news articles using a JSON schema with all nullable fields. During evaluation, you observe the model frequently generates plausible but incorrect values for fields not mentioned in the article–for example, outputting \"500\" for attendee_count when the source contains no attendance information.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to reduce these false extractions?","o":["Add a post-processing step using a second LLM call to verify each extracted value exists in the source document.","Add prompt instructions to return null for any field where information is not directly stated in the source.","Make all schema fields required (non-nullable) with strict validation rules to ensure the model only outputs verifiable data.","Upgrade to a more capable model tier with improved instruction-following to reduce hallucination tendencies."],"a":[1],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Model hallucinating probable values ​​for fields are not present in the source due to lack of clear 'no information' instructions. Adding explicit instruction 'return null if the information is not directly in the source' directly solves the problem.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"Model hallucinating probable values ​​for fields are not present in the source due to lack of clear 'no information' instructions. Adding explicit instruction 'return null if the information is not directly in the source' directly solves the problem.","t":"Not in source? Say NULL, don't guess.","k":["null","hallucination","nullable fields"]},{"id":"ccar-f-23","d":"prompt-output","x":"After implementing tool use with strict schema definitions, JSON syntax errors are eliminated, but 5% of extractions still have valid JSON with empty arrays or null values for required fields like citations and methodology. Spot-checking reveals that source documents contain this information, but in varied formats–inline citations vs. bibliographies, methodology sections vs. details embedded in introductions.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to address these failures?","o":["Implement retry logic that re-sends requests when validation detects empty required fields.","Build a regex-based post-processing layer that scans source documents for citation patterns and methodology keywords, populating empty fields when the model fails to extract.","Modify your schema to make citations and methodology optional, and flag incomplete records for manual review rather than failing validation.","Add few-shot examples demonstrating extractions from documents with varied structures–showing how to identify citations in different formats and locate methodology details across section types."],"a":[3],"w":["This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Manual review can be useful as a fallback, but it does not solve the system behavior that the question is asking you to improve.","Since the same information appears in many different formats (inline vs bibliography, section vs embedded), these few-shot examples showing how to identify and extract from these formats are the most effective way to improve extraction."],"e":"Since the same information appears in many different formats (inline vs bibliography, section vs embedded), these few-shot examples showing how to identify and extract from these formats are the most effective way to improve extraction.","t":"Same info, many layouts = FEW-SHOT on varied docs.","k":["few-shot","varied structures","citations"]},{"id":"ccar-f-24","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction system implements automatic retries when validation fails. On each retry, the specific validation error is appended to the prompt. This retry-with-error-feedback approach resolves most failures within 2-3 attempts\n\nFor which failure pattern would additional retries be LEAST effective?","o":["The model extracts \"et al.\" for co-authors when the full list exists only in an external document not in the input","The model extracts keywords as a nested object organized by category when the schema requires a flat array of strings","The model extracts citation counts as locale-formatted strings (\"1,234\") when the schema requires integers","The model extracts dates as ISO 8601 datetime strings (\"2023-03-15T00:00:00Z\") when the schema requires only the date portion (YYYY-MM-DD)"],"a":[0],"w":["No amount of retrying teaches the model information that isn't in the input. Retry-with-error-feedback only fixes mistakes the model could have gotten right from the source.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"No amount of retrying teaches the model information that isn't in the input. Retry-with-error-feedback only fixes mistakes the model could have gotten right from the source.","t":"Retries can't create MISSING info.","k":["retry with feedback","missing information"]},{"id":"ccar-f-25","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction pipeline processes restaurant menus and must output structured JSON with fields for item names, descriptions, prices, and dietary tags. Some menus use inconsistent formatting–prices as \"$12\" vs \"12.00\", dietary info as icons vs text.\n\nWhat's the most reliable approach?","o":["Request multiple extraction attempts per document and select the most common format.","Define a strict output schema and include format normalization rules in your prompt.","Use separate extraction calls for each field to ensure consistent handling of each type.","Extract data as-is and normalize formats in post-processing code after Claude returns."],"a":[1],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","For menus with inconsistent formatting, combining strict output schema (structure) with normalization rules right in the prompt (for example: 'prices as float without currency symbol') normalize right in the extraction step, is the most effective and simple.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"For menus with inconsistent formatting, combining strict output schema (structure) with normalization rules right in the prompt (for example: 'prices as float without currency symbol') normalize right in the extraction step, is the most effective and simple.","t":"Strict SCHEMA + NORMALIZATION rules in the prompt.","k":["output schema","normalization","menus"]},{"id":"ccar-f-26","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction uses tool use with a JSON schema where property_type is defined as an enum: ['house', 'apartment', 'condo', 'townhouse']. After deployment, 8% of extractions fail schema validation. Investigation reveals listings mention many uncommon property types –\"studio\", \"loft\", \"duplex\", \"mobile home\", \"tiny house\", \"converted warehouse\"–and new types continue appearing regularly.\n\nWhat's the most effective long-term solution?","o":["Add an \"other\" value to your enum with a separate property_type_detail string field for specifics when \"other\" is selected.","Add few-shot examples to your prompt demonstrating how to map unexpected property types to the closest existing enum value.","Continuously expand the enum to include newly observed property types and add monitoring for additional edge cases.","Change property_type from an enum to a free-form string and implement a normalization step in post-processing."],"a":[0],"w":["Because new property types constantly appear, hard enums will always be outdated. Adding 'other' value with its own property_type_detail field allows handling all current and future edge cases without constantly updating the enum.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"Because new property types constantly appear, hard enums will always be outdated. Adding 'other' value with its own property_type_detail field allows handling all current and future edge cases without constantly updating the enum.","t":"Open-ended enum = add OTHER + detail field.","k":["enum","other","schema evolution"]},{"id":"ccar-f-27","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your system has been operating with 100% human review for 3 months. Analysis shows that extractions with model confidence >90% have 97% accuracy overall. To reduce reviewer workload, you plan to automate high-confidence extractions\n\nBefore deploying, what validation step is most critical?","o":["Run a two-week pilot routing 25% of high-confidence extractions directly to downstream systems and monitor error reports.","Analyze accuracy by document type and field to verify high-confidence extractions perform consistently across all segments, not just in aggregate.","Compare accuracy at different confidence thresholds (85%, 90%, 95%) to find the optimal cutoff that maximizes automation while minimizing errors.","Verify that 97% accuracy meets requirements for all downstream systems that consume the extracted data."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Aggregate accuracy hides segment failures — one document type could be 70% while others are 99%. Auto-routing by overall number alone risks systemic errors in the weak segments.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Aggregate accuracy hides segment failures — one document type could be 70% while others are 99%. Auto-routing by overall number alone risks systemic errors in the weak segments.","t":"Aggregate hides weak SEGMENTS. Slice by type and field.","k":["segment analysis","aggregate accuracy","automation"]},{"id":"ccar-f-28","d":"prompt-output","x":"Documents arrive continuously throughout business hours and need structured data extracted. To reduce costs, you want to use the Message Batches API (50% discount, up-to-24-hour processing window). Your SLA specifies that extraction results must be available within 30 hours of document arrival with 99.9% reliability.\n\nWhich batching strategy is most appropriate?","o":["Submit batches every 6 hours containing documents from that window","Submit a single batch at end of day containing all documents from that day","Submit batches every 4 hours containing documents from that window","Use the real-time API for all documents instead of batch processing"],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Submitting batches every 4 hours ensures that in the worst case, documents wait a maximum of 4 hours + 24 hours of processing = 28 hours < 30 hours SLA. Submitting every 6 hours may violate the SLA (6 + 24 = 30 hours without a safety buffer).","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Submitting batches every 4 hours ensures that in the worst case, documents wait a maximum of 4 hours + 24 hours of processing = 28 hours < 30 hours SLA. Submitting every 6 hours may violate the SLA (6 + 24 = 30 hours without a safety buffer).","t":"Wait + 24h must be < SLA. 4h + 24h = 28h.","k":["Message Batches API","SLA","batch window"]},{"id":"ccar-f-29","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your schema includes a skills: string[] field. Production monitoring reveals three consistency issues: (1) compound phrases like \"Python and SQL\" are sometimes kept as one entry, sometimes split; (2) implied but unstated skills occasionally appear in extractions; (3) similar documents produce wildly different array lengths (5-10 vs 40+ entries). Your prompt currently says \"Extract all skills mentioned.\"\n\nWhat's the most effective improvement?","o":["Add few-shot examples demonstrating compound phrase handling, explicit mention criteria, and appropriate entry granularity.","Add constraints: \"Extract 10-20 skills maximum, one skill per entry, only explicitly named skills.\"","Add post-extraction normalization that maps skills to a canonical taxonomy and deduplicates similar entries.","Enrich the schema to {skill: string, confidence: float, source_quote: string[]} to capture extraction metadata."],"a":[0],"w":["The three problems (compound phrases, implied skills, length inconsistency) all stem from the lack of specific examples of expected behavior. Few-shot examples demonstrating compound phrase handling, explicit mention criteria, and granularity address all three issues directly.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"The three problems (compound phrases, implied skills, length inconsistency) all stem from the lack of specific examples of expected behavior. Few-shot examples demonstrating compound phrase handling, explicit mention criteria, and granularity address all three issues directly.","t":"Fuzzy rules in many places = FEW-SHOT shows the pattern.","k":["few-shot","granularity","skills extraction"]},{"id":"ccar-f-30","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction system processes two document types: standard monthly reports (archived after processing) and urgent exception reports (must trigger business alerts within 30 minutes of receipt). Both use the same JSON schema. You want to minimize API costs while meeting latency requirements.\n\nHow should you architect the processing pipeline?","o":["Route standard reports to the Batch API for 50% cost savings, and route urgent exception reports to the real-time Messages API.","Queue all documents and submit hourly batches, flagging urgent documents for expedited handling when batch results return.","Submit all documents to the real-time Messages API to ensure consistent processing latency across document types.","Submit all documents to the Batch API with custom_ids for tracking. When results arrive, immediately process urgent documents and trigger delayed alerts for exceptions."],"a":[0],"w":["Standard reports (archived) conforms to the Batch API to save 50% on costs. Urgent exception reports need to trigger alerts within 30 minutes so must use the real-time Messages API. This is the optimal architecture for both cost and latency","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"Standard reports (archived) conforms to the Batch API to save 50% on costs. Urgent exception reports need to trigger alerts within 30 minutes so must use the real-time Messages API. This is the optimal architecture for both cost and latency","t":"Can wait = BATCH. Urgent = REAL-TIME.","k":["Batch API","Messages API","latency vs cost"]},{"id":"ccar-f-31","d":"architecture","x":"The document analysis agent has a single analyze_document tool that takes a document and a free-text instruction parameter. During evaluation, requests like \"extract the key financial metrics\" often return narrative summaries, while \"summarize the methodology\" sometimes returns raw data tables. The synthesis agent reports that 35% of analysis results require re-requests with clarified instructions.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve reliability?","o":["Enhance the tool description with detailed examples showing how different instruction phrasings should map to different output formats.","Split the generic tool into purpose-specific tools– extract_data_points, summarize_content, verify_claim_against_source –each with defined input/output contracts.","Keep the single tool but add an analysis_type enum parameter requiring explicit selection between extraction, summarization, and verification modes.","Have the coordinator pre-classify each analysis request before passing instructions to the document analysis agent."],"a":[1],"w":["Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","Generic analyze_document with free-text instructions leads to ambiguous and inconsistent outputs. Splitting into specialized tools with defined input/output contracts completely eliminates ambiguity, ensuring the agent always receives the correct output format.","Collapsing everything into one path reduces precision and usually makes the system harder to control, not easier.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"Generic analyze_document with free-text instructions leads to ambiguous and inconsistent outputs. Splitting into specialized tools with defined input/output contracts completely eliminates ambiguity, ensuring the agent always receives the correct output format.","t":"Generic tool confusing? SPLIT into purpose-specific tools.","k":["purpose-specific tools","contracts","tool design"]},{"id":"ccar-f-32","d":"architecture","x":"The coordinator agent has AgentFunctions configured for all four specialized subagents, each with appropriate descriptions and restrictions. During testing, you find the coordinator sometimes fails to delegate—it writes \"I'll ask the web search agent to find sources\" without actually invoking it.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":["The coordinator's allowed Tools configuration doesn't include \"Task\", so while it can describe delegation, it cannot actually invoke the tool required to spawn subagents.","Subagent context limit descriptions from the coordinator don't provide enough context; you need to configure explicit context content between coordinator and tool descriptions.","The coordinator's max_tokens setting is too low, causing the Task tool invocation to be truncated before the subagent parameter can be specified.","The AgentFunctions are configured correctly, but the coordinator's system prompt includes a statement that prevents it from knowing the available subagent types."],"a":[0],"w":["The coordinator can describe delegation (generating text), but cannot actually invoke subagents because the 'Task' tool is not in the allowed tools configuration. This is a common configuration error in multi-agent setup.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The coordinator can describe delegation (generating text), but cannot actually invoke subagents because the 'Task' tool is not in the allowed tools configuration. This is a common configuration error in multi-agent setup.","t":"Talks about delegating but can't = 'Task' missing from allowedTools.","k":["Task tool","allowedTools","subagents"]},{"id":"ccar-f-33","d":"architecture","x":"When researching \"renewable energy adoption,\" the web search agent returns recent statistics (2024: 35% adoption) while the document analysis agent extracts data from internal reports (2022: 18% adoption). The synthesis agent incorrectly flags these as contradictory sources rather than recognizing the data shows growth over time.\n\nWhat change would best enable the synthesis agent to correctly interpret such temporal differences?","o":["Instruct the synthesis agent to always treat the most recent data as authoritative and place older findings in a separate historical appendix.","Require subagents to include publication or data collection dates in their structured outputs.","Add a conflict resolution agent that automatically discards older data when newer data exists for the same metric.","Configure the web search agent to only return results from the past 6 months."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The synthesis agent misreads the data because it never sees the dates. Making each data point carry its own timestamp in the structured output lets synthesis reason about trends instead of contradictions.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The synthesis agent misreads the data because it never sees the dates. Making each data point carry its own timestamp in the structured output lets synthesis reason about trends instead of contradictions.","t":"Numbers need DATES to show trends.","k":["timestamps","structured output","temporal data"]},{"id":"ccar-f-34","d":"architecture","x":"Your multi-agent research pipeline crashed after processing 12 of 28 documents. The web search agent had identified relevant sources, the document analysis agent had partially completed extraction, and the synthesizer had begun pattern identification.\nYou need to resume processing without repeating work or losing fidelity of prior findings.\n\nWhat state management approach best balances information fidelity with context efficiency when restoring agent state?","o":["Index all agent outputs in a shared vector store. When resuming, each agent queries the store using semantic search to retrieve relevant prior findings.","Have each agent maintain its own persistent state file and reload it independently at the start of each session.","Persist the coordinator's conversation log containing all task delegations and responses, providing this to agents when resuming.","Have each agent persist a structured report to a known location. On resume, the coordinator loads the reports and injects relevant state into agent prompts."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Structured reports persist to known locations allowing the coordinator to load and inject state into agent prompts when resuming. This is more efficient than raw conversation logs (too verbose) or vector stores (more complicated than necessary)."],"e":"Structured reports persist to known locations allowing the coordinator to load and inject state into agent prompts when resuming. This is more efficient than raw conversation logs (too verbose) or vector stores (more complicated than necessary).","t":"Crash recovery = STRUCTURED reports + coordinator injects.","k":["state persistence","structured reports","resume"]},{"id":"ccar-f-35","d":"context-reliability","x":"Production reviews reveal inconsistent handling of uncertainty in final reports. Sometimes confidence calibrations are synthesized into standardized uncertainty expressions, while other times results produce vague estimates without clear methodology.\n\nWhich system-level improvement best addresses this inconsistency?","o":["Implement a confidence calibration layer where synthesis agent normalizes uncertainty expressions into standardized probability representations (0.0-1.0), then weights their calibrated confidence.","Configure subagents to only report findings with sufficient coverage breadth, source diversity, and quality criteria before passing results to the synthesis agent.","Instruct the synthesis agent to use explicit sections separating confirmed findings from contested analysis, preserving original source characterizations.","Add a verification subagent that cross-references findings across sources, only accepting synthesis corroborated by at least two independent sources."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The report structure clearly separates confirmed findings from those that are controversial, while preserving the original context – this is a way to ensure balance and accuracy without being too hedged in any case.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The report structure clearly separates confirmed findings from those that are controversial, while preserving the original context – this is a way to ensure balance and accuracy without being too hedged in any case.","t":"Separate CONFIRMED from CONTESTED.","k":["uncertainty","confirmed vs contested","report structure"]},{"id":"ccar-f-36","d":"architecture","x":"The coordinator provides detailed step-by-step instructions to the web search subagent, specifying exact search queries, source quality criteria (coverage breadth, source diversity), and content type classifications. The coordinator sometimes encounters challenges where instructions fail when the subagent encounters unexpected situations.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve subagent adaptability?","o":["Specify research goals and quality criteria (coverage breadth, source diversity, content type) rather than procedural instructions, letting the subagent determine execution.","Implement a task classification step where the coordinator categorizes requests as \"analytical\" vs \"exploratory\" and uses different instruction sets for each category.","Add explicit fallback directives to the detailed instructions: \"if specified searches fail, attempt alternative queries before reporting failure.\"","Remove procedural detail entirely, delegating with simple goals like \"research it thoroughly\" and relying on the subagent's general capabilities."],"a":[0],"w":["Step-by-step instructions are too rigid, making the subagent unable to adapt when encountering unexpected situations. Specifying goals and quality criteria instead allows the subagent to use judgment to handle unforeseen situations.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"Step-by-step instructions are too rigid, making the subagent unable to adapt when encountering unexpected situations. Specifying goals and quality criteria instead allows the subagent to use judgment to handle unforeseen situations.","t":"Give GOALS + criteria, not step-by-step scripts.","k":["goal-based delegation","adaptability","quality criteria"]},{"id":"ccar-f-37","d":"architecture","x":"A user is expanding the research system beyond its single web search agent by adding specialized data sources. They add a financial API agent that returns structured JSON with revenue, margins, and growth rates; a news monitoring agent that returns prose summaries of recent developments; and a patent analysis agent that returns structured lists of technology areas. The synthesis agent combines these into executive briefings. Currently, it converts everything to bullet points, causing financial comparisons to lose tabular clarity and news summaries to lose narrative flow.\n\nWhat change would most improve briefing quality?","o":["Standardize all subagent outputs to prose summaries with inline citations.","Update the synthesis agent to render each content type appropriately–financial data as tables, news as prose.","Standardize all subagent outputs to JSON with fields for claim, evidence, source, and confidence.","Add a format conversion layer between subagents and synthesis that transforms all outputs to a common intermediate representation."],"a":[1],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","The problem is that the synthesis agent converts everything to bullet points. The most direct solution is to update the synthesis agent to render each content type properly: financial data → tables, news → prose. No need to add layers or standardize outputs.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"The problem is that the synthesis agent converts everything to bullet points. The most direct solution is to update the synthesis agent to render each content type properly: financial data → tables, news → prose. No need to add layers or standardize outputs.","t":"Render each type in its natural FORMAT.","k":["content rendering","tables","prose"]},{"id":"ccar-f-38","d":"architecture","x":"The web search agent has gathered several relevant sources for a research topic. The document analysis agent now needs to examine these sources.\n\nHow does information typically flow between these two specialized subagents?","o":["The coordinator agent receives the web search agent's output and includes relevant findings in the prompt when invoking the document analysis agent.","The web search agent directly invokes the document analysis agent, passing the discovered sources as parameters.","Both agents access a shared memory store where the web search agent writes findings and the document analysis agent reads them.","The agents communicate through an event-driven message queue, with the document analysis agent subscribing to web search completion events."],"a":[0],"w":["In multi-agent architecture with coordinator, information flows through the coordinator: the web search agent returns output to the coordinator, the coordinator includes relevant findings in the prompt when invoking the document analysis agent. Subagents do not directly communicate with each other.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","This adds infrastructure but does not directly solve the failure mode described in the question.","That adds infrastructure or indirection, but the question is really about choosing a simpler coordination pattern that fits the current workflow."],"e":"In multi-agent architecture with coordinator, information flows through the coordinator: the web search agent returns output to the coordinator, the coordinator includes relevant findings in the prompt when invoking the document analysis agent. Subagents do not directly communicate with each other.","t":"Subagents talk through the COORDINATOR.","k":["coordinator","hub and spoke","context passing"]},{"id":"ccar-f-39","d":"architecture","x":"After the web search agent and document analysis agent complete their tasks, the coordinator invokes the synthesis agent. However, the synthesis agent responds that it cannot complete the task because no research findings were provided.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of this issue?","o":["The subagents need to share a single API connection to enable automatic context sharing between invocations.","The coordinator did not include the outputs from the previous agents in the synthesis agent's prompt.","The synthesis agent needs tools that can fetch results directly from the other agents' conversation histories.","The synthesis agent's context window is not large enough to hold the combined outputs from both previous agents."],"a":[1],"w":["It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Subagents do not automatically share context. The coordinator must proactively include outputs from previous agents into the prompt when invoking the synthesis agent. This is the most common error in multi-agent orchestration.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"Subagents do not automatically share context. The coordinator must proactively include outputs from previous agents into the prompt when invoking the synthesis agent. This is the most common error in multi-agent orchestration.","t":"No shared memory: coordinator must PASS outputs explicitly.","k":["context passing","coordinator","subagent isolation"]},{"id":"ccar-f-40","d":"architecture","x":"In production, final reports frequently contain claims without proper source attribution. Investigation shows that while the web search and document analysis agents correctly attach citations to their outputs, the synthesis agent loses track of which sources support which conclusions when combining findings.\n\nWhat's the most effective architectural change?","o":["Add a verification step where the report generator uses semantic similarity matching against original sources to reconstruct which claims came from which documents.","Maintain complete transcripts of all subagent interactions and add a citation-resolution agent to analyze logs and determine attributions before report generation.","Require all subagents to output structured claim-source mappings that the synthesis agent must preserve and merge when combining findings from multiple sources.","Have the coordinator inject source identifier prefixes into text before each handoff, then parse these prefixes at report generation to reconstruct citations."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","The failure is information loss at the synthesis stage, so the fix is to carry attribution as structured data end-to-end: subagents emit explicit claim-source mappings and the synthesis agent is required to preserve and merge them. This matches Anthropic's multi-agent best practice of structured outputs over fragile text-prefix conventions (A), costly post-hoc log analysis (B), or unreliable semantic-similarity reconstruction (D).","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way."],"e":"The failure is information loss at the synthesis stage, so the fix is to carry attribution as structured data end-to-end: subagents emit explicit claim-source mappings and the synthesis agent is required to preserve and merge them. This matches Anthropic's multi-agent best practice of structured outputs over fragile text-prefix conventions (A), costly post-hoc log analysis (B), or unreliable semantic-similarity reconstruction (D).","t":"Keep CLAIM-SOURCE pairs as structured data end to end.","k":["claim-source mapping","attribution","structured data"]},{"id":"ccar-f-41","d":"architecture","x":"Production monitoring shows that follow-up queries like \"summarize what we learned about market trends\" consistently take 40+ seconds. Investigation reveals the coordinator spawns the synthesis subagent for each summarization request, passing 80K+ tokens of accumulated findings. The coordinator already has these findings in its context from orchestrating the research.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve response time for these follow-up summaries?","o":["Spawn the synthesis subagent with reduced context and have it request specific findings from the coordinator on-demand.","Pre-generate and cache summaries at multiple granularities whenever new findings accumulate.","Have the coordinator handle straightforward summarization requests directly using its existing context, reserving subagent spawning for complex analysis.","Enable prompt caching on the synthesis subagent to reduce the overhead of repeatedly transferring the same research findings."],"a":[2],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The coordinator already has findings in context from the orchestration process. Spawn synthesis subagent just to summarize is overkill (80K+ token transfer overhead). It is simply the most efficient way for the coordinator to handle summary requests themselves.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The coordinator already has findings in context from the orchestration process. Spawn synthesis subagent just to summarize is overkill (80K+ token transfer overhead). It is simply the most efficient way for the coordinator to handle summary requests themselves.","t":"Already have the context? Answer DIRECTLY, skip the subagent.","k":["coordinator","latency","unnecessary subagent"]},{"id":"ccar-f-42","d":"architecture","x":"After the web search agent finds 25 sources (120K tokens of raw content), the document analysis agent extracts key insights (15K tokens), and the synthesis agent produces a coherent narrative draft (3K tokens), the coordinator must pass context to the report generation agent for the final output with proper source citations.\n\nWhat context-passing strategy provides the best balance of completeness and efficiency?","o":["Pass a condensed summary of all prior stages that preserves the main findings and attributes them to sources by name only.","Pass the full accumulated context from all prior agents.","Pass only the synthesis draft and have a separate post-processing pipeline match claims to sources and insert citations after the report is generated.","Pass the synthesis draft along with a structured source index that maps key claims to their source URLs and relevant excerpts."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Pass synthesis draft (3K tokens) + source index mapping claims to URLs/excerpts provides enough information for the report generator without transferring the entire 150K+ tokens raw content. Source index ensures accurate citations."],"e":"Pass synthesis draft (3K tokens) + source index mapping claims to URLs/excerpts provides enough information for the report generator without transferring the entire 150K+ tokens raw content. Source index ensures accurate citations.","t":"Draft + SOURCE INDEX, not the whole pile.","k":["source index","context efficiency","citations"]},{"id":"ccar-f-43","d":"architecture","x":"When analyzing complex legal cases that cite multiple precedents, the document analysis subagent processes each sequentially. A landmark case citing 12 precedents takes over 3 minutes to analyze completely.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to reduce this latency while preserving the coordinator's ability to monitor and debug the system?","o":["Have the coordinator spawn parallel document analysis subagents, each handling a subset of precedents, then aggregate results before synthesis.","Enable the document analysis subagent to spawn its own specialized subagents dynamically when it encounters cases with many citations.","Create a recursive agent hierarchy where analysis agents subdivide work among child agents until reaching single-precedent granularity.","Implement a message queue where precedent analysis tasks are processed asynchronously by a pool of worker agents."],"a":[0],"w":["Anthropic's recommended multi-agent design is the orchestrator-worker pattern with a flat hierarchy: the coordinator itself spawns parallel subagents and aggregates their results, which cuts latency through parallelism while keeping all spawning, monitoring, and debugging centralized in one place. Options B and C create nested/recursive agent hierarchies that severely degrade observability and debuggability, and D introduces asynchronous queue infrastructure that decouples work from the coordinator's direct visibility.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"Anthropic's recommended multi-agent design is the orchestrator-worker pattern with a flat hierarchy: the coordinator itself spawns parallel subagents and aggregates their results, which cuts latency through parallelism while keeping all spawning, monitoring, and debugging centralized in one place. Options B and C create nested/recursive agent hierarchies that severely degrade observability and debuggability, and D introduces asynchronous queue infrastructure that decouples work from the coordinator's direct visibility.","t":"Parallel workers spawned by the COORDINATOR (flat).","k":["parallel subagents","orchestrator-worker","flat hierarchy"]},{"id":"ccar-f-44","d":"architecture","x":"In production, you're distributing complex queries to specialized subagents. The query distribution is uneven and evolving as users discover new applications.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to optimize for query complexity?","o":["Create a fast-track path for factual questions that bypasses subagents entirely, routing only analytical queries through the complete pipeline.","Train a query complexity classifier based on labeled historical data to predict optimal subagent combination.","Have the coordinator analyze each query dynamically and selectively route subagents based on the query's complexity and routing characteristics.","Implement pattern-based routing that categorizes queries based on factual vs analytical patterns and maps each to a predefined subagent combination."],"a":[0],"w":["For distribution queries that are mostly factual/simple, create fast-track path bypass subagents for simple factual questions, only route analytical queries through the full pipeline. This method reduces latency and cost for the majority of queries.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"For distribution queries that are mostly factual/simple, create fast-track path bypass subagents for simple factual questions, only route analytical queries through the full pipeline. This method reduces latency and cost for the majority of queries.","t":"Simple questions skip the pipeline = FAST TRACK.","k":["fast-track","routing","latency"]},{"id":"ccar-f-45","d":"architecture","x":"The synthesis agent receives summarized findings from the web search and document analysis agents, then passes a consolidated summary to the report generator. During testing, you discover the generated reports make factual claims without proper citations–the report generator cannot attribute statements to their original sources because that metadata was lost during the summarization steps.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to ensure proper source attribution in the final reports?","o":["Instruct the synthesis agent to embed source references inline within its summary text using a consistent citation format.","Have the report generator query the web search agent to re-locate sources for claims in the final report.","Skip summarization and pass full raw outputs from web search and document analysis directly to the report generator.","Have each agent output structured data separating content summaries from source metadata (URLs, document names, page numbers)."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Instead of fixing attribution after loss, each agent needs to output structured data with separate content summaries and source metadata (URLs, document names, page numbers). This ensures metadata is not lost through summarization steps."],"e":"Instead of fixing attribution after loss, each agent needs to output structured data with separate content summaries and source metadata (URLs, document names, page numbers). This ensures metadata is not lost through summarization steps.","t":"Separate CONTENT from SOURCE METADATA.","k":["source metadata","structured output","attribution"]},{"id":"ccar-f-46","d":"tools","x":"When implementing your lookup_order MCP tool, the backend sometimes returns errors (e.g., \"Order not found\" or temporary database failures).\n\nWhat is the correct pattern for communicating these errors back to the agent?","o":["Return a success response with a \"status\" field indicating the error type","Throw an exception from the tool handler so the agent framework can catch and log it","Log the error server-side and return an empty result to avoid confusing the model","Return the error message in the tool result content with the isError flag set to true"],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","MCP's designed pattern: put the error text in the content field and mark isError=true. Claude sees both the failure flag and a readable message to reason about."],"e":"MCP's designed pattern: put the error text in the content field and mark isError=true. Claude sees both the failure flag and a readable message to reason about.","t":"Tool error = content message + isError TRUE.","k":["isError","MCP errors","tool result"]},{"id":"ccar-f-47","d":"context-reliability","x":"After investigating a billing dispute over 25+ turns, you've identified that duplicate charges occurred due to a payment gateway timeout triggering retry logic. The required refund ($847) exceeds your $500 authorization limit. You need to call escalate_to_human, and the human agent won't have access to your conversation transcript.\n\nWhat context should you pass to enable effective resolution?","o":["A structured summary: customer ID, root cause, refund amount, and recommended action.","The complete conversation transcript with all tool results.","The customer's original complaint verbatim plus the tool result excerpts showing duplicate transactions.","Your diagnosis and the refund amount only."],"a":[0],"w":["Human agents need enough information to resolve quickly, but do not need the entire transcript. Structured summary with customer ID, root cause, refund amount and recommended action provides exactly what is needed in a concise and actionable way.","Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"Human agents need enough information to resolve quickly, but do not need the entire transcript. Structured summary with customer ID, root cause, refund amount and recommended action provides exactly what is needed in a concise and actionable way.","t":"Handoff = ID + ROOT CAUSE + AMOUNT + ACTION.","k":["structured handoff","escalation","summary"]},{"id":"ccar-f-48","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your agent has called lookup_order multiple times while investigating a customer's return requests. Each response includes 40+ fields (items, shipping details, payment info, status history). Tool outputs now represent the majority of the conversation's context. The customer mentions two more orders they want to discuss.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach before making additional lookups?","o":["Move all tool responses to a vector database with semantic indexing, retrieving relevant portions as the conversation continues","Proceed with additional lookups without modifying the existing tool output context","Have the model generate a natural language summary of each order's key details, replacing structured responses with prose descriptions","Extract only return-relevant fields (items, purchase date, return window, status) from each existing order response, removing verbose details"],"a":[3],"w":["That adds infrastructure or indirection, but the question is really about choosing a simpler coordination pattern that fits the current workflow.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","When tool outputs take up most of the context, you need to trim down to only keep the return-relevant fields (items, purchase date, return window, status) before adding lookups. Frees the context window without losing important information."],"e":"When tool outputs take up most of the context, you need to trim down to only keep the return-relevant fields (items, purchase date, return window, status) before adding lookups. Frees the context window without losing important information.","t":"Context full of tool output? KEEP only relevant fields.","k":["trim tool output","context management"]},{"id":"ccar-f-49","d":"context-reliability","x":"A customer sends: \"This is frustrating. I've explained my issue twice and nothing is being resolved. I want to talk to a real person NOW.\" The agent has not yet called any tools to investigate their account.\n\nWhat should the agent do?","o":["Immediately call escalate_to_human with the conversation history.","First call get_customer and lookup_order to gather account context, then escalate to a human agent.","Acknowledge the frustration and ask one targeted question to understand the specific issue before escalating.","Briefly explain what the agent can help with and offer to resolve the issue quickly, escalating only if the customer repeats their request."],"a":[2],"w":["Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","The customer says 'twice' but you don't have context yet. An empathetic question, intended to give you the opportunity to resolve it the first time without incurring frustration or delaying a potential handoff.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The customer says 'twice' but you don't have context yet. An empathetic question, intended to give you the opportunity to resolve it the first time without incurring frustration or delaying a potential handoff.","t":"Empathize + ONE targeted question, then decide.","k":["empathy","clarifying question","escalation"]},{"id":"ccar-f-50","d":"context-reliability","x":"You're implementing the escalation logic for when the agent should call escalate_to_human. Your team proposes four different approaches for triggering escalation.\n\nWhich approach will most reliably identify cases that genuinely require human intervention?","o":["Implement sentiment analysis that monitors for frustration indicators (negative language, repeated questions, exclamation marks) and trigger escalation when the frustration score exceeds a configured threshold.","Instruct the agent to escalate when the customer requests a human, when the issue requires policy exceptions, or when the agent cannot make meaningful progress.","Configure the agent to escalate after three consecutive tool calls that fail to resolve the customer's stated issue, ensuring a reasonable attempt before involving a human.","Build a rules engine that maps specific issue types, customer segments, and product categories to escalation decisions, removing the need for model judgment calls."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Defining clear escalation conditions (customer requests human, policy exceptions needed, no meaningful progress) is the most comprehensive and flexible approach. Rules engine (D) is too rigid, sentiment analysis (C) is not reliable enough, fixed retry count (A) is too mechanical.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Defining clear escalation conditions (customer requests human, policy exceptions needed, no meaningful progress) is the most comprehensive and flexible approach. Rules engine (D) is too rigid, sentiment analysis (C) is not reliable enough, fixed retry count (A) is too mechanical.","t":"Escalate on: ASKED for human, policy EXCEPTION, NO progress.","k":["escalation criteria","policy exception"]},{"id":"ccar-f-51","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your agent is handling a billing dispute. After calling get_customer and lookup_order, it identifies that the dispute involves a promotional pricing error requiring manager approval–beyond the agent's authorization level.\n\nHow should the workflow handle this mid-process escalation?","o":["Attempt the refund with process_refund anyway, escalating only if the system rejects the transaction.","Persist the complete conversation and tool response history to a database, then call escalate_to_human with a reference ID.","Call escalate_to_human passing only the customer's original message.","Compile a structured handoff with customer details, order info, and the identified issue before calling escalate_to_human."],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Mid-process escalation needs to provide enough context for the human agent. Compile structured handoff with customer details, order information and identified issue ensures the human agent has enough information to resolve without needing to re-investigate from the beginning."],"e":"Mid-process escalation needs to provide enough context for the human agent. Compile structured handoff with customer details, order information and identified issue ensures the human agent has enough information to resolve without needing to re-investigate from the beginning.","t":"Mid-process escalation = STRUCTURED handoff.","k":["structured handoff","authorization limit"]},{"id":"ccar-f-52","d":"context-reliability","x":"During a billing dispute resolution, your agent successfully retrieves customer info via get_customer and order details via lookup_order, but when attempting to process the refund via process_refund, the tool returns a timeout error. The agent has enough information to explain the billing, confirm the refund eligibility, and verify refund eligibility, but cannot actually process the refund due to the backend failure.\n\nWhat approach best balances first-contact resolution with appropriate error handling?","o":["Implement automatic retries with exponential backoff for process_refund, keeping the conversation open until the refund is successfully processed","Explain the billing, confirm refund eligibility, acknowledge the system issue preventing immediate processing, and offer escalation or retry later","Escalate immediately to a human agent since the refund action cannot be completed","Confirm the refund will be processed and close the conversation, since the system has all necessary information to complete it automatically"],"a":[1],"w":["This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","Deliver the partial value you can (explanation + eligibility), be honest about the failure, and let the customer choose between human escalation or a retry. Classic graceful degradation.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing."],"e":"Deliver the partial value you can (explanation + eligibility), be honest about the failure, and let the customer choose between human escalation or a retry. Classic graceful degradation.","t":"Graceful degradation: give what you can + be HONEST + offer options.","k":["graceful degradation","timeout","honesty"]},{"id":"ccar-f-53","d":"tools","x":"Production logs reveal inconsistent error responses when lookup_order fails. The agent sometimes retries (wasting 3-4 turns) before concluding on the error. Your MCP tool currently returns only a plain text error message to Claude.\n\nWhat's the most effective improvement?","o":["Create an analyze_error MCP tool the agent calls to determine the error type before deciding how to handle it.","Return structured error responses with retryable: false for business errors and a customer-friendly explanation for Claude to use.","Implement retry logic with exponential backoff in your MCP server for all errors, returning only successful results to the agent.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt showing how to distinguish retryable from non-retryable errors by parsing error message text."],"a":[1],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","When the tool returns only plain text, the agent cannot distinguish transient vs permanent errors. Structured error response with retryable: false for business errors allows agents to know immediately that there is no need to retry, saving turns and improving customer experience.","Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient."],"e":"When the tool returns only plain text, the agent cannot distinguish transient vs permanent errors. Structured error response with retryable: false for business errors allows agents to know immediately that there is no need to retry, saving turns and improving customer experience.","t":"Structured errors: RETRYABLE flag + friendly message.","k":["structured errors","retryable","business errors"]},{"id":"ccar-f-54","d":"architecture","x":"When the agent calls lookup_order and receives order details showing the item was purchased 45 days ago, how does the agentic loop determine whether to call process_refund or escalate_to_human next?","o":["The order details are added to the conversation and the model reasons about which action to take.","The orchestration layer automatically routes to the next tool based on the order's status field.","The agent executes the remaining steps in a tool sequence planned at the start of the request.","The agent follows a pre-configured decision tree mapping order attributes to specific tool calls."],"a":[0],"w":["In Claude's agentic loop, after the tool returns results, order details are added to the conversation context and the model reasons about the next action based on that information. There is no hardcoded decision tree or orchestration layer to automatically route.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"In Claude's agentic loop, after the tool returns results, order details are added to the conversation context and the model reasons about the next action based on that information. There is no hardcoded decision tree or orchestration layer to automatically route.","t":"Tool result -> context -> MODEL decides next step.","k":["agentic loop","model reasoning","tool results"]},{"id":"ccar-f-55","d":"context-reliability","x":"A customer writes: \"I've been going back and forth on this return for days. I just want to speak to someone who can actually help me.\" The agent has confirmed via lookup_order that the return is straightforward–within policy and eligible for immediate processing.\n\nWhat should the agent do?","o":["Process the refund via process_refund to resolve the underlying issue, then inform them it's complete","Ask what specifically hasn't worked in previous attempts before deciding whether to escalate or resolve automatically","Call escalate_to_human immediately to honor the customer's request","Acknowledge frustration, inform them this is resolvable now, and offer to complete it or escalate"],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Return eligible and can be processed immediately, but customer is frustrated. Agents should not decide to escalate (A) or process without asking (C). The best approach is to acknowledge frustration, inform that the issue can be resolved immediately, and let the customer choose."],"e":"Return eligible and can be processed immediately, but customer is frustrated. Agents should not decide to escalate (A) or process without asking (C). The best approach is to acknowledge frustration, inform that the issue can be resolved immediately, and let the customer choose.","t":"Frustrated but solvable: ACKNOWLEDGE + say it's fixable now + let them CHOOSE.","k":["customer choice","empathy","resolution"]},{"id":"ccar-f-56","d":"context-reliability","x":"Compliance requires that refunds exceeding $500 must automatically escalate to a human agent–this rule cannot be left to model discretion. Despite clear system prompt instructions, production logs show the agent occasionally processes high-value refunds directly (3% failure rate).\n\nHow should you achieve guaranteed compliance?","o":["Modify the refund tool to return an error with message \"Amount exceeds policy limit–please escalate\" when threshold is exceeded.","Strengthen the system prompt with emphatic language: \"CRITICAL POLICY: Refunds over $500 MUST trigger human escalation. NEVER process these directly.\"","Implement a hook to intercept tool calls; when the refund process amount exceeds $500, block it and invoke human escalation.","Add few-shot examples to the prompt showing correct escalation behavior at various refund amounts ($400, $500, $600)."],"a":[2],"w":["Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","When compliance is mandatory and cannot be left to model discretion, programmatic protection needs to be implemented at the infrastructure level. Hook intercept tool calls and block refund >$500 are guaranteed compliance, regardless of model behavior or prompt interpretation.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"When compliance is mandatory and cannot be left to model discretion, programmatic protection needs to be implemented at the infrastructure level. Hook intercept tool calls and block refund >$500 are guaranteed compliance, regardless of model behavior or prompt interpretation.","t":"MUST-never-fail rule = HOOK in code, not prompt.","k":["hooks","deterministic enforcement","compliance"]},{"id":"ccar-f-57","d":"context-reliability","x":"A customer raises three separate issues during one session: a refund inquiry (turns 1-15), a subscription question (turns 16-30), and a payment method update (turns 31-45). At turn 48, the customer asks \"What happened with my refund?\" The conversation is approaching context limits.\n\nWhat strategy best maintains the agent's ability to address all issues throughout the session?","o":["Rely on MCP tools to re-fetch relevant information on demand when the customer references earlier issues.","Extract and persist structured issue data (order IDs, amounts, statuses) into a separate context layer.","Implement sliding window context that retains the most recent 30 turns.","Summarize earlier turns into a narrative description, preserving full message history only for the active issue."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Progressive summarization compresses already resolved topics while keeping the thread active verbatim – this is the classic pattern for long multi-issue conversations approaching the context limit."],"e":"Progressive summarization compresses already resolved topics while keeping the thread active verbatim – this is the classic pattern for long multi-issue conversations approaching the context limit.","t":"Old resolved = SUMMARY. Active issue = FULL text.","k":["progressive summarization","multi-issue","context limits"]},{"id":"ccar-f-58","d":"context-reliability","x":"The agent verifies customer identity through a multi-step process before resetting passwords. During testing, you notice that after the customer answers the third verification question, the agent asks them to provide their name again, as if the earlier exchange never happened.\n\nWhat's the most likely cause of this behavior?","o":["The conversation history isn't being passed in subsequent API requests.","The verification tool is clearing the agent's internal state after each successful validation step.","The prompt lacks instructions telling Claude to remember information across multiple exchanges.","Claude's memory retention is limited to two conversational turns by default, requiring explicit configuration to extend it."],"a":[0],"w":["Claude has no inherent memory between API calls. If the agent asks again for the information provided, the most common reason is that the conversation history was not passed during subsequent API requests. Prompt instructions (A) or tool behavior (C) are not the main cause.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Claude has no inherent memory between API calls. If the agent asks again for the information provided, the most common reason is that the conversation history was not passed during subsequent API requests. Prompt instructions (A) or tool behavior (C) are not the main cause.","t":"Claude forgets? You didn't SEND the history.","k":["stateless API","conversation history","messages array"]},{"id":"ccar-f-59","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your process_refund tool returns two types of errors: technical errors (\"503 Service Unavailable\", \"Connection timeout\") that are transient (5% of calls), and business errors (\"Order exceeds 30-day return window\", \"Item already refunded\") that are permanent (12% of calls). Monitoring shows the agent wastes 3-4 turns retrying business errors that can never succeed. Currently, both error types return only a plain text message to Claude.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to reduce wasted retries while improving customer-facing response quality?","o":["Add a check_refund_eligibility tool that must be called before process_refund to prevent business rule violations.","Implement automatic retry logic at the tool level for technical errors only, passing business errors to Claude without retries.","Return structured error responses with retryable: false for business errors and a customer-friendly explanation for Claude to use.","Add few-shot examples showing how to distinguish retryable from non-retryable errors by parsing error message text."],"a":[2],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing.","Adding retryable: false flag to business errors allows Claude to know immediately without retry, and customer-friendly explanation to communicate with customers. Separating retry (technical) vs no-retry (business) logic at the tool level is the cleanest solution.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient."],"e":"Adding retryable: false flag to business errors allows Claude to know immediately without retry, and customer-friendly explanation to communicate with customers. Separating retry (technical) vs no-retry (business) logic at the tool level is the cleanest solution.","t":"Permanent error = RETRYABLE false + explanation.","k":["retryable false","business errors","structured errors"]},{"id":"ccar-f-60","d":"context-reliability","x":"A customer returns 4 hours after their initial session about the same billing dispute. The previous 32-turn session contains context: lookup_status_result messages containing \"Status: Pending Refund\" and significant tokens from prior lookup results. The agent needs to be prepared to answer the customer fully.\n\nWhat approach most reliably handles returning customers?","o":["Resume with the full conversation and add a system prompt instruction that instructs the agent to prioritize resolving the pending refund.","Create a new session with a structured summary of the previous interaction (issue type, resolution steps, current status), then make fresh tool calls as needed.","Start a new session, inject a structured summary of the previous interaction (issue type, resolution steps, current status), then make fresh tool calls as needed.","Resume with state and configure the agent to automatically re-call all previous tool_results messages to ensure data freshness."],"a":[2],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","After 4 hours, cached data can become stale. Starting a new session with a structured summary of the previous interaction (issue type, steps taken, current status) provides the necessary context, while fresh tool calls ensure data accuracy.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem."],"e":"After 4 hours, cached data can become stale. Starting a new session with a structured summary of the previous interaction (issue type, steps taken, current status) provides the necessary context, while fresh tool calls ensure data accuracy.","t":"Returning later = NEW session + SUMMARY + FRESH tool calls.","k":["returning customer","stale data","structured summary"]},{"id":"ccar-f-61","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction pipeline processes invoices and extracts line items, subtotals, tax amounts, and grand totals. During evaluation, you discover that in 18% of extractions, the sum of extracted line item amounts doesn't match the extracted grand total—sometimes due to OCR errors in the source document, sometimes due to extraction mistakes by the model. Downstream accounting systems reject records with mismatched totals.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to improve extraction reliability?","o":["Implement post-processing that automatically adjusts line item amounts proportionally when their sum doesn't match the stated total.","Add few-shot examples demonstrating invoices where extracted line items sum correctly to the stated total, encouraging the model to produce mathematically consistent extractions.","Extract line items and totals independently, then use a separate validation model to reconcile discrepancies by determining which extracted values are most likely correct.","Add a \"calculated_total\" field where the model sums extracted line items alongside a \"stated_total\" field. Flag records for human review when values differ."],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Capturing both calculated_total and stated_total turns discrepancy into a first-class signal – uniformly detects both OCR errors and extraction mistakes, and routes only 18% mismatched records to human review. Auto-adjust and reconciliation model both silently fabricate/hide incorrect data for the accounting system."],"e":"Capturing both calculated_total and stated_total turns discrepancy into a first-class signal – uniformly detects both OCR errors and extraction mistakes, and routes only 18% mismatched records to human review. Auto-adjust and reconciliation model both silently fabricate/hide incorrect data for the accounting system.","t":"Calculated vs STATED total. Mismatch = flag.","k":["calculated_total","stated_total","human review"]},{"id":"ccar-f-62","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer asks your agent to identify untested code paths in a legacy payment processing module spanning 45 files. After reading the first 8 source files, the agent's responses are becoming noticeably less accurate — it's forgetting previously discussed code patterns and hasn't yet located all test files or traced critical payment flows.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to complete this investigation?","o":["Document all current findings in a summary report, clear context completely, then use that report as the sole reference for continuing the investigation.","Spawn subagents to investigate specific questions (e.g., \"find all test files for payment processing\", \"trace refund flow dependencies\") while the main agent coordinates findings and preserves high-level understanding.","Clear context with /clear, then selectively re-read only the most critical files discovered so far, writing key findings to a scratchpad file that persists between context resets.","Switch to using Grep to search for specific function names instead of reading full files, reducing the content loaded into context for remaining exploration."],"a":[1],"w":["A summary can help, but turning it into the only source of truth strips away detail that may still be needed for accurate follow-up work.","The strongest approach is to delegate well-scoped investigations to subagents with fresh context while the main agent keeps the architectural overview. This lets the system continue deep exploration without overloading a single context window, and it preserves both detailed findings and high-level coordination.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"The strongest approach is to delegate well-scoped investigations to subagents with fresh context while the main agent keeps the architectural overview. This lets the system continue deep exploration without overloading a single context window, and it preserves both detailed findings and high-level coordination.","t":"Context overflowing = delegate scoped questions to SUBAGENTS.","k":["subagents","delegation","context"]},{"id":"ccar-f-63","d":"architecture","x":"The coordinator agent has AgentDefinitions configured for all four specialized subagents, each with appropriate descriptions, prompts, and tool restrictions. During testing, you notice the coordinator correctly reasons about when to delegate — it generates messages like \"I'll ask the web search agent to find sources on this topic\" — but no subagent execution ever occurs. The coordinator then proceeds as if the delegation happened and continues with incomplete information. Logs show no errors.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":["The coordinator's allowedTools configuration doesn't include \"Task\", so while it can reason about delegation, it cannot invoke the tool required to spawn subagents.","The AgentDefinitions are configured correctly, but the coordinator's system prompt doesn't explicitly list the available subagent types, preventing the model from knowing they can be invoked.","Subagent context isolation means task descriptions from the coordinator don't automatically reach subagents; you need to configure explicit context forwarding in ClaudeAgentOptions.","The coordinator's max_tokens setting is too low, causing the Task tool invocation to be truncated before the subagent type parameter can be specified."],"a":[0],"w":["Without the Task tool in allowedTools, the coordinator can talk about delegating but has no way of actually calling the subagent – ​​true to the 'reasons about it, no execution, no errors' symptom.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"Without the Task tool in allowedTools, the coordinator can talk about delegating but has no way of actually calling the subagent – ​​true to the 'reasons about it, no execution, no errors' symptom.","t":"Delegation talk, no execution, no error = 'Task' not allowed.","k":["Task tool","allowedTools","coordinator"]},{"id":"ccar-f-64","d":"architecture","x":"In production, you observe that simple fact-checking queries (e.g., \"What year was the Paris Climate Agreement signed?\") traverse all four subagents sequentially, consuming 40+ seconds and significant tokens per query. Complex comparative research benefits from the full pipeline. Your query distribution is diverse and evolving as users discover new applications.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to optimize for varying query complexity?","o":["Create a fast-path for factual questions that bypasses subagents entirely, routing all other queries through the complete pipeline to ensure research thoroughness.","Train a query complexity classifier on labeled historical data to predict optimal subagent combinations, retraining periodically as query patterns evolve.","Have the coordinator analyze each query and dynamically decide which subagents to invoke based on its assessment of query requirements.","Implement pattern-based routing that categorizes queries by structure (single-fact vs. comparative vs. analytical) and maps each category to a predefined subagent combination."],"a":[2],"w":["It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Let the LLM coordinator independently reason about each query and select only the subagents that are truly necessary – this approach adapts naturally to diverse and evolving query distributions.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"Let the LLM coordinator independently reason about each query and select only the subagents that are truly necessary – this approach adapts naturally to diverse and evolving query distributions.","t":"Evolving query mix = coordinator decides DYNAMICALLY.","k":["dynamic routing","coordinator","query complexity"]},{"id":"ccar-f-65","d":"tools","x":"Production logs reveal inconsistent error handling: when lookup_order fails, the agent sometimes retries 5+ times (wasteful when the order ID doesn't exist), sometimes escalates immediately (premature for temporary network issues), and sometimes asks users for clarification (inappropriate when the issue is a backend permission error). Investigation shows your MCP tool returns uniform error responses: {\"isError\": true, \"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Operation failed\"}]}. The agent cannot distinguish between error types.\n\nWhat's the most effective improvement?","o":["Enhance error responses with structured metadata: include errorCategory (transient/validation/permission), isRetryable boolean, and a description of what caused the failure.","Create an analyze_error MCP tool the agent calls after any failure to determine the error category and recommended action.","Implement retry logic with exponential backoff in your MCP server for all errors, returning to the agent only after retries are exhausted.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating how to interpret error message patterns and select appropriate responses for each."],"a":[0],"w":["Provide agents with the necessary information to make the right decision: category, retryability and human-readable cause.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"Provide agents with the necessary information to make the right decision: category, retryability and human-readable cause.","t":"Error needs CATEGORY + RETRYABLE + CAUSE.","k":["errorCategory","isRetryable","structured errors"]},{"id":"ccar-f-66","d":"architecture","x":"Users report that final reports sometimes lack depth on specific subtopics. Investigation shows that the document analysis agent frequently identifies gaps—for instance, noting \"the retrieved sources discuss API authentication but lack details on token refresh patterns\"—but under the current strict pipeline, this insight isn't actionable since search has already completed.\n\nWhat's the most effective architectural change?","o":["Have the coordinator review analysis output for gap indicators and re-invoke search with gapinformed queries when gaps are detected.","Have the analysis agent report specific gaps to the coordinator, which triggers targeted searches and re-invokes analysis until sufficient.","Have the synthesis agent attach confidence scores to each section and flag areas with insufficient coverage for manual review.","Add a research planning agent before the search phase that decomposes topics into specific sub- questions."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The core problem is a rigid one-way pipeline where gap insights arrive after search is finished. The best fix is a closed feedback loop: the analysis agent reports specific gaps as structured output, the coordinator launches targeted follow-up searches, and analysis runs again until coverage is sufficient. That makes the missing-information signal actionable instead of leaving it as a passive observation.","This defers the problem rather than solving the system behavior that caused it.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The core problem is a rigid one-way pipeline where gap insights arrive after search is finished. The best fix is a closed feedback loop: the analysis agent reports specific gaps as structured output, the coordinator launches targeted follow-up searches, and analysis runs again until coverage is sufficient. That makes the missing-information signal actionable instead of leaving it as a passive observation.","t":"Gaps found? LOOP back: analysis -> coordinator -> targeted search.","k":["feedback loop","gap analysis","iterative research"]},{"id":"ccar-f-67","d":"context-reliability","x":"This currently requires manually copy-pasting content into conversations. The team wants the agent to access this standard Jira ticket data directly.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Use the Bash tool with curl to call Jira's REST API, including authentication headers and parsing JSON responses inline.","Build a custom MCP server wrapping Jira's API with tools designed specifically for this team's code review workflow.","Export Jira tickets to markdown files in the repository that the agent accesses using the Read tool.","Integrate an existing Jira MCP server that exposes tickets, comments, and metadata through discoverable tool interfaces."],"a":[3],"w":["This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","For standard third-party service data like Jira tickets, Anthropic's recommended practice is to integrate an existing MCP server, which provides discoverable, typed tool interfaces with managed authentication. Building a custom MCP server (B) is unnecessary effort for standard ticket data, markdown exports (A) go stale and require manual syncing, and ad-hoc curl calls (D) are brittle and expose credentials inline."],"e":"For standard third-party service data like Jira tickets, Anthropic's recommended practice is to integrate an existing MCP server, which provides discoverable, typed tool interfaces with managed authentication. Building a custom MCP server (B) is unnecessary effort for standard ticket data, markdown exports (A) go stale and require manual syncing, and ad-hoc curl calls (D) are brittle and expose credentials inline.","t":"Standard service = use an EXISTING MCP server.","k":["existing MCP server","Jira","integration"]},{"id":"ccar-f-68","d":"context-reliability","x":"A critical bug is affecting production users. Error logs show exceptions in the OrderProcessing module with a clear stack trace pointing to a specific area, but you haven't worked with this module before.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Use direct execution to examine the stack trace, read the relevant code, and implement a fix once you identify the root cause.","Start with direct execution to gather initial information, then switch to plan mode to design a comprehensive solution before implementing.","Enter plan mode to explore the module's architecture and dependencies before attempting any fix.","Use plan mode to analyze the error in context of the module's design, enumerate potential root causes, and prioritize fixes systematically."],"a":[0],"w":["Debugging is adaptive — each file you read changes the most useful next step, so let the agent follow the evidence. The stack trace already points straight at the failure, so direct execution (read the trace read the relevant code fix once the root cause is identified) is the fastest, most reliable path.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"Debugging is adaptive — each file you read changes the most useful next step, so let the agent follow the evidence. The stack trace already points straight at the failure, so direct execution (read the trace read the relevant code fix once the root cause is identified) is the fastest, most reliable path.","t":"Clear stack trace = DIRECT execution, not plan mode.","k":["direct execution","stack trace","debugging"]},{"id":"ccar-f-69","d":"context-reliability","x":"After deploying the automated review, you notice high precision but low recall — real bugs are slipping through undetected. Investigation reveals your review prompt instructs Claude to \"only report high- confidence issues you are certain about\" and \"err on the side of not commenting.\" Developers appreciate the low noise, but a race condition that caused a production outage was visible in a reviewed PR and went unreported. You need to substantially improve bug detection while keeping false positive rates manageable for your team.\n\nWhat is the most effective approach?","o":["Add detailed few-shot examples demonstrating bug categories Claude should flag — race conditions, null dereferences, error handling gaps — while keeping the high-confidence filtering instruction to maintain current precision levels.","Expand the context window by including related test files, recent git history, and the module's dependency graph alongside the diff, giving Claude richer signals to assess issue severity.","Remove the conservative filtering instructions and prompt Claude to report all potential issues, then apply a programmatic filter to deduplicate and suppress categories that historically generate false positives.","Split the review into a finding stage where Claude's goal is coverage — flagging every potential issue with confidence and severity metadata — and a separate stage that thresholds those findings."],"a":[3],"w":["Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to separate coverage from filtering. Let the review stage report every plausible finding together with confidence and severity metadata, then apply thresholding in a later stage. This improves recall substantially without forcing the team to accept raw unfiltered output as the final review result."],"e":"The best approach is to separate coverage from filtering. Let the review stage report every plausible finding together with confidence and severity metadata, then apply thresholding in a later stage. This improves recall substantially without forcing the team to accept raw unfiltered output as the final review result.","t":"Separate FIND (coverage) from FILTER (precision).","k":["two-stage review","recall","severity metadata"]},{"id":"ccar-f-70","d":"tools","x":"Your remove_team_member tool uses a dry_run: boolean parameter for previewing impacts before execution. Production monitoring shows the agent bypasses the preview step in 15% of calls by calling with dry_run=false directly. You need to ensure every removal is preceded by a preview that the user explicitly confirms.\n\nWhat is the most reliable approach?","o":["Add server-side validation that permits dry_run=false only when a dry_run=true call with identical parameters occurred within the past 60 seconds.","Replace with two tools: preview_remove_member returns impact details and a single-use confirmation token; execute_remove_member requires that token, binding execution to the specific previewed action.","Annotate the tool as requiring confirmation and configure the orchestration layer to prompt the user for approval before forwarding any calls to annotated tools.","Add detailed instructions and few-shot examples to the tool description requiring the agent to always call with dry_run=true first and wait for user confirmation before calling with dry_run=false."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Replace with two tools: preview_remove_member returns impact details and a single-use confirmation token; execute_remove_member requires that token, binding execution to the specific previewed action. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace with two tools: preview_remove_member returns impact details and a single-use confirmation token; execute_remove_member requires that token, binding execution to the specific previewed action. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Force preview = PREVIEW tool issues TOKEN, EXECUTE requires it.","k":["confirmation token","two-step tool","preview"]},{"id":"ccar-f-71","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your invoice extraction uses tool use with strict JSON schemas. JSON syntax errors never occur, but 12% of extractions fail semantic validation--for example, line Item amounts don't extracted total, or vendor IDs don't match valid formats. These failures currently route to manual review.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to reduce manual review volume while maintaining accuracy?","o":["Implement post-processing logic that automatically corrects common amors, such as recalculating totais from line items when sums don't match.","When validation falls, make a follow-up request with the document, extraction, and validation errors for model correction.","Retry the extraction up to 3 times when validation fallis, accepting the first result that passes validation.","Add stricter schema constraints with detailed field descriptions to prevent the model from generating invalid values initially."],"a":[1],"w":["This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","The best approach is to When validation falls, make a follow-up request with the document, extraction, and validation errors for model correction. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"The best approach is to When validation falls, make a follow-up request with the document, extraction, and validation errors for model correction. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Semantic fail = send ERRORS back for self-correction.","k":["validation feedback","self-correction","retry with errors"]},{"id":"ccar-f-72","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your system must extract event details from calendar invitations and output JSON that strictly conforms to a schema with fields for title, date, time, location, and attendees. Downstream reject any malformed or non- conformant JSON.\n\nWhat approach provides the most reliable schema compliance?","o":["Append instructions like \"Output only valid JSON matching the schema exactly\" and implement retry logic to re-prompt when JSON parsing fails.","Define a tool with your target schema as input parameters and have Claude call it with the extracted data.","Include detailed JSON formatting instructions and the target schema in your prompt, then parse Claude's text response as JSON.","Pre-fill Claude's response with an opening brace to force JSON output, then complete and parse the response."],"a":[1],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Define a tool with your target schema as input parameters and have Claude call it with the extracted data. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way."],"e":"The best approach is to Define a tool with your target schema as input parameters and have Claude call it with the extracted data. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Guaranteed JSON shape = TOOL with schema as input.","k":["tool use","JSON schema","structured output"]},{"id":"ccar-f-73","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your extraction system uses tool_use with a JSON schema containing 12 fields and detailed descriptions, totaling approximately 2,500 tokens for the complete tool definition. Processing documents under 150K tokens yields 98% accuracy. For documents between 175-190K tokens, accuracy drops to 71%, with information from the final third consistently missed. The model's context window is 200K tokens.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":["Very long documents exceed the model's effective attention span regardless of context limits, causing accuracy degradation for content farther from the prompt instructions.","Tool definitions consume input context tokens. Combined with system prompts and document content, the total approaches the context limit, degrading end-of-document processing.","The model distributes attention proportionally across input length, causing fields mentioned only once near the document's end to receive insufficient processing focus.","Schemas exceeding 8-10 fields increase decision complexity during parameter generation, reducing extraction accuracy independent of document length."],"a":[1],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Tool definitions consume input context tokens. Combined with system prompts and document content, the total approaches the context limit, degrading end-of-document processing. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"The best approach is to Tool definitions consume input context tokens. Combined with system prompts and document content, the total approaches the context limit, degrading end-of-document processing. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Tools + prompt + doc all count toward the LIMIT.","k":["context budget","tool definitions","token limit"]},{"id":"ccar-f-74","d":"prompt-output","x":"The extraction pipeline receives documents of varying types—some are invoices, others are contracts, and some are receipts. You've defined separate extraction tools, each with its own schema tailored to the document type. During testing, you observe that with tool_choice: \"auto\", Claude sometimes returns conversational text instead of calling an extraction tool, causing downstream parsing failures. You need guaranteed structured output without knowing the document type in advance.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Keep tool_choice: \"auto\" with system prompt instructions requiring tool use.","Add a preliminary classification call, then make a second call with tool_choice forced to the identified extraction tool.","Consolidate all document types into a single unified-schema extraction tool and force that tool.","Set tool_choice: \"any\" with all extraction tools defined."],"a":[3],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Collapsing everything into one path reduces precision and usually makes the system harder to control, not easier.","When you need guaranteed structured output but do not know the document type upfront, the most reliable approach is a two-step flow: classify first, then force tool_choice to the correct extractor. Leaving selection on auto still allows conversational responses, while collapsing everything into one giant schema usually hurts precision."],"e":"When you need guaranteed structured output but do not know the document type upfront, the most reliable approach is a two-step flow: classify first, then force tool_choice to the correct extractor. Leaving selection on auto still allows conversational responses, while collapsing everything into one giant schema usually hurts precision.","t":"Must call SOME tool, model picks which = 'ANY'.","k":["tool_choice any","structured output"]},{"id":"ccar-f-75","d":"prompt-output","x":"Monitoring shows 12% of extractions fall Pydantic validation with specific errors like \"expected float for quantity, got '2 to 3\". Retrying these requests without modification produces failures.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to recover from these validation failures?","o":["Set temperature to 0 to eliminate output variability and ensure consistent formatting","Pre-process source documents to standardize problematic formats before sending them for extraction","Implement a secondary pipeline using a larger model tier to reprocess documents that fail validation","Send a follow-up request including the validation error, asking the model to correct its output"],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Changing model size may affect cost or quality, but it does not directly address the root cause in this scenario.","The best approach is to Send a follow-up request including the validation error, asking the model to correct its output. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early."],"e":"The best approach is to Send a follow-up request including the validation error, asking the model to correct its output. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Same retry = same fail. Send the ERROR back.","k":["Pydantic","validation error","feedback loop"]},{"id":"ccar-f-76","d":"context-reliability","x":"After three months of weekly sessions, your conversation history has grown to 85,000 tokens. When users ask \"What did we conclude about the theme of isolation?\", the assistant provides generic literary analysis rather than referencing the group's specific insights from earlier sessions. Discussions often build on previous meetings' conclusions, so maintaining narrative context is important.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Implement progressive summarization where older conversation blocks are replaced with concise summaries that explicitly extract key conclusions, decisions, and recurring themes, keeping recent exchanges verbatim.","Implement rolling window truncation to keep only the most recent 25,000 tokens.","Use semantic embedding to index the full conversation history and retrieve only relevant past exchanges for each user query, replacing the linear conversation format with retrieved segments.","Add structured XML tags to mark significant discussion conclusions throughout the conversation history."],"a":[0],"w":["When a conversation grows long but still depends on earlier conclusions, progressive summarization is the most balanced approach. It compresses older discussion into reusable takeaways while preserving recent exchanges verbatim.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem."],"e":"When a conversation grows long but still depends on earlier conclusions, progressive summarization is the most balanced approach. It compresses older discussion into reusable takeaways while preserving recent exchanges verbatim.","t":"Old blocks -> summaries of CONCLUSIONS. Recent stays verbatim.","k":["progressive summarization","conclusions","long history"]},{"id":"ccar-f-77","d":"context-reliability","x":"You're Implementing a feature where users refine their playlist preferences through multiple conversation turns. After deploying, you notice Claude's responses don't reflect what us earlier in the same conversation— for example, a user says they love jazz, but two messages later Claude asks what genres they enjoy.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":["Claude requires a vector database connection to maintain conversation memory","Your application isn't including prior messages in the messages array","The model's context window has been exceeded by the conversation length","The Claude API requires a session_id parameter that you haven't configured"],"a":[1],"w":["This adds infrastructure but does not directly solve the failure mode described in the question.","If Claude immediately forgets what was said a few turns ago in the same conversation, it's usually because the application doesn't retransmit the full message history in each API call. Claude does not maintain state across requests if the application does not provide context again.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"If Claude immediately forgets what was said a few turns ago in the same conversation, it's usually because the application doesn't retransmit the full message history in each API call. Claude does not maintain state across requests if the application does not provide context again.","t":"Forgets within chat = messages array missing history.","k":["messages array","stateless","conversation history"]},{"id":"ccar-f-78","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your home renovation planning assistant uses a system prompt defining an expert contractor persona with specific guidelines: always ask about budget, suggest alternatives at multiple price points, and confirm timeline requirements. During testing, responses follow these guidelines for turns 1-4, but by turn 7, the assistant gives generic advice without asking about budget or timeline. The conversation totals only 2,500 tokens.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":["System prompts only establish initial behavior and don't persist across all turns.","The assistant's accumulated responses are diluting the system prompt's influence.","The system prompt is only sent with the first API request.","The model's attention on system prompt instructions naturally weakens as turns accumulate."],"a":[1],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","In this case the conversation is still short compared to the context window, so the error does not come from the token limit. The most likely problem is that the influence of system prompts is diluted as conversation history accumulates, causing the model to adhere less closely to the initial guidelines.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"In this case the conversation is still short compared to the context window, so the error does not come from the token limit. The most likely problem is that the influence of system prompts is diluted as conversation history accumulates, causing the model to adhere less closely to the initial guidelines.","t":"Short chat, drifting persona = system prompt DILUTED by history.","k":["system prompt dilution","persona drift"]},{"id":"ccar-f-79","d":"context-reliability","x":"Users report that during extended conversations, the AI loses track of specific topics, examples, and preferences they mentioned earlier in the session. Your current implementation uses a sliding window that keeps only the most recent 25 message pairs to stay within context limits.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to maintain awareness of earlier conversation content while managing context size?","o":["Implement vector similarity search over the full conversation history, retrieving relevant past messages for each user query.","Add a separate API call each turn to summarize messages being dropped, prepending this running summary to the conversation.","Increase the window size to 50 message pairs to retain more conversation history before truncation.","Replace the sliding window with a hybrid approach: summarize older messages while keeping recent messages verbatim."],"a":[3],"w":["Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The best approach is to Replace the sliding window with a hybrid approach: summarize older messages while keeping recent messages verbatim. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace the sliding window with a hybrid approach: summarize older messages while keeping recent messages verbatim. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Sliding window forgets = HYBRID summary + recent verbatim.","k":["hybrid context","summarization","sliding window"]},{"id":"ccar-f-80","d":"prompt-output","x":"During QA testing, you notice that Claude follows your system prompt guidelines consistently in the first 10- 15 turns, but by turn 25-30, responses begin deviating—using informal tone when formality was specified, occasionally skipping required formatting, or providing information types the guidelines restrict. Conversation length is well within context limits (typically 30,000 tokens out of 200,000 available).\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to maintain consistent behavior throughout extended conversations?","o":["Implement post-response validation that regenerates each response until it conforms to the specified guidelines.","Automatically start a new conversation after 20 turns, passing a summary of the prior context to maintain continuity.","Insert user-role messages that reinforce critical guidelines at natural conversation breakpoints, especially before complex requests.","Move behavioral guidelines from the system prompt into the first user message."],"a":[2],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The best approach is to Insert user-role messages that reinforce critical guidelines at natural conversation breakpoints, especially before complex requests. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Insert user-role messages that reinforce critical guidelines at natural conversation breakpoints, especially before complex requests. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Mid-chat drift = REINFORCE guidelines at breakpoints.","k":["guideline reinforcement","drift","long conversations"]},{"id":"ccar-f-81","d":"context-reliability","x":"During a conversation about order tracking, your external system receives a webhook indicating the user's package has shipped. The user is actively chatting and will likely send a follow-up message soon. You want the assistant to naturally incorporate this status change in its next response.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Add the current shipping status to the system prompt before the next API call.","Immediately send an API request with the update as a synthetic user message, generating an unsolicited assistant response.","Append the status update as a prefix to the next user message before calling the API.","Configure the assistant to call a get_order_status tool at the start of every response."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Add the current shipping status to the system prompt before the next API call. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Add the current shipping status to the system prompt before the next API call. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"New background fact = put it in the SYSTEM prompt next call.","k":["system prompt update","webhook","context injection"]},{"id":"ccar-f-82","d":"context-reliability","x":"Users report that responses feel repetitive across turns—each message begins with phrases like \"Certainly!\" or \"I'd be happy to help!\" even deep into conversations. You want responses to feel more natural, without these repetitive openers.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Append a partial assistant message with a direct response opening that the model will continue from","Add system prompt instructions specifying phrases to avoid, such as \"Never begin responses with 'Certainly' or similar affirmations\"","Implement post-processing to detect and strip common greeting phrases from response beginnings","Lower the temperature parameter to make response openings more deterministic and less variable"],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Append a partial assistant message with a direct response opening that the model will continue from. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Append a partial assistant message with a direct response opening that the model will continue from. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Control the opening = PREFILL the assistant turn.","k":["prefill","assistant message","openers"]},{"id":"ccar-f-83","d":"context-reliability","x":"Users frequently send ambiguous requests like \"book a venue for the party\" without specifying date, guest count, or budget. Your evaluation shows the assistant asks an average of 4.2 clarifying questions before taking any action, causing 35% of users to abandon mid-conversation. However, when you reduce questions, users sometimes receive recommendations that don't match their preferences.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to improve this trade-off?","o":["Configure the assistant to proceed with reasonable defaults (medium-sized venue, next weekend, moderate budget) without explicitly stating these assumptions, allowing users to provide corrections if results don't match expectations.","Instruct the assistant to state explicit assumptions based on conversation history, proceed with recommendations while inviting corrections, and reserve clarifying questions only for irreversible actions like confirming bookings.","Configure the assistant to consolidate all clarifying questions into a single compound question (e.g., \"What date, guest count, and budget are you considering?\") to reduce the total number of conversational turns.","Implement a structured intake form that collects all required parameters (date, guest count, budget, venue type) upfront before the assistant begins providing any recommendations."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Instruct the assistant to state explicit assumptions based on conversation history, proceed with recommendations while inviting corrections, and reserve clarifying questions only for irreversible actions like confirming bookings. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Collapsing everything into one path reduces precision and usually makes the system harder to control, not easier.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Instruct the assistant to state explicit assumptions based on conversation history, proceed with recommendations while inviting corrections, and reserve clarifying questions only for irreversible actions like confirming bookings. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"STATE assumptions, act, ASK only before irreversible steps.","k":["explicit assumptions","clarifying questions","irreversible actions"]},{"id":"ccar-f-84","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your conversational AI tutor has a 2,800-token system prompt containing teaching methodology, persona guidelines, and detailed written instructions for adapting explanations to different proficiency levels. User testing reveals that in conversations exceeding 12 turns (approximately 4,000 tokens of conversation history), the assistant increasingly ignores the proficiency-adaptation guidelines, defaulting to intermediate-level explanations regardless of the learner's stated level.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach to ensure consistent adherence to these guidelines throughout extended conversations?","o":["After each assistant response, make a separate API call to evaluate whether the difficulty level matched the learner's profile, regenerating responses that don't align.","Restructure the system prompt to place the proficiency-adaptation rules in a clearly-marked final section immediately before the conversation history begins.","Replace the verbose proficiency guidelines with few-shot examples demonstrating appropriate responses at each proficiency level, showing concrete differences in vocabulary, complexity, and explanation depth.","Inject a condensed reminder of the proficiency requirements into the conversation as a system message every 4-5 turns."],"a":[2],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Replace the verbose proficiency guidelines with few-shot examples demonstrating appropriate responses at each proficiency level, showing concrete differences in vocabulary, complexity, and explanation depth. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace the verbose proficiency guidelines with few-shot examples demonstrating appropriate responses at each proficiency level, showing concrete differences in vocabulary, complexity, and explanation depth. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Rules fading? Replace with concrete FEW-SHOT examples per level.","k":["few-shot","proficiency levels","adherence"]},{"id":"ccar-f-85","d":"context-reliability","x":"The system routes documents with extraction confidence below 85% to human review. A quarterly audit reveals that 12% of high-confidence extractions (>85%) also contain errors—cases where the model finds plausible-but-incorrect values. Error sources vary: comparison tables showing competitor specs, appendices referencing different product variants, and ambiguous phrasing the model misinterprets. You need a sustainable strategy to catch these high-confidence errors and measure whether improvements reduce the error rate over time.\n\nWhat approach is most effective?","o":["Lower the confidence threshold from 85% to 70%, routing a larger volume of extractions to human review.","Implement heuristic rules that flag documents containing comparison tables or appendices for review regardless of confidence score.","Implement stratified random sampling reviewing a fixed percentage of high-confidence extractions weekly, enabling error rate measurement and novel pattern detection.","Add a verification pass that re-extracts from each high-confidence document, flagging cases where the two extraction attempts produce different results."],"a":[2],"w":["Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Implement stratified random sampling reviewing a fixed percentage of high-confidence extractions weekly, enabling error rate measurement and novel pattern detection. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"The best approach is to Implement stratified random sampling reviewing a fixed percentage of high-confidence extractions weekly, enabling error rate measurement and novel pattern detection. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Measure hidden errors = STRATIFIED random SAMPLING.","k":["stratified sampling","high-confidence errors","measurement"]},{"id":"ccar-f-86","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your research assistant helps users analyze academic papers over extended conversations. User testing reveals a recurring issue: after conversations exceed 60K tokens, users ask follow-up questions requiring precise numerical details from papers discussed earlier—sample sizes, exact p-values, specific inclusion criteria. Your current approach summarizes paper discussions after 8 turns to stay within context limits. Users report that responses to these precision-dependent questions are often hedged or inaccurate.\n\nWhat's the most effective architectural change?","o":["Use a separate Claude call with explicit instructions to generate higher-fidelity summaries that preserve all numerical details and statistical values.","Implement retrieval that re-injects relevant paper sections when the user's question suggests they need specific numerical details.","Keep source text from methodology and results sections in context permanently, while summarizing only the conversational discussion and interpretation portions.","Maintain a structured database of key facts extracted from each paper (sample sizes, statistics, methods) and retrieve relevant entries into context when precision-dependent questions are detected."],"a":[3],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The best approach is to Maintain a structured database of key facts extracted from each paper (sample sizes, statistics, methods) and retrieve relevant entries into context when precision-dependent questions are detected. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall."],"e":"The best approach is to Maintain a structured database of key facts extracted from each paper (sample sizes, statistics, methods) and retrieve relevant entries into context when precision-dependent questions are detected. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Precise numbers = STRUCTURED fact store, retrieve on demand.","k":["structured facts","retrieval","precision"]},{"id":"ccar-f-87","d":"context-reliability","x":"A security audit requires updating your authentication library from v2 to v3. The migration guide documents breaking changes: authenticate() now returns a Promise instead of accepting a callback, the User type has restructured fields, and three deprecated methods were removed. Grep shows the library is imported in 45 files across several modules.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Update the dependency version, run the test suite, and use Claude Code to fix each failure as it appears.","Enter plan mode to explore library usage across modules, map affected code paths, then create a migration strategy before implementing.","Paste the migration guide's breaking changes into your prompt and use direct execution to update all usages across the 45 files.","Create a custom slash command encapsulating the migration transformations, then execute it against each file without prior codebase exploration."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Enter plan mode to explore library usage across modules, map affected code paths, then create a migration strategy before implementing. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Enter plan mode to explore library usage across modules, map affected code paths, then create a migration strategy before implementing. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Big multi-file breaking change = PLAN MODE first.","k":["plan mode","migration","breaking changes"]},{"id":"ccar-f-88","d":"claude-code","x":"You've asked Claude Code to build a PDF report generation feature. The initial implementation queries the database correctly, but the output has formatting issues: table columns are too narrow causing content truncation, dates display without proper formatting, and page break handling is incorrect. You've noticed these issues interact— changing column widths affects how dates render, and page breaks depend on content height.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach for iterating toward a working solution?","o":["Show Claude an example of a correctly formatted report and ask it to match that output, rather than listing the specific technical issues.","Start fresh with a detailed prompt specifying all formatting requirements upfront.","Address the column width issue first with specific measurements, verify it works, then fix date formatting within the corrected columns, then adjust page breaks— testing after each change.","Provide all three issues in a single detailed message with exact specifications for each, allowing Claude to address them together in one update."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem.","The best approach is to Address the column width issue first with specific measurements, verify it works, then fix date formatting within the corrected columns, then adjust page breaks— testing after each change. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Address the column width issue first with specific measurements, verify it works, then fix date formatting within the corrected columns, then adjust page breaks— testing after each change. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Interacting issues = fix ONE at a time, test each.","k":["iterative fixes","dependencies","incremental testing"]},{"id":"ccar-f-89","d":"context-reliability","x":"You're implementing a new payment processing module that must follow your project's established patterns for database transactions, error handling, and audit logging. You've identified three existing modules that exemplify these patterns: db_utils.py, error_handlers.py, and audit_logger.py. This is a one-off integration task—these patterns are well-documented in your team wiki and don't need additional project-level documentation.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Describe the patterns from the three modules in natural language in your prompt, explaining the transaction handling approach, error format, and logging conventions Claude should follow.","Ask Claude to explore your codebase to find and understand the transaction, error handling, and logging patterns before generating the new module.","Use @references to include the three modules directly in your prompt, giving Claude concrete code examples of the patterns to follow.","Add documentation of each pattern to your CLAUDE.md file, establishing them as project conventions that Claude will apply automatically."],"a":[2],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Use @references to include the three modules directly in your prompt, giving Claude concrete code examples of the patterns to follow. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Documentation may give useful background, but it is not a reliable way to discover every real caller or usage path in code."],"e":"The best approach is to Use @references to include the three modules directly in your prompt, giving Claude concrete code examples of the patterns to follow. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"One-off + example files = @REFERENCE them.","k":["@references","code examples","one-off task"]},{"id":"ccar-f-90","d":"claude-code","x":"Your monorepo contains shared coding standards in /docs/standards/security-rules.md (for services handling user data), testing-patterns.md (for all packages), and api-conventions.md (for API-facing services). Your 15 packages are organized by feature domain (/packages/auth/, /packages/billing/, /packages/notifications/, etc.) without naming conventions indicating which handle user data or expose APIs. Package maintainers are expected to configure their own local development settings, as they understand their package's domain requirements. Currently, all package CLAUDE.md files duplicate all three standards, applying irrelevant guidance.\n\nWhat's the most effective approach?","o":["Create claude/rules/ files for each standard with YAML frontmatter paths listing every package directory where that standard should apply.","Put all standards in the root CLAUDE.md with override instructions like \"ignore security-rules.md when working in packages that don't handle user data.\"","Use @imports in each package's CLAUDE.md to reference only the specific standard files relevant to that package, based on the maintainer's domain knowledge.","Create a shared-standards.nd that uses @imports to combine all three standards, then have each package's CLAUDE.md import that combined file."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Use @imports in each package's CLAUDE.md to reference only the specific standard files relevant to that package, based on the maintainer's domain knowledge. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Use @imports in each package's CLAUDE.md to reference only the specific standard files relevant to that package, based on the maintainer's domain knowledge. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Each package picks its own standards = @IMPORTS per CLAUDE.md.","k":["@imports","CLAUDE.md","monorepo"]},{"id":"ccar-f-91","d":"prompt-output","x":"The system needs to extract candidate information (name, contact details, skills, work experience, education) from uploaded resumes. The extracted data must strictly conform to a predefined JSON schema, as missing required fields or incorrect data types will cause downstream validation failures.\n\nWhat is the most reliable approach to ensure Claude's output consistently matches the schema?","o":["Define a tool with an input schema matching your required JSON structure and extract the data from Claude's tool_use response.","Include detailed JSON formatting instructions and a template example in the system prompt, asking Claude to output only valid JSON.","Make two separate API calls—first extracting information as text, then asking Claude to format that text as JSON.","Parse Claude's text response with regex patterns to extract JSON objects, using retry logic for malformed responses."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Define a tool with an input schema matching your required JSON structure and extract the data from Claude's tool_use response. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient."],"e":"The best approach is to Define a tool with an input schema matching your required JSON structure and extract the data from Claude's tool_use response. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Schema compliance = TOOL input schema.","k":["tool use","JSON schema","resume extraction"]},{"id":"ccar-f-92","d":"context-reliability","x":"Anthropic's tool use documentation states: \"Write instructive error messages. Instead of generic errors like 'failed', include what went wrong and what Claude should try next.\" A billing dispute agent uses lookup_order, which catches all exceptions and returns a tool_result with is_error: true and the message \"execution failed\". Monitoring shows two failure modes: the agent retries the identical call until hitting the turn limit, or it immediately calls escalate_to_human without trying alternative tools.\n\nWhich change follows the documented recommendation and gives Claude the information it needs to select the correct recovery action for each error type?","o":["Remove is_error: true and return the error details as normal tool content, so Claude reasons about the response as data rather than treating it as a flagged failure condition that biases retry behavior.","Return error-type-specific messages with is_error: true, e.g., \"order not found-try get_customer to search by phone\" for data errors and \"Database timeout (transient)-retry should succeed\" for infrastructure errors.","Add an error classification step in the agentic loop that intercepts tool errors before Claude sees them, tags each as \"retry\" \"try_alternative,\" or \"escalate,\" and adds that recommendation to the tool result.","Implement retry logic with exponential backoff inside each tool implementation so transient errors are resolved transparently within the tool before any failure result is surfaced to Claude in the agentic loop."],"a":[1],"w":["This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","The best approach is to Return error-type-specific messages with is_error: true, e.g., \"order not found-try get_customer to search by phone\" for data errors and \"Database timeout (transient)-retry should succeed\" for infrastructure errors. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient."],"e":"The best approach is to Return error-type-specific messages with is_error: true, e.g., \"order not found-try get_customer to search by phone\" for data errors and \"Database timeout (transient)-retry should succeed\" for infrastructure errors. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Error message should say WHAT happened + WHAT to try next.","k":["is_error","actionable error messages","recovery"]},{"id":"ccar-f-93","d":"tools","x":"Production logs reveal inconsistent error handling: when tool_code fails, the agent sometimes retries 5 times (even if the tool_id doesn't exist), sometimes escalates immediately (premature for temporary network issues), and sometimes adds user-friendly explanation (inappropriate when the issue is a backend permission error). Investigation shows four MCP tool returns uniform error responses: {\"status\": \"error\", \"content\": \"{\"type\": \"Error\", \"message\": \"Operation failed.\"}\"}.\n\nThe agent learns different types. What's the most effective improvement?","o":["Implement retry logic with exponential backoff in your MCP server for all errors, returning to the agent only after retries are exhausted.","Enhance error responses with structured metadata. Include error_category (transient/retriable/permission), reason, and a description of what caused the failure.","Create an analyze_error MCP tool the agent calls after any failure to determine the error category and recommended action.","Add a few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating how to interpret error message patterns and select appropriate responses for each."],"a":[1],"w":["Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","The best approach is to Enhance error responses with structured metadata. Include error_category (transient/retriable/permission), reason, and a description of what caused the failure. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"The best approach is to Enhance error responses with structured metadata. Include error_category (transient/retriable/permission), reason, and a description of what caused the failure. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Structured error: CATEGORY + REASON + CAUSE.","k":["error_category","structured errors","MCP"]},{"id":"ccar-f-94","d":"claude-code","x":"Your code review prompts include both implementation changes and the corresponding test file, but the LLM's review comments fail to point out untested code paths. Analysis reveals the model correctly flags functions that have no tests at all, but fails to identify when conditional branches or error-handling paths within tested functions that have no tests at all, but fails to identify when conditional branches or error-handling paths within tested functio lack coverage.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to improve detection of branch-level coverage gaps without overcomplicating the pipeline?","o":["Include few-shot examples showing code with an uncovered branch paired with the review comment identifying the specific missing test case.","Restructure the prompt to interleave implementation and tests, presenting each function followed immediately by its test cases","Implement a multi-pass pipeline where separate LLM calls first extract all conditional branches, then cross-reference each against test assertions in a second pass.","Add explicit instructions directing the model to enumerate each conditional branch and exception path, then verify each has a corresponding test assertion."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Include few-shot examples showing code with an uncovered branch paired with the review comment identifying the specific missing test case. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Include few-shot examples showing code with an uncovered branch paired with the review comment identifying the specific missing test case. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Show an EXAMPLE of a caught branch gap.","k":["few-shot","branch coverage","code review"]},{"id":"ccar-f-95","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your pipeline uses a tool called extract_metadata with a JSON schema for paper details. You've also defined lookup_citations and verify_doi tools for enrichment. During testing, you notice that when users include requests like \"extract the metadata and tell me how cited it is,\" Claude sometimes calls lookup_citations first, which fails because it needs the DOI that extract_metadata would provide.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to ensure structured metadata extraction happens first?","o":["Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_metadata\"} for every API call in the pipeline, ensuring Claude always extracts metadata before any enrichment can occur.","Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_metadata\"} and process the enrichment requests in subsequent turns after receiving the extracted metadata.","Set tool_choice to \"any\" so Claude must use a tool, combined with system prompt instructions prioritizing extract_metadata.","Set tool_choice to \"auto\" and reorder the tool definitions so extract_metadata appears first in the tools array, since Claude prioritizes earlier-listed tools."],"a":[1],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","tool_choice=specific-tool deterministically forces extract_metadata on the first turn. Then you hand control back to 'auto' to let the model use citations/DOI enrichment with the metadata in context.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing."],"e":"tool_choice=specific-tool deterministically forces extract_metadata on the first turn. Then you hand control back to 'auto' to let the model use citations/DOI enrichment with the metadata in context.","t":"Force step 1 by NAME, then go back to auto for enrichment.","k":["tool_choice","forced first tool","dependent tools"]},{"id":"ccar-f-96","d":"claude-code","x":"Your pipeline reviews every PR using a single API call with a static prompt containing the diff and full text of each changed file — unchanged files are not included. Reviews are posted asynchronously and don't block PR creation. Developers report that reviews consistently miss bugs involving cross-file interactions — for example, a PR renames a function's parameters but the review doesn't flag callers in unchanged files that still use the old argument order. Evaluation shows cross-file bugs account for 35% of production incidents from reviewed PRs.\n\nWhat is the most effective change to your review design?","o":["Use static analysis to build a dependency graph of changed code, then expand the prompt to include all files within two dependency hops of any changed file.","Add chain-of-thought instructions asking the model to list all external references in the diff, then reason step-by-step about how each change might affect callers in other files.","Run parallel review passes per changed file with direct dependents included in each pass, then aggregate and deduplicate findings using a final summarization call.","Redesign the review as a turn-limited agentic task where the model can read files and search the codebase via tools, following references to verify cross-file findings."],"a":[3],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","The failure is a missing-context problem: callers live in unchanged files the static prompt never includes, so chain-of-thought (B) cannot help the model reason about code it cannot see, and static heuristics (C, D) bloat context while still missing dynamic or multi-hop references. Anthropic's agent guidance favors agentic search — letting the model retrieve exactly the context it needs via file-read/search tools — and the asynchronous, nonblocking review pipeline tolerates the added latency, while a turn limit bounds cost."],"e":"The failure is a missing-context problem: callers live in unchanged files the static prompt never includes, so chain-of-thought (B) cannot help the model reason about code it cannot see, and static heuristics (C, D) bloat context while still missing dynamic or multi-hop references. Anthropic's agent guidance favors agentic search — letting the model retrieve exactly the context it needs via file-read/search tools — and the asynchronous, nonblocking review pipeline tolerates the added latency, while a turn limit bounds cost.","t":"Can't see other files? Make review AGENTIC with read/search tools.","k":["agentic review","cross-file bugs","tools"]},{"id":"ccar-f-97","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your search Flights tool calls an external airline API that occasionally returns a 503 Service Unavailable error.\n\nWhat is the most effective way to handle this error in your tool implementation?","o":["Return an empty flight list as if the search succeeded but found no matching flights.","Return an error message in the tool result explaining the service is temporarily unavailable.","Log the error internally and return an empty response, letting the model continue without the flight data.","Automatically retry the request up to five times with exponential backoff before returning results to the agent."],"a":[3],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Automatically retry the request up to five times with exponential backoff before returning results to the agent. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early."],"e":"The best approach is to Automatically retry the request up to five times with exponential backoff before returning results to the agent. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Transient 503 = RETRY inside the tool with backoff.","k":["503","exponential backoff","transient errors"]},{"id":"ccar-f-98","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your agent has a log_workout tool that accepts exercise_type (string), value (number), and measurement (string). Production monitoring shows the agent frequently passes mismatched combinations-using measurement: \"reps\" for cardio exercises like running, or measurement: \"miles\" for strength exercises like bench press. Your exercises naturally divide into two categories: cardio (measured in time or distance) and strength (measured in reps and sets). 23% of tool calls have invalid combinations.\n\nWhat approach would most effectively reduce these errors?","o":["Add enum constraints on measurement limiting values to \"minutes\", \"miles\", \"reps\", or \"sets\" to prevent arbitrary measurement strings.","Implement server-side validation returning descriptive errors for invalid combinations, allowing the agent to retry with corrections.","Split into log_cardio_workout (with duration_minutes or distance_miles parameters) and log_strength_workout (with reps and sets parameters).","Add explicit examples to the tool description showing valid combinations (e.g., \"For running: use minutes or miles. For push-ups: use reps\") with constraints for each exercise category."],"a":[2],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","The best approach is to Split into log_cardio_workout (with duration_minutes or distance_miles parameters) and log_strength_workout (with reps and sets parameters). This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"The best approach is to Split into log_cardio_workout (with duration_minutes or distance_miles parameters) and log_strength_workout (with reps and sets parameters). This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Invalid combos? SPLIT tools so they can't happen.","k":["tool split","parameter design","invalid combinations"]},{"id":"ccar-f-99","d":"context-reliability","x":"During initial testing, you notice that Claude doesn't seem to remember vocabulary words from earlier in the conversation. When a student asks \"Can you quiz me on those words?\", responds as if no words have been discussed.\n\nWhat is the most likely explanation?","o":["You need to enable conversation persistence by passing a session ID parameter with each API call.","Your system prompt needs explicit instructions telling Claude to remember information from earlier turns.","You're not including prior messages in each API request—the stateless API doesn't retain conversation history.","The model's context window has filled up, causing earlier conversation content to be dropped."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to You're not including prior messages in each API request—the stateless API doesn't retain conversation history. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to You're not including prior messages in each API request—the stateless API doesn't retain conversation history. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Stateless API: SEND prior messages every time.","k":["stateless","messages history"]},{"id":"ccar-f-100","d":"context-reliability","x":"A new user's first message is \"Set up my focus music,\" This could mean configure preferences, create a playlist, or play music immediately. Your system supports all three actions.\n\nWhat's the effective approach?","o":["Create a new \"Focus\" playlist with curated tracks and notify the user it's ready.","Play popular focus tracks Immediately and let the user redirect if needed","Ask one clarifying question about action type: play now or configure for later","Start preference configuration by asking about genres, temps, and artists they prefer for focus."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Ask one clarifying question about action type: play now or configure for later. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Ask one clarifying question about action type: play now or configure for later. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Truly ambiguous action = ONE clarifying question.","k":["clarifying question","ambiguity"]},{"id":"ccar-f-101","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your conversational assistant frequently generates multiple clarifying questions when users make ambiguous requests. When a user asks \"Can you help me with the report?\", the assistant responds: \"I'd be happy to help! Could you tell me: 1) Which report? 2) What kind of help—drafting, reviewing, or formatting? 3) What's your deadline?\" User analytics show a 40% conversation abandonment rate after these multi-question responses.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to reduce friction while appropriately handling ambiguity?","o":["Modify the system prompt to instruct the assistant to make reasonable assumptions from available context, state those assumptions explicitly, and offer to adjust if the interpretation is wrong.","Add a preprocessing step using a smaller model to classify request ambiguity on a 1-5 scale, routing high-ambiguity requests to a clarification dialog and low-ambiguity requests directly to the assistant.","Limit the assistant to one clarifying question per turn, using conversation history to accumulate answers over multiple exchanges rather than requesting everything upfront.","Create a lookup table of common request patterns with predefined default interpretations, having the assistant respond with those defaults without stating the assumptions made."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Modify the system prompt to instruct the assistant to make reasonable assumptions from available context, state those assumptions explicitly, and offer to adjust if the interpretation is wrong. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Changing model size may affect cost or quality, but it does not directly address the root cause in this scenario.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Modify the system prompt to instruct the assistant to make reasonable assumptions from available context, state those assumptions explicitly, and offer to adjust if the interpretation is wrong. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Assume + STATE it + offer to adjust.","k":["explicit assumptions","friction","abandonment"]},{"id":"ccar-f-102","d":"prompt-output","x":"You need to add a date validation check ensuring event dates are in the future. This requires adding a conditional statement to one existing function in a single file.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate approach?","o":["Start with extended thinking mode enabled to ensure thorough reasoning about the validation logic","Enter plan mode first to create a detailed implementation strategy before making the change","Use direct execution to make the change","Enter plan mode to analyze how the validation might impact other parts of the reservation flow"],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Use direct execution to make the change. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Use direct execution to make the change. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Tiny single-file change = DIRECT execution.","k":["direct execution","plan mode","small change"]},{"id":"ccar-f-103","d":"claude-code","x":"Your team's CLAUDE.md includes a rule: \"Use 4-space indentation and always run Prettier formatting.\" Despite this, code reviews reveal that roughly 30% of files Claude Code generates use inconsistent formatting — sometimes 2-space indentation, sometimes missing trailing commas. Adding emphasis (\"IMPORTANT: You MUST use Prettier formatting\") reduces violations to about 15%, but doesn't eliminate them.\n\nWhat is the most effective way to ensure all generated code is consistently formatted?","o":["Add a Stop hook with a prompt-based check that evaluates whether generated code follows formatting standards and prompts Claude to fix violations.","Extract the formatting rules into a dedicated skill that Claude loads automatically when generating code, with more detailed examples of correct formatting.","Configure a Post ToolUse hook with an Edit|Write matcher that automatically runs Prettier on each file Claude modifies.","Split the formatting rules into path-scoped .claude/rules/ files that load when Claude works on matching file types."],"a":[2],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","The best approach is to Configure a Post ToolUse hook with an Edit|Write matcher that automatically runs Prettier on each file Claude modifies. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Configure a Post ToolUse hook with an Edit|Write matcher that automatically runs Prettier on each file Claude modifies. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Must ALWAYS happen = PostToolUse HOOK.","k":["PostToolUse hook","Prettier","deterministic"]},{"id":"ccar-f-104","d":"context-reliability","x":"You're implementing a caching layer for API responses to speed up the /products endpoint. You have a rough idea—Redis with a 5-minute TTL—but you're new to production caching and aren't sure what other considerations a robust implementation requires.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to start your iterative workflow?","o":["Write a specification with your known requirements and \"TBD\" markers for uncertain areas, having Claude propose solutions for each TBD as it implements.","Use plan mode to analyze the current/products endpoint implementation, then provide your caching requirements once Claude explains how the existing code is structured.","Start with a minimal request: \"Add Redis caching to/products with 5-minute TTL.\" Add features and fix issues through follow-up prompts as problems surface during testing.","Ask Claude to interview you about the caching requirements before implementing, surfacing considerations like invalidation strategies, cache layers, consistency guarantees, and failure modes."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Ask Claude to interview you about the caching requirements before implementing, surfacing considerations like invalidation strategies, cache layers, consistency guarantees, and failure modes. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives."],"e":"The best approach is to Ask Claude to interview you about the caching requirements before implementing, surfacing considerations like invalidation strategies, cache layers, consistency guarantees, and failure modes. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Don't know what you don't know? Let Claude INTERVIEW you.","k":["interview","requirements discovery","caching"]},{"id":"ccar-f-105","d":"context-reliability","x":"You've asked Claude to write a data migration script, but the initial output doesn't correctly handle records with null values in required fields.\n\nWhat's the most effective way to iterate toward a working solution?","o":["Provide a test case with example input containing null values and the expected output, then ask Claude to fix it.","Manually edit the generated code to fix the null handling, then continue working with Claude on other parts.","Describe the null value problem in detail and ask Claude to regenerate the entire script with improved edge case handling.","Add \"think harder about edge cases\" to your prompt and request a complete rewrite of the migration logic."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Provide a test case with example input containing null values and the expected output, then ask Claude to fix it. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Provide a test case with example input containing null values and the expected output, then ask Claude to fix it. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Bug fix = give a TEST CASE with expected output.","k":["test case","expected output","iteration"]},{"id":"ccar-f-106","d":"architecture","x":"Production monitoring shows the research phase takes longer than expected. Analysis reveals the coordinator invokes the web search subagent, then invokes the document analysis subagent and waits again. These tasks are independent—neither requires the other's output.\n\nWhat is the most effective way to run these subagents concurrently?","o":["Structure the coordinator to emit both Task tool calls (for web search and document analysis) in a single response message.","Create an async orchestration layer outside the agent that spawns parallel threads, each running a separate coordinator.","Switch both subagents to use a Haiku-tier model instead of Sonnet to reduce their individual execution time.","Add detailed instructions to the coordinator's system prompt explaining the performance benefits of parallel execution at the same time."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Structure the coordinator to emit both Task tool calls (for web search and document analysis) in a single response message. This fits the coordination problem described and makes responsibilities, delegation, or handoff behavior more reliable.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Structure the coordinator to emit both Task tool calls (for web search and document analysis) in a single response message. This fits the coordination problem described and makes responsibilities, delegation, or handoff behavior more reliable.","t":"Parallel subagents = multiple Task calls in ONE response.","k":["parallel Task calls","coordinator","latency"]},{"id":"ccar-f-107","d":"claude-code","x":"Your automated review calls the Claude API for each PR, using tool_use with a report_findings tool that returns a JSON array of finding objects (each with file_path, line_number, severity, category, and description). During testing on a large PR touching 30+ files, the response hits the max_tokens limit and the output is truncated mid-JSON, causing your pipeline's parser to fail.\n\nWhat is the most effective way to handle this?","o":["Increase max_tokens to the model's maximum and instruct Claude to keep finding descriptions under 50 words each.","Split the review into multiple API calls that each analyze a subset of the changed files, then merge the resulting findings arrays.","Add retry logic that detects truncated JSON and re-sends the request with instructions to report only critical and high severity findings.","Switch from tool_use to prompting Claude to return findings as a markdown list."],"a":[1],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","The best approach is to Split the review into multiple API calls that each analyze a subset of the changed files, then merge the resulting findings arrays. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Split the review into multiple API calls that each analyze a subset of the changed files, then merge the resulting findings arrays. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Output too big = SPLIT the input into batches, merge results.","k":["max_tokens","truncation","chunking"]},{"id":"ccar-f-108","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer sees an unfamiliar error message \"SYNC_CONFLICT: entity version mismatch detected\" in production logs but doesn't know which of the 12 services in the codebase generates it. They ask the agent to help locate the source code.\n\nWhat exploration approach will most efficiently find the responsible code?","o":["Use Glob to find files in directories commonly associated with error handling (such as errors/, exceptions/, or handlers/) across services, then Read each matching file.","Read the project's README and service configuration files to understand the architecture, then systematically Read source files in service directory.","Use Grep to search for distinctive text from the error message (like \"SYNC_CONFLICT\" or \"entity version mismatch\"), then Read the matching files to understand context.","Use Grep to find all files that import the project's error handling module, then Read those files to locate custom error definitions."],"a":[2],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Use Grep to search for distinctive text from the error message (like \"SYNC_CONFLICT\" or \"entity version mismatch\"), then Read the matching files to understand context. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"The best approach is to Use Grep to search for distinctive text from the error message (like \"SYNC_CONFLICT\" or \"entity version mismatch\"), then Read the matching files to understand context. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Distinctive error text = GREP it directly.","k":["Grep","error message","locate source"]},{"id":"ccar-f-109","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer asks your agent to add comprehensive tests to a legacy codebase with 200 files and minimal existing test coverage. The engineer hasn't specified which modules to prioritize.\n\nHow should the agent decompose this open-ended task?","o":["Start writing tests for the first module alphabetically, using test failures and imports to discover related files organically.","Create a fixed testing schedule upfront based on directory structure, allocating equal effort to each top-level directory regardless of code complexity or business importance.","Use Glob and Grep to map codebase structure, identify heavily-coupled modules, create a prioritized plan for high-impact areas, and revise as dependencies are discovered.","Systematically read all 200 files to create a complete function inventory before writing any tests, ensuring the testing plan accounts for every function before beginning."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Anthropic's agent best practices favor efficient context gathering with search tools (Glob/Grep) over exhaustively reading every file, which would waste context and time. Prioritizing high-impact, heavily-coupled modules and iteratively revising the plan as dependencies surface is the correct way to decompose an open-ended task; A is wasteful, B ignores impact/complexity, and C is arbitrary and unplanned.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"Anthropic's agent best practices favor efficient context gathering with search tools (Glob/Grep) over exhaustively reading every file, which would waste context and time. Prioritizing high-impact, heavily-coupled modules and iteratively revising the plan as dependencies surface is the correct way to decompose an open-ended task; A is wasteful, B ignores impact/complexity, and C is arbitrary and unplanned.","t":"Open-ended = MAP, PRIORITIZE high-impact, REVISE.","k":["prioritized plan","Glob","Grep"]},{"id":"ccar-f-110","d":"tools","x":"Your productivity agent connects to three MCP servers: an issue tracker (search_issues, get_issue, create_comment), a documentation wiki (search_docs, get_page, list_spaces), and a database explorer (run_query, get_schema, list_databases). When engineers ask cross-system questions like \"What database tables are affected by the authentication refactor in PROJ-1234?\", monitoring shows the agent makes 8-10 sequential tool calls, frequently issues exploratory calls because it lacks visibility into what content each server contains, and exhausts context space before completing complex investigations.\n\nWhat architectural change best leverages MCP capabilities to address these issues?","o":["Add a prepare_investigation tool to each server that accepts a natural language question and returns relevant content summaries","Add an orchestrator that routes questions to a single server based on keywords","Consolidate all three servers into a unified MCP server with cross-referencing capabilities","Expose each server's content catalog as MCP resources—issue summaries, documentation hierarchy, database schemas"],"a":[3],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","Collapsing everything into one path reduces precision and usually makes the system harder to control, not easier.","MCP resources are the protocol primitive designed for exactly this problem: servers expose browsable, application-controlled context (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, database schemas) that the agent can discover and read without exploratory tool-call round trips, directly fixing the visibility gap and context exhaustion. A rebuilds a monolith against MCP's composable design, C just adds more tools without leveraging MCP capabilities, and D's single-server keyword routing breaks inherently cross-system questions."],"e":"MCP resources are the protocol primitive designed for exactly this problem: servers expose browsable, application-controlled context (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, database schemas) that the agent can discover and read without exploratory tool-call round trips, directly fixing the visibility gap and context exhaustion. A rebuilds a monolith against MCP's composable design, C just adds more tools without leveraging MCP capabilities, and D's single-server keyword routing breaks inherently cross-system questions.","t":"Agent doesn't know what's there = expose MCP RESOURCES.","k":["MCP resources","content catalog","discovery"]},{"id":"ccar-f-111","d":"architecture","x":"The synthesis agent completes its initial pass but flags that three key research questions remain unanswered because the web search and document analysis agents didn't find relevant information on those specific subtopics. The coordinator currently proceeds directly to report generation, producing reports with incomplete coverage.\n\nWhat change would most effectively improve research completeness?","o":["Have the coordinator evaluate synthesis output for gaps, then re-delegate to web search and document analysis with targeted queries before Invoking synthesis again.","Increase the initial breadth of queries sent to web search and document analysis to reduce the probability of missing relevant information.","Give the synthesis agent direct access to web search tools so it can autonomously fill knowledge gaps without returning control to the coordinator.","Have the report generation agent note which research questions couldn't be answered, so users understand the limitations of the final output."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Have the coordinator evaluate synthesis output for gaps, then re-delegate to web search and document analysis with targeted queries before Invoking synthesis again. This fits the coordination problem described and makes responsibilities, delegation, or handoff behavior more reliable.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient."],"e":"The best approach is to Have the coordinator evaluate synthesis output for gaps, then re-delegate to web search and document analysis with targeted queries before Invoking synthesis again. This fits the coordination problem described and makes responsibilities, delegation, or handoff behavior more reliable.","t":"Gaps found = coordinator RE-DELEGATES targeted queries.","k":["gap filling","coordinator","iterative research"]},{"id":"ccar-f-112","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your documents (query) tool returns results as \"Found 3 documents: Q2 Budget Proposal, Q2 Budget Forecast, Annual Review\". You want the agent to document (4, multi) and doc (24, multi).\n\n What return format would best enable these multi-step workflows?","o":["A JSON array of document titles extracted from the search results.","More detailed human-readable descriptions including the size and authors.","Structured data containing document IDs and metadata for each result.","URLs that users can click to open the document in their browser."],"a":[2],"w":["This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix.","The best approach is to Structured data containing document IDs and metadata for each result. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Structured data containing document IDs and metadata for each result. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Next steps need IDs = return STRUCTURED IDs + metadata.","k":["document IDs","structured output","multi-step"]},{"id":"ccar-f-113","d":"tools","x":"Your agent has access to 50+ specialized API connectors for different external services. As the connector library grew, tool selection accuracy dropped to 58%. You design a search_connectors(description) tool that finds matching connectors, but in testing agents frequently skip searching and call connectors directly (often incorrectly), or search select wrong connectors from the filtered results.\n\nHow should you design the tool composition pattern to address both issues?","o":["Design connectors with built-in compatibility validation that return descriptive errors for mismatched requests.","Enhance all connector descriptions with detailed usage samples, edge cases, and input requirements. Add few-shot examples showing the correct search-then-use workflow.","Design a find_and_execute(description, params) composite tool that searches and immediately executes the best matching connector.","Design search_connectors to dynamically add matched connectors to the agent's available tools. Connectors start unavailable and persist once discovered."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","The best approach is to Design search_connectors to dynamically add matched connectors to the agent's available tools. Connectors start unavailable and persist once discovered. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task."],"e":"The best approach is to Design search_connectors to dynamically add matched connectors to the agent's available tools. Connectors start unavailable and persist once discovered. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","t":"Tools appear only after SEARCH = dynamic tool loading.","k":["dynamic tools","search then use","tool selection"]},{"id":"ccar-f-114","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your publish article tool calls an external CMS API that occasionally returns transient errors (network timeouts, 503s) and non-transient errors (403 permission denied, 422 validation failure). Currently, every error is returned directly to the agent, which leads to the agent retrying non-transient errors and wasting turns on failures that will never succeed.\n\nHow should you partition error-handling responsibility between the tool implementation and the agent?","o":["Handle all errors inside the tool: Implement retries with exponential backoff for every error type, and only surface a failure to the agent after a fixed number of retry attempts have been exhausted.","Surface all errors to the agent immediately with detailed context, and let the agent decide which errors to retry and how many times-keeping the tool implementation stateless and simple.","Handle transient errors (timeouts, 503s) with automatic retries inside the tool implementation, and surface non-transient errors (permission denied, validation fallures) to the agent with descriptive messages so it can take corrective action.","Implement a universal error handler that catches all exceptions and returns a generic \"tool unavailable- try again later\" message, shielding the agent from error complexity."],"a":[2],"w":["Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","The best approach is to Handle transient errors (timeouts, 503s) with automatic retries inside the tool implementation, and surface non-transient errors (permission denied, validation fallures) to the agent with descriptive messages so it can take corrective action. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"The best approach is to Handle transient errors (timeouts, 503s) with automatic retries inside the tool implementation, and surface non-transient errors (permission denied, validation fallures) to the agent with descriptive messages so it can take corrective action. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Transient = tool retries. Permanent = tell the agent.","k":["transient vs permanent","error partitioning"]},{"id":"ccar-f-115","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your scheduling agent uses get_available_slots(date, provider_id) to retrieve open appointment times, then book_appointment(provider_id, slot_time, patient_id) to reserve a slot. tickets show that 15% of booking attempts fall with \"slot no longer available\" because another user booked the slot between the availability check and the booking call.\n\nHow should you refactor these tools?","o":["Combine both tools into a single find_and_book_appointment that atomically checks availability and books, returning either the confirmed booking or available alternatives.","Modify book_appointment to return detailed failure information including currently available alternative slots when the requested slot is unavailable, enabling the agent to retry with a different time.","Add a hold_slot(provider_id, slot_time) tool that creates a 60 second temporary reservation, requiring the agent to call it between checking availability and booking.","Keep both tools but add retry logic to the agent's system prompt, instructing it to call get_available_slots again and select a different time if booking fails."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Combine both tools into a single find_and_book_appointment that atomically checks availability and books, returning either the confirmed booking or available alternatives. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","This is incorrect because retrying by itself does not resolve the underlying cause unless the failure is clearly transient.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Combine both tools into a single find_and_book_appointment that atomically checks availability and books, returning either the confirmed booking or available alternatives. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Race condition = make it ATOMIC (one tool).","k":["atomic operation","race condition","tool design"]},{"id":"ccar-f-116","d":"context-reliability","x":"Production monitoring shows your search_catalog tool fails 12% of the time: 8% are network timeouts that succeed when immediately retried, while 4% are query syntax errors from malformed user-provided filters that never succeed regardless of retry attempts. Currently, both error types are returned to the agent identically, causing it to waste turns retrying syntax errors and telling users to \"try again later\" for timeouts.\n\nHow should you modify the tool's error handling?","o":["Return all errors with a retryable boolean flag and error type details.","Implement automatic retry with backoff for network timeouts inside the tool; return syntax errors immediately with parameter validation details.","Apply exponential backoff retry logic to all errors uniformly, returning a generic \"service temporarily unavailable\" message after max retries are exhausted.","Add few-shot examples to your system prompt demonstrating how to distinguish network errors from syntax errors and handle each case appropriately."],"a":[1],"w":["Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","The best approach is to Implement automatic retry with backoff for network timeouts inside the tool; return syntax errors immediately with parameter validation details. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Retrying only helps when the problem is transient; it does not fix a design or classification problem by itself.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"The best approach is to Implement automatic retry with backoff for network timeouts inside the tool; return syntax errors immediately with parameter validation details. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Timeout = retry inside. Syntax = return details now.","k":["timeouts","syntax errors","retry policy"]},{"id":"ccar-f-117","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your document extraction tool uses ML models to extract invoice fields (vendor, amount, date). The models return confidence scores (0.0-1.0) for each extracted field. In production, you observe: (1) the agent proceeds with low-confidence extractions that are incorrect 23% of the time, and (2) the agent requests unnecessary human review for 31% of extractions that were actually correct.\n\nHow should you restructure the tool's output?","o":["Return fields organized into verified and needs_verification objects based on confidence thresholds.","Return fields with confidence scores, plus a requires_review boolean computed using your tested confidence thresholds, along with a review_reasons array explaining which fields triggered review.","Return fields with their raw confidence scores and add detailed few-shot examples to your system prompt demonstrating how to interpret different confidence ranges and when to request human review.","Compute an aggregate extraction quality score across all fields and return it alongside the extracted values. Include a text summary describing the overall extraction reliability."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Return fields with confidence scores, plus a requires_review boolean computed using your tested confidence thresholds, along with a review_reasons array explaining which fields triggered review. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Return fields with confidence scores, plus a requires_review boolean computed using your tested confidence thresholds, along with a review_reasons array explaining which fields triggered review. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Return SCORES + requires_review flag + REASONS.","k":["requires_review","confidence thresholds","review_reasons"]},{"id":"ccar-f-118","d":"tools","x":"Your agent includes an update_game_score tool that accepts game_date (string), home_team (string), and away_team (string) parameters. Production logs reveal recurring issues: the agent uses team nicknames instead of official names, applies inconsistent date formats, and selects the wrong game when teams have rematches in the same season.\n\nWhat tool interface change would effectively prevent these errors?","o":["Add detailed examples to the tool description showing the required date format and complete list of official team names.","Add enum constraints listing valid team names for both team parameters, and add a regex pattern enforcing ISO 8601 format for the date parameter.","Add a season parameter to disambiguate rematches, and add a confirm_before_update flag that returns the resolved game details for the agent to verify before the score is committed.","Replace the three parameters with a single game_id parameter and a separate search_games lookup tool that returns matching game IDs."],"a":[3],"w":["Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Replace the three parameters with a single game_id parameter and a separate search_games lookup tool that returns matching game IDs. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace the three parameters with a single game_id parameter and a separate search_games lookup tool that returns matching game IDs. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Ambiguous identity = use an ID + a LOOKUP tool.","k":["game_id","lookup tool","disambiguation"]},{"id":"ccar-f-119","d":"architecture","x":"You've configured the system so that all four subagents have access to the complete set of 18 tools. During testing, agents frequently call tools outside their specialization—the synthesis agent attempts web searches, and the report generator tries to analyze documents.\n\nWhat is the primary cause of this poor tool selection behavior?","o":["Choosing from 18 tools instead of 4-5 relevant ones increases decision complexity beyond reliable selection thresholds.","The coordinator cannot track which capabilities each subagent has, leading to misrouted tasks.","The agents' role descriptions in their system prompts conflict with having access to tools outside that role.","The tool definitions consume too much context window space, leaving insufficient room for task content."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Choosing from 18 tools instead of 4-5 relevant ones increases decision complexity beyond reliable selection thresholds. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place."],"e":"The best approach is to Choosing from 18 tools instead of 4-5 relevant ones increases decision complexity beyond reliable selection thresholds. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","t":"Too many tools = poor selection. Give 4-5.","k":["tool count","tool scoping","specialization"]},{"id":"ccar-f-120","d":"claude-code","x":"You've documented API error handling conventions in a CLAUDE.md file at your project root, specifying that endpoint handlers should use a custom ApiError class. After several sessions, you notice Claude Code sometimes follows these conventions and sometimes uses generic try/catch blocks with string messages. The inconsistency appears random across different coding sessions.\n\nWhat's the most efficient first diagnostic step?","o":["Search for conflicting instructions in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md or ~/.claude/rules/ that might override your project conventions.","Run /memory to check which memory files are loaded and verify your CLAUDE.md is included.","Add more detailed code examples to your CLAUDE.md showing the exact ApiError usage pattern for different endpoint types.","Create path-specific rules in claude/rules/handlers.md with YAML frontmatter scoping the error handling instructions to your API handler files."],"a":[1],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Run /memory to check which memory files are loaded and verify your CLAUDE.md is included. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Run /memory to check which memory files are loaded and verify your CLAUDE.md is included. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Instructions ignored? First run /MEMORY.","k":["/memory","CLAUDE.md","diagnostics"]},{"id":"ccar-f-121","d":"claude-code","x":"Your CI pipeline performs security-focused code reviews on approximately 50 PRs daily, currently costing $150/day using the synchronous API. Reviews are non-blocking—developers merge after tests pass and address findings in follow-up commits. You're evaluating the Message Batches API for its 50% cost reduction.\n\nWhat factor most determines whether batch processing is appropriate for this use case?","o":["Whether reducing per-review latency from 30-60 seconds to near-instant matters for your workflow.","Whether your result processing can handle reviews arriving in a different order than submitted.","Whether review feedback arriving up to 24 hours after PR creation remains actionable.","Whether you can structure each review as a single request without multi-turn refinement."],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The Message Batches API is asynchronous — most batches finish within 1 hour but can take up to 24 hours — so the decisive suitability factor is latency tolerance: whether review findings delivered up to 24 hours after PR creation are still actionable in a non-blocking, merge-then-fix workflow. Batching increases latency (B is backwards), results are correlated via custom_id so ordering is a non-issue (D), and single-request structuring (A) is secondary to the latency question.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The Message Batches API is asynchronous — most batches finish within 1 hour but can take up to 24 hours — so the decisive suitability factor is latency tolerance: whether review findings delivered up to 24 hours after PR creation are still actionable in a non-blocking, merge-then-fix workflow. Batching increases latency (B is backwards), results are correlated via custom_id so ordering is a non-issue (D), and single-request structuring (A) is secondary to the latency question.","t":"Batch fits only if 24h LATE results are still useful.","k":["Message Batches","latency tolerance","cost"]},{"id":"ccar-f-122","d":"context-reliability","x":"After a 40-minute session helping plan a dinner party, the conversation has grown to 78,000 tokens. The history includes: (1) the user mentioning a guest has a severe shellfish allergy, (2) measurements for scaling recipes to 8 servings, (3) the user's clarification that \"room temperature butter\" means 68°F in their kitchen, and (4) general back-and-forth about meal timing and presentation. You need to implement context management before the window limit is reached.\n\nWhat approach best balances information preservation with token reduction?","o":["Summarize the entire conversation history into a concise summary capturing main topics discussed, then append new messages going forward.","Implement a sliding window retaining only the most recent 20,000 tokens relying on users to re-state important information when relevant.","Store the full conversation externally and use semantic search to retrieve relevant portions for each turn, loading only matching segments into context.","Extract critical structured data (allergies, serving counts, user-defined terms) into a compact reference section, summarize general discussion, and retain recent exchanges verbatim."],"a":[3],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Dumping the full transcript adds noise and makes it harder for the next actor to find the key facts quickly.","The best approach is to Extract critical structured data (allergies, serving counts, user-defined terms) into a compact reference section, summarize general discussion, and retain recent exchanges verbatim. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall."],"e":"The best approach is to Extract critical structured data (allergies, serving counts, user-defined terms) into a compact reference section, summarize general discussion, and retain recent exchanges verbatim. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Critical facts PINNED + rest summarized + recent verbatim.","k":["pinned facts","summarization","allergy"]},{"id":"ccar-f-123","d":"tools","x":"Your resource allocation tool returns a simple acknowledgment message after provisioning is requested. Users frequently approve allocations and immediately ask \"how much did that cost?\" or \"which project was that?\" - indicating they confirmed without understanding the request.\n\nWhat tool design change would most effectively address this?","o":["Add a user_acknowledged: boolean parameter that must be set true, with instructions for the agent to only set it after the user explicitly confirms they reviewed the details","Implement a 60-second hold before execution completes, allowing users time to review pending allocations and cancel if needed","Add a detail_level parameter with options \"minimal\" or \"comprehensive\" that controls how much context the agent presents in confirmations","Return structured data including cost estimate, target project, resource specifications, and impact summary in the tool response"],"a":[3],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Return structured data including cost estimate, target project, resource specifications, and impact summary in the tool response. This preserves the link between conclusions and supporting evidence, which is the key requirement in the scenario."],"e":"The best approach is to Return structured data including cost estimate, target project, resource specifications, and impact summary in the tool response. This preserves the link between conclusions and supporting evidence, which is the key requirement in the scenario.","t":"Informed approval = show COST + PROJECT + IMPACT.","k":["structured response","informed consent","tool output"]},{"id":"ccar-f-124","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your search products tool queries an external catalog API that returns paginated results (50 items per request). Production logs show queries frequently match 200+ products, and the design that auto-fetches all pages causes 15-20 second delays.\n\nHow should you redesign the pagination handling?","o":["Add a max pages parameter (default: 2) that controls how many pages are fetched internally.","Create separate search products and fetch more results tools for pagination.","Implement server-side relevance ranking and return only the top 50 most relevant items.","Return the first page with total match count and cursor for additional pages."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Return the first page with total match count and cursor for additional pages. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives."],"e":"The best approach is to Return the first page with total match count and cursor for additional pages. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Pagination = first page + TOTAL + CURSOR.","k":["pagination","cursor","latency"]},{"id":"ccar-f-125","d":"tools","x":"Your MCP server implements a check_availability tool that queries an external calendar API. During testing, you encounter three error conditions: (1) the tool is called with a malformed request, missing the required user_email parameter (2) the calendar API returns a 404 because the specified user doesn't exist in the calendar system (3) the calendar API returns a 503 because the service is temporarily unavailable.\n\nHow should each error be reported according to MCP's error handling design?","o":["Report all three as JSON-RPC protocol errors.","Report errors 1 and 2 as JSON-RPC protocol errors, report error 3 as a tool result with isError: true","Report all three as tool results with isError: true","Report error 1 as a JSON-RPC protocol error, report errors 2 and 3 as tool results with isError: true"],"a":[3],"w":["This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Report error 1 as a JSON-RPC protocol error, report errors 2 and 3 as tool results with isError: true. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early."],"e":"The best approach is to Report error 1 as a JSON-RPC protocol error, report errors 2 and 3 as tool results with isError: true. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Bad CALL = protocol error. Bad OUTCOME = isError result.","k":["JSON-RPC error","isError","MCP error design"]},{"id":"ccar-f-126","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your system has been running for 3 weeks and human reviewers have corrected 847 extractions. Analysis reveals a recurring pattern: when recipes use informal measurements like \"a handful\" or \"a splash,\" the model either invents specific amounts or leaves fields empty—accounting for 23% of all corrections.\n\nHow should you use this feedback to improve extraction accuracy?","o":["Update your JSON schema to add a \"measurement_type\" enum field (precise/informal).","Add few-shot examples to your prompt demonstrating correct handling of informal measurements— extracting them verbatim rather than converting or omitting them.","Fine-tune the model on the 847 corrected extractions.","Implement a post-processing layer that uses pattern matching to detect informal measurement phrases in source text and automatically populate values when the extraction is empty."],"a":[1],"w":["This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","The best approach is to Add few-shot examples to your prompt demonstrating correct handling of informal measurements— extracting them verbatim rather than converting or omitting them. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Add few-shot examples to your prompt demonstrating correct handling of informal measurements— extracting them verbatim rather than converting or omitting them. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Recurring pattern in corrections = FEW-SHOT for that pattern.","k":["few-shot","informal measurements","feedback"]},{"id":"ccar-f-127","d":"context-reliability","x":"\"You're the Lead Data Scientist/Engineer on a critical project. Something is not completely right with technical details or the data, and it negative impacts expected outputs. They need to independently develop both approaches to evaluation data... \n\nHow do you manage this scenario?\"","o":["Start two fresh sessions, having each re-read the relevant source files before beginning.","Continue in the original session, developing end-to-end tests first, then snapshot tests sequentially.","Export the analysis session's key findings to a file, then create two new sessions that reference this file.","Resume the analysis session with fork_session enabled, creating a separate branch for each testing strategy."],"a":[3],"w":["Starting over throws away useful context that the scenario is trying to preserve, so it adds cost without solving the underlying problem.","Handling both paths sequentially misses the point of keeping the branches separate and makes comparison or independent exploration harder.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Resume the analysis session with fork_session enabled, creating a separate branch for each testing strategy. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives."],"e":"The best approach is to Resume the analysis session with fork_session enabled, creating a separate branch for each testing strategy. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Two independent paths from one analysis = FORK.","k":["fork_session","branches"]},{"id":"ccar-f-128","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your automated reviewer uses a single prompt covering security issues, API design, and business logic correctness. Your evaluation suite shows strong recall findings (82%) but poor recall for business logic edge cases in quiz scoring (34%). When you add few-shot examples of logic bugs to the prompt, logic recall is 41% but API design recall drops to 68%. \n\nHow should you address this trade-off to improve detection across both categories?","o":["Replace the few-shot examples with a detailed checklist of specific logic edge cases to verify, such as division-by-zero in score calculation or grading thresholds.","Split the review into separate focused prompts - one for security and API design, another for business logic - each with dedicated examples, then combine findings before posting.","Provide the full repository as context instead of just the changed files and surrounding code, giving the model deeper visibility into business logic.","Upgrade to a more capable model tier, since its stronger reasoning will handle both concern types in a single prompt and eliminate the recall trade-off."],"a":[1],"w":["Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","The best approach is to Split the review into separate focused prompts - one for security and API design, another for business logic - each with dedicated examples, then combine findings before posting. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Split the review into separate focused prompts - one for security and API design, another for business logic - each with dedicated examples, then combine findings before posting. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Recall trade-off between concerns = SPLIT into focused prompts.","k":["focused prompts","recall trade-off","code review"]},{"id":"ccar-f-129","d":"claude-code","x":"Your code review assistant needs to analyze pull requests and provide feedback on three aspects: code style compliance, potential security issues, and documentation completeness. Each aspect requires reading files, running analysis tools, and generating a report section. The review process follows the same three-step workflow for every PR.\n\nWhich task decomposition pattern is most appropriate for this workflow?","o":["Prompt chaining—break the review into sequential steps where each aspect (style, security, documentation) is analyzed separately, with outputs combined in a final synthesis step.","Routing—classify each PR by type (feature, bugfix, refactor) first, then route to different review prompts optimized for that category.","Single comprehensive prompt—include all instructions in one prompt and let the model handle all three aspects simultaneously.","Orchestrator-workers—have a central LLM analyze each PR to dynamically determine which checks are needed, then delegate to specialized worker LLMs for each identified subtask."],"a":[0],"w":["The scenario explicitly states the review \"follows the same three-step workflow for every PR,\" which per Anthropic's \"Building Effective Agents\" guidance is the textbook case for the prompt chaining workflow: a fixed, predictable sequence of subtasks each handled by a focused prompt, then synthesized. Orchestrator-workers (D) is only warranted when the needed subtasks vary unpredictably per input, and routing (B) applies when inputs fall into distinct categories needing different handling—neither fits a fixed, identical workflow.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The scenario explicitly states the review \"follows the same three-step workflow for every PR,\" which per Anthropic's \"Building Effective Agents\" guidance is the textbook case for the prompt chaining workflow: a fixed, predictable sequence of subtasks each handled by a focused prompt, then synthesized. Orchestrator-workers (D) is only warranted when the needed subtasks vary unpredictably per input, and routing (B) applies when inputs fall into distinct categories needing different handling—neither fits a fixed, identical workflow.","t":"Same fixed steps every time = PROMPT CHAINING.","k":["prompt chaining","fixed workflow","building effective agents"]},{"id":"ccar-f-130","d":"architecture","x":"After the web search and document analysis subagents complete their tasks, the coordinator needs to spawn the synthesis subagent to synthesize the findings.\n\nWhat is the correct approach for providing the synthesis subagent with the information it needs?","o":["Spawn the subagent with only a brief task description, relying on automatic context inheritance from the coordinator","Pass reference identifiers and configure the subagent with read access to a shared memory store where other subagents deposited their results","Provide the subagent with tool definitions that allow it to request outputs from other subagents via callbacks","Include the complete findings from both subagents directly in the synthesis subagent's prompt"],"a":[1],"w":["It does not make the coordination boundary clearer, so the workflow would still be fragile or inefficient.","Anthropic's multi-agent best practice is for subagents to deposit their full outputs as artifacts in external/shared storage and pass lightweight references, so the synthesis subagent reads the prior results directly rather than receiving lossy or token-heavy copies through prompts. Callbacks between subagents (A) and automatic context inheritance (D) don't exist in this architecture, and inlining complete findings into the prompt (B) bloats context and risks information loss.","This adds coordination overhead and is not justified unless the work is clearly separable into independent subtasks.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"Anthropic's multi-agent best practice is for subagents to deposit their full outputs as artifacts in external/shared storage and pass lightweight references, so the synthesis subagent reads the prior results directly rather than receiving lossy or token-heavy copies through prompts. Callbacks between subagents (A) and automatic context inheritance (D) don't exist in this architecture, and inlining complete findings into the prompt (B) bloats context and risks information loss.","t":"Big outputs = store as ARTIFACTS, pass REFERENCES.","k":["shared memory","references","artifacts"]},{"id":"ccar-f-131","d":"tools","x":"Developer Productivity An engineer asks the agent to find all files in the monorepo that import the @company/auth package to understand how authentication is used across services.\n\nWhich built-in tool is most appropriate for this task?","o":["Bash, to execute find . -type d -name \"*auth*\" and explore matching directories","Read, starting with package.json files to trace dependency declarations","Glob, to find files with \"auth\" in their filename or path","Grep, to search for the import statement pattern across file contents"],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The task is to find every file whose contents contain an import of @company/auth, which is a content- search problem — exactly what the built-in Grep tool (ripgrep-based) is designed for. Glob and find/Bash only match filenames or directory names, which would miss the many importing files that don't have \"auth\" in their path, and reading package.json files only shows declared dependencies, not actual usage sites."],"e":"The task is to find every file whose contents contain an import of @company/auth, which is a content- search problem — exactly what the built-in Grep tool (ripgrep-based) is designed for. Glob and find/Bash only match filenames or directory names, which would miss the many importing files that don't have \"auth\" in their path, and reading package.json files only shows declared dependencies, not actual usage sites.","t":"Inside file contents = GREP. File names = GLOB.","k":["Grep","Glob","content search"]},{"id":"ccar-f-132","d":"tools","x":"Your MCP server includes archive_file(file_id) and delete_file(file_id) tools. Production logs show the agent calls delete_file when users ask to \"remove old backups,\" policy requires archiving backup files. Both tools currently have minimal descriptions: \"Archives a file\" and \"Deletes a file.\" \n\nWhich change most directly improves tool selection?","o":["Expand tool descriptions to clarify use cases, adding guidance like \"Do not use for backup files\" to delete_file.","Add a confirmation step that requires users to type \"CONFIRM DELETE\" before delete_file executes.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating that requests involving \"backup\" or \"old\" should use archive_file.","Implement server-side validation that rejects delete_file calls for files tagged as backups, returning an error message suggesting archive_file."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Expand tool descriptions to clarify use cases, adding guidance like \"Do not use for backup files\" to delete_file. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place."],"e":"The best approach is to Expand tool descriptions to clarify use cases, adding guidance like \"Do not use for backup files\" to delete_file. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","t":"Wrong tool chosen = clarify DESCRIPTIONS with when-not-to-use.","k":["tool descriptions","archive vs delete"]},{"id":"ccar-f-133","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your CRM agent's delete_contact tool handles requests like \"delete the duplicate entry for Acme Corp.\" The database contains similarly named records (e.g., \"Acme Corp,\" \"Acme Corporation,\" \"ACME Corp Inc.\"), and analytics show 8% of deletions are reversed within 24 hours due to misidentified records. Users have also complained that the current multi-step confirmation flow adds too much friction to routine cleanup tasks.\n\nWhich approach most effectively reduces the error rate while maintaining workflow efficiency?","o":["Implement soft-delete with a 30-day recovery window so users can undo mistakes without slowing down the deletion workflow.","Deploy automated duplicate detection that identifies and merges probable duplicates, removing the need for manual deletion requests.","Present matched records with differentiating fields and require single-click confirmation of the intended target before executing deletion.","Require users to supply the exact record ID from the CRM Interface rather than using natural language references to contact names."],"a":[2],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing.","The best approach is to Present matched records with differentiating fields and require single-click confirmation of the intended target before executing deletion. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"The best approach is to Present matched records with differentiating fields and require single-click confirmation of the intended target before executing deletion. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Look-alike records = SHOW differences + ONE-click confirm.","k":["disambiguation","confirmation","friction"]},{"id":"ccar-f-134","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your team is extracting structured data from 50,000 legacy legal contracts under a two-week deadline. Initial testing with 500 sample documents shows 82% pass JSON schema first attempt, while the remaining 18% fall due to diverse issues—missing required fields, malformed dates, and incorrectly identified parties. Documents that fail typically need refinements targeting their specific failure modes before extraction succeeds.\n\nWhich batch processing strategy is the most cost-efficient while still meeting the deadline?","o":["Use the real-time API for all 50,000 documents since the batch API's 24-hour processing window creates unacceptable deadline risk.","Split documents into 10 sequential batches of 5,000 each, analysing results and refining prompts between batches to improve extraction quality progressively.","Submit all 50,000 documents via batch API, then submit failed extractions in successive batches— refining prompts between each batch—until all documents pass validation.","Process 2,000 sample documents via real time API to identify failure patterns and refine prompts, then batch process all 50,000 with the optimized prompts."],"a":[2],"w":["That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Submit all 50,000 documents via batch API, then submit failed extractions in successive batches— refining prompts between each batch—until all documents pass validation. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Submit all 50,000 documents via batch API, then submit failed extractions in successive batches— refining prompts between each batch—until all documents pass validation. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Batch ALL, then re-batch only FAILURES with refined prompts.","k":["Batch API","iterative refinement","cost"]},{"id":"ccar-f-135","d":"prompt-output","x":"After deploying an updated system prompt that improves response quality, users with multi-session conversations spanning several weeks report that the assistant now contradicts its earlier statements and has a noticeably different communication style. New users don't experience these issues.\n\nWhat's the best approach to resolve this?","o":["Add a transition message when sessions resume explaining that the assistant has been updated and behavior may differ.","Add instructions to the new system prompt directing the assistant to maintain consistency with any prior statements in the conversation history.","Regenerate summaries of existing conversations using the new prompt and replace the stored histories to align past context with current behavior.","Version system prompts and associate each conversation with the prompt version under which it started, applying updates only to new conversations."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","The best approach is to Version system prompts and associate each conversation with the prompt version under which it started, applying updates only to new conversations. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives."],"e":"The best approach is to Version system prompts and associate each conversation with the prompt version under which it started, applying updates only to new conversations. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Old chats keep OLD prompt version. Version your prompts.","k":["prompt versioning","consistency","multi-session"]},{"id":"ccar-f-136","d":"context-reliability","x":"Evaluation shows 94% extraction accuracy on short meeting transcripts (<30 minutes) but only 68% on longer transcripts (>60 minutes) where discussions meander and key information is scattered throughout. Transcripts of both lengths fit within the model's context window.\n\nWhat pattern most effectively improves accuracy on complex, lengthy documents?","o":["Split lengthy transcripts Into chunks, extract from each chunk separately, then merge and deduplicate the results.","Add a pre-extraction step where the model summarizes key discussions and conclusions before performing structured extraction.","Add few-shot examples demonstrating correct extraction from lengthy meetings with scattered Information.","Upgrade to a more capable model tier for the extraction task"],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Split lengthy transcripts Into chunks, extract from each chunk separately, then merge and deduplicate the results. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem."],"e":"The best approach is to Split lengthy transcripts Into chunks, extract from each chunk separately, then merge and deduplicate the results. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Scattered info in long docs = CHUNK, extract, MERGE.","k":["chunking","merge","long documents"]},{"id":"ccar-f-137","d":"claude-code","x":"You're implementing a complex graph traversal algorithm with specific performance requirements and edge cases to handle (disconnected nodes, cycles, weighted edges). You want to structure your workflow for efficient iterative refinement with Claude.\n\nWhat approach will most effectively enable progressive improvement across multiple iterations?","o":["Provide Claude with a reference implementation from documentation, then ask it to rewrite the code to match your codebase style and add the required edge case handling, comparing outputs against the reference.","Write a test suite covering expected behavior, edge cases, and performance requirements before implementation. Ask Claude to write code that passes the tests, then iterate by sharing test failures with each refinement request.","Have Claude extensively research the algorithm and create a detailed implementation plan using extended thinking, then implement the complete solution based on that plan.","Provide Claude with a detailed natural language specification of the algorithm, including all requirements and edge cases. Review each output manually and provide descriptive feedback on what behavior needs to change."],"a":[1],"w":["Documentation may give useful background, but it is not a reliable way to discover every real caller or usage path in code.","The best approach is to Write a test suite covering expected behavior, edge cases, and performance requirements before implementation. Ask Claude to write code that passes the tests, then iterate by sharing test failures with each refinement request. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Write a test suite covering expected behavior, edge cases, and performance requirements before implementation. Ask Claude to write code that passes the tests, then iterate by sharing test failures with each refinement request. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"TESTS first, then iterate on failures.","k":["test-driven","iteration","edge cases"]},{"id":"ccar-f-138","d":"claude-code","x":"Your infrastructure-as-code repository includes Terraform modules (/terraform/), Kubernetes manifests (/kubernetes/), and CI/CD pipeline scripts (/pipelines/). Each requires different conventions, but your single root CLAUDE.md has grown to 500+ lines. When developers work on Kubernetes files, Terraform-specific rules load into context unnecessarily, consuming tokens. \n\nWhat is the best approach to reorganize so only relevant guidance loads when editing specific file types?","o":["Split content into subdirectory CLAUDE.md files (/terraform/CLAUDE.md, /kubernetes/CLAUDE.md), so Claude loads directory-specific guidance.","Create files in .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter path-scoping (e.g., paths: [\"terraform/**/*\"]), loading rules only when editing matching files.","Keep the root CLAUDE.md and use @path/to/import syntax to modularly include tool-specific guidance files from separate documents.","Restructure the root CLAUDE.md into clearly labeled sections with headers (e.g., \"## Terraform Conventions\"), improving organization and readability."],"a":[1],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The best approach is to Create files in .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter path-scoping (e.g., paths: [\"terraform/**/*\"]), loading rules only when editing matching files. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem."],"e":"The best approach is to Create files in .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter path-scoping (e.g., paths: [\"terraform/**/*\"]), loading rules only when editing matching files. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Load rules by FILE PATH = .claude/rules/ with paths frontmatter.","k":[".claude/rules","path scoping","CLAUDE.md size"]},{"id":"ccar-f-139","d":"context-reliability","x":"After expanding the agent's MCP tools with delivery-specific capabilities (check_delivery_status, contact_driver, issue_credit, apply_promo_code, update_delivery_address, reschedule_delivery), the total tool count has grown from 4 to 10. Your evaluation suite shows tool selection accuracy has dropped to 71%. Log analysis reveals the majority of errors involve the agent selecting between semantically overlapping tools- calling issue_credit when process_refund is correct, and calling check_delivery_status when lookup_order already returns the needed data.\n\nWhich approach structurally eliminates the semantic overlaps that are being logged as the error source?","o":["Consolidate semantically overlapping tools-merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single resolve_compensation tool with an optional include_tracking flag.","Split the tools across two sub-agents - a \"financial resolution\" agent with process_refund, issue_credit, and apply_promo_code, and a \"delivery\" agent with the remaining delivery tools - with a coordinator routing between them.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating correct selection for each ambiguous tool pair, such as showing when issue_credit or process_refund is appropriate.","Enable the tool search tool with defer_loading on the six new tools, keeping the original four always loaded, so the agent dynamically calls it when needed."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Consolidate semantically overlapping tools-merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single resolve_compensation tool with an optional include_tracking flag. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place."],"e":"The best approach is to Consolidate semantically overlapping tools-merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single resolve_compensation tool with an optional include_tracking flag. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","t":"Overlapping tools = MERGE them.","k":["tool consolidation","semantic overlap"]},{"id":"ccar-f-140","d":"tools","x":"After expanding the agent's MCP tools with delivery-specific capabilities [apply_promo_code, update_delivery_address, reconcile_delivery], the total tool count has grown from 1 to 7. We have observed that the agent now shows tool selection accuracy has dropped from 86% to 71%. Log analysis reveals the majority of errors involve the agent selecting between semantically overlapping tools — calling issue_credit when process_refund was correct, and calling check_delivery_status when looking order_allready_returns_the_needed_data.\n\nWhich approach structurally eliminates the semantic overlap identified in the error source?","o":["Split the tools across two sub-agents — a 'financial resolution' agent with issue_credit, process_refund, return_order, and apply_promo_code and a 'delivery operations' agent with the remaining delivery tools — with a coordinating routing between them.","Consolidate semantically overlapping tools – merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single handle_promotions tool with an action parameter and fold check_delivery_status into lookup_order with an optional include_tracking flag.","Enable the tool search tool with defer_loading on the six new tools, keeping the original two always loaded, so the agent dynamically discovers specialized tools only when needed.","Add few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating correct selection for each ambiguous tool, such as showing when issue_credit applies versus when process_refund is appropriate."],"a":[1],"w":["It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","The best approach is to Consolidate semantically overlapping tools – merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single handle_promotions tool with an action parameter and fold check_delivery_status into lookup_order with an optional include_tracking flag. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","It does not reduce the ambiguity that is causing the agent to pick the wrong tool in the first place.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure."],"e":"The best approach is to Consolidate semantically overlapping tools – merge issue_credit and process_refund into a single handle_promotions tool with an action parameter and fold check_delivery_status into lookup_order with an optional include_tracking flag. This reduces tool-selection mistakes by making the decision surface clearer and better aligned with the agent's task.","t":"Remove overlap = MERGE + optional flag.","k":["tool consolidation","action parameter","include_tracking"]},{"id":"ccar-f-141","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your order management system requires tools for three distinct operations: issuing refunds (requires amount and reason), canceling orders (requires reason), and res (requires shipping address). Each operation shares an order id parameter but has different additional requirements. You notice during testing that with your current frequently omits required parameters or includes irrelevant ones.\n\nWhat design change will most effectively improve parameter accuracy?","o":["Keep one unified tool with a nested operation object parameter whose internal structure varies by operation type, documented in the tool description.","Keep one unified tool with all parameters marked optional, but add few-shot examples in the system prompt showing correct parameter combinations for each operation.","Keep one unified tool but add JSON Schema if-then-else conditionals to enforce that parameters like amount are required only when the operation type is \"refund\".","Split into three separate tools (each defining only the parameters required for that specific operation."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","The best approach is to Split into three separate tools (each defining only the parameters required for that specific operation. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives."],"e":"The best approach is to Split into three separate tools (each defining only the parameters required for that specific operation. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Different required params = SEPARATE tools.","k":["tool split","required parameters"]},{"id":"ccar-f-142","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your fitness coaching assistant uses a system prompt with detailed conditional logic: \"If the user mentions being a beginner, provide step-by-step form instructions. If they use term 'progressive overload' or 'superset', respond concisely. If they ask about injury history, always recommend consulting a physician.\" During evaluation, you find the assistant correct explicit expertise declarations but struggles when users don't clearly state their level-often defaulting to overly detailed responses regardless of contextual cues like technical terms.\n\nWhich change to the system prompt would most directly address this failure to pick up on implicit expertise signals?","o":["Add an explicit instruction for the model to ask a clarifying question about experience level whenever the user's expertise isn't immediately clear from their first message.","Replace most conditionals with a general principle: \"Adapt explanation depth to match user expertise, mirroring their terminology.\" Keep only the safety-critical conditional abou consultations.","Add more conditional branches to cover additional expertise signals, such as \"If user mentions specific rep ranges or asks about periodization, treat as advanced.\"","Implement a pre-conversation intake that asks users to rate their experience level, then inject that rating into the system prompt as context for all subsequent responses."],"a":[1],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","The best approach is to Replace most conditionals with a general principle: \"Adapt explanation depth to match user expertise, mirroring their terminology.\" Keep only the safety-critical conditional abou consultations. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace most conditionals with a general principle: \"Adapt explanation depth to match user expertise, mirroring their terminology.\" Keep only the safety-critical conditional abou consultations. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Many rigid IFs? Replace with one general PRINCIPLE (keep safety rule).","k":["general principle","implicit signals","system prompt"]},{"id":"ccar-f-143","d":"context-reliability","x":"You're building a security scanning workflow. \n\nWhen engineers need to locate all occurrences of a dangerous function like eval() across a large codebase, which tool should your agent use for content search?","o":["Use Glob with a pattern like /*eval* to find files, then Read each matching file.","Use Bash to run ls -R | grep eval to recursively list files containing eval.","Read the project's main entry file and follow import statements to trace where eval might be used.","Use Grep to search for the pattern \"eval(\" across all files in the codebase."],"a":[3],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Grep is the purpose-built content-search tool (ripgrep-based) for finding patterns inside file contents across a large codebase, which is exactly what locating all eval( call sites requires. Glob only matches file names, reading entry files and tracing imports doesn't scale or guarantee coverage, and ls -R | grep only matches file names, not file contents."],"e":"Grep is the purpose-built content-search tool (ripgrep-based) for finding patterns inside file contents across a large codebase, which is exactly what locating all eval( call sites requires. Glob only matches file names, reading entry files and tracing imports doesn't scale or guarantee coverage, and ls -R | grep only matches file names, not file contents.","t":"Find code patterns inside files = GREP.","k":["Grep","security scan"]},{"id":"ccar-f-144","d":"claude-code","x":"Your test generation produces unit tests for new code, but reviews show 55% are low-value: trivial assertions that only verify functions don't throw exceptions, tests duplicating existing coverage, or tests ignoring your team's fixture conventions.\n\nHow do you reduce the rate of low-value tests being generated in the first place?","o":["Document testing standards in CLAUDE.md including valuable test criteria, available fixtures with intended use cases, and examples distinguishing meaningful behavioral tests from trivial assertions.","Add post-generation coverage analysis that automatically filters out any generated test that doesn't increase line coverage beyond what existing tests provide.","Implement a two-phase generation where a second Claude call scores each test against quality criteria, filtering out low-scoring tests before presenting results to developers.","Restrict test generation to directories where historical quality metrics show higher acceptance rates, disabling it for areas where generated tests consistently require heavy editing."],"a":[0],"w":["The question explicitly asks how to reduce low-value tests \"being generated in the first place,\" which means fixing the upstream cause: Claude lacks context about what the team considers a valuable test. Documenting testing standards, fixture usage, and good-vs-trivial examples in CLAUDE.md gives Claude that context at generation time, whereas A and D only filter bad output after the fact and B avoids the problem rather than solving it.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The question explicitly asks how to reduce low-value tests \"being generated in the first place,\" which means fixing the upstream cause: Claude lacks context about what the team considers a valuable test. Documenting testing standards, fixture usage, and good-vs-trivial examples in CLAUDE.md gives Claude that context at generation time, whereas A and D only filter bad output after the fact and B avoids the problem rather than solving it.","t":"Fix it upstream: testing standards in CLAUDE.md.","k":["CLAUDE.md","testing standards","fixtures"]},{"id":"ccar-f-145","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your expense reimbursement agent processes employee requests using a process reimbursement tool. Company policy requires that reimbursements above $500 must be approved before funds are disbursed. \n\nThe agent handles hundreds of requests daily, and you need the threshold enforcement to be tamper-proof regardless of how the agent is prompted ensures the $500 approval threshold cannot be bypassed?","o":["The process reimbursement tool accepts amount and details, and internally enforces the threshold; amounts <$500 are auto-disbursed and the tool returns a success confirmation. Amounts >$500 cause the tool to create a pending approval request and return a status indicating manager review is pending.","Provide two tools: auto reimburse (hard-coded limit of $500) and manager approval. Include detailed system prompt instructions telling the agent to check the amount and use the appropriate tool. Add a Post ToolUse hook that logs which tool was called for auditing.","The process reimbursement tool accepts an approved by manager parameter. The system prompt instructs the agent to only set this to true after confirming that a manager approved the request. A nightly audit script reviews all reimbursements where approved by manager was set to true.","Implement the threshold check in a PreToolUse hook that inspects the amount parameter before process reimbursement executes. If the amount exceeds $500, the hook modifies the context to add a requires approval: true flag, which the tool checks before disbursing."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to The process reimbursement tool accepts amount and details, and internally enforces the threshold; amounts <$500 are auto-disbursed and the tool returns a success confirmation. Amounts >$500 cause the tool to create a pending approval request and return a status indicating manager review is pending. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to The process reimbursement tool accepts amount and details, and internally enforces the threshold; amounts <$500 are auto-disbursed and the tool returns a success confirmation. Amounts >$500 cause the tool to create a pending approval request and return a status indicating manager review is pending. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Tamper-proof rule = enforce INSIDE the tool.","k":["tool-level enforcement","approval threshold"]},{"id":"ccar-f-146","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your portfolio value tool returns the total value of a user's investment portfolio. You're deciding between returning a structured JSON object with explicit fields versus returning information as a formatted text string.\n\nWhat is the primary advantage of using structured output with defined fields?","o":["The agent can reliably extract specific values without parsing free form text, reducing errors in subsequent operations.","JSON schemas automatically validate that the underlying API returned correct data before the agent processes it.","Structured JSON is processed deterministically by the model, significantly improving accuracy when extracting values.","Structured JSON consumes significantly fewer tokens than natural language, substantially reducing API costs."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to The agent can reliably extract specific values without parsing free form text, reducing errors in subsequent operations. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way."],"e":"The best approach is to The agent can reliably extract specific values without parsing free form text, reducing errors in subsequent operations. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Structured output = reliable VALUE extraction.","k":["structured output","parsing"]},{"id":"ccar-f-147","d":"context-reliability","x":"Performance analysis reveals your context is composed of accumulated RAG results from all previous queries, which is crowding out conversation history and causing coherence degradation after 15+ turns.\n\nWhich approach best addresses this issue?","o":["Implement a sliding window for RAG results from the last 2-3 queries while preserving conversation history","Shift context budget to favor RAG results while reducing conversation history allocation","Implement semantic deduplication to identify and remove redundant information across the accumulated RAG results and conversation turns","Compress all RAG results into a consolidated summary document that updates incrementally after each retrieval"],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Implement a sliding window for RAG results from the last 2-3 queries while preserving conversation history. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Collapsing everything into one path reduces precision and usually makes the system harder to control, not easier."],"e":"The best approach is to Implement a sliding window for RAG results from the last 2-3 queries while preserving conversation history. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"RAG crowding history = WINDOW the RAG results.","k":["sliding window","RAG results","context budget"]},{"id":"ccar-f-148","d":"claude-code","x":"Your team frequently migrates React components to Vue. You've written a step-by-step workflow for Claude Code to follow during each migration, and you want every developer on the team to invoke it by typing /migrate-component. The workflow should stay in sync as the team iterates on it.\n\nWhere should you place the skill file?","o":["As a detailed instruction block in the project's root CLAUDE.md file","In the project's .claude/settings.json using a skillOverrides entry to register and define the workflow","In .claude/skills/migrate-component/SKILL.md at the project root, committed to version control","In ~/.claude/skills/migrate-component/SKILL.md on each developer's machine"],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","The best approach is to In .claude/skills/migrate-component/SKILL.md at the project root, committed to version control. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to In .claude/skills/migrate-component/SKILL.md at the project root, committed to version control. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Team skill = project .claude/skills/ + COMMIT it.","k":["project skills","SKILL.md","slash command"]},{"id":"ccar-f-149","d":"prompt-output","x":"The system processes product reviews using tool use with a defined schema: rating (integer 1-5), pros (string array), cons (string array), and overall_sentiment (enum: positive, negative, mixed). Testing reveals two issues with brief or ambiguous reviews (~20% of the dataset): (1) for reviews like \"Great product!\", Claude fabricates specific pros and cons rather than indicating this information isn't explicitly stated, and (2) for sarcastic reviews like \"Well that was... interesting\", Claude picks sentiment arbitrarily since there's no option for ambiguous cases.\n\nWhat schema modification best addresses both issues?","o":["Allow empty arrays for pros/cons as valid output, and add \"unclear\" to the sentiment enum.","Allow null values for pros/cons, and add \"unclear\" to the sentiment enum.","Add an extraction_confidence field (0.0-1.0) for each value, and filter outputs where any confidence falls below a threshold.","Make pros and cons optional fields, and add \"neutral\" and \"unclear\" to the sentiment enum."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Allow empty arrays for pros/cons as valid output, and add \"unclear\" to the sentiment enum. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure.","That approach is too brittle for a case where the output has to stay consistently aligned with a required structure."],"e":"The best approach is to Allow empty arrays for pros/cons as valid output, and add \"unclear\" to the sentiment enum. This is the most reliable way to keep the output aligned with the required structure and reduce downstream parsing or validation failures.","t":"Let it say NOTHING (empty array) and 'UNCLEAR'.","k":["empty arrays","unclear enum","schema design"]},{"id":"ccar-f-150","d":"context-reliability","x":"Production logs show that when the agent handles complex billing disputes requiring 6+ tool calls, it sometimes exhausts its max_turns limit after gathering data and before completing resolution or escalating. The team's goal is to guarantee that every customer interaction ends with either a completed resolution or a human escalation, regardless of how the agent loop terminates.\n\nWhich approach achieves this guarantee?","o":["Add system prompt instructions telling the agent to call escalate_to_human with a summary of its findings whenever it determines it cannot resolve the dispute.","Implement a pre-tool-use hook that counts tool invocations and terminates the loop with an automatic escalation once the agent reaches 80% of its remaining actions.","Add orchestration-layer code that checks the agent's outcome after each loop termination - if the loop ended without a completed resolution or escalation, programmatically call escalate_to_human with the accumulated conversation context and tool results.","Split the workflow into two sequential agent invocations — a first agent gathers information via get_customer and lookup_order, then the second agent uses that data and handles process_refund or escalate_to_human, each with separate turn budgets."],"a":[2],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing.","The best approach is to Add orchestration-layer code that checks the agent's outcome after each loop termination - if the loop ended without a completed resolution or escalation, programmatically call escalate_to_human with the accumulated conversation context and tool results. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Escalation is too early here because the scenario is asking for a better system behavior, not a handoff as the primary fix."],"e":"The best approach is to Add orchestration-layer code that checks the agent's outcome after each loop termination - if the loop ended without a completed resolution or escalation, programmatically call escalate_to_human with the accumulated conversation context and tool results. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Guarantee = CODE checks outcome after loop ends.","k":["orchestration layer","guaranteed escalation","max_turns"]},{"id":"ccar-f-151","d":"claude-code","x":"A developer uses Claude Code to refactor a function during their development session. Before committing, they ask the same Claude session to review the code for issues. Later, a separate automated CI review catches several bugs that the same-session review missed.\n\nWhat best explains this discrepancy?","o":["Claude retains context about its prior reasoning in the session, making it less likely to question its own decisions","The CI review uses a more specific prompt tailored for catching bugs, while the developer's request was too general","The CI environment has access to the full codebase context while the local session only sees the current file","The extended session length caused the context window to fill with conversation history, leaving less room for thorough analysis"],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Claude retains context about its prior reasoning in the session, making it less likely to question its own decisions. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Claude retains context about its prior reasoning in the session, making it less likely to question its own decisions. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Same session reviews its own work = BIASED. Use fresh eyes.","k":["self-review bias","independent review"]},{"id":"ccar-f-152","d":"tools","x":"You've configured your Claude agent with three MCP servers: one for git operations, one for Jira ticket management, and one for documentation search.\n\nWhen a user asks the agent to \"create a branch for JIRA- 123 and add documentation links to the ticket,\" how does the agent access tools across these servers?","o":["You must specify which MCP server to use for each turn, and the agent can only access one server's tools at a time.","Tools from all configured MCP servers are discovered at connection time and available simultaneously to the agent.","The agent queries each server sequentially to determine which handles each tool, routing calls based on tool name prefixes.","The agent automatically selects the most relevant server based on the request and loads only that server's tools."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Tools from all configured MCP servers are discovered at connection time and available simultaneously to the agent. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","Leaving the choice on auto still keeps the critical decision in the same place that is already failing."],"e":"The best approach is to Tools from all configured MCP servers are discovered at connection time and available simultaneously to the agent. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"All MCP servers' tools = available SIMULTANEOUSLY.","k":["MCP servers","tool discovery"]},{"id":"ccar-f-153","d":"context-reliability","x":"An engineer submits two requests: • Request A: \"Rename the getUserData function to fetchUserProfile everywhere it's used.\" • Request B: \"Improve error handling throughout the data processing module—add try/catch blocks, meaningful error messages, and ensure failures don't silently corrupt data.\"\n\nFor which request does specifying an explicit multi-phase workflow (such as analyze propose implement with review) most improve outcome quality?","o":["Request A, the function rename task","Neither request benefits significantly","Request B, the error handling task","Both requests benefit equally"],"a":[2],"w":["It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action.","Request B is open-ended and judgment-heavy — \"improve error handling\" requires analyzing current behavior, deciding where try/catch belongs, and choosing meaningful messages, so an explicit analyze propose implement-with-review workflow materially raises quality and catches bad design before code changes. Request A is a mechanical, well-defined rename that a simple find-and-replace-style execution handles fine, so the multi-phase structure adds little.","It still leaves the failure too ambiguous, so the agent would have little basis for choosing the right recovery action."],"e":"Request B is open-ended and judgment-heavy — \"improve error handling\" requires analyzing current behavior, deciding where try/catch belongs, and choosing meaningful messages, so an explicit analyze propose implement-with-review workflow materially raises quality and catches bad design before code changes. Request A is a mechanical, well-defined rename that a simple find-and-replace-style execution handles fine, so the multi-phase structure adds little.","t":"Judgment-heavy, open-ended task = MULTI-PHASE workflow.","k":["multi-phase workflow","open-ended tasks"]},{"id":"ccar-f-154","d":"architecture","x":"During initial testing of the automated review pipeline, you notice that reviews on large PRs (50+ changed files) sometimes take over 20 minutes and cost $8-12 per run due to extensive agentic loops — Claude reads files, runs analysis tools, and iterates many times. Your team needs each invocation to abort once it reaches a fixed iteration count and a fixed dollar amount, enforced by Claude Code itself rather than the surrounding job runner.\n\nWhich configuration change directly enforces both of those per-invocation caps?","o":["Add --max-turns 10 --max-budget-usd 2.00 to the claude -p invocation to cap iterations and spend.","Switch the --model flag to a smaller, cheaper model so each iteration uses fewer tokens and lower per- call cost.","Set timeout-minutes: 5 on the GitHub Actions job step and monitor per-run costs via the Anthropic Console usage dashboard.","Set --permission-mode dontAsk to auto-deny any tool permission requests not in the explicitly allowed set."],"a":[0],"w":["The required controls are native per-invocation limits inside Claude Code itself: one that caps the number of agentic turns and one that caps dollar spend for that run. Using those built-in runtime limits is the direct way to stop oversized reviews without depending on external job settings or indirect cost reductions.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","This still leaves the critical decision to model discretion, which is exactly what the scenario says is failing."],"e":"The required controls are native per-invocation limits inside Claude Code itself: one that caps the number of agentic turns and one that caps dollar spend for that run. Using those built-in runtime limits is the direct way to stop oversized reviews without depending on external job settings or indirect cost reductions.","t":"Cap turns + dollars = --max-turns + --max-budget-usd.","k":["--max-turns","--max-budget-usd","CI"]},{"id":"ccar-f-155","d":"prompt-output","x":"Your update_user_profile tool accepts a user_id (required) and an optional fields_to_update object. In testing, Claude frequently omits user_id or passes incorrectly structured data.\n\nWhat is most critical for helping Claude understand what parameter values to provide?","o":["Detailed error responses explaining why invalid parameter values were rejected","Clear parameter descriptions explaining expected format, such as \"user_id : UUID of the user to update (required)\"","Verbose parameter names encoding format hints, such as user_id_string_uuid_format","Strict JSON Schema type constraints marking user_id as required and defining fields_to_update as an object type"],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Clear parameter descriptions explaining expected format, such as \"user_id : UUID of the user to update (required)\". This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way."],"e":"The best approach is to Clear parameter descriptions explaining expected format, such as \"user_id : UUID of the user to update (required)\". This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Parameter DESCRIPTIONS tell Claude what to pass.","k":["parameter descriptions","tool design"]},{"id":"ccar-f-156","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your conversation history includes two types of content: persistent story elements (character backgrounds, plot structure, world rules) that must remain consistent throughout, and extensive brainstorming discussion that's mostly ephemeral. After 40+ turns, you're hitting context limits and users report the assistant \"forgets\" established character traits, breaking narrative consistency.\n\nWhich approach best ensures persistent story elements remain available to the model while reclaiming context space?","o":["Summarize the entire conversation history into a condensed synopsis every 20 turns, replacing the full history to free up tokens.","Separate persistent story elements into a retained \"story bible\" section at context start, applying trimming or summarization only to brainstorming discussion.","Apply a sliding-window approach keeping only the most recent 25 turns, relying on the model to infer earlier context from recent discussion flow.","Store all history in a vector database and retrieve semantically similar passages for each new message, replacing conversation history with retrieved chunks."],"a":[1],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","The best approach is to Separate persistent story elements into a retained \"story bible\" section at context start, applying trimming or summarization only to brainstorming discussion. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","That adds infrastructure or indirection, but the question is really about choosing a simpler coordination pattern that fits the current workflow."],"e":"The best approach is to Separate persistent story elements into a retained \"story bible\" section at context start, applying trimming or summarization only to brainstorming discussion. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Permanent facts in a STORY BIBLE, trim only the chatter.","k":["story bible","persistent context","summarization"]},{"id":"ccar-f-157","d":"context-reliability","x":"Users frequently refine their search criteria mid-conversation. You notice a pattern: when users say things like \"Actually, let's raise the budget to $650K\" or \"I'd prefer a condo now instead of a house,\" the assistant sometimes continues referencing the original preferences in later responses—even though the updates are clearly present in the conversation history. Context usage is only at 35% capacity.\n\nWhich solution most reliably ensures the model uses the current preferences?","o":["Implement conversation pruning to remove turns containing outdated preferences, ensuring only current ones remain in context.","Include few-shot examples showing the assistant correctly acknowledging and applying preference changes in responses.","Maintain a structured state object with current preferences, update it on changes, and include it in each request.","Add system prompt instructions emphasizing that the model should always prioritize the most recently stated preferences over earlier ones."],"a":[2],"w":["It does not preserve the right information in the right form, so it fails to solve the real context-management problem.","Examples may improve behavior at the margin, but they do not address the deeper control or architecture issue behind this failure.","The best approach is to Maintain a structured state object with current preferences, update it on changes, and include it in each request. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide."],"e":"The best approach is to Maintain a structured state object with current preferences, update it on changes, and include it in each request. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Changing prefs = STATE OBJECT sent every request.","k":["state object","preference updates"]},{"id":"ccar-f-158","d":"claude-code","x":"After deploying automated code review, developers report that approximately 35% of flagged findings are false positives falling into consistent patterns: style suggestion contradicting team conventions, security warnings for patterns safe in your deployment context, and performance suggestions that would degrade your specific use case. You want to reduce false positives while maintaining the ability to catch genuine issues.\n\nWhich approach best enables the model to generalize its judgment to novel code patterns it hasn't seen before?","o":["Add instructions to your system prompt to \"be conservative,\" \"only flag definite issues,\" and \"consider that some patterns may be intentional.\"","Create a comprehensive written specification of all patterns that should not be flagged, then include this full documentation in the system prompt.","Include few-shot examples in your prompt showing annotated code snippets that distinguish acceptable patterns from genuine issues in each category.","Implement post-processing that uses keyword matching to filter out findings containing terms like \"convention,\" \"context-dependent,\" or \"trade-off.\""],"a":[2],"w":["Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Documentation may give useful background, but it is not a reliable way to discover every real caller or usage path in code.","The best approach is to Include few-shot examples in your prompt showing annotated code snippets that distinguish acceptable patterns from genuine issues in each category. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"The best approach is to Include few-shot examples in your prompt showing annotated code snippets that distinguish acceptable patterns from genuine issues in each category. This manages context more deliberately so the model keeps the most important information available without depending on brittle long-history recall.","t":"Generalize judgment = annotated FEW-SHOT contrasts.","k":["few-shot","false positives","generalization"]},{"id":"ccar-f-159","d":"claude-code","x":"You are setting up a non-interactive automated code review pipeline using Claude Code. You want Claude to analyze a pulled Git diff (git diff) against the main branch and apply a custom set of code review instructions. However, you notice that when you run the pipeline, Claude only looks at the raw diff text itself and completely stops using its file-reading or code navigation tools. As a result, it fails to inspect the broader codebase repository context, which is critical because the diff modifies a core function called by many other external modules.\n\nWhich change to the CLI invocation will cause Claude to read related files in the repository while still successfully applying your custom review instructions?","o":["Replace --system-prompt with --append-system-prompt so your review instructions are added to Claude Code's default prompt instead of overwriting the built-in guidance for using file-reading and code navigation tools.","Stop piping the diff via stdin and instead embed the diff contents inside the prompt string, so Claude Code treats the invocation as an agentic session rather than a stream-processing one.","Remove --system-prompt entirely and place the review instructions in a CLAUDE.md file at the repo root, since --system-prompt is incompatible with tool use under -p.","Keep --system-prompt and add --allowedTools \"Read, Glob, Grep\" so that the non-interactive mode permits file system tools that it otherwise disables."],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Replace --system-prompt with --append-system-prompt so your review instructions are added to Claude Code's default prompt instead of overwriting the built-in guidance for using file-reading and code navigation tools. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Prompt wording alone is not enough here, because the scenario needs a stronger structural mechanism than instructions by themselves can provide.","Surface-level searching is too narrow here because the task depends on richer structural understanding than simple text matching provides."],"e":"The best approach is to Replace --system-prompt with --append-system-prompt so your review instructions are added to Claude Code's default prompt instead of overwriting the built-in guidance for using file-reading and code navigation tools. This gives the agent clearer signals about the failure so it can choose the right recovery action instead of retrying blindly or escalating too early.","t":"Keep default tool guidance = APPEND, don't replace.","k":["--append-system-prompt","--system-prompt","CLI"]},{"id":"ccar-f-160","d":"claude-code","x":"Your development team is using Claude Code to automate test generation across a large codebase. However, developers are frequently rejecting the generated test suites because Claude creates a high volume of trivial assertions or tests that merely maximize line coverage without validating meaningful behavioral logic or edge cases. You want to guide Claude to generate high-quality, production-ready tests directly without introducing high latency or modifying the core pipeline script.\n\nWhich strategy best ensures that high-quality, meaningful tests are generated in the first place?","o":["Restrict test generation to directories where historical quality metrics show higher acceptance rates, disabling it for areas where generated tests consistently require heavy editing.","Document testing standards in CLAUDE.md including valuable test criteria, available fixtures with intended use cases, and examples distinguishing meaningful behavioral tests from trivial assertions.","Add post-generation coverage analysis that automatically filters out any generated test that doesn't increase line coverage beyond what existing tests provide.","Implement a two-phase generation where a second Claude call scores each test against quality criteria, filtering out low-scoring tests before presenting results to developers."],"a":[1],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to Document testing standards in CLAUDE.md including valuable test criteria, available fixtures with intended use cases, and examples distinguishing meaningful behavioral tests from trivial assertions. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to Document testing standards in CLAUDE.md including valuable test criteria, available fixtures with intended use cases, and examples distinguishing meaningful behavioral tests from trivial assertions. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Quality at source = standards in CLAUDE.md.","k":["CLAUDE.md","testing standards"]},{"id":"ccar-f-161","d":"context-reliability","x":"Your music discovery assistant should consistently maintain an enthusiastic tone, explain its reasoning for each recommendation, and ask clarifying questions to better understand user preferences. You want this behavior to persist reliably across all user interactions.\n\nWhere should you define these behavioral guidelines?","o":["Prepended to each user message before sending to the API","In the first assistant message, instructing Claude to follow these guidelines going forward","In the system prompt","In environmental variables that your application passes to the API client"],"a":[2],"w":["It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on.","The best approach is to In the system prompt. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","It sounds plausible, but it does not solve the specific failure mode or constraint the scenario is focused on."],"e":"The best approach is to In the system prompt. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Persistent behavior = SYSTEM PROMPT.","k":["system prompt","persona"]},{"id":"ccar-f-162","d":"tools","x":"Your team is configuring MCP servers in Claude Code. You want to add a shared venue lookup server that all team members should have access to, and you personally want to add an experimental music playlist server that only you are testing.\n\nWhich configuration approach correctly applies MCP server scopes?","o":["Add venue server to .mcp.json and playlist server to ~/.claude.json","Add both servers to your local ~/.claude.json","Add both servers to the project-level .mcp.json file","Add venue server to ~/.claude.json and playlist server to .mcp.json"],"a":[0],"w":["The best approach is to Add venue server to .mcp.json and playlist server to ~/.claude.json. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way.","This still relies on fragile text-generation behavior rather than enforcing the structure in a more reliable way."],"e":"The best approach is to Add venue server to .mcp.json and playlist server to ~/.claude.json. This most directly addresses the failure or constraint described in the scenario and is more reliable than weaker, more ambiguous alternatives.","t":"Team = .mcp.json (project). Just me = ~/.claude.json.","k":[".mcp.json","~/.claude.json","MCP scopes"]}],"desc":"Decoupled systems, prompt synthesis, tool governance, structured outputs, multi-agent orchestration and context caching."},{"id":"ccar-p","code":"CCAR-P","title":"Claude Certified Architect — Professional","vendor":"Anthropic","desc":"Advanced Claude architecture, multi-agent orchestration, tool governance, evaluation and guardrails.","exam":{"count":63,"minutes":120,"passPercent":72},"domains":{"d1":"Solution Design & Architecture","d2":"Claude Models, Prompting & Context Engineering","d3":"Integration","d4":"Evaluation, Testing & Optimization","d5":"Governance, Safety & Risk Management","d6":"Stakeholder Communication & Lifecycle Management","d7":"Developer Productivity & Operational Enablement","general":"General"},"qs":[{"id":"ccar-p-c1","d":"d1","x":"You are comparing patterns for a batch document-classification job that follows fixed steps: extract metadata, classify, summarize, and persist. Which pattern is the best fit and why?","o":["A workflow pattern, because the steps are well-defined and predictable per-request token cost is preferred over flexibility.","An agentic pattern, because agents tend to be more accurate than workflows, so this is a reasonable default choice for most classification tasks.","An agentic pattern, because the open-ended planning capability of agents produces more consistent structured outputs than a fixed workflow graph on classification tasks.","A workflow pattern, because agentic patterns generally cost more per request than a workflow graph, regardless of the task's structure."],"a":[0],"e":"Because every step is known in advance, a predefined code path gives bounded per-request cost and auditable, retryable stages. The pattern is chosen from the task's structure, not from blanket claims about agents being more accurate or always more expensive.\n\nThe steps are fixed and known in advance, so a workflow (predefined code path) fits: per-request cost is predictable and each stage can be audited and retried. Agents are not inherently more accurate or consistent (B, C). D picks the right pattern for the wrong reason: the choice should follow the task's structure, not a blanket cost rule.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Fixed, dependent steps -> workflow; open-ended steps -> agent.","t":"Fixed, known steps → workflow for predictable cost and auditability","k":["batch document-classification job","follows fixed steps","extract, classify, summarize, persist"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-30","d":"d1","x":"A solutions architect is analyzing stakeholder feedback collected after the first quarter of a Claude-powered procurement automation deployment. The feedback includes four statements: (1) 'Our procurement team is processing 3x more purchase orders per analyst per day.' (2) 'We have eliminated manual data entry role entirely and redeployed those staff to vendor relationship management.' (3) 'The API integration costs are running 40% over projected per-transaction budget.' (4) 'Response latency during end-of-month batch runs is averaging 11 seconds, against our committed 5-second response SLA.' Which of the following correctly identifies the primary business value pillar each stakeholder statement represents?","o":["Statement 1: Efficiency; Statement 2: Transformation; Statement 3: Solution Cost; Statement 4: Performance SLA","Statement 1: Productivity; Statement 2: Efficiency; Statement 3: Solution Cost; Statement 4: Performance SLA","Statement 1: Efficiency; Statement 2: Productivity; Statement 3: Performance SLA; Statement 4: Solution Cost","Statement 1: Transformation; Statement 2: Productivity; Statement 3: Solution Cost; Statement 4: Efficiency"],"a":[0],"e":"Each statement maps to the pillar that names what actually changed: per-person throughput is efficiency, a removed role is an operating-model change, a budget overrun is money, and a missed committed threshold is an SLA.\n\nProcessing 3x more orders per analyst is an efficiency gain (more throughput for the same effort). Eliminating a role and redeploying staff to new work changes the operating model, so it is transformation. Running 40% over the per-transaction budget is solution cost. Missing the committed 5-second latency is a performance SLA issue.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Lead with decision, outcome and trade-offs; tailor depth to each audience.","t":"Throughput → efficiency; role change → transformation; budget → cost; committed latency → SLA","k":["3x more purchase orders per analyst","eliminated manual data entry role","40% over per-transaction budget","committed 5-second response SLA"],"w":["Statement 1 represents Efficiency because the team is processing significantly more purchase orders per analyst, demonstrating improved operational efficiency.\nStatement 2 represents Transformation because the workflow has changed the way work is performed, eliminating a manual role and redeploying employees to a higher-value activity.\nStatement 3 represents Solution Cost because it directly concerns the actual cost of operating the API integration compared with the planned budget.\nStatement 4 represents Performance SLA because the observed 11-second latency is being compared directly against the committed 5-second SLA.","Although Statement 3 and Statement 4 are correctly classified, Statement 2 describes a broader organizational/workflow transformation, not merely an efficiency improvement. The elimination of manual data entry and redeployment of staff represents a change in how the organization operates.","This option reverses the classifications for Statements 3 and 4. The 40% cost overrun is a Solution Cost issue, while the 11-second response time versus the 5-second SLA is a Performance SLA issue.","Statement 1 is primarily about efficiency, since it measures purchase orders processed per analyst. Statement 2 represents transformation, while Statement 4 clearly concerns an SLA rather than general efficiency."]},{"id":"ccar-p-c21","d":"d1","x":"A company is building a research workflow in which a lead agent breaks a market-analysis request into independent sub-questions, assigns them to specialized subagents, and synthesizes the results. Early tests show subagents duplicating each other's work and the lead agent's context filling up with raw search output. Which two design changes should you recommend? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Have the lead agent give each subagent an explicit objective, output format, and boundary of what is out of scope for its sub-question.","Have subagents return condensed findings to the lead agent rather than passing their full raw tool output into its context.","Replace the subagents with a single agent that runs every sub-question sequentially in one shared context.","Allow every subagent to spawn further subagents without limit so that coverage of each sub-question increases.","Give every subagent the full tool set and the complete conversation history of the lead agent by default."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Duplication comes from vague delegation and flooding comes from raw returns. Scoped task briefs prevent overlap at the source, and condensed results keep the raw material isolated in each subagent's own context.\n\nDuplicated work is fixed by giving each subagent a clear objective, output format and scope boundary. A flooded lead-agent context is fixed by having subagents return condensed findings instead of raw tool output. One sequential agent (C) loses parallelism and context isolation, unlimited spawning (D) multiplies cost and duplication, and giving everyone every tool and the full history (E) causes bloat.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Decompose bloated agents by domain before adding routers or more models.\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Duplicate work + flooded lead context → scoped briefs + condensed returns","k":["subagents duplicating each other's work","context filling up with raw search output","independent sub-questions"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c29","d":"d1","x":"A managed agent deployment for claims triage has grown from 6 tools to 34 tools over 18 months as product teams added capabilities. Triage accuracy has declined from 91 percent to 78 percent, and average tool selection latency has increased by 2.3 seconds. A junior engineer has proposed adding a tool router agent in front of the current agent to filter the tool list per request. Which two findings should you present to justify capability decomposition before adding the router? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Tool descriptions overlap across multiple claim categories within the agent's tool set.","The 34 tools serve four distinct claim workflow domains within the triage scope.","The proposed router introduces an additional model call on every incoming request.","Several tools have not been invoked across the most recent 90 days of traffic.","The router pattern is well-documented across publicly available agent literature."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Overlapping tool descriptions and distinct workflow domains show that the single agent's boundary is wrong, an ambiguity a router would inherit. The other findings argue about router cost, pruning or popularity, not about decomposition.\n\nOverlapping tool descriptions across claim categories and four distinct workflow domains inside one agent are direct evidence that the capability should be decomposed (for example one agent per domain) before you add a router. The router's extra model call (C) is a cost argument, unused tools (D) argue for pruning rather than decomposition, and the popularity of the pattern (E) is not evidence.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Decompose bloated agents by domain before adding routers or more models.\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Overlapping tools + distinct domains → decompose before adding router","k":["grown from 6 tools to 34","accuracy declined from 91 to 78 percent","adding a tool router agent","justify capability decomposition"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c33","d":"d1","x":"You are sequencing decomposed components in an invoice-processing pipeline. For each of the decomposed components, select the execution layer it belongs to: 'Pre-Processing', 'Model Stage', or 'Post-Processing'. Which option assigns every item correctly?","o":["Claude-based classification of invoice type → Post-Processing; persistence of validated records to the system of record → Model Stage; schema validation of the extracted fields → Post-Processing; Claude-based extraction of structured invoice fields → Model Stage; PII redaction on extracted text prior to model invocation → Pre-Processing; optical character recognition on scanned invoice images → Pre-Processing","Claude-based classification of invoice type → Model Stage; persistence of validated records to the system of record → Post-Processing; schema validation of the extracted fields → Post-Processing; Claude-based extraction of structured invoice fields → Model Stage; PII redaction on extracted text prior to model invocation → Pre-Processing; optical character recognition on scanned invoice images → Pre-Processing","Claude-based classification of invoice type → Model Stage; persistence of validated records to the system of record → Pre-Processing; schema validation of the extracted fields → Post-Processing; Claude-based extraction of structured invoice fields → Model Stage; PII redaction on extracted text prior to model invocation → Pre-Processing; optical character recognition on scanned invoice images → Post-Processing","Claude-based classification of invoice type → Model Stage; persistence of validated records to the system of record → Post-Processing; schema validation of the extracted fields → Post-Processing; Claude-based extraction of structured invoice fields → Pre-Processing; PII redaction on extracted text prior to model invocation → Pre-Processing; optical character recognition on scanned invoice images → Model Stage"],"a":[1],"e":"Placement follows the model call: whatever prepares input before invocation is pre-processing, the language-and-judgment work is the model stage, and whatever checks or stores the output is post-processing.\n\nPre-Processing happens before the model: OCR on scanned images and PII redaction before invocation. The Model Stage is where Claude works: classifying the invoice type and extracting structured fields. Post-Processing handles the model's output: schema validation of extracted fields and persisting validated records to the system of record.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Before model → pre; language judgment → model; check/store output → post","k":["sequencing decomposed components","prior to model invocation","persistence of validated records"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c34","d":"d1","x":"You are reviewing a peer's end-to-end design for a Claude-based platform expected to scale to thousands of concurrent users. For each statement, indicate yes if the statement reflects a sound architectural practice. Otherwise, select no. Select every statement whose answer is \"Yes\". Select 2","o":["Authentication and authorization run before retrieval so retrieval can filter by identity.","An asynchronous queue absorbs bursty traffic between intake and the model invocation layer.","Tax computation is encoded directly in the system prompt rather than in code.","Conversation logs include unredacted government identifiers to maximize signal for tuning."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Identity must scope data before it can enter the context, and a queue decouples bursty intake from rate-limited model calls. Exact rules belong in testable code, and raw identifiers in logs create privacy exposure with no real benefit.\n\nSound: authenticate and authorise before retrieval so results can be filtered by identity, and use an asynchronous queue to absorb bursty traffic before model invocation. Not sound: deterministic logic such as tax computation belongs in code, not in a system prompt, and logs must never keep unredacted government identifiers.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.","t":"Auth before retrieval, queues for bursts; math in code, redacted logs","k":["thousands of concurrent users","sound architectural practice","bursty traffic"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c41","d":"d1","x":"You are a solution architect evaluating candidate use cases for a Claude-based program. For each scenario, select yes if Claude is appropriate as the primary solution at the architectural level. Otherwise, select no. Select every statement whose answer is \"Yes\". Select 3","o":["drafting first-pass investigative reports from semi-structured incident logs for analyst review","computing real-time fraud scores at sub-50-millisecond latency on a streaming pipeline","long-context contract review with structured clause extraction and deviation flagging","replacing a vector index for semantic retrieval over a multi-million-document corpus","routing inbound support tickets into 30 categories with reasoning for the chosen routing"],"a":[0,2,4],"e":"Claude fits where the input is unstructured language that needs judgment. Hard millisecond budgets and corpus-scale indexing are jobs for specialised components that Claude works alongside, not ones it should replace.\n\nYes: drafting first-pass reports from semi-structured logs for analyst review, long-context contract review with clause extraction, and routing tickets into 30 categories with reasoning all play to Claude's language and reasoning strengths. No: sub-50-millisecond streaming fraud scoring needs a specialised low-latency model, and replacing a vector index over millions of documents is a retrieval-infrastructure job, not an LLM job.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Unstructured language + judgment → Claude; ms scoring or indexing → specialised system","k":["primary solution at the architectural level","sub-50-millisecond latency","replacing a vector index","reasoning for the chosen routing"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c56","d":"d1","x":"A healthcare organization is evaluating two Claude-powered AI architectures for a clinical documentation assistant. Architecture X produces higher output quality scores but costs $0.18 per documentation session and averages 4.2 seconds per response. Architecture Y scores slightly lower on quality metric but costs $0.09 per session and averages 2.1 seconds per response. The stated SLA requires responses under 3 seconds, and the annual volume is projected at 2 million documentation sessions. Which evaluation approach correctly applies business value pillar analysis to this decision?","o":["Eliminate Architecture X on SLA grounds, then evaluate Architecture Y against the efficiency and solution cost pillars by calculating annual cost difference and assessing whether the quality delta materially impacts clinical workflow productivity.","Select Architecture Y based solely on the 50% cost reduction, since solution cost is the most important value pillar in healthcare budget-constrained environments.","Recommend a hybrid approach using Architecture X for complex cases and Architecture Y for routine cases, without additional analysis, since this preserves quality where it matters most.","Select Architecture X because the higher quality scores justify the cost premium, and any SLA gap can be addressed through infrastructure optimization after deployment."],"a":[0],"e":"A committed SLA is a gate, not a weight: an option that breaches it is removed first, and only the survivors are compared on annualised cost and on whether the quality gap matters to the clinical workflow.\n\nArchitecture X averages 4.2 seconds and breaks the 3-second SLA, so it is eliminated first. Architecture Y is then evaluated on solution cost (the annual saving across 2 million sessions) and on whether its slightly lower quality materially affects clinical workflow productivity. Cost-only selection (B), an unanalysed hybrid (C) or ignoring the SLA (D) misapply the value pillars.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"SLA breach → eliminate first; compare survivors on cost and quality","k":["SLA requires responses under 3 seconds","averages 4.2 seconds per response","2 million documentation sessions"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c68","d":"d1","x":"A retailer uses Claude to process inbound freight quotes. Each quote must first have its costs and service terms extracted, then be validated against procurement policy, and only then turned into a carrier recommendation. The process must be predictable and auditable. Which architecture fits best?","o":["A fixed workflow of sequenced LLM calls for extraction, policy validation and recommendation, with checks between steps.","A single prompt that interprets each quote and decides on its own whether further processing is needed.","An autonomous agent that plans its own steps and chooses tools for every batch of quotes.","Parallel LLM calls that extract, validate and recommend at the same time to reduce latency."],"a":[0],"e":"Known, ordered, dependent steps fit prompt chaining: code fixes the order, gates between steps stop bad data early, and every intermediate output can be audited. Autonomy and parallelism add nothing when the steps depend on each other.\n\nThe steps are known, ordered and dependent, so a prompt-chaining workflow gives predictable cost, intermediate outputs to audit and validation gates between stages. A single prompt (B) hides which stage failed, an agent (C) adds autonomy the task does not need, and parallel calls (D) ignore the data dependencies between stages.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Fixed, dependent steps -> workflow; open-ended steps -> agent.","t":"Sequential dependent steps + auditable → prompt chain with gates","k":["first ... then ... only then","predictable and auditable","validated against procurement policy"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c70","d":"d1","x":"A hospital network asks your team to build a Claude assistant that summarizes discharge notes for follow-up care coordinators. The innovation group already has a hackathon prototype and wants to move straight to choosing a model and an agent framework. Before committing to an architecture, which two pieces of information should you capture because they most change the design? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["The model tier used in the hackathon prototype, so the production design starts from a configuration already shown to work.","Whether summaries are needed interactively during calls or can be produced overnight in batch, and the expected daily and peak volume.","A complete list of features stakeholders may want in later phases, so the first release can be designed to support all of them.","The number of prompting techniques the team intends to compare during the first sprint of prototype refinement.","The data classification of the discharge notes and whether each summary must be traceable for audit and compliance review."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Constraints such as latency mode, volume, data sensitivity and audit obligations determine model tier, hosting, redaction and pattern choice, so they must be fixed before design decisions. Prototype settings, wish lists and implementation details constrain nothing.\n\nLatency mode and volume (B) decide model tier, queuing, batching and cost; data sensitivity and audit needs (E) decide redaction, retention, hosting and whether a traceable workflow is required. Discovering either late usually forces a redesign. The prototype's model tier (A) is a candidate to test, not a constraint. Speculative future features (C) inflate scope rather than shape the core design, and the number of prompting techniques (D) is an implementation detail decided after the architecture.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Discovery turns vague asks into constraints, success criteria and failure thresholds.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Before architecture → capture latency, volume, sensitivity and audit constraints","k":["before committing to an architecture","discharge notes","most change the design"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c71","d":"d1","x":"A payments company scores card transactions with a streaming ML model that must decide within 40 milliseconds. Fraud analysts then spend about 25 minutes per flagged case writing an investigation narrative from transaction history, device data and prior notes. A product manager proposes moving fraud scoring to Claude so every decision comes with a natural-language explanation. What should you recommend?","o":["Move scoring to Claude on a lighter model tier with streaming responses, so the 40-millisecond budget is met while explanations are generated.","Keep the ML model for real-time scoring and use Claude to draft investigation narratives for flagged cases, which analysts review and finalize.","Replace both the ML model and the analysts' narratives with a Claude agent that scores, investigates and closes cases from end to end.","Keep the ML model but have Claude re-score every transaction asynchronously, so its explanations can override scores the model gets wrong."],"a":[1],"e":"Claude belongs where the work is unstructured drafting, not where a hard millisecond budget applies. The hybrid keeps a purpose-built scorer on the hot path and puts Claude on the step that actually consumes analyst time.\n\nReal-time scoring within 40 milliseconds is a job for a specialized low-latency model; an LLM call is too slow and variable for it. The real business pain is the 25-minute narrative, which is unstructured drafting where Claude is strong, and analyst review keeps a human on the decision (B). A lighter tier (A) still cannot reliably meet a millisecond budget. An end-to-end agent (C) removes both the fast scorer and human judgment on high-impact outcomes. Re-scoring every transaction (D) adds cost on the wrong problem and lets a slower component override a purpose-built one.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Millisecond scoring → specialised model; narrative drafting → Claude with review","k":["decide within 40 milliseconds","25 minutes per flagged case","moving fraud scoring to Claude"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c72","d":"d1","x":"A customs brokerage uses Claude to read commercial invoices and return the import duty owed for each shipment. The system prompt contains the tariff schedule and instructs the model to calculate duty per line item. Audits find that about 3% of duty amounts are wrong, even when the goods were classified correctly. What is the best architectural change?","o":["Add worked calculation examples for each tariff band to the system prompt and ask the model to show its arithmetic step by step.","Move to the most capable model tier for the calculation step and keep the lighter tier for reading and classifying invoices.","Run the calculation prompt three times per invoice and accept the duty amount that at least two of the three runs agree on.","Have Claude extract and classify line items, compute duty in code from the tariff table, and validate results before persisting."],"a":[3],"e":"Exact, auditable calculations need deterministic code: the model supplies the extracted inputs and code computes the result, which removes the failure class instead of merely lowering how often it happens.\n\nDuty is an exact, repeatable calculation, so it belongs in code that is unit-testable and gives the same answer every time. Claude does the language work (reading invoices and classifying goods), code computes the amounts, and post-processing validation runs before records reach the system of record (D). Worked examples or step-by-step reasoning (A), a stronger model (B) and majority voting (C) may lower the error rate but cannot make a model's arithmetic deterministic or auditable.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Exact calculation in prompt → extract with Claude, compute in code","k":["return the import duty owed","tariff schedule in the system prompt","classified correctly but amounts wrong"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c73","d":"d1","x":"An expense-processing service calls Claude synchronously from its upload API to extract and categorize receipts. During the last three days of each month, uploads rise about tenfold; the service hits rate-limit errors, uploads time out for employees, and some receipts are silently lost. Most month-end receipts only need to be processed before the finance close two days later. Which two changes best address the problem? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Place an asynchronous queue between the upload API and the model layer, with retries and backoff, so uploads are acknowledged immediately.","Move the extraction step to the most capable model tier so each request completes faster and fewer retries are needed at peak.","Raise the client timeout on the upload API so employees wait until the synchronous model call completes during peak periods.","Route non-urgent month-end receipts to batch processing that completes before the finance close, instead of real-time calls.","Skip schema validation of extracted fields during month-end peaks and re-validate the affected records the following week."],"a":[0,3],"e":"Bursty, deadline-tolerant load is solved by decoupling: a queue makes intake durable and lets the model stage drain at its own pace, and batch processing suits work that only has to finish before a later deadline.\n\nA queue (A) decouples intake from the rate-limited model stage: uploads are accepted and stored immediately, nothing is lost, and work is retried with backoff. Because most receipts only need to finish before the close, batch processing (D) absorbs the peak at lower cost. A more capable tier (B) is usually slower and pricier and does not address rate limits. Longer timeouts (C) keep employees waiting and still lose requests. Skipping validation (E) trades a throughput problem for bad data in the system of record.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Bursty traffic + rate limits → async queue; non-urgent work → batch","k":["uploads rise about tenfold","rate-limit errors","receipts are silently lost","before the finance close two days later"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c74","d":"d1","x":"A contract-extraction system has run for nine months. Reviewers correct extracted fields in the review UI before records are saved to the contract repository, but only the final values are stored. Field accuracy has slowly declined as new supplier templates appear, and the team learns about problems only from reviewer complaints. Which change best closes the gap?","o":["Store each reviewer correction with the original output, add it to the eval set, and re-run evals before shipping prompt changes.","Schedule a quarterly prompt rewrite in which the original developers update instructions based on the error types they remember from complaints.","Ask the model to return a confidence score for each field and save fields above a fixed threshold to the repository without reviewer involvement.","Switch extraction to the most capable model tier whenever a document arrives from a supplier template that has not been seen before."],"a":[0],"e":"A production system improves only if its failures flow back as measurable evidence. Reviewer corrections paired with original outputs become eval cases that both guide and verify every later change.\n\nThe architecture lacks a feedback loop. Human corrections are the most valuable signal because they show the right answer; storing them with the original output turns drift into labelled eval cases and lets every change be tested before release (A). Memory-based quarterly rewrites (B) are slow and unmeasured. Self-reported confidence (C) is not a reliable control and removes review exactly where the model is failing. Escalating to a bigger model (D) guesses at the cause and still gives no measurement of whether it helped.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Silent drift + human corrections → feed corrections into eval set","k":["only the final values are stored","accuracy has slowly declined","new supplier templates appear"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c75","d":"d1","x":"A telecom company receives 40,000 support emails a day. About 70% are routine billing or plan questions; the rest are technical faults or contract disputes that need deeper reasoning and different knowledge sources. Today one large prompt on the most capable model handles every email, and both cost and answer quality on routine emails are poor. Which pattern fits best?","o":["An autonomous agent with every knowledge source and tool, which decides for each email how many steps to take and which sources to use.","Parallel voting, in which three model calls answer each email independently and the most common answer is sent to the customer.","Routing, in which a first step classifies each email and sends it to a specialized prompt and model tier for its category.","Prompt chaining, in which every email passes through the billing, technical and dispute prompts in sequence before a reply is drafted."],"a":[2],"e":"When inputs split into distinct categories with different needs, classifying first lets each category get a focused prompt and the lightest adequate model, cutting cost on routine traffic without starving the complex cases.\n\nThe inputs fall into distinct categories that are better handled separately, which is the routing pattern: a classifier sends routine billing questions to a focused prompt on a lighter model and complex cases to stronger, specialised paths (C). An agent (A) adds variable cost and steps to a problem whose structure is known. Voting (B) triples cost on every email without fixing the mismatch between categories. Chaining every email through all three prompts (D) adds cost and latency for steps most emails do not need.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.\n• Fixed, dependent steps -> workflow; open-ended steps -> agent.","t":"Distinct input categories → routing to specialised prompt and tier","k":["70% are routine billing","need deeper reasoning and different knowledge","one large prompt on the most capable model"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c76","d":"d1","x":"Before an architecture review board meeting, a platform team proposes patterns for five Claude workloads. You must flag which proposals are sound so the board can approve them without rework. Which two pairings of workload and pattern are sound? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["A nightly report that always runs the same four dependent steps, assigned to an autonomous agent so it can adapt each night.","An extract, then validate, then summarize pipeline, run as parallel calls because the three stages are separate concerns.","A review of one vendor contract for legal, financial and security issues, run as parallel sectioned calls whose findings are merged.","Marketing copy with no agreed quality criteria, run as an evaluator-optimizer loop until the evaluator is satisfied with it.","A library upgrade whose affected files are only known after analysis, run as orchestrator-workers that plan subtasks at runtime."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Each pattern has a precondition: sectioning needs independent subtasks and orchestrator-workers needs subtasks that only emerge at runtime. The unsound pairings ignore dependencies, add autonomy to fixed steps, or iterate without criteria.\n\nSectioning (C) fits because the legal, financial and security reviews are independent and can run concurrently before merging. Orchestrator-workers (E) fits because the subtasks cannot be known until the codebase is analysed. A fixed four-step nightly report (A) needs a workflow, not autonomy. Stages where validation needs the extraction output (B) are dependent and must be chained, not parallelised. An evaluator-optimizer loop (D) only helps when there are clear evaluation criteria; without them it iterates without converging.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Fixed, dependent steps -> workflow; open-ended steps -> agent.","t":"Independent parts → sectioning; runtime-discovered subtasks → orchestrator-workers","k":["legal, financial and security issues","only known after analysis","same four dependent steps"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c77","d":"d1","x":"A lender's loan-file assistant uses one long prompt that parses bank statements, computes debt-to-income ratios, checks policy thresholds, flags missing documents and drafts an underwriter memo. About 8% of runs produce a wrong memo, and engineers cannot tell which part of the prompt failed or retry just that part. What decomposition should you recommend?","o":["Keep the single prompt but add few-shot examples covering each failure type seen so far, and re-run the whole prompt when a memo looks wrong.","Move parsing, ratios and threshold checks into code, and split gap detection and memo drafting into separate, individually tested calls.","Convert the prompt into an autonomous agent that decides the order of parsing, calculation and drafting for each loan file it receives.","Run the single prompt three times per loan file and send the underwriter whichever memo appears most often across the three runs."],"a":[1],"e":"Separating exact logic into code and the remaining model work into small, individually testable calls turns an opaque failure into one that can be located, measured and retried at the stage where it happens.\n\nDecomposition has two parts: move deterministic stages (parsing, ratio maths, threshold checks) into code, and break the remaining model work into small sub-tasks that can be evaluated, retried and debugged individually (B). That makes the 8% failure traceable to a stage. More examples (A) keep the failure opaque and still require re-running everything. An agent (C) adds variability to steps whose order is already known. Majority voting (D) multiplies cost and hides, rather than locates, the failing step.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Fixed, dependent steps -> workflow; open-ended steps -> agent.","t":"Opaque monolithic prompt → deterministic stages in code + small testable calls","k":["one long prompt","computes debt-to-income ratios","cannot tell which part failed"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c78","d":"d1","x":"A company is designing an employee-services assistant that will cover payroll questions, leave requests and IT access requests, with about 25 planned tools. Leave balances and access approvals follow fixed policy rules. The first draft puts every tool behind one agent with a single long system prompt. Which two decomposition choices should you make before building? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Keep one agent with all 25 tools and write longer tool descriptions so the model can tell similar payroll, leave and access tools apart.","Put a router agent in front of the single agent to filter its 25 tools per request, leaving the agent's prompt and scope unchanged.","Give each domain agent the complete set of 25 tools so that any agent can still finish a request that happens to cross domains.","Compute leave balances and evaluate access-approval rules in code, with Claude extracting the request details and explaining the outcome.","Group the tools by business domain into payroll, leave and IT-access agents, each holding only the tools its own workflow needs."],"a":[3,4],"e":"Fixed rules are exact and testable only in code, and grouping tools by business domain keeps each agent's selection problem small; both choices shrink what the model must decide instead of adding layers to manage it.\n\nDecompose along two lines: deterministic policy logic such as balances and approval rules goes into code that behaves the same way every time (D), and capabilities are grouped by business domain so each agent has a small, focused tool set (E). One agent with every tool (A) keeps overlapping choices in one context; better descriptions help but do not remove the bloat. A router (B) treats the symptom of an agent that should not be one agent. Giving every domain agent all tools (C) recreates the bloat and widens the security surface.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Decompose bloated agents by domain before adding routers or more models.\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Distinct domains + fixed rules → small domain agents; rules in code","k":["payroll, leave and IT access","about 25 planned tools","follow fixed policy rules"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c79","d":"d1","x":"A team built a five-agent system to answer employee questions about a single 20-page travel policy: a lead agent delegates to subagents for flights, hotels, meals, per-diem rules and approvals, then merges their answers. Each question is short, the policy fits easily in one context, and token cost is about five times that of the original single-call prototype with no measured quality gain. What should you recommend?","o":["Keep the design but move all five subagents to the lightest model tier, so the multi-agent structure stays while per-question cost falls.","Add a sixth reviewer subagent that checks the merged answer, so the additional cost buys a measurable improvement in answer quality.","Let every subagent read the lead agent's full conversation history, so that fewer clarification rounds are needed between the agents.","Return to a single augmented call with the policy in a cacheable prefix, since the task has no real parallelism or context-isolation need."],"a":[3],"e":"Multiple agents earn their cost only through parallel breadth or context isolation. When the material fits one context and the extra agents bring no measured gain, the simplest pattern that works wins.\n\nMulti-agent designs multiply token cost and latency and must be justified by real parallel breadth or by material too large for one context. Neither applies here: questions are short, the policy fits in one context, and the extra spend bought no measured quality gain, so a single augmented call with the stable policy cached is the right design (D). A lighter tier (A) cuts unit cost but keeps the unjustified structure. A reviewer agent (B) adds more cost to a design that has not earned it. Sharing full history (C) inflates context and removes the isolation that was the only argument for subagents.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"No parallelism or isolation need → single augmented call, not multi-agent","k":["single 20-page travel policy","fits easily in one context","about five times the cost","no measured quality gain"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c80","d":"d1","x":"Six months after rolling out Claude assistants to a tax advisory firm's associates and its internal engineering group, the CFO asks you to report productivity gains specifically, kept separate from efficiency, transformation, cost and SLA results. Which two stakeholder statements are primarily productivity evidence? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Tax associates now spend their afternoons on client advisory work that they previously had no time to take on.","Each tax associate now completes about 35% more standard returns per week than before the rollout began.","Engineers now spend review time on architecture risks instead of hunting for formatting and naming issues.","The firm closed its manual return-preparation desk and moved those staff into a new advisory service line.","Peak-season response times stayed within the committed 4-second target throughout the filing deadline."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Productivity is about the value of the work people now do, not the speed of the same work. Per-person throughput is efficiency, reshaped roles are transformation, and committed thresholds are SLAs.\n\nProductivity means people produce more valuable output with AI help: associates taking on advisory work (A) and engineers focusing review on architecture risk (C) both describe higher-value work. More standard returns per person (B) is the same work done faster, which is efficiency. Closing a desk and creating a new service line (D) changes roles and the operating model, which is transformation. Meeting a committed response-time target (E) is a performance SLA result.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Lead with decision, outcome and trade-offs; tailor depth to each audience.","t":"Higher-value work → productivity; same work faster → efficiency","k":["productivity gains specifically","client advisory work","architecture risks instead of formatting"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c7","d":"d2","x":"You are selecting a model for a new production workload and must complete the upstream steps before testing candidate models. Which two steps must be completed BEFORE running a representative sample on a candidate model? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Select the lightest model that consistently meets the quality bar across the sample.","Choose a candidate model based on the requirements profile and known capabilities.","Define the quality bar, latency tolerance, and expected volume for the workload.","Sign off the production rollout plan with the platform and security teams.","Retest the model choice when a new model version becomes available."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Model selection is evidence-driven: the requirements profile defines what the sample is scored against, and the candidate is derived from that profile. Choosing the lightest passing model, sign-off and retesting all depend on test results, so they come after.\n\nModel selection starts by defining the requirements profile (quality bar, latency tolerance, volume) and then choosing a candidate model from that profile and known capabilities; only then do you run a representative sample. Picking the lightest model that meets the bar (A) happens after testing, production sign-off (D) comes later, and retesting on new versions (E) is an ongoing lifecycle step.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Selection order → profile, candidate, sample test, lightest passing, retest","k":["BEFORE running a representative sample","upstream steps","new production workload"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-25","d":"d2","x":"A document analysis service processes legal filings averaging 80,000 tokens each. Each filing is queried by attorneys an average of 14 times during the life of a case. The current architecture sends the full filing on every query. The CFO has asked you to reduce per-query costs while preserving response quality. The security officer requires that filing contents not be stored outside the organization's tenancy. Which optimization approach should you recommend?","o":["Move the workload to a smaller Claude model to reduce the per-token cost paid.","Cache the filing as the prompt prefix for reuse across the 14 queries per case.","Summarize each filing once at intake and run all subsequent queries against the summary.","Index filings in a vector store and retrieve only the relevant passages per query."],"a":[1],"e":"The cost driver is the same large document being reprocessed on every query; caching that prefix removes the repetition while the model still reads the full filing, and it creates no new persistent store to govern.\n\nEach 80,000-token filing is re-sent about 14 times, so caching it as the prompt prefix removes most of the repeated input cost and latency while the model still sees the full filing, which preserves quality. A smaller model (A) or a one-time summary (C) risks answer quality. A vector store (D) creates another persistent copy of the filings to govern and can miss passages that a query needs.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Same document queried repeatedly + quality + tenancy → cache it as prefix","k":["80,000 tokens each","queried 14 times","preserving response quality","not stored outside tenancy"],"ns":1,"w":["A smaller model may reduce cost, but it could also affect response quality, especially for complex legal-document analysis. The requirement specifically calls for preserving quality, so reducing model capability is not the most direct optimization.","Caching the repeated filing content as a prompt prefix can reduce the cost associated with repeatedly processing the same large context. Since the same 80,000-token filing is reused across approximately 14 queries, caching is particularly valuable. The implementation can also be designed to keep the filing within Fabrikam's controlled environment and approved data-handling boundaries.","Summarizing the filing once can reduce the amount of context used later, but it may discard important legal details needed for subsequent attorney questions. For legal filings, losing precise language or specific clauses can reduce response quality.","Retrieval can reduce the amount of context sent for each query, but it requires storing and indexing the filing content in a vector store. Given the explicit requirement that filing contents not be stored outside Fabrikam's tenancy, an externally hosted vector store would be unsuitable unless it is explicitly within the required tenancy and security boundary."]},{"id":"ccar-p-c17","d":"d2","x":"You are evaluating model-selection claims used by a peer team. For each statement, select yes if the statement is generally true. Otherwise, select no. Select every statement whose answer is \"Yes\". Select 3","o":["Heavier-reasoning models typically consume more of the rate limit and run more slowly than lighter models on the same input.","A representative-sample evaluation on the target workload is the right basis for confirming a model selection.","The most capable model is always the right choice regardless of the workload profile.","Tiered routing can serve routine traffic with a lighter model while reserving a heavier model for traffic that needs its capability.","Selecting a model based on which version was most recently announced is the right basis for production decisions."],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"Valid selection claims rest on measured trade-offs: heavier tiers cost latency and rate-limit capacity, evaluation on the real workload confirms fit, and routing matches tier to request difficulty. Absolutes and recency are not evidence.\n\nYes: heavier models use more rate limit and run slower; a representative sample on the real workload is the right basis for confirming a choice; tiered routing sends routine traffic to a lighter model and reserves a heavier one for hard cases. No: the most capable model is not always right (cost, latency and volume matter), and 'most recently announced' is not a selection criterion.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"Absolutes or 'newest' → false; measurement and tiered routing → true","k":["generally true","always the right choice","most recently announced"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c23","d":"d2","x":"A contract-analysis assistant sends a 120,000-token agreement and a short user question on each request. The current prompt places the question first, followed by the full agreement as unstructured text, and answers about clauses deep in the document are frequently incomplete. Which prompt-structure change is most appropriate as the first adjustment?","o":["Place the agreement at the top of the prompt wrapped in descriptive XML tags, put the question at the end, and ask the model to quote the relevant clauses before answering.","Repeat the user question three times throughout the prompt so that it stays close to every part of the agreement.","Split the agreement into separate requests per page and concatenate the individual answers without a synthesis step.","Move the agreement into the system prompt as plain text and raise the sampling temperature to encourage broader coverage."],"a":[0],"e":"Long-context recall depends on layout: documents first in labelled tags with the question last lets the model take in the whole agreement before the task, and quoting first forces it to locate deep clauses before answering.\n\nFor long documents, place the document first in clearly tagged blocks, put the question at the end, and ask the model to quote the relevant clauses before answering; this improves recall of content deep in the document. Repeating the question (B) is noise, per-page requests without synthesis (C) break cross-references, and raising temperature (D) reduces reliability.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Long-document misses → docs first in tags, question last, quote first","k":["120,000-token agreement","places the question first","unstructured text","clauses deep in the document"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c27","d":"d2","x":"You are designing the prompt for a ticket-triage classifier with thirty well-defined categories and clear category descriptions in the prompt. Which technique is most appropriate as the starting point?","o":["a zero-shot prompt that specifies the categories with their descriptions and the required output format","a chain-of-thought prompt for a routing decision that does not require multi-step reasoning","a prompt with no category descriptions, expecting the model to infer the category set","a prompt-chaining setup that routes each ticket through a coarse grouping step before selecting the final category"],"a":[0],"e":"When the categories are fully described in the prompt, the model already has the information it needs; extra techniques add tokens, latency and moving parts without addressing any demonstrated gap.\n\nWith thirty well-defined categories and clear descriptions in the prompt, a zero-shot prompt that lists them and fixes the output format is the right starting point. Chain-of-thought (B) adds tokens for a decision that needs no multi-step reasoning, removing descriptions (C) hurts accuracy, and a two-step chain (D) adds latency and complexity before you know you need it.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"Well-defined, described categories → zero-shot plus fixed output format","k":["thirty well-defined categories","clear category descriptions","starting point"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-5","d":"d2","x":"You are designing a content moderation classifier that processes high volumes of user-generated comments under a tight per-message latency budget using well-defined classification labels. Which model selection best aligns with the workload?","o":["Fable","Sonnet","Haiku","Opus"],"a":[2],"e":"High volume with a tight per-message budget makes speed and rate-limit consumption the binding constraints, and well-defined labels need no deep reasoning, so the lightweight tier meets the bar at the lowest cost.\n\nHigh volume, a tight per-message latency budget and well-defined labels describe the profile of the fastest, most cost-efficient tier, Haiku. Sonnet and Opus (and other flagship tiers) add reasoning capacity this task does not need, at higher latency and cost.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"High volume + tight latency + well-defined labels → lightweight tier (Haiku)","k":["high volumes","tight per-message latency budget","well-defined classification labels"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c48","d":"d2","x":"You are classifying token-management tactics by where in the request lifecycle each tactic applies: 'Input Preparation', 'Prompt Construction', or 'Output Handling'. Which option assigns every item correctly?","o":["Persist the validated output for downstream consumption and audit. → Output Handling; Order the prompt sections so cacheable content sits before per-request content. → Prompt Construction; Summarize prior conversation history when full history is no longer needed. → Input Preparation; Validate the model's structured output against the expected schema. → Output Handling; Move stable repeated content into a cacheable prefix at the head of the prompt. → Prompt Construction; Trim retrieved passages to spans relevant to the user's question. → Input Preparation","Persist the validated output for downstream consumption and audit. → Prompt Construction; Order the prompt sections so cacheable content sits before per-request content. → Output Handling; Summarize prior conversation history when full history is no longer needed. → Input Preparation; Validate the model's structured output against the expected schema. → Output Handling; Move stable repeated content into a cacheable prefix at the head of the prompt. → Prompt Construction; Trim retrieved passages to spans relevant to the user's question. → Input Preparation","Persist the validated output for downstream consumption and audit. → Output Handling; Order the prompt sections so cacheable content sits before per-request content. → Input Preparation; Summarize prior conversation history when full history is no longer needed. → Input Preparation; Validate the model's structured output against the expected schema. → Output Handling; Move stable repeated content into a cacheable prefix at the head of the prompt. → Prompt Construction; Trim retrieved passages to spans relevant to the user's question. → Prompt Construction","Persist the validated output for downstream consumption and audit. → Output Handling; Order the prompt sections so cacheable content sits before per-request content. → Prompt Construction; Summarize prior conversation history when full history is no longer needed. → Input Preparation; Validate the model's structured output against the expected schema. → Output Handling; Move stable repeated content into a cacheable prefix at the head of the prompt. → Input Preparation; Trim retrieved passages to spans relevant to the user's question. → Prompt Construction"],"a":[0],"e":"Each tactic belongs to the stage where it operates: shaping raw material before assembly, ordering content during assembly so the prefix is cacheable, and checking and storing results after the response.\n\nInput Preparation shapes what goes in: summarising old history and trimming retrieved passages to relevant spans. Prompt Construction arranges the prompt: putting cacheable content before per-request content and moving stable repeated content into a cacheable prefix. Output Handling deals with the result: validating structured output against the schema and persisting validated output for downstream use and audit.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.","t":"Trim/summarize → input; order/cache → construction; validate/persist → output","k":["where in the request lifecycle","cacheable prefix","expected schema"]},{"id":"ccar-p-46","d":"d2","x":"The compliance team has authored a regulatory disclosure procedure that must be applied identically across customer service, sales, and onboarding workflows. The procedure changes when regulators issue updates, currently four to six times per year. You are designing how the procedure will be packaged for use by Claude across all three workflows. Which two design decisions should you include? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Package the procedure as a Claude Skill owned by the compliance team directly.","Have each workflow team rewrite the procedure for that team's specific context.","Embed the procedure text into each workflow's system prompt at integration time.","Reference the same Claude Skill from all three of the workflow integrations directly.","Store the procedure in a shared retrieval corpus accessed by all three workflows."],"a":[0,3],"e":"One owned, versioned Skill makes each regulatory update a one-place change, and referencing that same Skill from every workflow guarantees identical application; copies drift and retrieval fragments apply the procedure only partially.\n\nA procedure that must be applied identically in three workflows and changes 4-6 times a year belongs in one Claude Skill owned by the compliance team and referenced by all three workflows, so each update happens in one place. Per-team rewrites (B) and copies embedded in each system prompt (C) drift apart, and a retrieval corpus (E) does not guarantee that the full procedure is applied consistently.\n\n**Related rules**\n• One owned, versioned source (Skill, template) referenced by every consumer.\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.","t":"Shared procedure, frequent updates → one owned Skill, referenced by all","k":["applied identically","three workflows","four to six times per year","compliance team has authored"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c60","d":"d2","x":"You are reviewing a peer's draft system prompt that contains contradictory instructions: one section says to never speculate beyond the supplied source text and another says to confidently fill in any gaps. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["Add a priority-ordering instruction that tells the model to evaluate all available instructions and apply the most contextually appropriate one on each request.","Resolve the contradiction by removing or rewording the gap-filling instruction so the prompt consistently constrains the model to source-supported content.","Keep both contradictory instructions in place and rely on the model to select which one to follow on each individual request, accepting inconsistent behavior as a result.","Keep both contradictory instructions in place and add a third instruction telling the model to use its best judgment based on the query's context."],"a":[1],"e":"Conflicting instructions push a design decision onto the model and make behaviour non-deterministic; editing the prompt so it points one way restores predictable, testable, source-grounded output.\n\nContradictory instructions make behaviour unpredictable. Resolve the conflict in the prompt itself by removing or rewording the gap-filling instruction so the model stays within the source text. Asking the model to pick the 'most appropriate' instruction (A), accepting inconsistency (C) or adding a 'use judgment' rule (D) leaves the contradiction in place.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Contradictory instructions → remove or reword one; never 'use judgment'","k":["contradictory instructions","never speculate beyond the source","confidently fill in any gaps"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c65","d":"d2","x":"An application sends the same 8,000-token system prompt and policy document on every request, followed by a short user message that changes each time. Latency and cost both matter. Which optimization addresses both most directly?","o":["Truncate the policy document to its first 1,000 tokens.","Switch to the smallest available model regardless of task fit.","Put the static system prompt and policy before the dynamic content and enable prompt caching.","Move the policy document into a block of few-shot examples."],"a":[2],"e":"The cost and latency come from reprocessing an identical prefix on every call; ordering it first lets the cache reuse it, cutting time to first token and cost without removing any context.\n\nWith stable content first, the repeated prefix can be cached and reused, cutting both time to first token and per-request cost without dropping required context. Truncation (A) loses policy, a blind downsize (B) risks quality, and few-shot examples (D) do not create a reusable cached prefix.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.","t":"Repeated static prefix → prompt caching; dynamic content last","k":["same 8,000-token system prompt","short user message that changes","latency and cost both matter"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c81","d":"d2","x":"A claims-intake assistant writes a free-text reply, and a downstream service parses that text with regular expressions to decide whether to open a case in the claims system. After a prompt update, the integration began failing whenever the model phrased its decision differently, and two malformed decisions were acted on before anyone noticed. Which change most directly fixes the root cause?","o":["Lower the sampling temperature so that the model phrases its decision the same way on every request.","Add more regular-expression patterns to the parser to cover each new phrasing as it appears in the logs.","Add an instruction telling the model to always use the exact wording 'OPEN CASE' or 'NO CASE' in its reply.","Request output in a defined JSON schema and validate it in code before the claims service acts on a decision."],"a":[3],"e":"A downstream consumer needs a contract, not prose: a schema defines the shape and code-side validation enforces it before any action, so failures become rejections instead of wrong executions.\n\nThe root cause is a downstream system depending on unstructured text. Requesting a defined schema states the intent, and validating it in code before acting enforces it, so malformed decisions are rejected or retried instead of executed (D). Lower temperature (A) reduces variation but guarantees nothing and is not a guardrail. More regex patterns (B) chase symptoms and break on the next phrasing. A wording instruction (C) still relies on scraping free text and has no enforcement when the model deviates.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Downstream system consumes output → structured schema + validation in code","k":["parses text with regular expressions","phrased its decision differently","malformed decisions were acted on"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c82","d":"d2","x":"A retail bank's card-support assistant has a system prompt that says only \"be careful with sensitive topics.\" Evaluation shows it sometimes gives investment suggestions and sometimes discusses other customers' accounts, and in a red-team test it followed instructions hidden inside a pasted customer email. Retrieved policy text and the customer message are concatenated with the instructions as plain text. Which two changes should you make to the prompt design? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["List explicit out-of-scope categories, such as investment advice and other customers' accounts, with a fixed handoff response.","Lower the sampling temperature so that responses on sensitive topics become more conservative and repeatable.","Log every response that mentions a sensitive topic so that the compliance team can review them each week.","Add an instruction telling the model to use its best judgment about which topics are too sensitive to answer.","Wrap the policy text and the customer message in separate XML tags and instruct the model to treat them as data."],"a":[0,4],"e":"Explicit categories with fixed behaviour turn a vague boundary into testable rules, and tag-separated content lets the model distinguish trusted instructions from untrusted text.\n\nVague safety wording produces inconsistent behaviour; naming the out-of-scope categories with a fixed handoff response makes refusals consistent and testable (A). Separating instructions from retrieved and user content with tags, and telling the model that content is data, is one layer of defence against injected instructions (E). Temperature (B) and logging (C) are not guardrails: they neither define the boundary nor prevent the behaviour. 'Use best judgment' (D) restates the vague instruction that caused the problem.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Vague safety wording + injection → explicit out-of-scope list + tagged content","k":["be careful with sensitive topics","instructions hidden in pasted email","concatenated as plain text"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c83","d":"d2","x":"A procurement team uses a zero-shot prompt to extract clause types from supplier contracts into a fixed JSON format. Evaluation on 400 labelled contracts shows high accuracy overall, but outputs for multi-party indemnities and clauses amended by later addenda frequently use the wrong field structure. The task description and category definitions are already complete. What is the most appropriate next change?","o":["Add step-by-step reasoning instructions to every request so the model works through each clause before answering.","Add a few diverse, tagged examples that show the correct output for multi-party indemnities and amended clauses.","Move the workload to the most capable model tier so it handles the edge cases without further prompt changes.","Add forty examples of the most common clause type so the model sees the target JSON format reinforced many times."],"a":[1],"e":"Examples teach output shape at the boundaries where descriptions fall short; matching the technique to the observed failure fixes the edge cases without paying reasoning or tier costs on every call.\n\nThe failure is inconsistent format on specific edge cases while the instructions are already complete, which is where a few diverse, clearly marked examples help (B). Chain-of-thought on every request (A) adds tokens and latency for a formatting problem, not a reasoning one. A heavier tier (C) raises cost without addressing the cause and would still need evaluation. Many examples of the common type (D) add tokens and bias the model toward one pattern while leaving the edge cases uncovered.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"Format wrong on specific edge cases → few diverse, tagged examples","k":["wrong field structure","multi-party indemnities","definitions are already complete"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c84","d":"d2","x":"An internal operations agent is connected to 70 tools across HR, finance, facilities and IT. Every tool is used by at least one request type, but a typical request needs only two or three. Tool definitions now account for most of the input tokens on every call, time to first token has grown, and the agent increasingly confuses tools with similar names. Which change best reduces token usage while keeping every capability available?","o":["Keep a small core set of tools loaded and let the agent discover the remaining definitions on demand via tool search.","Move to a model configuration with a larger context window so all 70 tool definitions fit with room to spare.","Shorten every tool description to a single line so that each definition consumes as few tokens as possible.","Copy the tool definitions into the system prompt as plain text so they are cached together with the policies."],"a":[0],"e":"Paying context for tools only when they are needed preserves capability breadth while cutting per-call tokens, and a smaller visible tool set also reduces confusion between similar tools.\n\nEvery definition is sent on every call although few are used. Keeping a core set loaded and deferring the rest behind tool search keeps capability breadth without paying for it up front, and fewer visible tools also improves selection (A). A larger window (B) adds capacity, not efficiency, and leaves cost, latency and confusion unchanged. One-line descriptions (C) save tokens but strip the information the model uses to choose, worsening the similar-name errors. Plain text in the system prompt (D) is not a callable tool definition and still loads everything.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.","t":"Many tools, few used per request → core set + on-demand tool discovery","k":["connected to 70 tools","needs only two or three","most of the input tokens"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c85","d":"d2","x":"An IT helpdesk assistant runs sessions that often exceed 60 turns. Each request resends the full transcript plus the top ten retrieved knowledge-base articles in full. Per-turn cost and latency rise steadily through a session, and late-session answers increasingly reference fixes from earlier, unrelated issues. You must cut tokens without lowering quality on the evaluation set. Which two changes should you make? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Increase retrieval to the top twenty articles so that the answer is more likely to be present somewhere in context.","Summarize older turns while keeping recent turns verbatim and key identifiers, decisions and constraints explicit.","Remove the out-of-scope and output-format sections from the system prompt to free room for conversation history.","Trim retrieved articles to the passages relevant to the current question and drop duplicate or overlapping chunks.","Move the assistant to a configuration with a larger context window so that the full transcript always fits."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Context is a budget: removing stale history and irrelevant passages cuts tokens and also removes the distractors that degraded accuracy, while critical instructions and relevant evidence stay.\n\nSummarizing older turns while keeping recent turns and critical facts explicit removes stale history that both costs tokens and distracts the model (B). Trimming retrieved articles to relevant spans and deduplicating keeps the evidence while dropping noise (D). More articles (A) add noise, cost and latency. Removing critical instructions (C) saves tokens by sacrificing behaviour the product depends on. A larger window (E) lets the noise keep growing and does nothing about the distraction from unrelated earlier issues.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Growing history + full articles → summarize old turns, trim to relevant spans","k":["often exceed 60 turns","top ten articles in full","fixes from earlier, unrelated issues"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c86","d":"d2","x":"A support assistant sends a 6,000-token system prompt containing the tone guide and return policy, followed by the conversation. Prompt caching was enabled a month ago, but monitoring shows almost no cache hits and no change in time to first token. A review of the request builder finds that the current timestamp and the customer's first name are inserted on the first line of the system prompt. What should you do?","o":["Shorten the system prompt below the size at which caching applies, since caching is not producing any savings.","Send the policy as a retrieved document after the user message so that the system prompt itself stays small.","Move the timestamp and customer name to the end of the request so the static prompt forms an identical prefix.","Send a warm-up request every few minutes so that the cached prefix does not expire between customer requests."],"a":[2],"e":"Cache reuse needs an identical prefix; any per-request value near the top invalidates everything after it, so the fix is ordering, not size or keep-alive traffic.\n\nCaching matches an exact prefix, so per-request values on the first line make every prefix unique and nothing is reused. Moving them to the dynamic tail lets the static tone guide and policy be cached (C). Shrinking the prompt (A) gives up the savings instead of fixing the miss. Placing the policy after the user message (B) puts stable content behind dynamic content, which also prevents reuse. Warm-up requests (D) cannot help when no two real prefixes are identical in the first place.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Caching enabled, no hits → per-request values in prefix; move them last","k":["almost no cache hits","timestamp and first name","first line of the system prompt"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c3","d":"d3","x":"You are reviewing instrumentation in a multi-agent system. Which two findings constitute valid observability gaps in the instrumentation? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["Tool-call payloads and outcomes are recorded with redaction applied to known sensitive fields.","Request-scoped correlation identifiers do not propagate across agent and tool calls.","Latency and token usage on every span are emitted to the central metrics pipeline.","Model identity and version on each turn are not recorded with the turn artifacts.","Trace spans for each agent step are exported to the shared distributed-tracing backend."],"a":[1,3],"e":"A gap is a missing control, not one already in place: without a propagated correlation ID the spans of one request cannot be joined, and without per-turn model version a regression cannot be attributed to a model change.\n\nWithout request-scoped correlation IDs you cannot follow one request across agents and tools, and without the model identity and version per turn you cannot attribute a regression to a model change. Options A, C and E describe controls that are already in place (good practice), so they are not gaps.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Which are gaps? → read for negation; missing correlation ID, model version","k":["correlation identifiers do not propagate","model version are not recorded","valid observability gaps"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c12","d":"d3","x":"You are selecting a protocol for a single low-latency stateless tool call from a Claude-based assistant to an internal pricing service that already exposes a stable HTTP API. Which integration mechanism is the most appropriate?","o":["a direct API call to the existing endpoint with the appropriate scoped credentials","a bespoke streaming protocol layered over an unrelated asynchronous message bus","an agent-to-agent handoff that introduces another Claude-based agent in front of the pricing service","a long-lived stateful session protocol maintained between the assistant and the pricing service"],"a":[0],"e":"A single stateless, latency-sensitive call to an endpoint that already exists needs no extra protocol layer; every added hop (agent, session, bus) costs latency and complexity with no benefit.\n\nA single, stateless, latency-sensitive call to a service that already has a stable HTTP API is best served by a direct API call with scoped credentials. A bespoke streaming protocol (B), an extra agent in front of the service (C) or a long-lived stateful session (D) add latency, cost and complexity without any benefit.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Direct API for stateless calls, MCP for portability, agent-to-agent for stateful long tasks.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Single stateless call to stable endpoint → direct API, scoped credentials","k":["single low-latency stateless tool call","already exposes a stable HTTP API"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c14","d":"d3","x":"You are operating an interactive assistant whose dominant performance constraint is per-turn latency. Quality on routine turns is already acceptable. Which configuration adjustment most directly improves latency without disproportionately damaging quality?","o":["Precompute and cache retrieval embeddings offline, keeping retrieval depth and model tier unchanged.","Cache the full corpus in memory ahead of each request.","Switch to a smaller model for every turn in place of prompt caching.","Reduce retrieval depth to the top-k passages that historically cover the answer, and cache stable system-prompt content."],"a":[3],"e":"When latency dominates and quality is already fine, shrink what the model processes per turn with low-risk levers: caching the stable prefix is accuracy-neutral, and sizing top-k from historical answer ranks keeps the evidence.\n\nWhen latency is the dominant constraint and routine quality is already fine, the most direct lever is to send fewer tokens per turn: limit retrieval to the top-k passages that historically contain the answer, and cache the stable system-prompt content. Precomputing embeddings offline (A) is normally already done and barely changes per-turn latency, loading the full corpus (B) does not help, and a smaller model on every turn (C) risks quality and drops caching.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.","t":"Latency dominant, quality OK → right-size top-k + cache stable prefix","k":["dominant constraint is per-turn latency","routine turns already acceptable","without disproportionately damaging quality"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c18","d":"d3","x":"You are evaluating retrieval-strategy claims used by a peer team. For each claim, select yes if the statement is generally accurate. Otherwise, select no. Select every statement whose answer is \"Yes\". Select 3","o":["Dense vector retrieval is well suited to recovering paraphrased intent against unstructured text.","Sparse term-matching retrieval recovers exact terms (such as identifiers and rare tokens) more reliably than dense alone.","Structured query retrieval is the right fit for filter-and-aggregate workloads against a relational store with schema and indexes.","Hybrid retrieval is identical to dense-only retrieval and shares the same recall characteristics.","Random sampling of a data store is a sound retrieval strategy for production Q&A."],"a":[0,1,2],"e":"Each retrieval method wins on its own data shape: meaning for paraphrase, term overlap for identifiers, schema queries for aggregates. Hybrid exists precisely because its recall differs from dense-only, and random sampling has no relevance signal.\n\nYes: dense retrieval handles paraphrased intent in unstructured text; sparse term matching finds exact identifiers and rare tokens more reliably; structured queries fit filter-and-aggregate questions on relational data. No: hybrid retrieval combines dense and sparse and has different (usually better) recall than dense alone, and random sampling is not a retrieval strategy.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.","t":"Paraphrase → dense; exact IDs → sparse; aggregates → SQL; hybrid ≠ dense","k":["paraphrased intent","identifiers and rare tokens","filter-and-aggregate workloads"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c20","d":"d3","x":"You are designing a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline over long technical policy manuals in which answers frequently depend on a section heading and the clauses beneath it. Users report that retrieved passages are often fragments that omit the governing section context. Which chunking strategy most directly addresses this issue?","o":["Split documents into fixed 256-token chunks with no overlap to maximize the number of indexed passages.","Chunk along the document's structural boundaries (sections and clauses) and prepend the section heading path to each chunk before embedding.","Embed each entire manual as a single vector so that no section context is ever lost at retrieval time.","Increase top-k retrieval from 5 to 50 so that neighboring fragments are more likely to be returned together."],"a":[1],"e":"The context is lost at the moment the chunk is cut, so the fix belongs at chunking: structure-aligned chunks that carry their heading path keep their meaning for both the embedding and the model. Retrieval depth cannot restore context that was never in the chunk.\n\nAnswers depend on a section heading and the clauses beneath it, so chunks must follow the document's structure and carry the heading path as context before they are embedded. Fixed-size chunks (A) cut through that structure, one vector per manual (C) destroys retrieval precision, and a huge top-k (D) adds noise and cost without guaranteeing the right context.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.","t":"Fragments missing section context → structural chunks + heading path","k":["depend on a section heading","fragments that omit the governing section context"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c25","d":"d3","x":"You are reviewing a customer-support agent's configuration. Each candidate tool falls into one of four categories: (1) frequently used and reduces hand-offs, (2) occasionally used to complete a defined task, (3) speculative future utility, (4) never used for any of the agent's defined tasks. Which categories should typically remain in the agent configuration?","o":["Category 1 only, because least privilege means the minimal set that completes defined tasks.","All 4 categories, because broader tool access tends to help agent performance, whether or not a tool maps to a defined task.","Categories 1 and 2 only, because they map to defined tasks and the agent's regular hand-offs.","Categories 1, 2, and 3, because retaining occasionally useful tools avoids reconfiguring the agent when new requests arrive."],"a":[2],"e":"Least privilege is measured against defined tasks, not usage frequency: an occasional tool still serves a defined task, while speculative and unused tools only add attack surface and selection noise.\n\nLeast privilege keeps the tools needed for the agent's defined tasks, including ones used only occasionally (categories 1 and 2), and removes speculative and unused tools. Keeping only category 1 (A) breaks defined tasks that run occasionally, while keeping everything (B) or speculative tools (D) causes capability bloat and widens the attack surface.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Keep task-mapped tools, even occasional → remove speculative and unused","k":["occasionally used to complete a defined task","speculative future utility","never used for any defined task"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c37","d":"d3","x":"You are comparing two configurations for an internal compliance Q&A assistant. Configuration X reduces top-k retrieval and serves with a lighter model, lowering p95 latency by 38 percent at a measured 2 percent drop on the accuracy benchmark. Configuration Y keeps top-k and the heavier model, with p95 latency unchanged and no measurable accuracy change. The deployment's stated priorities are accuracy first and latency second. Which configuration is the better recommendation?","o":["Configuration X, because lower latency generally matters more than accuracy, independent of this deployment's stated priorities.","Configuration X, because a 38 percent latency improvement outweighs a 2 percent accuracy reduction as a general rule across compliance deployments.","Configuration Y, because the deployment's stated priority is accuracy first, and Configuration X reduces measured accuracy.","Configuration X, because the 2 percent accuracy reduction falls within normal benchmark variance and is not a meaningful regression here."],"a":[2],"e":"The stated priority ranking is a hard gate: a configuration that measurably worsens the top priority is out, however large its gain on the second priority.\n\nThe deployment's stated priorities are accuracy first and latency second, and Configuration X measurably lowers accuracy, so Configuration Y wins. A and B apply generic rules that ignore the stated priorities, and D dismisses a measured regression without evidence that it is noise.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Accuracy first + candidate lowers measured accuracy → reject candidate","k":["accuracy first and latency second","measured 2 percent drop","no measurable accuracy change"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c39","d":"d3","x":"You are reviewing an integration specification for security gaps. Which two findings constitute valid security gaps in the specification? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["Tool inputs and outputs are encrypted in transit using transport layer security between services.","Service credentials are placed in the prompt context where they can leak into logs and traces.","Tool calls execute server-side under a least-privilege service principal scoped to the requested action.","Role-based access control (RBAC) is enforced only at the response-rendering layer after the model accesses restricted data.","Per-user OAuth tokens are exchanged with scope-restricted permissions and refreshed within the active session."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Both findings let secrets or restricted data reach places they should not: credentials in context leak through logs and traces, and authorization applied after the model has read the data does not protect the model's context at all.\n\nCredentials placed in the prompt context can leak into logs and traces, and enforcing RBAC only when rendering the response means the model has already read restricted data. TLS in transit (A), least-privilege server-side execution (C) and per-user scoped OAuth (E) are good controls, not gaps.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Secrets in prompt or RBAC only at rendering → security gaps","k":["credentials are placed in the prompt context","only at the response-rendering layer","after the model accesses restricted data"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c44","d":"d3","x":"You are compiling factors that should drive the choice between Model Context Protocol (MCP), direct API integration, and agent-to-agent handoff. Which two factors belong on the list? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["whether the integration must be portable across multiple AI clients in the ecosystem","whether the interaction is stateless and latency-sensitive or stateful and longer-running","whether the underlying transport supports encryption in transit between the integrated services","whether the organization has a documented naming convention for internal API endpoints","whether the team has prior implementation experience with any of the candidate protocols"],"a":[0,1],"e":"The mechanism choice hinges on reuse and interaction shape: portability across clients favours a shared protocol, a stateless call favours direct integration, long stateful work favours delegation. Baseline requirements and team habits do not separate the options.\n\nChoosing between MCP, a direct API and agent-to-agent handoff depends on whether the integration must be portable across several AI clients (favours MCP) and on whether the interaction is stateless and latency-sensitive (direct API) or stateful and long-running (agent-to-agent). Encryption (C) is required for every option, and naming conventions (D) or prior team experience (E) do not decide the architecture.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Direct API for stateless calls, MCP for portability, agent-to-agent for stateful long tasks.","t":"Choose mechanism → portability + stateless vs stateful interaction","k":["portable across multiple AI clients","stateless and latency-sensitive","stateful and longer-running"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c45","d":"d3","x":"A platform team operates a self-hosted multi-agent system on Kubernetes that orchestrates seven specialized agents for invoice processing over an internal message bus. Leadership is evaluating a migration to managed agents, and the security team requires that invoice data never leave the organization's network boundary. Which factor should most heavily influence your recommendation?","o":["whether managed agents reduce per-invoice token costs across the existing processing volume","whether managed agents support the current bus topology used by the platform team","whether the current seven agents map cleanly to the patterns supported by managed agents","whether managed agent data handling satisfies the network boundary required by security"],"a":[3],"e":"A security-mandated network boundary is a hard constraint that can veto the migration outright, so it is evaluated before any cost, topology or fit factor.\n\nThe security team's network-boundary requirement is a hard constraint: if managed-agent data handling cannot satisfy it, nothing else matters. Token cost (A), bus-topology support (B) and how cleanly the agents map to managed patterns (C) are secondary considerations to evaluate only after the constraint is met.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.","t":"Hard data-boundary requirement → evaluate first; cost and fit later","k":["security team requires","never leave the organization's network boundary"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c46","d":"d3","x":"You are auditing a procurement-assistant agent whose defined responsibility is to draft purchase requests for review. For each tool currently configured on the agent, select yes if the tool should remain after a least-privilege audit. Otherwise, select no if it should be removed. Select every statement whose answer is \"Yes\". Select 3","o":["a read-only catalog lookup tool that returns vendor and SKU information","an inbox-send tool that emails purchase requests to vendors directly","a draft-write tool that creates purchase-request drafts in the review queue","a production-database admin tool unrelated to procurement","a read-only policy-document tool that returns the relevant procurement policies"],"a":[0,2,4],"e":"A drafting-for-review role needs read access plus a write path into the review queue; a direct-send tool bypasses the human review the role depends on, and unrelated admin access is pure attack surface.\n\nKeep: the read-only catalog lookup, the draft-write tool that puts drafts into the review queue, and the read-only policy-document tool, because they serve the agent's job of drafting purchase requests for review. Remove: the inbox-send tool, which bypasses review by emailing vendors directly, and the unrelated production-database admin tool.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Draft-for-review agent → keep read + draft; remove send and admin","k":["draft purchase requests for review","emails purchase requests to vendors directly","unrelated to procurement"]},{"id":"ccar-p-16","d":"d3","x":"You are a solution architect designing a Claude-based assistant with access to 60 internal tools across multiple business domains. Loading every tool definition on every request increases token usage and time to first response. Which design pattern best addresses this issue without sacrificing capability breadth?","o":["Use a separate model call to condense all 60 tool definitions into a compact index that is prepended to each user turn for tool selection.","Increase the maximum context length so all 60 tool definitions fit within a single request without exceeding the limit.","Apply progressive tool discovery so a curated initial subset is exposed and additional tools are loaded on demand based on the task.","Group the 60 tools into five fixed categories and load one category per request based on a static mapping of task type to category."],"a":[2],"e":"Loading definitions on demand keeps the whole catalog reachable while paying context only for what each task uses; bigger windows, per-turn summaries and static groupings either keep the cost or lose breadth.\n\nProgressive tool discovery exposes a curated initial subset and loads more tools on demand, which cuts tokens and time to first response without losing breadth. Condensing definitions with an extra model call (A) adds latency and still loads everything, a larger context (B) keeps the waste, and fixed static categories (D) break tasks that span domains.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.","t":"Many tools + token and latency cost + keep breadth → progressive discovery","k":["60 internal tools","token usage and time to first response","without sacrificing capability breadth"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-61","d":"d3","x":"A security audit uncovers two issues: (1) all end users share a single API key, and (2) tool calls are executed without logging the initiating user identity. Which two mitigations directly address these specific findings? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Move credentials out of the prompt context and resolve them from a server-side secret store.","Enforce RBAC at the retrieval layer before content enters the model context.","Replace the shared API key with per-user OAuth tokens carrying scope-restricted permissions.","Add actor attribution to tool-call logs so each call records the initiating user identity.","Validate structured outputs against a schema before downstream actions are executed."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Each finding has a direct counterpart: a shared key erases per-user identity and scope, which per-user scoped tokens restore, and missing identity in logs is fixed only by recording the actor on every call.\n\nA shared API key is fixed by per-user OAuth tokens with scope-restricted permissions, and missing user identity in tool-call logs is fixed by adding actor attribution to each call. Moving credentials out of the prompt (A), RBAC at retrieval (B) and schema validation (E) are good practices but do not address these two findings.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Shared key → per-user scoped OAuth; no actor in logs → attribution","k":["share a single API key","without logging the initiating user identity","directly address these specific findings"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c64","d":"d3","x":"A team exposes a customer-support agent that can read tickets, draft replies, issue refunds and delete user accounts. Support staff only ever need to read tickets and draft replies. Applying least-privilege principles, which change best reduces risk?","o":["Add audit logging to the refund and delete tools so misuse can be investigated later.","Remove the refund and delete tools from the agent configuration entirely.","Keep every tool but require a confirmation prompt before refunds and deletions.","Move the agent to a larger model that follows instructions more reliably."],"a":[1],"e":"Removing the capability eliminates the attack surface; logging and confirmations only detect or slow misuse of a capability the role never needed.\n\nLeast privilege removes capabilities the role does not need, which eliminates the attack surface. Logging (A) and confirmations (C) are detective or compensating controls that leave the capability in place, and model size (D) has nothing to do with authorization scope.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Unneeded dangerous tools → remove, not log or confirm","k":["only ever need to read tickets and draft replies","issue refunds and delete user accounts","least-privilege principles"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c87","d":"d3","x":"A research assistant built from an orchestrator and five subagents has doubled its monthly model spend over two months. The service dashboard shows total input and output tokens rising, but the team cannot say which subagent or step is responsible. Before changing any configuration, which instrumentation change most directly enables the team to find the root cause?","o":["Switch every subagent to the fastest, cheapest model tier and compare next month's total bill with this month's.","Add a budget alert that fires when total monthly token spend crosses a fixed threshold set by the finance team.","Emit a tracing span for every agent step with its token usage and latency, joined by a propagated correlation ID.","Log the full raw prompt and response of every request so engineers can manually review the longest conversations."],"a":[2],"e":"Attribution requires measurements at the granularity of the question: per-span token counts tied together by a correlation ID turn 'which step costs more' into a query instead of a guess.\n\nCost attribution needs per-step data: a span per agent step carrying tokens and latency, linked by a correlation ID, shows exactly which subagent or step grew (C). A blanket model downgrade (A) acts before measuring, risks quality and still does not say where the cost came from. A budget alert (B) detects overspend but cannot attribute it. Raw prompt logging (D) creates a sensitive, unredacted data copy and leaves engineers guessing instead of querying structured per-span metrics.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Cost rising, cause unknown → per-span tokens + correlation ID first","k":["doubled its monthly model spend","cannot say which subagent or step","before changing any configuration"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c88","d":"d3","x":"A post-incident review of a patient-scheduling agent finds two problems: engineers could not reconstruct what a failed rescheduling tool call sent and received, nor which user request triggered it, and several existing log lines contain patient phone numbers copied from tool arguments. Which two changes best address these findings? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Log tool-call inputs, outputs and status with known sensitive fields redacted before they reach the log store.","Stop logging tool-call payloads entirely and keep only the success or error status code for each call.","Copy full unredacted prompts and tool payloads to a separate restricted bucket with longer retention.","Run a nightly batch job that scans the log store and deletes any line matching a phone-number pattern.","Propagate a request-scoped correlation ID through the agent, every tool call and downstream services."],"a":[0,4],"e":"Debuggability and privacy are both met at the point of capture: redact sensitive fields as payloads are logged, and carry one ID across every hop so a failure can be joined to its originating request.\n\nRedacted payload logging (A) makes failed tool calls replayable without writing patient identifiers to logs, and a propagated correlation ID (E) links each tool call back to the user request and agent step that triggered it. Dropping payloads (B) removes the very data needed for replay. A separate unredacted bucket (C) creates a second copy of regulated data with weaker controls. A nightly scrubbing job (D) leaves data exposed until it runs and depends on fragile pattern matching instead of redacting at the source.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.","t":"Cannot replay + PII in logs → redacted payloads + correlation ID","k":["could not reconstruct what a failed tool call sent","which user request triggered it","patient phone numbers"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c89","d":"d3","x":"A knowledge assistant over 40,000 engineering documents was performing well until last week, when the platform team switched the embedding model used to encode incoming user queries. Since then, answer relevance has dropped sharply across all document types, while the generation model, prompts and latency are unchanged. What is the most appropriate fix?","o":["Raise retrieval top-k from 5 to 20 so that relevant passages are more likely to appear somewhere in the returned set.","Move generation to the most capable model tier so it can better reason over loosely related passages.","Add a system-prompt instruction telling Claude to answer that it does not know when passages look unrelated.","Re-embed the whole corpus with the new embedding model, then run a retrieval regression set before releasing."],"a":[3],"e":"Embeddings are only comparable within one model's vector space, so a query-side model change silently breaks retrieval until the corpus is re-embedded to match.\n\nQuery vectors from the new embedding model no longer live in the same space as the corpus vectors from the old one, so similarity scores are meaningless; re-embedding the full corpus with the same model and checking a retrieval regression set fixes the root cause (D). A larger top-k (A) returns more mismatched results and adds noise. A stronger generation model (B) cannot repair retrieval that hands it the wrong passages. A refusal instruction (C) hides the symptom while relevance stays broken.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Embedding model changed + relevance collapses → re-embed corpus, regression test","k":["switched the embedding model","used to encode incoming user queries","generation model, prompts and latency are unchanged"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c90","d":"d3","x":"A law firm is designing a RAG pipeline over client contracts and matter files. Partners require that every answer can be traced to the exact clause it relies on, and that lawyers only ever see content from matters they are staffed on. Which two design choices best meet these requirements? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Embed each contract as one whole-document vector so every citation points to a complete, self-contained file.","Assemble context with stable chunk source IDs, require clause citations, and verify cited IDs were retrieved.","Let retrieval search all matters, then hide passages from unstaffed matters in the interface after generation.","Capture matter access lists at ingestion as chunk metadata and filter on them at retrieval, before context assembly.","Increase top-k so that answers draw on more contracts and are less likely to miss a relevant clause."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Traceability and access control are both pipeline properties: citations need stable chunk IDs carried through assembly, and permissions must be enforced where content enters the context, not where it leaves.\n\nStable source IDs with required, verified clause citations make every answer auditable down to the clause (B). Capturing access lists as metadata at ingestion and filtering at retrieval keeps unstaffed matters out of the model context entirely (D). Whole-document vectors (A) destroy clause-level precision and cannot point to the exact clause. Hiding content in the interface (C) happens after the model has already read restricted data. A larger top-k (E) adds noise and does nothing for traceability or access control.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.","t":"Auditable answers + per-user access → cited chunk IDs + ACL filter at retrieval","k":["traced to the exact clause","only matters they are staffed on"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c91","d":"d3","x":"Finance analysts ask an internal assistant questions such as total refunds over a given amount by region for last quarter. Refund records live in a relational warehouse with a well-indexed schema. The current design embeds each refund row into a vector index and asks Claude to add up the retrieved rows, and the totals are frequently wrong. Which redesign best fixes this?","o":["Give Claude a constrained query tool that runs read-only SQL against the warehouse and explains the returned result.","Raise top-k substantially so that more matching refund rows are retrieved before Claude computes each total.","Switch the vector index to hybrid dense and keyword retrieval so that exact region names are matched reliably.","Route these questions to the most capable model tier so that it performs the multi-row arithmetic more accurately."],"a":[0],"e":"Similarity search returns the most similar rows, not all qualifying rows, so aggregates computed from it are wrong by design; exact filters and sums require a structured query against the source.\n\nFilter-and-aggregate questions over relational data belong in the database: a constrained, read-only query tool computes exact totals using the schema and indexes, and Claude explains the result (A). Similarity retrieval returns a handful of similar rows rather than the complete matching set, so a larger top-k (B) still misses rows and bloats context. Hybrid retrieval (C) improves recall for text but still cannot guarantee a complete set for aggregation. A stronger model (D) cannot sum rows it never received.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.","t":"Totals, counts, by region over tables → structured SQL tool","k":["total refunds ... by region","relational warehouse with a well-indexed schema","embeds each refund row"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c92","d":"d3","x":"A SaaS vendor runs one support assistant for many customer tenants over a shared document index. User queries mix paraphrased symptoms such as 'my device will not wake up' with exact error codes such as 'E-4471', and dense-only retrieval often misses the error-code articles. An audit also found one tenant's private runbooks quoted in another tenant's answers. Which two changes best address these issues? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Combine dense and sparse keyword retrieval, fuse the ranked results, and rerank the candidates before assembly.","Keep dense-only retrieval but replace the current embedding model with a larger general-purpose embedding model.","Apply a tenant metadata filter inside retrieval so other tenants' content never competes for the top-k slots.","Scan each generated answer and delete sentences that mention another tenant's name before it is shown to users.","Raise top-k for any query containing an error code so the exact-match article is more likely to be returned."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Mixed query patterns need retrievers matched to each pattern, fused into one ranking; scope must be enforced as a filter during retrieval so out-of-scope content never enters the candidate set.\n\nHybrid retrieval (A) pairs dense matching for paraphrased symptoms with sparse matching for exact codes, and reranking keeps the context small. A tenant metadata filter applied within retrieval (C) stops cross-tenant content from ever reaching the model. A larger embedding model (B) still blurs rare tokens such as error codes. Post-generation scrubbing (D) acts after the model has read another tenant's data and misses content that does not name the tenant. Raising top-k (E) adds noise without fixing the mismatch between query type and retriever.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Paraphrase + exact codes → hybrid; multi-tenant leak → metadata filter in retrieval","k":["exact error codes such as 'E-4471'","dense-only retrieval often misses","one tenant's private runbooks quoted"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c93","d":"d3","x":"An operations assistant has 45 tools across billing, shipping and returns. A recent audit confirmed that every tool maps to a defined task, but definitions consume a large share of each request, time to first token is high, and Claude sometimes picks the wrong one of several similarly named tools. The team lead proposes deleting the 20 least-used tools. What is the best recommendation?","o":["Accept the proposal, since removing the 20 least-used tools reduces both the token load and the selection errors.","Keep the core frequently used tools loaded and defer the rest behind tool search with clear, domain-prefixed descriptions.","Merge similar tools into a few broad multipurpose tools that take a mode parameter selecting the underlying action.","Move to a model with a larger context window so all 45 definitions fit comfortably with room for the conversation."],"a":[1],"e":"When every tool is legitimate, the question is when a definition enters context, not whether the tool exists; on-demand loading keeps breadth while shrinking the per-request load and the set the model chooses from.\n\nThe audit already showed every tool serves a defined task, so this is a context-strategy problem, not capability bloat. Keeping a small core loaded and deferring the rest behind tool search with clear, domain-prefixed descriptions cuts tokens and latency and improves selection while keeping full breadth (B). Deleting legitimate tools (A) breaks defined tasks. Broad mode-switching tools (C) widen each tool's permissions and make selection and validation harder. A bigger window (D) still pays the cost and distraction on every request.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"All tools legitimate + heavy context → defer behind tool search, not delete","k":["every tool maps to a defined task","definitions consume a large share","similarly named tools"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c94","d":"d3","x":"An internal assistant's system prompt has grown to include a 30-page compliance procedure, a 20-page brand style guide and several product reference manuals, yet each is relevant to only a minority of requests. Cost and latency have risen, and Claude occasionally applies compliance steps to unrelated requests. Which two changes best address both the cost and the misapplication? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Summarize all three bodies of content into a two-page digest that stays in the system prompt on every request.","Package the compliance procedure and the style guide as Skills that load their full content only when a task matches.","Keep everything in the system prompt but enable prompt caching so the long static prefix is cheaper to reprocess.","Move to a model with a larger context window so the full content no longer crowds out the user's conversation.","Replace the pasted product manuals with a retrieval tool that fetches only the relevant passages when needed."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Content needed by a minority of requests should enter the context only for those requests; on-demand loading removes both the per-request cost and the distraction that causes misapplication.\n\nSkills (B) expose a short description and load the full procedure or guide only when a task matches, so unrelated requests neither pay for nor get distracted by them. A retrieval tool (E) brings in just-in-time manual passages instead of whole manuals on every request. A two-page digest (A) loses the detail the procedures need and still sits in every prompt. Prompt caching (C) lowers repeated-prefix cost but leaves the distracting content in context, so misapplication continues. A larger window (D) changes neither the cost nor the distraction.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.\n• One owned, versioned source (Skill, template) referenced by every consumer.","t":"Long occasional content in every prompt → Skills + retrieval on demand","k":["relevant to only a minority of requests","cost and latency have risen","applies compliance steps to unrelated requests"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c95","d":"d3","x":"A sales team uses a Claude-based assistant to draft proposal sections from a fixed 6,000-token instruction and template block plus deal notes retrieved per request. Leadership's stated priorities are that draft quality on the evaluation rubric must not drop, and that users should wait less before seeing output. Which change should the architect make first?","o":["Serve every draft from a lighter model tier, since lighter models respond faster and most drafts are routine.","Halve the maximum output length so that each proposal section finishes generating in roughly half the time.","Cache the fixed instruction and template prefix ahead of the deal notes, and stream the draft as it is generated.","Reduce retrieved deal notes from eight passages to two so that each request sends fewer tokens to the model."],"a":[2],"e":"When quality must not drop, exhaust levers that cannot change the output first: caching a stable prefix and streaming reduce wait without touching what the model reads or writes.\n\nWith quality as a hard constraint, start with accuracy-neutral levers: caching the stable 6,000-token prefix (placed before the per-request deal notes) cuts time to first token, and streaming shows output sooner without changing content (C). A lighter model (A), shorter output (B) and fewer retrieved passages (D) can all lower draft quality, so each would need evaluation evidence before adoption and is not the right first move.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Quality fixed, wait time second → cache stable prefix + stream first","k":["fixed 6,000-token instruction and template block","quality ... must not drop","wait less before seeing output"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-10","d":"d4","x":"A research summarization assistant has been deployed for six months. A user has flagged that a generated summary contained a fabricated citation. The product team has asked whether the incident requires architectural action or whether it is an isolated case. Which two Diligence-competency actions should you take? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Lower the model's sampling temperature across the pipeline in response to the incident.","Sample recent summaries to estimate the fabrication frequency across the population.","Review whether citation grounding controls exist anywhere in the current generation pipeline.","Communicate to users that the assistant does not fabricate output as a matter of design.","Disable the assistant immediately for all current users without any prior diagnostic analysis."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Diligence means establishing the scale of a failure and whether a control is missing before changing anything. One report cannot show whether fabrication is systemic, and only a grounding gap would justify an architectural fix.\n\nDiligence means measuring before acting: sample recent summaries to estimate how often fabrication happens, and check whether citation-grounding controls exist in the pipeline. Lowering temperature (A) does not fix missing grounding, claiming the assistant never fabricates (D) is false, and disabling it without any diagnosis (E) is a disproportionate reaction.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.","t":"Single flagged incident → sample frequency + check grounding controls","k":["one fabricated citation","isolated case or architectural","Diligence-competency actions"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-43","d":"d4","x":"A revenue projection assistant has missed its monthly cost target by 38 percent. Profiling shows three contributors: a 6,000-token policy preamble repeated on every call (45 percent of cost), retrieval of historical sales chunks averaging 3,000 tokens per call (30 percent), and inference on a flagship tier model (25 percent). Stakeholders require that projection accuracy remain unchanged. Which two optimizations should you sequence first to reduce cost without affecting accuracy? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Truncate the policy preamble to remove non-essential clauses from the prompt.","Cache common retrieved sales chunks accessed across many of the daily queries.","Reduce the number of historical sales chunks retrieved across each query run.","Enable prompt caching on the static policy preamble across the recurring calls.","Switch the workload to a smaller, faster Claude model tier across all queries."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Caching changes only what you pay for repeated tokens, not what the model sees, so it is the only lever that respects an accuracy-unchanged constraint. It also targets the two largest cost shares.\n\nThe constraint is 'no change to accuracy', so start with optimisations that do not alter what the model sees: prompt caching for the static 6,000-token preamble (the largest cost) and caching sales chunks that are retrieved again and again. Truncating the preamble (A), retrieving fewer chunks (C) or a smaller model (E) can all reduce accuracy.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.","t":"Accuracy must not change → cache static prefix + repeated chunks","k":["6,000-token preamble repeated","accuracy remain unchanged","chunks averaging 3,000 tokens"],"ns":1,"w":["Although removing unnecessary content could reduce token usage, changing the policy preamble may remove information that affects model behavior or compliance. It is therefore less reliable than caching the unchanged content.","Frequently reused historical sales content can benefit from caching. This reduces repeated processing of the same context while preserving the underlying sales information, making it a safer cost optimization than reducing retrieval depth.","Reducing the number of retrieved chunks could lower token usage, but it may remove relevant historical information and therefore affect projection accuracy. Since accuracy must remain unchanged, this is not the safest first optimization.","The policy preamble accounts for 45% of the total cost and is repeated unchanged on every call. Prompt caching directly targets this dominant cost driver while preserving the full policy content, so it can reduce cost without changing the information provided to the model.","A smaller model could reduce inference costs, but it could also change the quality and accuracy of revenue projections. Since stakeholders explicitly require unchanged accuracy, changing the model is not the best first optimization."]},{"id":"ccar-p-40","d":"d4","x":"A Claude architect observes that after a recent model-version upgrade, grounded responses began including claims not supported by the retrieved source documents. Which mitigation is most directly targeted at this failure mode?","o":["scoring each new model version against a stable adversarial evaluation set before promoting it to production","treating retrieved passages as untrusted input and screening them with classifiers before they enter the prompt context","tightening the retrieval configuration so fewer, higher-relevance passages are returned for each incoming user query","constraining responses to source-supported content combined with required citations and a verification step"],"a":[3],"e":"Unsupported claims are a grounding failure, so the fix must constrain generation to the sources and check the result. Regression gates, injection screening and retrieval tuning address other failure modes.\n\nThe failure is claims that the retrieved sources do not support. The direct mitigation is to constrain answers to source-supported content, require citations and add a verification step. An adversarial eval gate (A) helps catch future regressions but does not fix this one, screening for injected content (B) targets a different threat, and fewer passages (C) does not stop unsupported additions.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Claims beyond retrieved sources → source-only + citations + verification","k":["not supported by the sources","grounded responses","most directly targeted"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c30","d":"d4","x":"You are building an evaluation pipeline for a Claude-based deployment and must complete the specification steps before running the deployment against the dataset. Which two steps must be completed BEFORE running the deployment against the evaluation dataset? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Review failure cases with subject matter experts to refine the scoring rubric.","Define the metrics and slices the framework will report across representative, edge, and adversarial cases.","Score the deployment outputs against the reference labels and aggregate the metrics.","Curate and label the evaluation dataset to match the defined slices.","Publish the aggregated metrics to a dashboard and gate releases on threshold checks."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Running and scoring need something defined to score against. The metrics and slices determine what the dataset must contain, so both are specification steps; everything else consumes run results.\n\nBefore running the deployment against the dataset you must define the metrics and slices to report, and curate and label a dataset that matches those slices. Scoring (C), reviewing failures with SMEs (A) and publishing dashboards and release gates (E) all come after the run.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Before eval run → define metrics/slices, curate labeled dataset","k":["BEFORE running the deployment","specification steps","against the evaluation dataset"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-18","d":"d4","x":"You are designing a test strategy for a Claude-based pipeline that handles sensitive financial data. Which two test types should be prioritized to cover both safety-under-attack and cross-component correctness? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["regression tests against a stable reference set of previously known good outputs","adversarial tests using prompt-injection and malformed-input cases","integration tests that verify end-to-end pipeline behavior across all components","unit tests targeting individual prompt-template rendering logic only","smoke tests to verify core paths after each deployment"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Each named risk maps to the test type that exercises it: attack inputs probe behavior under adversarial pressure, and only end-to-end runs verify that components interact correctly.\n\nSafety under attack calls for adversarial tests (prompt injection, malformed input), and cross-component correctness calls for end-to-end integration tests. Regression tests (A) protect known-good behaviour, unit tests on template rendering (D) are too narrow, and smoke tests (E) only check core paths after a deploy.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Attack risk → adversarial tests; cross-component → integration tests","k":["safety under attack","cross-component correctness","sensitive financial data"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-48","d":"d4","x":"You are evaluating an evaluation set used to score a Claude-based hiring-support tool. The set is drawn from one geographic region and one tenure band, while the tool will serve candidates across many regions and tenure bands. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["Continue using the narrow evaluation set drawn from one region and one tenure band because the existing benchmark scores are already high on that subset.","Expand the evaluation set to cover the geographic regions and tenure bands the tool will serve, and rescore the system on the expanded set before broader release.","Replace quantitative evaluation with qualitative impressions from a small pilot group.","Narrow the evaluation set to a single demographic subgroup to simplify score interpretation."],"a":[1],"e":"An evaluation only speaks for the population it samples. A score on one region and tenure band says nothing about the others and can hide unfair behavior, so coverage must match the deployment before release.\n\nAn evaluation set drawn from one region and one tenure band cannot show how a hiring tool behaves for the populations it will serve. Expand it to the regions and tenure bands in scope and rescore before a broader release. High scores on a narrow subset (A), anecdotal impressions (C) or an even narrower set (D) hide fairness and quality risks.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Fairness: agreement with humans and approval parity do not prove fairness.","t":"Eval population ≠ served population → expand coverage, rescore","k":["one region and one tenure band","serve many regions and bands","hiring-support tool"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c50","d":"d4","x":"A Claude-based research assistant begins producing responses that confidently contradict its retrieved source documents despite no change to the retrieval pipeline. Which two diagnostic actions most directly identify the root cause of this behavior? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Switch the retrieval index to a denser embedding model to improve chunk relevance scores.","Isolate whether the failure reproduces on the previous model version to test for model mismatch.","Increase the context window size to allow more retrieved chunks per query.","Inspect system-prompt grounding instructions to determine whether citation constraints remain intact.","Reduce the temperature setting to lower response variance across all query types."],"a":[1,3],"e":"With retrieval unchanged, the remaining variables are the model and the instructions. Reproducing on the previous version isolates model mismatch, and checking the grounding constraints tests for prompt failure, one variable at a time.\n\nWhen answers contradict the retrieved sources and retrieval has not changed, test for model mismatch by checking whether the failure reproduces on the previous model version, and inspect whether the system prompt's grounding and citation constraints are still intact. A new embedding model (A), more chunks (C) or lower temperature (E) do not target the root cause.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Contradicts sources, retrieval unchanged → test prior model + grounding prompt","k":["contradict its retrieved source","no change to retrieval pipeline","identify the root cause"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-35","d":"d4","x":"You are identifying the highest-impact optimization for a deployment whose token cost is dominated by a long, repeated system prompt and a large retrieved context per request. Which optimization most directly targets the dominant cost driver?","o":["Batch requests so that several queries share a single call and the system prompt is transmitted once for the whole group.","Route simpler requests to a smaller model and reserve the larger model for cases that a lightweight classifier flags as complex.","Compress the system prompt by rewriting its instructions more concisely and removing examples that overlap with one another.","Move the long, repeated system prompt into a cacheable prefix and trim retrieved context to the spans relevant to each query."],"a":[3],"e":"The fix must hit the dominant drivers: caching stops paying full price for an identical prefix, and trimming removes tokens that never contribute, both without degrading quality.\n\nThe dominant cost drivers are a long repeated system prompt and large retrieved context, so cache the repeated prompt as a prefix and trim retrieval to the relevant spans. Batching (A) does not remove per-query context, routing (B) targets model price rather than the drivers, and compressing the prompt (C) gives smaller savings than caching and may lose instructions.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.","t":"Repeated prompt + large context → cacheable prefix + trim retrieval","k":["long, repeated system prompt","large retrieved context","dominant cost driver"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-24","d":"d4","x":"You are rolling out monitoring for a Claude-based deployment and must complete the specification steps before instrumenting the deployment to emit metrics and traces. Which two steps must be completed BEFORE instrumenting the deployment? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Document the dashboards, alerts, and runbooks for the on-call rotation that will respond.","Define the metrics and slices the deployment will monitor across normal and adversarial traffic.","Tune the alert thresholds based on observed normal-state distributions to reduce false positives.","Define the service-level objectives (SLOs) and the error budgets the deployment will be held to.","Build the dashboards that surface metrics across slices at the cadence the team operates on."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Instrumentation has to emit something specific: SLOs, error budgets, metrics and slices define what to emit and what counts as healthy. Dashboards, runbooks and tuned thresholds need emitted data first.\n\nBefore instrumenting you must define what to measure and the targets: the metrics and slices across normal and adversarial traffic, and the SLOs and error budgets the deployment is held to. Dashboards (E), runbooks (A) and alert tuning based on observed baselines (C) come after the instrumentation exists.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Before instrumenting → SLOs/error budgets + metrics/slices","k":["BEFORE instrumenting","specification steps","emit metrics and traces"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-64","d":"d4","x":"You are running a controlled experiment to compare two prompts and must complete the design steps before executing the experiment with random assignment. Which two steps must be completed BEFORE executing the experiment? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Decide whether to promote, reject, or iterate the candidate based on the analysis.","Determine the minimum detectable effect size and the sample size needed for power.","Define the hypothesis and the primary success metric for the comparison.","Monitor interim metrics daily during the experiment and stop as soon as one prompt appears ahead.","Document the recommendation, the trade-offs accepted, and the alternatives considered."],"a":[1,2],"e":"An experiment can only answer the question it was designed for. Hypothesis and primary metric fix what success means, and MDE plus power fix how much data proves it, before any user is randomized.\n\nExperiment design comes before execution: define the hypothesis and primary success metric, then determine the minimum detectable effect and the sample size for adequate power. The promotion decision (A) and the write-up (E) come afterwards, and stopping as soon as one prompt looks ahead (D) invalidates the statistics.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Before A/B run → hypothesis + metric, MDE + sample size","k":["design steps","BEFORE executing the experiment","random assignment"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c66","d":"d4","x":"A RAG system suddenly returns confident but incorrect answers right after a document refresh. Latency and the model version are unchanged. Where should you investigate first?","o":["A silent change to the model weights.","The retrieval and indexing step returning stale or irrelevant chunks.","A sampling temperature that is set too low.","A context window that has become smaller."],"a":[1],"e":"The only variable that changed was the document set, so diagnosis starts where that change enters the system: re-indexing and retrieval. Unchanged latency and model version rule out model-side causes.\n\nOnly the documents changed, so retrieval is the prime suspect: a broken re-index or mismatched embeddings feed the model poor context. The model and latency are unchanged, and temperature or context size would not be triggered by a document refresh.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Structure-aware chunking and retrieval matched to data shape and query pattern.","t":"Confident wrong answers after doc refresh → check retrieval/index first","k":["right after a document refresh","model version unchanged","confident but incorrect"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c67","d":"d4","x":"A contract-review assistant will replace a manual first review. The client requires at least 95% accuracy on each of its five contract types, measured before go-live. On 1,000 contracts already reviewed by lawyers, overall accuracy is 96%. What should the team do next?","o":["Go live, because 96% overall accuracy exceeds the 95% requirement.","Measure accuracy separately for each contract type and fix any type below 95% before go-live.","Switch to the most capable model to raise accuracy further, then go live.","Go live and track complaints from the client's lawyers to find weak contract types."],"a":[1],"e":"A per-type requirement can only be proven per type. A 96% aggregate can be carried by frequent types while a rare type sits below 95%.\n\nThe requirement is per contract type, and a 96% aggregate can hide one type well below 95%. Option A relies on the aggregate, C changes the model without checking the per-type requirement, and D measures after go-live although the client requires measurement before it.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Per-slice requirement + aggregate score → measure per slice before go-live","k":["95% on each contract type","overall accuracy is 96%","measured before go-live"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c96","d":"d4","x":"A procurement team is deploying Claude to screen incoming supplier contracts for uncapped indemnity clauses before signature. A missed clause has previously exposed the company to seven-figure liability, while each false flag costs a lawyer about two minutes of review. The team asks which metric should be the primary go-live gate. Which metric should you recommend?","o":["Overall accuracy across all clauses in the reviewed contracts, because it summarizes both kinds of error in one number.","Recall on uncapped indemnity clauses, with precision tracked as a secondary metric so lawyer review load stays manageable.","Precision on flagged indemnity clauses, because every false flag consumes lawyer time that the business wants to reduce.","Mean time to complete each contract screen, because the team will only adopt the assistant if signature cycles stay short."],"a":[1],"e":"When error costs are asymmetric, the primary metric must track the expensive error. Misses here cost orders of magnitude more than false alarms, so recall leads and precision becomes a secondary guardrail.\n\nA missed indemnity clause is the expensive error, so recall on that clause type is the right primary gate; precision still matters, but only as a secondary constraint, because a false flag costs minutes. Overall accuracy (A) is dominated by the many ordinary clauses and can look high while risky clauses are missed. Optimizing precision (C) favors the cheap error over the expensive one. Screening time (D) is an operational metric that says nothing about whether risky clauses are caught.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Discovery turns vague asks into constraints, success criteria and failure thresholds.","t":"Misses costly, false alarms cheap → recall primary, precision secondary","k":["missed clause, seven-figure liability","false flag costs two minutes","primary go-live gate"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c97","d":"d4","x":"An IT service desk agent resolves tickets by calling Claude several times per ticket, with tool lookups and occasional retries. The contract SLA states that 95 percent of chat replies must arrive within six seconds, and finance wants to compare the current setup with a cheaper model tier that may need more retries. Which two metrics should the evaluation report to support both decisions? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["p95 response latency per chat reply, measured against the six-second SLA target.","Average response latency across all chat replies, reported as a weekly trend line.","Cost per API call at list price for each of the model tiers under consideration.","Total monthly token volume across all tickets, compared between the two tiers.","Cost per resolved ticket, including retries and tool round-trips for each tier."],"a":[0,4],"e":"Metrics must match how the requirement is stated: a percentile SLA needs a tail percentile, and agent economics need cost normalized to completed work, because per-call prices hide retries and extra calls.\n\nThe SLA is stated as a percentile, so p95 latency per reply (A) is the matching metric; an average (B) hides the slow tail that users and the SLA care about. For an agent, one ticket needs several calls and possibly retries, so cost per resolved ticket (E) is the honest comparison. Cost per call (C) makes a cheaper tier look better even if it needs more retries, and total monthly tokens (D) is not normalized to completed work and ignores price differences between tiers.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"Percentile SLA → p95; agent cost → cost per completed task","k":["95 percent within six seconds","several calls per ticket","may need more retries"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c98","d":"d4","x":"Every night a support platform re-classifies about 200,000 closed tickets for a weekly trends report that executives read on Monday. The classification prompt and model tier were validated last quarter, and accuracy must not change. The nightly job now costs more than the live chat assistant, and nobody needs its results before morning. Which change should you make first?","o":["Concatenate twenty tickets into each request so the long classification prompt is sent once per group of tickets.","Move the nightly job to the smallest model tier, since closed-ticket classification is low risk for the business.","Truncate each ticket to its first few hundred tokens so that every classification request is shorter and cheaper.","Submit the unchanged requests through the Message Batches API for asynchronous processing at the lower batch price."],"a":[3],"e":"Non-urgent bulk work can trade response time for price without touching the prompt or model, so validated accuracy is preserved; the other levers change the inputs or the reasoning quality.\n\nThe workload is non-urgent and the validated prompt and model must not change, so asynchronous batch processing (D) lowers cost without altering what the model sees. Packing many tickets into one call (A) merges unrelated tasks, complicates parsing and risks cross-contamination, so it changes behavior. A smaller tier (B) and truncation (C) are quality-affecting levers that would need new evals, and the stated constraint is that accuracy must not change.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Accuracy-neutral optimizations first: cache stable prefixes and repeated content.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Non-urgent bulk job + accuracy fixed → asynchronous batch processing","k":["nobody needs results before morning","accuracy must not change","200,000 closed tickets nightly"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c99","d":"d4","x":"A team is A/B testing a revised prompt for a claims-intake assistant, with random assignment by user and a planned sample size that will be reached on day 14. On day 3, the variant leads on the primary metric by six points with a p-value under 0.05, and all guardrail metrics are within limits. The product manager wants to stop the test and promote the variant today. What should you advise?","o":["Continue to the planned sample size and decide then, stopping early only if a guardrail metric is breached.","Stop the test and promote the variant now, because the result is already statistically significant.","Stop the test and start a new one with three more variants to find an even stronger prompt.","Promote the variant now for the user segments where its lead is largest, and keep testing the rest."],"a":[0],"e":"The significance threshold assumes one analysis at the planned sample size. Repeated interim looks inflate false positives, so only a safety signal such as a guardrail breach justifies stopping early.\n\nPeeking and stopping as soon as a variant looks ahead inflates false positives; early leads are often noise even when one interim check shows significance. The decision belongs at the planned sample size, with early stops reserved for guardrail breaches (A). Promoting now (B) is exactly the early-stop error. Restarting with more variants (C) discards valid data and adds no evidence. Promoting by segment (D) chases subgroups chosen after looking at the results, which compounds the problem.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Variant ahead early → run to planned sample; stop only on guardrail breach","k":["planned sample on day 14","day 3 lead of six points","stop and promote today"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c100","d":"d4","x":"In an A/B test for a multilingual support assistant, variant B combined a rewritten system prompt with a higher retrieval top-k. At the planned sample size, B improved the overall resolution rate beyond the minimum detectable effect, but resolution for Spanish-language users fell by five points. The team must decide what to do next. Which two actions are most appropriate? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Promote variant B to all users, because the overall primary metric beat the minimum detectable effect.","Run a follow-up test whose variants differ by a single change, so that the effect can be attributed.","Drop the Spanish-language slice from the analysis, because it is only a small share of the traffic.","Record an iterate decision with the slice regression, and add failing Spanish cases to the reference set.","Pool these results with last quarter's experiment data to raise statistical power before deciding."],"a":[1,3],"e":"A confounded variant cannot show which change helped, and a slice regression blocks promotion. Isolating one variable and recording an iterate decision turns the result into a cleaner next experiment.\n\nTwo changes moved together, so the result cannot be attributed to either; a follow-up that isolates one change (B) fixes that. A gain overall with a regression in one slice is the classic iterate outcome, documented with the trade-off, and the failing cases belong in the reference set for the next round (D). Promoting on the aggregate (A) ships a known regression, dropping the slice (C) hides it, and pooling with older data (E) mixes experiments run under different conditions.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Two changes + slice regression → isolate one change, iterate, document","k":["rewritten prompt with higher top-k","Spanish resolution fell five points","improved overall resolution rate"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c101","d":"d4","x":"Six weeks after launch, a policy Q&A assistant shows no rise in error rates, and p95 latency is within its SLO. However, department heads report that answers have gradually become less accurate since the HR handbook was reorganized. Current monitoring covers only HTTP status codes, latency and token cost. Which monitoring change would most directly detect this kind of problem going forward?","o":["Lower the alert threshold on HTTP 5xx error rates so that smaller increases page the on-call engineer sooner.","Log the complete prompt and response for every request indefinitely so engineers can investigate any complaint.","Score sampled production outputs on a schedule with the offline eval checks and rubrics, sliced by index version.","Add a thumbs-down button and alert the team whenever negative feedback exceeds a fixed count on a given day."],"a":[2],"e":"Quality degradation is silent at the transport layer; only scheduled scoring of real outputs against the offline rubric measures it, and slicing by index version links the drift to its cause.\n\nQuality drift produces no error code, so it can only be seen by scoring real outputs. Sampling production traffic and grading it with the same code checks and judge rubrics as offline evaluation, sliced by index version, ties the decline to the handbook change (C). Tighter 5xx alerts (A) watch the wrong signal. Logging everything indefinitely (B) creates privacy and retention problems and still scores nothing. Thumbs-down counts (D) are sparse, biased and not tied to a baseline.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Silent quality drift → scheduled scoring of sampled outputs, sliced by version","k":["no rise in error rates","gradually become less accurate","HR handbook was reorganized"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c102","d":"d4","x":"Overnight, cost per task for a document-analysis assistant rose 40 percent and time-to-first-token roughly doubled. The engineering team says no code was deployed, but the on-call engineer cannot tell what changed, because traces record only request IDs, total tokens and total latency. Which two additions to the per-request trace would most directly let the team diagnose this kind of change? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["The full raw prompt text, including customer content, retained without any fixed retention limit.","A daily cost total imported from the provider invoice and attached to each of the request records.","The model identifier, prompt version and retrieval index version that served each request.","A static alert on every metric emitted by the service, using thresholds that were set before launch.","Input tokens split into cached and uncached counts, recorded alongside time-to-first-token."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Diagnosis needs the variables that can change without a code deploy recorded per request. Version IDs locate the change, and cache-split token counts expose a broken prefix cache, a common cause of a combined cost and TTFT jump.\n\nA jump in cost and time-to-first-token with no code deploy often comes from a configuration change, such as a new prompt version that breaks the cache, or a model or index change. Version identifiers on each trace (C) let the team correlate the jump with what changed, and cached versus uncached input tokens (E) show directly whether the cache hit rate collapsed. Unbounded raw prompts (A) create privacy exposure, a daily invoice total (B) has no per-request detail, and static alerts on every metric (D) add noise without diagnosis.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.\n• Stable content first, dynamic content last, so the prefix can be cached.","t":"Unexplained cost/TTFT jump → per-request versions + cached-token split","k":["cost per task rose 40 percent","time-to-first-token roughly doubled","no code was deployed"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c4","d":"d5","x":"You are defining transparency practices for a customer-facing assistant whose responses are materially shaped by AI. Which transparency practice most directly supports responsible deployment?","o":["Disclose AI involvement to end users in line with the organization's transparency policy and provide a documented path to reach a human when needed.","Disclose AI involvement only to internal staff and operators while withholding that information from the end users whose interactions are materially shaped by the AI system.","Disclose AI involvement in the terms of service at signup and rely on that single notice to cover all subsequent interactions.","Refuse to answer any user question about how the responses were produced or whether AI was involved, treating the AI's role as confidential operational information."],"a":[0],"e":"Transparency only works when it reaches the affected person at the point of interaction and comes with recourse to a human. Buried, internal-only or refused disclosure informs no one who is affected.\n\nResponsible deployment means telling the people whose interactions are shaped by AI, in line with policy, and giving them a documented route to a human. Hiding it from end users (B), relying on a single sign-up notice (C) or refusing to explain (D) all undermine transparency.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"AI shapes user responses → disclose to end users + path to human","k":["customer-facing assistant","materially shaped by AI","responsible deployment"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-11","d":"d5","x":"A customer support team has proposed delegating customer refund decisions to a Claude-driven workflow with no human review for less expensive refunds. The team's reasoning is that small refunds are low-risk and human review would erase the efficiency gain. Which Delegation-competency principle should guide your response?","o":["A delegation scope should include human review on every decision the workflow produces, regardless of transaction size.","A delegation scope should be set primarily by maximizing efficiency gains across the workflow.","Delegation should be avoided in most cases wherever financial transactions occur in the workflow.","Delegation scope should reflect the type of risk involved, not the transaction size alone."],"a":[3],"e":"Even small refunds can involve fraud, policy exceptions, or cumulative financial loss. The type and consequences of the risk determine how much autonomy Claude should have and when a person should review a decision.\n\nRoutine refunds that meet clear policy criteria can be delegated within defined limits. Suspicious patterns, policy exceptions, or refunds that exceed cumulative limits can be escalated for human review. A applies mandatory review to every decision. B prioritizes efficiency before assessing risk. C rules out most financial delegation even where safeguards are appropriate.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Match human oversight to the risk and consequences of the decision.\n• Consider cumulative exposure when setting limits for repeated transactions.","t":"Set delegation and review thresholds using the risks of the decision.","k":["small refunds assumed to be low-risk, removal of human review, Delegation competency"]},{"id":"ccar-p-37","d":"d5","x":"A loan pre-qualification assistant shows 94 percent approval recommendations that match the human underwriter decision. The fairness team reviewed approval rate parity across protected groups and reported no significant difference. A board member has asked whether this evidence is sufficient to declare the assistant fair. Which two Discernment-competency findings should you report? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["Match with human underwriters does not establish freedom from underwriter-introduced bias.","A larger sample is needed before any meaningful fairness claim can be made about the model.","Approval rate parity does not by itself assess error rate parity across protected groups.","The fairness team's review process likely missed at least some of the protected groups studied.","The 94 percent match rate is sufficient evidence of fairness for the assistant's decisions."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Agreement with humans measures consistency with past practice, which can encode bias, and equal approval rates can coexist with unequal false rejections. Each piece of evidence leaves a different gap.\n\nMatching human underwriters 94% of the time can simply reproduce the underwriters' own bias, so it does not prove fairness. And equal approval rates across groups say nothing about whether error rates (wrong approvals or wrong denials) are equal. Options B and D are speculation without evidence, and E repeats the flawed claim.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Fairness: agreement with humans and approval parity do not prove fairness.","t":"Match rate + approval parity ≠ fairness → inherited bias, error-rate parity","k":["match the human underwriter decision","approval rate parity","sufficient to declare fair"],"ns":1,"w":["A 94% match with human decisions only shows that the assistant agrees with the existing decision process. If human underwriters have existing biases, the model could reproduce those biases rather than eliminate them.","Sample size can affect statistical confidence, but the key issue here is that approval-rate parity and agreement with humans are insufficient fairness measures even when the sample is large.","Equal or similar approval rates do not necessarily mean the system performs equally well across groups. A proper fairness assessment should also consider error patterns, such as false positives and false negatives, across protected groups.","There is no evidence that the fairness team omitted protected groups. This conclusion cannot be drawn from the information provided.","Agreement with human decisions measures consistency, not fairness. High agreement alone cannot establish that the decisions are unbiased or equitable."]},{"id":"ccar-p-c22","d":"d5","x":"A European retailer plans to use Claude to analyze customer support transcripts that contain the names, email addresses, and order histories of EU residents. The data protection officer asks what must be in place before processing begins. Which approach best aligns with GDPR obligations?","o":["Proceed with processing because the transcripts were collected through the retailer's own support channel.","Establish a lawful basis and documented purpose, minimize and pseudonymize personal data before it reaches the model, and confirm the data processing agreement and retention settings with the provider.","Remove only the email addresses and send the remaining transcript content unchanged to the model.","Instruct the model in the system prompt not to remember any personal data contained in the transcripts."],"a":[1],"e":"GDPR obligations are met by a legal basis, minimization before data leaves your control, and contract and retention controls with the processor. Owning the channel or instructing the model creates none of these.\n\nGDPR requires a lawful basis and a documented purpose, data minimisation (for example pseudonymising before the data reaches the model), and a data processing agreement and retention settings with the provider. Owning the channel (A) is not a lawful basis, removing only emails (C) leaves names and order histories, and a prompt instruction (D) is not a data-protection control.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"EU personal data to model → lawful basis, minimize/pseudonymize, DPA + retention","k":["names, emails, order histories","EU residents","before processing begins"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-44","d":"d5","x":"You are building an ethics review checklist for deployments supported by artificial intelligence (AI). Which two checks belong on the list? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["Restrict the ethics review to outputs that exceed a defined model-confidence threshold.","Confirm that vendor licensing terms permit the planned production use of the model.","Verify that outputs do not rely on generalizations about people that the underlying data does not support.","Confirm that high-impact decisions retain human accountability rather than being attributed to the model.","Confirm that latency and throughput remain consistent across regions and user populations."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Ethics review targets harm to people: unsupported generalizations about groups and high-impact decisions without an accountable human. Licensing, latency and confidence gating are legal, operational or backward checks.\n\nAn ethics checklist should verify that outputs do not rely on generalisations about people that the data does not support, and that high-impact decisions keep human accountability. A confidence threshold (A) leaves low-confidence harms unchecked, licensing (B) is a legal/procurement check, and latency consistency (E) is an operational concern.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Fairness: agreement with humans and approval parity do not prove fairness.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Ethics checklist → no unsupported generalizations + human accountability","k":["ethics review checklist","checks belong on the list"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-1","d":"d5","x":"You are compiling guardrail tactics for a customer-facing assistant. Which two tactics belong on the guardrail list? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Embed approved override phrases that let trusted users relax guardrails on demand.","Validate the model output against a structured schema before downstream actions are taken.","Lower sampling temperature globally to reduce the chance of off-policy completions.","Layer prompt-level guardrails with runtime content checks rather than relying on either alone.","Rely on a single hardened system prompt that enumerates every disallowed behavior."],"a":[1,3],"e":"A guardrail must sit in the request or response path and be able to block. Layering covers each control's blind spots, and schema validation stops malformed actions before they execute; override phrases, temperature and a single prompt can all be bypassed.\n\nValidating output against a structured schema before downstream actions, and layering prompt-level guardrails with runtime content checks, are real guardrails. Override phrases (A) create a bypass, temperature (C) is not a safety control, and relying on a single 'hardened' system prompt (E) is a single point of failure.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Guardrail list → layered prompt + runtime checks, schema before actions","k":["compiling guardrail tactics","customer-facing assistant"],"ns":1,"w":["Allowing users to bypass guardrails through special phrases can create a security and policy risk. Guardrails should not be easily disabled through conversational instructions, even for trusted users.","Structured output validation helps ensure that the model response conforms to the expected format and required fields before it is passed to downstream systems. This reduces the risk of malformed or unexpected model output triggering unintended actions.","Lowering temperature can make outputs more consistent and predictable, but it is not a reliable substitute for explicit safety controls. Model randomness settings alone cannot enforce policy boundaries.","Using multiple guardrail layers provides defense in depth. Prompt-level instructions establish behavioral boundaries, while runtime checks can detect or block problematic content before it reaches users or downstream systems.","A system prompt is an important layer, but relying on one control alone is not sufficient. Complex customer-facing systems benefit from multiple independent safeguards."]},{"id":"ccar-p-c54","d":"d5","x":"You are defining where human review must remain in a planned automated pipeline. Which placement reflects sound human-in-the-loop design?","o":["Place a human reviewer between each incoming request and the model so inputs are checked before generation.","Place a human reviewer between the model's output and every action, regardless of impact or reversibility.","Place a human reviewer between the model's output and any action a confidence score marks as uncertain.","Place a human reviewer between the model's output and any high-impact, irreversible action."],"a":[3],"e":"Review effort belongs where an error is costly or cannot be undone. Input review checks nothing the model decided, universal review breeds rubber-stamping, and confidence scores miss confidently wrong outputs.\n\nHuman review belongs between model output and actions that are high-impact or irreversible. Reviewing every input (A) or every action (B) destroys the value of automation without adding proportional safety, and a confidence score alone (C) misses confident but wrong high-impact outputs.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Where human review sits → between output and high-impact, irreversible actions","k":["where human review must remain","automated pipeline","sound human-in-the-loop design"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c55","d":"d5","x":"You are preparing a HIPAA-eligible deployment for a healthcare customer. Which configuration supports HIPAA compliance using Anthropic-offered tools?","o":["Claude Team with a signed Business Associate Agreement but Zero Data Retention left disabled and audit logging not yet configured for the deployment.","Claude Pro with no signed Business Associate Agreement, since a paid consumer subscription is assumed to meet HIPAA requirements out of the box.","an ad-hoc personal Claude account used by individual clinicians for PHI-related tasks, with no Business Associate Agreement, no Zero Data Retention, and no audit logging configured","Claude Enterprise with a signed Business Associate Agreement, Zero Data Retention enabled, and audit logging configured for compliance tracking"],"a":[3],"e":"HIPAA compliance comes from contract and configuration together: a BAA on an offering it covers, minimal retention for PHI and an audit trail. Missing any one element leaves the deployment incomplete.\n\nOnly option D combines everything a HIPAA-eligible deployment needs: a signed Business Associate Agreement on an enterprise offering, a data-retention configuration suited to PHI (Zero Data Retention or the provider's HIPAA-ready setup) and audit logging. Leaving retention and audit logging unconfigured (A), using a consumer plan without a BAA (B) or using personal accounts for PHI (C) do not meet the requirements. In practice, always confirm in the provider's current documentation and your BAA which products and features are covered.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.","t":"PHI → enterprise offering + BAA + ZDR + audit logging","k":["HIPAA-eligible deployment","healthcare customer","Anthropic-offered tools"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-2","d":"d5","x":"A Claude architect is implementing safety controls for a customer-facing advice assistant that must never provide regulated investment recommendations. Which two guardrail implementations most directly enforce this constraint? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Increase response temperature to introduce variability that reduces the likelihood of specific recommendations.","Log all model responses to a SIEM and route flagged records to a compliance reviewer.","Add an output classifier that detects and blocks responses containing regulated investment-recommendation language.","Define explicit out-of-scope categories in the system prompt with fixed refusal phrasing for investment advice requests.","Limit session length to reduce the volume of queries processed per user per day."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Enforcement needs controls that can say no in the response path: scoped refusals instruct, and an output classifier blocks what slips through. Logging detects after harm, and temperature or session limits change nothing about content.\n\nEnforce the constraint directly: an output classifier that detects and blocks regulated investment-recommendation language, plus explicit out-of-scope categories with fixed refusal phrasing in the system prompt. Higher temperature (A) increases unpredictability, logging to a SIEM (B) only detects after the fact, and shorter sessions (E) do not prevent the advice.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Must never give X → out-of-scope refusal + output classifier","k":["must never provide","regulated investment recommendations","most directly enforce"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c103","d":"d5","x":"An email-triage agent reads inbound customer emails and has tools to update CRM records, draft replies and forward messages to any external address. During red-teaming, an email containing hidden text that told the agent to forward the latest account export to an outside address caused the agent to attempt the forward. Which response best addresses this risk?","o":["Add a system-prompt rule telling the agent to ignore any instructions that appear inside customer emails.","Log every forwarding action to the SIEM and have the security team review flagged events each week.","Let the agent follow embedded instructions only when the sender's domain is on an approved list.","Remove external forwarding from the tools and screen email content as untrusted data before the agent acts."],"a":[3],"e":"An injected instruction is only dangerous if the agent holds a tool that can act on it. Removing that tool eliminates the impact, and screening untrusted content adds a preventive layer instead of an after-the-fact one.\n\nThis is indirect prompt injection: attacker text inside content the agent reads. The strongest response removes the capability the attack needs and treats email content as data that is screened before the agent acts (D). A prompt rule alone (A) is a single layer that can be talked around. Weekly SIEM review (B) detects the leak after it has happened. An approved-sender list (C) is a bypass, because trusted senders can be spoofed or compromised.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Injection via content the agent reads → remove risky tool, treat content as untrusted","k":["hidden text in an email","forward to any external address","attempted the forward"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c104","d":"d5","x":"An insurance claims assistant drafts settlement summaries that adjusters approve before payment. After four months, adjusters accept 97 percent of drafts unchanged, and the operations director proposes removing the second-level audit of approved claims to save cost. No one has compared the accepted drafts against verified claim outcomes. Which concern should you raise first?","o":["The acceptance rate is too high to be credible, so the assistant should be replaced with a more capable model.","Acceptance shows what adjusters kept, not what was correct, and a usually-right system breeds over-reliance.","The audit can be removed safely, because adjusters remain accountable for every claim that they approve.","The assistant should run at a lower temperature first, so that its drafts become more consistent overall."],"a":[1],"e":"Acceptance is a behavior metric, not a quality metric. When a system is usually right, reviewers stop scrutinizing, so removing a check must be justified by correctness measured against ground truth.\n\nA high acceptance rate measures what reviewers kept, not whether the drafts were right, and reviewers who usually see good drafts stop checking closely: classic over-reliance. Correctness must be measured on sampled claims against verified outcomes before any review layer is removed (B). Calling the rate not credible and swapping models (A) is unsupported by the scenario. Nominal accountability (C) does not help if reviewers are rubber-stamping. Lower temperature (D) changes variability, not correctness.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"High acceptance ≠ correctness → measure against outcomes before cutting review","k":["accept 97 percent unchanged","removing the second-level audit","not compared against verified outcomes"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c105","d":"d5","x":"A finance team uses Claude to extract line items from supplier invoices and compute VAT totals for posting to the ledger. Auditors found that totals for the same invoice occasionally differ by a few cents between runs, even with identical input. The controller requires totals to be exact and reproducible. What is the most appropriate design change?","o":["Set the temperature to zero for all invoice calls so that each run returns the same totals every time.","Run each invoice three times and post the total that appears most often across the separate runs.","Have the model extract line items into a validated schema and compute the totals in deterministic code.","Move the workload to the most capable model tier, since stronger reasoning will remove arithmetic errors."],"a":[2],"e":"Exact, reproducible results require deterministic computation. Shrinking the model's role to structured extraction that code can validate means run-to-run variability can no longer reach the ledger.\n\nModel output is non-deterministic, and exact arithmetic is a job for code. Let the model do what it is good at, extracting fields into a validated schema, and compute the totals deterministically (C). Temperature zero (A) reduces variety but does not guarantee identical or correct arithmetic. Majority voting (B) triples cost and can still post a wrong value. A more capable model (D) may lower error rates but does not make results exact or reproducible.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Exact calculation + varying outputs → schema extraction, math in code","k":["differ by a few cents","identical input","exact and reproducible"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c106","d":"d5","x":"A procurement agent turns approved purchase requests into JSON that is posted directly to the ERP to create purchase orders. Recent incidents include an order with a negative quantity and an order to a vendor that is not on the approved list. Policy also says that orders above a set amount need a manager's sign-off. Which two controls most directly enforce these rules? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Add a system-prompt instruction asking the model to double-check quantities and vendors before it responds.","Validate each JSON payload in code against a strict schema with types, ranges and allowed vendor values.","Retry with a higher temperature whenever the ERP rejects an order, so the model produces a different output.","Log every created purchase order and have the finance team audit a sample of them at the end of each month.","Apply the amount limit as a code rule that routes orders above the threshold to a manager for approval."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Rules that must hold every time belong in code on the action path: schema checks reject invalid payloads before execution, and a threshold rule guarantees the human sign-off the policy requires.\n\nThe model proposes and deterministic code decides. Strict schema validation (B) blocks negative quantities and unapproved vendors before the ERP call, and an amount rule in code (E) enforces the sign-off policy by routing large orders to a manager. A prompt instruction (A) is guidance, not enforcement. Retrying at a higher temperature (C) adds randomness, not control. A month-end audit (D) only detects bad orders after they exist.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Model output drives action → schema validation + business rules in code","k":["posted directly to the ERP","negative quantity, unapproved vendor","above a set amount"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c107","d":"d5","x":"A US federal agency wants a Claude-based assistant to summarize case files for its staff. The team's prototype calls a commercial API endpoint chosen for price, stores embeddings in a SaaS vector database, and sends logs to a third-party monitoring tool. The agency's authorizing official says the workload requires FedRAMP Moderate. What should determine the production architecture?","o":["Every component in the data path, including model access, vector store and logging, must run in environments authorized at the required level.","The model must be reached through an authorized environment, while the vector store and logging tools can stay on their current services.","The team should select the most capable model tier, because the agency's decision rests mainly on the quality of the case summaries.","A signed Business Associate Agreement with the model provider, together with a system-prompt rule forbidding retention of case data."],"a":[0],"e":"Authorization applies wherever the data flows, not just to the model call. One unauthorized component makes the whole path non-compliant, and model quality or contracts from other regimes cannot substitute.\n\nFedRAMP is a hard constraint on every service in the data path: model access, vector store and logging must all run in environments authorized at the required impact level (A). Authorizing only the model endpoint (B) leaves case data flowing through unauthorized services. Model capability (C) is irrelevant if the environment is not authorized. A BAA (D) is a HIPAA instrument, and a prompt rule is not a retention control.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Federal workload → every data-path component authorized at required FedRAMP level","k":["US federal agency","requires FedRAMP Moderate","SaaS vector database, third-party monitoring"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c108","d":"d5","x":"An internal IT helpdesk agent answers employee questions and resets an employee's own password on request. To move fast, it was given a shared administrator service account that can also delete accounts and change group memberships across the directory. A security review asks how to reduce the impact of a hallucinated or injected instruction. Which two changes should you make? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Remove the account-deletion and group-membership tools that the helpdesk task does not require.","Require the agent to ask the employee for confirmation in chat before deleting any account.","Replace the shared admin account with credentials scoped to the requesting employee's own account.","Send every administrative action the agent performs to the SIEM for review by the security team.","Move the agent to the most capable model tier so that it misinterprets fewer employee requests."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Limiting what the agent can do bounds the damage of every failure mode at once; confirmations, logs and stronger models all leave the destructive capability in place.\n\nExcessive agency multiplies the damage of any hallucination or injection, so the fix is least privilege: remove tools the task does not need (A) and scope credentials to the requesting user (C), so the agent cannot touch other accounts. A chat confirmation (B) keeps the dangerous capability and can itself be manipulated. SIEM logging (D) detects after the damage is done. A more capable model (E) may reduce mistakes but leaves the blast radius unchanged.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Over-permissioned agent → remove unneeded tools + per-user scoped credentials","k":["shared administrator service account","delete accounts, change group memberships","reduce the impact"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c109","d":"d5","x":"A legal operations team wants an agent to manage vendor contract renewals end to end, including sending termination notices when a contract should not renew. Once sent, a termination notice cannot be withdrawn. To save time, the team proposes that the agent send notices automatically whenever its confidence score exceeds 0.9. Which design should you recommend?","o":["Allow automatic sending above the confidence threshold and route only lower-confidence notices to a lawyer.","Require a lawyer to approve every agent step, including contract retrieval, date checks and renewal reminders.","Allow automatic sending for contracts below a set annual value and require lawyer approval for larger ones.","Have the agent draft notices into a review queue without a send tool, and have a named lawyer approve each one."],"a":[3],"e":"Review belongs exactly where errors cannot be undone, and removing the send capability makes that review structural rather than optional. Confidence and contract size are proxies that let consequential errors through.\n\nA termination notice is high impact and irreversible, so a human must approve each one; giving the agent only a drafting tool makes sending impossible without that approval (D). Confidence scores (A) miss confidently wrong outputs. Reviewing every step (B) causes review fatigue and adds nothing on low-impact steps. A contract-value cutoff (C) uses size as a proxy for risk, but even a small termination is binding and cannot be undone.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.","t":"Irreversible action → draft-for-review, human commits, no send tool","k":["cannot be withdrawn","confidence score exceeds 0.9","send notices automatically"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c110","d":"d5","x":"A SaaS company is adding a Claude-powered document assistant to a product used by several EU enterprise customers. Customer contracts require that their data stay in the EU and that one customer's documents never appear in another customer's answers. The current prototype uses one shared vector index and a US-hosted deployment. Which two changes address these requirements? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Keep the shared index, and instruct the model in the system prompt to use only the current customer's documents.","Pseudonymize customer names in the documents, since pseudonymized data then falls outside the scope of the GDPR.","Keep full prompts and retrieved documents in long-term logs so that any cross-customer leak can be investigated.","Separate the indexes per customer, or apply tenant filters at retrieval before any document enters the context.","Choose a deployment region and access path that keep prompts, indexes and logs within the EU, as contracted."],"a":[3,4],"e":"Contractual residency and isolation are enforced by where data lives and what retrieval can return; a model told to ignore another tenant's documents has already been given them.\n\nTenant isolation must happen at retrieval, before documents reach the context window, through separate indexes or enforced tenant filters (D). Residency is an architectural choice of region and deployment path for every component, including indexes and logs (E). A prompt instruction (A) is not an isolation control, because the other tenant's data has already been retrieved. Pseudonymized data is still personal data under the GDPR (B). Long-term logging of full content (C) increases exposure and conflicts with minimal retention.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Compliance needs contracts and configuration (BAA, ZDR, DPA, audit), not prompts.\n• Identity done right: per-user scoped tokens, secrets server-side, RBAC before retrieval.","t":"Residency + tenant isolation → region-pinned deployment + per-tenant retrieval filtering","k":["data stay in the EU","another customer's answers","one shared vector index"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c2","d":"d6","x":"An architect at an organization is reviewing two draft communication packages for the same architectural decision. Package 1 contains a threat model, residual-risk register, and control-mapping table. Package 2 contains a list of capabilities delivered and feature-level roadmap dependencies. Which two audiences are correctly matched to these packages? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Package 2 → executive sponsor","Package 2 → product manager owning the roadmap","Package 1 → security and compliance reviewer","Package 1 → engineering implementation team","Package 2 → security and compliance reviewer"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Each audience gets the package that answers its own question: security reviewers judge whether risk is understood and controlled, while product managers plan what users get and when.\n\nPackage 1 (threat model, residual-risk register, control mapping) is what a security and compliance reviewer needs. Package 2 (capabilities delivered and roadmap dependencies) serves the product manager who owns the roadmap. An executive sponsor needs outcomes, risk and trade-offs rather than a feature list, and the engineering team needs implementation detail rather than a risk register as its primary package.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Lead with decision, outcome and trade-offs; tailor depth to each audience.","t":"Threat model/control mapping → security; capabilities/roadmap → PM","k":["threat model, residual-risk register","control-mapping table","feature-level roadmap dependencies"]},{"id":"ccar-p-c11","d":"d6","x":"You are running a discovery session with a business sponsor who asks for \"an AI system to fix our customer escalations.\" Which approach best yields actionable requirements?","o":["Defer the discovery session on the grounds that the sponsor must fully specify the system before the architect can ask clarifying or scoping questions.","Accept the sponsor's original phrasing as a complete and final requirement and proceed directly to design.","Assume the requirement matches the architect's previous engagement in a different industry and proceed with that context.","Decompose the request by asking targeted questions about who escalates, what triggers escalation, what good resolution looks like, and which constraints (latency, cost, audit, data sensitivity) apply."],"a":[3],"e":"A vague ask describes a pain, not a system; targeted questions about actors, triggers, outcomes and constraints convert it into requirements that can be designed and tested against.\n\nVague asks become actionable requirements when you decompose them with targeted questions: who escalates, what triggers an escalation, what a good resolution looks like, and which constraints apply (latency, cost, audit, data sensitivity). Refusing discovery (A), accepting the phrase as final (B) or reusing assumptions from another industry (C) all skip real requirement gathering.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Discovery turns vague asks into constraints, success criteria and failure thresholds.","t":"Vague sponsor ask → decompose: who, trigger, good outcome, constraints","k":["an AI system to fix","actionable requirements","discovery session"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-41","d":"d6","x":"You are distinguishing functional from non-functional requirements during discovery. Which item is a non-functional requirement?","o":["The system must respond at p95 latency under 800 milliseconds at the expected request volume.","The system must produce a draft response that a human reviewer can edit before sending.","The system must extract a defined set of specific fields from invoice attachments and populate a downstream data record.","The system must classify inbound tickets into a defined set of categories and route each to the owning team."],"a":[0],"e":"A non-functional requirement sets a threshold any implementation must meet rather than naming a behaviour; a percentile latency at a stated volume is a quality gate, not an action.\n\nA p95 latency target at an expected volume describes how well the system must perform, so it is a non-functional (quality) requirement. Drafting editable responses, extracting invoice fields and classifying/routing tickets describe what the system does, so they are functional requirements.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Discovery turns vague asks into constraints, success criteria and failure thresholds.\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.","t":"Threshold (latency, cost, availability) → non-functional; action/output → functional","k":["p95 latency under 800 milliseconds","expected request volume","non-functional requirement"],"w":["This defines a performance constraint rather than a specific business function. Latency, throughput, scalability, reliability, and similar characteristics are typically non-functional requirements.","This specifies a system capability or behavior: generating an editable draft for human review. Therefore, it is a functional requirement.","This describes what the system must do: extract specific invoice fields and populate another record. That is a functional requirement.","Ticket classification describes what the system is required to perform, making it a functional requirement."]},{"id":"ccar-p-c32","d":"d6","x":"You are compiling continuity practices that span the deployment lifecycle. Which two practices belong on the list? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["Maintain a stakeholder register and notify the listed parties at every phase transition event.","Carry the evaluation framework and reference set forward across iterations rather than rebuilding each time.","Capture lessons learned at the end of each phase and surface them as inputs to the next phase.","Archive every phase deliverable in long-term storage to preserve a record of what was produced.","Lock decisions made in early phases to prevent revisiting them as later phases begin."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Continuity means the outputs of one phase become inputs to the next: a stable yardstick keeps results comparable across iterations, and lessons turned into inputs change what the next phase does. Records and notifications preserve history without transferring knowledge.\n\nContinuity across the lifecycle comes from carrying the evaluation framework and reference set forward instead of rebuilding them, and from capturing lessons learned at the end of each phase as input to the next. Notifying everyone at every transition (A) and archiving everything (D) are record-keeping, and locking early decisions (E) blocks learning.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• ADRs record alternatives and trade-offs; scope changes get transparent options.","t":"Lifecycle continuity → reuse eval set + lessons feed next phase","k":["continuity practices","span the deployment lifecycle","across iterations"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c36","d":"d6","x":"A pilot AI assistant for procurement specialists shows 89 percent first-response acceptance, but follow-up surveys reveal that specialists frequently override the assistant's vendor recommendations after consulting unrelated criteria the assistant did not consider. The pilot owner wants to ship the assistant as is, given the strong acceptance rate. Which two discernment-competency observations should you raise BEFORE approving the launch? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["The pilot duration was likely too short to establish reliability across the full year.","The unconsidered criteria represent a scope gap in the assistant's input space directly.","The survey response rate may not be statistically representative of all the specialists.","Acceptance does not measure whether recommendations are right after specialist review.","The acceptance rate, combined with the pilot's sample size, is enough evidence to proceed without further review."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Acceptance measures user behaviour, not whether the output is right, and systematic overrides on criteria the assistant never saw reveal missing inputs that no prompt tuning can fix.\n\nSpecialists overriding recommendations because of criteria the assistant never saw is a scope gap in its inputs. And first-response acceptance does not show that the recommendations were correct after review. Options A and C are speculative without evidence, and E repeats the flawed argument for launching.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.","t":"High acceptance + expert overrides → correctness unproven; input scope gap","k":["89 percent first-response acceptance","override vendor recommendations","criteria the assistant did not consider"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c40","d":"d6","x":"A Claude architect is leading the discovery phase for a new AI-powered customer service solution. Which two activities are characteristic of structured discovery and requirement gathering for a Claude-based deployment? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select 2","o":["facilitating stakeholder workshops to surface latency, accuracy, and compliance constraints before scoping begins","deferring constraint documentation until the integration design phase to avoid scope creep","generating an initial prototype and iterating based on user reaction","documenting explicit success criteria and failure thresholds that will gate production deployment","selecting the Claude model tier based on the architect's prior project experience before stakeholder input is collected"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Discovery exists to surface constraints early and define measurable gates for production; both shape every later design choice, whereas prototyping first or picking a model tier early anchors on a solution before the problem is specified.\n\nStructured discovery facilitates stakeholder workshops that surface latency, accuracy and compliance constraints before scoping, and documents explicit success criteria and failure thresholds that gate production. Deferring constraints (B), prototyping first and iterating on reactions (C) or choosing a model tier before stakeholder input (E) skip structured discovery.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Discovery turns vague asks into constraints, success criteria and failure thresholds.\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Discovery → workshops for constraints + success criteria and failure thresholds","k":["leading the discovery phase","structured discovery","requirement gathering"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-17","d":"d6","x":"You are presenting an architectural decision to a mixed audience that includes an executive sponsor and the engineering leads who will implement the decision. Which presentation strategy best serves both audiences?","o":["Open with a deep technical dive into implementation details for the engineering leads.","Announce the chosen decision and move directly to implementation planning.","Lead with the decision, the business outcomes it serves, and the trade-offs accepted; follow with the technical rationale, alternatives, and implementation implications for the engineering audience.","Present a single undifferentiated narrative that addresses technical and business concerns with equal weight throughout, treating both audiences as requiring the same depth on every section."],"a":[2],"e":"A pyramid structure gives the executive everything in the first minute and gives engineers the context that makes the technical detail meaningful; stating trade-offs up front builds trust with both.\n\nMixed audiences are best served by leading with the decision, the business outcomes and the trade-offs accepted (for the executive), then following with the technical rationale, alternatives and implementation implications (for engineering). Opening with deep technical detail (A), announcing without rationale (B) or giving everyone the same depth on every point (D) serves neither group well.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Lead with decision, outcome and trade-offs; tailor depth to each audience.","t":"Mixed audience → decision, outcome, trade-offs first; depth after","k":["mixed audience","executive sponsor","engineering leads who will implement"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c51","d":"d6","x":"You are selecting the documentation set that should accompany a Claude-based deployment at handoff. Which set is most complete?","o":["a project retrospective document, a lessons-learned summary, and a list of contributors","architecture overview, ADRs, component-level diagrams, runbooks for common operations, on-call playbook, and a list of known limitations","the original design proposal, its review comments, meeting notes from design sessions, and the approved requirements document","user-facing release notes, a marketing one-pager describing the deployment, and a customer FAQ"],"a":[1],"e":"A handoff set must let a team that was not in the room operate, change and defend the system: how it is built, why, and what to do when it fails. Historical, retrospective and user-facing documents answer none of these.\n\nA complete handoff set lets another team operate the system: architecture overview, ADRs, component diagrams, runbooks for common operations, an on-call playbook and known limitations. Retrospectives (A), design-history artefacts (C) and marketing or user-facing material (D) do not support operation.\n\n**Related rules**\n• ADRs record alternatives and trade-offs; scope changes get transparent options.","t":"Handoff → overview, ADRs, diagrams, runbooks, on-call playbook, limitations","k":["accompany a Claude-based deployment","at handoff","most complete"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-50","d":"d6","x":"You are responding to a stakeholder request that adds significant scope to a deployment already in flight. Which response best aligns expectations while respecting the stakeholder's underlying need?","o":["Acknowledge the request, name the scope and timeline implications transparently, and propose options for the stakeholder to choose.","Acknowledge the request, explain that the current release is locked, and offer to revisit it once the deployment has shipped.","Acknowledge the request, add it to the backlog with a note on its origin, and confirm that it will be considered in the next planning cycle.","Accept the additional scope into the current release, extend the timeline to absorb it, and notify the affected teams of the new date."],"a":[0],"e":"Making the impact visible and offering choices keeps the priority decision with the stakeholder who owns it while respecting the underlying need; refusing, deferring or absorbing silently removes that choice.\n\nAcknowledge the request, state the scope and timeline implications transparently, and offer options so the stakeholder chooses the trade-off. Refusing because the release is locked (B) or quietly backlogging it (C) ignores the underlying need, and accepting it unilaterally (D) moves dates without the stakeholder making the trade-off.\n\n**Related rules**\n• ADRs record alternatives and trade-offs; scope changes get transparent options.","t":"Scope change mid-flight → acknowledge, state impact, offer options","k":["adds significant scope","already in flight","underlying need"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c62","d":"d6","x":"You are reviewing a peer's draft ADR. The ADR states the decision and the technical detail but does not state the alternatives considered or the trade-offs accepted. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["Approve the ADR as written since the decision is documented, treating the missing alternatives and trade-offs as unnecessary detail.","Ask the author to add the alternatives considered and the trade-offs accepted before publishing the ADR.","Ask the author to link the design-discussion thread in place of a written alternatives section.","Publish the ADR as written and open a follow-up task to add alternatives in a later revision."],"a":[1],"e":"An ADR's value is explaining why the alternatives lost; without that, later teams relitigate or unknowingly break the decision, and incomplete records are rarely fixed once published.\n\nAn ADR exists to record why a decision was made, so it must state the alternatives considered and the trade-offs accepted before it is published. Approving it as is (A), linking a discussion thread instead (C) or deferring the missing sections (D) leaves the record incomplete.\n\n**Related rules**\n• ADRs record alternatives and trade-offs; scope changes get transparent options.","t":"ADR missing alternatives/trade-offs → complete before publishing","k":["does not state the alternatives","trade-offs accepted","draft ADR"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c111","d":"d6","x":"Three months after a document-review assistant was handed to the operations team, two incidents occurred. During a quality-drop alert, the on-call engineer could not tell whether the latest prompt change could be rolled back, or how. Separately, the release evaluation suite had silently stopped running after launch because no one knew it was their job. Which two additions to the handoff documentation most directly address these gaps? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["A lessons-learned retrospective describing the decisions the project team made during the design phase.","A customer FAQ explaining what the assistant does and which document types it currently supports.","A runbook and on-call playbook covering alert triage and prompt rollout and rollback through a version pin.","The design meeting notes and review comments, so the operations team understands the original reasoning.","A named owning team for the evaluation suite, with how to run it and the thresholds that gate each release."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Handoff succeeds when the receiving team can act during an incident and knows who owns quality; operational procedures and named ownership address both, while history and user material do not.\n\nThe first incident is a missing operational procedure: a runbook and on-call playbook that explain alerts, triage and how to roll a prompt change forward or back via the version pin (C). The second is missing ownership: evals stop running when no team owns them, so name the owner and document how to run the suite and its release thresholds (E). A retrospective (A) and design meeting notes (D) explain the past, not how to operate the system. A customer FAQ (B) is written for end users, not operators.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Operators can't act, evals lapse → runbooks + named eval-suite owner","k":["could not tell ... rolled back","suite silently stopped running","no one knew it was their job"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c112","d":"d6","x":"Pre-handoff evaluation of a benefits-claims assistant showed accuracy well below the release threshold on handwritten scanned forms and on claims written in Portuguese, both a small share of traffic. The product manager asks you to leave these results out of the handoff documents so that the support team and auditors do not lose confidence in the launch. How should you handle these results?","o":["Leave them out of the handoff set and plan to address both weak slices quietly in the next release cycle.","Record them only in the project retrospective, where the team can revisit them during the next phase.","Document them as known limitations, with the affected inputs, residual risk and how they are routed to humans.","Mention them in the customer FAQ with positive wording that encourages users to submit typed English forms."],"a":[2],"e":"Disclosing weak slices turns a hidden failure mode into a managed one: operators can route around it and auditors can see the risk was understood.\n\nKnown limitations tell support teams where the system should not be trusted, give auditors evidence that risk was understood rather than hidden, and keep the next team from rediscovering the failures. Stating the affected inputs, residual risk and human-routing path makes the limitation operable (C). Omitting the results (A) hides a known risk from operators and auditors. A retrospective (B) is not where operators look. A customer FAQ (D) targets end users, spins the risk and gives operators nothing to act on.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Weak eval slices at handoff → known limitations with routing to humans","k":["well below the release threshold","handwritten scanned forms","leave these results out"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c113","d":"d6","x":"A contract-summarization service has run on one model version for eight months, and the team has kept its versioned reference set and release thresholds. A newer model version has just been released, and the business sponsor asks you to switch production to it this week because the announcement says it reasons better. What should you do?","o":["Rerun the existing reference set and thresholds on the new version per slice, check SLAs and cost, then roll out behind a version pin.","Switch production to the new version this week, since a newer release should perform at least as well as the current one.","Stay on the current version until the provider retires it, because it already meets every one of the release thresholds.","Build a new evaluation set around the new version's announced strengths, then decide whether to switch based on it."],"a":[0],"e":"Keeping the yardstick fixed makes the upgrade decision evidence-based and comparable, and a gradual, pinned rollout limits the blast radius if behaviour shifts.\n\nA model upgrade is a change like any other: rerun the existing reference set and thresholds per slice, confirm latency and cost against SLAs and budget, and roll out gradually behind a version pin with a documented rollback (A). Switching on the strength of an announcement (B) skips evidence, and behaviour can shift in ways that matter. Ignoring the release (C) forgoes possible gains in quality or cost. A new eval set built around the announced strengths (D) moves the yardstick, so results are no longer comparable with the current version.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"New model version released → retest on existing evals, then pinned gradual rollout","k":["newer model version","kept its versioned reference set","announcement says it reasons better"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c114","d":"d6","x":"ADR-007, accepted at launch a year ago, put all traffic of a claims-triage assistant on the most capable model tier. Rerunning the carried-forward reference set now shows a lighter tier meeting the quality bar in aggregate at much lower cost. A team lead argues that accepted decisions should stay fixed for consistency. Which two actions should you take? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Write a new ADR that records the new evidence and supersedes ADR-007, leaving the original record visible.","Edit ADR-007 in place to describe the lighter tier, so that only one current decision record exists.","Check the lighter tier per slice against the release thresholds before routing production traffic to it.","Keep ADR-007 unchanged, since revisiting accepted decisions undermines stakeholder trust in the design.","Move all traffic to the lighter tier now, since the aggregate result on the reference set already passes."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Evidence justifies revisiting a decision, but through a superseding record and per-slice validation; aggregates hide failures and edited ADRs erase the reasoning trail.\n\nDecisions should be stable but revisable when evidence changes; a new ADR that records the evidence and supersedes ADR-007 keeps the history visible (A). An aggregate pass can hide failing slices, so validate the lighter tier per slice against the release thresholds before routing traffic (C). Editing an accepted ADR (B) erases the record of why the first choice was made. Locking the decision (D) ignores evidence and wastes cost. Switching on the aggregate alone (E) risks shipping a regression on important slices.\n\n**Related rules**\n• ADRs record alternatives and trade-offs; scope changes get transparent options.\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Choose the lightest model that meets the quality bar on a representative sample.","t":"New evidence vs old decision → superseding ADR + per-slice validation","k":["accepted at launch a year ago","quality bar in aggregate","stay fixed for consistency"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c115","d":"d6","x":"Production monitoring of an invoice-processing assistant shows that extraction quality drops on invoices from a recently acquired subsidiary, which uses a different layout. The finding is written up in the monitoring-phase retrospective, and planning for the next iteration starts next week. Which action best ensures this lesson actually changes the next iteration?","o":["Archive the retrospective in the long-term project repository so the finding is preserved for future reference.","Send the retrospective to every party on the stakeholder register as part of the phase-transition notification.","Schedule a review of the finding at the end of the next iteration, once more production data has been collected.","Turn it into inputs: add subsidiary invoices to the reference set, a discovery question and a design option to test."],"a":[3],"e":"Knowledge transfers between phases only when a finding is converted into artefacts the next phase consumes: eval cases, requirement questions and design candidates.\n\nA lesson only helps when it becomes an input to the next phase: new cases in the reference set make the regression measurable, a discovery question confirms layouts and volumes with the subsidiary, and a design option gives the iteration something concrete to evaluate (D). Archiving (A) preserves the record but nobody uses it. Notifying the register (B) is communication hygiene, not knowledge transfer. Reviewing at the end of the next iteration (C) means that iteration runs without the lesson.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Lesson in retrospective → convert into eval cases, discovery and design inputs","k":["recently acquired subsidiary","written up in the retrospective","actually changes the next iteration"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c116","d":"d6","x":"Six weeks after launch of a proposal-drafting assistant, sales leads in one region say it \"feels too slow to use during client calls.\" The SLA agreed in discovery commits to a p95 draft latency, and the organization-wide dashboard shows p95 comfortably within it. The regional director asks you to act on the complaint. What should you do first?","o":["Move all drafting traffic to a lighter model tier right away so that every region experiences faster drafts.","Map the complaint to the latency SLA and measure p95 for that region before choosing a fix or renegotiation.","Close the complaint, because the organization-wide p95 latency is within the SLA committed in discovery.","Add a progress indicator to the drafting screen so users perceive the wait as shorter during client calls."],"a":[1],"e":"An aggregate within SLA can hide a failing slice; measuring the complaining segment against the commitment decides whether to fix, renegotiate or simply note the feedback.\n\nVague feedback should first be mapped to the value pillar or SLA it concerns. Measuring p95 for the region's slice shows whether this is a breach to fix, a need the SLA never captured (live client calls) to renegotiate, or an opinion to note (B). Changing the model for everyone (A) acts before measuring and may cost quality. Closing on the aggregate (C) ignores that aggregates hide slice failures. A progress indicator (D) treats perception without establishing whether the commitment is met.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Per-slice requirements need per-slice measurement; aggregates hide failures.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Vague 'too slow' complaint → map to SLA, measure that slice first","k":["sales leads in one region","feels too slow","organization-wide dashboard"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c117","d":"d6","x":"You are finalizing service commitments for a Claude-based underwriting assistant with the business sponsor and the platform operations team. The sponsor wants a clear quality and latency commitment to report against, and operations wants warning of trouble before that commitment is broken. Which two arrangements should you put in place? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Set the internal targets equal to the stakeholder commitment so that there is a single number everyone tracks.","Report to stakeholders against the SLA agreed in discovery, expressed as measurable latency and quality thresholds.","Report the stricter internal targets to stakeholders as the commitment so that the sponsor sees ambitious numbers.","Configure alerts to fire only once the SLA has been breached so that the operations team avoids alert fatigue.","Operate against stricter internal SLOs with an error budget so that alerts fire before the SLA is put at risk."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Separating the external commitment from a stricter internal target creates a buffer: stakeholders get a stable promise, and operators get early warning while there is still time to act.\n\nStakeholders need a commitment they can hold the system to, so report against the agreed SLA with measurable thresholds (B). Operations needs headroom, so operate against stricter internal SLOs with an error budget that surface problems before the commitment breaks (E). Equal targets (A) and alerting only on breach (D) both make the breach itself the first signal. Presenting internal targets as the commitment (C) creates expectations the team never agreed to and removes the safety margin.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Eliminate options that break a hard constraint (SLA, compliance, stated priority) first.\n• Specification before execution: metrics, slices, SLOs and hypothesis come first.","t":"Commitment + early warning → report against SLA, operate on stricter SLO","k":["commitment to report against","warning before commitment is broken","platform operations team"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c5","d":"d7","x":"You are integrating AI-assisted tooling into the team's documentation workflow. The team wants generated documentation that stays grounded in code. Which integration approach best fits this requirement?","o":["Configure subagents that read the relevant code files and draft documentation, then have a single reviewer skim only the summary section before publication.","Configure subagents that read the relevant code files and generate documentation, then publish it directly to the team's shared documentation site.","Configure subagents that read the relevant code files via filesystem and code-search tools, generate the documentation, and emit changes through the team's normal review workflow.","Configure subagents that read a small sample of the repository's files and generate documentation for the whole codebase from the sample, then route it through review."],"a":[2],"e":"Grounding comes from the subagent actually reading the code it documents through file and search tools, and correctness comes from the same review gate every change passes. Sampling, skimming or direct publishing each removes one of those two controls.\n\nDocumentation stays grounded when subagents read the actual code through filesystem and code-search tools and their changes go through the team's normal review. A skim of the summary (A) or direct publishing (B) skips real verification, and a small sample (D) cannot ground documentation for the whole codebase.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Grounding: source-only answers, required citations and a verification step.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Grounded docs → subagents read real code + normal review workflow","k":["stays grounded in code","team's documentation workflow","AI-assisted tooling"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c13","d":"d7","x":"An engineering organization is adopting Claude Code across 200 developers. A team lead has proposed that AI-generated pull requests bypass the standard code review process for changes under 50 lines, on the grounds that small changes are low-risk and review capacity is constrained. Which two Diligence-competency objections should you raise? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select 2","o":["AI-generated pull requests should be rejected as a default policy across the engineering organization.","Code review capacity should be expanded to handle every code change submitted across the team.","Line count is a poor proxy for the actual risk a code change introduces into the system.","The 50-line threshold should be raised to 200 lines instead to capture more changes overall.","Removing review removes the verification mechanism that AI-generated output requires across the workflow."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Risk depends on what a change touches, not how many lines it has, and review is the verification step that plausible-but-wrong AI output needs. Moving the threshold or changing review capacity keeps the flawed premise.\n\nLine count says little about risk: a two-line change to authentication can be far more dangerous than a 500-line refactor. And AI-generated code needs verification, so removing review removes the main control. Rejecting all AI pull requests (A) or reviewing literally everything with more people (B) are not the objection, and moving the threshold (D) keeps the flawed premise.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.","t":"Skip review for small AI PRs → line count is not risk; review verifies","k":["bypass the standard code review","changes under 50 lines","Diligence-competency objections"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c15","d":"d7","x":"You are supporting a team whose Claude Code sessions consistently load 60 or more MCP tools from many servers, exhausting context budget before the session begins. Which adjustment most directly addresses this issue without removing capability?","o":["Split the workflow across more sessions, each connecting to a smaller subset of servers, so no single session loads every tool definition.","Automate the review of the server list before each session and disable servers the engineer thinks are unneeded for that day's task.","Raise the session's context window to a larger model tier so all tool definitions fit within the available budget.","Enable Tool Search so tool definitions are deferred and discovered on demand rather than loaded into context upfront."],"a":[3],"e":"Deferring tool definitions and loading them on demand frees the context budget while every tool stays reachable. The alternatives either remove capability or simply pay for more tokens spent on rarely used definitions.\n\nTool Search defers tool definitions and discovers them on demand, so the context is not consumed up front and every tool is still available. Splitting sessions (A) or disabling servers (B) removes capability, and a larger context window (C) still wastes tokens and cost on definitions that are rarely used.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Progressive discovery (tool search, Skills on demand) beats loading everything.\n• Org-wide Claude Code: managed settings + approved plugin marketplace + scoped permissions.","t":"Many MCP tools exhaust context → Tool Search defers definitions","k":["60 or more MCP tools","exhausting context budget","without removing capability"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-29","d":"d7","x":"You are reviewing a peer's Claude Code permission rules for an enterprise rollout. The rules grant unrestricted Bash access to all projects across all developers. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["Allow-list Bash to the specific commands the workflows require, relying on the allow-list's implicit default-deny.","Replace unrestricted Bash with narrowly-scoped tool patterns that allow only the specific commands the workflows require, and add explicit deny rules for sensitive operations.","Replace unrestricted Bash access with narrowly-scoped tool patterns, but apply the same scoped patterns identically across every project regardless of each project's actual workflow needs.","Approve the rules as written, treating Bash scoping as a matter for each project team to tighten later."],"a":[1],"e":"A command that matches no allow rule only triggers a prompt, so sensitive operations need explicit deny rules. Scoping allows to each workflow's real needs keeps least privilege without blocking legitimate work.\n\nReplace unrestricted Bash with narrowly scoped patterns that allow only the commands the workflows require, and add explicit deny rules for sensitive operations. Relying only on an implicit default-deny (A) leaves dangerous patterns unguarded, identical patterns for every project (C) ignore real needs, and approving as written (D) accepts the risk.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.\n• Org-wide Claude Code: managed settings + approved plugin marketplace + scoped permissions.","t":"Unrestricted Bash → scoped allow patterns + explicit deny rules","k":["unrestricted Bash access","all projects across all developers","enterprise rollout"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c69","d":"d7","x":"An organization standardizing Claude Code across many teams must enforce security settings that developers cannot override and distribute approved tools and workflows centrally. Which two actions should it take? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Run a managed plugin marketplace that distributes approved skills, agents, hooks and MCP servers.","Let developers install tools freely and audit local configurations every quarter.","Enforce organization-wide controls with server-managed settings that lower levels cannot override.","Publish recommended settings in a shared repository and ask each team to apply them.","Keep approved tools in project-level configuration so each team can change or disable them."],"a":[0,2],"e":"Managed settings sit above every other level, so nothing local can override them, and a curated marketplace is one vetted channel for extensions. Recommendations, audits and team-editable config all leave room for drift.\n\nManaged settings sit at the top of the settings hierarchy, so project, user, local and command-line settings cannot override them, and a managed marketplace gives one vetted source for extensions. After-the-fact audits (B), recommended settings (D) and team-editable project configuration (E) all allow drift.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Org-wide Claude Code: managed settings + approved plugin marketplace + scoped permissions.\n• One owned, versioned source (Skill, template) referenced by every consumer.","t":"Non-overridable controls + central tools → managed settings + marketplace","k":["developers cannot override","distribute approved tools centrally","standardizing across many teams"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c118","d":"d7","x":"An SRE team wants to give a Claude-based on-call assistant cluster-admin credentials on production Kubernetes so it can diagnose and fix incidents faster. The assistant reads logs and support tickets, some of which contain customer-supplied text. Which access design should you recommend?","o":["Grant cluster-admin credentials but log every command the assistant runs, so any harmful action can be traced and reversed afterwards.","Grant cluster-admin only during declared incidents and add a CLAUDE.md instruction telling the assistant to avoid destructive commands.","Provide read-only log, trace and metrics tools by default, plus narrowly scoped remediation tools that run only after on-call approval.","Deny the assistant all production access, so engineers copy relevant log excerpts into the session by hand during each incident."],"a":[2],"e":"Least privilege removes the dangerous capability instead of watching for its misuse: read-only tools cover investigation, and the few remediation actions are narrow and human-approved, which also blunts instructions injected through log content.\n\nInvestigation needs read-only access to logs, traces and metrics; remediation such as a restart or rollback should be a narrow, scoped tool executed only after an on-call engineer approves, under a least-privilege identity with an audit trail (C). Logging alone (A) detects harm only after it happens. Time-boxed admin rights plus a CLAUDE.md instruction (B) rely on guidance rather than enforcement, and customer text in logs could inject instructions. Removing all access (D) throws away the speed the team needs and makes manual copying a new source of error.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Least privilege: remove unneeded capability instead of logging or confirming it.\n• Humans review high-impact or irreversible actions, not every step.","t":"Assistant in production → read-only tools + scoped, approved remediation","k":["cluster-admin credentials","diagnose and fix incidents faster","customer-supplied text"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c119","d":"d7","x":"Twenty minutes after a deploy, checkout-api starts returning intermittent 502 errors. An engineer opens Claude Code and asks it to 'find the bug and push a fix' to the main branch. Error logs, distributed traces with correlation IDs and the deploy history are available through read-only tools. Which two steps should happen before any fix is merged? (Select two.) Select 2","o":["Have Claude apply its most plausible fix directly to production, then confirm the diagnosis afterwards from the error rate.","Follow failing requests across services by correlation ID and line up the first errors with the deploy and config history.","Switch the session to the most capable model tier, since a stronger model is more likely to guess the root cause first time.","Restart every checkout-api pod immediately and close the incident if the error rate drops below the alert threshold.","Reproduce the failure outside production, ideally as a failing test that the proposed fix must then make pass."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Evidence and reproduction turn a plausible story into a confirmed root cause, and the failing test becomes the proof that the fix works; guessing harder or restarting only masks the symptom.\n\nDiagnosis comes before change. Correlating failing requests by ID and aligning them with the deploy timeline (B) ties each hypothesis to evidence, and reproducing the failure as a failing test (E) turns a guess into a fact and becomes the regression test for the fix, which then goes through normal review. Pushing a plausible fix to production (A) skips both verification and review. A stronger model (C) does not replace evidence. Restarting pods (D) may hide the symptom while leaving the root cause in place.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Measure before acting: sample, isolate one variable, then fix the root cause.\n• Observability: correlation IDs, per-span tokens/latency, model version on every turn.","t":"Incident → correlate evidence, reproduce, then fix through review","k":["twenty minutes after a deploy","push a fix to the main branch","correlation IDs and deploy history"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-c120","d":"d7","x":"A platform team's CLAUDE.md tells Claude Code to run the formatter and linter after every file edit and never to modify files under db/migrations. Reviewers still find unformatted code in AI-generated pull requests, and last week a session edited an already-applied migration. The team wants both behaviors enforced every time across all repositories. What should you recommend?","o":["Add hooks that format after edits and block protected paths, distributed centrally through the managed plugin marketplace.","Rewrite both instructions in capital letters near the top of CLAUDE.md and repeat them in each developer's personal CLAUDE.local.md file.","Ask reviewers to reject any AI-generated pull request with formatting problems or migration changes until developers adjust their prompts.","Move the instructions into each repository's README so that Claude reads them together with the build and test documentation."],"a":[0],"e":"Instructions shape behavior probabilistically while hooks execute deterministically at lifecycle points, so an 'every time' requirement moves from text into automation that is distributed centrally.\n\nCLAUDE.md is guidance that Claude usually follows, but not with certainty. Behavior that must always happen belongs in hooks, which run at fixed lifecycle points: a post-edit hook runs the formatter and a pre-edit hook blocks protected paths. Distributing the approved hooks centrally keeps every repository consistent (A). Louder or duplicated instructions (B) and README text (D) are still guidance. Catching problems in review (C) finds them after the fact and does nothing to stop an applied migration from being edited.\n\n**Related rules**\n• Org-wide Claude Code: managed settings + approved plugin marketplace + scoped permissions.\n• Layer guardrails (input, prompt, output, schema); temperature and logs are not guardrails.","t":"Must always happen → hooks, not CLAUDE.md instructions","k":["enforced every time","CLAUDE.md tells Claude Code","edited an already-applied migration","across all repositories"],"ns":1},{"id":"ccar-p-3","d":"d5","x":"You are configuring tool permissions for a Claude-based assistant. The assistant's defined responsibilities require read access to a knowledge base and write access to a draft queue, and nothing else.\nWhich scoping design best applies least privilege?","o":["Allow access to every tool in the catalog and rely on the model to decline tools it should not use.","Per-role allow-list of exactly the read-knowledge-base and write-draft-queue tools, enforced at the orchestration layer.","Disable all tools entirely to achieve a minimal permission surface, accepting that the assistant can no longer perform the read-knowledge-base or write-draft-queue tasks it was designed for.","Allow access to all tools with read permissions globally and restrict write permissions to the draft- queue tool, without scoping to only the specific tools required for defined tasks."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B. Per-role allow-list of exactly the read-knowledge-base and write-draft-queue tools, enforced at the orchestration layer.\n\nA) This does not follow the least-privilege principle because the assistant receives access to tools it does not need. Relying on the model to voluntarily avoid those tools is not an adequate security control.\n\nB) This is the strongest least-privilege design. The assistant receives access to only the two tools required for its defined responsibilities. Enforcing the allow-list at the orchestration layer provides a technical permission boundary rather than relying on the model's behavior.\n\nC) Although disabling all tools minimizes permissions, it also prevents the assistant from performing its required responsibilities. Least privilege means granting the minimum permissions necessary to accomplish the intended task, not removing necessary access.\n\nD) This still grants unnecessary access to tools outside the assistant's responsibilities. Least privilege requires permissions to be scoped to the specific capabilities the assistant actually needs.","ns":1,"w":["This does not follow the least-privilege principle because the assistant receives access to tools it does not need. Relying on the model to voluntarily avoid those tools is not an adequate security control.","This is the strongest least-privilege design. The assistant receives access to only the two tools required for its defined responsibilities. Enforcing the allow-list at the orchestration layer provides a technical permission boundary rather than relying on the model's behavior.","Although disabling all tools minimizes permissions, it also prevents the assistant from performing its required responsibilities. Least privilege means granting the minimum permissions necessary to accomplish the intended task, not removing necessary access.","This still grants unnecessary access to tools outside the assistant's responsibilities. Least privilege requires permissions to be scoped to the specific capabilities the assistant actually needs."],"t":"Exactly what's needed, ENFORCED outside the model.","k":["least privilege","allow-list","orchestration layer"]},{"id":"ccar-p-4","d":"d7","x":"You are compiling team-setup practices for a Claude Code rollout across an engineering organization.\nWhich two practices belong on the list? (Select two.)\nEach correct answer presents a complete solution.","o":["Use project scope for personal editor preferences so the preferences apply consistently within the project.","Apply managed configuration centrally for security-critical settings that must not be overridden by individual engineers.","Use local scope for security-critical permission rules so each engineer can adapt them to ongoing work.","Use user scope for team-shared MCP servers so every engineer on the team has consistent access.","Use project scope for team-shared Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers and permission rules under version control."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Final Answer: B and E\n\nA) Project scope is intended for team-shared configuration, not personal preferences. Personal editor preferences should generally remain at the user or local scope so they do not affect other engineers working on the project.\n\nB) Managed configuration is appropriate for security-critical controls that need consistent enforcement across the organization. Central management prevents individual engineers from overriding important security policies.\n\nC) Security-critical permission rules should not be individually adjustable when organizational consistency is required. Local configuration could allow engineers to weaken or bypass important security controls.\n\nD) User scope applies to an individual engineer, not the entire project team. Team-shared MCP servers should be configured at a shared project or managed organizational level.\n\nE) Project scope is appropriate for team-shared configuration. Keeping MCP server settings and permission rules under version control improves consistency, reviewability, and change tracking across the engineering organization.","ns":1,"w":["Project scope is intended for team-shared configuration, not personal preferences. Personal editor preferences should generally remain at the user or local scope so they do not affect other engineers working on the project.","Managed configuration is appropriate for security-critical controls that need consistent enforcement across the organization. Central management prevents individual engineers from overriding important security policies.","Security-critical permission rules should not be individually adjustable when organizational consistency is required. Local configuration could allow engineers to weaken or bypass important security controls.","User scope applies to an individual engineer, not the entire project team. Team-shared MCP servers should be configured at a shared project or managed organizational level.","Project scope is appropriate for team-shared configuration. Keeping MCP server settings and permission rules under version control improves consistency, reviewability, and change tracking across the engineering organization."],"t":"Security = MANAGED. Team-shared = PROJECT. Personal = USER.","k":["managed scope","project scope","MCP"]},{"id":"ccar-p-6","d":"d7","x":"You are supporting an engineer whose Claude Code session reports a permission denial when the engineer expected the action to be allowed.\nWhich resolution step is most appropriate?","o":["Inspect the active permission rules across all scopes-managed, command-line, local, project, and user-to identify which rule is denying the action and confirm whether it should be amended or remain denied.","Tell the engineer to retry the denied action verbatim repeatedly until the denial stops appearing, without inspecting the active permission rules to determine whether the denial is intentional.","Disable the permission system entirely across all scopes to remove the denial, eliminating all tool-pattern and deny-rule controls rather than identifying and amending the specific rule.","Grant the engineer unrestricted Bash access to bypass the specific rule causing the denial, removing all tool-pattern constraints rather than amending only the rule in"],"a":[0],"e":"Final Answer: A. Inspect the active permission rules across all applicable scopes to identify and resolve the specific denial.\n\nA) The appropriate approach is to diagnose the active permission configuration rather than bypassing the security controls. Reviewing the applicable rules across scopes helps identify the exact rule responsible for the denial and determine whether the rule is intentional or needs to be changed.\n\nB) Repeated retries do not address the underlying permission configuration. The denial may be intentional, so the correct approach is to inspect and understand the active rules first.\n\nC) Disabling the permission system removes important security controls and violates the principle of least privilege. Only the specific rule that is incorrectly denying the required action should be considered for amendment.\n\nD) Unrestricted Bash access creates an unnecessarily broad security exposure. The appropriate solution is to identify and adjust the specific permission rule if necessary, rather than granting unrestricted capabilities.","ns":1,"w":["The appropriate approach is to diagnose the active permission configuration rather than bypassing the security controls. Reviewing the applicable rules across scopes helps identify the exact rule responsible for the denial and determine whether the rule is intentional or needs to be changed.","Repeated retries do not address the underlying permission configuration. The denial may be intentional, so the correct approach is to inspect and understand the active rules first.","Disabling the permission system removes important security controls and violates the principle of least privilege. Only the specific rule that is incorrectly denying the required action should be considered for amendment.","Unrestricted Bash access creates an unnecessarily broad security exposure. The appropriate solution is to identify and adjust the specific permission rule if necessary, rather than granting unrestricted capabilities."],"t":"Denied? DIAGNOSE the rule across scopes, don't bypass.","k":["permission rules","scopes","diagnose"]},{"id":"ccar-p-8","d":"d3","x":"A technical team is debating whether to implement a new capability for generating regulatory filings from internal data as a Claude Skill or as an MCP server. The capability requires a fixed authoring procedure with embedded examples, does not need to call live external systems, and must be portable across three Claude deployments: claude.ai, an internal API integration, and Claude Code.\nWhich factor most strongly favors implementing the capability as a Skill?","o":["The capability needs to query a live database for the most recent regulatory filings.","The capability requires direct authentication against the corporate identity provider.","The capability is procedural knowledge with no live external-system calls required.","The capability must also be callable from a developer's command-line interface."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C. The capability is procedural knowledge with no live external-system calls\n\nA) A capability that needs to interact with a live external system is better suited to an MCP-based integration. MCP is designed to connect Claude with external tools, services, and data sources.\n\nB) Direct authentication and integration with enterprise systems are characteristics that favor an MCP server or another external integration, rather than a Skill focused on procedural knowledge.\n\nC) This is the strongest reason to choose a Skill. Skills are well suited to reusable procedures, instructions, examples, and domain-specific workflows that Claude can apply when relevant. Since this capability does not need to call live external systems, an MCP server is unnecessary.\n\nD) Being usable from Claude Code does not by itself make MCP the better choice. The key distinction is whether the capability primarily provides procedural knowledge or requires external tools and system access.","ns":1,"w":["A capability that needs to interact with a live external system is better suited to an MCP-based integration. MCP is designed to connect Claude with external tools, services, and data sources.","Direct authentication and integration with enterprise systems are characteristics that favor an MCP server or another external integration, rather than a Skill focused on procedural knowledge.","This is the strongest reason to choose a Skill. Skills are well suited to reusable procedures, instructions, examples, and domain-specific workflows that Claude can apply when relevant. Since this capability does not need to call live external systems, an MCP server is unnecessary.","Being usable from Claude Code does not by itself make MCP the better choice. The key distinction is whether the capability primarily provides procedural knowledge or requires external tools and system access."],"t":"Procedural knowledge, no live calls = SKILL. Live systems = MCP.","k":["Skill vs MCP","procedural knowledge","portable"]},{"id":"ccar-p-9","d":"d7","x":"A Claude architect is auditing configuration scope assignments.\nWhich two statements correctly identify an appropriate use of user-scope configuration versus other scopes?\n(Select two.)","o":["Defining MCP server endpoints shared by all contributors to a specific repository.","Storing API authentication keys so they are not committed to version control.","Enforcing a company-wide policy that disables a feature for all engineers.","Persisting personal editor theme preferences that follow an engineer across projects.","Saving a preferred Claude response language that applies to all repositories the engineer uses."],"a":[3,4],"e":"Final Answer: D and E\n\nA) MCP servers shared by contributors to a particular repository are better suited to project scope, because project-level configuration can be shared and maintained with the project rather than tied to one individual engineer.\n\nB) Authentication keys and other secrets require secure secret-management practices. Simply placing them in user-scope configuration is not the appropriate general solution for protecting sensitive credentials.\n\nC) Company-wide security or policy requirements should be enforced through managed/organizational configuration, rather than user scope. Individual engineers should not be able to override centrally required controls.\n\nD) User scope is appropriate for personal preferences that should follow an individual engineer across different projects. An editor theme is a good example because it does not need to be shared with the entire team.\n\nE) A preferred response language is also an individual user preference. User scope allows the preference to persist across the engineer's different repositories and projects without imposing it on other team members.","ns":1,"w":["MCP servers shared by contributors to a particular repository are better suited to project scope, because project-level configuration can be shared and maintained with the project rather than tied to one individual engineer.","Authentication keys and other secrets require secure secret-management practices. Simply placing them in user-scope configuration is not the appropriate general solution for protecting sensitive credentials.","Company-wide security or policy requirements should be enforced through managed/organizational configuration, rather than user scope. Individual engineers should not be able to override centrally required controls.","User scope is appropriate for personal preferences that should follow an individual engineer across different projects. An editor theme is a good example because it does not need to be shared with the entire team.","A preferred response language is also an individual user preference. User scope allows the preference to persist across the engineer's different repositories and projects without imposing it on other team members."],"t":"User scope = things that follow ME everywhere.","k":["user scope","personal preferences"]},{"id":"ccar-p-12","d":"d6","x":"You must present an architectural recommendation to deploy a Claude-based contract review assistant to a steering committee that includes the CFO, the general counsel, and the CIO. Each stakeholder cares about different aspects of the decision.\nHow should you structure the recommendation document?","o":["Lead with detailed cost projections across the full multi-year horizon before the rationale section.","Lead with the technical architecture diagram and the full component list before any other section.","Present the same dense narrative throughout with no stakeholder differentiation in any section.","Lead with the architectural decision, then address each stakeholder's primary concerns directly."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D. Lead with the architectural decision, then address each stakeholder's primary concerns directly.\n\nA) Cost is highly relevant to the CFO, but leading exclusively with detailed financial projections may not address the legal, technical, and strategic concerns of the other stakeholders.\n\nB) The technical architecture is particularly important to the CIO, but beginning with a detailed component list may overwhelm stakeholders who primarily need to understand the decision, rationale, risks, and business impact.\n\nC) Different stakeholders have different responsibilities and concerns. A one-size-fits-all narrative may fail to address the specific information each decision-maker needs.\n\nD) The strongest approach is to state the recommendation clearly at the beginning and then address the key concerns of each stakeholder. For example, the CFO may focus on cost and ROI, the General Counsel on legal risk and compliance, and the CIO on architecture, security, integration, and operational considerations.\nThis structure makes the recommendation decision-oriented, concise, and relevant to the entire steering committee.","ns":1,"w":["Cost is highly relevant to the CFO, but leading exclusively with detailed financial projections may not address the legal, technical, and strategic concerns of the other stakeholders.","The technical architecture is particularly important to the CIO, but beginning with a detailed component list may overwhelm stakeholders who primarily need to understand the decision, rationale, risks, and business impact.","Different stakeholders have different responsibilities and concerns. A one-size-fits-all narrative may fail to address the specific information each decision-maker needs.","The strongest approach is to state the recommendation clearly at the beginning and then address the key concerns of each stakeholder. For example, the CFO may focus on cost and ROI, the General Counsel on legal risk and compliance, and the CIO on architecture, security, integration, and operational considerations.\nThis structure makes the recommendation decision-oriented, concise, and relevant to the entire steering committee."],"t":"Decision FIRST, then each stakeholder's worry.","k":["stakeholders","decision first"]},{"id":"ccar-p-13","d":"d3","x":"You are investigating an MCP server that fails on first launch but succeeds on subsequent runs. System permission dialogs appeared during the first launch.\nWhich response is most appropriate?","o":["Treat the first-run failure as a permanent fault, replace the MCP server, and do not verify whether subsequent runs succeed.","Reinstall the operating system to clear all permission state without first confirming whether the one- time permission grant caused the failure.","Recognize the first-run permission grant as the cause, document the expected behavior in onboarding guidance, and confirm that subsequent runs succeed.","Disable operating-system permission dialogs entirely, accept the resulting security implications, and proceed without confirming whether the failure recurs."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C. Recognize the first-run permission grant as the cause, document the expected behavior in onboarding guidance, and confirm that subsequent runs succeed.\n\nA) The fact that the MCP server succeeds on subsequent runs strongly suggests that the initial failure may be related to first-run permissions or authorization. Replacing the server without investigating would be unnecessary and could fail to address the actual cause.\n\nB) Reinstalling the operating system is an extreme and unnecessary troubleshooting step. The permission dialogs provide a much more relevant clue that should be investigated first.\n\nC) The appearance of system permission dialogs during the first launch is a strong indication that the initial failure was related to one-time operating-system authorization. The appropriate response is to document this expected first-run behavior and verify that the MCP server works normally afterward.\n\nD) Disabling operating-system permission controls unnecessarily weakens security protections. The better approach is to understand the permission requirement, document it, and verify successful subsequent operation.","ns":1,"w":["The fact that the MCP server succeeds on subsequent runs strongly suggests that the initial failure may be related to first-run permissions or authorization. Replacing the server without investigating would be unnecessary and could fail to address the actual cause.","Reinstalling the operating system is an extreme and unnecessary troubleshooting step. The permission dialogs provide a much more relevant clue that should be investigated first.","The appearance of system permission dialogs during the first launch is a strong indication that the initial failure was related to one-time operating-system authorization. The appropriate response is to document this expected first-run behavior and verify that the MCP server works normally afterward.","Disabling operating-system permission controls unnecessarily weakens security protections. The better approach is to understand the permission requirement, document it, and verify successful subsequent operation."],"t":"First-run fail + permission popup = expected. Document it.","k":["first-run permission","MCP server","onboarding"]},{"id":"ccar-p-14","d":"d6","x":"You are producing an architecture guide for a new deployment and must complete the planning steps before\ndrafting each section.\nWhich two steps must be completed BEFORE drafting each section of the guide? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution.","o":["Translate the guide into the supported regional languages for the candidate population.","Establish the document under version control with a defined review cadence and approver list.","Identify the audience and the questions the guide must answer for that audience.","Outline the guide sections covering the overview, components, contracts, flows, runbooks, and limitations.","Validate the guide with the implementation team and incorporate corrections."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Final Answer: C and D\n\nA) Translation is a later-stage activity. Before drafting, the priority is to establish who the guide is for and what information it needs to contain.\n\nB) Version control, review cadence, and approval processes are valuable for document governance, but they are not the primary content-planning steps required before drafting the sections.\n\nC) Before writing, you should understand the intended audience and their information needs. This ensures that each section is written at the appropriate technical level and addresses the questions stakeholders actually need answered.\n\nD) Creating an outline establishes the guide's structure and scope before detailed writing begins. It helps ensure important architecture topics are covered systematically and prevents unnecessary duplication or omissions.\n\nE) Validation with the implementation team is an important review and refinement step, but it happens after the relevant content has been drafted rather than before drafting each section.","ns":1,"w":["Translation is a later-stage activity. Before drafting, the priority is to establish who the guide is for and what information it needs to contain.","Version control, review cadence, and approval processes are valuable for document governance, but they are not the primary content-planning steps required before drafting the sections.","Before writing, you should understand the intended audience and their information needs. This ensures that each section is written at the appropriate technical level and addresses the questions stakeholders actually need answered.","Creating an outline establishes the guide's structure and scope before detailed writing begins. It helps ensure important architecture topics are covered systematically and prevents unnecessary duplication or omissions.","Validation with the implementation team is an important review and refinement step, but it happens after the relevant content has been drafted rather than before drafting each section."],"t":"Before writing: WHO reads + WHAT outline.","k":["audience","outline","architecture guide"]},{"id":"ccar-p-15","d":"d5","x":"You are responding to an adversarial input pattern in which users include text claiming admin authority and instructing the model to bypass safety restrictions.\nWhich combination of controls most effectively mitigates this attack pattern?","o":["Prompt-level instructions that treat user content as untrusted data, runtime classifiers that detect override attempts, scoped tool permissions that cannot be elevated by user content, and audit logging of attempts.","Granting users any privilege level they assert in their message content, on the assumption that cooperative behavior requires honoring self-declared authority without independent verification.","Trusting that the model will intrinsically recognize and reject all bypass attempts without prompt-level instructions, runtime classifiers, scoped permissions, or audit logging.","Removing all safety restrictions and guardrails to eliminate the attack surface that bypass attempts target, accepting that this makes the assistant unrestricted for all inputs."],"a":[0],"e":"Final Answer: A. Use layered prompt, runtime, permission, and audit controls.\n\nA) This approach provides defense in depth. User-provided claims of authority are treated as untrusted input, runtime controls can identify suspicious override attempts, and scoped permissions prevent the user from escalating privileges simply through prompt content. Audit logging also supports monitoring, investigation, and incident response.\n\nB) User-supplied text is not a reliable source of authorization. Allowing users to declare their own privileges creates a serious privilege-escalation risk.\n\nC) A model should not be treated as the sole security boundary. Multiple technical and procedural controls are needed because adversarial inputs can sometimes bypass model-level instructions.\n\nD) Removing safety controls does not mitigate the attack; it eliminates the protections that are intended to prevent unsafe behavior.","ns":1,"w":["This approach provides defense in depth. User-provided claims of authority are treated as untrusted input, runtime controls can identify suspicious override attempts, and scoped permissions prevent the user from escalating privileges simply through prompt content. Audit logging also supports monitoring, investigation, and incident response.","User-supplied text is not a reliable source of authorization. Allowing users to declare their own privileges creates a serious privilege-escalation risk.","A model should not be treated as the sole security boundary. Multiple technical and procedural controls are needed because adversarial inputs can sometimes bypass model-level instructions.","Removing safety controls does not mitigate the attack; it eliminates the protections that are intended to prevent unsafe behavior."],"t":"Claimed authority = untrusted. Layer prompt + classifier + scoped perms + audit.","k":["untrusted input","privilege escalation","layered controls"]},{"id":"ccar-p-19","d":"d5","x":"You are running a risk assessment on a planned Claude-based deployment and must complete the inventory steps before assessing threats against assets.\nWhich two steps must be completed BEFORE assessing threats against assets to estimate likelihood and impact?\n(Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution.","o":["Recommend mitigations and residual-risk acceptance for the risks that remain after analysis.","Recommend mitigations and residual-risk acceptance for risks that remain.","Enumerate the threat actors and attack vectors relevant to the deployment.","Document the assessment outcome with risks, mitigations, residual risk, and acceptance owners.","Identify the assets that the deployment touches, along with the sensitivity of each asset."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Final Answer: C and E\nC = Identify relevant threats and attack vectors\nE = Identify affected assets and their sensitivity\n\nA) Mitigations and residual-risk acceptance are later stages of the risk-assessment process. You first need to identify the assets and relevant threats before determining appropriate controls.\n\nB) This is also a post-analysis activity. Mitigation decisions depend on understanding the risks, their likelihood, their impact, and the controls already available.\n\nC) Identifying relevant threat actors and attack vectors is necessary before estimating risk. Understanding who could attack the system and how they might do so helps establish the likelihood and potential impact of threats against the identified assets.\n\nD) Documenting the final assessment is generally performed after the analysis has been completed. You need the risk findings and mitigation decisions before producing the final assessment record.\n\nE) Asset identification is a foundational step. Knowing which assets are involved and how sensitive they are provides the basis for evaluating the potential impact if those assets are compromised.","ns":1,"w":["Mitigations and residual-risk acceptance are later stages of the risk-assessment process. You first need to identify the assets and relevant threats before determining appropriate controls.","This is also a post-analysis activity. Mitigation decisions depend on understanding the risks, their likelihood, their impact, and the controls already available.","Identifying relevant threat actors and attack vectors is necessary before estimating risk. Understanding who could attack the system and how they might do so helps establish the likelihood and potential impact of threats against the identified assets.","Documenting the final assessment is generally performed after the analysis has been completed. You need the risk findings and mitigation decisions before producing the final assessment record.","Asset identification is a foundational step. Knowing which assets are involved and how sensitive they are provides the basis for evaluating the potential impact if those assets are compromised."],"t":"Inventory first: ASSETS + THREAT actors, then likelihood.","k":["risk assessment","assets","threat actors"]},{"id":"ccar-p-20","d":"d6","x":"A senior architect is managing stakeholder expectations for a Claude-based reporting assistant midway through development. Stakeholders have escalating concerns about response latency.\nWhich two actions most directly address stakeholder expectation alignment in this situation? (Select two.)","o":["Replace the current Claude model with a third-party model that may offer lower latency without evaluation.","Pause all development and reallocate engineering resources entirely to latency optimization.","Present measured p50 and p95 latency baselines against the agreed SLA thresholds so stakeholders have accurate data.","Revise the SLA definition collaboratively with stakeholders if current targets are not achievable given production constraints.","Communicate that latency concerns are a known LLM limitation and outside the architecture team's control."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Final Answer: C and D\nC = Establish objective latency baselines against the SLA\nD = Collaboratively revise the SLA when justified by production constraints\n\nA) Replacing the model without proper evaluation does not address stakeholder alignment. It could introduce new quality, security, compatibility, or performance risks. Model changes should be based on measured requirements and controlled evaluation.\n\nB) This is a broad project-management decision rather than a direct stakeholder-alignment action. It may also unnecessarily disrupt other development priorities without first establishing the actual performance gap.\n\nC) Providing objective performance measurements gives stakeholders a clear understanding of the current state. Comparing p50 and p95 latency with the agreed SLA helps distinguish actual performance gaps from perception and supports informed decisions.\n\nD) If the original SLA is unrealistic given validated production constraints, the appropriate approach is to review and revise it collaboratively with stakeholders. This creates shared expectations rather than silently accepting an unachievable target.\n\nE) This approach dismisses stakeholder concerns instead of addressing them. Architecture teams should provide measured evidence, options, and trade-offs rather than treating performance as completely outside their responsibility.","ns":1,"w":["Replacing the model without proper evaluation does not address stakeholder alignment. It could introduce new quality, security, compatibility, or performance risks. Model changes should be based on measured requirements and controlled evaluation.","This is a broad project-management decision rather than a direct stakeholder-alignment action. It may also unnecessarily disrupt other development priorities without first establishing the actual performance gap.","Providing objective performance measurements gives stakeholders a clear understanding of the current state. Comparing p50 and p95 latency with the agreed SLA helps distinguish actual performance gaps from perception and supports informed decisions.","If the original SLA is unrealistic given validated production constraints, the appropriate approach is to review and revise it collaboratively with stakeholders. This creates shared expectations rather than silently accepting an unachievable target.","This approach dismisses stakeholder concerns instead of addressing them. Architecture teams should provide measured evidence, options, and trade-offs rather than treating performance as completely outside their responsibility."],"t":"Show real NUMBERS vs SLA, renegotiate if needed.","k":["p95 latency","SLA","expectations"]},{"id":"ccar-p-21","d":"d5","x":"A Claude architect is designing a HIPAA-compliant pipeline that processes patient records.\nWhich two design decisions directly support HIPAA compliance requirements? (Select two.)","o":["Setting max_tokens to a low value to minimize the volume of text generated per request.","Selecting the highest-capability Claude model to maximize diagnostic accuracy.","Enforcing role-based access controls so that PH is retrievable only by authorized personnel.","Ensuring patient data is never included in training feedback loops sent to the model provider without a BAA in place.","Using streaming responses to reduce perceived latency for clinical users."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Final Answer: C and D\nC = Enforce role-based access to PHI\nD = Apply appropriate PHI data-handling and BAA safeguards\n\nA) Reducing the maximum number of generated tokens can help control cost and output length, but it does not directly address HIPAA requirements such as access control, protection of protected health information, or appropriate contractual safeguards.\n\nB) Model capability and HIPAA compliance are separate concerns. Choosing a more capable model does not by itself provide the privacy, access-control, contractual, or data-handling protections required for compliant processing.\n\nC) Role-based access control (RBAC) limits access to protected health information (PHI) based on authorized roles. Restricting PHI access to appropriate personnel supports the principle of controlled access required when handling sensitive healthcare information.\n\nD) Patient information requires appropriate privacy and contractual safeguards when processed by an external service provider. Ensuring PHI is not improperly used in provider training or feedback processes, and having the appropriate Business Associate Agreement (BAA) where required, directly supports HIPAA compliance.\n\nE) Streaming can improve the user experience and perceived response speed, but it does not directly establish HIPAA privacy or security compliance.","ns":1,"w":["Reducing the maximum number of generated tokens can help control cost and output length, but it does not directly address HIPAA requirements such as access control, protection of protected health information, or appropriate contractual safeguards.","Model capability and HIPAA compliance are separate concerns. Choosing a more capable model does not by itself provide the privacy, access-control, contractual, or data-handling protections required for compliant processing.","Role-based access control (RBAC) limits access to protected health information (PHI) based on authorized roles. Restricting PHI access to appropriate personnel supports the principle of controlled access required when handling sensitive healthcare information.","Patient information requires appropriate privacy and contractual safeguards when processed by an external service provider. Ensuring PHI is not improperly used in provider training or feedback processes, and having the appropriate Business Associate Agreement (BAA) where required, directly supports HIPAA compliance.","Streaming can improve the user experience and perceived response speed, but it does not directly establish HIPAA privacy or security compliance."],"t":"HIPAA = RBAC on PHI + BAA before data leaves.","k":["HIPAA","PHI","RBAC","BAA"]},{"id":"ccar-p-22","d":"d7","x":"You are preparing an operational runbook for a Claude-based service.\nWhich content is essential to include in the runbook?","o":["Dashboard and log references only, without alert definitions, triage steps, escalation paths, or rollback procedures for the on-call engineer to act on.","Common alerts and their triage steps, escalation paths, rollback procedures, and references to the relevant dashboards and logs.","Alert definitions and triage steps only, without escalation paths, rollback procedures, or references to dashboards and logs for on-call use.","Escalation paths and rollback procedures only, without alert definitions, triage steps, or dashboard references to guide initial incident response."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B. Common alerts, triage steps, escalation paths, rollback procedures, and dashboard/log references.\n\nA) Dashboards and logs are useful, but references alone do not tell the on-call engineer what the alert means, how to investigate it, who to contact, or how to recover the service.\n\nB) A strong operational runbook should provide actionable guidance for incident response. It should explain common alerts, initial triage, escalation procedures, rollback or recovery steps, and where to find the relevant monitoring information.\n\nC) Alert definitions and triage are important, but they are incomplete without escalation paths, recovery procedures, and monitoring references.\n\nD) Rollback and escalation procedures are valuable, but the on-call engineer also needs to know how to recognize and investigate the incident before escalating or rolling back.","ns":1,"w":["Dashboards and logs are useful, but references alone do not tell the on-call engineer what the alert means, how to investigate it, who to contact, or how to recover the service.","A strong operational runbook should provide actionable guidance for incident response. It should explain common alerts, initial triage, escalation procedures, rollback or recovery steps, and where to find the relevant monitoring information.","Alert definitions and triage are important, but they are incomplete without escalation paths, recovery procedures, and monitoring references.","Rollback and escalation procedures are valuable, but the on-call engineer also needs to know how to recognize and investigate the incident before escalating or rolling back."],"t":"Runbook = alerts + triage + escalation + rollback + dashboards.","k":["runbook","triage","rollback"]},{"id":"ccar-p-23","d":"d5","x":"A technical team is cataloguing risks specific to Claude's use in a document-grounded Q & A system.\nWhich two items represent failure modes intrinsic to LLM-based systems rather than generic software defects?\n(Select two.)","o":["An expired TLS certificate blocks outbound API calls to Claude.","A database connection timeout causes retrieval to return an empty result set.","The model refuses a legitimate query because surface features trigger an overly broad safety pattern.","A misconfigured load balancer routes requests to a deprecated API version.","The model generates a plausible-sounding answer unsupported by any retrieved document."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Final Answer: C and E\nC = Over-refusal of legitimate requests\nE = Plausible but unsupported generated answers\n\nA) An expired TLS certificate is a general infrastructure and security configuration problem. It can affect many types of applications and is not specific to LLM behavior.\n\nB) A database connection timeout is a generic integration or infrastructure failure. It is not an intrinsic failure mode of the language model itself.\n\nC) This is an LLM-specific failure mode. Safety systems can sometimes produce over-refusals, where a legitimate request is incorrectly classified as unsafe because of particular words or patterns in the input.\n\nD) This is a deployment and infrastructure configuration issue, not an intrinsic LLM failure mode.\n\nE) This is a classic hallucination/grounding failure. An LLM can generate an answer that sounds convincing even when the available source documents do not support the claim. This is particularly important in document-grounded Q&A systems.","ns":1,"w":["An expired TLS certificate is a general infrastructure and security configuration problem. It can affect many types of applications and is not specific to LLM behavior.","A database connection timeout is a generic integration or infrastructure failure. It is not an intrinsic failure mode of the language model itself.","This is an LLM-specific failure mode. Safety systems can sometimes produce over-refusals, where a legitimate request is incorrectly classified as unsafe because of particular words or patterns in the input.","This is a deployment and infrastructure configuration issue, not an intrinsic LLM failure mode.","This is a classic hallucination/grounding failure. An LLM can generate an answer that sounds convincing even when the available source documents do not support the claim. This is particularly important in document-grounded Q&A systems."],"t":"LLM-only bugs: OVER-REFUSAL + HALLUCINATION.","k":["over-refusal","hallucination","LLM failure modes"]},{"id":"ccar-p-26","d":"d6","x":"When communicating an architectural decision to a security and compliance reviewer, which content set is most aligned with that audience's primary concerns?","o":["Feature delivery schedule, roadmap dependencies, and scope boundaries for the product manager.","Implementation timeline, component interfaces, and deployment sequence for the engineering team.","Threat model, control mappings, residual-risk acceptance criteria, and audit traceability.","Decision rationale, business outcomes, and high-level risk summary for executive review."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C. Threat model, control mappings, residual-risk acceptance criteria, and audit traceability.\n\nA) This information is primarily relevant to a product manager. While delivery timelines and scope can matter to security teams, they do not directly address the reviewer's main concerns around security controls, threats, compliance, and auditability.\n\nB) These details are mainly intended for the engineering implementation team. They explain how the system will be built and deployed but do not provide sufficient evidence about security risks and compliance controls.\n\nC) This content directly addresses the primary concerns of security and compliance reviewers. They need to understand the threats being considered, which controls mitigate those threats, what residual risks remain, who accepts those risks, and how the decisions can be demonstrated during an audit.\n\nD) These elements are more appropriate for an executive audience. Executives generally need the decision, business value, and high-level risks rather than detailed control mappings and audit evidence.","ns":1,"w":["This information is primarily relevant to a product manager. While delivery timelines and scope can matter to security teams, they do not directly address the reviewer's main concerns around security controls, threats, compliance, and auditability.","These details are mainly intended for the engineering implementation team. They explain how the system will be built and deployed but do not provide sufficient evidence about security risks and compliance controls.","This content directly addresses the primary concerns of security and compliance reviewers. They need to understand the threats being considered, which controls mitigate those threats, what residual risks remain, who accepts those risks, and how the decisions can be demonstrated during an audit.","These elements are more appropriate for an executive audience. Executives generally need the decision, business value, and high-level risks rather than detailed control mappings and audit evidence."],"t":"Security audience = THREATS + CONTROLS + RESIDUAL risk + AUDIT.","k":["threat model","control mapping","audit"]},{"id":"ccar-p-27","d":"d5","x":"You are integrating Claude Code into a workflow that runs against a production database.\nWhich guardrail design most directly preserves safety on data-modifying operations?","o":["Allow Claude Code to write directly to the production database without subagent scoping, read-only credential defaults, or human confirmation gates on data-modifying operations.","Configure the database MCP server with a fully privileged credential that can perform any read or write operation, and allow all operations to proceed without explicit human confirmation.","Disable all logging and auditing on database operations through the MCP server to reduce alert noise, removing the observability needed to detect unintended data modifications.","Configure the database MCP server with a read-only credential by default, restrict the subagent's tool list to read-only operations, and require explicit human confirmation on any operation that would modify data."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D. Use read-only defaults, restrict tool permissions, and require human confirmation for data modifications.\n\nA) This design provides excessive privileges and removes important safety controls. A production database should not be exposed to unrestricted automated write operations because an unintended action could modify or delete critical data.\n\nB) A fully privileged credential violates the principle of least privilege. Removing confirmation for write operations also eliminates an important human control over potentially irreversible changes.\n\nC) Disabling logging removes valuable auditability and observability. Production database operations should generally be traceable so unexpected or unauthorized changes can be investigated.\n\nD) This provides defense in depth. Read-only credentials establish a safe default, tool scoping limits what the subagent can access, and explicit human confirmation creates an additional control before potentially destructive or consequential operations are performed.","ns":1,"w":["This design provides excessive privileges and removes important safety controls. A production database should not be exposed to unrestricted automated write operations because an unintended action could modify or delete critical data.","A fully privileged credential violates the principle of least privilege. Removing confirmation for write operations also eliminates an important human control over potentially irreversible changes.","Disabling logging removes valuable auditability and observability. Production database operations should generally be traceable so unexpected or unauthorized changes can be investigated.","This provides defense in depth. Read-only credentials establish a safe default, tool scoping limits what the subagent can access, and explicit human confirmation creates an additional control before potentially destructive or consequential operations are performed."],"t":"Read-only by default + scoped tools + human OK for writes.","k":["read-only","human confirmation","production database"]},{"id":"ccar-p-28","d":"d6","x":"You are identifying signals that a deployment should re-enter design rather than continue iterating in place.\nWhich signal most directly indicates the need for a new design cycle?","o":["A runbook step requires a clarification edit to improve on-call guidance accuracy, which can be handled as a documentation update without changes to component responsibilities or core contracts.","A dashboard alert threshold needs a small numerical adjustment to reduce false-positive noise, which can be handled as an operational configuration change without a new design cycle.","A minor copy edit is requested in a customer-facing string within the existing Ul, which can be handled as a localized content change without altering component responsibilities or contracts.","The system's current architecture cannot meet the new requirements without changes to component responsibilities or core contracts."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D. The current architecture cannot meet the new requirements without changing component responsibilities or core contracts.\n\nA) This is a documentation-level change. It does not alter the system's architecture, component responsibilities, or contracts, so a new design cycle is unnecessary.\n\nB) A small alert-threshold adjustment is an operational tuning change. It can be made without revisiting the underlying architecture.\n\nC) A copy change is a localized content modification and does not require architectural redesign when the existing components and contracts remain unchanged.\n\nD) This is the clearest signal that the system needs to re-enter the design cycle. Changes to component responsibilities or core contracts affect fundamental architectural decisions and should be evaluated through a new design process rather than handled as routine iteration.","ns":1,"w":["This is a documentation-level change. It does not alter the system's architecture, component responsibilities, or contracts, so a new design cycle is unnecessary.","A small alert-threshold adjustment is an operational tuning change. It can be made without revisiting the underlying architecture.","A copy change is a localized content modification and does not require architectural redesign when the existing components and contracts remain unchanged.","This is the clearest signal that the system needs to re-enter the design cycle. Changes to component responsibilities or core contracts affect fundamental architectural decisions and should be evaluated through a new design process rather than handled as routine iteration."],"t":"Must change RESPONSIBILITIES or CONTRACTS = redesign.","k":["redesign trigger","core contracts"]},{"id":"ccar-p-31","d":"d4","x":"An operations engineer reports that a Claude-based pipeline began returning malformed JSON responses after a scheduled maintenance window, causing downstream processing failures.\nWhich two investigative steps most directly isolate the root cause? (Select two.)","o":["Clear the prompt cache and resubmit all pending requests to eliminate stale cached prefixes.","Compare the current system prompt and output schema configuration against the last known good version from before the maintenance window.","Switch to a different Claude model tier to rule out provider-side changes as a contributing factor.","Increase the max_tokens limit to determine whether output truncation is causing incomplete JSON structures.","Replay a set of premaintenance requests against the current configuration and inspect the raw model output before downstream parsing."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Final Answer: B and E\nB = Compare current and last known-good configuration\nE = Replay known requests and inspect raw output before parsing\n\nA) There is no evidence that stale cached prefixes caused the problem. Clearing the cache before establishing a baseline could introduce unnecessary changes and make the investigation harder to isolate.\n\nB) The problem started immediately after a maintenance window, so comparing the current configuration with the last known good configuration is an effective way to identify changes that may have introduced the malformed JSON behavior.\n\nC) Changing the model introduces another variable into the investigation. The existing configuration should first be examined to determine whether a maintenance-related configuration change caused the issue.\n\nD) Output truncation can cause malformed JSON, but there is no evidence that token limits are the cause here. Increasing max_tokens would be a configuration change before the underlying cause is established.\n\nE) Replaying known premaintenance requests provides a controlled way to compare behavior. Inspecting the raw model output before downstream parsing helps determine whether Claude is producing malformed JSON or whether the problem occurs later in the pipeline.","ns":1,"w":["There is no evidence that stale cached prefixes caused the problem. Clearing the cache before establishing a baseline could introduce unnecessary changes and make the investigation harder to isolate.","The problem started immediately after a maintenance window, so comparing the current configuration with the last known good configuration is an effective way to identify changes that may have introduced the malformed JSON behavior.","Changing the model introduces another variable into the investigation. The existing configuration should first be examined to determine whether a maintenance-related configuration change caused the issue.","Output truncation can cause malformed JSON, but there is no evidence that token limits are the cause here. Increasing max_tokens would be a configuration change before the underlying cause is established.","Replaying known premaintenance requests provides a controlled way to compare behavior. Inspecting the raw model output before downstream parsing helps determine whether Claude is producing malformed JSON or whether the problem occurs later in the pipeline."],"t":"Broke after change? DIFF the config + REPLAY old requests.","k":["last known good","replay","malformed JSON"]},{"id":"ccar-p-32","d":"d7","x":"You are compiling a diagnostic toolkit for Claude Code operational issues.\nWhich two diagnostic actions belong in the toolkit? (Select two.)\nEach correct answer presents a complete solution.","o":["Increase the model sampling temperature so that intermittent issues surface more frequently for analysis.","File a support ticket with vendor support before any local reproduction or evidence collection.","Reproduce the issue with a minimal reproduction case that isolates one variable at a time.","Roll back to the previous Claude Code version immediately to confirm whether the issue is version specific.","List the configured Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers and inspect server status to identify connection failures."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Final Answer: C and E\nC = Reproduce the issue using a minimal reproduction case.\nE = Inspect configured MCP servers and their connection status.\n\nA) Changing the sampling temperature introduces another variable and can make the system's behavior less consistent. It is not a reliable first-line diagnostic technique for isolating Claude Code operational issues.\n\nB) Support escalation can be appropriate when internal investigation is insufficient, but you should first reproduce the issue and collect relevant evidence whenever possible. This gives the support team useful diagnostic information.\n\nC) A minimal reproduction case is a fundamental troubleshooting technique. By reducing the workflow and changing one variable at a time, you can determine which component or configuration is responsible for the failure.\n\nD) An immediate rollback changes the environment before the issue has been properly characterized. Version comparison can be useful later, but first you should collect evidence and isolate the problem.\n\nE) MCP servers are important dependencies in Claude Code workflows. Checking the configured servers and their status can quickly identify connection, availability, or configuration problems that may be causing the operational issue.","ns":1,"w":["Changing the sampling temperature introduces another variable and can make the system's behavior less consistent. It is not a reliable first-line diagnostic technique for isolating Claude Code operational issues.","Support escalation can be appropriate when internal investigation is insufficient, but you should first reproduce the issue and collect relevant evidence whenever possible. This gives the support team useful diagnostic information.","A minimal reproduction case is a fundamental troubleshooting technique. By reducing the workflow and changing one variable at a time, you can determine which component or configuration is responsible for the failure.","An immediate rollback changes the environment before the issue has been properly characterized. Version comparison can be useful later, but first you should collect evidence and isolate the problem.","MCP servers are important dependencies in Claude Code workflows. Checking the configured servers and their status can quickly identify connection, availability, or configuration problems that may be causing the operational issue."],"t":"Minimal REPRO + check MCP server STATUS.","k":["minimal reproduction","MCP status"]},{"id":"ccar-p-33","d":"d5","x":"You are assessing data-exfiltration risk in a Claude-based assistant that has tools for both internal-document retrieval and outbound HTTP calls.\nWhich scenario most directly indicates a data-exfiltration risk?","o":["The outbound HTTP tool returns a 200 response when the assistant calls an allow-listed domain to fulfil a user-initiated data-lookup request.","The user submits a routine question and receives a routine answer.","Adversarial content in a retrieved document instructs the model to call the outbound HTTP tool and send sensitive content to an attacker-controlled URL.","The internal-document retrieval tool returns a document that the user is authorized to view."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C\nC = Adversarial retrieved content attempts to send sensitive data to an attacker-controlled URL.\n\nA) This describes a normal, authorized tool interaction. The destination is allow-listed, and the request was initiated by the user for a legitimate purpose. There is no indication that sensitive information is being sent to an unauthorized destination.\n\nB) A routine question producing a routine answer does not indicate data exfiltration. There is no evidence of unauthorized data access or transmission.\n\nC) This is a direct data-exfiltration scenario. The retrieved document contains adversarial instructions attempting to manipulate the model into sending sensitive information to an attacker-controlled destination. This is a classic example of indirect prompt injection combined with an outbound tool, creating a serious security risk.\n\nD) The user is authorized to access the document, so the retrieval itself does not indicate unauthorized disclosure or exfiltration.","ns":1,"w":["This describes a normal, authorized tool interaction. The destination is allow-listed, and the request was initiated by the user for a legitimate purpose. There is no indication that sensitive information is being sent to an unauthorized destination.","A routine question producing a routine answer does not indicate data exfiltration. There is no evidence of unauthorized data access or transmission.","This is a direct data-exfiltration scenario. The retrieved document contains adversarial instructions attempting to manipulate the model into sending sensitive information to an attacker-controlled destination. This is a classic example of indirect prompt injection combined with an outbound tool, creating a serious security risk.","The user is authorized to access the document, so the retrieval itself does not indicate unauthorized disclosure or exfiltration."],"t":"Retrieval + outbound tool + hidden instruction = EXFILTRATION.","k":["data exfiltration","indirect prompt injection"]},{"id":"ccar-p-34","d":"d4","x":"After a prompt-template update, several previously passing test cases now produce unexpected outputs.\nWhich test type is specifically designed to detect this category of failure?","o":["Integration tests that validate cross-component pipeline behavior.","Adversarial tests that probe for prompt-injection vulnerabilities.","Regression tests scored against a stable reference set of known-good behavior.","Smoke tests that confirm high-level system availability after the change."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C\nC = Regression tests against a stable reference set of known-good behavior.\n\nA) Integration tests focus on whether multiple components work correctly together. They are useful for detecting interface and integration problems, but this scenario specifically concerns a change causing previously passing cases to behave differently.\n\nB) Adversarial tests are designed to identify security weaknesses and attack behaviors, such as prompt injection. They are not specifically intended to detect regressions caused by a prompt-template change.\n\nC) Regression testing compares the current behavior against a stable set of previously validated results. If a prompt-template update causes previously passing test cases to produce unexpected outputs, regression tests can identify those unintended changes.\n\nD) Smoke tests primarily verify that the system's basic functionality and availability remain intact after a change. They generally do not provide detailed comparison against previously known-good outputs.","ns":1,"w":["Integration tests focus on whether multiple components work correctly together. They are useful for detecting interface and integration problems, but this scenario specifically concerns a change causing previously passing cases to behave differently.","Adversarial tests are designed to identify security weaknesses and attack behaviors, such as prompt injection. They are not specifically intended to detect regressions caused by a prompt-template change.","Regression testing compares the current behavior against a stable set of previously validated results. If a prompt-template update causes previously passing test cases to produce unexpected outputs, regression tests can identify those unintended changes.","Smoke tests primarily verify that the system's basic functionality and availability remain intact after a change. They generally do not provide detailed comparison against previously known-good outputs."],"t":"Used to pass, now fails = REGRESSION test.","k":["regression tests","reference set"]},{"id":"ccar-p-36","d":"d6","x":"You are designing a feedback session for a deployment in flight.\nWhich structure best supports productive stakeholder feedback?","o":["Hold an open-ended meeting with no pre-distributed agenda or artifacts, and rely on participants' memory to carry decisions and follow-up owners forward.","Define a focused agenda, share the artifacts in advance, capture decisions and follow-ups in writing, and confirm action owners and dates.","Distribute artifacts at the session start rather than in advance, so stakeholders review materials in real timewithout preparation before the discussion begins.","End the session without recording decisions, follow-up items, action owners, or dates, relying on participant memory to carry the session's outcomes forward."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B\nB = Focused agenda + advance artifacts + documented decisions + clear owners and dates.\n\nA) An unstructured meeting makes it difficult to keep the discussion focused and can lead to unclear decisions and missing follow-up actions. Relying on memory also increases the chance that important commitments will be lost.\n\nB) This creates a structured and accountable feedback process. Sharing materials beforehand gives stakeholders time to prepare, while documenting decisions, owners, and deadlines ensures that feedback turns into clear, trackable actions.\n\nC) Providing materials only at the start leaves stakeholders with insufficient preparation time. This can reduce the quality of discussion and make it harder to reach informed decisions efficiently.\n\nD) Without written decisions and assigned owners and dates, accountability becomes unclear. Effective stakeholder feedback should produce documented, actionable outcomes.","ns":1,"w":["An unstructured meeting makes it difficult to keep the discussion focused and can lead to unclear decisions and missing follow-up actions. Relying on memory also increases the chance that important commitments will be lost.","This creates a structured and accountable feedback process. Sharing materials beforehand gives stakeholders time to prepare, while documenting decisions, owners, and deadlines ensures that feedback turns into clear, trackable actions.","Providing materials only at the start leaves stakeholders with insufficient preparation time. This can reduce the quality of discussion and make it harder to reach informed decisions efficiently.","Without written decisions and assigned owners and dates, accountability becomes unclear. Effective stakeholder feedback should produce documented, actionable outcomes."],"t":"Agenda + pre-reads + written decisions + owners/dates.","k":["feedback session","action owners"]},{"id":"ccar-p-38","d":"d6","x":"You are an architect supporting the iteration phase of a deployed Claude-based system.\nWhich activity most directly fits this phase?","o":["Rebuild the entire system architecture from scratch at the start of every iteration cycle regardless of what production telemetry, evaluations, and stakeholder feedback indicate is needed.","Stop measuring production outcomes once the deployment has successfully launched, treating the go-live milestone as the end of the evaluation and iteration cycle.","Discard the evaluation framework and reference set once the deployment is in production, accepting that future iterations will have no structured basis for measuring the impact of changes.","Review production telemetry, sampled output evaluations, and stakeholder feedback to identify the highest-impact change for the next cycle, then plan the change against the evaluation framework."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D\nD = Use production telemetry, evaluations, and stakeholder feedback to identify and plan the highest-impact improvement.\n\nA) Iteration does not mean rebuilding the entire system every cycle. Changes should be evidence-driven, based on production results, evaluations, and stakeholder feedback.\n\nB) Deployment is not the end of evaluation. Production monitoring and ongoing evaluation are essential for identifying performance issues, quality changes, and opportunities for improvement.\n\nC) The evaluation framework and reference set provide a consistent baseline for measuring whether changes improve or degrade the system. They should be maintained throughout iteration.\n\nD) This directly reflects an evidence-driven iteration cycle. Production telemetry, output evaluations, and stakeholder feedback reveal where improvements are most valuable. The proposed change can then be evaluated against the existing framework to measure its impact.","ns":1,"w":["Iteration does not mean rebuilding the entire system every cycle. Changes should be evidence-driven, based on production results, evaluations, and stakeholder feedback.","Deployment is not the end of evaluation. Production monitoring and ongoing evaluation are essential for identifying performance issues, quality changes, and opportunities for improvement.","The evaluation framework and reference set provide a consistent baseline for measuring whether changes improve or degrade the system. They should be maintained throughout iteration.","This directly reflects an evidence-driven iteration cycle. Production telemetry, output evaluations, and stakeholder feedback reveal where improvements are most valuable. The proposed change can then be evaluated against the existing framework to measure its impact."],"t":"Iterate on EVIDENCE: telemetry + evals + feedback.","k":["iteration","telemetry","evaluation framework"]},{"id":"ccar-p-39","d":"d7","x":"You are explaining the precedence of Claude Code configuration scopes to the team.\nWhich precedence ordering, from highest to lowest, is correct?","o":["Local # managed # user # command-line arguments # project","Project # user # managed # local # command-line arguments","Managed # command-line arguments # local # project # user","User # project # local # command-line arguments # managed"],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C\nC = Managed → Command-line arguments → Local → Project → User\n\nA) This ordering places local configuration above managed and command-line settings. It does not reflect the intended precedence hierarchy for Claude Code configuration.\n\nB) Project configuration is not the highest-precedence scope in this ordering. This option also places command-line arguments too low.\n\nC) This ordering correctly places the scopes from highest to lowest precedence:\nManaged → Command-line arguments → Local → Project → User\nThis precedence determines which configuration takes effect when settings from multiple scopes apply to the same behavior.\n\nD) This reverses the hierarchy by placing user scope above managed configuration and puts managed configuration at the lowest level.","ns":1,"w":["This ordering places local configuration above managed and command-line settings. It does not reflect the intended precedence hierarchy for Claude Code configuration.","Project configuration is not the highest-precedence scope in this ordering. This option also places command-line arguments too low.","This ordering correctly places the scopes from highest to lowest precedence:\nManaged → Command-line arguments → Local → Project → User\nThis precedence determines which configuration takes effect when settings from multiple scopes apply to the same behavior.","This reverses the hierarchy by placing user scope above managed configuration and puts managed configuration at the lowest level."],"t":"M-C-L-P-U: 'My Company Locks Personal Use'.","k":["scope precedence","managed","user"]},{"id":"ccar-p-42","d":"d7","x":"You are integrating Claude Code into the team's pull-request workflow. The team wants Al-assisted review without removing human approval.\nWhich integration design best fits this requirement?","o":["Claude Code reviews the pull request and posts a structured analysis as a comment, while a human reviewer etains the approval decision under the existing branch-protection rules","Claude Code merges every pull request automatically after completing its analysis, bypassing human approval and the existing branch-protection rules.","Claude Code disables all existing branch-protection rules to streamline the merge process, removing human approval as a required gate.","Claude Code silently deletes pull requests it assesses as low quality without posting a comment or notifying the author."],"a":[0],"e":"Final Answer: A\nA = Claude provides structured AI-assisted review while human reviewers retain the final approval decision.\n\nA) This design keeps Claude Code in an assistive role while preserving human accountability. Claude can analyze the pull request and provide useful feedback, but the human reviewer remains responsible for the final approval decision. Existing branch-protection rules also maintain an important safety and governance control.\n\nB) Automatic merging removes the required human approval gate and bypasses established branch protections. This does not meet the team's requirement for AI assistance while retaining human oversight.\n\nC) Disabling branch protection weakens the team's existing security and governance controls. It also directly conflicts with the requirement to retain human approval.\n\nD) Deleting pull requests without human review or notification creates an unaccountable and potentially destructive workflow. Claude should assist reviewers rather than independently take irreversible actions.","ns":1,"w":["This design keeps Claude Code in an assistive role while preserving human accountability. Claude can analyze the pull request and provide useful feedback, but the human reviewer remains responsible for the final approval decision. Existing branch-protection rules also maintain an important safety and governance control.","Automatic merging removes the required human approval gate and bypasses established branch protections. This does not meet the team's requirement for AI assistance while retaining human oversight.","Disabling branch protection weakens the team's existing security and governance controls. It also directly conflicts with the requirement to retain human approval.","Deleting pull requests without human review or notification creates an unaccountable and potentially destructive workflow. Claude should assist reviewers rather than independently take irreversible actions."],"t":"Claude COMMENTS, human APPROVES.","k":["PR review","human approval","branch protection"]},{"id":"ccar-p-45","d":"d4","x":"A Claude architect at a health services organization is defining evaluation metrics for a clinical-summary pipeline. The pipeline must remain within a per-query cost ceiling and must never surface patient data to unauthorized roles.\nWhich two metrics directly address these requirements? (Select two.)","o":["BLEU score computed against a human-annotated reference summary set","Role-based access-control enforcement rate measured on a red-team dataset","Throughput measured as successful requests processed per minute","Per-query token cost measured against the defined cost ceiling","Response latency at the 95th percentile across a one-week sample window"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Final Answer: B and D\nB = Measure role-based access-control enforcement.\nD = Measure per-query token cost against the defined ceiling.\n\nA) BLEU measures text similarity between generated summaries and reference summaries. It can be useful for evaluating output quality, but it does not directly measure access-control enforcement or whether the pipeline stays within its cost ceiling.\n\nB) This directly addresses the requirement that unauthorized roles must not receive patient data. Testing role-based access controls with adversarial or red-team cases can measure whether the system consistently prevents unauthorized data exposure.\n\nC) Throughput measures system capacity and performance. It does not directly indicate whether patient data is protected from unauthorized users or whether individual requests remain within the specified cost ceiling.\n\nD) This directly measures whether each query remains within the required cost constraint. Comparing actual token cost against the defined ceiling provides a clear operational metric for cost compliance.\n\nE) p95 latency is a useful performance metric, but the stated requirements concern cost control and authorization, not response speed.","ns":1,"w":["BLEU measures text similarity between generated summaries and reference summaries. It can be useful for evaluating output quality, but it does not directly measure access-control enforcement or whether the pipeline stays within its cost ceiling.","This directly addresses the requirement that unauthorized roles must not receive patient data. Testing role-based access controls with adversarial or red-team cases can measure whether the system consistently prevents unauthorized data exposure.","Throughput measures system capacity and performance. It does not directly indicate whether patient data is protected from unauthorized users or whether individual requests remain within the specified cost ceiling.","This directly measures whether each query remains within the required cost constraint. Comparing actual token cost against the defined ceiling provides a clear operational metric for cost compliance.","p95 latency is a useful performance metric, but the stated requirements concern cost control and authorization, not response speed."],"t":"Requirement = metric: privacy -> RBAC rate, budget -> token cost.","k":["RBAC enforcement","per-query cost","metrics"]},{"id":"ccar-p-47","d":"d1","x":"You are defining when to introduce a project subagent versus relying on Claude Code's general capabilities.\nWhich scenario most directly justifies a dedicated subagent?","o":["The team has a recurring specialized task, such as database schema review, that requires a focused system prompt, narrow tool permissions, and a specific model selection across many sessions.","The team has a one-time ad hoc question that will not recur and does not require a focused system prompt, narrow tool permissions, or dedicated model selection.","The team has no recurring specialized tasks and uses Claude Code only for isolated general-purpose work that does not justify a dedicated system prompt or tool scope.","The team wants every Claude Code interaction to use the same generic system prompt with no task- specific specialization, narrow tool permissions, or dedicated model selection."],"a":[0],"e":"Final Answer: A\nA = Use a dedicated subagent for recurring specialized work that benefits from focused instructions, narrow permissions, and task-specific model selection.\n\nA) A dedicated subagent is appropriate when a task is recurring, specialized, and requires consistent configuration. A focused system prompt, restricted tool permissions, and task-appropriate model selection allow the subagent to operate within a clearly defined scope while reducing unnecessary access.\n\nB) A one-time, general-purpose task does not justify the additional configuration and maintenance overhead of a dedicated subagent. Claude Code's general capabilities are sufficient.\n\nC) Without a recurring specialized workload, there is little reason to create a dedicated subagent. The general Claude Code environment is more appropriate.\n\nD) A generic configuration without task specialization does not provide the main benefits of a subagent. Dedicated subagents are valuable precisely when they can provide focused instructions, scoped permissions, and task-specific behavior.","ns":1,"w":["A dedicated subagent is appropriate when a task is recurring, specialized, and requires consistent configuration. A focused system prompt, restricted tool permissions, and task-appropriate model selection allow the subagent to operate within a clearly defined scope while reducing unnecessary access.","A one-time, general-purpose task does not justify the additional configuration and maintenance overhead of a dedicated subagent. Claude Code's general capabilities are sufficient.","Without a recurring specialized workload, there is little reason to create a dedicated subagent. The general Claude Code environment is more appropriate.","A generic configuration without task specialization does not provide the main benefits of a subagent. Dedicated subagents are valuable precisely when they can provide focused instructions, scoped permissions, and task-specific behavior."],"t":"Recurring + specialized + scoped = SUBAGENT.","k":["subagent","specialized task","tool scoping"]},{"id":"ccar-p-49","d":"d5","x":"During an architectural review, the security team identifies a risk that adversarial content injected into retrieved documents could manipulate the model's behavior.\nWhich mitigation most directly addresses this threat?","o":["Treat all retrieved content as untrusted input and apply input classifiers with output validation.","Require citations for each claim and constrain responses to source-supported content.","Restrict outbound tool calls to an approved destination allow-list.","Score outputs against a stable adversarial evaluation set on each model-version change."],"a":[0],"e":"Final Answer: A\nA = Treat retrieved content as untrusted input, apply input classification, and validate outputs.\n\nA) Retrieved documents should be treated as untrusted data, because they may contain malicious instructions designed to manipulate the model. Input classification can help identify suspicious content, while output validation provides an additional layer of protection before the model's response is used downstream.\n\nB) These controls are primarily aimed at grounding and hallucination risks. They can improve traceability and factual support, but they do not directly prevent adversarial instructions inside retrieved content from influencing the model.\n\nC) An allow-list is an effective control against unauthorized outbound communication and data exfiltration, but it does not directly address the initial prompt-injection threat within retrieved documents.\n\nD) Adversarial evaluation is useful for detecting regressions and measuring security behavior, especially after model changes. However, it is an evaluation mechanism rather than the most direct runtime mitigation for malicious content in retrieved documents.","ns":1,"w":["Retrieved documents should be treated as untrusted data, because they may contain malicious instructions designed to manipulate the model. Input classification can help identify suspicious content, while output validation provides an additional layer of protection before the model's response is used downstream.","These controls are primarily aimed at grounding and hallucination risks. They can improve traceability and factual support, but they do not directly prevent adversarial instructions inside retrieved content from influencing the model.","An allow-list is an effective control against unauthorized outbound communication and data exfiltration, but it does not directly address the initial prompt-injection threat within retrieved documents.","Adversarial evaluation is useful for detecting regressions and measuring security behavior, especially after model changes. However, it is an evaluation mechanism rather than the most direct runtime mitigation for malicious content in retrieved documents."],"t":"Retrieved text = UNTRUSTED. Classify in, validate out.","k":["untrusted content","input classifier","output validation"]},{"id":"ccar-p-51","d":"d5","x":"The compliance team at a firm has approved a Claude Skill that generates client-facing investment summaries.\nThe Skill includes the firm's required disclaimers and prohibited-language list. A product manager has asked whether additional guardrails are needed at the application layer or whether the Skill alone is sufficient.\nWhich two guardrail responsibilities should remain at the application layer rather than the Skill? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents part of the solution.","o":["Log every generated summary to the firm's compliance audit trail for retention.","Verify the requesting user is authorized to generate investment summaries at all.","Format output sections according to the firm's standardized house style guidelines.","Apply the disclaimer template that the compliance team has standardized firm-wide.","Apply the prohibited-language list that the compliance team maintains and updates."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Final Answer: A and B\nA = Maintain the compliance audit trail at the application layer.\nB = Enforce user authorization at the application layer.\n\nA) Audit logging is an application-level responsibility because it concerns system-wide observability, compliance records, retention, and traceability. A Skill can provide procedural guidance, but the application should enforce and record the actual audit trail.\n\nB) Authorization is an access-control responsibility. The application must independently verify the user's identity and permissions before allowing the operation. A Skill should not be relied upon to enforce whether a user is authorized to perform the task.\n\nC) Formatting and presentation instructions are well suited to the Skill, particularly when they are part of the standardized procedure for producing investment summaries.\n\nD) The disclaimer is explicitly part of the approved Skill's compliance procedure. Therefore, it is appropriate for the Skill to provide this instruction.\n\nE) The prohibited-language list is already part of the compliance-owned Skill. Maintaining and applying this procedural guidance is an appropriate responsibility for the Skill.","ns":1,"w":["Audit logging is an application-level responsibility because it concerns system-wide observability, compliance records, retention, and traceability. A Skill can provide procedural guidance, but the application should enforce and record the actual audit trail.","Authorization is an access-control responsibility. The application must independently verify the user's identity and permissions before allowing the operation. A Skill should not be relied upon to enforce whether a user is authorized to perform the task.","Formatting and presentation instructions are well suited to the Skill, particularly when they are part of the standardized procedure for producing investment summaries.","The disclaimer is explicitly part of the approved Skill's compliance procedure. Therefore, it is appropriate for the Skill to provide this instruction.","The prohibited-language list is already part of the compliance-owned Skill. Maintaining and applying this procedural guidance is an appropriate responsibility for the Skill."],"t":"App layer keeps AUDIT + AUTHZ. Skill keeps content rules.","k":["application layer","audit trail","authorization"]},{"id":"ccar-p-52","d":"d6","x":"You are defining an SLA for a Claude-based assistant.\nWhich SLA definition is most operationally meaningful?","o":["A target tied to a stakeholder sentiment measure such as \"the team feels satisfied,\" which cannot be measured objectively or used to trigger a documented breach response.","A measurable target with a defined metric, threshold, evaluation window, and consequence for breach- for example,\"p95 per-request latency under 800 ms over a 28-day window.\"","A qualitative commitment such as \"the system will be fast and reliable,\" which names no metric, threshold, or evaluation window.","A target without a defined metric, threshold, evaluation window, or breach consequence."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B\nB = Define a measurable metric, threshold, evaluation window, and breach consequence.\n\nA) An SLA should be objective and measurable. Stakeholder sentiment alone does not provide a precise metric, threshold, or defined method for determining whether the SLA has been breached.\n\nB) This is a strong SLA because it clearly defines:\n\n• Metric: p95 per-request latency\n\n• Threshold: under 800 ms\n\n• Evaluation window: 28 days\n\n• Breach condition/consequence: a defined response when the target is not met\n\nThis makes the SLA objective, measurable, and operationally actionable.\n\nC) “Fast and reliable” is too vague to function as an operational SLA. There is no measurable definition of how fast, how reliable, or over what period performance should be evaluated.\n\nD) Without measurable criteria and a defined evaluation period, stakeholders cannot objectively determine whether the service has met or breached the SLA.","ns":1,"w":["An SLA should be objective and measurable. Stakeholder sentiment alone does not provide a precise metric, threshold, or defined method for determining whether the SLA has been breached.","This is a strong SLA because it clearly defines:\n\n• Metric: p95 per-request latency\n\n• Threshold: under 800 ms\n\n• Evaluation window: 28 days\n\n• Breach condition/consequence: a defined response when the target is not met\n\nThis makes the SLA objective, measurable, and operationally actionable.","“Fast and reliable” is too vague to function as an operational SLA. There is no measurable definition of how fast, how reliable, or over what period performance should be evaluated.","Without measurable criteria and a defined evaluation period, stakeholders cannot objectively determine whether the service has met or breached the SLA."],"t":"SLA = METRIC + THRESHOLD + WINDOW + CONSEQUENCE.","k":["SLA","p95","measurable"]},{"id":"ccar-p-53","d":"d6","x":"You are transitioning a Claude-based deployment from design into implementation.\nWhich handoff package most directly supports a clean transition?","o":["The most recent set of design presentation slides without component-level diagrams, interface contracts, an evaluation framework with a reference set, runbooks, or a known-limitations register.","A verbal walkthrough conducted on the day of handoff with no written architecture overview, ADRS, omponent contracts, evaluation framework, runbooks, playbook, or known limitations.","Architecture overview, ADRs, component contracts, evaluation framework with reference set, runbooks, on-call playbook, and known limitations.","Source code alone with no integrating architecture overview, ADRs, component contracts, evaluation amework, runbooks, on-call playbook, or known-limitations register to support the delivery team."],"a":[2],"e":"Final Answer: C\nC = Architecture overview + ADRs + component contracts + evaluation framework + runbooks + on-call playbook + known limitations.\n\nA) Presentation slides alone rarely provide enough implementation detail. The delivery team needs architecture details, contracts, evaluation criteria, operational procedures, and known limitations to implement and operate the system correctly.\n\nB) A verbal walkthrough can supplement documentation, but it should not replace it. Without written artifacts, important decisions and operational knowledge can be lost or interpreted inconsistently.\n\nC) This provides the implementation team with the technical, evaluation, and operational information required for a smooth transition.\nIt includes:\n\n• Architecture overview for understanding the overall system\n\n• ADRs for understanding important architectural decisions\n\n• Component contracts for integration boundaries\n\n• Evaluation framework and reference set for validating behavior\n\n• Runbooks and on-call playbook for operational support\n\n• Known limitations for understanding risks and constraints\n\nD) Source code does not provide the complete architectural and operational context needed for a clean handoff. The implementation team also needs documentation explaining decisions, interfaces, evaluation, operations, and limitations.","ns":1,"w":["Presentation slides alone rarely provide enough implementation detail. The delivery team needs architecture details, contracts, evaluation criteria, operational procedures, and known limitations to implement and operate the system correctly.","A verbal walkthrough can supplement documentation, but it should not replace it. Without written artifacts, important decisions and operational knowledge can be lost or interpreted inconsistently.","This provides the implementation team with the technical, evaluation, and operational information required for a smooth transition.\nIt includes:\n\n• Architecture overview for understanding the overall system\n\n• ADRs for understanding important architectural decisions\n\n• Component contracts for integration boundaries\n\n• Evaluation framework and reference set for validating behavior\n\n• Runbooks and on-call playbook for operational support\n\n• Known limitations for understanding risks and constraints","Source code does not provide the complete architectural and operational context needed for a clean handoff. The implementation team also needs documentation explaining decisions, interfaces, evaluation, operations, and limitations."],"t":"Handoff = docs + decisions + contracts + evals + runbooks + limits.","k":["handoff","ADRs","runbooks"]},{"id":"ccar-p-54","d":"d5","x":"A security team is evaluating two proposed controls. Control A adds an outbound tool allow-list with destination restrictions and per-call review. Control B scores responses against a stable adversarial evaluation set after each model-version change.\nWhich two risk categories are correctly matched to these controls? (Select two.)","o":["Control A - prompt injection from adversarial content in retrieved data","Control A - silent quality drift after a model-version upgrade","Control A - data exfiltration via outbound tool calls","Control B - data exfiltration via outbound tool calls","Control B - silent quality drift after a model-version upgrade"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Final Answer: C and E\nC = Control A → Data exfiltration via outbound tool calls.\nE = Control B → Silent quality drift after a model-version upgrade.\n\nA) Control A primarily restricts outbound tool destinations and calls. Prompt injection from retrieved content is more directly addressed by treating retrieved content as untrusted input, input classification, and output validation.\n\nB) An outbound allow-list does not detect quality or behavioral drift after a model update. This type of risk requires evaluation and comparison against a stable reference set.\n\nC) An outbound destination allow-list and per-call review directly reduce the risk of sensitive data being transmitted to unauthorized external destinations through tools.\n\nD) Control B is an evaluation control designed to identify changes in model behavior. It does not directly prevent outbound data exfiltration.\n\nE) Comparing each new model version against a stable adversarial evaluation set can reveal unexpected changes in behavior and identify quality or safety regressions after an upgrade.","ns":1,"w":["Control A primarily restricts outbound tool destinations and calls. Prompt injection from retrieved content is more directly addressed by treating retrieved content as untrusted input, input classification, and output validation.","An outbound allow-list does not detect quality or behavioral drift after a model update. This type of risk requires evaluation and comparison against a stable reference set.","An outbound destination allow-list and per-call review directly reduce the risk of sensitive data being transmitted to unauthorized external destinations through tools.","Control B is an evaluation control designed to identify changes in model behavior. It does not directly prevent outbound data exfiltration.","Comparing each new model version against a stable adversarial evaluation set can reveal unexpected changes in behavior and identify quality or safety regressions after an upgrade."],"t":"Allow-list stops EXFILTRATION. Stable eval set catches DRIFT.","k":["exfiltration","quality drift","allow-list"]},{"id":"ccar-p-55","d":"d1","x":"A Claude Architect is reviewing a post-deployment performance report for an Al-assisted legal-document summarization system. The report includes these observations:\n* Average summarization time decreased from 47 minutes to 6 minutes per document.\n* Associates spend less time on summaries, but overall billable output has not measurably changed.\n* Infrastructure costs increased by 22% because redundant retry logic generated additional API calls.\n* Some summaries require attorney correction, adding an average of 8 minutes of review per document.\nWhich analysis correctly attributes each observation to the appropriate business-value pillar?","o":["Observations 1 and 2 both indicate efficiency gains; Observation 3 is a solution-cost issue; Observation 4 is a performance-SLA issue.","Observation 1 is a transformation outcome; Observation 2 is an efficiency gain; Observation 3 is a performance-SLA degradation; Observation 4 is a solution-cost issue.","Observations 1 and 4 together indicate a net performance-SLA improvement; Observation 2 is a transformation gap; Observation 3 is a productivity drain from over-engineering.","Observation 1 indicates an efficiency gain; Observation 2 shows that productivity has not yet been realized;Observation 3 is a solution-cost issue; Observation 4 is an efficiency loss that partially offsets Observation 1."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D\nD = Efficiency gain + unrealized productivity + solution-cost issue + partially offsetting efficiency loss.\n\nA) Observation 2 specifically states that overall billable output has not measurably changed, so it does not demonstrate a realized efficiency/productivity outcome. Observation 4 concerns additional attorney review time, not a performance SLA.\n\nB) Observation 1 is primarily an efficiency gain, not necessarily a transformation outcome. Observation 3 explicitly concerns increased infrastructure/API costs, making it a solution-cost issue, not a performance-SLA issue.\n\nC) Observation 1 measures time saved per document, while Observation 4 measures human correction/review effort. Neither directly establishes a performance-SLA improvement. Observation 3 is explicitly about increased infrastructure costs.\n\nD) Each observation is correctly mapped:\n\n• Observation 1: 47 minutes → 6 minutes demonstrates a clear efficiency gain.\n\n• Observation 2: Less time spent on summaries has not translated into increased billable output, so the expected productivity benefit has not yet been realized.\n\n• Observation 3: A 22% increase in infrastructure/API costs is a solution-cost issue.\n\n• Observation 4: Attorney correction adds 8 minutes of work, creating an efficiency loss that partially offsets the initial time savings.","ns":1,"w":["Observation 2 specifically states that overall billable output has not measurably changed, so it does not demonstrate a realized efficiency/productivity outcome. Observation 4 concerns additional attorney review time, not a performance SLA.","Observation 1 is primarily an efficiency gain, not necessarily a transformation outcome. Observation 3 explicitly concerns increased infrastructure/API costs, making it a solution-cost issue, not a performance-SLA issue.","Observation 1 measures time saved per document, while Observation 4 measures human correction/review effort. Neither directly establishes a performance-SLA improvement. Observation 3 is explicitly about increased infrastructure costs.","Each observation is correctly mapped:\n\n• Observation 1: 47 minutes → 6 minutes demonstrates a clear efficiency gain.\n\n• Observation 2: Less time spent on summaries has not translated into increased billable output, so the expected productivity benefit has not yet been realized.\n\n• Observation 3: A 22% increase in infrastructure/API costs is a solution-cost issue.\n\n• Observation 4: Attorney correction adds 8 minutes of work, creating an efficiency loss that partially offsets the initial time savings."],"t":"Faster = efficiency. More output = productivity. Bills = cost. Rework = efficiency loss.","k":["efficiency","productivity","solution cost"]},{"id":"ccar-p-57","d":"d5","x":"You are listing characteristics of robust guardrail design for an enterprise deployment.\nWhich two characteristics belong on the list? (Select two.)\nEach correct answer presents a complete solution.","o":["Centralized log retention for guardrail violations with quarterly review by the security team.","Per-role tool allow-lists enforced at the orchestration layer before any tool call executes.","Adversarial-input coverage in the evaluation set with regression tracking on guardrail performance.","User feedback channels that route reported guardrail failures into the product backlog for triage.","Periodic refresh of the system prompt wording to keep refusal language current and clear."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Final Answer: B and C\nB = Per-role tool allow-lists enforced before tool execution.\nC = Adversarial evaluation coverage with regression tracking.\n\nA) Centralized logging and periodic review are useful for monitoring and governance, but they are not as directly related to the core guardrail enforcement and evaluation mechanisms represented by the correct answers.\n\nB) Enforcing per-role tool allow-lists at the orchestration layer applies least privilege before a tool can execute. This provides a strong preventive control because the model cannot simply decide to use a tool outside the permissions granted to its role.\n\nC) Robust guardrails need continuous adversarial testing and regression tracking. Including attack-oriented cases in the evaluation set helps verify that guardrails continue to work as prompts, models, and system components change.\n\nD) User feedback is valuable for identifying issues, but it is primarily a feedback and improvement mechanism, rather than a direct preventive or evaluation control for guardrail robustness.\n\nE) Refreshing refusal wording can improve clarity, but changing prompt language alone is not sufficient to establish robust guardrail enforcement. Strong guardrails should include controls outside the model's instructions as well.","ns":1,"w":["Centralized logging and periodic review are useful for monitoring and governance, but they are not as directly related to the core guardrail enforcement and evaluation mechanisms represented by the correct answers.","Enforcing per-role tool allow-lists at the orchestration layer applies least privilege before a tool can execute. This provides a strong preventive control because the model cannot simply decide to use a tool outside the permissions granted to its role.","Robust guardrails need continuous adversarial testing and regression tracking. Including attack-oriented cases in the evaluation set helps verify that guardrails continue to work as prompts, models, and system components change.","User feedback is valuable for identifying issues, but it is primarily a feedback and improvement mechanism, rather than a direct preventive or evaluation control for guardrail robustness.","Refreshing refusal wording can improve clarity, but changing prompt language alone is not sufficient to establish robust guardrail enforcement. Strong guardrails should include controls outside the model's instructions as well."],"t":"PREVENT (allow-lists) + TEST (adversarial regression).","k":["per-role allow-list","adversarial evaluation","regression"]},{"id":"ccar-p-59","d":"d2","x":"You are identifying inefficiency in a Claude Code workflow where each engineer manually re-explains the project's conventions and architecture in every session.\nWhich adjustment most directly removes this inefficiency?","o":["Forbid the use of Claude Code on the project entirely to avoid the session-initialization overhead, accepting that the team loses all Al-assisted development productivity for this codebase.","Capture the project's conventions and architecture in a project-scoped CLAUDE.md (or equivalent persistent project-context file) committed to the repository, so each session loads it automatically.","Tell each engineer to retype the project conventions and architecture context more quickly at the start of eachsession, reducing time lost without eliminating the repeated manual effort.","Remove all documented project conventions and architectural standards so engineers have nothing to re-explain at session start, accepting the loss of consistency and shared coding standards."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B\nB = Store project conventions and architecture in a project-scoped CLAUDE.md so every Claude Code session can load the shared context automatically.\n\nA) This eliminates the inefficiency by eliminating Claude Code itself, but it also removes the team's AI-assisted development capability. It is not an effective solution to the underlying context-management problem.\n\nB) A project-scoped CLAUDE.md provides persistent project context that can be reused across sessions. This eliminates the need for engineers to repeatedly explain the same architecture, conventions, and project instructions, while keeping the information version-controlled and shared with the team.\n\nC) This only makes the existing inefficient process faster. It does not eliminate the repeated manual work or provide consistent persistent context.\n\nD) Removing project standards sacrifices consistency and governance rather than solving the context problem. The correct approach is to preserve the standards in persistent project context.","ns":1,"w":["This eliminates the inefficiency by eliminating Claude Code itself, but it also removes the team's AI-assisted development capability. It is not an effective solution to the underlying context-management problem.","A project-scoped CLAUDE.md provides persistent project context that can be reused across sessions. This eliminates the need for engineers to repeatedly explain the same architecture, conventions, and project instructions, while keeping the information version-controlled and shared with the team.","This only makes the existing inefficient process faster. It does not eliminate the repeated manual work or provide consistent persistent context.","Removing project standards sacrifices consistency and governance rather than solving the context problem. The correct approach is to preserve the standards in persistent project context."],"t":"Repeating yourself every session = write CLAUDE.md.","k":["CLAUDE.md","project context","persistent"]},{"id":"ccar-p-62","d":"d4","x":"You are assessing a Claude-based system whose dominant risk is silent quality drift on safety-relevant outputs\nafter a model-version upgrade.\nWhich assessment activity most directly addresses this risk?","o":["Disable adversarial evaluation entirely during model-version upgrade cycles to reduce evaluation cost, accepting that safety drift will go undetected until it appears in production.","Rotate adversarial inputs randomly so no two upgrades are scored on the same set.","Skip evaluation on each model-version upgrade and rely on user-submitted complaints to surface safety drift after the upgraded model has already served production traffic.","Maintain an adversarial evaluation set with version-attributed scoring so each upgrade is measured against the same set before promotion."],"a":[3],"e":"Final Answer: D\nD = Maintain a stable adversarial evaluation set and compare each model version against it before promotion.\n\nA) Disabling adversarial evaluation removes an important mechanism for detecting safety regressions before deployment. This increases the risk that a model-version change introduces undetected problems into production.\n\nB) Changing the evaluation set every time makes it difficult to establish a consistent baseline between model versions. Stable test cases are important for accurately identifying behavioral drift.\n\nC) Waiting for user complaints means the system is being evaluated after exposure to production users. Safety-relevant regressions should be detected before promotion whenever possible.\n\nD) A stable adversarial evaluation set provides a consistent baseline for comparing model versions. Version-attributed scoring makes it possible to identify quality or safety drift and prevent a problematic version from being promoted.","ns":1,"w":["Disabling adversarial evaluation removes an important mechanism for detecting safety regressions before deployment. This increases the risk that a model-version change introduces undetected problems into production.","Changing the evaluation set every time makes it difficult to establish a consistent baseline between model versions. Stable test cases are important for accurately identifying behavioral drift.","Waiting for user complaints means the system is being evaluated after exposure to production users. Safety-relevant regressions should be detected before promotion whenever possible.","A stable adversarial evaluation set provides a consistent baseline for comparing model versions. Version-attributed scoring makes it possible to identify quality or safety drift and prevent a problematic version from being promoted."],"t":"Same adversarial set, every version, before promote.","k":["adversarial eval set","version scoring","drift"]},{"id":"ccar-p-63","d":"d3","x":"You are a platform architect designing an internal Claude-based assistant that serves both finance analysts and external auditors. Each population must access only documents permitted by its role.\nWhere should role-based access control be enforced in the pipeline?","o":["Inside the system prompt as a natural-language instruction for Claude to ignore unauthorized documents.","At the retrieval layer, before any role-restricted content reaches the prompt-construction step or the model.","Nowhere in the pipeline; rely on the model's general refusal behavior to reject unauthorized document access without any enforced access control.","After the response is generated, by post-filtering content that should not have been retrieved."],"a":[1],"e":"Final Answer: B\nB = Enforce RBAC at the retrieval layer before unauthorized content can enter the model context.\n\nA) A system prompt is an instruction to the model, not an enforcement boundary. Relying on the model to follow access-control instructions could allow unauthorized content to enter the model context.\n\nB) RBAC should be enforced before retrieval results are passed into the model context. The retrieval layer can verify the user's role and return only documents that the user is authorized to access.\nThis follows the principle of enforcing authorization at the data-access boundary, rather than relying on model behavior.\n\nC) Model refusal behavior is not an access-control mechanism. Authorization must be explicitly enforced by the application or retrieval layer.\n\nD) Post-filtering is too late. Unauthorized information may already have entered the model's context and influenced the generated response. Access control should occur before the content reaches the model.","ns":1,"w":["A system prompt is an instruction to the model, not an enforcement boundary. Relying on the model to follow access-control instructions could allow unauthorized content to enter the model context.","RBAC should be enforced before retrieval results are passed into the model context. The retrieval layer can verify the user's role and return only documents that the user is authorized to access.\nThis follows the principle of enforcing authorization at the data-access boundary, rather than relying on model behavior.","Model refusal behavior is not an access-control mechanism. Authorization must be explicitly enforced by the application or retrieval layer.","Post-filtering is too late. Unauthorized information may already have entered the model's context and influenced the generated response. Access control should occur before the content reaches the model."],"t":"Block at RETRIEVAL, before the model sees it.","k":["RBAC","retrieval layer","authorization"]},{"id":"ccar-p-58","d":"general","x":"A Claude architect is leading the discovery phase for a new Al-powered customer service solution.\nWhich two activities are characteristic of structured discovery and requirement gathering for a Claude-based\ndeployment? (Select two.)","o":["Facilitating stakeholder workshops to surface latency, accuracy, and compliance constraints before scoping begins.","Selecting the Claude model tier based on the architect's prior project experience before stakeholder input is collected.","Generating an initial prototype and iterating based on user reaction rather than written requirements.","Documenting explicit success criteria and failure thresholds that will gate production deployment.","Deferring constraint documentation until the integration design phase to avoid scope creep."],"a":[0,3],"w":["Structured discovery begins by understanding the stakeholders, business needs, constraints, and expectations. Workshops can identify important requirements such as latency, accuracy, compliance, security, and operational constraints before architectural decisions are made.","Model selection should be driven by requirements and evidence, not simply by prior experience. Choosing a model before understanding stakeholder needs can result in an inappropriate architecture or unnecessary cost.","Prototyping can be valuable, but relying primarily on user reactions without documented requirements and success criteria does not represent structured discovery.","Clearly defined success criteria and failure thresholds turn stakeholder expectations into measurable requirements. They also provide objective criteria for determining whether the solution is ready for production.","Constraints should be identified and documented during discovery. Delaying them can cause major architectural changes later and increase delivery risk."],"e":"Structured discovery begins by understanding the stakeholders, business needs, constraints, and expectations. Workshops can identify important requirements such as latency, accuracy, compliance, security, and operational constraints before architectural decisions are made. Clearly defined success criteria and failure thresholds turn stakeholder expectations into measurable requirements. They also provide objective criteria for determining whether the solution is ready for production.","t":"Discovery = WORKSHOPS + written SUCCESS criteria.","k":["discovery","workshops","success criteria"]},{"id":"ccar-p-60","d":"general","x":"You are integrating Al-assisted tooling into the team's documentation workflow. The team wants generated documentation that stays grounded in the actual code.\nWhich integration approach best fits this requirement?","o":["Generate documentation from the model's training-data recall without reading any of the actual repository code, cepting that the output will not reflect the current implementation.","Have the subagents publish generated documentation directly to the public-facing site without passing through the team's normal review workflow or any human approval step.","Configure subagents that read the relevant code files via filesystem and code-search tools, generate the documentation, and emit changes through the team's normal review workflow.","Disable all filesystem and code-search tools so the subagents cannot read any repository code, accepting that cumentation generation will be entirely disconnected from the actual implementation."],"a":[2],"w":["Training-data recall may be outdated or unrelated to the current repository. Without access to the actual code, the generated documentation cannot reliably reflect the current implementation.","Direct publication bypasses the team's review and approval process and could allow inaccurate or inappropriate documentation to reach users. AI-generated changes should follow the existing review workflow.","This approach grounds the documentation in the actual repository implementation. Filesystem and code-search tools allow the subagents to inspect current code, while the normal review workflow provides human oversight and quality control before publication.","Removing access to the repository prevents the system from grounding its documentation in the current codebase, which directly conflicts with the requirement."],"e":"This approach grounds the documentation in the actual repository implementation. Filesystem and code-search tools allow the subagents to inspect current code, while the normal review workflow provides human oversight and quality control before publication.","t":"Read the REAL code + go through REVIEW.","k":["grounded docs","subagents","review workflow"]}]},{"id":"ccao-f","code":"CCAO-F","title":"Claude Certified Associate — Foundations","vendor":"Anthropic","desc":"Claude workflows, prompt design, Projects, output verification and responsible data handling.","exam":{"count":60,"minutes":90,"passPercent":80},"domains":{"general":"General","practice-sets":"Practice sets"},"qs":[{"id":"ccao-f-c1","d":"general","x":"A strategy manager reviews a Claude-generated recommendation to merge the company’s two regional warehouses into a single location. The recommendation references three sources estimating cost savings and one source that confirms consolidation is operationally feasible. Two source documents also include estimates of one-time transition costs and a stakeholder concern about regional service levels, neither of which is included in the recommendation.\n\nWhich concern is most justified?","o":["The recommendation is acceptable for an initial round, but the missing material should be added in a separate appendix rather than reprompting the original output.","The recommendation reflects selective use of the source documents and should be re-prompted to address the transition costs and the service-level concern before it is used.","The recommendation should be rejected because it relies on only four sources, which is insufficient for a decision of this size.","The recommendation is sound because every source it cites supports the consolidation conclusion."],"a":[1],"e":"A decision recommendation must account for material evidence that could affect the outcome. Omitting documented transition costs and a concern about regional service levels presents only the favorable evidence for consolidation, so the recommendation should be re-prompted to address those issues before it is used."},{"id":"ccao-f-c2","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead classifying proposed Claude use cases according to whether they are acceptable for your team to pursue.\n\nWhich two proposed use cases are acceptable for the team to pursue with Claude?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["synthesizing themes across approved customer feedback under aggregation rules","summarizing performance review notes across a team to support a manager’s promotion decisions","drafting a preliminary symptom summary to support a clinician’s diagnostic review","drafting a legal summary that an attorney will review and edit before sharing with a client","drafting an internal training summary from approved internal documentation"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Synthesizing approved customer feedback under aggregation controls and creating internal training summaries from approved internal documentation are bounded, low-risk information-processing tasks. Promotion decisions are employment-related high-impact decisions, while diagnostic support and client-facing legal drafting involve healthcare and legal guidance, respectively; these domains require heightened safeguards and are not routine team-approved use cases merely because a human reviews the output."},{"id":"ccao-f-c3","d":"general","x":"You are an analyst tackling a one-off, complex problem that truly requires multi-step reasoning across an extended chain of thought. Which model is most appropriate for this work?","o":["A non-Anthropic model, because the Claude lineup has no option for complex reasoning.","Opus, because it is positioned as the most capable for advanced reasoning on complex specialized tasks.","Haiku, because lightweight models are always preferred regardless of complexity.","Any model, because reasoning depth does not vary across the lineup."],"a":[1],"e":"Opus is Anthropic’s highest-capability model tier and is positioned for advanced reasoning and complex, specialized tasks. That makes it the appropriate choice when a one-off problem requires an extended, multi-step reasoning process."},{"id":"ccao-f-c4","d":"general","x":"Before uploading a customer file for Code Execution analysis, a customer-success associate at Noventra, Ltd. is reviewing the planned task.\n\nWhich two practices most closely align with sound data handling?\n\nChoose two","o":["Confirm that the planned Code Execution task aligns with the organization’s data classification policy and escalate to the appropriate contact if the data sensitivity exceeds approved use.","Review the file for sensitive, confidential, or regulated fields before upload, and remove or redact any fields that are not required for the planned Code Execution analysis.","Skip the upload review entirely on the basis of urgency, even though the file might contain regulated fields that organizational policy requires the user to redact before any code execution.","Bypass the data classification policy because the task is internal, treating sandboxed processing as an exemption from the review the policy requires before sensitive data is uploaded.","Upload the full unredacted customer file without review, on the assumption that a sandboxed execution environment removes the need to apply the organization’s data classification policy."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Data should be handled according to the organization’s classification policy, with escalation when the planned use exceeds approved sensitivity limits. Files should also be reviewed before upload so that sensitive, confidential, or regulated fields not needed for the analysis are removed or redacted. A sandboxed execution environment does not eliminate these policy and data-minimization obligations."},{"id":"ccao-f-c5","d":"general","x":"You are assessing proposed workflow changes to determine whether they materially improve the workflow. Which two proposed changes meaningfully improve a recurring Claude workflow?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["updating the team’s shared workspace name to reflect the new workflow process","labeling each workflow step with descriptive names to improve readability for new team members","extracting the standard reference material into a Project to avoid repeating it","reorganizing the workflow document’s section headings to improve navigation","adding a quality check on outputs before they enter the downstream process"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Claude Projects use uploaded knowledge as context across all chats in the Project, so placing standard reference material there avoids repeatedly supplying the same context in a recurring workflow. A quality check before output enters a downstream process prevents incorrect or unsuitable results from being propagated. Workspace names, step labels, and document headings improve organization or readability but do not add reusable Claude context or an output-control mechanism."},{"id":"ccao-f-c6","d":"general","x":"You are evaluating a Claude-generated report that maintains a confident tone throughout. Which question most effectively distinguishes plausibility from verification?","o":["Can each specific claim be traced to a credible source or to the supplied input material?","Does the response open with a clear, well-framed statement of the main finding?","Does the response cover the topic comprehensively based on its length and section count?","Does the response use varied sentence structure across sections?"],"a":[0],"e":"Verification requires checking whether individual claims are supported by credible sources or by the supplied input material. Confidence, framing, length, coverage, and sentence variety can make content seem plausible but do not establish that its claims are substantiated."},{"id":"ccao-f-c7","d":"general","x":"You are a marketing manager determining what to put in custom instructions for a writing-focused Project.\n\nWhich two items should be included in the custom instructions?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["the brand voice and the tonal expectations the writing must follow","a representative sample of past writing that reflects the team’s preferred style and structure","the prompt author’s preferred response length for internal messages unrelated to external deliverables","the preferred writing style of the marketing manager who will review all final deliverables","the standard structure and the length expectations for typical deliverables"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Writing-project instructions should set the reusable requirements that apply to the project’s outputs, including the brand voice and tonal requirements, as well as the standard structure and expected length of typical deliverables. Project instructions can direct how ChatGPT responds within that project, including style, concision, and formatting."},{"id":"ccao-f-c8","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker who broke a “draft a board memo” request into fifteen small steps and noticed that the output now feels disjointed because related ideas were separated.\n\nWhich adjustment is most appropriate?","o":["Combine the original request back into one unstructured sentence.","Subdivide each tiny step into ten more tiny steps for additional precision.","Remove all sequencing so the steps run in random order.","Consolidate related steps into fewer cohesive sections that group naturally connected ideas."],"a":[3],"e":"Related work should be consolidated into cohesive sections so naturally connected ideas remain together, improving coherence without discarding the useful structure of the task."},{"id":"ccao-f-c9","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker reviewing a Claude-generated brief that claims a “well-known industry standard” but does not identify any source. How should this claim be handled?","o":["Treat the assertion as unverified, and either source it from an authoritative reference or remove it.","Accept the assertion because the phrase \"well-known\" is reassuring.","Republish the brief unchanged because the claim sounds authoritative.","Strengthen the assertion by adding more confident wording."],"a":[0],"e":"Claims about an industry standard require support from an authoritative, identifiable reference. An unnamed assertion remains unverified and should be sourced or removed rather than accepted or strengthened based on its wording alone."},{"id":"ccao-f-c10","d":"general","x":"You are breaking down a “build a customer journey map” request.\n\nWhich sequence of decomposed steps is correct?\n\n1. Identify the touchpoints the customer experiences during each stage.\n2. Define the persona and the journey scope that will be mapped.\n3. Document pain points and opportunities at every touchpoint.\n4. Synthesize the findings into the finished journey-map artifact.\n5. Identify the primary stages the persona progresses through.","o":["2, 5, 1, 3, 4","1, 2, 5, 3, 4","5, 2, 1, 3, 4","2, 1, 5, 3, 4"],"a":[0],"e":"A customer journey map is grounded in a defined persona and journey scope. Its structure is then established by identifying the persona’s major stages, followed by the touchpoints within those stages. Pain points and opportunities are evaluated at those touchpoints before all findings are synthesized into the final journey map."},{"id":"ccao-f-c11","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is using the AI Fluency Framework’s Description competency to revise an underperforming prompt. Which revision most accurately reflects Description principles?","o":["Remove the existing context from the prompt and submit only the bare task statement, eliminating the audience, format, and constraint information that Description principles call for in a clear specification.","Replace the specific instructions with generic phrasing that broadens the task scope, undoing the specification work that Description principles require for a reliable, useful output on the next run.","Specify the task scope, the required output format, the audience, and the relevant constraints with sufficient clarity that the prompt produces a useful, reliable output on the next run.","Add length to the prompt by inserting filler text that does not specify task scope, output format, audience, or constraints, leaving the original specification gaps in the underperforming prompt unaddressed."],"a":[2],"e":"Description involves communicating goals clearly enough to prompt useful AI behaviors and outputs. Defining the task scope, required output format, intended audience, and applicable constraints provides the specification needed for a useful, reliable result."},{"id":"ccao-f-c12","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker moving from a long, drifting conversation to a fresh, focused session. What is the correct sequence of transition steps?\n\n1. Start a new chat and paste the summary as its initial context.\n2. Summarize the goals, key decisions, and open questions from the lengthy conversation.\n3. Determine which references should remain available beyond the current chat.\n4. Place persistent references in a Project’s knowledge base or attach them as needed.\n5. Verify that the new chat is on-task before resuming the work.","o":["1, 2, 3, 4, 5","2, 3, 4, 1, 5","2, 4, 3, 1, 5","3, 2, 4, 1, 5"],"a":[1],"e":"A focused continuation requires a usable summary before a new chat can receive its starting context. References intended for reuse should be identified and added to Project sources or attached before continuing, because Projects keep related chats, files, and instructions together to preserve context and stay on-task. The new chat can then be validated for alignment before work resumes."},{"id":"ccao-f-c13","d":"general","x":"An operations assistant is redesigning a recurring data-processing workflow step to use Code Execution. Which two design decisions best promote reliable integration?\n\nChoose two","o":["Vary the input file format from run to run to accommodate the different source systems that supply the data each week.","Define a brief output verification step in which the human reviewer confirms the computed result against an expected range or sanity check before the output advances to downstream consumers.","Embed the Code Execution step inside the workflow without documenting the expected output format or the fields it produces.","Standardize the input file format the step expects, so each run of the Code Execution step receives consistent input and produces comparable, verifiable output across recurring weekly cycles.","Eliminate the human review step that follows Code Execution, since the sandboxed environment already verifies that the code ran without errors."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Recurring automated processing is most reliable when each execution receives a consistent input structure, allowing outputs to be compared and verified across cycles. Successful code execution only establishes that the code ran; it does not establish that the computed result is valid for the business context. A human reviewer’s expected-range or sanity check provides a control before downstream consumers receive the output."},{"id":"ccao-f-c14","d":"general","x":"You are assessing proposed evaluation criteria for a Claude-generated business brief. Which two criteria are valid for evaluating a brief produced by Claude?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["factual accuracy of the claims made against the supplied source material","consistency of the writing style with the organization’s established tone guidelines","total word count of the output relative to industry benchmarks for business briefs","presence of an executive summary section as a structural completeness check","alignment of the output with the requested format and length constraints"],"a":[0,4]},{"id":"ccao-f-c15","d":"general","x":"You are a member of an operations team preparing input to summarize customer feedback. The feedback file combines public quotations with personal contact information.\n\nWhich preparation method is most appropriate?","o":["Extract only the feedback content needed for the summary, redact or anonymize personal contact details, and submit the trimmed input.","Submit the entire file unchanged so Claude has complete context.","Replace the feedback content with the personal contact details and ask Claude to invent the missing feedback.","Strip out the feedback content and submit only the personal contact details."],"a":[0],"e":"Only the feedback necessary for the summary should be provided, while personal contact details should be removed, redacted, or anonymized. This preserves the information needed for the task while minimizing unnecessary exposure of personally identifiable information."},{"id":"ccao-f-c16","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead and observe that a Project’s outputs have been drifting away from the team’s current expectations. Which maintenance action should be attempted first?","o":["Review the Project’s instructions and reference materials against current standards.","Switch the Project to a different Claude model in the hope it behaves better.","Continue using the Project and accept the drift as the new baseline of behavior.","Retire the Project and rebuild a new one from scratch with fresh content."],"a":[0],"e":"Project instructions define how Claude should behave and respond across the Project’s chats, while uploaded project knowledge supplies the shared context. Reviewing those instructions and reference materials against current standards addresses the most direct, controllable cause of output drift before considering a model change or rebuilding the Project."},{"id":"ccao-f-c17","d":"general","x":"A manager is determining which changes to make during a memory-maintenance review for a long-running workflow. Which two actions most effectively support memory quality over time?\n\nChoose two","o":["Delete all memories as the maintenance action to ensure a clean slate, then allow Claude to rebuild context organically through subsequent workflow runs.","Disable Memory entirely to eliminate the risk of stale entries surfacing in future workflow runs.","Identify and edit or delete entries that are stale, inaccurate, or no longer relevant, and add context Claude has not automatically captured but the workflow continues to need.","Leave all memories untouched on the basis that the workflow has been running consistently and no output quality issues have been reported.","Export the current memories as a backup before making changes, so prior context can be restored if a maintenance edit unintentionally removes information the workflow still depends on."],"a":[2,4],"e":"Effective memory maintenance includes reviewing and updating stored context so it remains current, accurate, and useful; obsolete or incorrect entries should be edited or removed, while necessary missing context should be added. Backing up existing memories before changes provides a recovery path if an edit accidentally removes context the workflow still requires. Anthropic’s memory guidance recommends regularly reviewing and updating memory as projects evolve."},{"id":"ccao-f-c18","d":"general","x":"An HR business partner is creating a long-running workspace in which a team handbook, a leveling guide, and a policies-FAQ document will be used across many separate chats by several team members.\n\nWhich Claude feature is intended for this use case?","o":["Connect the team’s Google Drive folder where the three documents live, so the connector surfaces them inside each chat at the moment the chat needs them.","Generate an Artifact containing the three documents, which gives the team an editable surface for the reference content that persists across chats.","Create a Project, which provides a persistent workspace with its own knowledge base and custom instructions shared across chats and members.","Attach all three documents to each chat as needed, since per-chat attachments give the chat direct access to the documents without additional setup."],"a":[2],"e":"Claude Projects provide a persistent workspace with a project knowledge base whose uploaded content is used across chats within that project. Projects can also be shared with other organization members on Team and Enterprise plans, and project instructions apply to all chats in the project."},{"id":"ccao-f-c19","d":"general","x":"You are an analyst verifying facts in a Claude-generated report. Two cited sources provide conflicting dates for the same event. Which approach is correct?","o":["Choose the more recent date on the basis that later sources typically reflect updated or corrected information.","Average the two conflicting dates and use the average.","Locate a primary or authoritative source for the event and use the date that source confirms.","Publish both dates without resolving the conflict and let the reader choose."],"a":[2],"e":"A primary or authoritative source is the strongest basis for establishing an event date and resolving a conflict between cited sources. Recency alone does not establish accuracy, dates cannot be meaningfully averaged, and an unresolved conflict should not be presented as verified fact."},{"id":"ccao-f-c20","d":"general","x":"A business analyst is transforming a stakeholder’s sticky-note request into a structured prompt for Claude. What is the correct sequence of conversion steps?\n\n1. Confirm the audience and the decision that the response needs to support.\n2. Submit the structured prompt to Claude.\n3. Identify the underlying task concealed in the sticky-note language.\n4. Specify the required length, tone, and output format.\n5. Add the supporting facts and constraints that are relevant to the task.","o":["3, 5, 1, 4, 2","1, 3, 5, 4, 2","3, 1, 5, 4, 2","2, 3, 1, 5, 4"],"a":[2],"e":"A useful structured prompt progresses from the underlying task and intended audience or decision to the relevant context and constraints, followed by explicit output requirements such as length, tone, and format. The complete prompt is submitted only after those elements are defined. Anthropic guidance likewise recommends clear instructions, relevant context, and explicit expected output."},{"id":"ccao-f-c21","d":"general","x":"You are a bank manager who receives a request to use Claude as the sole decision-maker for customer credit-line approvals. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["Accept the sole-decision-maker design because Claude can process applications faster than a human review team.","Expand the design so Claude both evaluates applications and issues final approval notices to customers directly.","Implement the design on a pilot basis and notify compliance only if issues arise during production use.","Decline the sole-decision-maker design and propose Claude assisting analysts who retain decision authority."],"a":[3],"e":"Customer credit-line approvals are consequential financial decisions that require meaningful human oversight and accountable decision authority. Claude may assist analysts with evaluating applications, but analysts should retain the final approval authority."},{"id":"ccao-f-c22","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is determining the bottleneck in a content workflow that is taking too long to publish. The workflow includes five steps: research, drafting, internal review, legal review, and publication. Cycle-time data is available for each step from the past month.\n\nWhich signal most directly identifies the bottleneck?","o":["the step where individual contributors report the highest workload based on their weekly status updates","the step most recently added to the workflow, based on the assumption that process changes introduce the most friction","the step with the longest queue or the largest backlog of items waiting to enter it","the step with the highest average cycle time per item across the past month’s runs"],"a":[3],"e":"A bottleneck is the process step whose capacity constrains the throughput of the entire workflow. In a sequential workflow measured by cycle time, the step with the highest average cycle time is the slowest stage and most directly limits the rate at which items can progress and be published. Queue size may support that finding, but it is an indirect and potentially temporary symptom."},{"id":"ccao-f-c23","d":"general","x":"You are setting up a new Claude Project for a particular recurring workload. Which Project-configuration step should be completed first?","o":["Define the purpose of the Project and the recurring tasks it must support.","Invite collaborators to the Project and confirm the appropriate access levels.","Add reference materials and instructions that the Project will rely on.","Test the configured Project on a representative request from the workload."],"a":[0],"e":"A Project’s purpose and the recurring tasks it will support establish the context for its configuration. Reference materials, project instructions, collaborator permissions, and representative testing should be chosen and assessed against that defined workload."},{"id":"ccao-f-c24","d":"general","x":"You are a business analyst assessing prompt drafts prepared by teammates.\n\nWhich two components should be included in a well-structured prompt for a business task?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["a friendly, conversational tone instruction to make responses feel more approachable","a comprehensive list of company products and services to provide the model with business context","a short personal anecdote to make the prompt feel more conversational","a clear statement of the task that the model must perform","relevant context such as the audience and the goal of the task"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Effective business prompts specify the task the model is expected to perform and provide relevant context—such as the intended audience and the task goal—so the response can be targeted, appropriate, and useful."},{"id":"ccao-f-c25","d":"general","x":"A project lead is assessing a Claude-generated status report against the project charter. The report will be presented to a steering committee that expects an accurate status update against committed milestones.\n\nWhich group of criteria offers the strongest evaluation framework?","o":["length, formatting consistency, and tone appropriateness for a steering committee audience","accuracy of facts against the charter, completeness against required reporting fields, and relevance to the audience receiving the report","readability, narrative flow, and whether the report tells a coherent story about the project’s progress","match to the prior status report’s structure, since the steering committee expects consistent reporting from period to period"],"a":[1],"e":"A strong evaluation framework for a project status report verifies factual accuracy against the charter, confirms that all required reporting fields are complete, and assesses whether the content is relevant to the steering committee audience. These criteria directly support dependable reporting on committed milestones; presentation quality and structural consistency alone do not."},{"id":"ccao-f-c26","d":"general","x":"A senior manager is reviewing a colleague’s practice of never updating their team’s Project configurations because “if it works once, it works forever.” Which feedback is most appropriate?","o":["Configurations should be updated on a fixed schedule regardless of whether the underlying policies and source documents have changed, since regular maintenance prevents drift.","Configurations should be updated only when output quality visibly degrades, since proactive updates risk introducing regressions in working configurations.","Configurations should be reviewed and updated periodically because policies, terminology, and source documents change over time, even when output quality has not visibly degraded.","Configurations should be left alone if the team is satisfied with current outputs, and any required updates should be handled at the chat level rather than the Project level."],"a":[2],"e":"Project configurations should be reviewed and updated periodically because the policies, terminology, and source documents they rely on can change even before any decline in output quality is apparent."},{"id":"ccao-f-c27","d":"general","x":"You are organizing connected data sources for a team and must finish the planning steps before authorizing a connection. Which two planning steps must be completed BEFORE authorizing a new Connector?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Migrate every existing report off the prior data source onto the new Connector.","Confirm the Connector is on the approved-vendor list maintained by IT.","Confirm the data classification of the source and the team’s permission to use it.","Train every team member on the new Connector through formal training sessions.","Decommission any unused legacy connectors that may still be authorized."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Authorizing a connector requires governance validation: the connector must be approved by IT, and the data source’s classification and the team’s permission to use it must be confirmed. These checks establish vendor and data-access compliance before a connection is authorized."},{"id":"ccao-f-c28","d":"general","x":"While preparing a stakeholder communication about a proposed Claude use case, a senior manager is applying the AI Fluency Framework Description competency.\n\nWhich framing most closely reflects Description principles for stakeholder communication?","o":["Describe what Claude can reliably produce for the use case, the known limitations, and the human oversight that will remain in place, calibrated to the audience’s familiarity with AI.","Overstate Claude’s capabilities to secure stakeholder support, even though Description principles require accurate framing of what Claude can and cannot reliably produce for the proposed use case.","Omit the human oversight that will remain in place from the communication, even though Description principles require setting expectations that reflect the actual capability boundaries of the use case.","Understate Claude’s capabilities to lower stakeholder expectations, even though Description principles require accuracy in both directions rather than systematic minimization of what Claude can produce."],"a":[0],"e":"Effective Description uses clear, purposeful communication to convey intended AI outputs and behavior. Accurate stakeholder framing should communicate what Claude can reliably produce, its known capability boundaries, and the human oversight that remains, in terms appropriate to the audience’s AI familiarity."},{"id":"ccao-f-c29","d":"general","x":"You are a Claude associate conducting an ethics review of a Claude-assisted deliverable before it is published. What is the correct sequence of review steps?\n\n1. Confirm that transparency requirements are satisfied for the audience that will receive the deliverable.\n2. Record the review result and any modifications made.\n3. Identify the audience and the deliverable’s potential impact on that audience.\n4. Make focused revisions, or escalate when a concern needs greater authority to resolve.\n5. Review the content for unsupported generalizations, fairness concerns, and accuracy issues.","o":["5, 3, 1, 4, 2","3, 1, 5, 4, 2","3, 5, 1, 4, 2","1, 3, 5, 4, 2"],"a":[2],"e":"An ethics review should establish who may be affected and how, assess the content for fairness and accuracy problems, determine the transparency required for that audience, address or escalate identified concerns, and retain a record of the final outcome and changes."},{"id":"ccao-f-c30","d":"general","x":"You are a business analyst assessing a Claude-generated business case against the agreed acceptance criteria. What is the correct sequence of evaluation steps?\n\n1. Compare the business case against the source data and required topics.\n2. Record gaps and inaccuracies for follow-up or correction.\n3. Reconfirm the acceptance criteria for the business case.\n4. Determine whether to use the business case, iterate, or escalate.\n5. Read the business case from start to finish to form an initial impression.","o":["3, 1, 5, 2, 4","1, 3, 5, 2, 4","5, 3, 1, 2, 4","3, 5, 1, 2, 4"],"a":[3],"e":"A sound evaluation establishes the acceptance criteria before review, reads the complete business case for context, validates it against source data and required content, records deficiencies, and only then decides whether it is acceptable, needs iteration, or should be escalated."},{"id":"ccao-f-c31","d":"general","x":"A project manager is turning a Claude-generated draft into a polished board memo. The board reviews the memo before the meeting and expects it to be scannable, factually verified, and consistent with the organization’s standard terminology.\n\nWhich set of refinements is most suitable?","o":["Apply consistent headings, tighten wording for executive scanability, align terminology with the organization’s standards, and verify each fact against source data.","Apply consistent headings, expand each section to give the board full context, and align terminology with the organization's standards.","Tighten wording for executive scanability and verify each fact against source data; leave heading structure and terminology as written to preserve the draft’s voice.","Apply consistent headings and align terminology with the organization’s standards; rely on Claude’s verification of facts, since the draft was generated from the source data."],"a":[0],"e":"A board memo should use consistent headings and concise wording for quick executive review, follow the organization’s standard terminology, and have every factual claim verified against source data. AI-generated content does not remove the need for independent fact checking."},{"id":"ccao-f-c32","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead assessing prompting strategies proposed by teammates for different task types.\n\nWhich two strategies are suitable matches between the strategy and task type?\n\nEach correct answer provides part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["rigid templated prompting for early-stage creative naming and tagline ideation","open exploratory prompting for legal contract redlining against a clause library","open exploratory prompting for early-stage strategic brainstorming on a new market","rigid templated prompting for casual customer-feedback summarization with no schema","rigid templated prompting for monthly compliance reports against a fixed schema"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Open exploratory prompting supports divergent thinking during early-stage strategic brainstorming for a new market. Rigid templated prompting supports repeatable monthly compliance reports that must conform to a fixed schema."},{"id":"ccao-f-c33","d":"general","x":"A learning specialist is setting up a Claude Project for a recurring course-design workflow.\n\nWhich configuration approach makes the best use of a Skill together with other Project elements?","o":["Configure the Project with custom instructions that contradict the Skill’s documented purpose, creating ambiguity about which guidance Claude should follow within the recurring course-design workflow.","Add the relevant Skill to the Project, upload the supporting knowledge sources, and write custom instructions that reference both the Skill and the knowledge.","Upload the supporting knowledge sources but disable the relevant Skill, eliminating the packaged capability the workflow needs and forcing each chat to reconstruct the procedure manually.","Add the Skill to the Project but omit the supporting knowledge sources and any custom instructions, so the Project lacks the reference content and persistent guidance the workflow requires."],"a":[1],"e":"Skills provide specialized, repeatable procedures, while a Project’s knowledge base provides persistent reference context and its project instructions provide guidance for chats in that Project. Adding the relevant Skill, supporting knowledge sources, and aligned custom instructions combines those complementary capabilities for a recurring workflow."},{"id":"ccao-f-c34","d":"general","x":"An operations assistant is using Claude to help optimize an existing intake workflow. The assistant can access the current process documentation and recent cycle-time data.\n\nWhich activity makes the best use of Claude to support this process-optimization task?","o":["Generate an optimized version of the intake process based on industry benchmarks, then compare it to the current process to identify gaps.","Apply Claude to predict the cycle-time impact of each proposed change and rank them so the team can implement improvements in priority order.","Map the current intake process, identify likely bottlenecks based on the cycle-time data, and propose optimization options for human review.","Use Claude to interview each team member who works on the intake process and synthesize the interview output into a redesigned workflow."],"a":[2],"e":"Claude can analyze provided documentation and cycle-time data to map the current workflow, surface likely bottlenecks, and suggest optimization alternatives. Human review remains necessary because language-model outputs should be grounded in supplied evidence and validated before operational decisions are made."},{"id":"ccao-f-c35","d":"general","x":"An associate is responding to an executive who asserts that Claude will remove the need for any human review of customer-facing content. The executive approves the team’s AI-tools budget.\n\nWhich response is most appropriate?","o":["Escalate the executive’s claim to a senior leader outside the team before responding directly, since the issue affects more than just the associate’s team.","Pilot the executive’s proposal on a low-risk subset of customer-facing content first, and use the pilot results to make the case for retaining human review on higher-risk content.","Defer to the executive’s authority and remove the human review step from customer-facing content, monitoring the results closely for the first several weeks.","Acknowledge the productivity gains, then explain that human review remains essential on customer-facing content because of accuracy and brand risk, with specific examples."],"a":[3],"e":"Generative-model output can still contain factual errors, violate organizational policies, or fail to meet brand standards. Human review remains an important control for customer-facing material, particularly where accuracy and reputation matter; acknowledging efficiency gains while explaining those risks with concrete examples is the appropriate stakeholder response. Anthropic’s customer-support guidance recommends guardrails, fact-checking, and verification that agent responses align with company policies and known facts."},{"id":"ccao-f-c36","d":"general","x":"A support representative is setting up a Project to draft customer-facing apology emails following service outages. The current custom instructions tell Claude to “be empathetic and casual, keep it short, and offer a goodwill credit.” The customer-service team has reported that recent drafts are too brief, offer goodwill credits when policy does not permit them, and use contractions prohibited by the brand voice guide.\n\nWhich change best aligns the configuration with the use case?","o":["Replace “be empathetic and casual” with “match the brand voice guide,” remove the instruction to offer goodwill credits, and let each draft determine its own length based on the incident.","Move the brand voice guide and the goodwill-credit policy into the Project knowledge base and rely on the documents alone to govern tone, length, and credit decisions, without changing the custom instructions.","Specify the brand voice attributes (empathetic, formal, no contractions), set a minimum length, and instruct Claude to recommend a goodwill credit only when the attached eligibility policy permits one.","Keep the existing instructions and add a sentence telling Claude to consult the brand voice guide and the goodwill-credit policy that are attached to the Project knowledge base."],"a":[2],"e":"Custom instructions should state the required voice attributes and operational constraints explicitly: empathetic but formal language with no contractions, a minimum draft length, and goodwill-credit recommendations only when the attached eligibility policy authorizes them. This prevents the overly brief, casual, and policy-inconsistent outputs."},{"id":"ccao-f-c37","d":"general","x":"You are an HR specialist assessing model selections made by colleagues for different tasks. Which two model choices are appropriate matches between the model and the task?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["a higher-capability model selected solely because it is the most expensive option","a higher-capability model for one-line keyword extraction at high request volume","a higher-capability model for complex multi-step legal reasoning under review","a lighter, faster model for nuanced multi-document strategic synthesis","a lighter, faster model for routine FAQ-style replies under tight latency budgets"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Higher-capability models are appropriate for complex, multi-step reasoning tasks such as legal analysis, where stronger reasoning quality is needed and human review can validate the result. Lighter, faster models are appropriate for routine, well-defined FAQ responses when low latency is a priority. Model selection should be based on task complexity, quality needs, cost, and latency—not price alone."},{"id":"ccao-f-c38","d":"general","x":"You are a project manager at Orinexa, Inc. You are selecting a Claude model for a new production workload and must finish the requirements steps before testing candidates.\n\nWhich two steps must be completed BEFORE testing candidate models on a representative sample?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Define the quality bar, the latency tolerance, and the expected request volume.","Retest the selected model whenever a new model version is released.","Choose a candidate model based on the requirements profile and known capabilities.","Promote the selected model to production traffic for the workload.","Sign off on the production rollout plan with the platform and security teams."],"a":[0,2],"e":"A production model evaluation needs measurable success criteria and operational constraints—including quality, latency, and expected request volume—so results can be judged against the workload’s requirements. Candidate models must also be shortlisted according to that requirements profile and their known capabilities before they are evaluated on representative traffic. Anthropic guidance calls for defining measurable success criteria before building evaluations, and AWS guidance describes benchmarking candidate models against a workload-specific evaluation set after determining the required quality and latency characteristics."},{"id":"ccao-f-c39","d":"general","x":"You are a communications specialist who is preparing to explain Claude’s role in a workflow to several stakeholder groups and must finish the preparation activities before drafting messages.\n\nWhich two preparation activities must be completed BEFORE drafting the stakeholder messages?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Archive the drafted messages in the corporate communications repository.","Identify the stakeholder groups and what each group needs to understand","Define the key facts about Claude’s role and the boundaries that apply.","Send the drafted messages out and gather feedback for revision.","Schedule a follow-up forum to handle questions raised by the messages."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Effective stakeholder messaging requires identifying each stakeholder group and what it needs to understand, along with defining the key facts about Claude’s role and the boundaries that apply. These inputs establish the message content and audience framing before drafting begins."},{"id":"ccao-f-c40","d":"general","x":"You are an operations manager creating a quick-reference card for a team.\n\nWhich description accurately characterizes the three Claude model tiers in general terms?","o":["Haiku is the most capable model for the deepest reasoning, while Opus is the lightweight model for quick replies.","All three models offer the same reasoning depth and differ only in response speed and cost.","Haiku is fast and lightweight, Sonnet is the balanced default for most everyday work, and Opus is the most capable for complex reasoning at higher latency and cost.","Sonnet is the fastest of the three models because its architecture is optimized for speed rather than reasoning depth."],"a":[2],"e":"Claude Haiku is the lightweight, fast, lower-cost tier; Claude Sonnet balances capability, efficiency, and cost for general work; and Claude Opus is the highest-capability tier for demanding, complex reasoning, with higher cost and typically greater latency."},{"id":"ccao-f-c41","d":"general","x":"You are a project manager having an extended planning conversation with Claude and need to keep it productive as it expands. Which two practices help keep a long planning conversation productive?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Open a new thread for every minor follow-up question to keep threads short.","Restate constraints and goals when the focus shifts to a new subtopic.","Periodically summarize decisions made so far to compress the working state.","Switch context aggressively between unrelated planning topics within one thread","Avoid summarizing so the model retains every original word it has produced."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Reasserting goals and constraints when the discussion moves to a new subtopic maintains alignment with the project’s requirements. Periodically summarizing decisions preserves the important working state in a compact form, helping the model maintain coherence as the conversation approaches context-window limits."},{"id":"ccao-f-c42","d":"general","x":"A finance manager is preparing to discuss a confidential pre-announcement figure with Claude. Which two settings most closely follow sound privacy practices for this discussion?\n\nChoose two","o":["Skip the privacy setting decision and proceed with default behavior, since the conversation will not explicitly instruct Claude to remember the figure.","Disable Memory contribution for the conversation if Incognito mode is not used, so the confidential figure is not retained as persistent context that could surface in later unrelated sessions.","Treat the conversation as appropriate for default Memory settings, since the figure will only be discussed briefly and is unlikely to be retained.","Enable Memory contribution for the conversation so the context carries forward to support follow-up analysis in future sessions.","Open the conversation in Incognito mode, which prevents the conversation from contributing to Memory and avoids persistent retention of the confidential pre-announcement figure across sessions."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Confidential financial information should not be allowed to become persistent context that could be surfaced in later, unrelated sessions. Disable the conversation’s Memory contribution when using a standard conversation, or use Incognito mode to prevent the conversation from contributing to Memory. Anthropic also advises care when sharing highly sensitive financial information and confidential business documents."},{"id":"ccao-f-c43","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is explaining how feedback can be used to improve Claude-supported work over time.\n\nWhich description is the most accurate?","o":["Capture specific feedback after each run, translate it into targeted prompt or workflow changes, and observe whether subsequent runs improve.","Capture feedback only after several runs so patterns emerge from the volume of observations, then apply changes based on the patterns rather than any single run.","Capture feedback as an aggregate quality score per run, since aggregate scores show whether quality is improving without requiring detail on individual issues.","Capture general impressions after each run and apply them as broad prompt rewrites, since broad rewrites cover more potential issues than targeted changes."],"a":[0],"e":"An effective improvement loop captures specific feedback after each run, maps it to targeted prompt or workflow adjustments, and checks later runs for measurable improvement. Specific, timely feedback makes the cause of a change and its effect on output quality easier to evaluate."},{"id":"ccao-f-c44","d":"general","x":"You are a Claude associate investigating a recurring instance of poor output.\n\nWhich diagnostic-step sequence is correct?\n\n1. Develop a hypothesis about the prompt element most likely responsible for the deficiency.\n2. Make one targeted change and observe its effect on the output.\n3. Read the output from beginning to end and identify the specific deficiency.\n4. Compare the deficient output with the prompt to find mismatches between the input and output.\n5. Record the root cause and resolution for future reference.","o":["1, 3, 4, 2, 5","4, 3, 1, 2, 5","3, 1, 4, 2, 5","3, 4, 1, 2, 5"],"a":[3],"e":"Effective prompt-output troubleshooting first identifies the precise output defect and compares it with the prompt to establish the relevant mismatch. The resulting evidence supports a causal hypothesis, which should be tested by changing only one targeted element. Documenting the confirmed cause and resolution preserves the result for later reuse."},{"id":"ccao-f-c45","d":"general","x":"You have revised a Claude prompt eight times, and output quality has plateaued during the last four attempts. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","o":["Iteration should stop and the current output should be accepted as-is regardless of remaining issues.","Iteration should stop and the task should be abandoned without communicating to stakeholders.","Iteration has reached diminishing returns and the next step is human review or a different approach.","Iteration should continue indefinitely because more attempts always improve quality."],"a":[2],"e":"When several successive prompt iterations no longer improve output quality, further revisions are producing diminishing returns. Human review or a different approach is the appropriate next step."},{"id":"ccao-f-c46","d":"general","x":"You are a project manager assessing workflow steps by the amount of leverage Claude integration provides. Which workflow step generally delivers the greatest Claude leverage for a knowledge-work team?","o":["approving final deliverables for external publication without further review","negotiating contractual terms with external parties on behalf of the company","drafting and synthesizing across many sources for a downstream reviewer","making final personnel decisions about hiring, promotion, or termination"],"a":[2],"e":"Claude is most useful for drafting and synthesizing information from many sources, where it can accelerate preparation of material while a downstream reviewer retains responsibility for validation and final judgment."},{"id":"ccao-f-c47","d":"general","x":"You are conducting a weekly feedback-and-adjustment cycle.\n\nWhat is the correct sequence of cycle steps?\n\n1. Turn feedback into one or two focused adjustments for the next cycle.\n2. Run the following cycle and compare its results with the previous cycle.\n3. Record the specific feedback received during the review.\n4. Document what changed, the effect it created, and any unresolved issues.\n5. Determine whether to keep iterating, change approaches, or escalate.","o":["2, 3, 1, 4, 5","1, 3, 2, 4, 5","3, 1, 2, 4, 5","3, 2, 1, 4, 5"],"a":[2],"e":"A closed feedback loop records the review feedback before selecting targeted adjustments. The adjusted cycle is then run and measured against the prior cycle; its changes and outcomes are documented so there is a basis for deciding whether to iterate further, change direction, or escalate."},{"id":"ccao-f-c48","d":"general","x":"You are a content creator. Claude’s initial response is accurate but uses inconsistent terminology that does not align with your organization’s glossary. Which iterative revision addresses this issue most directly?","o":["Increase the requested length so the inconsistent terminology appears more often.","Ask Claude to invent additional terms unrelated to the glossary.","Remove the topic from the prompt to avoid the terminology issue entirely.","Provide the organization's glossary and instruct Claude to use only those terms throughout the response."],"a":[3],"e":"Providing the organization’s glossary and requiring exclusive use of its terms gives Claude the approved vocabulary and a clear constraint to apply it consistently throughout the response."},{"id":"ccao-f-c49","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge manager organizing source material within the context window for a lengthy Claude evaluation task.\n\nWhich two practices result in more effective use of the context window?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Paste every available document into context regardless of relevance to the task.","Mark each source clearly with delimiters so the model can attribute claims correctly.","Repeat the same instruction at the start, middle, and end to reinforce it.","Mix sources together in a single block so the model can synthesize them freely.","Place the most reference-critical material near the beginning or end of context."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Clear, descriptive delimiters such as XML tags separate documents, metadata, instructions, and inputs, reducing ambiguity and supporting correct source attribution. For long-context work, placing long-form reference material near the top and the task query near the end improves performance; therefore, reference-critical content should be positioned near a context boundary rather than buried in the middle."},{"id":"ccao-f-c50","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker regularly switches between Claude and another AI service while working on a long-running project.\n\nWhich Claude feature most directly minimizes repetitive context-setting across sessions?","o":["Enable Memory and use the import capability to bring relevant context from the other AI service into Claude as a starting point for the project’s work.","Rely on a single static Artifact pasted into each chat as the only continuity mechanism, ignoring the Memory feature that is purpose-built for retaining work-relevant context across sessions.","Open every Claude session in Incognito mode, which prevents the conversation from contributing to Memory and therefore eliminates the cross-session continuity the project would benefit from.","Disable Memory entirely and re-paste the same context paragraphs at the start of every Claude session, even though Memory is designed to retain that context across conversations automatically."],"a":[0],"e":"Claude Memory retains work-related context for use in new conversations, and its built-in import flow can bring relevant memory from another AI service into Claude. This reduces the need to repeatedly provide the same project context across sessions."},{"id":"ccao-f-c51","d":"general","x":"An HR business partner is setting up a new Project for recurring policy questions from managers. The Project will be used across many chats by several HR team members.\n\nWhich content should belong in the Project knowledge base?","o":["the current employee handbook plus prior versions of the handbook going back five years, so Claude can answer questions about how policies have changed","the current employee handbook, the leveling guide, the policies-FAQ, and every email the HR team has sent about policy in the past year, for additional context","the current employee handbook only, with the leveling guide and the policies-FAQ attached at the start of each chat as needed for that chat’s topic","the current employee handbook, the leveling guide, and the policies-FAQ document referenced across the Project’s chats"],"a":[3],"e":"A Project knowledge base supplies context across all chats in that Project, so it should contain the current, broadly relevant reference documents needed repeatedly for manager policy questions: the current employee handbook, leveling guide, and policies FAQ. Superseded policies and incidental historical emails can introduce outdated or unnecessary context, while recurring reference materials should not need to be attached separately to each chat."},{"id":"ccao-f-c52","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is briefing a stakeholder group about Claude’s limitations. The stakeholders will use Claude for customer-facing work, where confident-sounding output containing errors could cause real harm.\n\nWhich limitation is most important to communicate clearly?","o":["Claude’s responses vary across runs of the same prompt, so stakeholders should run each prompt several times and compare outputs before using any single response.","Claude can produce confident-sounding output that contains unsupported claims, so human review remains essential for high-impact work.","Claude can produce long, detailed responses that take time to read, so stakeholders should request shorter outputs when working under tight deadlines.","Claude’s knowledge has a training cutoff, so stakeholders must verify any time-sensitive claims against current sources before using them."],"a":[1],"e":"Claude can sometimes hallucinate, producing text that is factually incorrect or inconsistent with the provided context despite sounding plausible or confident. Hallucination-mitigation measures cannot eliminate this risk, so critical information and high-impact customer-facing output require human review and verification."},{"id":"ccao-f-c53","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker preparing material for a Claude prompt.\n\nWhich data type most clearly needs additional handling, such as redaction or anonymization, before it is included in the prompt?","o":["public press releases the company has already published","customer government identifiers and full payment-card numbers tied to named individuals","job titles taken from the company’s public organizational chart","generic product descriptions available on the company’s marketing site"],"a":[1],"e":"Government identifiers and full payment-card numbers associated with identifiable people are sensitive personal and financial data. Redacting or anonymizing them reduces unnecessary exposure of personally identifiable information; public company materials and generic public product information do not create the same privacy risk."},{"id":"ccao-f-c54","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead who is integrating Claude into a workflow used by multiple people.\n\nWhich integration step should be completed first?","o":["Identify where Claude adds value and where each handoff to a human occurs.","Pilot the integrated workflow with a small group on representative work.","Iterate the integrated workflow based on the findings from the pilot.","Document the integrated workflow and onboard the rest of the team to it."],"a":[0],"e":"A human–AI workflow must first define the tasks where Claude provides value and the points at which responsibility passes to a human. Those boundaries establish the workflow that can later be piloted, refined from pilot results, documented, and rolled out. Anthropic guidance likewise emphasizes keeping humans in the loop and defining organizational gates around agentic work."},{"id":"ccao-f-c55","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is assessing a proposed use case in which Claude would make final hiring decisions without any human review, using the AI Fluency Framework’s Delegation competency.\n\nWhich classification most accurately reflects Delegation principles?","o":["Inappropriate for full AI delegation, because hiring decisions involve human judgment, accountability, and consequences that Delegation criteria reserve for human ownership rather than autonomous AI execution.","Appropriate with light human review applied only to a sample of decisions, even though Delegation criteria reserve final hiring decisions for human ownership rather than sample-based oversight after the fact.","Appropriate for full AI delegation, on the basis that processing speed outweighs the human judgment, accountability, and consequence considerations that Delegation criteria require for final hiring decisions.","Inappropriate only because of system reliability concerns rather than accountability, even though Delegation criteria classify final hiring decisions as requiring human ownership regardless of system performance."],"a":[0],"e":"Delegation involves strategically distributing work between people and AI based on the task’s nature, the system’s capabilities and limitations, and the need for human expertise and judgment. Final hiring decisions carry consequential human judgment and accountability, so they require human ownership rather than autonomous AI execution."},{"id":"ccao-f-c56","d":"general","x":"You are assessing a Claude output that seems to include hallucinated content. Which response step should be taken first when a hallucination is suspected?","o":["Discard the entire output and regenerate the output with a fresh prompt.","Document the hallucination pattern in the team’s risk register for future reference.","Notify the user community that the output contains a hallucinated claim.","Pinpoint the specific claim that appears to be unsupported by available evidence."],"a":[3],"e":"A hallucination is an assertion that lacks factual support or conflicts with reliable evidence. Identifying the exact unsupported claim establishes what must be verified, corrected, withdrawn, or escalated; regeneration, risk documentation, and user notification are subsequent actions. Anthropic recommends verifying claims with supporting quotations and sources, and withdrawing a claim when no supporting citation can be found."},{"id":"ccao-f-c57","d":"general","x":"You are optimizing a recurring Claude workflow and must finish the diagnostic steps before modifying the workflow. Which two diagnostic steps must be completed BEFORE changing the recurring workflow?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Notify executive leadership of the projected savings from the optimization.","Identify the steps that contribute most to time, cost, or quality issues.","Deploy the updated workflow across the team and monitor for adoption issues.","Measure the current workflow's quality, time, and cost across recent runs.","Document the revised workflow steps and update the team’s standard operating procedures."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Workflow optimization requires a baseline measurement of current quality, time, and cost, plus identification of the steps creating the greatest time, cost, or quality issues. Those diagnostics establish what to change and provide a basis for evaluating whether the revised workflow improves outcomes."},{"id":"ccao-f-c58","d":"general","x":"Before sharing a Claude-generated brief, an analyst applies the AI Fluency Framework’s Discernment competency to assess it. Which review most accurately reflects Discernment principles?","o":["Accept the brief on the basis that it reads fluently and the subject matter aligns with the analyst’s professional knowledge of the topic.","Skip the source material check during review and rely on overall tone and logical flow as proxies for factual accuracy.","Review the opening paragraph and the conclusion of the brief, since these sections typically reflect the quality of the full document.","Conduct a systematic check of the brief against the task requirements, source material, and professional standards before sharing it."],"a":[3],"e":"Discernment involves thoughtfully and critically evaluating AI outputs for quality, including accuracy, relevance, coherence, and appropriateness. Checking the complete brief against its requirements, underlying sources, and professional standards provides the evidence needed before it is shared."},{"id":"ccao-f-c59","d":"general","x":"You are using Claude to redesign an employee onboarding workflow and must finish the high-level decomposition steps before defining detailed artifacts.\n\nWhich two decomposition steps must be completed before specifying the exact email content and schedule?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Pilot the onboarding workflow with a small cohort of new hires.","Identify the major phases of the onboarding experience.","Negotiate the onboarding budget with the finance department.","Define the overall objective of the onboarding experience.","Roll out the onboarding workflow to every new hire across the company."],"a":[1,3],"e":"A sound high-level decomposition begins by defining the onboarding experience’s overall objective and identifying its major phases. Those decisions establish the structure and purpose that detailed artifacts, such as exact email copy and scheduling, must follow."},{"id":"ccao-f-c60","d":"general","x":"You are refining a prompt across several iterations. With every change, the output drifts farther from the requested template. Which step is most appropriate?","o":["Discard all prior versions and start with no record of what worked.","Continue making large simultaneous changes until the output happens to align.","Revert to the version of the prompt that produced the closest match and adjust from that baseline.","Stop iterating and accept the most off-target version."],"a":[2],"e":"Prompt refinement is most effective when it resumes from the version that most closely met the required template. That baseline preserves the successful elements and enables small, controlled adjustments instead of compounding drift."},{"id":"ccao-f-c61","d":"general","x":"You are assessing items proposed for inclusion in a Project that supports policy summaries.\n\nWhich two items are suitable to include in the Project?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["the summary format and the audience expectations the summaries must meet","the current policy library that the summaries are drawn from","presentation slides from a recent team training session on internal communication standards","active client case files maintained by a separate legal team for a different workstream","employee onboarding records used in a separate HR workflow managed by the same team"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Project instructions can define how ChatGPT should respond, including required format and audience expectations. A Project can also contain reference materials that ChatGPT uses to produce informed results, so the current policy library belongs in the policy-summary workflow. Unrelated training, legal-case, and HR-onboarding materials are outside that workflow and should not be added as its context."},{"id":"ccao-f-c62","d":"general","x":"You are categorizing structural defects in Claude output by how much they undermine clarity. Which structural defect most seriously impairs the output’s clarity?","o":["A single subheading is shorter than the other subheadings in the document.","Two adjacent paragraphs present the same information using inconsistent terminology.","Typographic inconsistencies appear throughout multiple sections of the document.","The main conclusion contradicts the evidence presented in the body."],"a":[3],"e":"A main conclusion that conflicts with the evidence in the body creates a fundamental logical inconsistency. It leaves the reader unable to determine which claim is supported, undermining the clarity and reliability of the output’s central message."},{"id":"ccao-f-c63","d":"general","x":"As a communications manager, you are validating a Claude-generated newsletter that contains a specific industry-growth figure. The figure is reported in three trade-press articles, but none links to the underlying study.\n\nWhich validation step is appropriate?","o":["Accept the figure because three trade-press articles repeat it.","Remove the source attribution and present the figure as common knowledge.","Locate the original study referenced by the trade-press articles and verify the figure in the primary source.","Use the highest figure across the three articles to make the newsletter more compelling."],"a":[2],"e":"A figure repeated across secondary trade-press articles can originate from the same unverified claim. Validating it requires locating the underlying study and confirming the figure, its methodology, scope, and context in the primary source."},{"id":"ccao-f-c64","d":"general","x":"You are preparing a document with mixed-sensitivity content for use with Claude.\n\nWhich data-handling action should be completed first?","o":["Redact sensitive fields and replace them with appropriate placeholders.","Classify the content of the document and identify which fields are sensitive.","Submit the redacted document to Claude for the intended task.","Review the redacted document with a peer to confirm the redaction is complete."],"a":[1],"e":"Sensitive fields must be identified through content classification before they can be safely redacted or replaced. This also supports the least-privilege principle: Claude should not receive sensitive information that it does not need."},{"id":"ccao-f-c65","d":"general","x":"A finance consultant is revising a Claude-generated narrative for a quarterly investor update. Investors have a fixed time window for the call and expect the headline result and forward outlook to be made clear early.\n\nWhich adaptation is most suitable for this audience?","o":["Lead with the quarter’s operational highlights, present the financial result in the middle for context, and close with the forward outlook.","Lead with the forward outlook, since investors are most interested in what comes next, and present the headline result and drivers afterward.","Lead with the headline result, present the key drivers concisely, and close with the forward outlook.","Lead with a chronological recap of the quarter’s events, present the headline result at the natural end of the recap, and conclude with the forward outlook."],"a":[2],"e":"Quarterly investor communications should prioritize the headline financial result early, followed by a concise explanation of its key drivers and the forward outlook. This structure makes the most decision-relevant information clear within a limited time window."},{"id":"ccao-f-c66","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is describing the purpose of an organizational AI governance framework to a colleague.\n\nWhich description is the most accurate?","o":["A governance framework defines approved tools, allowed data, required reviews, and reporting paths so AI is used consistently and accountably.","A governance framework lists the AI tools the organization licenses and the cost of each, so users can pick the most cost-effective tool for each task.","A governance framework defines the training every employee must complete before using AI tools, with separate requirements for each tool the organization licenses.","A governance framework documents the AI policies the organization is legally required to adopt, focusing on regulatory compliance rather than day-to-day use."],"a":[0],"e":"An AI governance framework sets organizational policies, processes, controls, roles, and accountability for AI throughout its use. It can define permitted tools and data, review requirements, and reporting or escalation paths so AI is used consistently, responsibly, and accountably. NIST identifies governance as establishing transparent policies, procedures, and controls, with documented roles and communication lines for AI risk management."},{"id":"ccao-f-c67","d":"general","x":"A senior project coordinator has been asked to use Claude to create an end-of-year report covering finance, operations, and customer outcomes. The three areas have distinct data owners and separate source documents.\n\nWhich decomposition approach is most likely to result in a strong report?","o":["Define the report goals, gather inputs per section, draft each section in its own prompt, then synthesize the executive summary from the drafted sections.","Draft the executive summary first to anchor the report's narrative, then draft each section so it supports the summary already written.","Draft the three sections in parallel, each using a section-specific prompt, then concatenate and lightly edit the outputs into the final report.","Prompt Claude once with all source documents and request the full report, including the executive summary, in a single pass."],"a":[0],"e":"A strong cross-functional report is developed by defining its goals, collecting the appropriate inputs for each section, drafting those sections with focused prompts, and deriving the executive summary from the completed sections. This supports traceability to each area’s source material and ensures that the summary reflects the report’s actual findings."},{"id":"ccao-f-c68","d":"general","x":"A customer-success associate is preparing a prompt asking Claude to summarize a customer issue from a case file. The case file contains the customer’s full government identifier, home address, name, account number, and a description of the issue. The associate needs a summary focused on the issue.\n\nWhich approach is most appropriate?","o":["Submit the full case file and include an instruction telling Claude not to reference the government identifier or the home address in the summary.","Replace the government identifier with the customer’s name and the home address with the account number, since names and account numbers are less sensitive than identifiers and addresses.","Remove or redact the government identifier and the home address before submitting the prompt, since neither is necessary for a summary of the issue.","Submit only the description of the issue and add the customer’s name for context, since the description plus a name is sufficient to produce a summary."],"a":[2],"e":"Data minimization requires providing only the information needed for the intended task. Because the summary is about the issue, the government identifier and home address are unnecessary sensitive data and should be removed or redacted before submission."},{"id":"ccao-f-c69","d":"general","x":"You are a process consultant incorporating Claude into an existing weekly workflow. Which sequence of integration steps is correct?\n\n1. Pilot the integrated workflow with a small group and collect feedback.\n2. Document the current workflow’s steps, owners, and decision points.\n3. Update the documented workflow and deploy it to the broader team.\n4. Identify the steps where Claude can augment work and where humans must remain.\n5. Define the inputs Claude requires, the outputs Claude provides, and the hand-offs around each step.","o":["2, 4, 5, 1, 3","2, 5, 4, 1, 3","4, 2, 5, 1, 3","1, 2, 4, 5, 3"],"a":[0],"e":"Workflow integration should begin with documenting the current process so its ownership and decision points are understood. That enables appropriate allocation of Claude-assisted and human-controlled work, followed by a clear specification of inputs, outputs, and hand-offs. A limited pilot validates the integrated process and gathers feedback before the workflow is revised and rolled out broadly. Anthropic guidance similarly recommends breaking an interaction into tasks, defining success criteria, and evaluating it systematically before production deployment."},{"id":"ccao-f-c70","d":"general","x":"A customer-success team member reviews a Claude-generated customer health-check report against the agreed deliverable list. The report addresses all five required topics, but its renewal-risk section identifies only one risk factor, whereas the deliverable specification requires renewal risk to be evaluated across pricing, product fit, and stakeholder change.\n\nWhich conclusion is best supported?","o":["The report’s completeness cannot be assessed from the deliverable specification alone; the customer should review the output and confirm whether the renewal-risk depth is sufficient.","The report does not meet the deliverable specification because a section that addresses only one of three required risk dimensions counts as a missing section.","The report meets the deliverable specification because all five required topics appear and the renewal-risk section is present in the output.","The report meets the topic-level requirements but the renewal-risk section is under-scoped against the specification and should be expanded before delivery."],"a":[3],"e":"A deliverable can satisfy the presence of each required topic while still failing a topic-specific depth requirement. Renewal risk must cover pricing, product fit, and stakeholder change; addressing only one factor leaves that section under-scoped and requires expansion before delivery."},{"id":"ccao-f-c71","d":"general","x":"An analyst has been asked to calculate summary statistics from an uploaded sales-data file and create a chart for a weekly report. Which output approach is the best fit for this task?","o":["Ask Claude to estimate the summary statistics from a brief preview of the file, accepting unverified figures rather than running the calculations against the full uploaded dataset.","Produce a written placeholder for the chart in the chat response without generating the actual visualization, leaving the weekly report without the visual output it requires.","Request a narrative description of the data in the chat response without computing values, leaving the summary statistics and the chart unproduced and the weekly report incomplete.","Use Code Execution to perform the calculations and generate the chart, then verify the computed results against the source data before sharing the output."],"a":[3],"e":"Code Execution can calculate statistics from the full uploaded dataset, create the required chart, and support verification of the computed outputs against the underlying source data before they are shared."},{"id":"ccao-f-c72","d":"general","x":"You are preparing system-level instructions for a Project that supports recurring writing tasks.\n\nWhich action should be completed first when creating the system-level instructions?","o":["Refine the drafted instructions based on observed gaps from validation runs.","Validate the drafted instructions against representative requests for the Project.","Define the purpose of the Project and the boundaries the instructions must respect.","Distribute the finalized instructions to collaborators on the Project."],"a":[2],"e":"Effective system-level instructions begin by establishing the Project’s purpose and the boundaries that govern its behavior. Those requirements determine what the instructions should direct; drafting, validation, refinement, and distribution can occur only after that foundation is defined. Project instructions provide project-specific guidance for how ChatGPT should help within the Project."},{"id":"ccao-f-c73","d":"general","x":"A business analyst is asked to use Claude to extract requirements from a recorded stakeholder-interview transcript. The transcript is about 90 minutes long and addresses multiple topics.\n\nWhich approach is most likely to yield usable structured output?","o":["Provide the transcript along with a structured prompt that requests must-haves, nice-to-haves, open questions, and conflicts as separate sections.","Provide the transcript and ask Claude to produce a single ranked list of requirements, since a single list is easier for stakeholders to review than separate sections.","Provide the transcript and ask Claude to summarize it; extract requirements from the summary in a second pass since two passes catch more detail.","Provide the transcript and ask Claude to produce a chronological list of every statement that contains a verb, then classify each statement as a requirement or not."],"a":[0],"e":"Explicitly requesting separate categories for mandatory requirements, optional requirements, unresolved questions, and conflicts gives the model a clear output structure and preserves distinctions needed for requirements analysis. Clear, specific formatting instructions improve control over Claude’s response format."},{"id":"ccao-f-c74","d":"general","x":"You received an imprecise request to “look at our pricing.” After discovery, the objective is confirmed: create a structured competitor-pricing summary the team can act on, rather than develop new pricing ideas.\n\nWhich strategy best suits the clarified task?","o":["Use a divergent brainstorming approach to generate a wide range of possible competitor-pricing observations, then narrow to the most likely ones.","Use a comparative analysis strategy that contrasts the company’s pricing against a single named competitor, since pairwise comparison produces the clearest output.","Use a research strategy that gathers structured information about competitor pricing, names sources, and notes confidence in each finding.","Use an open conversational strategy that explores competitor pricing iteratively across several prompts, allowing the structure to emerge from the conversation."],"a":[2],"e":"A structured competitor-pricing summary requires research that collects comparable pricing information, identifies the sources for each finding, and records confidence or uncertainty so the team can judge how actionable the information is. This is an evidence-gathering and synthesis task, not brainstorming, a restricted single-competitor comparison, or open-ended conversation."},{"id":"ccao-f-c75","d":"general","x":"You are a customer-success manager deciding whether to escalate a Claude-generated response. Which factor most strongly increases the output’s impact and warrants escalation before it is used externally?","o":["The response is an internal draft summarizing the associate’s notes from a customer call for their own reference.","The response is a casual rephrasing of an internal team status update.","The response is a brief internal announcement about an upcoming team all-hands meeting.","The response is an unchanged draft for a paying customer that includes a service-level commitment."],"a":[3],"e":"An unchanged customer-facing draft that makes a service-level commitment can create an external promise with contractual, operational, and financial consequences. Its direct effect on a paying customer makes human review and escalation appropriate before use. Anthropic advises review by a qualified professional before disseminating sensitive content or decisions to consumers."},{"id":"ccao-f-c76","d":"general","x":"An HR associate is getting ready to use Claude for a sensitive personnel matter. The organization’s AI policy expressly says that information concerning ongoing investigations must not be entered into AI tools.\n\nWhich response most closely follows the policy?","o":["Enter the investigation-related information into Claude only after the investigation closes, since the policy applies during the investigation period.","Refrain from entering investigation-related information into Claude and consult HR leadership for an approved alternative way to handle the matter.","Enter the investigation-related information into Claude in an Incognito chat, since Incognito chats do not retain information and therefore fall outside the policy’s intent.","Enter a paraphrased version of the investigation-related information that removes identifying details, since paraphrasing addresses the spirit of the policy."],"a":[1],"e":"Information connected to an ongoing investigation must not be entered into an AI tool under the organization’s explicit policy. The appropriate course is to withhold the information from Claude and consult HR leadership for an approved alternative process."},{"id":"ccao-f-c77","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is refining a prompt that has generated a partially acceptable response.\n\nWhich refinement practice yields the strongest learning across iterations?","o":["Change one identifiable element per iteration, capture the change and its effect, and adjust another element only after the first is settled.","Restart from a fresh prompt each iteration so that prior wording does not bias the next attempt, and pick the strongest response at the end.","Make several related changes in one iteration when they target the same weakness, and revert the whole set if the combined change makes the output worse.","Address every weakness the response showed in a single revision, then compare the revised output against the prior version."],"a":[0],"e":"Controlled prompt refinement changes one identifiable variable at a time and records the resulting effect. This isolates which change caused an improvement or regression, enabling reliable learning and deliberate subsequent adjustments."},{"id":"ccao-f-c78","d":"general","x":"You are assessing Connector configurations that colleagues have suggested for different tasks. Which two Connector configurations are safe and suitable uses?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["a Connector that links to the personnel-records system for team leads to support scheduling tasks","a Connector that links to the team's project management workspace under approved scope","a Connector that links to a personal cloud storage account where team member’s store working drafts alongside personal files","a read-only Connector that links to the public knowledge base for reference content","a Connector that links to the payroll system to allow team members to submit expense reports directly"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Appropriate Connector use follows least privilege, approved role-scoped access, and read-only access where writing is unnecessary. A Connector limited to an approved team project-management workspace and a read-only Connector to public reference content meet those constraints. Personnel, personal mixed-use storage, and payroll systems expose sensitive data or high-impact capabilities beyond the stated needs."},{"id":"ccao-f-c79","d":"general","x":"A communications manager is preparing a prompt for Claude to create a quarterly newsletter for store managers across three regions. The agreed key messages, regional information, and a previous newsletter the team liked are all available.\n\nWhich prompt strategy is most likely to yield a usable first draft?","o":["Attach the prior newsletter, and ask Claude to match its tone and structure while incorporating the new key messages for the three named regions.","Attach the prior newsletter and the agreed key messages, and ask Claude to produce a similar newsletter for this quarter.","Provide the agreed key messages and the three regions, and ask Claude to choose an appropriate length and tone for store managers.","Name the audience, the three regions, the length, the tone, and the agreed key messages, and reference the prior newsletter only as a tone example."],"a":[3],"e":"Clear, explicit instructions about the intended audience, regional scope, output length, tone, and required key messages give Claude the constraints needed to produce a suitable draft. A prior newsletter can effectively guide the requested tone without substituting for the current content requirements. Anthropic’s prompt-engineering guidance recommends clearly specifying the desired output and using relevant examples and details."},{"id":"ccao-f-c80","d":"general","x":"You are an HR specialist categorizing potential ethical risks in a Claude-supported workflow.\n\nWhich two risks raise the greatest ethical concern in an HR-adjacent workflow?\n\nEach correct answer represents a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["decisions that materially affect a person’s employment without human accountability","a clerical typo in a routine internal calendar invitation","inconsistent tone and formatting across internal HR communications sent to employees","inconsistent use of the company’s preferred dash style across documents","outputs that systematically disadvantage members of a protected group"],"a":[0,4],"e":"High-impact employment decisions require human accountability because they can materially affect a person’s livelihood. Outputs that systematically disadvantage a protected group create discriminatory harm and are therefore a critical ethical concern."},{"id":"ccao-f-c81","d":"general","x":"You are an operations assistant asking Claude to summarize 200 weekly metric records across several regions. Which output format presents the data most clearly?","o":["a bulleted list of observations grouped by region without consistent metrics across entries","a narrative summary organized by region, describing trends and notable records in prose","a series of regional narrative briefs, each describing weekly performance in paragraph form","a structured table with one record per row and one metric per column, sorted by region"],"a":[3],"e":"A structured table makes a large, multi-region dataset easy to scan, compare, sort, and analyze because records and metrics occupy consistent rows and columns."},{"id":"ccao-f-c82","d":"general","x":"You are an analyst reviewing a Claude response that answers two of three required questions but omits the third. Which prompt element most likely needs to be strengthened?","o":["The context provided to Claude should be expanded with additional background detail about the subject matter.","The output format should be revised to allow Claude to determine the appropriate structure for each question’s answer.","The role assigned to Claude should be changed to a more specialized expert persona to increase response depth.","The task statement should explicitly enumerate the three required questions and require an answer to each."],"a":[3],"e":"Explicitly enumerating the three required questions and directing Claude to answer each one makes task-completeness requirements unambiguous and reduces the risk that a required item is skipped."},{"id":"ccao-f-c83","d":"general","x":"An HR team member is reviewing a Claude-generated set of interview questions meant to be used with every candidate for one role. The questions will be asked in the same sequence to each candidate.\n\nWhich review practice best promotes fairness?","o":["Review the questions to ensure they apply equally to all candidates, avoid assumptions about candidate backgrounds, and focus on the role’s actual requirements.","Review the questions for length and tone, since the questions Claude drafts are well-aligned to role requirements by default and need only stylistic adjustment.","Review the questions and add follow-up questions tailored to each candidate’s resume so each interview can explore the candidate’s specific experience.","Review the questions and rotate the order across candidates so no candidate is consistently asked the hardest questions first."],"a":[0],"e":"Fair interview questions are job-related, avoid assumptions about protected or personal backgrounds, and can be applied consistently to every candidate. The EEOC identifies asking the same questions for a job and focusing inquiries directly on the position as practices that support nondiscriminatory treatment."},{"id":"ccao-f-c84","d":"general","x":"You are a marketing manager using Claude to research a new market segment. Which research step should be completed first?","o":["Generate a final research report combining every Claude output produced.","Submit a series of Claude prompts to gather information across the segment.","Validate the gathered information against authoritative external sources.","Define the specific research questions that must be answered for the segment."],"a":[3],"e":"Research must begin by defining the specific questions to be answered, because they set the scope for information gathering, source validation, and the final report. Anthropic’s guidance likewise emphasizes clearly defining specific, measurable objectives before subsequent evaluation work."},{"id":"ccao-f-c85","d":"general","x":"An HR business partner is setting up two separate Projects for two different client engagements.\n\nWhich configuration most effectively prevents context bleed between the two engagements?","o":["Set up each Project with its own distinct memory context, knowledge sources, and instructions, and verify that information from one Project does not appear in the other Project’s responses.","Use a single shared Project for both client engagements with combined memory, knowledge sources, and instructions, on the assumption that Claude will keep the two clients’ contexts separate without configuration.","Configure both Projects to share the same memory context to reduce setup effort, even though shared memory between unrelated client engagements is precisely the configuration that produces context bleed.","Skip Project configuration entirely and use general chats for both engagements, which provides no scoped memory contexts and offers no structural separation between the two clients’ work streams."],"a":[0],"e":"Separate Claude Projects provide self-contained workspaces with project-specific knowledge and instructions. Assigning distinct context, knowledge sources, and instructions to each client engagement preserves the boundary between their work and prevents one client’s information from being used in the other engagement."},{"id":"ccao-f-c86","d":"general","x":"You are an operations assistant choosing a model for a recurring meeting-summary task that needs no deep reasoning and operates at moderate volume.\n\nWhich choice best balances cost, speed, and quality?","o":["Haiku, which is suitable only when the task requires the fastest possible turnaround.","Opus, which prioritizes depth at higher latency and rate-limit consumption.","Sonnet, which provides solid quality at moderate latency and rate-limit consumption for everyday work.","A custom model, which the Associate-level user would need to build and train independently."],"a":[2],"e":"Sonnet is the balanced Claude model tier for routine work that needs solid output quality without the higher cost and latency associated with Opus. Haiku is optimized for simpler, high-speed and high-volume workloads, while Opus is intended for the most demanding reasoning tasks."},{"id":"ccao-f-c87","d":"general","x":"You are a project manager assessing Claude usage methods that teammates have proposed for a project workflow.\n\nWhich two workflow steps are appropriate for Claude support?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["approving a final external press release without any human review","issuing legally binding contractual commitments on behalf of the company","deciding promotion outcomes for staff based solely on the model’s judgment","synthesizing a literature review across many uploaded research documents","drafting a structured project status update from a set of meeting notes"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Claude can synthesize information across uploaded research documents to support a literature review and can turn meeting notes into a structured project-status-update draft. These are assistive drafting and summarization tasks that remain subject to human review, unlike autonomous final press approval, legally binding commitments, or sole-model employment decisions."},{"id":"ccao-f-c88","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead aligning production tasks with Claude’s model strengths to establish realistic expectations.\n\nWhich two production tasks most clearly match Claude’s strengths?\n\nEach correct answer represents a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["monitoring a live sensor data stream and flagging anomalies as they occur in real time","issuing a binding legal determination based solely on Claude’s analysis of case documents","drafting a tone-and-audience-aware customer-facing email under human review","calculating the precise settlement value of a derivative contract using live market inputs","synthesizing a single executive summary across many long source documents"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Claude is designed for language and analysis tasks, including generating text and summarizing source material. It can draft a customer-facing email using specified tone and audience requirements under human review, and its large context capacity supports synthesizing an executive summary from numerous long documents. Binding legal judgments, exact financial settlement calculations using live inputs, and real-time anomaly detection require specialized controls, authoritative data, or deterministic and accountable decision processes beyond those language-model strengths."},{"id":"ccao-f-c89","d":"general","x":"A learning specialist is setting up a Project for course-design work. The organization has an internal instructional-design standard and a preferred format for lesson outlines.\n\nWhich set of custom instructions best supports this use case?","o":["Name Claude’s role as a course designer; let each chat’s prompt specify the standards and output structure for that lesson, since lessons vary widely.","Name Claude’s role as a course designer and require alignment to the organization’s instructional-design standards; leave output structure to Claude based on the topic of each lesson.","Specify only the preferred output structure for lesson outlines, since the structure carries the standards implicitly and naming the role adds no value.","Name Claude’s role as a course designer, require alignment to the organization’s instructional-design standards, and specify the preferred output structure for lesson outlines."],"a":[3],"e":"Project instructions apply to all chats within a Project and are intended to establish project-specific context, workflow guidelines, task requirements, and the role or perspective Claude should adopt. Including the course-designer role, the organization’s instructional-design standards, and the required lesson-outline structure provides consistent guidance for every lesson."},{"id":"ccao-f-c90","d":"general","x":"You are a project manager evaluating stakeholder messages that teammates have drafted regarding Claude’s role in a workflow. Which two messages are appropriate for stakeholder communication about Claude?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["a claim that the model never makes errors and requires no quality review","a vague reassurance that “the AI is taking care of it” without further detail","a claim that Claude will handle every task without any human involvement","a clear statement of the limits and known failure modes of the integration","an honest description of where Claude assists and where humans retain decisions"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Claude can generate factually incorrect or contextually inconsistent output, and mitigation techniques do not eliminate that risk; critical information still requires validation. Appropriate stakeholder communication therefore states the integration’s limitations and known failure modes, while honestly defining Claude’s assistance role and the decisions that remain with humans."},{"id":"ccao-f-c91","d":"general","x":"You are reviewing a competitor brief generated by Claude for unsupported claims.\n\nWhat is the correct sequence of review steps?\n\n1. Cross-check each specific claim against the provided source material or reliable external references.\n2. Determine whether to correct, re-prompt, or escalate based on the findings.\n3. Record every unsupported or inconsistent claim with its location and severity.\n4. Identify the specific factual claims, statistics, and citations in the brief.\n5. Read the brief from start to finish and flag any claims that seem surprising.","o":["1, 4, 5, 3, 2","4, 5, 1, 3, 2","5, 1, 4, 3, 2","5, 4, 1, 3, 2"],"a":[3],"e":"An effective unsupported-claim review starts with a complete read-through to spot potentially questionable content, followed by identifying the exact claims, statistics, and citations requiring validation. Those items are then checked against authoritative evidence, documented when unsupported or inconsistent, and used to determine the appropriate remediation or escalation."},{"id":"ccao-f-c92","d":"general","x":"You are creating a checklist to evaluate Claude outputs and must finish the upstream steps before scoring an output. Which two steps must be completed BEFORE scoring a Claude output against the checklist?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Define the evaluation criteria that matter for the task at hand.","Promote the approved output into the downstream production process.","Forward unacceptable outputs to a senior reviewer for second-pass review.","Specify the threshold that distinguishes acceptable from unacceptable output.","Document the final score and the reviewer comments for the audit trail."],"a":[0,3],"e":"An evaluation checklist requires task-relevant criteria to determine what quality dimensions are assessed and an acceptance threshold to determine whether the assessed output passes. Those definitions must exist before any score can be assigned; production routing, escalation, and audit recording are downstream activities."},{"id":"ccao-f-c93","d":"general","x":"You are a business analyst determining which output-formatting and context-window practices to recommend to teammates. Which two practices constitute effective use of the context window?\n\nEach correct answer provides part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Reuse the same context block across multiple requests to avoid rebuilding it each time.","Concatenate all source documents end-to-end with no separators between them.","Paste full email threads related to the project into context so the model has complete conversational history.","Trim irrelevant material before submitting the request to keep context focused.","Use clear section headers to separate different parts of the input material."],"a":[3,4],"e":"A context window has finite input capacity, so removing irrelevant content keeps the available tokens focused on information needed for the task. Clear section headers delineate separate input materials and make their relationships and purposes easier for the model to interpret. Oversized inputs can otherwise require truncation or fail when they exceed the model’s input limit."},{"id":"ccao-f-c94","d":"general","x":"When preparing an AI-assisted work product for external delivery, a senior manager is applying the AI Fluency Framework’s Diligence competency.\n\nWhich step most clearly reflects Diligence principles?","o":["Deliver the work product without independent verification on the basis that AI assistance shifts accountability away from the human, even though Diligence holds the human fully accountable for the output.","Delegate accountability for the AI-assisted work product to Claude itself rather than retaining it as a human obligation, contrary to the Diligence principle that humans remain accountable for AI-assisted outputs.","Skip verification of any element on the assumption that fluent output implies accuracy, even though Diligence requires the human to verify and correct AI-assisted outputs before they are acted upon.","Identify the elements of the work product that must be independently verified or corrected before delivery, and take ownership of those verifications as a professional accountability obligation."],"a":[3],"e":"Deployment Diligence requires the human using AI to verify and vouch for outputs that will be used or shared. Professional accountability remains with the human, so identifying what needs independent verification or correction and personally owning those checks before external delivery fulfills the requirement."},{"id":"ccao-f-c95","d":"general","x":"You are reviewing colleagues’ claims from a recent training about prompt iteration. Which two claims about prompt iteration are accurate?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Repeated resubmission of the same prompt is the most reliable way to improve output.","Output comparison against original criteria validates whether iteration helped.","Iteration is most productive when each change targets a specific diagnosed weakness.","Iteration should stop only after every possible alternative phrasing has been tried.","More iterations always produce a better output regardless of what was changed."],"a":[1,2],"e":"Prompt iteration should be guided by explicit evaluation criteria: comparing revised output with the original criteria shows whether the change helped. Iterations are most effective when each revision addresses a specific identified weakness, rather than making arbitrary or repeated changes."},{"id":"ccao-f-c96","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is examining Claude-generated customer-segment descriptions for an internal marketing brief. One description makes a generalization about a demographic group’s purchasing behavior that is not true for individual customers within that segment.\n\nWhich response is most appropriate?","o":["Revise the description to focus on observed behaviors rather than generalizations about people, and validate the revision against actual customer data.","Remove the entire customer-segment description from the brief, since any segmentation that involves demographic characteristics is likely to introduce similar issues.","Keep the description as written for the internal brief, since internal use does not carry the same risk as external publication of demographic generalizations.","Add a disclaimer to the description, noting that the generalization may not apply to all individuals in the segment, and keep the underlying description unchanged."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c97","d":"general","x":"You are conducting an optimization pass on a recurring workflow.\n\nWhat is the correct sequence of optimization steps?\n\n1. Apply targeted optimization to the bottleneck step\n2. Roll out the optimized workflow to the wider team and document the change\n3. Identify the bottleneck step by using measured time per step and queue length\n4. Map the existing workflow steps, owners, and decision points\n5. Pilot the change with a small group and measure its effect against the previous baseline","o":["4, 1, 3, 5, 2","4, 3, 1, 5, 2","3, 4, 1, 5, 2","1, 4, 3, 5, 2"],"a":[1],"e":"Workflow optimization begins by mapping the current process, then using performance measurements to identify the bottleneck. A targeted improvement is applied to that constraint, tested in a limited pilot against the established baseline, and only then rolled out broadly with documentation."},{"id":"ccao-f-c98","d":"general","x":"You are a customer-success lead operating an interactive workflow in which every turn must respond quickly and the reasoning required per turn is light. Which option best matches these requirements?","o":["Choose any model because latency does not vary by model.","Choose Haiku to keep latency low and to handle a light reasoning load efficiently at scale.","Choose Opus to maximize reasoning depth despite higher latency.","Choose a model based on the alphabetical order of its name."],"a":[1],"e":"Haiku is the appropriate model family for simple, high-volume, latency-sensitive work. Anthropic’s guidance recommends using Haiku for simple tasks and reserving more capable models for complex reasoning, so it aligns with fast interactive turns that need only light reasoning."},{"id":"ccao-f-c99","d":"general","x":"As part of a workflow redesign, a process analyst is applying the AI Fluency Framework’s Delegation competency. Which two criteria most directly guide Delegation decisions?\n\nChoose two","o":["The reversibility of the task and the consequence of an error, which together indicate whether the step is safe to delegate to Claude or should be retained for human decision-making.","The need for human creativity, empathy, or judgment in the step, which indicates whether the task is appropriate for AI delegation or requires the human capabilities Claude cannot provide.","The visual complexity of the workflow diagram, since steps with more connections and dependencies are more likely to require human oversight.","The seniority of the team member who currently performs the step, since more senior roles typically involve higher-stakes tasks that warrant human retention.","The length of time the workflow has existed in its current form, since well-established processes have been sufficiently validated for AI delegation."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Delegation strategically divides work between humans and AI based on the nature and stakes of each task. Error consequence and reversibility determine whether delegation is acceptably safe, while tasks requiring human expertise, creativity, empathy, or judgment should remain human-led. The AI Fluency Framework specifically identifies human expertise, creativity, and judgment as considerations when distributing work between people and AI."},{"id":"ccao-f-c100","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is assessing whether to enable a Skill supplied by an unfamiliar third party. Which two checks best support a sound trust decision?\n\nChoose two","o":["Verify the source of the Skill against the organization’s approved-Skill list before enabling it within claude.ai or any Project the team uses for its work.","Treat the Skill's appearance in a public listing as sufficient validation, without confirming the source, the requested permissions, or alignment with the organization’s approved-Skill list.","Skip permission review on the assumption that the Skill cannot exceed the boundaries of the workflow, even though Skills can request access that the task does not require.","Review the permissions and capabilities the Skill requests and confirm that the requested access is appropriate for the task the Skill is intended to perform.","Enable the Skill immediately on the basis of strong external reviews, even though the source has not been validated against the organization’s approved-Skill list and the permissions remain unverified."],"a":[0,3],"e":"Third-party Skills should be enabled only from trusted, organization-approved sources, and their requested capabilities and access should be reviewed to ensure they are necessary and appropriate for the intended task. Public listings or favorable reviews do not substitute for validating provenance and applying least-privilege access."},{"id":"ccao-f-c101","d":"general","x":"You are a research associate assembling inputs for a lengthy task that refers to two long regulatory documents, but only particular sections are relevant.\n\nWhich approach best preserves context-window capacity?","o":["Skip the documents entirely and rely on Claude to invent the missing context.","Split each document into one-character pieces and feed them one at a time.","Extract only the relevant sections from each document and include those, omitting unrelated chapters.","Paste the full text of both documents without trimming to maximize available material."],"a":[2],"e":"Extracting and supplying only the relevant sections retains the necessary regulatory context while avoiding token consumption by unrelated chapters, preserving more context-window capacity for the task."},{"id":"ccao-f-c102","d":"general","x":"You are a project lead evaluating a teammate’s practice of using the same prompt template for every task, regardless of task type. Which feedback is most appropriate?","o":["The prompting strategy should be adapted to each task type because one template does not produce equally strong outputs across all tasks.","The habit is ideal because a single template guarantees consistent quality across all task types.","The prompting strategy should not change between tasks because adapting it wastes time and adds no value.","Task type has no effect on the appropriate prompting strategy."],"a":[0],"e":"Prompting strategy should be adapted to the task type because different tasks require different instructions, context, output formats, constraints, and evaluation criteria. A single template cannot reliably produce equally strong results across every type of task."},{"id":"ccao-f-c103","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker preparing a summary of common prompt-failure patterns for the team.\n\nWhich pattern is most likely to result in vague or off-target output?","o":["a prompt that names a topic but does not state the task, audience, or required output structure","a prompt that adds clear constraints on length and tone","a prompt that explicitly names the role, task, audience, and required output format","a prompt that includes a representative example of the desired output"],"a":[0],"e":"A prompt that identifies only a topic leaves the intended task, audience, and response structure undefined. Those missing constraints force the model to infer essential requirements, increasing the likelihood of a generic or misaligned response."},{"id":"ccao-f-c104","d":"general","x":"You are an HR specialist assessing data items proposed for inclusion in a Claude prompt. Which two data items are suitable to include in a Claude prompt for a routine task?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["a redacted job description that the model will adapt for a posting","accommodation request notes from an employee’s HR file needed to draft a role adjustment memo","employee ID numbers and hire dates for the team roster included to help Claude personalize onboarding communications","a compensation band summary that includes employee names and current salary figures for a specific team","an anonymized summary of an HR policy that the model will rephrase"],"a":[0,4],"e":"A redacted job description and an anonymized HR-policy summary are appropriately minimized and do not contain identifiable or sensitive employee information. Employee accommodation records, identifiers and hire dates, and named salary information are confidential HR data and are unsuitable for routine prompting."},{"id":"ccao-f-c105","d":"general","x":"You are a Claude associate evaluating a proposed Claude use case before approving it for your team. What is the correct sequence of assessment steps?\n\n1. Compare the use case with the organization’s policies and the relevant regulatory considerations.\n2. Document the assessment outcome and rationale for traceability.\n3. Identify the audience, decision impact, and reversibility of the outputs.\n4. Decide whether the use case proceeds, needs modification, or is declined.\n5. Identify the data types involved and whether sensitive data is implicated.","o":["3, 1, 5, 4, 2","1, 3, 5, 4, 2","5, 3, 1, 4, 2","3, 5, 1, 4, 2"],"a":[3],"e":"A sound assessment begins by establishing who will receive or rely on the output, the consequences of an incorrect result, and whether the resulting action can be reversed (3). This initial impact analysis determines the level of scrutiny required. The assessor should then identify the data involved, especially personal, confidential, regulated, or otherwise sensitive information (5). Once the use case and its information exposure are understood, it can be compared meaningfully against organizational AI policies, legal requirements, and regulatory restrictions (1). The next step is to decide whether the use case should proceed, be modified with safeguards, or be rejected (4). Documentation comes last because it records the completed assessment, decision, supporting evidence, restrictions, and rationale for auditability (2).\n\nStep numbering corrected: An earlier version of this question listed the five steps in a different order from the one its answer options refer to, which made the marked sequence document the outcome before deciding it. The steps now follow the source numbering, and the answer is 3, 5, 1, 4, 2. Another source marks 1, 3, 5, 4, 2, which would compare the use case against policy before its audience, impact and data are known."},{"id":"ccao-f-c106","d":"general","x":"Claude’s first output is partly useful, but it has issues with tone and detail.\n\nWhich two refinement actions are most likely to result in a meaningfully better output?\n\nEach correct answer provides a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Provide an example of an output that meets the desired standard.","Append the phrase “please do better” to the end of the original prompt.","Add a specific tone descriptor and a concrete audience to the prompt.","Increase the temperature setting and resubmit the unchanged prompt.","Resubmit the same prompt several times and select the best response."],"a":[0,2],"e":"A representative example gives Claude a concrete standard for the desired response, including its tone and level of detail. Explicitly naming the desired tone and a specific audience also supplies actionable constraints that guide style, vocabulary, and appropriate depth. Anthropic recommends clear, explicit instructions and notes that Claude pays attention to details and examples."},{"id":"ccao-f-c107","d":"general","x":"You are reviewing colleagues’ replies to feedback received on communications drafted by Claude. Which two feedback responses are productive?\n\nEach correct answer represents a complete solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["declining to revise sections that were generated by Claude on the basis that the model’s output should be trusted as written","incorporating all feedback received, including contradictory suggestions, into the next draft simultaneously","deferring all feedback to a later revision cycle to avoid disrupting the current draft’s consistency","asking clarifying questions when feedback is ambiguous before acting on it","mapping each piece of feedback to the specific change made in the next draft"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Ambiguous feedback should be clarified before it is applied so that revisions reflect the reviewer’s intended meaning. Linking every feedback item to its corresponding change creates an auditable revision trail and helps confirm that feedback has been addressed. Claude-generated content should still be reviewed and verified rather than treated as automatically correct."},{"id":"ccao-f-c108","d":"general","x":"A project manager is determining how Claude can assist with requirements analysis for an upcoming engagement. The team will conduct stakeholder interviews and review existing process documentation during discovery.\n\nWhich activity fits this domain?","o":["Use Claude to generate a complete requirements list from the project name and a short description, since requirements for similar engagements are largely standard.","Use Claude to prioritize the requirements the team has already gathered, leaving the gathering itself to manual analyst work since prioritization is more time-consuming.","Use Claude to synthesize stakeholder inputs and existing process documentation into a structured list of requirements with traceability to each source.","Use Claude to conduct the stakeholder interviews directly, with the analyst reviewing the recordings afterward to confirm the requirements Claude extracted."],"a":[2],"e":"Claude can synthesize information from stakeholder interviews and process documents into a structured requirements set. Source traceability makes each requirement auditable against the underlying input, helping analysts validate the result and reduce unsupported claims. Anthropic’s documentation supports analyzing supplied documents and generating citations that point to specific source locations."},{"id":"ccao-f-c109","d":"general","x":"An account manager used Claude to extract requirements from stakeholder notes. Three requirements seem to be in conflict: two stakeholders specified mutually exclusive workflow steps, and a third requirement presumes an outcome that the first two would prevent.\n\nWhich action is most appropriate?","o":["Present all three conflicting requirements as alternative options in the requirements document, leaving the resolution to the project’s decision-making process later.","Apply Claude to propose a compromise wording for each conflict and submit the compromise to stakeholders as the proposed resolution.","Select the requirement that has the strongest business justification in the source notes, set the conflicting requirements aside, and proceed with the selected one.","Flag the conflicts in the requirements document, document the source for each side, and route the conflicts back to the originating stakeholders for resolution."],"a":[3],"e":"Requirements conflicts should be explicitly documented with traceability to each originating source and returned to the relevant stakeholders for resolution. This preserves accountability and prevents an analyst or AI tool from making an unauthorized business decision."},{"id":"ccao-f-c110","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is describing the cost, speed, and quality trade-offs among Claude models to a colleague. Which generalization accurately characterizes this trade-off?","o":["Speed and rate-limit consumption are independent of model capability; the difference between Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus is reasoning depth alone.","More capable models generally consume more of the rate limit and run more slowly, while lighter models are faster and consume less of the rate limit.","More capable models generally consume less of the rate limit because their reasoning is more efficient, even though they run more slowly than lighter models.","Lighter models consume more of the rate limit per request than more capable models, since lighter models require multiple passes to match heavier-model quality."],"a":[1],"e":"Claude’s model lineup generally trades efficiency for capability: lighter models such as Haiku are intended for simpler, fast, lower-cost work, while more capable models such as Sonnet and Opus are used for more complex reasoning and have higher token pricing and resource consumption. This supports the general pattern of greater rate-limit use and slower execution for more capable models."},{"id":"ccao-f-c111","d":"general","x":"A finance manager needs to choose a Claude feature for work requiring deterministic calculations across a multi-tab spreadsheet. Which option best meets this requirement?","o":["Use an Artifact alone for the calculations, which provides a stable editable surface for the result but does not perform the deterministic computation that Code Execution would run.","Use Code Execution, which runs code in a sandbox to perform calculations on the uploaded file and produce verified outputs the associate can check.","Use the Research feature for the calculations, which gathers and synthesizes external sources and is not the appropriate feature for deterministic computation on an uploaded spreadsheet.","Use a standard chat prompt for the calculations, which generates text without running code and does not produce the verified deterministic output the multi-tab calculation task requires."],"a":[1],"e":"Claude Code Execution can run code in a secure sandbox, process uploaded files, and perform complex calculations. That makes it the appropriate feature for reproducible computations across a multi-tab spreadsheet."},{"id":"ccao-f-c112","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is investigating a Claude response that answered only one of three required questions in a prompt. The prompt presented all three questions in one paragraph, separated by commas.\n\nWhich root cause is most likely?","o":["The prompt was too long and Claude focused on the question with the strongest grounding in the surrounding context.","The prompt’s required questions were addressed implicitly across the response, even though the response did not visibly answer each question.","The prompt’s task statement did not enumerate the three required questions explicitly enough for Claude to address each one in turn.","The prompt’s audience and tone specification overshadowed the task statement, so Claude prioritized voice over coverage."],"a":[2],"e":"When a prompt contains several required items, explicitly numbering or otherwise enumerating each item makes the required coverage unambiguous and helps ensure that each is answered in sequence. Combining all three questions into a comma-separated paragraph leaves the task structure insufficiently explicit."},{"id":"ccao-f-c113","d":"general","x":"A sales manager is refining a Claude-generated solution prototype after a stakeholder review identified two particular concerns. The remainder of the prototype was approved as written.\n\nWhich iteration approach is most likely to create a strong next version?","o":["Rewrite the prototype from scratch incorporating the two specific concerns, since starting fresh integrates the feedback more cleanly than targeted edits.","Address the two specific concerns and add a third improvement the associate identified, since combining stakeholder feedback with self- identified improvements is more efficient.","Address the two specific concerns directly, leave unrelated parts unchanged, and document the changes for the next review.","Address the two specific concerns and also revise unrelated sections to maintain consistent voice across the prototype after the edits."],"a":[2],"e":"A focused revision incorporates the specific stakeholder feedback while retaining content that was already accepted. Documenting the changes also makes the subsequent review clearer and more traceable."},{"id":"ccao-f-c114","d":"general","x":"As a senior analyst, you use Claude to analyze requirements from several stakeholder sources.\n\nWhat is the correct sequence of steps?\n\n1. Group related requirements and flag conflicts or ambiguities for stakeholder review.\n2. Validate the structured output against the original sources for accuracy and completeness.\n3. Submit the consolidated inputs to Claude using a structured extraction prompt.\n4. Gather and label the source materials so that every requirement can be traced.\n5. Document the final requirements set with source traceability and status.","o":["4, 1, 3, 2, 5","4, 3, 1, 2, 5","1, 4, 3, 2, 5","3, 4, 1, 2, 5"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccao-f-c115","d":"general","x":"You are assessing whether a request to “redesign new-hire onboarding” should be broken into parts before prompting Claude. Which characteristic of the request most strongly suggests that decomposition would be useful?","o":["The request is for a single structured document that can be produced from one set of inputs in one prompt.","The request requires multiple distinct outputs: a process map, supporting materials, and a measurement plan.","The request involves a single well-defined output with a clear input and a familiar format.","The request involves content the associate has produced many times before and knows can be handled in a single well-structured prompt."],"a":[1],"e":"Decomposition is most valuable when work contains multiple distinct deliverables that require different kinds of content and evaluation. A process map, supporting materials, and a measurement plan are separate outputs, so treating them as coordinated subtasks improves clarity and control."},{"id":"ccao-f-c116","d":"general","x":"You are a procurement associate breaking down a vendor-evaluation task. Which dependency sequence represents sound ordering?","o":["Define evaluation criteria first, gather vendor data against those criteria, then score and rank.","Rank vendors before any data is collected.","Score vendors first and decide on the criteria afterward.","Skip criteria and ask Claude to produce a vendor recommendation from no inputs."],"a":[0],"e":"A defensible vendor evaluation defines the assessment criteria before collecting evidence, then gathers vendor information aligned to those criteria, and finally uses that information to score and rank the vendors."},{"id":"ccao-f-c117","d":"general","x":"You are a marketing manager using Claude to develop ideas for a campaign and must complete the framing steps before generating concepts. Which two framing steps must be completed BEFORE asking Claude to generate concepts?\n\nEach correct answer represents part of the solution.\n\nChoose two","o":["Select a finalist concept and prepare it for stakeholder approval.","Define the goal of the campaign and the success metrics for the campaign.","Review the Claude-generated concepts and select the two strongest for further development.","Schedule the campaign launch and the rollout dates with the agency.","Define the brand voice and the audience the concepts will speak to."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Campaign ideation should begin with a clear objective and measurable success metrics, plus the target audience and brand voice that constrain relevance, tone, and style. Concept review, finalist selection, approval preparation, launch scheduling, and rollout planning take place after concepts have been generated."},{"id":"ccao-f-c118","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker determining whether to escalate Claude-generated output. What is the correct sequence of decision steps?\n\n1. Identify the audience and the impact if the output is incorrect.\n2. Document the decision and its rationale for traceability.\n3. Compare the output’s risk profile with the organization’s escalation criteria.\n4. Decide whether to use the output, iterate further, or escalate.\n5. Evaluate the output’s accuracy, completeness, and any unverified claims.","o":["5, 1, 3, 4, 2","3, 1, 5, 4, 2","1, 5, 3, 4, 2","1, 3, 5, 4, 2"],"a":[2],"e":"Assessing the audience and consequences establishes the potential impact, while evaluating accuracy, completeness, and unverified claims establishes the output’s quality risk. Those factors can then be measured against organizational escalation criteria before selecting use, iteration, or escalation; recording the decision and rationale provides traceability. Claude outputs can contain factual errors or unsupported claims, so critical and high-stakes information requires validation."},{"id":"ccao-f-c119","d":"general","x":"You are a product designer using Claude in a solution-design exercise. Which solution-design step should be completed first when using Claude?","o":["Articulate the problem the solution must address and the constraints in play.","Compare the candidate concepts against the constraints and select a finalist.","Produce detailed mock-ups of the chosen solution for stakeholder review.","Generate candidate solution concepts using Claude across diverse styles."],"a":[0],"e":"Defining the problem to solve and the applicable constraints establishes the goals, boundaries, and evaluation criteria for subsequent concept generation. Candidate concepts can then be generated, assessed against those constraints, selected, and developed into detailed mock-ups. Anthropic’s prompting guidance recommends clear, explicit instructions and relevant context so Claude can produce targeted results."},{"id":"ccao-f-c120","d":"general","x":"A marketing manager is preparing a constrained brainstorming task: produce ten campaign ideas, each tied to a stated audience segment and each fitting one of three defined budget bands.\n\nWhich prompting approach fits the task?","o":["Ask Claude for ten distinct ideas, stating the audience constraints in the prompt and leaving the budget unconstrained so creativity is not limited at the ideation stage.","Ask Claude for ten distinct ideas with the audience and budget constraints stated in the prompt, and require each idea to name which audience and budget band it addresses.","Ask Claude for thirty ideas with the audience and budget constraints stated in the prompt, then keep the strongest ten so a wider initial pool produces stronger finalists.","Ask Claude for ten distinct ideas without budget constraints, then filter the output afterward to keep only the ideas that fit the three budget bands."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccao-f-c121","d":"general","x":"An analyst is working in a long Claude conversation. The conversation contains roughly 60 turns covering an initial requirements analysis, several rounds of data review, and a current drafting phase. Recent responses have started to drift from the agreed requirements.\n\nWhich step most directly addresses the drift while preserving the analyst's progress?","o":["Switch the conversation to a model with a larger context window so the accumulated history no longer causes drift, and continue without restructuring the inputs.","Open a new chat and paste only the current draft, then continue refining it there without the prior requirements and data-review context.","Move the source data and requirements into a Project knowledge base, then continue the drafting work in a new chat inside that Project.","Summarize the agreed requirements, key data findings, and current draft state, then continue in the same chat by reasserting the summary as the working context."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccao-f-c122","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker notices that a Claude output may have repeated a confidential financial figure from an internal source that the user was told to treat as restricted. The output has not yet been shared outside the user.\n\nWhich immediate action is most appropriate?","o":["Stop using the output, document the issue-including the prompt and the source-and report it through the documented governance or incident channel.","Edit the output to remove the figure, save the edited version for the intended use, and document the change in a personal log without involving the governance channel.","Stop using the output and start a new chat with a more carefully scoped prompt, since the issue is resolved as long as the original output is not used.","Continue using the output for the intended internal purpose, since the figure has not left the user's possession and no external disclosure has occurred."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c123","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is defining what \"workflow optimization\" means at the Associate level for the team's recurring AI-supported workflows. Which description is most accurate?","o":["Reducing the number of steps in the workflow to the smallest possible count, since fewer steps mean faster cycle time and lower error rates.","Reducing redundancy, removing low-value steps, and applying Claude where it adds measurable value, while keeping required reviews and quality gates.","Replacing manual steps with Claude-supported steps wherever possible, since each replacement adds value by reducing the time the team spends on the workflow.","Standardizing every step of the workflow to a fixed template so each run produces identical output, without adjusting for variation in the workflow's inputs."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccao-f-c124","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker has noticed that the same prompt for a recurring weekly task produces inconsistent outputs across runs. Some weeks the output uses the right sections but the wrong tone; other weeks the tone is right but a section is missing. The source inputs change each week but follow the same structure.\n\nWhich cause is most worth investigating first?","o":["The recurring prompt under-specifies the audience, tone, format, and required sections, so each run drifts depending on what the model emphasizes that time.","The model is non-deterministic, so identical prompts naturally produce different outputs even when the prompt itself is well-written, and the team should accept the variation.","The source inputs vary too much from week to week, and the team should standardize the inputs before changing the prompt.","The team should split the prompt into separate prompts for tone and structure, since combining both in one prompt produces the mixed inconsistency the team is seeing."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c125","d":"general","x":"A Claude associate is applying the AI Fluency Framework Diligence competency to ongoing Claude usage. Which practice best reflects Diligence in a governance context?","o":["Apply organizational AI policies consistently as a sustained professional habit, identify gaps between actual usage and the policies, and take corrective action to close those gaps over time.","Apply organizational AI policies only in high-visibility situations where usage is likely to be observed or reviewed by others.","Apply organizational AI policies during onboarding as a foundational training step, document that each associate has reviewed them, and treat that record as evidence of ongoing compliance.","Defer all policy application to a centralized compliance team, treating individual usage as outside personal responsibility."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c126","d":"general","x":"After importing context from another AI platform into Claude's Memory, a knowledge worker notices that some imported details did not appear in subsequent responses. Which explanation is most accurate?","o":["The import transfers every detail as a rule, and any missing content reflects a deliberate choice by Claude to omit the information rather than the documented experimental nature of imports.","Memory imports overwrite all prior memories on completion, so any details that appear missing must have been deleted by the import process rather than not incorporated during the import itself.","Memory imports are experimental, and Claude might not successfully incorporate all imported content; missing details can be added manually through Settings > Capabilities as a fallback.","Imported memories are stored separately from regular memories and cannot be referenced in any chat, so missing details reflect an architectural separation rather than imperfect import incorporation."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c127","d":"general","x":"You are a knowledge worker evaluating which Claude feature matches each proposed use case. Which two feature-to-use-case matches are appropriate? (Select two.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution.","o":["Long-context summarization for a quarterly report distilled from many sources.","Long-context summarization for drafting a reply to a single short customer email.","Web search invocation to retrieve the established formula for calculating compound interest.","Tool use to invoke a calculator for rephrasing a product description in simpler language.","Tool use to invoke a calculator for a complex, multi-step pricing calculation."],"a":[0,4]},{"id":"ccao-f-c128","d":"general","x":"Your conversation context has grown long, and the model is producing inconsistent outputs. Which context-management step should be performed first?","o":["Switch to a different model in the hope that the new model handles long context better.","Continue the existing conversation and hope that the model recovers consistency.","Summarize the prior conversation into a concise statement of the current state.","Start a fresh conversation and repaste only the essential prior context."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c129","d":"general","x":"A procurement manager is evaluating a Claude-generated vendor proposal summary against the original RFP. The RFP lists twelve numbered requirements across pricing, security, and service levels.\n\nHow should the summary be checked for completeness?","o":["Compare the section headings in the summary with the section headings in the RFP, since matching headings indicate matching content.","Spot-check three random requirements from the RFP against the summary, since random sampling is the standard completeness check for summaries.","Map each numbered requirement in the RFP to a section of the summary and confirm that every requirement is represented.","Read the summary end to end and confirm that it forms a coherent response to the RFP, since coherent summaries typically reflect coverage of the source."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c130","d":"general","x":"You are setting up a Connector for a shared data source that several colleagues will use. Which Connector setup step should be performed first?","o":["Document the Connector's purpose and the appropriate use cases for the team.","Use the Connector in a representative task to evaluate its real-world utility.","Connect the data source and confirm that authentication is working as expected.","Confirm that the Connector is approved and that the data classification is appropriate."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccao-f-c131","d":"general","x":"You are a product manager asking Claude to produce an interactive prototype of a small landing-page mockup that the team will iterate on and share. Which Claude feature is best suited to this output?","o":["A Research run that produces a multipage external survey of competitor sites.","A short conversational reply embedded inline in the chat history.","An Artifact, which presents standalone editable content in a dedicated side panel for iteration.","A new Project containing only the team's prior meeting notes."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c132","d":"general","x":"You are an operations lead choosing actions when a Claude conversation has degraded in quality. Which recovery action should be tried first when conversation quality degrades?","o":["Summarize the current state and continue with a focused follow-up prompt.","Replace the model with a different vendor's model immediately.","Escalate to a senior architect for a manual review of the conversation history.","Discard the entire conversation and rebuild the work from the original prompt."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c133","d":"general","x":"A knowledge worker is explaining context-window basics to a teammate. Which statement most accurately describes a context window?","o":["The subset of the conversation that the model determines is most relevant to the current input, which can be smaller than the full conversation.","A bounded amount of text the model can consider at once, including the conversation history, attached documents, and the current input.","The complete conversation history retained across sessions, which the model can reference whenever it produces a response in any chat.","An adjustable setting that determines how much of the conversation history the model includes in each response, configurable per chat."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccao-f-c134","d":"general","x":"A product manager is using Claude to ideate options for a new self-service portal. The team needs breadth before narrowing and will evaluate trade-offs across the options in a separate session.\n\nWhich prompting strategy fits this stage?","o":["Request multiple distinct options ordered by Claude's assessment of feasibility, so the team can focus its trade-off discussion on the most feasible options first.","Request a single recommended option with strong reasoning, since well-reasoned single options give the team a clearer starting point than several weakly reasoned alternatives.","Request multiple distinct options with stated trade-offs, and avoid committing to a single recommendation in this round.","Request multiple distinct options and ask Claude to recommend the strongest one with reasoning, so the team starts the trade-off discussion from a leading candidate."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c135","d":"general","x":"A prompt describes a multi-step setup process, such as configuring an environment and then running a script, but Claude sometimes performs the steps out of order. What change would make the required order unambiguous?","o":["Provide the instructions as a numbered list of steps in the exact order they must run","Combine all of the steps into a single long sentence separated only by commas","Describe the steps in a random order and let Claude infer the correct sequence","Remove the step numbers and rely only on paragraph breaks to imply the correct order"],"a":[0],"e":"Providing instructions as sequential steps using numbered lists or bullet points is recommended specifically when the order or completeness of the steps matters, removing ambiguity about sequence.","w":["Providing instructions as sequential steps using numbered lists or bullet points is recommended specifically when the order or completeness of the steps matters, removing ambiguity about sequence.","A single comma-separated sentence obscures step boundaries and order even more than a plain paragraph would, making the sequence less clear rather than more.","Describing steps out of order and relying on inference is the opposite of making the required sequence unambiguous.","Relying only on paragraph breaks without numbering removes the explicit ordering cue that numbered lists provide for steps where sequence matters."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c136","d":"general","x":"A marketing manager needs to draft a persuasive email campaign to launch a new product. The email should capture attention, highlight benefits, and include a call to action. Which prompting approach is best?","o":["Prompt Claude to draft an email announcing the new product, leaving the structure and content entirely to the model's discretion without providing any specific guidelines on audience, tone, or key product benefits to highlight.","Instruct Claude to write a thorough product description report covering all features, specifications, technical details, and pricing info, formatted as a formal document that is suitable for internal stakeholders rather than a marketing audience.","Guide Claude to create a marketing email: first, write an attention-grabbing subject line; then, open with a hook; next, present three key benefits with persuasive language; and finally, include a clear call to action linked to the product page.","Ask Claude to produce several email drafts with distinct tones (formal, casual, urgent), then assess each draft's persuasiveness, clarity, and brand alignment, and choose the best one for final edits, and then iterate if needed."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c137","d":"general","x":"Over eight rounds of revision, a team notices that fixing the newest complaint often brings back a problem they had solved three rounds earlier. Nothing about their requirements is written down.\n\nWhat is the greatest risk?","o":["The team will spend more time on revisions than they would have spent writing the document themselves.","The wording of the request will become long and repetitive as fixes for each separate complaint accumulate.","The eighth version will read less naturally than the first, because it has been adjusted so many times over.","With no record of what the document must satisfy, each round can undo an earlier fix and nobody notices."],"a":[3],"e":"Without a written list of everything the document must satisfy, each round is checked only against the latest complaint. Requirements settled earlier are not re-tested, so the work circles instead of converging.","w":["Time spent is a real cost, but the team would accept it if the rounds were converging. The problem is that they are not.","An accumulating request is an untidy side effect, and it does not by itself cause earlier fixes to be lost.","Prose quality drifting under repeated revision is worth watching, but it is a milder issue than requirements silently reversing.","Without a written list of everything the document must satisfy, each round is checked only against the latest complaint. Requirements settled earlier are not re-tested, so the work circles instead of converging."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c138","d":"general","x":"A business analyst is deciding whether to send one large request or break it into stages. Which two features of a request are the strongest signals that it should be decomposed? Select two.","o":["It bundles several distinct deliverables that are meant for different audiences and uses.","It concerns a subject area the analyst has not personally worked in for several years.","It runs to a long prompt because a good deal of background has been pasted in with it.","It asks for the final answer to be presented as a table rather than as flowing paragraphs.","Later parts are only worth producing if the earlier factual work is correct."],"a":[0,4],"w":["Multiple deliverables with different readers pull the output in different directions, and one response has to compromise. Splitting lets each part be specified properly.","Unfamiliarity affects how carefully the analyst must review the result, but it says nothing about whether the request itself is structurally overloaded.","A long prompt is not the same as an overloaded one. Pasting rich source material is good practice and often makes a single-step request work better.","Output format is a presentation choice. A table can be produced in one step just as easily as prose, so it is not a reason to break the work up.","Dependency is a separate signal from bundling: when step two builds on step one, a single pass propagates any early error into everything downstream unchecked."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c139","d":"general","x":"A support lead finds a Claude-drafted troubleshooting article accurate but written at a reading level too advanced for the average customer.\n\nWhich follow-up instruction best refines the draft without sacrificing accuracy?","o":["Rewrite the article with shorter sentences and everyday words, keeping every step accurate","Remove the least common troubleshooting steps so the article reads a bit shorter","Add more technical detail so customers understand the underlying cause in full","Convert the article into a formal specification format used by the engineering team"],"a":[0],"e":"Simplifying sentence structure and vocabulary while preserving the accurate steps directly targets the readability problem without losing correctness.","w":["Simplifying sentence structure and vocabulary while preserving the accurate steps directly targets the readability problem without losing correctness.","Removing steps changes the article's completeness and could omit cases some customers need, which does not fix a reading-level problem.","Adding more technical detail moves the article further from, not closer to, the audience's reading level.","A formal specification format is written for engineers and would make the article harder for typical customers to follow."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c140","d":"general","x":"A retention lead asked why cancellations rose sharply in the last quarter. The answer explains at length what cancellation rates are and how they are generally measured. How should she judge this output?","o":["As accurate but off target, because it explains the topic instead of addressing the rise she asked about.","As adequate for now, because background on how the measure works is a reasonable place to begin the analysis.","As evidence that the question cannot be answered, since the reply avoided the specific rise she was asking about.","As a sound answer that simply needs shortening, since the explanation contains more detail than she required here."],"a":[0],"e":"Everything present may be correct while the question remains unanswered. Accuracy and relevance are separate tests, and this output passes the first and fails the second.","w":["Everything present may be correct while the question remains unanswered. Accuracy and relevance are separate tests, and this output passes the first and fails the second.","Accepting background as progress leaves the actual question open, and the material she now has does not move her any closer to a cause.","A general answer indicates that the request was read broadly, not that the specific question is unanswerable given her own data.","Shortening reduces the volume of material that does not address her question, leaving a briefer answer to the wrong question."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c141","d":"general","x":"A Claude draft attributes a sentence to a named industry report as a direct quotation. What is the appropriate validation step?","o":["Open the report and find that exact sentence, since a close paraphrase may not appear in it.","Ask Claude for the page number, and accept the quotation once a number has been supplied to her.","Search for the sentence's key phrase and accept it if similar wording appears anywhere.","Confirm that the report exists and was published, which establishes the quotation's origin."],"a":[0],"e":"A quotation asserts that specific words appear in a specific document, so only locating those words in the report validates it.","w":["A quotation asserts that specific words appear in a specific document, so only locating those words in the report validates it.","A page number can be generated as readily as the quotation, so it adds no independent confirmation.","Similar wording elsewhere does not establish that this report contains this sentence.","The report existing says nothing about whether the quoted sentence is in it."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c142","d":"general","x":"A manager needs a comparison of six suppliers across five criteria. The information is currently spread across several separate Claude answers. What should she do first?","o":["Ask Claude to combine all of the earlier answers into one long document she can read through.","Agree which suppliers and which criteria belong in the comparison before asking for any output.","Choose the output format now, so that every further answer can be produced in that same shape.","Ask Claude to rank the six suppliers straight away and work backwards from the ranking it gives."],"a":[1],"e":"A comparison is only as good as its frame. Fixing the rows and columns first is what stops the result being shaped by whatever happened to come up in earlier chats.","w":["Combining unreconciled answers produces a long document that still contains whatever gaps and conflicts the originals had.","A comparison is only as good as its frame. Fixing the rows and columns first is what stops the result being shaped by whatever happened to come up in earlier chats.","Format is a downstream decision; choosing it before the content is defined locks in a shape that may not fit.","A ranking produced before the criteria are agreed reflects unstated assumptions she cannot inspect or defend."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c143","d":"general","x":"A communications officer must publish a Claude-drafted market briefing on the company blog within the hour. Claude used web search to produce it, and every figure in the draft carries a citation.\n\nWhat is the appropriate first step before she publishes?","o":["Add a line at the foot of the briefing noting that it was prepared with the help of an AI assistant.","Open the cited sources and confirm that each figure in the draft does appear in the page it points to.","Ask Claude how confident it is in each figure, and revise the ones it rates as least confident.","Regenerate the briefing in a fresh conversation, and publish if the two drafts report the same figures."],"a":[1],"e":"Where web search was used, the help centre's recommendation is to review Claude's cited sources, because reading the original content reveals anything that was misread or taken out of context. With citations present, checking them is faster and stronger than any other available check.","w":["A disclosure line may well be required, but it does nothing about accuracy. Adding it and publishing would put unverified figures in front of an external audience.","Where web search was used, the help centre's recommendation is to review Claude's cited sources, because reading the original content reveals anything that was misread or taken out of context. With citations present, checking them is faster and stronger than any other available check.","A self-reported confidence rating is not evidence. It can sound reassuring about a figure that the cited page does not actually support.","Two runs can repeat the same error, so agreement between them is not confirmation. The sources are already cited and can be checked directly."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c144","d":"general","x":"A communications assistant receives an answer containing a clearly incorrect statement about her organisation. She corrects her own draft in a follow-up message, and also wants the problem itself recorded rather than just worked around.\n\nWhat should she do?","o":["Add a line to the conversation noting that the earlier statement was wrong, so the correction sits in the chat history.","Start the same task again in a new conversation, on the basis that a clean chat is less likely to repeat the mistake.","Rephrase her original prompt in more forceful language so that the incorrect statement is less likely to recur next time.","Use the thumbs-down control on the response that contained the incorrect statement, in addition to fixing her own draft."],"a":[3],"e":"The thumbs-down feedback control on the response is the mechanism provided for flagging an incorrect or misleading answer, and it complements, rather than replaces, correcting the draft she is working on.","w":["A note in the chat helps her own record of what happened, but it stays inside her conversation. It does nothing to report the response as problematic beyond her own session.","A fresh conversation may or may not produce a better answer, and starting over is a workaround for her immediate task rather than a way of recording that the response was wrong.","Emphatic rewording is a weak remedy and addresses only her future prompts. It neither reports the faulty response nor makes the problem visible to anyone else.","The thumbs-down feedback control on the response is the mechanism provided for flagging an incorrect or misleading answer, and it complements, rather than replaces, correcting the draft she is working on."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c145","d":"general","x":"A marketer is midway through a one-off brainstorm that will not be revisited, and the conversation is approaching its limit. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Let the conversation continue and condense itself, since nothing in it needs to outlive this session.","Build a Project and move the whole brainstorm into project knowledge before writing anything more.","Stop the brainstorm now and restart it in a new chat so the ideas come from a completely clean window.","Split the brainstorm across four separate chats so that no single conversation ever nears its limit."],"a":[0],"e":"When the output is disposable, allowing automatic context management to make room is the lowest-effort choice; there is no durable knowledge worth persisting.","w":["When the output is disposable, allowing automatic context management to make room is the lowest-effort choice; there is no durable knowledge worth persisting.","A project is for context that recurs. Setting one up for work that will never be revisited is unnecessary overhead.","Restarting discards the ideas generated so far and forces her to regenerate them at further cost.","Fragmenting the session means no chat holds the full set of ideas, which undermines the brainstorm itself."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c146","d":"general","x":"A market analyst must produce a competitor briefing drawn from many public sources, and the team will keep editing that briefing for weeks afterwards. Which two Claude features fit this job? Select two.","o":["Keep the briefing inline in the chat and re-paste the newest version whenever an edit lands.","Turn on a mail connector first so that the competitor facts are drawn from the team inbox.","Use Research mode, so the briefing is built by multi-step searching and returns citations.","Ask for the briefing as an artifact, so the team can edit and reuse it outside the chat.","Open a separate chat for each competitor so that no single conversation ever grows long."],"a":[2,3],"w":["Re-pasting an inline answer for every edit is precisely the friction artifacts exist to remove, and versions drift apart quickly.","An internal mailbox is not the source of public competitor information, and connecting one does not answer the sourcing need here.","Research mode runs an agentic, multi-step search and returns citations, which is exactly what a sourced competitor briefing needs.","An artifact is the durable, editable deliverable format, so weeks of later editing happen on one object rather than in chat replies.","Splitting by competitor fragments the briefing and does nothing about sourcing or about the editing the team has ahead of it."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c147","d":"general","x":"A support operations lead must classify a large daily backlog of short inbound messages, and separately needs one deep analysis of a tangled contract dispute. Which two choices fit? Select two.","o":["Use whichever tier the team already has open, since the tier rarely changes what comes back.","Use the deepest-reasoning tier for the contract dispute, accepting the larger quota it uses.","Use the fastest, cheapest tier for the high-volume classification, where throughput matters.","Use the fastest, cheapest tier for both jobs, since the tiers differ mainly in reply speed.","Use the deepest-reasoning tier for both of the jobs, since the strongest option is never bad."],"a":[1,2],"w":["Tier choice changes depth, speed and quota use, so treating it as incidental is how both of these jobs go wrong.","A tangled one-off analysis is the case where deeper reasoning is worth the meaningfully larger quota it consumes.","High-volume, low-complexity classification is exactly where the fastest and cheapest tier earns its place.","The tiers differ in reasoning depth as well as speed, so the lightest tier is a poor fit for a tangled dispute.","Using the deepest tier for routine classification spends quota on work that does not need the extra reasoning depth."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c148","d":"general","x":"A new team member asks how the Claude model tiers actually differ from one another in everyday use. Which two statements are accurate? Select two.","o":["The deepest tier suits genuinely hard problems, and it consumes meaningfully more allowance.","The tiers differ only in writing style, so any of them may be used for any task at any time.","The lightest tier gives answers of the very same depth, only more slowly than the other two.","The mid tier is the fast, capable default, and it is the right pick for most everyday work.","Moving up one tier is the standard fix whenever a prompt comes back with a vague or shallow answer."],"a":[0,3],"w":["The deepest tier buys reasoning depth for harder problems and costs meaningfully more of the usage allowance.","The difference is reasoning depth, speed and cost, not writing style, and the tiers are not interchangeable.","The lightest tier is the fastest of the three, and it trades some reasoning depth for that speed and low cost.","The middle tier is positioned as the fast, capable default that handles most knowledge work well.","A vague answer usually signals an under-specified prompt, and a deeper tier cannot supply context that was never given."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c149","d":"general","x":"A team is setting up a new recurring weekly task and has to decide how much reasoning depth it needs. Which sequence is correct?","o":["Pick a tier by how important the task feels, then run it for a month, then write down what good output means, then compare the month's results against that.","Run the task on several tiers at once, then write down what good output means, then compare the results against that definition, then keep the tier that did best.","Compare a sample of results with what colleagues produce by hand, then write down what good output means, then pick a tier on that basis, then run the task each week from then on.","Write down what good output means, then run a real sample on the default tier, then compare that sample against the definition, then change tier only where it falls short."],"a":[3],"e":"Define the quality bar, test the default tier on real work, check that sample against the bar, and change tier only where the evidence shows the default falls short or is more than the task needs.","w":["Choosing on instinct and defining quality a month later means there is no standard to judge the intervening results against, and a month of work has already been committed to an unexamined choice.","Committing several tiers to the task before any standard exists means the trial is designed without knowing what it is testing for, and it duplicates the work and the allowance draw on every run.","Comparing results before the task has been run is out of order, and benchmarking against manual work is not the same as agreeing what this task's output has to achieve.","Define the quality bar, test the default tier on real work, check that sample against the bar, and change tier only where the evidence shows the default falls short or is more than the task needs."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c150","d":"general","x":"A team runs two workloads that are alike in volume and subject matter. One is edited line by line by a specialist before use; the other is published automatically with no human reading it.\n\nWhich consideration should weigh most heavily when choosing a tier for each?","o":["The subject matter the two workloads share, since comparable material should be handled the same way for consistency of output.","The volume of the two workloads, since equal volumes imply equal throughput needs and therefore the same balance of speed and cost.","How much a downstream human check would catch, since the unchecked workload has to carry its own quality with no safety net.","The seniority of the people who own each workload, since the more senior owner is accountable for the more visible of the two."],"a":[2],"e":"The strength of the downstream check changes how much the output has to get right on its own, so the unchecked workload justifies more reasoning depth while the edited one can lean towards speed and allowance.","w":["Shared subject matter makes the two workloads look alike, but it is not what determines the risk. Consistency for its own sake ignores the fact that only one of them has anyone checking the result.","Equal volume does mean similar throughput demands, and that matters. It is still secondary to the fact that one workload reaches the outside world without any person having read it.","The strength of the downstream check changes how much the output has to get right on its own, so the unchecked workload justifies more reasoning depth while the edited one can lean towards speed and allowance.","Ownership determines who answers for a problem, not how likely a problem is. Seniority is an accountability question and does not tell the team where errors would actually escape unnoticed."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c151","d":"general","x":"Reviewing a backlog of intake forms, an analyst notices that most entries name a solution rather than a need, for example \"we need a dashboard.\" How can Claude best help with this backlog?","o":["Ask Claude to design the dashboard described in each entry so submitters can confirm what they wanted.","Ask Claude to reject entries that name a tool and ask the submitters to resubmit them in the right form.","Ask Claude to group the entries by the department that submitted them and report the totals to sponsors.","Ask Claude to restate each entry as the outcome sought, then flag entries where that outcome is unclear."],"a":[3],"e":"The analytical move is to convert stated solutions back into the outcomes behind them, which is language work Claude does well at volume. Flagging the entries where no outcome can be recovered tells the analyst exactly which submitters to go back to.","w":["Designing what was asked for skips the analysis entirely. If the submitter needed a weekly exception alert rather than a dashboard, building the dashboard confirms only that both parties misread the need together.","Pushing the work back onto submitters is slow and often futile, since people describe needs in terms of solutions naturally. It also discards information the analyst already has in the entries.","Counting entries by department produces a useful management view but leaves every entry still expressed as a solution. The backlog would be tidier and no better understood.","The analytical move is to convert stated solutions back into the outcomes behind them, which is language work Claude does well at volume. Flagging the entries where no outcome can be recovered tells the analyst exactly which submitters to go back to."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c152","d":"general","x":"In a pilot of a new Claude-assisted briefing arrangement, three of eight testers report that the output is \"not quite right\" but say nothing more specific. What should the pilot's owner do first?","o":["Rewrite the instructions to cover the areas the owner believes are most likely to be causing the trouble.","Ask the three testers for specific examples, with what the output said and what it should have said.","Ask the five satisfied testers what they did differently and circulate that as guidance to everyone.","Pause the pilot until the owner can sit with each tester and watch a full briefing being produced."],"a":[1],"e":"\"Not quite right\" is not actionable, and the cheapest way to make it actionable is to ask for cases. A concrete pair of what was produced and what was wanted turns vague dissatisfaction into a specific gap the instructions can address.","w":["Rewriting on a hunch changes the arrangement without knowing what is wrong. If the guess misses, the pilot now has two versions and still no diagnosis, and the testers' original complaint remains unexplained.","\"Not quite right\" is not actionable, and the cheapest way to make it actionable is to ask for cases. A concrete pair of what was produced and what was wanted turns vague dissatisfaction into a specific gap the instructions can address.","Learning from the satisfied testers may eventually help, but it assumes the difference lies in how the tool was used rather than in the briefings themselves. It also leaves the three unresolved complaints uninvestigated.","Direct observation is thorough but slow, and pausing the pilot stops the flow of evidence just when it is needed. Asking for examples costs an email and usually locates the problem immediately."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c153","d":"general","x":"A manager is about to tell her team that Claude will now produce the first draft of every incident report. What should the announcement make clear above all?","o":["How much time the change is expected to save the team over the course of a full year.","What changes, what stays with each person, and where their review is still required.","Which other departments have already adopted the same approach to their own reports.","How the manager tested the new arrangement before deciding to introduce it to the team."],"a":[1],"e":"People need to know exactly where the boundary now sits: which part Claude drafts, which judgments remain theirs, and that their review is still a required step. Without that, reports are either double-handled or sent out with nobody checking them.","w":["A projected annual saving is the business case rather than the operating instruction. It tells the team why the change is happening but leaves them unsure what they are now responsible for.","People need to know exactly where the boundary now sits: which part Claude drafts, which judgments remain theirs, and that their review is still a required step. Without that, reports are either double-handled or sent out with nobody checking them.","Naming other departments builds confidence in the decision but says nothing about how this team should work. It is reassurance, not direction.","Describing the testing is good practice and supports trust in the change. It still leaves the team without a clear statement of their own remaining responsibilities."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c154","d":"general","x":"A strategy lead is planning a market-entry study and wants Claude to help with the research phase of it. Which two approaches are sound? Select two.","o":["Accept the market figures Claude gives as final, since the answer reads confident and full.","Use Research mode and check the citations it returns back against the original sources.","Ask for the finished conclusion in a single prompt and publish it with no intermediate step.","Leave out the decision the study must support, so Claude is not steered toward an answer.","Ask Claude to draft the research plan and its open questions, then have the team refine it."],"a":[1,4],"w":["Confident phrasing is not evidence, and market figures used for a real decision need checking against sources.","Research mode returns citations, and checking them against the originals is how a claim is actually verified.","Collapsing a study into one prompt and publishing the result removes every point where a human could catch an error.","Stating the decision the study supports is what makes the research useful; withholding it produces unfocused output.","Drafting the plan and the open questions is planning work Claude does well, and the team refining it keeps ownership where it belongs."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c155","d":"general","x":"An analyst is presenting a Claude-assisted workflow to sceptical senior stakeholders next week. Which two things should the presentation include? Select two.","o":["A comparison of technical benchmark scores across each of the underlying model tiers.","Where a human still reviews the output, and why that review step has been kept in place.","A claim that Claude will not make any factual errors once all its instructions are written well.","A specific, measured example of what the workflow improved during the pilot it ran on.","A promise that the workflow removes any need to check the output from this point onward."],"a":[1,3],"w":["Benchmark scores are not what a business audience needs, and they say nothing about this workflow's value.","Naming where human review remains, and why, is honest limitation-setting and is what makes the value claim credible.","Good instructions reduce error but do not eliminate it, so this claim is false and will damage trust later.","A measured pilot result is concrete evidence of value rather than an assertion stakeholders are asked to take on trust.","Promising that checking is no longer needed oversells the tool and sets the expectation that will break first."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c156","d":"general","x":"An operations manager has two things to add to a project: the team's two-page approved abbreviation list, and the rule that answers must never use abbreviations outside that list. What is the best placement?","o":["Put both the reference list and the rule into the project instructions so that the two are always read together.","Upload both the reference list and the rule as documents in the project knowledge base, for consistency.","Keep the reference list on the team wiki and paste the rule at the start of each chat that needs it.","Upload the reference to project knowledge and put the rule about unapproved abbreviations in the instructions."],"a":[3],"e":"Reference material belongs in knowledge; the standing behaviour rule belongs in the instructions.","w":["Two pages of reference text in the instructions field buries the rule that is meant to stand out.","A behaviour rule stored as background material is far weaker than one stated as project direction.","This puts the reference out of reach and turns a standing rule back into manual repetition.","Reference material belongs in knowledge; the standing behaviour rule belongs in the instructions."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c157","d":"general","x":"A project shared with a Team plan workspace has just had its escalation matrix replaced with a new version. What else should the project owner do?","o":["Tell the members who use the project what changed, so they know earlier answers may no longer hold.","Ask each member to create their own copy of the project so everyone controls their own knowledge base.","Delete the project's chat history, because past conversations would otherwise override the new material.","Leave a note in the project title, since members are prompted to read the title before each new chat."],"a":[0],"e":"Maintaining shared configuration includes informing the people whose past work relied on the old source.","w":["Maintaining shared configuration includes informing the people whose past work relied on the old source.","Copies multiply the maintenance problem and guarantee that some members keep using the old matrix.","Old conversations do not override project knowledge, and deleting them destroys a useful record.","A title is not a change log, and members are not prompted to read it before chatting."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c158","d":"general","x":"A policy team keeps a handbook in a Drive folder that changes weekly, and also has one annual report that never changes. Which two set-ups fit best? Select two.","o":["Connect the Drive folder, so the weekly handbook changes are picked up from the source.","Connect the annual report and delete it from Drive, so that only one copy ever exists.","Upload a copy of the handbook just once and then rely on that copy for the rest of this year.","Paste the whole handbook into every new chat, so the newest version is always present.","Upload the fixed annual report into Project knowledge, since its content will not change."],"a":[0,4],"w":["A connector reaches the live source, which is what a document that changes every week needs.","Deleting the original to leave a single copy destroys the source of truth for no benefit.","A one-time copy of a weekly-changing handbook goes stale fast and produces confidently outdated answers.","Re-pasting a whole handbook per chat is the manual work Project knowledge and connectors exist to remove.","A stable document is well suited to a one-time upload into Project knowledge, where it stays available to every chat."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c159","d":"general","x":"An operations lead creates a project on an Enterprise plan and shares it with the whole organisation. To save time she uploads a complete team folder into the project knowledge, and that folder includes a spreadsheet of individual salary bands.\n\nWhat is the greatest risk?","o":["Anyone who opens the shared project can draw the salary details out through ordinary questions.","The extra documents will slow down every answer in the project until the folder has been trimmed back.","The salary spreadsheet can no longer be removed once other members have opened the project.","Claude will decline every request in the project while a salary document remains uploaded."],"a":[0],"e":"Project knowledge is background for every chat in the project, so sharing the project shares everything uploaded to it with everyone who can open it.","w":["Project knowledge is background for every chat in the project, so sharing the project shares everything uploaded to it with everyone who can open it.","A larger knowledge base is a performance and tidiness concern, not the serious exposure here.","Documents can be removed from project knowledge at any time; the exposure is the real problem.","Claude does not refuse to work in a project because a sensitive file has been uploaded to it."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c160","d":"general","x":"A developer integrating Claude into an internal analytics agent wants to give it broad, unsupervised authority to automatically send emails and modify production database records without any human approval step.\n\nWhat ethical concern does this design most directly raise?","o":["None, as long as the agent uses the Claude API rather than the consumer chat product","None, unless the agent is built using a third-party framework instead of Anthropic's own official tools","It removes human oversight from hard-to-reverse actions, risking harm before anyone can intervene","It is acceptable because agentic use is encouraged without oversight requirements in the policy"],"a":[2],"e":"Granting an agent unsupervised authority over irreversible, high-impact actions removes the human oversight needed to catch and correct model errors before they cause real harm.","w":["The oversight concern applies regardless of whether the integration uses the API or consumer product; both are subject to the same responsible-use principles.","The choice of framework does not change the underlying risk of removing human oversight from consequential actions.","Granting an agent unsupervised authority over irreversible, high-impact actions removes the human oversight needed to catch and correct model errors before they cause real harm.","Agentic use must still comply with the full Usage Policy, and unsupervised high-stakes actions are not exempted from oversight expectations."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c161","d":"general","x":"A Team-plan owner is uneasy about conversation content leaving the organisation when members rate Claude's answers. What control is available to them?","o":["Owners can set ratings to be stored only inside the organisation's own private workspace.","Owners can have conversations redacted automatically before any rating is submitted.","Owners can switch off the chat rating feature for the whole organisation in its settings.","Owners can require that every rating is approved by a manager before it is finally sent."],"a":[2],"e":"Team and Enterprise owners can disable rating so that conversations are not submitted with feedback.","w":["Ratings are feedback to Anthropic; they are not retained locally instead.","No automatic redaction step is applied to submitted feedback.","Team and Enterprise owners can disable rating so that conversations are not submitted with feedback.","There is no approval workflow layered on top of ratings."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c162","d":"general","x":"A team lead asks whether using incognito chats on his personal plan makes it acceptable to paste regulated customer records into Claude. What is the best response?","o":["Incognito chats are equivalent to the company's managed workspace, so no separate approval would be required.","Incognito chats are never used to improve Claude, but the policy's rules for regulated data still apply.","Incognito chats satisfy the policy, because nothing from the conversation is retained anywhere after it ends.","Incognito chats are acceptable for regulated data provided customer names are replaced with reference codes."],"a":[1],"e":"Incognito chats are not used to improve Claude, but the policy question is whether regulated data may be processed in that environment at all, and a privacy toggle does not answer it.","w":["A personal plan in incognito mode is not the managed workspace and carries none of its contractual or administrative controls.","Incognito chats are not used to improve Claude, but the policy question is whether regulated data may be processed in that environment at all, and a privacy toggle does not answer it.","Overstating what incognito does is the trap here; the policy governs where regulated data may go, not only whether it trains a model.","Pseudonymising fields does not make regulated records unregulated, and the approval question remains open."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c163","d":"general","x":"A consultant is about to send a client a report she drafted with Claude. Her firm's AI policy sets out several requirements for client-facing work produced with AI, and she is unsure which of them apply to a report of this kind.\n\nWhat should she do first?","o":["Ask the client whether they have any objection to AI assistance having been used on the report.","Add a short line stating the report was drafted with AI assistance, and send it to the client.","Ask her engagement manager to sign the report off, so responsibility for it rests with him instead.","Establish what the policy requires for a deliverable of this kind, before the report goes out."],"a":[3],"e":"The firm has a written standard covering exactly this situation, so the first step is to read it and identify which requirements apply to this deliverable. Everything else she might do is a guess at what the policy says.","w":["The client's comfort does not replace the firm's own requirements. Asking them cannot tell her what her firm obliges her to do, and their agreement would not excuse skipping it.","This guesses at one requirement and treats it as the whole of the policy. Disclosure may be needed, but so may review, sign-off or restrictions on the client data involved.","A sign-off may well be one of the requirements, but choosing it because it shifts responsibility is not following the policy. She still does not know what the policy asks for.","The firm has a written standard covering exactly this situation, so the first step is to read it and identify which requirements apply to this deliverable. Everything else she might do is a guess at what the policy says."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c164","d":"general","x":"A school district's operations team wants Claude to work through the admissions criteria and produce the letters telling families whether their child has a place at an oversubscribed school.\n\nWhat should the team do first?","o":["Ask Claude to restate the district's admissions criteria so the team can confirm it has them right.","Run the letters for one school first and see whether any family complains about the wording.","Separate deciding who gets a place from writing the letters, and keep the decision with staff.","Add an instruction telling Claude to apply the admissions criteria exactly as they are written."],"a":[2],"e":"A school place materially affects a child's life and the decision is hard to reverse once families have been told, so it belongs with accountable staff. Drafting the letters afterwards is a text task where an error is recoverable, and splitting the two makes the appropriate part safe to delegate.","w":["Confirming that Claude can repeat the criteria says nothing about whether it should be the thing applying them. A correct restatement would make the team more comfortable handing over a decision it should not hand over.","Using one school as the trial means real families receive decisions from an untested arrangement, and complaints are a late and unreliable signal of whether the allocation was right.","A school place materially affects a child's life and the decision is hard to reverse once families have been told, so it belongs with accountable staff. Drafting the letters afterwards is a text task where an error is recoverable, and splitting the two makes the appropriate part safe to delegate.","A firmer instruction addresses how faithfully the criteria are applied, not whether an allocation affecting children's schooling should be made this way at all."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c165","d":"general","x":"A market research manager has been asked to summarise survey free-text responses that include respondents' names and employers, using the company's Claude for Work plan. In which order should these steps be taken?","o":["Remove the names and employers that the summary does not need; check what the participant consent and company policy allow; run the summary and review it before it is circulated; note what was removed so the result can be interpreted properly.","Run the summary and review it before it is circulated; check what the participant consent and company policy allow; remove the names and employers that the summary does not need; note what was removed so the result can be interpreted properly.","Check what the participant consent and company policy allow; remove the names and employers that the summary does not need; run the summary and review it before it is circulated; note what was removed so the result can be interpreted properly.","Check what the participant consent and company policy allow; run the summary and review it before it is circulated; remove the names and employers that the summary does not need; note what was removed so the result can be interpreted properly."],"a":[2],"e":"Consent and policy set the boundary for what may be used at all; that boundary determines which identifiers can be stripped before anything is uploaded; the summary is then produced and reviewed within those limits; and recording what was removed lets colleagues read the findings knowing what the data no longer contains.","w":["Data is edited before anyone has established what participants agreed to, so the amount of personal detail removed is guesswork and may be either too little or more than the analysis can afford.","The personal data is processed and the output reviewed before either the consent position or the minimisation step, which is the sequence that causes the exposure rather than preventing it.","Consent and policy set the boundary for what may be used at all; that boundary determines which identifiers can be stripped before anything is uploaded; the summary is then produced and reviewed within those limits; and recording what was removed lets colleagues read the findings knowing what the data no longer contains.","The consent check happens first, but the full identifiable transcripts are still put into the tool before the names and employers are removed, so the minimisation step comes too late to protect anyone."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c166","d":"general","x":"A support-ticket triage prompt gives Claude a single sentence: \"Categorize this ticket.\" Categories come back inconsistent between runs, and the team wants a fix that addresses the root cause rather than adding post-processing.\n\nWhat should they change first?","o":["Lower the temperature parameter to zero so sampling becomes deterministic and the same ticket always returns the same category.","Route every ticket to a larger model on the assumption that added model capacity will infer the missing category definitions.","Rewrite the prompt to list the exact categories, the desired output format, and the criteria for choosing between them.","Have a reviewer manually relabel any ticket that looks miscategorized before the ticket reaches a customer-facing queue."],"a":[2],"e":"Claude responds well to clear, explicit instructions; a vague one-line prompt lacking the category list, output format, and selection criteria is the classic underperformance pattern, and the documented fix is to be specific about desired output and constraints.","w":["Temperature affects sampling randomness, not whether the model understands what categories exist or how to choose between them; it does not address an underspecified prompt.","Swapping models does not resolve ambiguity in the instructions themselves; an underspecified prompt will still under perform on a larger model.","Claude responds well to clear, explicit instructions; a vague one-line prompt lacking the category list, output format, and selection criteria is the classic underperformance pattern, and the documented fix is to be specific about desired output and constraints.","Manual relabeling is a workaround applied after generation, not a fix to the prompt causing the inconsistency."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c167","d":"general","x":"An analyst wants to get more done inside her weekly allowance without the quality of her work dropping. Which sequence of working habits is correct?","o":["Put the request and the relevant material in the opening message; work through follow-ups that refer back to it; open a new chat for the next unrelated task; decide what she needs before starting.","Open a new chat for the next unrelated task; decide what she needs before starting; work through follow-ups that refer back to it; put the request and the relevant material in the opening message.","Decide what she needs before starting; put the request and the relevant material in the opening message; work through follow-ups that refer back to it; open a new chat for the next unrelated task.","Work through follow-ups that refer back to it; open a new chat for the next unrelated task; decide what she needs before starting; put the request and the relevant material in the opening message."],"a":[2],"e":"Planning before starting, front-loading the material in one well-specified message, then building on it, is what keeps the exchange short; unrelated work starts fresh rather than dragging old context along.","w":["Everything here is done before she has decided what she needs, so the opening message is assembled blind.","Opening a chat for the next task before the current one has been started puts the last step first.","Planning before starting, front-loading the material in one well-specified message, then building on it, is what keeps the exchange short; unrelated work starts fresh rather than dragging old context along.","Following up before the request has even been made, and deciding what is needed last, inverts the whole habit."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c168","d":"general","x":"A researcher is doing straightforward drafting work while several connectors and tools she does not need for it remain switched on. What is a reasonable step to make routine work leaner?","o":["Ask Claude in each message to avoid using any connector while she works on the drafting.","Do the drafting inside a separate project that has no documents in its knowledge at all.","Turn off the connectors and tools this task does not need, and switch them back on later.","Remove the connectors from her account permanently, so nothing can call on them by mistake."],"a":[2],"e":"Turning off tools and connectors that a task does not need is a recognised way to keep routine work efficient, and they can be re-enabled when the next task needs them.","w":["An instruction in every message is a per-message reminder rather than a change to the setup.","An empty project addresses knowledge documents, not the tools and connections that stay enabled.","Turning off tools and connectors that a task does not need is a recognised way to keep routine work efficient, and they can be re-enabled when the next task needs them.","Removing them permanently costs her the reconnection work every time the other tasks come round."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c169","d":"general","x":"Several engineers tell a site coordinator that Claude's shift-handover digests 'skip the open risks', but nobody says which risks or which shifts. Which two steps should the coordinator take first? Select two.","o":["Ask for two digests where an open risk was missed, with the missing risk actually named.","Rewrite the shared project instructions straight away to stress that open risks matter most of all.","Compare those digests against the shift logs to see exactly what was left out of them.","Ask everyone to score every future digest out of ten so that a trend can be tracked over time.","Switch the digests into a table format, since open risks may then be easier to spot in a table."],"a":[0,2],"w":["Vague feedback cannot be acted on; specific failing examples turn an impression into something diagnosable.","Changing shared instructions before the cause is known risks fixing the wrong thing for everyone at once.","on separate grounds: only the source shows whether the risk was absent from the log, buried, or genuinely dropped.","A score captures sentiment but still never says which risks went missing, or why they went missing.","A format change may help presentation, but it cannot add a risk the digest never captured."]},{"id":"ccao-f-c170","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are reviewing a Claude-generated competitor brief for unsupported claims.\nWhich is the correct order of review steps?\n1. Cross-check each specific claim against the supplied source material or trusted external references.\n2. Decide whether to correct, re-prompt, or escalate based on the findings.\n3. Document each unsupported or inconsistent claim with its location and severity.\n4. Identify the specific factual claims, statistics, and citations in the brief.\n5. Read the brief from beginning to end and flag any claims that feel surprising.","o":["5, 1, 4, 3, 2","1, 4, 5, 3, 2","5, 4, 1, 3, 2","4, 5, 1, 3, 2"],"a":[2],"w":["Wrong order: cross-checking sources before systematically identifying which claims need checking swaps two steps.","Starts verifying before understanding the whole document and identifying which claims need review.","Correct: read the whole thing, identify the claims, verify them, document the issues, then decide how to handle them.","Wrong order: read the full text for context first, before building a detailed list of claims."],"t":"Read through → list what needs verifying → verify → document → decide. A 'surprising' claim is only a signal to check — it doesn't prove the claim is wrong.","k":["unsupported claims","review order","trustworthy"],"e":"Correct: read the whole thing, identify the claims, verify them, document the issues, then decide how to handle them."},{"id":"ccao-f-c171","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are running an optimization pass on a recurring workflow.\nWhich is the correct order of optimization steps?\n(1) Apply targeted optimization to the bottleneck step\n(2) Roll out the optimized workflow to the broader team and document the change\n(3) Identify the bottleneck step using measured time per step and queue length\n(4) Map the current workflow steps, owners, and decision points\n(5) Pilot the change with a small group and measure the effect against the prior baseline","o":["4, 1, 3, 5, 2","4, 3, 1, 5, 2","3, 4, 1, 5, 2","1, 4, 3, 5, 2"],"a":[1],"w":["Optimizing before finding the bottleneck can pour effort into the wrong step.","Correct: understand the process → measure and find the bottleneck → make targeted changes → pilot and compare → roll out with documentation.","Wrong sequence: you need a map of the steps, owners and decisions before you can diagnose bottlenecks between them.","Changing things before mapping or measuring the current state leaves no basis for spotting the problem or proving the impact."],"t":"Map → Measure → Improve → Pilot → Roll out. A broad rollout always comes after a measured pilot.","k":["repeatable process","bottleneck","baseline"],"e":"Correct: understand the process → measure and find the bottleneck → make targeted changes → pilot and compare → roll out with documentation."},{"id":"ccao-f-c172","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a senior analyst using Claude to analyze requirements from several stakeholder sources.\nWhich is the correct order of steps?\n(1) Group related requirements and flag conflicts or ambiguities for stakeholder review.\n(2) Validate the structured output against the original sources for accuracy and completeness.\n(3) Submit the consolidated inputs to Claude with a structured extraction prompt.\n(4) Gather and label the source materials so each requirement can be traced.\n(5) Document the final requirements set with source traceability and status.","o":["4, 1, 3, 2, 5","4, 3, 1, 2, 5","1, 4, 3, 2, 5","3, 4, 1, 2, 5"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccao-f-c173","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a knowledge worker deciding whether to escalate a Claude-generated output.\nWhich is the correct order of decision steps?\n(1) Identify the audience and the impact of the output if it is wrong.\n(2) Document the decision and the rationale for traceability.\n(3) Compare the output’s risk profile to the organization’s escalation criteria.\n(4) Decide whether to use, iterate further, or escalate.\n(5) Assess the output’s accuracy, completeness, and any unverified claims.","o":["5, 1, 3, 4, 2","3, 1, 5, 4, 2","1, 5, 3, 4, 2","1, 3, 5, 4, 2"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c174","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a process consultant integrating Claude into an existing weekly workflow.\nWhich is the correct order of integration steps?\n1. Pilot the integrated workflow with a small group and gather feedback.\n2. Document the current workflow steps, owners, and decision points.\n3. Update the documented workflow and roll it out to the broader team.\n4. Identify the steps where Claude can augment work and where humans must remain.\n5. Define the inputs Claude needs, the outputs Claude returns, and the hand-offs around each step.","o":["2, 4, 5, 1, 3","2, 5, 4, 1, 3","4,2, 5, 1, 3","1, 2, 4, 5, 3"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: understand the current process, split AI vs. human roles, design the hand-off points, pilot small, then refine and scale.","Defining inputs/outputs before deciding which steps Claude takes part in reverses the dependency order.","Choosing where to use Claude before mapping the process risks missing owners and decision points.","Piloting before understanding and designing the flow leaves you without the criteria and configuration you need."],"t":"Integration runs current state → roles → inputs/outputs → pilot → rollout. Follow the dependencies instead of memorizing numbers.","k":["Claude integration","weekly workflow","sequence"],"e":"Correct: understand the current process, split AI vs. human roles, design the hand-off points, pilot small, then refine and scale."},{"id":"ccao-f-c175","d":"practice-sets","x":"Claude turns a response into a separate Artifact rather than an inline chat reply mainly when the content is:","o":["Substantial, self-contained, and reusable","A response that simply runs past a few paragraphs of prose","Written in a formal, professional tone","Generated during an active Research session"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: Artifacts suit standalone outputs that can be viewed, edited, reused or interacted with outside the chat flow.","Length is secondary — a long answer can still work fine as a direct reply.","Writing tone doesn't decide whether content needs its own workspace.","Using Research doesn't automatically turn every result into an Artifact."],"t":"Spot outputs with a life of their own: documents, code, interactive tools, or drafts you'll edit many times. Don't just count paragraphs.","k":["Artifact","direct reply","nature of the output"],"e":"Correct: Artifacts suit standalone outputs that can be viewed, edited, reused or interacted with outside the chat flow."},{"id":"ccao-f-c176","d":"practice-sets","x":"A Pro-plan user avoids Haiku 4.5 entirely, believing it is only a lesser model for people who cannot access better\nones. Which statement corrects this?","o":["Haiku 4.5 is offered mainly to Free-plan users as an entry-level option","Haiku 4.5 is best reserved for tasks needing the deepest reasoning","Haiku 4.5 produces the highest-quality reasoning of the four","Haiku 4.5 is the deliberate choice for quick, high-volume tasks"],"a":[3],"w":["A model's role shouldn't be defined by plan tier or by assuming users have limited access.","Tasks that need the deepest reasoning usually call for a more capable model, not the lightweight one.","The advantage in question is speed and efficiency, not a claim that Haiku tops every reasoning benchmark.","Correct: picking Haiku for fast, high-volume work is the optimal fit for the job — not settling for a lower tier."],"t":"Choosing a model is about fit for the task, not ranking users. Fast and cost-efficient may be the most important requirement.","k":["Haiku 4.5","Pro plan","misconception"],"e":"Correct: picking Haiku for fast, high-volume work is the optimal fit for the job — not settling for a lower tier."},{"id":"ccao-f-c177","d":"practice-sets","x":"A business user is unsure which model to pick for typical everyday writing and analysis.\nAccording to Anthropic's guidance, which model is the recommended default?","o":["Opus 4.8, reserved for complex high-stakes reasoning","Haiku 4.5, the fastest and lightest model","Sonnet 5, the balanced default for most work","Fable 5, the newest and most capable model"],"a":[2],"w":["Wrong for this need: Opus fits hard tasks, but maximum capability isn't necessary for everyday work.","Wrong as a balanced default: Haiku fits light, fast, high-volume tasks.","Correct: the Sonnet line balances capability, cost and speed for most writing and analysis work.","Newest or most powerful doesn't make it the right default for routine business work."],"t":"Everyday, balanced work → Sonnet. Fast / high-volume → Haiku. Hard reasoning → the higher-capability line.","k":["everyday writing and analysis","default","model"],"e":"Correct: the Sonnet line balances capability, cost and speed for most writing and analysis work."},{"id":"ccao-f-c178","d":"practice-sets","x":"For external-facing or sensitive content, Anthropic recommends disclosing Al involvement in addition to which\nother safeguard?","o":["Qualified professional review before it reaches consumers","Switching to the account's strongest available model","Attaching citations to the generated draft for source traceability","Turning off the model-improvement training toggle"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: disclosing AI assistance supports transparency, and expert review checks accuracy, fit and risk before publishing.","A stronger model can still be wrong; switching models doesn't replace an accountable human reviewer.","Wrong as a substitute: citations help traceability, but you still have to check the sources exist and actually support the claims.","Training opt-out settings are about data handling, not about certifying the quality of content before release."],"t":"Transparency, privacy and quality control are three complementary layers — none replaces the others.","k":["sensitive","external-facing","disclosure"],"e":"Correct: disclosing AI assistance supports transparency, and expert review checks accuracy, fit and risk before publishing."},{"id":"ccao-f-c179","d":"practice-sets","x":"Regenerating the same prompt gives you three noticeably different values for a key metric across three tries.\nYou need a single reliable number for a finance summary due to leadership today. Given the inconsistency across\nregenerations, what is the most appropriate action?","o":["Pick the value that appeared in two of the three tries","Use whichever value is closest to what you already expected","Average the three values and report the resulting mean figure","Treat the inconsistency as a warning; verify the source data"],"a":[3],"w":["Re-running doesn't prove a value is backed by the source data — the question already says the three values differ.","Choosing the value you expected invites confirmation bias instead of verification.","An average of unverified numbers can still be wrong and has no basis.","Correct: check against the source data or a reproducible calculation, then use the verified figure."],"t":"With numbers, voting on or averaging AI outputs never replaces calculating from the real source data.","k":["three different values","financial","reliable"],"e":"Correct: check against the source data or a reproducible calculation, then use the verified figure."},{"id":"ccao-f-c180","d":"practice-sets","x":"A team plans to run a Research session for every routine status question in a daily workflow. What is the key cost\nconsideration they should weigh?","o":["Research runs on a separate budget that does not touch message limits","Research is unavailable on paid plans for daily use","Research consumes usage quota faster than a normal message","Research lowers the account's usage limit ceiling for the rest of the day"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccao-f-c181","d":"practice-sets","x":"A strategist needs both careful reasoning and heavy multi-source gathering for a detailed market-entry analysis.\nWhich combination does the guidance support for this task?","o":["Neither is needed, since a single Web Search covers everything","Brainstorming alone, since ideation naturally includes reasoning and sources","Research combined with Extended Thinking for reasoning plus source-gathering","Concise Style, which balances depth and brevity for you"],"a":[2],"w":["A single web search doesn't guarantee enough evidence, coverage or analysis for a complex market question.","Generating ideas isn't the same as gathering, vetting and cross-checking sources.","Correct: Research gathers and synthesizes information; Extended Thinking supports deep reasoning over it.","A concise style only changes presentation; it doesn't replace research or reasoning."],"t":"Split the two needs: finding evidence and analyzing evidence. Don't confuse style settings with capability tools.","k":["careful reasoning","many sources","market entry"],"e":"Correct: Research gathers and synthesizes information; Extended Thinking supports deep reasoning over it."},{"id":"ccao-f-c182","d":"practice-sets","x":"One section of a long Markdown document in an Artifact needs a tone change for a different audience, while the\nrest is correct. What is the most appropriate way to revise it?","o":["regenerate the entire document from scratch with a new prompt","start a new conversation and rebuild the whole document","highlight the section and request a targeted edit of that part","retry the original prompt to generate an alternate full version"],"a":[2],"w":["Regenerating everything is unnecessary and may change parts that were already right.","Rebuilding in a new chat wastes time and can lose approved context and structure.","Correct: editing the specific section fixes the problem while leaving everything else untouched.","Full-text variants don't directly target the tone issue in one section."],"t":"Small problem → small edit scope. Preserving what's already correct is part of quality control.","k":["one section","rest already correct","Artifact"],"e":"Correct: editing the specific section fixes the problem while leaving everything else untouched."},{"id":"ccao-f-c183","d":"practice-sets","x":"A prompt that has produced excellent results all week returns one oddly disorganized answer. The user believes\nthe prompt itself is fine. What is the best first move?","o":["Use Retry to regenerate a fresh answer to the prompt","Rewrite the prompt from scratch to prevent future errors","Switch to a more capable model for this request","Start a brand-new conversation and re-explain the whole task"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: for a one-off glitch, regenerating is the least disruptive change when the prompt has a good track record.","There isn't enough evidence of a systemic prompt flaw to justify rewriting from scratch.","One odd result doesn't prove the model lacks capability for the task.","Rebuilding all the context is heavier than needed for one messy response."],"t":"Tell a one-off glitch from a recurring problem. Retry fits a single anomaly; persistent errors need diagnosis and a targeted fix.","k":["all week","one response","first step"],"e":"Correct: for a one-off glitch, regenerating is the least disruptive change when the prompt has a good track record."},{"id":"ccao-f-c184","d":"practice-sets","x":"You're briefing stakeholders on where Claude's output needs extra scrutiny. Which single category should you tell\nthem to always check against an authoritative primary source before use?","o":["Formatting and layout choices in a routine internal draft memo","Brainstormed lists of rough, early-stage project ideas","Statistics, legal claims, citations, and direct quotes","Minortone adjustments applied to a casual internal team message"],"a":[2],"w":["Wrong priority: layout needs a fit check, but it usually isn't a factual claim that needs a primary source.","Rough ideas need evaluating when implemented, but they aren't automatically claims you can prove from a source.","Correct: figures, legal points, sources and quotes can be fabricated — check the original and its context.","Style edits mainly need a communication-fit check, not fact verification against primary sources."],"t":"Whenever content asserts a fact, number, rule or quote, find the original — don't trust fluent wording.","k":["primary source","authoritative","before use"],"e":"Correct: figures, legal points, sources and quotes can be fabricated — check the original and its context."},{"id":"ccao-f-c185","d":"practice-sets","x":"Which of the following features lets a user build an interactive working mockup that iterates in its own\nside panel?","o":["Styles, which set tone and format per conversation","Memory, which synthesizes background insights automatically","Web search, which returns quick factual lookups","Artifacts, which hold reusable, iterable content separately"],"a":[3],"w":["Styles shape how responses are written; they don't give you a space to build interactive tools.","Memory keeps useful context between sessions; it isn't a prototyping tool.","Web Search retrieves online information; it doesn't hold or run a prototype.","Correct: an Artifact can hold standalone output like code, documents or sample apps to view and edit over many turns."],"t":"Interactive output in its own panel → Artifacts. Tone → Styles, background info → Memory, lookups → Search.","k":["interactive prototype","separate panel","conversation"],"e":"Correct: an Artifact can hold standalone output like code, documents or sample apps to view and edit over many turns."},{"id":"ccao-f-c186","d":"practice-sets","x":"You enabled a custom Skill and a colleague worries it will bloat every prompt the way project instructions do.\nWhich fact reassures them?","o":["A Skill is injected into every prompt like instructions","A Skill overrides profile instructions across the whole account","A Skill can be created only by an org administrator","A Skill loads only when Claude judges the task relevant"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccao-f-c187","d":"practice-sets","x":"With the Gmail connector active, a user asks Claude to prepare a reply to a vendor. Before actually creating that\ndraft, the connector requires:","o":["A one-time authorization that covers later email actions","The user's explicit approval for that specific draft action","Admin sign-off before a reply draft can be generated","Nothing further, since the connector itself was already enabled"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccao-f-c188","d":"practice-sets","x":"A business user gets a vague, unhelpful first draft from Claude. According to Anthropic's guidance, what does an\neffective prompt most reliably need?","o":["The most capable model paired with a high effort setting","Explicit context, a specific task, and output format","Forceful, capitalized wording that demands compliance","A single sweeping instruction covering the whole task"],"a":[1],"w":["Model capability and effort can't make up for a missing goal or requirements.","Correct: context, task and output format tell the model what to use, what to do and how to present it.","A commanding tone doesn't replace specific, checkable instructions.","Overly broad instructions tend to miss the audience, constraints and output standards."],"t":"Look for the trio context–task–format. When output is vague, fix the specification before raising model capability or effort.","k":["vague","effective prompt","task"],"e":"Correct: context, task and output format tell the model what to use, what to do and how to present it."},{"id":"ccao-f-c189","d":"practice-sets","x":"Within a single Project, which mechanism makes uploaded material available to every chat in that Project?","o":["The Project knowledge base, shared across all chats","The conversation content of any one chat in the Project","The Project description field entered when creating the Project","A Custom Style applied to the current conversation only"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: the knowledge base holds shared reference material, so you don't re-attach the same files in every chat.","One chat's content doesn't automatically become shared reference material for the whole Project.","The description states the Project's purpose; it isn't a store for source documents.","Style controls how Claude communicates; it doesn't share reference files across chats."],"t":"Documents reused across many chats belong in Project knowledge — not in one chat's content or in tone settings.","k":["every chat","Project","uploaded documents"],"e":"Correct: the knowledge base holds shared reference material, so you don't re-attach the same files in every chat."},{"id":"ccao-f-c190","d":"practice-sets","x":"Which SIAM process is responsible for restoring a service that has been degraded or interrupted?","o":["incident management","event management","measuring and monitoring","problem management"],"a":[0],"w":["Correct: incident management prioritizes restoring normal operation quickly and minimizing the impact of the disruption.","Event management detects and handles state-change signals; restoring service isn't its main job.","Measurement and monitoring track health and performance; they help detection but don't replace incident management.","Problem management targets root causes and prevents recurrence, not immediate recovery."],"t":"Incident = restore the service. Problem = find and fix the root cause.","k":["SIAM","restore","disruption"],"e":"Correct: incident management prioritizes restoring normal operation quickly and minimizing the impact of the disruption."},{"id":"ccao-f-c191","d":"practice-sets","x":"A knowledge worker is preparing to use the Gmail connector with Claude for the first time.\nWhich statement most accurately describes the connector's behavior at the user level?","o":["Claude searches and reads emails the user has access to and labels them according to user-defined rules,\nwith reply drafting handled in a separate workflow.","Claude searches and reads emails the user has access to and drafts replies for the user to send manually,\nwith each action requiring explicit user approval.","Claude searches and reads emails the user has access to in a read-only mode, and the user must compose\nany reply manually outside the connector.","Claude searches and reads emails the user has access to and sends replies on the user's behalf for low-risk\nmessages, with higher-risk messages routed to the user for approval."],"a":[1],"e":"with each action requiring explicit user approval."},{"id":"ccao-f-c192","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a Claude associate troubleshooting a Claude response that introduces facts not present in the supplied\nsource.\nWhich root cause is most directly indicated?","o":["The prompt asks Claude to act as a researcher rather than a writer.","The prompt's heading-based structure encourages Claude to generate placeholder content under each\nheading even when the source doesn't cover it.","The prompt does not constrain Claude to use only facts present in the supplied source.","The prompt asks for a comprehensive response, which leads Claude to fill perceived gaps with plausible-\nsounding but unverified detail."],"a":[2],"w":["The role name doesn't by itself cause unsupported facts; the source constraint matters more.","Not the most direct cause: a title can create pressure to fill gaps, but it's safe alongside a no-inference, flag-missing-data rule.","Correct: without a grounded / source-only constraint, the output can go beyond the data provided.","It can raise risk but isn't the most direct cause: a request for completeness can still be paired with a source-only limit."],"t":"When output must stick to the documents, say it: 'use only the sources; if something is missing, say so; don't add anything.' This lowers risk but doesn't replace verification.","k":["not in the source","most direct","prompt"],"e":"Correct: without a grounded / source-only constraint, the output can go beyond the data provided."},{"id":"ccao-f-c193","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a solution architect designing a Claude-based assistant with access to 60 internal tools across multiple\nbusiness domains. Loading every tool definition on every request increases token usage and time to first response.\nWhich design pattern best addresses this issue without sacrificing capability breadth?","o":["Increase the maximum context length so all 60 tool definitions fit within a single request without exceeding\nthe limit.","Apply progressive tool discovery so a curated initial subset is exposed and additional tools are loaded on\ndemand based on the task.","Group the 60 tools into five fixed categories and load one category per request based on a static mapping of\ntask type to category.","Use a separate model call to condense all 60 tool definitions into a compact index that is prepended to each\nuser turn for tool selection."],"a":[1],"w":["Raising the context limit doesn't reduce the tokens loaded or the initial latency.","Correct: discovering and loading tools on demand cuts the initial input while keeping access to the full tool set.","Not the best: fixed groups reduce load but are inflexible when a task needs tools from several groups.","Not the best: building an index with a separate call still adds a processing step and repeats context every turn."],"t":"Many tools but only a few per task → think lazy loading / on-demand discovery, not raising limits.","k":["60 tools","tokens","time to first response"],"e":"Correct: discovering and loading tools on demand cuts the initial input while keeping access to the full tool set."},{"id":"ccao-f-c194","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are a platform architect designing an internal Claude-based assistant that serves both finance analysts and\nexternal auditors. Each population must access only documents permitted by their role.\nWhere should role-based access control be enforced in the pipeline?","o":["after the response is generated, by post-filtering content that should not have been retrieved","at the retrieval layer, before any role-restricted content reaches the prompt construction step or the model","inside the system prompt as a natural-language instruction that names the specific role-based access\ngroups Claude should treat as unauthorized","at the retrieval layer, but relying on a single organization-wide access token shared across all roles rather\nthan per-role scoping"],"a":[1],"w":["Unauthorized data has already reached the model and can shape the output; filtering afterwards is too late to be an access boundary.","Correct: filtering by user permissions before building the context stops unauthorized documents from ever reaching the model.","Prompt instructions aren't a security boundary and can't replace system-level permission checks.","An org-wide shared token can't tell the two user groups' permissions apart."],"t":"Data security must block before the model. Never put confidential data in the prompt and hope the model or an output filter keeps it secret.","k":["role-based access control","retrieval layer","before"],"e":"Correct: filtering by user permissions before building the context stops unauthorized documents from ever reaching the model."},{"id":"ccao-f-c195","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are selecting the documentation set that should accompany a Claude-based deployment at handoff.\nWhich set is most complete?","o":["the original design proposal, its review comments, meeting notes from design sessions, and the approved\nrequirements document","a project retrospective document, a lessons-learned summary, and a list of contributors","architecture overview, ADRs, component-level diagrams, runbooks for common operations, on-call playbook,\nand a list of known limitations","user-facing release notes, a marketing one-pager describing the deployment, and a customer FAQ"],"a":[2],"w":["Not enough: these give design history and goals but miss operations, incident handling and real-world limits.","Not enough: lessons learned and a contributor list don't equip the receiving team to run the system.","Correct: covers how the system is built, why the design was chosen, how to operate it, how to handle incidents and which limits to know.","Not enough: customer-facing material can't replace the technical and operational procedures a handover needs."],"t":"A good handover pack answers four questions: what the system is, why it's built that way, how to run it and handle failures, and what its limits are.","k":["handover","most complete","documentation"],"e":"Correct: covers how the system is built, why the design was chosen, how to operate it, how to handle incidents and which limits to know."},{"id":"ccao-f-c196","d":"practice-sets","x":"When refining a Claude output that does not meet expectations.\nWhich iterative refinement step should be performed first?","o":["Submit the revised prompt and capture the new output.","Compare the new output against the original criteria.","Identify the specific weaknesses in the current output.","Modify the prompt to address each identified weakness."],"a":[2],"w":["Wrong order: without a diagnosis you don't know what to fix before rerunning.","Wrong order: comparing a new version comes after a change has produced new results.","Correct: pinpointing the gap enables a targeted change and clear criteria to check it worked.","Not first: you can only fix a weakness after you've identified it."],"t":"A directed loop starts with diagnosis, then fix, rerun and compare — never with random edits.","k":["below expectations","first","weakness"],"e":"Correct: pinpointing the gap enables a targeted change and clear criteria to check it worked."},{"id":"ccao-f-c197","d":"practice-sets","x":"You are running a weekly feedback-and-adjustment cycle.\nWhich is the correct order of cycle steps?\n(1) Translate feedback into one or two targeted adjustments for the next cycle.\n(2) Run the next cycle and compare results against the prior cycle.\n(3) Capture the specific feedback received during the review.\n(4) Document what changed, what effect it produced, and any remaining issues.\n(5) Decide whether to continue iterating, switch approach, or escalate.","o":["2, 3, 1, 4, 5","1, 3, 2, 4, 5","3, 1, 2, 4, 5","3, 2, 1, 4, 5"],"a":[2],"w":["Runs a new cycle before collecting the feedback that should guide the change.","Picks an adjustment before recording specific feedback.","Correct: gather feedback → make targeted adjustments → test and compare → record results → decide the next step.","Has feedback but tests straight away, before turning it into a change worth verifying."],"t":"Follow the dependencies: feedback before change, change before testing, results before the next decision.","k":["feedback cycle","targeted adjustment","comparison","escalation"],"e":"Correct: gather feedback → make targeted adjustments → test and compare → record results → decide the next step."}]},{"id":"ccdv-f","code":"CCDV-F","title":"Claude Certified Developer — Foundations","vendor":"Anthropic","desc":"Claude Code, MCP tools, structured outputs, multi-agent workflows and developer automation.","exam":{"count":60,"minutes":90,"passPercent":80},"domains":{"general":"General"},"qs":[{"id":"ccdv-f-c1","d":"general","x":"A developer creates an AI coding assistant for a known workflow with repeatable steps. \nWhich design approach is appropriate?","o":["Allow unlimited autonomous exploration","Use a fixed sequence of prompts","Remove all workflow instructions","Use random decision paths"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c2","d":"general","x":"A team wants stable automated pull request review results from Claude Code. \nWhich method best supports automation?","o":["Use stricter markdown parsing rules","Post the entire response for manual review","Use JSON output with a defined schema","Split responses using headings"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c3","d":"general","x":"A team needs to update every file ending with `.fixture.json` across a monorepo. Searching for the word “fixture” creates noisy results and misses sparse files. \nWhich tool-use strategy should be adopted?","o":["Use Glob patterns like `**/*.fixture.json` and then Read matching files.","Use Grep for \"fixture\" and infer matching files.","Read all possible files before searching.","Use Bash commands and paste the entire directory listing."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c4","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nDeveloper Productivity with Claude Your CI job invokes Claude Code successfully and posts a single pull request summary comment. \nThe team now wants automated inline comments for each finding, but the integration frequently breaks because Claude sometimes adds prose, changes field names, or formats findings as markdown. \nWhat change best supports reliable automation?","o":["Parse Claude's markdown bullets with regular expressions.","Run Claude Code with `--output-format json` and `--json-schema`, then map validated findings to inline PR comments.","Strengthen the prompt to demand valid JSON only.","Store previous comments and ask Claude to rewrite them into records."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c5","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nMulti-Agent Research System The document analysis subagent receives coordinator-selected PDFs and reports from an approved catalog, but it has a generic URL retrieval tool. \nIt sometimes follows links inside documents to: l blogs l login pages l duplicate HTML summaries and then cites those pages instead of the approved sources. \nWhat change best addresses this?","o":["Keep fetch_url available, but add prompt instructions warning the subagent never to open links found inside documents.","Give the document analysis subagent web search tools too, so it can independently confirm whether linked pages are relevant.","Allow fetch_url for any link, then have synthesis discard citations whose domains are not in the approved catalog.","Replace fetch_url with a load_document tool that accepts catalog document IDs or approved URLs and validates before fetching."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c6","d":"general","x":"A coordinator launches subagents to analyze API handlers, database writes, and scheduled jobs. \nFinal architecture reports often attach claims to incorrect files or omit line ranges. \nWhat improves handoff reliability?","o":["Require structured handoff records with paths, symbols, line ranges, and evidence snippets.","Use regex parsing on summaries.","Ask the synthesizer to reconstruct locations.","Concatenate complete transcripts"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c7","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nStructured Data Extraction Your QA pipeline flags mistakes in supplier contracts. \nAnalysts accept many findings about missing renewal clauses but reject most findings involving informal pricing language. \nCurrent logs store only: l Document ID l Field name l Confidence l Finding text \nWhat change should you make first?","o":["Increase QA confidence threshold globally.","Store only analyst decisions.","Add a structured detected_pattern field to each QA finding, then analyze acceptance rates by pattern and document type.","Tell Claude to report only findings analysts accept."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c8","d":"general","x":"A multi-agent system uses a coordinator and specialized researchers. \nHow should required MCP server configuration be shared across a team repository?","o":["Combine all integrations into one MCP server","Store all servers in personal user configuration","Ask every user to recreate servers manually","Place required shared servers in project-scoped .mcp.json"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c9","d":"general","x":"A customer support agent alternates unpredictably between `get_customer` and `lookup_order` when handling mixed requests. \nThe MCP tool catalog only contains short autogenerated descriptions. \nWhat change should you make first to improve tool selection reliability?","o":["Update the system prompt to tell Claude to think carefully before selecting tools.","Consolidate get_customer and lookup_order into one broad lookup tool.","Rewrite each MCP tool description to specify purpose, accepted identifiers, returned fields, edge cases, and boundaries.","Add a keyword router that maps phrases like “order” or “refund” to one tool."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c10","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's content policy specifies categories of content it should not produce under any circumstance. \nThe application currently has no mechanism to enforce this policy, and content matching these categories is appearing in the application's output. \nHow would you enforce the content policy?","o":["Enhance the system prompt to contain explicit instructions for the categories to avoid, complete with examples of each category. Treat the strengthened prompt as the primary enforcement mechanism for the application's content policy across all responses.","Move enforcement to users by asking them to report content policy violations after the violating content has already reached them in the application's responses.","Add deterministic output filtering that checks responses against the content policy before they reach users.","Remove the content policy entirely and let any output reach users during normal operation, accepting whatever content the application produces in response to incoming traffic."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c11","d":"general","x":"A debugging agent investigates unfamiliar systems where the correct solution is unknown at the beginning. \nWhich strategy should it use?","o":["Follow a completely fixed workflow","Use adaptive planning based on discoveries","Ignore intermediate findings","Always restart from the beginning"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c12","d":"general","x":"Your team's Claude agent has accumulated several customizations that bypass the SDK's defaults, including custom history management, retry logic, and error handling. \nA new team member has proposed reverting all the customizations to maintain the codebase more easily. \nThe tech lead disagrees and says each customization was added for a reason. \nHow would you advise the team?","o":["Migrate the agent off the SDK and rebuild it with a custom loop.","Decide on each customization individually based on its original reason and the SDK's current capabilities.","Keep all customizations, trusting that the tech lead's original reasoning is still valid.","Revert all customizations to the SDK's defaults to standardize the codebase."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c13","d":"general","x":"An engineer asks Claude to add a caching layer around a legacy authorization module. \nThe system has multiple tenants, audit logging, and inconsistent invalidation behavior. \nWhat should happen before modifying files?","o":["Provide performance goals and let Claude infer rules.","Ask targeted questions about invariants, invalidation, audit requirements, and failure modes.","Copy caching patterns from another module.","Implement a simple cache wrapper and iterate later."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c14","d":"general","x":"A developer creates a multi-agent workflow where one agent researches documentation and another writes implementation plans. \nHow should information flow between agents be handled?","o":["Remove the coordinator role","Assume all agents share the same conversation context","Pass required findings through the coordinator","Allow each agent to access every previous message"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c15","d":"general","x":"A structured extraction system divides documents into sections and sends them to extraction subagents. \nOne subagent temporarily loses access to its source system. \nThe current implementation either returns an empty successful extraction or aborts the entire document. \nWhat should you change?","o":["Have subagents report unresolved failures with attempted section, failure type, partial fields, and recovery suggestions.","Retry the failed section until it succeeds before returning anything.","Return an empty extraction object for the failed section.","Terminate the entire document workflow whenever any subagent fails."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c16","d":"general","x":"A multi-agent research system occasionally generates reports while the synthesis agent is still incomplete, resulting in missing or unverifiable citations. \nWhat change prevents invalid reports?","o":["Block report-generation tasks until synthesis returns a structured citation manifest and success status.","Tell the report agent to refuse incomplete synthesis.","Generate reports immediately and remove invalid citations afterward.","Check whether synthesis messages contain words like “complete.”"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c17","d":"general","x":"A migration assistant sometimes starts dependency analysis before collecting required package manager and framework versions. \nWhat change most reliably enforces the correct first step?","o":["Strengthen the system prompt requiring metadata extraction first.","Set tool_choice to any for the first request.","Detect whether Claude mentions metadata completion in natural language.","Force the metadata extraction tool with tool_choice for the first request."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c18","d":"general","x":"A team wants Claude-generated pull request reviews to integrate with existing development systems. \nWhat output approach should be selected?","o":["Unformatted comments","Free-form text responses","Structured JSON with validation rules","Long markdown explanations only"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c19","d":"general","x":"A new Claude model release includes performance improvements for several reasoning tasks but has changed the format of its responses to system prompts that use multi-section instructions. \nYour application uses multi-section system prompts heavily. \nInitial evaluation on the application's actual workload shows the new model performs 8 percent better on reasoning tasks but produces malformed output on roughly 3 percent of requests because of the format change. \nThe team is debating whether to upgrade. \nHow would you decide?","o":["Stay on the previous model permanently to avoid the malformed output rate and any future format changes that subsequent model releases might introduce in the application.","Adapt the application's system prompt to the new model's format expectations and re-evaluate, then upgrade only if the adapted prompt eliminates the malformed output while preserving the reasoning improvements.","Upgrade and add a downstream validation step that catches the 3 percent malformed output before it reaches users, treating the validation step as the team's mitigation for the format change.","Upgrade immediately, because the 8 percent reasoning improvement outweighs the 3 percent malformed output rate across the application's typical request distribution."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c20","d":"general","x":"A teammate is debugging a Claude application whose system prompt has grown to several hundred lines and now contains overlapping, contradictory, and obsolete instructions. \nHow would you advise the teammate?","o":["Split the prompt across multiple system prompts so the model sees a smaller portion at any given time.","Audit the prompt for overlap, contradiction, and obsolete content, then refactor so each instruction is clear, current, and non-redundant.","Add more explicit instructions so the most recent rules dominate the model's interpretation of the prompt during each request.","Tighten only the contradictory rules first, treating the overlap and obsolete content as lower-priority work the team can address later."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c21","d":"general","x":"A developer wants Claude to return API responses that can be directly consumed by another application. \nWhich technique should be used?","o":["Request formatted paragraphs","Use structured output with defined fieldscorrect","Allow unrestricted responses","Ask Claude for longer explanations"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c22","d":"general","x":"Multiple backend problems are returned to an agent as the same message: “Operation failed.” \nProblems include invalid order IDs, temporary service timeouts, expired tokens, and policy restrictions. \nWhat change most improves recovery behavior?","o":["Map failures to customer sentiment levels.","Keep generic failures and let Claude infer retryability.","Return structured MCP errors with categories, retryability, and user-safe details.","Retry every failed tool call twice before escalation."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c23","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nMulti-Agent Research System You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. \nA coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. \nYour coordinator's document-analysis subagent depends on an internal library-catalog MCP server. \nIt works in your local Claude Code session, but two teammates who cloned the repository cannot access the catalog tools, so their runs fall back to weaker web searches. \nSeparately, one researcher is testing an experimental notes server that should not affect the team. \nWhat should you change?","o":["Combine the catalog and notes integrations into one MCP server, and expose only role-specific tools to subagents.","Move the required catalog server into project-scoped .mcp.json, and keep the experimental notes server in user-scoped ~/.claude.json.","Document per-user claude mcp add commands in README, and ask each researcher to recreate both servers locally.","Place both servers in each user's ~/.claude.json, and rely on onboarding scripts to synchronize changes across machines."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c24","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nDeveloper Productivity with Claude A Claude-powered helper updates legacy configuration files to a new format. \nInitial generations look reasonable, but repeated runs keep breaking different corner cases: l Missing optional sections l Duplicate keys l Large files with thousands of entries Developers have been sending prompts like \"fix the migration bugs\" and receiving inconsistent improvements. \nWhat refinement approach should they use next?","o":["Share the failing test names, inputs, expected versus actual outputs, and stack traces, then iterate on minimal fixes.","Ask Claude to reread the entire legacy codebase and rewrite the migration helper from scratch after each failed run.","Tell Claude the output is still wrong, request a more careful implementation, and continue until reviewers approve it.","Remove the most difficult corner-case checks temporarily so Claude can stabilize the happy-path migration behavior first."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c25","d":"general","x":"Supplier contract extraction returns null values for effective dates and renewal terms even though information exists in cover letters, footnotes, and table captions. \nWhat change most improves accuracy?","o":["Retry until all metadata fields contain values.","Add targeted few-shot examples showing extraction from varied document sections.","Tell Claude null values are unacceptable for supplier contracts.","Make fields required and non-null."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c26","d":"general","x":"A legacy subscription service has cancellation behavior distributed across command-line scripts, web handlers, and scheduled jobs. \nPrevious direct edits often targeted the wrong abstraction. \nWhich workflow should be recommended first?","o":["Read every repository file before implementing changes.","Directly edit and rely on test failures to guide corrections.","Start separate direct sessions for each module and combine changes later.","Use plan mode to map relevant flows and compare implementation points before modifications."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c27","d":"general","x":"A team builds an application where Claude must classify customer requests into predefined categories. \nWhich approach provides the most consistent results?","o":["Remove category definitions from the prompt","Provide examples of each category and expected classifications","Ask Claude to create new categories when needed","Use a random classification approach."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c28","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nCustomer Support Resolution Agent You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. \nThe agent handles high- ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. \nIt has access to backend systems through MCP tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). \nYour target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. \nDuring billing disputes, the agent creates condensed case notes before responding or escalating. \nHuman reviewers report that notes sometimes say things like \"refund approved\" or \"customer was promised free return shipping,\" but they cannot tell whether each claim came from the customer, an order record, a policy document, or the agent's inference. In several escalations, customer allegations were treated as verified backend facts. \nWhat change would most effectively reduce this reliability risk?","o":["Require summaries and handoffs to carry structured claim-source mappings with source type, excerpt or tool field, timestamp, and verification status.","Compress each case into a shorter narrative summary and instruct reviewers to ask follow-up questions when provenance seems unclear.","Store complete raw transcripts and tool outputs only, relying on human reviewers to reconstruct sources when disputes arise.","Ask the agent to include only high-confidence claims in final responses, omitting uncertain details from summaries and escalations."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c29","d":"general","x":"A research system creates coherent reports, but coverage gaps remain. \nFor example, AI regulation reports have strong EU coverage but weak Asia-Pacific coverage. \nWhat change improves completeness?","o":["Repeat broad assignments twice.","Increase coordinator iteration count to a fixed number.","Add a coordinator review loop that checks coverage, delegates follow-ups, and reruns synthesis.","Hide unsupported sections."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c30","d":"general","x":"A developer wants Claude to review code and return only actionable issues. \nWhat prompt design is most effective?","o":["Ask for unlimited suggestions","Avoid describing expected output","Request every possible improvement","Define what qualifies as an issue and provide examples."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c31","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nCustomer Support Resolution Agent Production logs show: l get_customer returns ISO 8601 timestamps and numeric loyalty tiers. l lookup_order returns Unix timestamps and numeric status codes. \nThe agent sometimes miscomputes return windows or mislabels order status. \nYou cannot modify upstream MCP servers. \nWhat change best addresses the reliability issue?","o":["Modify the final customer response parser.","Add system instructions telling Claude to remember each tool's format","Escalate all cases with mixed timestamp formats.","Add a PostToolUse hook that converts tool results into canonical dates, status labels, and tier fields before Claude sees it."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c32","d":"general","x":"A multi-agent research system assigns high confidence to most report claims, but customer audits find citation mismatches concentrated in regulatory topics and newly added source types. \nWhat review strategy should be implemented before further reducing reviewer coverage?","o":["Randomly sample high-confidence claims across topic, source type, and report section, then track errors by category.","Use one aggregate accuracy metric and reduce review after reaching the target.","Review only claims with low confidence scores.","Auto-publish high-confidence reports and investigate only customer defects."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c33","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nStructured Data Extraction A labeled validation run reports: l 97.2% aggregate extraction accuracy l coverage across NDAs, invoices, insurance forms, and purchase orders Leadership wants to bypass human review for any record where Claude returns high confidence, but pilot complaints suggest errors cluster in specific document families and fields. \nWhat should you do before reducing reviewer coverage?","o":["Analyze accuracy by document type and extracted field, then automate only segments meeting validated thresholds while routing others to review.","Use the 97.2% aggregate accuracy to auto-approve all high-confidence outputs, monitoring user complaints for emerging regressions.","Randomly review a fixed percentage of every extraction category, regardless of document type, field, or prior validation performance.","Raise the global confidence threshold until the aggregate validation accuracy reaches 99%, then disable review for remaining outputs."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c34","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nStructured Data Extraction You are building a structured data extraction system using Claude. \nThe system extracts information from unstructured documents, validates output using JSON schemas, and maintains high accuracy. \nIt must handle edge cases gracefully and integrate with downstream systems. \nYour QA prompt reviews a 90-page vendor onboarding packet and its extracted records in one call. \nIt catches detailed field errors in the first few forms, but misses mismatched tax IDs in middle attachments and contradicts itself about required signatures. \nWhich review architecture would best improve reliability?","o":["Run three full-packet reviews and accept only issues reported by at least two reviewers.","Increase max_tokens and add stronger instructions to be exhaustive while reviewing the entire packet once.","Run focused review passes on each packet section, then run a separate consistency pass across cross- section fields.","Ask the same extraction instance to critique its own output using extended thinking before returning final records."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c35","d":"general","x":"A team wants to build a production billing pipeline's core logic entirely around a platform capability currently labeled Beta in Anthropic's feature-availability table. \nA tech lead raises a concern before approving the design. \nWhat is the most accurate basis for that concern?","o":["Beta features are billed at a 50% premium compared to generally available features, making them unsuitable for cost-sensitive billing pipelines.","Beta features are capped at 200k tokens of context regardless of the underlying model, which would limit the pipeline's document processing.","Beta features may change significantly or be discontinued based on feedback and are not guaranteed for ongoing production use, unlike GA features.","Beta features cannot be used together with prompt caching or batch processing, which would prevent the pipeline from scaling cost-effectively."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c36","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nStructured Data Extraction You are building a structured data extraction system using Claude. \nThe system extracts information from unstructured documents, validates output using JSON schemas, and maintains high accuracy. \nProduction review shows inconsistent routing: \ndocuments with unreadable required values and unfamiliar layouts sometimes flow into downstream systems, \nwhile routine invoices missing optional purchase-order numbers are sent for manual review. \nThe extraction schema already permits nullable fields for data that is absent from the source. \nWhat change would most effectively improve routing decisions?","o":["Escalate documents based on frustrated wording, urgent language, or negative sentiment detected in the source text.","Add explicit human-review criteria with few-shot examples distinguishing absent optional fields, unreadable required fields, and unsupported document types.","Route documents to reviewers whenever the model reports confidence below a single global threshold after extraction.","Require retries until all schema fields are populated, then escalate only documents still containing null values"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c37","d":"general","x":"A development team uses Claude Agent SDK for different workflows. \nDependency reviews follow fixed rules, while debugging unknown systems requires exploration. \nWhich decomposition strategy is best?","o":["Use dynamic planning for every workflow","Use fixed steps for every workflow","Use one large prompt for all tasks","Use prompt chains for stable tasks and adaptive planning for investigations"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c38","d":"general","x":"A development team uses Claude Agent SDK with multiple subagents to analyze a legacy payment system. \nHow should the coordinator improve reliability when passing discovery results to a planning subagent?","o":["Replace specialized subagents with one general-purpose subagent","Ask the planner to infer missing dependencies from file names","Allow subagents to communicate directly without coordination","Maintain shared state and include relevant findings in later prompts"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c39","d":"general","x":"Scenario: \nDeveloper Productivity with Claude You are building developer productivity tools using the Claude Agent SDK. \nThe agent helps engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, generate boilerplate code, and automate repetitive tasks. \nDependency license reviews follow a stable rubric with known checks, while production bug investigations in unfamiliar services vary based on discovered call paths, logs, and failing tests. \nThe current single-pass prompt misses checklist items during reviews and wastes effort during investigations. \nWhich decomposition strategy is most appropriate?","o":["Use the same fixed sequential checklist for both workflows, requiring every investigation to complete every predefined step.","Use one large prompt for both request types, asking the agent to reason carefully before using any tools.","Use a fixed prompt chain for stable review rubrics, and let the agent adapt subtasks during open-ended investigations.","Use dynamic task planning for every workflow, allowing the agent to redefine review criteria as it discovers code patterns."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c40","d":"general","x":"A CI pipeline needs Claude Code findings to create automated pull request comments. \nWhich approach provides the most reliable integration?","o":["Rewrite previous comments into structured records","Ask Claude to always return JSON in the prompt","Use JSON output with JSON schema validation","Parse markdown output with regular expressions"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c41","d":"general","x":"A customer support agent using Claude Agent SDK receives multiple possible customer profiles when users provide common names and partial addresses. \nThe agent sometimes selects a profile based on recent activity or name similarity, causing incorrect refunds and privacy disclosures. \nWhat change best improves reliability without unnecessarily routing routine cases to humans?","o":["Rank matching customers by recent activity and continue with the highest ranked profile when confidence exceeds a threshold.","Escalate every request with duplicate customer records to a human agent before performing any lookup_order calls.","Call lookup_order for each matching customer profile, then select the account whose order details best match the message.","Treat multiple customer matches as unresolved identity and request a disambiguating identifier before any order or refund operations."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c42","d":"general","x":"A research report contains two credible studies with different productivity statistics. \nThe synthesis agent chooses the larger number as the definitive result. \nWhat change improves reliability?","o":["Preserve both statistics with source attribution, methodology notes, and conflict annotation","Select the larger study and remove the smaller one.","Average the two statistics.","Search until another source confirms one statistic"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c43","d":"general","x":"A structured extraction QA system produces valid JSON, but reviewer findings are inconsistent and difficult to act on. \nWhat should improve consistency?","o":["Request only concise high-confidence responses","Add examples showing complete actionable findings","Require every schema field to be non-null","Use regular expressions to repair missing details"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c44","d":"general","x":"You are setting up a Claude application that requires API keys for several external services. \nWhat is the best way to store the keys?","o":["Use a single shared key across all external services, so any developer working on the application can find the keys easily during development.","Store the keys in a secrets manager or environment-specific configuration that is not checked into source code, and load them at runtime.","Put the keys in the application's configuration file and check the configuration file into the team's repository alongside the rest of the source code.","Email the keys to each developer as needed and have each developer paste the keys into their local environment when they begin working on the application's code."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c45","d":"general","x":"You are designing a Claude application that maintains user sessions across multi-turn conversations. \nThe product team has asked how the application will handle session lifecycle: when sessions should expire, how state is reset, and how the application avoids carrying stale context into new conversations. \nHow would you design session lifecycle?","o":["Define explicit session expiration rules, state reset triggers, and rules for starting fresh sessions so stale context does not leak into new conversations.","Define a single short session timeout that applies across all conversations and treat the timeout as the application's complete session lifecycle mechanism.","Define state reset triggers tied to specific application events but apply them across all sessions globally, with no per-session expiration rules.","Define explicit session expiration rules but rely on users to start new conversations when they want fresh context, with no automatic reset triggers in the application."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c46","d":"general","x":"You are building an MCP server that exposes several internal data sources as MCP resources. \nThe server needs to be deployed so multiple Claude applications can integrate with it. \nHow would you approach the build and deployment?","o":["Author the server with clearly defined resources, tools, and prompts, choose a communication pattern, and deploy to an accessible hosting environment.","Bypass the MCP server and embed each data source directly in every Claude application that needs the data, with each application maintaining its own integration.","Build the MCP server with resource and tool definitions scoped to the first Claude application that needs it, and extend the definitions to additional applications as each integration is requested.","Deploy the MCP server only on individual developer machines, with the Claude applications unable to reach the server outside each developer's machine."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c47","d":"general","x":"Your team is preparing a new Claude application for production, and the product team has asked for a cost projection. \nThe team needs to estimate the cost based on expected request volume, average input length, and average output length. \nHow would you build the projection?","o":["Build a cost model based on expected request volume and the chosen model's pricing, treating average input and output token counts as variables to be estimated post-launch.","Build a cost model that uses the average per-request cost from a similar Claude application the team built last year, scaling that figure by expected request volume.","Build a cost model that combines expected request volume, average input tokens, average output tokens, the chosen model's pricing, and any caching benefits.","Build a cost model that combines expected request volume and average input tokens, treating output tokens as a small enough share of cost to leave out of the projection."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c48","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application uses structured output that is consumed by downstream code. \nThe team wants to handle malformed or unexpected output gracefully so it does not crash downstream systems. \nThe best choice for handling this issue would be to...","o":["Apply defensive parsing to handle unexpected fields, missing values, and type mismatches that the downstream code might otherwise crash on.","Use the output the model produces and leave schema enforcement to a later phase of production after the application is more complete.","Silently drop any output that does not match expectations so downstream systems do not see the malformed output the application receives from the model.","Crash on any unexpected output and require manual recovery on affected requests the application handles during normal operation."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c49","d":"general","x":"Your team uses several plugins across multiple Claude applications, and a recent plugin update introduced a regression. \nThe team had not been tracking plugin versions, so the team cannot easily identify which version was previously working. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Treat plugins as untrackable third-party code and rely on plugin authors to communicate breaking changes when they happen, with no internal version tracking.","Add explicit plugin version tracking to the project's configuration so the team can identify, pin, and upgrade plugin versions deliberately.","Stop using all plugins until the team can rebuild equivalent functionality directly into the application code, treating plugin avoidance as a way to remove version-related risk.","Upgrade every plugin to the latest version on a regular cadence to keep version drift small, on the grounds that drift contributes to regression risk."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c50","d":"general","x":"You are deciding between deploying a Claude-powered agent on Anthropic's hosted infrastructure or self-hosting under a \"bring your own cloud\" model in your own AWS account. \nThe agent processes customer data subject to your enterprise's data residency policies, but the team wants to ship quickly and avoid managing infrastructure. \nWhich deployment model would you recommend?","o":["Self-hosting under BYOC for an initial pilot, then evaluating whether to migrate to Anthropic-hosted infrastructure once the agent's data-handling patterns are better understood.","Deploying on Anthropic-hosted infrastructure while the team drafts a request to update the enterprise data residency policy to accommodate hosted Al deployments.","Deploying on Anthropic-hosted infrastructure to meet the team's shipping timeline, and flagging the data residency requirement for a follow-up compliance review after launch.","Self-hosting under BYOC to satisfy the data residency requirement, while working with the infrastructure team to reduce the operational overhead of managing the deployment."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c51","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application uses tool calling to fetch patient data and generate summary reports. \nThe flow occasionally fails because the model returns a tool_use block that references arguments not present in the schema, and your application code does not handle this case gracefully. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Validate the tool_use block's arguments against the tool schema before dispatching the tool and handle invalid arguments as a recognized error path.","Stop using tool calling entirely and replace tools with prompted text generation that asks the model to describe what it would do.","Log invalid tool_use blocks when they occur and allow the tool dispatch to proceed, relying on the tool's own error handling to surface failures back to the application.","Retry the same request repeatedly until the model returns a valid tool_use block that matches the schema as expected."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c52","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's prompt was written months ago and has not been updated. \nThe team has discovered through evals that the prompt produces good results on common cases but underperforms on a specific category of inputs that has grown in volume. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Tell users to avoid the underperforming category by adding warnings in the application's user interface about handled inputs.","Iterate on the prompt to address the underperforming category, validate the change with evals, and continue refining as needed.","Replace the prompt with a new one aligned to the underperforming category, treating any common-case performance change as a known tradeoff.","Add the underperforming category to a separate Claude application with its own prompt so the original prompt does not change."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c53","d":"general","x":"A teammate is reviewing the team's threat model for a Claude application and has asked you to identify the categories of Al-specific threats that the model should cover. \nThe teammate has already listed traditional web application threats and wants to know what additional categories apply to a Claude application. \nWhich Al-specific threat categories would you add?","o":["Prompt injection, data leakage from prompts or context, jailbreak attempts, and unsafe model output that bypasses application controls.","Network-level denial of service and physical infrastructure attacks, because these categories cover the threats most likely to affect any Claude application in production.","Supply chain attacks on the Claude SDK because the SDK itself is the only point of vulnerability that a Claude application introduces beyond traditional web application threats.","Cross-site scripting and SQL injection, because these traditional web application threats apply with equal weight to any application that uses Claude in any way."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c54","d":"general","x":"The Claude application your team built has grown over six months, and the prompt-handling code has accumulated duplication and tangled control flow. \nThe functionality is working, but new features are getting harder to add. How would you address this?","o":["Move all the prompt-handling code into a single large function to reduce the number of files developers have to navigate when reading the code.","Refactor the prompt-handling code in small increments as part of each new feature ticket, treating the cleanup as a side effect of feature work.","Continue adding features and plan a refactoring pass after the next two release cycles when the team has more bandwidth for internal work.","Plan a refactoring pass to consolidate duplicated logic, separate concerns, and simplify control flow before adding new features."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c55","d":"general","x":"You are building a Claude application that needs to deliver model output to end users as it is generated, instead of waiting for the full response to complete. \nThe Claude API mechanism you would use is...","o":["The Batch APl, which delivers full responses after a delay suitable for non-interactive workloads.","Prompt caching, which speeds up the cost profile of future requests and does not affect the delivery timing of the first response.","Streaming responses, which deliver tokens incrementally as the model generates them so users see output progressively.","Structured JSON output, which delivers responses only after the model has finalized the JSON shape across the entire response."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c56","d":"general","x":"The Anthropic API deprecated a request parameter that your Claude application uses in approximately 40 laces across the codebase. \nThe deprecation notice gives a six-month window before the parameter is removed and recommends a replacement parameter with slightly different semantics. \nYou would respond to the deprecation by...","o":["Adding regression tests to cover the parameter's behavior, then migrating call sites in batches that you validate against regression tests.","Swapping all 40 call sites in a single change right away to prevent drawn-out migration work that will delay ongoing functioning.","Keeping the deprecated parameter in place while writing a wrapper function around it to insulate the rest of the codebase from the eventual change.","Migrating all 40 call sites in a single change near the removal date to ensure the deprecated parameter continues to work as long as possible."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c57","d":"general","x":"Your application uses the Messages API to handle multi-turn conversations. \nEach new turn resends the entire conversation history, and your token costs are growing as conversations get longer. \nYou suspect there is a more efficient approach. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Truncate every conversation to the last two messages so that token usage stays low and costs remain predictable across the application's normal operation.","Use prompt caching to reuse the static portions of the conversation context across turns instead of paying for them at the normal input-token rate on every request the application sends.","Summarize each conversation after every turn and replace the full history with the summary on the next request, reducing token count at the cost of fidelity.","Switch to the Batch API for every turn so the per-call cost is reduced, treating the batch as the team's primary cost-control mechanism for multi-turn work."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c58","d":"general","x":"You are configuring Claude Code for a new project. \nThe team needs to set permissions, default model selections, and environment-specific behavior at the project level so the configuration is consistent across all developers working on the repository. \nThe Claude Code mechanism you would use is...","o":["Environment variables that each developer sets on their own machine when working with Claude Code on the project.","A system prompt embedded in every Claude Code conversation by each developer at the start of every session in the project.","The settings.json file, scoped at the project level so the configuration applies consistently across developers and persists with the repository.","A shared spreadsheet that lists configuration values for team members to reference and update by hand as the project evolves."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c59","d":"general","x":"Your team is preparing to roll out a configuration change that updates several prompt versions across a Claude application used by multiple downstream systems. \nThe change has already been tested in staging, but the team has not assessed how the prompt change will affect each downstream system that depends on the application's output. \nWhat would you do before rolling out the change?","o":["Document the prompt version changes in the application changelog and proceed with the rollout, treating the staging test results as sufficient evidence of impact across all downstream systems.","Assess the configuration impact on each downstream system before rolling out, and coordinate with downstream system owners as needed.","Notify downstream system owners that a change is coming and schedule the rollout for the following week, without conducting a formal impact assessment.","Limit the rollout to systems that were explicitly included in staging testing, and defer all other downstream systems until a later release cycle."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c60","d":"general","x":"A teammate has submitted a pull request that adds a Claude-powered feature to your service. \nThe code works, but the prompt and model selection are hard-coded inline, error handling is missing, and there are no tests for the integration. \nWhat would you request during code review?","o":["Approve the pull request and add the missing pieces yourself in a follow-up commit so the teammate can move on to other work immediately.","Request changes that move prompt and model configuration to a configurable location, add error handling for Claude API failures, and add tests for the integration.","Approve the pull request as-is, on the grounds that the feature works in the happy path and the missing pieces can be added in follow-up commits.","Request changes that move prompt and model configuration to a configurable location and add tests, treating the missing error handling as a follow-up release item."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c61","d":"general","x":"You are setting up Claude Code for a new project repository. \nYour team has shared coding standards, preferred libraries, and project-specific context that every developer working on the repository should have available when they use Claude Code. \nHow would you set this up?","o":["Document the standards in a separate wiki page maintained outside the repository so the documentation stays decoupled from the source code.","Initialize Claude Code in the repository and document the standards and project-specific context in a CLAUDE.mdfile at the repository root.","Add the coding standards and project context to the repository's existing README file and direct developers to reference it when starting Claude Code sessions.","Configure Claude Code through environment variables that each developer sets on their own machine when they begin working in the repository."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c62","d":"general","x":"You are reviewing an architectural diagram for a Claude-powered travel-booking system. \nThe diagram shows a top-level component that interprets user requests and three subordinate components that handle flights, hotels, and ground transportation. \nThe top-level component is responsible for routing each request, sequencing the subordinate components, and reconciling their outputs into a final itinerary. \nThe diagram also shows that each subordinate component has its own tool list and own short conversation history that is not shared with the others. \nWhich architectural pattern does this diagram most closely describe?","o":["A pipeline pattern, where each component processes the user request in sequence and passes the full conversation history along with the request to the next component in the pipeline.","A retrieval-augmented pattern, where the top-level component retrieves relevant context from the subordinate components' indexed data stores before generating each response.","A manager and supervisor pattern with shared context, where the top-level agent and the subagents all share a single conversation history that grows as the request flows through the system.","A manager and supervisor pattern with isolated context per subagent, where the top-level agent coordinates specialized subagents that each maintain their own conversation history and tool list."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c63","d":"general","x":"You are starting a new Claude application and have a small set of well-labeled examples that demonstrate the desired output format. \nYou want to use these examples to guide Claude's behavior. \nHow would you guide the application's behavior?","o":["Use zero-shot prompting and rely on the model's general capability to produce the desired output format consistently across all incoming requests.","Use multi-shot prompting by including the labeled examples in the prompt so Claude can match the desired output format on each request.","Train a custom model on the labeled examples before deployment so that the application does not need to include the examples in any prompt during operation.","Embed the examples in a database and retrieve them at runtime as reference material for the team."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c64","d":"general","x":"You are setting up the configuration management approach for a new Claude Code project. \nYour team will use CLAUDE.md files and settings.json files to control behavior, and you want to make sure changes are tracked and reviewable. \nThe configuration management approach would...","o":["Duplicate CLAUDE.md and settings.json files in multiple repositories to provide redundancy, on the grounds that a single source of truth is risky for project configuration.","Version-control CLAUDE.md and settings.json files in a separate repository from the project's source code, so configuration evolves independently from the application code over time.","Version-control CLAUDE.md and settings.json files alongside the project's source code, with changes reviewed through standard pull request workflows the team applies.","Version-control CLAUDE.md alongside the project's source code and settings. json files in a separate repository from the project's source code."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c65","d":"general","x":"Your agent is processing tasks that take 30 to 60 minutes to complete. \nEach task has well-defined intermediate checkpoints, and the team wants the agent to be able to resume from the most recent checkpoint if a process is interrupted. \nHow would you implement this resumability?","o":["Run two copies of the agent in parallel for every task and use whichever one finishes first as the source of truth.","Apply a checkpointing pattern that persists the agent's intermediate state and reloads that state when resuming an interrupted task.","Increase the agent's timeout to several hours so that interruptions become rare enough to ignore in practice.","Restart the task from the beginning whenever a process is interrupted."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c66","d":"general","x":"A team has deployed a multi-agent system in which a primary agent decomposes user requests and delegates subtasks to three specialized subagents: one for data retrieval, one for analysis, and one for report generation. \nIn production, the team observes that subagents are making redundant tool calls, occasionally exceeding token budgets, and sometimes producing outputs that contradict each other - all of which the primary agent passes along without catching. \nWhat is the most appropriate way to address these failures?","o":["Add retry logic to each subagent so that when a tool call fails, the subagent retries automatically before escalating - and configure each subagent to log its tool calls and outputs to a shared trace so the team can audit redundancy and contradictions after the fact.","Collapse the three subagents into a single large-context model call that handles retrieval, analysis, and generation in one pass.","Strengthen the primary agent's management layer to enforce per-subagent tool budgets, validate outputs against a defined schema before passing them forward, and establish explicit handoff contracts between stages.","Give each subagent read access to the other subagents' outputs so they can identify and resolve contradictions without routing back through the primary agent."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c67","d":"general","x":"Your agent makes 10 to 15 tool calls per task, and you have noticed it sometimes loses track of earlier results by the time it reaches later steps. \nThe agent's context window is large enough to hold all the messages, but the relevant information appears to get buried as the conversation grows. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Switch to a different agentic framework that advertises automatic context-window management as a built-in feature.","Reduce the number of tool calls per task by combining several existing tools into larger, multi-purpose tools.","Increase the context window further so all tool outputs from every prior step remain in full detail throughout the task.","Apply a context-management pattern that summarizes or prunes older tool outputs while preserving the active task state."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c68","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked why your Claude application sometimes produces a response that includes the prompt text repeated back, and other times produces a response with the prompt text rephrased. \nThey suspect a bug in the application's request construction. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Tell the teammate that the variation depends on which Claude model serves the request, and recommend pinning the application to a single model version to make the output behavior consistent.","Tell the teammate that the variation is caused by the application sending two different prompts on different runs and propose a code change that pins the system prompt to a single version.","Explain that LLMs generate output token by token, and variation in how prompt content appears in output is a property of generation, not a bug in request construction.","Confirm that the variation is a bug in the application's request construction and start investigating which part of the application is producing the inconsistent prompt text."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c69","d":"general","x":"You are designing a multi-step Claude workflow where some steps must reason without seeing the full prior conversation history. \nThe team wants to keep specific context isolated to specific steps. \nThe context engineering technique you would use is...","o":["Context isolation through subagents or multi-step agentic workflows that scope each step's context to only what the step needs.","Using a single global prompt that applies to every step in the workflow no matter what each step is reasoning about during its run.","Augmenting the context with all available content at every step so each step has access to the entire prior history of the workflow during its reasoning.","Embedding the full prior history in each step regardless of whether the step needs the prior history for its reasoning."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c70","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked you to explain why your Claude agent's tools include detailed descriptions in the tool definition, even when the tool name is already descriptive. \nThe teammate suggests removing the descriptions to simplify the tool definitions. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Explain that the model uses the tool description to decide when to call the tool, and descriptions disambiguate cases where the tool name is not enough.","Agree with the teammate because tool names are sufficient for the model to choose the right tool on every request the agent handles.","Suggest moving the descriptions out of the tool definition and into a separate documentation file the teamnaintains so the tool definitions stay short and the descriptions remain available.","Suggest replacing the descriptions with example calls embedded in the tool definition, treating example calls as a complete substitute for the prose description."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c71","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's error handling currently logs every API error with the same severity level. \nThe team wants to differentiate between errors that should page an on-call engineer and errors that should be logged for later review. \nHow would you structure the error handling?","o":["Log every error with the same severity, on the grounds that differentiating severity adds complexity that does it pay off in most application setups over time.","Categorize errors by severity based on impact and recoverability, then route each category to the appropriate channel for paging or logging.","Disable logging for any error that does not page, treating non-paging errors as not worth recording for later review either.","Page on every error, on the grounds that paging guarantees that no error is missed by the team during normal operation across the application's lifecycle."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c72","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application makes high-volume API calls during business hours and very few calls overnight. \nThe team is concerned about staying within rate limits during peak hours and wants to understand how the Claude API enforces those limits. \nHow would you proceed?","o":["Examine the peak-hour request patterns in your application logs and smooth traffic by distributing requests more evenly across the business-hours window.","Assess the average payload size of current API calls and consolidate requests where possible to reduce the total number of calls made during peak hours.","Review the API documentation for streaming endpoints and evaluate whether migrating peak-hour calls to streaming reduces exposure to rate limit enforcement.","Identify the rate limits, design the application to stay within them during peak hours, and use exponential backoff when limits are reached."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c73","d":"general","x":"You are extending a Claude agent with a capability that needs to be reusable across multiple teams in the organization, with each team able to invoke and use it independently. \nHow would you build the capability?","o":["As a wrapper around an existing built-in tool that adds the missing functionality, on the grounds that built-in tools cover the reuse pattern when extended carefully.","As a custom tool embedded in this team's agent only, with other teams able to copy the implementation into their own agents when they need the capability.","As a shared library that each team imports into its own Claude application code, with each team responsible or keeping the library up to date in its integration.","As a Skill or MCP server because both are purpose-built for cross-team reuse independently by each consuming team."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c74","d":"general","x":"You are choosing between using STDIO-based communication and HTTP-based communication for an ACP server. \nThe server will be invoked by a Claude Code session running locally. \nWhich communication pattern would you use?","o":["STDIO and HTTP simultaneously for redundancy, with the team running two communication patterns at the same time across Claude Code sessions.","STDIO because STDIO is well-suited to local process communication and avoids unnecessary network setup.","HTTP polling against the MCP server, treating HTTP as a substitute for the standard MCP communication patterns.","HTTP on the grounds that HTTP is preferable to STDIO in any context the application might encounter during normal operation."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c75","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's API keys are stored in a secrets manager. \nThe team is debating whether the same key should be used in development, staging, and production environments. \nHow would you handle the keys across environments?","o":["Use the same key across all environments for simplicity and treat the propagation of any compromise as a known operational tradeoff for the team's key management approach.","Rotate the same key across environments at random intervals on the grounds that random rotation provides isolation between environments without requiring distinct keys.","Use a single development key everywhere on the grounds that production keys are too risky to deploy across the application's three environments during normal operation.","Use distinct keys for each environment so a compromise in one environment does not affect the others during normal operation across the application's lifecycle."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c76","d":"general","x":"You are explaining to a stakeholder why running the same Claude prompt twice can produce slightly different results. \nThe stakeholder is concerned this means the application is broken. \nHow would you address the stakeholder's concern?","o":["Tell the stakeholder the variation is a bug that the team will fix in the next release of the application, then create a work ticket to fix the bug.","Explain that LLMs are non-deterministic by default due to sampling, and describe how the application handles this through validation, retries, or temperature adjustment.","Tell the stakeholder the variation is caused by Claude being updated continuously by Anthropic, and that switching to a fixed model snapshot will eliminate the variation entirely","Tell the stakeholder the variation comes from network latency and that switching to a faster network connection will produce more consistent results across runs."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c77","d":"general","x":"The product team has asked you to choose a Claude model for a new feature. \nThe team has provided functional requirements but has not specified performance, cost, or quality targets. \nThe team's product manager says, \"Use whatever model gives us the best results.\" \nHow would you respond?","o":["Choose the largest, highest-capability Claude model, on the grounds that \"best results\" is most likely to mean highest quality.","Ask the product team to specify quality, latency, and cost targets, then select the model whose tradeoffs best fit those targets.","Choose a mid-tier model and ship the feature, because mid-tier models work for most use cases without specified targets.","Run every Claude model on a representative sample and pick whichever scores best on a generic benchmark."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c78","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application is deployed to development, staging, and production environments. \nEach environment uses a different model version, different prompt versions, and different plugin dependencies, but the configuration is currently scattered across environment variables, hardcoded values, and undocumented setup scripts. \nHow would you manage the configuration?","o":["Use the latest available model version everywhere and stop pinning model versions, on the grounds that pinning adds maintenance overhead the team should aim to reduce.","Consolidate the configuration into a version-controlled system documenting model version pinning, prompt versioning, and plugin dependencies for each environment.","Standardize all environments to use the same configuration values to eliminate the differences betweendevelopment, staging, and production.","Move all configuration into hardcoded application code to reduce reliance on external configuration sources that are difficult to track over time."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c79","d":"general","x":"You are designing a Claude application that will require structured JSON output for downstream processing. \nThe output schema is well-defined, and downstream systems will reject malformed JSON. \nYour application design would ...","o":["define a clear schema and structure the prompt to request output in that schema, with downstream systems handling any validation needed.","structure the prompt to request output in a schema that is described in plain English, with downstream systems parsing whatever shape Claude produces.","avoid structured output and use free-form text everywhere instead, on the grounds that free-form text is more flexible and handles edge cases better than structured schemas.","define a clear schema, structure the prompt to request output in that schema, and validate Claude's output against the schema before passing it downstream."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c80","d":"general","x":"You are designing a Claude application that will process customer support tickets in two stages: a triage stage that classifies tickets and a response stage that drafts replies. \nThe team is debating whether to use a single Claude call that handles both stages or separate Claude calls for each stage. \nHow would you structure the application?","o":["Use a single Claude call for triage and then use a non-Claude rule-based system for response generation, on the grounds that rule-based systems are more reliable for drafting replies.","Use a single Claude call for both stages, on the grounds that a single call is cheaper than multiple calls in any production Claude application setup.","Use multiple Claude calls in parallel that each draft a complete ticket reply, then have a fourth Claude call select the best one to send to the customer.","Use separate Claude calls for triage and response, because each stage has distinct inputs, outputs, and success criteria that benefit from focused prompts."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c81","d":"general","x":"A new agent your team built handles customer support tickets, but it routinely gets confused when a single ticket spans billing, shipping, and product issues. \nThe agent often loses track of which sub-issue it has already addressed and revisits the same one. \nThe team is considering architectural changes. \nWhat architectural change would you recommend?","o":["Introduce an orchestrator agent that delegates billing, shipping, and product sub-issues to dedicated subagents.","Increase the size of the agent's context window so it can hold the full ticket history at once.","Switch to a deterministic workflow that handles billing, shipping, and product issues in a fixed sequence.","Add detailed prompting that instructs the agent to track which sub-issues have been resolved and which remain."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c82","d":"general","x":"Your Claude agent's hooks are currently triggered for every action, which slows down the agent significantly ven when actions pose no risk. \nThe team wants to scope hooks more carefully. \nHow would you scope the hooks?","o":["Disable the agent during peak hours so the hook overhead does not slow the application down during the busiest periods of the day across the application's operation.","Scope hooks to only the high-risk actions, such as destructive operations or sensitive data access, and remove hooks from low-risk actions to balance safety with performance.","Disable all hooks while the team re-scopes them, treating the period of no hook enforcement as a temporary state during the re-scoping work.","Replace hooks with system prompt instructions on the grounds that prompt instructions can produce the same enforcement effect that hooks produce on the agent's actions."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c83","d":"general","x":"You are designing a Claude application that processes user-submitted text. \nSome of that text could include sensitive information such as account numbers or passwords that the application should not send to Claude. \nHow would you design the application?","o":["Apply filtering at the boundary for the most commonly observed sensitive data patterns and expand coverage to additional patterns based on findings from production monitoring.","Define the application boundary explicitly, identify what content can leave the boundary for Claude, and add filtering or redaction at the boundary.","Log all user-submitted text before it is sent to Claude and review the logs periodically to identify whether sensitive information is reaching the model.","Add a prompt instruction in the system prompt specifying the categories of sensitive information Claude should disregard when processing user-submitted text."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c84","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application runs long agentic workflows where the agent makes many tool calls, and the conversation history grows quickly. \nAfter about 20 tool calls, you notice the agent's responses become less focused and sometimes ignore earlier task constraints. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Remove tool calling from the workflow entirely so the agent operates as a single text-generation step with no tool outputs accumulating in the context window.","Restart the agent every five tool calls to prevent any drift, with the agent losing all task state at each restart point during the workflow.","Apply context engineering techniques such as tool output pruning or compaction to keep the active task state visible while reducing the volume of older content.","Increase the model's context window so the agent can hold every tool output at full detail across the entire workflow no matter how many tool calls it accumulates."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c85","d":"general","x":"You are designing an agent that handles a complex claim-processing workflow. \nEach claim moves through fact extraction, eligibility evaluation, and a decision step. \nThe three subtasks have distinct success criteria, and some claims require iteration between fact extraction and eligibility evaluation before a decision can be reached. \nWhich agent pattern would you apply?","o":["A linear chain pattern that processes every claim through fact extraction, then eligibility evaluation, then decision, with no return paths between subtasks.","A graph-based pattern that lets the agent move between subtasks based on the state of each claim, with each subtask evaluated against its own criteria.","A streaming pattern that emits partial decisions as the agent processes each claim, refining the output until a final decision emerges from the stream.","A single tool-use loop pattern that gives one agent access to all the tools needed for fact extraction, eligibility evaluation, and decision-making."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c86","d":"general","x":"Your enterprise has a contract with AWS that requires Claude API calls to flow through Amazon Bedrock rather lan the direct Anthropic API. \nYour team is building a new Claude application and is unfamiliar with this constraint. \nHow would you build the application?","o":["Build the application against the direct Anthropic API and ignore the contractual requirement to route Claude calls through Amazon Bedrock.","Build two parallel implementations of every call, one for the direct Anthropic API and one for Bedrock, and pick the faster one at runtime.","Build the application against the direct Anthropic API now and migrate to Bedrock in a follow-up release once the team has more experience with the Bedrock API.","Configure the application to invoke Claude through the Bedrock-compatible API path while keeping the application's logic provider-agnostic."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c87","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application has been running for several conversation turns, and you notice the model occasionally references information that was discussed many turns ago but is no longer relevant. \nYou suspect context drift is causing the model to weight stale content too heavily. \nHow would you address the drift?","o":["Increase the context window size so all turns of the conversation remain visible to the model in full detail.","Truncate the conversation so the model sees only the most recent turn during each subsequent response.","Apply compaction to summarize older portions of the conversation so the gist remains while the specifics carry less weight.","Reset the conversation after every turn so the model loses all prior turns when generating a response."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c88","d":"general","x":"You are building a Claude application that needs to maintain a persistent connection to a service that streams real- time updates. \nThe team is unsure what communication pattern to use.\nWhich communication pattern would you use?","o":["A WebSocket, because WebSockets are designed for bidirectional, persistent, real-time communication between the client and the streaming service.","A single long HTTP request the server holds open indefinitely, with no standard WebSocket framing on the connection.","Repeated short-lived HTTP polling requests, where the application opens a new HTTP connection each time it checks for updates.","File-based communication where the service writes new updates to disk and the application polls the file system for changes."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c89","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked how the Claude SDK handles transient API errors, such as a temporary network issue or a brief rate-limit response. \nThey want to know whether the application code needs to handle every transient error or whether the SDK provides any default behavior. \nHow would you describe the SDK's default behavior?","o":["The SDK provides default retry behavior for network errors but surfaces rate-limit responses directly to the application code, which must implement its own retry logic for those cases.","The SDK provides default retry behavior for transient errors up to a fixed number of attempts, and this behavior is not configurable.","The SDK logs transient errors to a default error stream and continues execution without retrying, leaving the application code responsible for detecting and responding to failed calls.","The SDK provides default retry behavior for many transient errors, and the application code can configure or extend that behavior as needed."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c90","d":"general","x":"Your team is debating how to manage the prompts used in your Claude application. \nSome prompts are checked into the code repository, some live in a separate configuration file, and some are constructed inline at runtime. \nThe result is inconsistent, and a recent prompt change went out without code review. \nWhat steps would you take?","o":["Move all prompts to inline runtime construction so the team can update them quickly through a streamlined process outside the standard code review workflow.","Establish a single source of truth for prompts, version-control them alongside code, and require code review for prompt changes.","Establish a single source of truth for prompts but keep change review optional, allowing developers to update prompts directly when changes are urgent.","Move all prompts out of version control to a separate spreadsheet that team members can edit freely as the application evolves over time."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c91","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application is hitting context window limits when processing long customer service transcripts. \nA junior developer suggests increasing the temperature parameter to fix the issue. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Increase the temperature parameter as the junior developer suggested and observe whether the context window issue resolves over the next several runs of the application in production.","Remove the system prompt entirely to make room for longer transcripts in each request, freeing up context window space the system prompt would otherwise consume.","Adjust the temperature parameter together with the max_tokens parameter, treating the combined adjustment as the team's mechanism for managing context window pressure during long-transcript processing.","Explain that temperature controls sampling randomness and is unrelated to context capacity, then address the context issue through summarization or chunking."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c92","d":"general","x":"You are integrating Claude into an application written in Python. \nThe Claude SDK provides a Python client that wraps the underlying REST API. \nHow would you integrate the SDK?","o":["Call the REST API directly with raw HTTP requests so the application avoids the SDK's abstraction between the application code and the API.","Skip the SDK and embed Claude calls in shell commands invoked from Python, so that the application runs the calls outside the main Python process.","Use a different LLM provider's SDK and translate the responses into Claude's API shape so the application can switch providers in the future.","Use the Claude Python SDK and let it handle authentication, retries, and response parsing through its standard documented patterns for Python integrations."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c93","d":"general","x":"Your Claude agent has access to a tool that retrieves customer records. \nA teammate has noticed that the agent occasionally calls the tool with arguments the schema does not declare, and the tool's downstream service returns an error each time. \nThe teammate proposes loosening the schema so the tool accepts whatever arguments the model produces. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Keep the schema strict, validate arguments before dispatching, and return a structured error so the agent can retry.","Add a system prompt instruction telling the model to produce schema-conforming arguments, treating the prompt instruction as the primary mechanism for keeping the agent's tool calls valid.","Remove the schema entirely and rely on the downstream service to reject invalid calls, treating the downstream service as the team's primary enforcement layer.","Loosen the schema as the teammate proposed so the downstream service receives every call the agent makes during normal operation."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c94","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application is producing inconsistent outputs for similar inputs, even when using the same model and prompt. \nYou want to debug the issue systematically. \nYour debugging approach would...","o":["Lower the model's temperature and re-run the inconsistent inputs to determine whether the parameter change reduces output variability across runs.","Assume inconsistent outputs reflect normal LLM non-determinism and document the variability as an accepted characteristic of the application's behavior.","Capture full traces of input, system prompt, user messages, model output, and parameters, then analyze the differences between consistent and inconsistent runs.","Retry every request that produces an unexpected output and log the results to identify whether repeated calls converge on a consistent response pattern."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c95","d":"general","x":"You are building an agent that needs to call several internal APIs and a database in a structured, repeatable way. \nYour team has decided to use the Claude Agent SDK rather than build a custom loop. \nYou are setting up the agent's tool definitions and execution loop. \nHow would you set up the tools and execution loop?","o":["Call the Messages API directly and let the model format its tool calls in plain text.","Use the SDK's tool interface and loop, with conversation history stored in a separate team database.","Use the SDK's tool interface and let the SDK handle the loop, dispatch, and history.","Use the SDK's tool interface and write the loop and history layer in the team's own code."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c96","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's token costs have grown faster than expected. \nThe team has not been tracking token usage by feature, so the team cannot identify which features are driving cost. \nThe team is debating how to respona. \nHow would you respond?","o":["Reduce token usage uniformly across all features by half, applying the cut evenly across the application during normal operation.","Tell the team that cost growth is unavoidable as the application scales and that no investigation will change the trajectory of the application's cost over the next several quarters.","Add token usage tracking by feature to the application's logging so the team can identify which features drive cost before recommending changes.","Switch every feature to the smallest model to cut cost broadly across the application during normal operation."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c97","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application produces good responses for typical inputs but struggles with edge cases. \nYou have several labeled examples of edge-case inputs and the desired response for each. \nYou want to use these examples to improve the model's handling of edge cases. \nWhat is the best way to use these examples?","o":["Add the labeled edge-case examples to the prompt as few-shot examples so the model can learn the pattern.","Embed the examples in a database for the model to find during inference.","Train a custom model on the edge-case examples and deploy that custom model in place of the team's currentClaude integration.","Tell users to avoid submitting the edge-case inputs to the application by adding warnings in the application's user interface."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c98","d":"general","x":"You have just shipped a new Claude-powered application to production. \nThe development phase is complete, and the system is now in active use by internal teams. \nThe next phase of work for this system is...","o":["Handing the system over to a separate operations team that will manage it independently of the development team going forward.","Operating and maintaining the system, including monitoring, responding to issues, and planning evolution.","Retiring the system, since shipping is the final lifecycle stage for any application that reaches production.","Running a formal post-deployment review that assesses the development phase before the team does any further work on the system."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c99","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked how to extend Claude Code with a custom Skill that the team can invoke during sessions. \nThe Skill consists of a set of instructions and a few support scripts the team wants Claude to be able to call when the Skill is loaded. \nWhere is the right place to define the Skill?","o":["Define the Skill in a personal scratch directory on each developer's machine and load it manually before eachClaude Code session that needs it.","Define the Skill as a long inline instruction at the top of every CLAUDE.md file in the team's repositories soClaude has access to it on every session.","Define the Skill in a Skills directory recognized by Claude Code, where Claude can discover and load it during sessions for the team's repositories.","Define the Skill inside the application's source code as a regular library module and call it from the application code instead of from Claude Code."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c100","d":"general","x":"A Claude application is occasionally refusing to answer questions that should be in scope, including questions the application has answered correctly in the past. \nYou want to investigate. \nWhat is the first step of your investigation?","o":["Assess whether the refusals cluster around specific input patterns, question types, or time periods by analyzing the distribution of refused requests in the logs.","Identify which questions have been refused and compare them against previously answered questions to determine what changed between the successful and refused interactions.","Examine traces of the refused requests to identify what triggers the refusal, whether input patterns, system prompt content, or other context.","Test whether removing or adjusting those instructions resolves the behavior."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c101","d":"general","x":"The product team has described a new Claude feature in business terms: \"agents should help our analysts produce client memos faster.\" \nYou need to convert this into actionable technical requirements for the engineering team. \nYour first step would be to...","o":["Interpret the functional and infrastructure requirements implied by the business goal.","Assess what similar agent-based features have been built internally or in the industry and use those precedents to scope the technical approach.","Examine what model capabilities and tier options are available and determine which best supports the memo drafting workflow described by the product team.","Ask the analysts about the current memo production process to see where they think Claude could be introduced as a prompt-driven drafting step."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c102","d":"general","x":"You are designing an agent that handles a multi-step research task. \nYou want the agent to break the task into smaller pieces, hand each piece to a focused subagent, and consolidate the results. \nThe agent pattern you would apply is...","o":["An orchestrator and subagent pattern with specialized subagents assigned to each subtask.","A single tool-use loop that includes every tool the agent might need across all subtasks.","A memory pattern that stores the entire research history in advance, before any subtask begins execution.","A context-window pruning pattern that drops each subtask's content after the agent moves on."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c103","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application receives untrusted input from external sources. \nThe team is establishing how the application should treat this untrusted input. \nUntrusted input would be...","o":["Routed through a separate Claude application with more lax security controls so the original application does not handle the untrusted input directly.","Validated and sanitized before being incorporated into prompts, and treated as data the model should not interpret as instructions.","Treated identically to trusted input from internal sources, on the grounds that all input the application receives can be handled the same way.","Blocked entirely so the application accepts only trusted input, with no path for legitimate external input the application might process."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c104","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application's outputs are inconsistent in format. \nThe team's instructions are scattered across the system prompt and user messages, with some instructions stated only once and others repeated. \nHow would you fix the inconsistency?","o":["Consolidate behavioral and format instructions in the system prompt and keep user messages focused on the user's input.","Repeat all instructions in both the system prompt and the user message so the model sees each instruction more than once.","Place each instruction wherever the developer who first wrote it found most natural, retaining the original intent.","Move all instructions into the user message so the application's behavior is controlled at the input point."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c105","d":"general","x":"Your team uses Claude Code across multiple repositories. \nYou want the team's rules and general coding standards to apply to all repositories, and other rules to apply only to specific repositories. \nThe team is currently duplicating instructions across every repository's CLAUDE.md file. \nHow would you address this?","o":["Use a single repository's CLAUDE.md as the central source of truth and link to it from every other repository'sCLAUDE.md file.","Move all instructions to a separate documentation site that developers consult during Claude Code sessions across all repositories.","Use a CLAUDE.md hierarchy that scopes general standards broadly and project-specific context within each repository's local CLAUDE.md.","Stop using CLAUDE.md altogether and ask each developer to configure Claude Code manually for each project they work on."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c106","d":"general","x":"You are running Claude Code as part of an automated continuous integration pipeline. \nThe pipeline needs Claude Code to execute a set of well-defined tasks without prompting for confirmation, and the output needs to be captured for downstream processing. \nHow would you configure the pipeline?","o":["Run Claude Code in headless mode with the required permissions configured in settings.json and capture its output for downstream processing.","Disable Claude Code's confirmation prompts globally across all environments so the pipeline runs without interruption from any prompt.","Run Claude Code in interactive mode and have a developer manually approve every confirmation prompt while the pipeline executes its tasks.","Replace Claude Code with a different tool that does not require any configuration to operate without confirmation prompts in the pipeline."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c107","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked you to explain why the team's Claude application is billed for output tokens at a different rate than input tokens. \nThey had assumed the rate was the same for both. How would you explain the difference?","o":["Output tokens are typically billed at a lower rate than input tokens, because output tokens are cheaper to produce than input tokens are to process.","Output tokens are not billed at all, because cost is determined entirely by the input tokens sent to the model on each request.","Output tokens are typically billed at the same rate as input tokens, and the apparent rate difference is a billing error to report to Anthropic.","Output tokens are typically billed at a higher rate than input tokens, and cost models for the application should reflect both rates separately."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c108","d":"general","x":"A small internal tool needs to read files, run shell commands, and search a codebase for a one-off refactor task using the Claude Agent SDK. \nNo external systems or custom domain logic are involved. \nWhat is the most appropriate starting point?","o":["Connect an external MCP server that reimplements filesystem and shell access over a network protocol","Build a custom tool for reading files and another for running shell commands before writing any code","Write a Skill containing instructions for how to read files and run commands in place of built-in tools","Use the SDK's built-in tools such as Read, Edit, Bash, Glob, and Grep without building any custom tools or MCP servers"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c109","d":"general","x":"A fintech startup is building an internal support agent with the Claude Agent SDK. \nThe agent must look up account balances from a proprietary ledger service reachable only through an internal REST gateway with no existing MCP server or public SDK. \nWhich approach best fits this requirement?","o":["Write a Skill that documents the ledger schema and expects Claude to reason about balances from memory","Search for a community MCP server that might expose a similar ledger balance capability","Rely on the built-in WebFetch tool to query the gateway directly from the agent's prompt","Define a custom tool that calls the internal REST gateway and register it through an in-process MCP server"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c110","d":"general","x":"A team is building an automated agent using the Claude Agent SDK that runs unattended in a CI pipeline. \nIt needs to run a fixed set of tools (`Read`, `Grep`, `Bash(npm test)`) without any human available to approve prompts, and any tool call outside that fixed set must be rejected outright rather than hang waiting for approval. \nWhich configuration achieves this?","o":["Set `permission_mode` to `plan` and set `allowed_tools` to the fixed set, since plan mode auto-denies any tool call that is not explicitly pre-approved.","Set `permission_mode` to `bypassPermissions` and omit `allowed_tools` entirely, since bypass mode already restricts execution to a minimal safe tool set by default.","Set `permission_mode` to `default` and register a `canUseTool` callback that always throws an exception for tools outside the fixed set.","Set `allowed_tools` to the fixed set and `permission_mode` to `dontAsk`; listed tools run, and every other call is denied without invoking `canUseTool`."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c111","d":"general","x":"An engineer configuring a Claude Code hook wants it to fire for every Bash, Edit, and NotebookEdit tool call without listing each tool name explicitly. \nAccording to Anthropic's documentation on regex-based matchers, which configuration achieves this?","o":["A matcher like `Bash|Edit.*` is used; the system falls back to regex evaluation and matches any tool name containing those as substrings.","A matcher of `Bash+Edit+NotebookEdit`, where the plus sign acts as a concatenation operator, causing the pattern to be evaluated as a single exact-match string.","A matcher of `Bash, Edit, NotebookEdit` with commas, which triggers regex evaluation, causing the pattern to match any tool name containing those substrings.","Omitting the matcher field entirely, which causes the hook to fire only for tools with names longer than four characters, such as Bash and NotebookEdit."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c112","d":"general","x":"Which of the following statements about hook decision priority and configuration scope are accurate according to Anthropic's documentation? (Select 3)","o":["`.claude/settings.local.json` is gitignored and intended for local-only configuration, not for sharing hooks with a team","Async hook outputs can safely block, modify, or inject context into the operation as long as `asyncTimeout` is set high enough","When multiple hooks return conflicting decisions, deny takes priority over defer, which takes priority over ask, which takes priority over allow","Managed policy settings apply organization-wide and are admin-controlled, taking precedence in a way project-local settings cannot override","A hook matcher of `*` is functionally identical to an omitted matcher, since only `*` fully disables regex evaluation for that hook","PostToolUse hooks can block a tool call before it executes, just like PreToolUse hooks"],"a":[0,2,3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c113","d":"general","x":"A healthcare organization with a HIPAA-enabled Claude API organization sends a request that includes the code execution tool. \nThe API responds with a 400 invalid_request_error stating the feature is not available for HIPAA-regulated organizations without Zero Data Retention. \nWhat is the most appropriate immediate remediation for this specific request?","o":["Remove the code execution tool from the request and use only features listed as eligible for HIPAA readiness in the feature eligibility table","Retry the identical request with a higher max_tokens value, since the error is caused by insufficient token budget for the tool call","Ignore the error and resubmit the same request repeatedly, since HIPAA feature restrictions are only advisory warnings rather than enforced blocks","Switch the request to a different, unrelated organization ID without a BAA in place so the feature restriction no longer applies"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c114","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application processes 50-page legal contracts and produces summaries with citation references back to the source. \nThe team is debating whether to send each contract whole or split into smaller pieces. \nThe contracts fit within Claude's context window. \nInitial testing shows that whole- document processing produces summaries with stronger cross-section reasoning but occasionally drifts on citation accuracy in later sections. \nChunked processing produces stronger citation accuracy per chunk but loses cross-section reasoning. \nThe team has not decided which property matters more. \nHow would you guide the team's decision?","o":["Examine the use cases where cross-section reasoning failures occur and assess whether whole-document processing meets the application's accuracy requirements across a representative sample of contracts.","Identify which property matters more for the application's actual use case and let that decision drive the chunking approach, then validate the choice against representative contracts.","Review the citation accuracy results from chunked processing and determine whether the loss of cross-section reasoning produces summaries that still meet the application's quality bar.","Assess the cost and latency implications of both approaches against the application's performance requirements before recommending which processing strategy to adopt."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c115","d":"general","x":"You are writing a system prompt for a Claude application that needs to produce output in a specific JSON shape. \nThe downstream system will reject any output that does not match the schema. \nYour prompt would need to...","o":["Instruct Claude to return JSON sometimes and free text other times so the application's output captures both formats.","Include explicit constraints describing the required JSON schema and an instruction to produce only output matching that schema.","Instruct Claude to use whichever output format it considers most appropriate for each request the application handles.","Omit any reference to the format and rely on a post-processing step in the application to reshape Claude's output."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c116","d":"general","x":"You are building a Claude application that processes 10,000 customer emails overnight to extract structured data. \nThe work is non-interactive, runs once daily, and has a flexible completion window of several hours. \nWhich Claude API would you use?","o":["The streaming responses API to process each email and return partial results to a database as the model generates them.","The Batch API, which is designed for non-interactive workloads with flexible completion windows.","The real-time Messages API with concurrent requests to process the emails as fast as possible during the overnight window.","The real-time Messages API, processing the emails one at a time sequentially to ensure consistent ordering of results."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c117","d":"general","x":"The team is debating whether to integrate with the Claude API directly or through a third-party abstraction layer that supports multiple LLM providers. \nThe team has identified that all current and projected use cases run on Claude, no internal customer has requested LLM portability, and the team's product roadmap does not mention multi-provider support over the next two years. \nThe third-party abstraction would add roughly 15 percent overhead in code complexity and introduce one additional dependency. \nWhich integration approach would you recommend?","o":["The third-party abstraction layer, on the grounds that multi-provider support is valuable for any application as a matter of long-term flexibility across vendors.","A custom multi-provider abstraction layer the team builds in-house so that the team controls every part of the abstraction the application uses for its API calls.","Direct integration with Claude through its SDK, because no multi-provider need exists and abstraction would add complexity that does not pay off.","Both integration paths in parallel, where the application uses each path on different runs to compare which performs better in production over time."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c118","d":"general","x":"You are implementing a custom tool for your Claude agent. \nThe tool needs to interact with an external pricing service that returns product data. \nWhich of the following best practices would you apply as you develop this tool?","o":["Define the tool with a clear schema, write a precise description for when to call it, and handle pricing service errors explicitly.","Define the tool with a loose schema and let the model interpret the inputs flexibly on each call the agent makes.","Implement the tool with no error handling and let the agent loop catch failures whenever the pricing service returns an error during operation.","Omit the tool description and let the model infer when to use the tool based on the tool's name and the rest of the prompt context."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c119","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application has multi-step workflows where each step's output is needed only briefly before the agent moves on. \nThe cumulative tool output is filling the context window with content that is no longer relevant. \nHow would you handle the accumulating tool output?","o":["Keep every tool output in the context indefinitely so the agent has the full record of every step it has executed during the workflow.","Apply prompt caching to the accumulated tool outputs so the application does not re-pay for the older content on each subsequent step.","Apply tool output pruning to remove tool outputs that are no longer needed by later steps in the workflow.","Switch to a smaller Claude model that processes context more efficiently and treat any quality loss as a tradeoff for the cost reduction."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c120","d":"general","x":"A Claude application that worked well in testing is now occasionally returning outputs that mention information not present in the input. \nThe development team initially assumed the model was hallucinating, so they asked you to troubleshoot. \nWhat would you do first?","o":["Examine production traces to identify whether the issue is hallucination by the model, context loss, prompt injection, or another failure mode before recommending a fix.","Add a system prompt instruction telling the model not to invent information, on the grounds that prompt-level instructions are the fastest fix for hallucination concerns.","Replace the current model with a larger one to reduce the chance of hallucination, on the grounds that larger models tend to hallucinate less in typical applications.","Apply a retrieval-augmented generation pattern to ground the responses in source content before any further investigation of the production traces."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c121","d":"general","x":"Your team's Claude application has been in production for a year, and the team has decided to formalize its testing strategy. \nCurrently, the team writes ad-hoc tests for individual features but has no overall testing approach. \nWhat testing approach would you formalize?","o":["Define unit tests for individual functions, integration tests for the Claude integration, and end-to-end tests for critical user flows, applied consistently across the codebase.","Continue writing ad-hoc tests as features ship and introduce a peer review step to ensure each test adequately covers the feature being released.","Adopt a test-driven development practice where unit tests are written before each feature is implemented and must pass before code is merged.","Define a single testing approach that uses end-to-end tests and apply it consistently across all new features as they are added to the codebase."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c122","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked you to explain the difference between context engineering and prompt engineering. \nThey have heard the terms used interchangeably and are unsure how each applies to a Claude application that processes long-running multi-step tasks. \nHow would you describe the distinction?","o":["Prompt engineering shapes individual prompts for specific outputs, while context engineering manages how content flows across turns and steps and takes steps to keep relevant state visible.","Prompt engineering focuses on the model's response, while context engineering focuses on the user's input across many sessions in a long-running multi-step Claude application.","Prompt engineering is the older term for prompt design, while context engineering is the newer term that has replaced it in modern Claude applications across the industry.","Prompt engineering and context engineering each address content the team gives Claude, but the team can group them under a single workflow because the practices use overlapping techniques."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c123","d":"general","x":"The team is debating whether to build a new capability as a custom tool or to use an existing built-in tool that nearly covers the use case but lacks one specific feature. \nHow would you decide?","o":["Examine the custom tool option by scoping the implementation effort and determining whether the additional flexibility justifies the development and maintenance cost.","Evaluate whether the missing feature can be addressed by extending the built-in tool or warrants a custom tool, then choose accordingly.","Review the built-in tool's documentation and assess whether its existing capabilities are sufficient to cover the use case without any modification or extension.","Identify whether the team has previously built similar custom tools and use those precedents to determine which approach is more consistent with the existing codebase."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c124","d":"general","x":"Your Claude application returns confident-sounding answers, but occasionally those answers contain factual errors that downstream systems treat as ground truth. \nThe team is concerned about the application's confidence-versus-accuracy gap. \nHow would you address the gap?","o":["Lower the model's temperature so the model's responses sound less confident and downstream systems are less likely to treat the responses as ground truth in normal operation.","Reject every response the application produces until a manual accuracy review is conducted on each response by a human reviewer before any downstream system uses it.","Apply skepticism toward confident output by adding validation steps, sourcing requirements, or confidence calibration before treating outputs as ground truth.","Add a disclaimer to every output telling users to verify the accuracy of the output and treat the disclaimer as the primary mechanism for managing the confidence-versus-accuracy gap."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c125","d":"general","x":"You are designing an agent that processes vendor invoices. \nThe work involves a small number of well- understood steps, but occasionally an invoice arrives in an unexpected format that requires the system to decide between rerouting, requesting clarification, or flagging for human review. \nThe most appropriate architecture for this system is...","o":["A manager agent that delegates each step of standard invoice processing to a dedicated subagent, with a separate subagent handling each unexpected format.","A fully autonomous agent that handles every invoice from start to finish across all formats.","A workflow for the standard path with an agent invoked at the decision point for unexpected formats.","A single large prompt that processes every incoming invoice, both standard and unexpected, in one model call."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c126","d":"general","x":"A teammate has asked you to explain when a Skill would be the right choice over an MCP server. \nThe teammate is unsure how the two differ in practice when both can be reused across teams. \nHow would you explain the distinction?","o":["A Skill and an MCP server are equivalent extension mechanisms that the team can use interchangeably for any reusable capability that needs to be accessible across teams.","A Skill is the older mechanism and an MCP server is the newer one, so the team should prefer an MCP server for any reusable capability that the team builds going forward.","A Skill is preferable for cross-team reuse because it loads more efficiently than an MCP server during normal operation in the team's typical multi-team workloads.","A Skill bundles prompts, scripts, and data into a package the model loads as a unit while an MCP server exposes resources, tools, and prompts through a standard client interface."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c127","d":"general","x":"Your team is choosing how to add a capability to a Claude application. \nYou want to apply the appropriate option, whether built-in tool, custom tool, Skill, or MCP server, based on the use case. \nYou would choose the option that...","o":["Is the newest available option among built-in tools, custom tools, Skills, and MCP servers.","Is the most familiar to the team based on prior experience with similar capabilities.","Matches the use case's scope, reuse needs, and integration context.","Is the easiest to build given the team's current development tools and workflows."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c128","d":"general","x":"A code-review agent needs to read through 40 files, but the team wants the main conversation to stay short so later turns aren't paying for tokens spent on every file that was opened during the review. \nWhich architecture keeps the main agent's context lean?","o":["Set effort to \"low\" on the main query so each turn that reads a file consumes fewer reasoning tokens, even though all 40 files still load fully into the shared main conversation.","Delegate the review to a subagent defined with an AgentDefinition; its tool calls and file contents stay in its own context, and only a final summary returns to the parent.","Enable include_partial_messages so file contents stream back to the main conversation incrementally as text deltas instead of arriving all at once.","Increase max_turns on the main query so Claude has enough budget to read all 40 files directly in the main conversation without the loop stopping early."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c129","d":"general","x":"A long-running agent session is approaching its context window limit. \nThe team wants two things: a full, unabridged copy of the conversation saved before the SDK summarizes older history, and a guarantee that a specific formatting rule survives every compaction event without being paraphrased away. \nWhich combination of mechanisms addresses both needs?","o":["Register a PreCompact hook to archive the full transcript first, and put the formatting rule in CLAUDE.md so it reloads on every request instead of living only in the initial prompt.","Register a PostToolUse hook to archive the transcript after each tool call completes, and place the formatting rule inside a subagent's AgentDefinition prompt instead of the main session.","Increase max_turns so compaction never triggers, and repeat the formatting rule at the start of every single user message sent throughout the session.","Rely on the automatic compaction summary alone to preserve both the transcript and the formatting rule, since summaries are designed to retain conversation details verbatim."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c130","d":"general","x":"A software architect is comparing three open-source agent frameworks for a project that requires integration with multiple LLM providers and end-to-end observability via OpenTelemetry. \nThe project's agent logic is linear and does not require the manual specification of execution graphs. \nThe frameworks under consideration are Strands Agents SDK, LangGraph, and Pydantic AI. \nBased on your knowledge of these frameworks, which of the following statements are true? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Strands Agents SDK enforces that all agents are deployed on AWS and does not permit the use of third-party model APIs.","Pydantic AI includes a built-in OpenTelemetry collector that can be configured to send traces to CloudWatch out of the box.","LangGraph models agent workflows as directed graphs, giving developers precise control over branching and looping in execution paths.","LangGraph is best suited for simple, linear agent chains because its graph model restricts the developer to a single execution path.","LangGraph can only orchestrate agents that use the Anthropic API because it was developed by Anthropic.","The Strands Agents SDK is designed to be model-agnostic and includes first-class support for OpenTelemetry, allowing trace export to observability platforms like AWS CloudWatch."],"a":[2,5]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c131","d":"general","x":"A team wants higher-confidence answers from a fraud-detection prompt applied to a transaction description. \nRather than reviewing different angles, they run the exact same prompt against the same transaction five times and keep whichever verdict -- fraudulent or legitimate -- appears most often across the five runs. \nWhich pattern are they using?","o":["Parallelization by sectioning, where five different prompts each examine a distinct aspect of the same transaction independently.","Prompt chaining, where each of the five runs consumes the verdict produced by the run immediately before it.","Parallelization by voting, where the identical task runs multiple times and the majority verdict is selected.","Evaluator-optimizer, where a second LLM call critiques the first verdict and the prompt is revised until the criteria are satisfied."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c132","d":"general","x":"A support-ticket pipeline runs a simple sentiment classification step on every incoming ticket before the main conversation continues with more complex reasoning. \nThis classification happens far more often than any other step and doesn't need the team's most capable model. \nHow should the team reduce cost for this specific step?","o":["Run the classification step in the main conversation using the same model as the rest of the pipeline, since subagents cannot be configured with a different model than their caller.","Convert the classification step into an evaluator-optimizer loop, since that pattern always uses a cheaper model for the evaluator half of the loop.","Convert the pipeline into an agent team, since every teammate in an agent team automatically defaults to the cheapest available model.","Route the classification step to a dedicated subagent configured to use a faster, cheaper model, since subagents can be assigned their own model independent of the main conversation."],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c133","d":"general","x":"A long-running autonomous agent conversation regularly approaches the model's context window limit. \nThe architecture needs the platform to automatically summarize earlier parts of the conversation server-side, and to clear out stale tool results as the token limit approaches, without the client managing this manually. \nWhich two features should the architecture enable?","o":["Token counting","Files API","Context editing","Batch processing","Compaction","Prompt caching"],"a":[2,4]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c134","d":"general","x":"An agent built with the Claude Agent SDK reads an authentication module in one query call, and the team wants a second, later query call to reference 'it' meaning that same module without re-reading or re-explaining context. \nWhat should they capture and reuse from the first call to accomplish this?","o":["Capture the session_id from the SystemMessage of type 'init' during the first query, then pass it as the resume option on the second query call.","Capture the file contents read during the first query and hardcode them into the system prompt used for the second query call.","Capture the full text of every message from the first query and re-send it as the prompt string prefix on the second query call.","Capture the tool_use_id from the first query's Read tool call and pass it as the model parameter on the second query call."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c135","d":"general","x":"An engineer building an SDLC automation wants an agent to run git status, stage changes, and commit them as part of a scripted workflow, in addition to reading and editing source files. \nWhich built-in Agent SDK tool grants this ability to run version-control commands directly?","o":["AskUserQuestion, which prompts the user with clarifying multiple-choice questions rather than executing shell commands","Bash, which runs terminal commands and scripts, including git operations, in addition to other shell tasks","WebFetch, which retrieves and parses web page content and cannot execute local version-control commands","Grep, which searches file contents with regex and has no ability to invoke external commands like git"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c136","d":"general","x":"A batch-processing service runs thousands of independent, single-shot document classification tasks per hour using the Agent SDK. \nEach task is unrelated to the others, must never be resumable later, and the team wants to avoid unnecessary disk writes for these throwaway sessions in their TypeScript service. \nWhat is the correct session configuration?","o":["Use the Python SDK's `ClaudeSDKClient` instead, since only the Python SDK offers an option to skip writing session transcripts to disk","Call `resume` with a randomly generated session ID on each task, which creates a new in-memory-only session and skips disk persistence","Set `persistSession: false` on each `query()` call, so the session exists only in memory for the duration of that call and nothing is written to disk","Set `continue: true` on each call so every task shares a single reusable session instead of creating a new one each time"],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c137","d":"general","x":"A developer resumes a long-running session with `claude --resume` that was previously running on Opus 4.6. \nBetween the original session and the resume, the organization's admin retired Opus 4.6 and it is no longer reachable. \nWhat happens when the session resumes?","o":["The resumed session falls through to the normal precedence order for selecting a model, since the restored model is unavailable, rather than failing outright","The session silently resumes on Opus 4.6 anyway, since Claude Code caches the model's response format locally and does not need to reach the retired model to continue an existing transcript","The resume command fails immediately with an unrecoverable error, since resumed sessions are hard-locked to their originally saved model with no fallback path","The session automatically and permanently updates its saved `model` setting to whatever the resume happens to fall back to, overwriting the user's prior explicit `/model` choice for all future sessions"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c138","d":"general","x":"A team is scoping the operational limits of the Message Batches API before committing to it for a recurring nightly job. \nSelect the statements below that accurately describe these limits.","o":["Batches are visible across every Workspace in an organization by default, so a batch created in one Workspace can be listed and its results downloaded by any other Workspace.","Batch results remain available for download for 29 days after the batch creation, after which the batch record still exists but results can no longer be downloaded.","A batch expires if it has not finished processing within 24 hours of creation, and any requests still unprocessed at that point are automatically marked as expired.","A single Message Batch is restricted to a maximum of 100,000 requests or 256 MB, and the API will immediately reject any batch exceeding either of these limits.","A batch can include `stream: true` on individual requests as long as the overall batch call itself is submitted without streaming, letting each request specify its own streaming behavior."],"a":[1,2,3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c139","d":"general","x":"A platform team deploys `.claude/settings.json` to every repository with a `permissions.deny` rule blocking `Bash(curl *)`. \nAn individual developer adds the opposite rule, an `allow` entry for `Bash(curl *)`, in their own `.claude/settings.local.json` because they need curl for a personal debugging script. \nWhen that developer runs Claude Code in the repo, what happens to `curl` commands?","o":["Curl commands prompt for manual approval every time, because conflicting allow and deny rules across scopes cancel each other out and fall back to interactive confirmation","Curl commands stay blocked, because permission rules merge across scopes instead of one scope overriding another, so a local allow cannot remove a project-level deny","Curl commands stay blocked only until the developer restarts Claude Code, after which the local settings file's allow rule takes effect on the next session","Curl commands become allowed, because `settings.local.json` has higher precedence than `settings.json` and its permission rules fully replace the lower-scoped file's rules"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c140","d":"general","x":"A vendor wants to package a set of internal-workflow skills, a custom subagent, an audit-logging hook, and a database MCP server as one reusable unit that different project teams can load into their own Claude Code sessions without copying files into each project. \nWhich design satisfies this requirement?","o":["Publish the skills, subagent definition, hook scripts, and MCP server configuration as a single CLAUDE.md file with sections like `## Skills`, `## Agents`, `## Hooks`, and `## MCP`, and have each project load it by adding an import directive in the project configuration.","Package the skills, agent, hooks, and MCP server definition into a plugin directory (with `skills/`, `agents/`, `hooks/`, and `.mcp.json`) and have each project load it via the `plugins` option pointing at the plugin's path.","Register the skills, subagent logic, hook scripts, and MCP server configuration as a single custom tool function in the Agent SDK's tool registry, and have each project team enable it by adding the tool to their session's tool-calling loop.","Place the skills, subagent, hooks, and MCP config into a base directory; then have each team copy the skills into their `.claude/skills/` folder and manually add the subagent, hooks, and MCP settings to their project configuration."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c141","d":"general","x":"A product team needs a model for code generation, data analysis, and agentic tool use across a large user base. \nThey want the best combination of speed and intelligence rather than the highest-capability, most expensive model. \nWhich current Claude model fits this requirement?","o":["Claude Mythos 5.1","Claude Sonnet 5.5","Claude Opus 5.5","Claude Haiku 4.5"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c142","d":"general","x":"A team's usage sits well under their published requests-per-minute limit, yet they begin seeing 429 errors after a marketing campaign causes API traffic to triple within a few minutes. \nHistorical usage was steady before the spike. \nWhat is the most likely explanation, and what should the team change to avoid recurrence?","o":["The spike likely triggered acceleration limits designed to catch sharp usage increases; the team should ramp traffic up gradually and keep usage patterns more consistent","The 429 responses indicate the organization's API key was automatically revoked for suspected abuse and must be regenerated in the Console","The published RPM limit only applies to the Message Batches API, so any spike in synchronous Messages API traffic will always return 429 regardless of pacing","The spike exceeded the request size limit for the Messages API, so each oversized request was individually rejected with a rate-limit error"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c143","d":"general","x":"An engineer is deciding, for several failing requests, whether simply switching to the streaming API or increasing max_tokens would resolve the failure. \nSelect all of the following scenarios where neither of those two changes would fix the described error.","o":["A request fails with 504 timeout_error because a very large non-streaming generation ran past the idle-connection limit on an unreliable network","A request fails with 409 conflict_error because two workers concurrently modified the same underlying resource before this request was sent","A request fails with 413 request_too_large because the inlined message content itself exceeds the Messages API's maximum request payload size","A request fails with 401 authentication_error because the API key used to sign the request has expired","A response completes with stop_reason set to max_tokens because the generated content reached the configured output token cap"],"a":[1,2,3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c144","d":"general","x":"A billing-sensitive workload needs to know exactly how many tokens a large prompt will consume before it is sent to Claude, so the team can stay within a budget. \nWhat should they use?","o":["The Files API, since uploaded files automatically report their token size before generation begins","The budget_tokens field on extended thinking, which caps and reports total request token usage","The effort parameter set to low, which returns an estimated token count in the response metadata","A token counting call, to determine the number of tokens in a message before sending it"],"a":[3]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c145","d":"general","x":"While streaming a response with extended thinking enabled, a client receives a thinking content block that ends with a signature_delta event immediately before content_block_stop. \nWhat is the purpose of this signature_delta event?","o":["It signals that a fallback model has taken over generation for the remainder of the response.","It provides a signature used to verify the integrity of the thinking block's content.","It indicates that the thinking block has been redacted for policy compliance and its content should be discarded.","It marks the point at which the thinking block's token usage should be added to the cumulative usage total."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c146","d":"general","x":"A junior engineer asks why Claude appears to produce text piece by piece rather than generating the entire response instantly. \nWhich explanation best matches how Claude generates output?","o":["Claude autoregressively predicts the next token based on the prompt and previously generated tokens, repeating the process to build the full response.","Claude generates tokens in a random order, then applies a learned reordering function to arrange them into a coherent response before returning it.","Claude generates the full response as a complete string in one forward pass through the model, then splits it into tokens only for transmission to the client.","Claude retrieves the entire response from a precomputed lookup table by matching the prompt's embedding to a database of response embeddings and returning the nearest full text."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c147","d":"general","x":"A support-ticket triage service sends the same 6,000-token system prompt and tool definitions with every request, followed by a unique customer message. \nRequests arrive in bursts roughly every 30 seconds throughout the day. \nThe team wants to cut input token costs on the repeated prefix without adding infrastructure. \nWhich change addresses this most directly?","o":["Reduce the max_tokens parameter on every request to the minimum needed for a short triage response, so Claude's completions stay brief and the blended token cost per call decreases.","Mark the system prompt and tool definitions with a 5-minute ephemeral cache_control breakpoint; repeated requests then read the prefix from cache, avoiding the full input price.","Move the system prompt and tool definitions into each request's user message so that the fixed 6,000-token prefix is cached per message, cutting input costs across the 30-second burst cycle.","Switch the tool definitions to a compact JSON schema with shorter property names and fewer nested objects, so the 6,000-token prefix shrinks and each request consumes fewer input tokens."],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c148","d":"general","x":"An engineer is building a research agent that needs to read dozens of files across a large repository to answer one question, without letting all that file content pile up in the main conversation's context. \nWhich design best achieves this?","o":["Delegate the file exploration to a subagent, whose fresh context absorbs all the intermediate reads while only its final summary message returns to the parent","Read every file directly in the main agent loop, then issue a manual /compact command immediately afterward to shrink the accumulated history","Read every file directly in the main agent loop, but set effort to low so the extra file content consumes fewer tokens per turn","Store all file contents in a custom MCP resource and have the main agent fetch a single combined resource URI instead of individual files"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c149","d":"general","x":"A prompt written for a legal-document summarizer produces inconsistent structure across runs, and engineers on the team disagree about why. \nBefore tuning the prompt further, which practice would most directly reveal ambiguity in the current wording?","o":["Show the prompt to a colleague who has minimal context on the task and ask them to follow it exactly as written, since confusion for a human reader tends to predict confusion for Claude","Ask Claude to explain its own instructions back in a separate call, treating the model's paraphrase of the prompt as a reliable substitute for a human comprehension check","Reduce the temperature to zero for the summarizer's requests, on the assumption that ambiguity in phrasing is resolved by lower-variance sampling rather than by clearer instructions","Rerun the identical, unedited prompt against the model twenty times and keep only the most common resulting structure, treating majority voting as a substitute for locating the ambiguity"],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c150","d":"general","x":"A summarization service parses the response text as JSON. For very long transcripts, the JSON string is occasionally cut off mid-object and the parser raises a decode error. Investigating, the engineer finds stop_reason is \"max_tokens\" on every one of the failing calls. \nWhat should the defensive parsing logic do?","o":["Ignore stop_reason and retry parsing with a lenient JSON parser that tolerates trailing commas","Catch the decode error, check stop_reason for max_tokens, and treat the response as incomplete","Treat every JSON decode error as a transient network issue and resend the identical request","Lower max_tokens further so Claude is forced to produce shorter, safer completions"],"a":[1]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c151","d":"general","x":"During testing, an agent occasionally calls a create_ticket tool while omitting the required priority field. \nWhich of the following are appropriate ways to handle this within the conversation, according to recommended tool-use error handling? (Select all that apply)","o":["Automatically terminate the session and require the user to start an entirely new conversation","Return a tool_result with is_error: true naming the missing field so Claude retries with it supplied","Rewrite the schema to make priority optional so it can never be reported as missing again","Log the omission silently and proceed with ticket creation using a null priority value","Add strict: true to the tool definition so input is validated against the schema before your handler","Improve the tool's description so the priority-field requirement is stated more explicitly"],"a":[1,4,5]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c152","d":"general","x":"A prompt concatenates two source documents as plain, undifferentiated text, and Claude's responses sometimes misattribute which document a fact came from. \nWhat restructuring best fixes this attribution problem?","o":["Convert both documents into a single CSV file where each row holds one paragraph and the left column labels the source document, so Claude can read the tabular data and attribute facts to the correct source.","Insert a page-break control character between the two documents, such as a form feed, and prepend a short system note that instructs Claude to treat the page break as a document separator for attribution.","Wrap each document in its own tag with nested and subtags, placed inside an outer tag, thereby providing clear boundaries and metadata.","Prefix each document with a numbered markdown heading (e.g., \"# Document 1\") and then include a prompt instruction for Claude to attribute facts based solely on which heading they appear under."],"a":[2]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c153","d":"general","x":"A subagent is defined to automatically spawn further subagents to divide up its own workload, and those in turn may spawn more. \nWhat governs how deep this nesting can go, and how can a team stop a specific subagent from spawning any children at all?","o":["Nesting defaults to three levels below the main agent and can be changed with the `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_SUBAGENT_SPAWN_DEPTH` setting; omitting `Agent` from a subagent's tools (or adding it to `disallowedTools`) prevents that subagent from spawning children.","Nesting is unlimited regardless of execution mode, because there is no built-in depth limit, and to prevent a specific subagent from spawning children, the team must remove the `Bash` tool from its tools list to block the execution of child-spawning commands.","Nesting has no depth limit as long as each subagent runs in the background, because background execution bypasses the configured depth cap, and to prevent a particular subagent from spawning children, set its effort level to low, which prevents it from spawning children.","Nesting is capped at two levels only when the subagent runs in the foreground, because foreground execution limits nesting to two levels, and to prevent a subagent from spawning children, set its `permissionMode` to `plan` to restrict it to read-only and prevent spawning."],"a":[0]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c154","d":"general","x":"A support agent built on Claude has access to a customer database tool and forwards conversation transcripts to a third-party analytics vendor for quality monitoring. \nTranscripts routinely contain customers' full names, emails, and account numbers. \nWhich of these actions should the team take to prevent unnecessary PII exposure? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Apply the same PII minimization to data logged internally for debugging, not just to data sent to the external vendor","Ask Claude to verbally warn the customer at the start of each chat that their data might be shared externally, then proceed without further changes","Send the full unredacted transcripts as-is, since the analytics vendor's contract already covers general data handling and confidentiality obligations","Redact or mask personally identifiable information in the transcripts before they leave the system, sending the vendor only the fields it actually needs","Review which fields the analytics vendor's quality-monitoring use case genuinely requires before deciding what to include in each transcript export","Compress the transcripts before sending them, since compression is treated as equivalent to redaction for the purpose of protecting personal data"],"a":[0,3,4]},{"id":"ccdv-f-c155","d":"general","x":"A fintech company is deploying a multi-tenant Claude-powered advisor that reads account data for one customer per session and can place trades through an internal API. \nWhich combination of practices best supports authentication, authorization, and confidentiality across tenants? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Scope each session's tool access so it can only read and act on the data belonging to the authenticated customer for that session","Issue one shared service credential for the trading API that every customer session reuses, to simplify credential rotation","Require a fresh authorization check on the backend before executing any trade action the agent requests, rather than trusting the agent's own judgment alone","Log full account numbers and portfolio balances in plaintext application logs so support staff can search history freely","Let a session continue reading a different customer's data mid-conversation if the agent decides it is contextually relevant to the current advice","Screen inbound account notes and support messages for injected instructions before they are returned to Claude as tool results"],"a":[0,2,5]}]},{"id":"gh-300","code":"GH-300","title":"GitHub Copilot","vendor":"GitHub","desc":"Responsible AI, Copilot features, prompt engineering, context, productivity, privacy and org policies.","exam":{"count":60,"minutes":100,"passPercent":70},"domains":{"github-copilot":"GitHub Copilot"},"qs":[{"id":"gh-300-c1","d":"github-copilot","x":"What practices enhance the quality of suggestions provided by GitHub Copilot? (Select three)","o":["Accept all suggestions without reviewing them","Write clear and descriptive comments before the code","Use only one programming language per project","Keep related files open in the editor","Break down complex tasks into smaller, focused prompts"],"a":[1,3,4],"e":"Copilot generates suggestions based on surrounding context. Clear comments act as instructions; open files provide broader context; smaller prompts reduce ambiguity and help Copilot focus on one task at a time.","w":["Accepting all suggestions without review risks introducing bugs, security vulnerabilities, or deprecated patterns. Always review generated code.","Descriptive comments give Copilot explicit intent, helping it generate more accurate and relevant suggestions.","Using one language does not improve Copilot's suggestion quality. Copilot supports many languages and works best with rich context.","Open related files provide Copilot with broader project context, enabling it to generate suggestions that fit the overall codebase.","Breaking complex tasks into smaller prompts helps Copilot focus and produce more precise, targeted suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c2","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot Business activities are tracked in organization audit logs?","o":["User authentication events and Copilot settings changes","Every code suggestion generated by Copilot","All accepted code completions per developer","Individual developer keystrokes"],"a":[0],"e":"Audit logs in Copilot Business capture administrative and security-relevant events such as authentication and policy/settings changes — not the content of individual suggestions.","w":["Org audit logs track authentication events and administrative changes to Copilot settings, enabling security and compliance reviews.","Individual code suggestions are transient and not logged in audit logs — they would create an enormous and privacy-invasive record.","Accepted completions are surfaced via the Usage Metrics API, not the audit log.","Keystroke logging is not a feature of GitHub Copilot and would be a major privacy violation."]},{"id":"gh-300-c3","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where is the GitHub Copilot proxy service hosted?","o":["Microsoft Azure","Amazon Web Services","Google Cloud Platform","On-premises servers"],"a":[0],"e":"GitHub Copilot's proxy service is hosted on Microsoft Azure, which is consistent with Microsoft's ownership of GitHub and its cloud infrastructure strategy.","w":["The Copilot proxy service runs on Microsoft Azure, leveraging Microsoft's cloud infrastructure.","AWS is not used for the GitHub Copilot proxy.","Google Cloud is not the hosting platform for GitHub Copilot.","Copilot is a cloud-based service; it does not run on on-premises servers."]},{"id":"gh-300-c4","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot suggest code optimizations to developers?","o":["By analyzing context from open files and suggesting improvements inline","By running a full static analysis of the entire codebase","By executing the code and measuring performance metrics","By querying external databases for best practices"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot analyzes the current file's context — including code, comments, and open tabs — to suggest optimizations inline as you type.","w":["Copilot uses the surrounding context (open files, cursor position, comments) to provide inline optimization suggestions in real time.","Copilot does not run a full static analysis engine on the entire codebase.","Copilot does not execute code or benchmark performance — it generates suggestions based on patterns.","Copilot does not query external databases at runtime; suggestions come from its pre-trained model."]},{"id":"gh-300-c5","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which prompts would best guide GitHub Copilot Chat when refactoring code for quality? (Choose two)","o":["Refactor this function to improve readability","Delete all unused variables automatically","Run the tests and fix all failures","Suggest ways to reduce code duplication in this module"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Effective refactoring prompts are specific and goal-oriented. Asking for readability improvements or duplication reduction gives Copilot clear objectives to work with.","w":["This is a clear, specific prompt that guides Copilot to improve code structure and naming without changing behavior.","Copilot Chat does not automatically 'delete' code; this is too imperative and could cause errors if not reviewed.","Copilot Chat cannot directly run tests or fix test failures — it suggests code changes but does not execute code.","Asking Copilot to identify and suggest reductions in duplication is a valid and effective refactoring prompt."]},{"id":"gh-300-c6","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the key consideration when relying on GitHub Copilot Chat for code explanations?","o":["Copilot Chat always provides 100% accurate explanations","Explanations should be reviewed as they may reflect training data biases or inaccuracies","Chat explanations replace the need for official documentation","Copilot only explains Python code"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot Chat's explanations are generated from training data and can sometimes be inaccurate or reflect outdated patterns. Developers must critically review all explanations.","w":["Copilot can make mistakes or provide outdated/biased explanations — it is not infallible.","Since Copilot is trained on public data, its explanations can contain bias or inaccuracies and should always be verified.","Copilot Chat supplements documentation but never replaces authoritative official documentation.","Copilot supports many programming languages; it is not restricted to Python."]},{"id":"gh-300-c7","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which feature prevents GitHub Copilot from suggesting code that is publicly available, at the organization level?","o":["Token encryption","Duplication detection filter","IP forwarding rules","Code sandboxing"],"a":[1],"e":"The duplication detection filter checks Copilot suggestions against an index of public repositories and blocks suggestions that closely match publicly available code (approximately 150+ characters).","w":["Token encryption is a security mechanism, not a feature that filters code suggestions matching public repositories.","The duplication detection filter identifies and blocks suggestions that closely resemble code in public repositories, reducing IP and licensing risks.","IP forwarding is a networking concept unrelated to Copilot's suggestion filtering.","Code sandboxing isolates execution environments; it does not filter public-code matches in suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c8","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the correct way to access audit log events for GitHub Copilot Business?","o":["Through the organization's audit log in GitHub settings","Via the Copilot dashboard inside each IDE","By downloading logs from the Copilot REST API","Through GitHub Actions workflow run logs"],"a":[0],"e":"Audit log events for Copilot Business are accessed through the organization's audit log section in GitHub settings, where administrators can review security and configuration events.","w":["The organization audit log in GitHub settings is the official place to view Copilot Business audit events.","IDE dashboards show usage and suggestions, not organization-level audit logs.","While the REST API can surface some data, the primary access point for audit logs is the GitHub organization settings UI.","GitHub Actions logs capture CI/CD pipeline events, not Copilot Business audit events."]},{"id":"gh-300-c9","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Chat use training data and external sources to answer questions?","o":["It uses training data only and does not browse the internet in real time","It searches the web for current documentation in real time","It automatically pulls from internal company wikis","It uses both live internet search and training data equally"],"a":[0],"e":"GitHub Copilot Chat is a generative AI tool that relies purely on its pre-trained model. It does not have real-time internet access or the ability to browse external sources.","w":["Copilot Chat answers from its pre-trained model only. It cannot access the real-time web or live documentation.","Copilot Chat does not perform live web searches — it works offline from its training data.","Copilot cannot automatically access internal wikis unless they are explicitly provided as context.","Copilot does not combine live internet data with training data; it only uses its pre-trained knowledge."]},{"id":"gh-300-c10","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot Individual features are available in Visual Studio, VS Code, and JetBrains IDEs? (Choose two)","o":["Inline code suggestions","Copilot Chat","Company knowledge base access","Custom fine-tuned models","Enterprise-level audit logging"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot Individual includes inline code suggestions and Copilot Chat across major IDEs. Knowledge bases, custom models, and enterprise audit logging are Enterprise plan features.","w":["Inline code suggestions (autocomplete) are the core feature of Copilot and available in all supported IDEs.","Copilot Chat is available in VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains, and other IDEs with the Individual plan.","Company knowledge base access is an Enterprise plan feature, not available in the Individual plan.","Custom fine-tuned models are an Enterprise plan capability only.","Enterprise-level audit logging is part of Copilot Business and Enterprise plans, not Individual."]},{"id":"gh-300-c11","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can organizations measure the effectiveness of GitHub Copilot adoption? (Choose two)","o":["Monitor developer login frequency","Track suggestion acceptance rates","Count lines of code written per day","Review commit message quality","Analyze developer productivity metrics via the Copilot Metrics API"],"a":[1,4],"e":"The Copilot Metrics API provides data on acceptance rates and productivity impact. Acceptance rates show how often developers find suggestions useful, directly measuring Copilot's effectiveness.","w":["Login frequency measures engagement with GitHub overall, not Copilot effectiveness specifically.","Suggestion acceptance rate is a direct measure of how often developers find Copilot's output useful and adopt it.","Lines of code is a poor quality/productivity metric and does not isolate Copilot's contribution.","Commit message quality reflects developer habits, not Copilot suggestion effectiveness.","The Copilot Usage Metrics API provides analytical data including productivity and adoption metrics across the organization."]},{"id":"gh-300-c12","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot plan allows the collection of prompts and suggestions for training purposes?","o":["Free","Individual","Enterprise","Business"],"a":[2],"e":"Only Copilot Enterprise allows organizations to enable the collection of prompts and suggestions (with user consent) for model training and customization purposes.","w":["The Free plan does not include controls for enterprise-level data collection or prompt/suggestion retention policies.","Copilot Individual users can choose whether their data is used for training, but the Enterprise plan is specifically designed for org-level collection.","Copilot Enterprise allows organizations to configure data collection policies, including collecting prompts and suggestions for improving custom models.","Copilot Business does not retain prompts or suggestions for training — data is discarded after the session."]},{"id":"gh-300-c13","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Enterprise assist teams during code reviews? (Select two)","o":["Automatically merges approved pull requests","Suggests code improvements during pull request reviews","Automatically assigns reviewers based on code complexity","Summarizes pull request changes in natural language"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Copilot Enterprise enhances the review process by suggesting code improvements inline and generating natural language summaries of PR changes, helping reviewers understand changes faster.","w":["Copilot never automatically merges pull requests — this is a human decision requiring explicit approval.","During PR reviews, Copilot Enterprise can suggest improvements, refactoring ideas, and highlight potential issues.","Reviewer assignment is handled by GitHub's CODEOWNERS or manual selection, not Copilot.","Copilot Enterprise can generate natural language summaries of PR diffs, helping reviewers quickly understand what changed and why."]},{"id":"gh-300-c14","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which Microsoft Responsible AI principle focuses on making AI systems understandable and providing clear information about how they work?","o":["Fairness","Transparency","Inclusiveness","Reliability and Safety"],"a":[1],"e":"Transparency means making AI systems explainable and providing clear information about how they make decisions, so users can understand and trust the system.","w":["Fairness is about ensuring AI treats all groups equitably and avoids discrimination — not about explainability.","Transparency refers to making AI understandable, explainable, and clear about its decision-making processes.","Inclusiveness focuses on ensuring AI empowers and benefits people across all demographics, including accessibility.","Reliability and Safety focuses on consistent, dependable performance and avoiding harmful outcomes."]},{"id":"gh-300-c15","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can GitHub Copilot aid in modernizing legacy applications?","o":["By automatically rewriting the entire codebase to modern standards","By suggesting modern equivalents for deprecated patterns and libraries","By deleting all legacy code and replacing it with templates","By running performance benchmarks on legacy code"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot helps modernize legacy code by recognizing outdated patterns and suggesting contemporary replacements — but the developer retains full control over which suggestions to adopt.","w":["Copilot cannot autonomously rewrite an entire codebase. It provides suggestions in context but does not perform bulk rewrites.","Copilot recognizes deprecated syntax, old libraries, and outdated patterns, and can suggest modern equivalents as developers work through the code.","Copilot never deletes code — it only suggests; all changes are made by the developer.","Performance benchmarking is a separate tooling concern, not a Copilot capability."]},{"id":"gh-300-c16","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Chat primarily help developers understand an existing codebase?","o":["By generating test coverage reports","By creating architecture diagrams automatically","By answering natural language questions about code functionality and structure","By executing the code and displaying runtime outputs"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot Chat can explain what code does, why patterns are used, and how components relate — all through natural language conversation, making it a powerful tool for onboarding and code comprehension.","w":["Test coverage reports are generated by testing frameworks (e.g., Jest, pytest), not Copilot Chat.","Copilot Chat does not generate visual architecture diagrams automatically.","Developers can ask Copilot Chat questions like 'What does this function do?' or 'How does this module connect to the rest of the project?' and receive clear explanations.","Copilot Chat does not execute code or display runtime results — it is a conversational assistant."]},{"id":"gh-300-c17","d":"github-copilot","x":"What settings can be configured using GitHub Copilot CLI? (Choose two)","o":["Usage analytics and telemetry settings","Default execution confirmation behavior","Default code editor","GitHub CLI subcommands"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot CLI allows configuring telemetry/analytics preferences and whether commands require explicit confirmation before execution. Editor and GitHub CLI subcommand settings are outside Copilot CLI's scope.","w":["Copilot CLI allows users to configure telemetry and usage analytics settings to control data sharing preferences.","The default execution confirmation setting in Copilot CLI controls whether the CLI asks for confirmation before executing suggested commands.","The default code editor is configured at the system level or via Git config, not through Copilot CLI.","Copilot CLI operates as a separate assistant tool and does not configure native GitHub CLI subcommands."]},{"id":"gh-300-c18","d":"github-copilot","x":"What are the potential risks of relying heavily on GitHub Copilot-generated code without review? (Choose two)","o":["Introduction of security vulnerabilities","Improved code readability automatically","Use of deprecated libraries or outdated patterns","Faster overall development cycles"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Copilot generates code from training data patterns and may inadvertently include vulnerable code or reference deprecated libraries. Human review is essential to catch these issues.","w":["Copilot may suggest code with security vulnerabilities such as SQL injection risks, hardcoded secrets, or insecure API usage if these patterns existed in training data.","Improved readability is not guaranteed. Copilot may generate verbose or inconsistent code that still needs refactoring.","Copilot's training data includes older code, so it may recommend libraries or patterns that have since been deprecated.","While Copilot can speed up development, over-reliance without review creates technical debt and security risks rather than benefits."]},{"id":"gh-300-c19","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which methods can you use to generate sample test data with GitHub Copilot? (Choose two)","o":["Ask Copilot Chat to generate sample datasets in a desired format","Use Copilot to automatically run and query a database","Import production data automatically through Copilot","Use inline comments and prompts to guide generation of mock data structures"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Copilot can generate sample/mock data through Chat by describing the schema, or inline by writing a comment describing the data structure and letting Copilot complete it.","w":["You can ask Copilot Chat to generate sample data in JSON, CSV, SQL, or other formats by describing the schema and requirements.","Copilot cannot execute database queries or connect to databases directly — it only generates code/data as text.","Importing production data would be a security risk, and Copilot has no mechanism to do this automatically.","Writing a comment like '// Generate 10 sample users with name, email, age' above a variable declaration prompts Copilot to suggest mock data inline."]},{"id":"gh-300-c20","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the process behind identifying public code matches when the duplication filter is enabled?","o":["Copilot compares suggestions against an indexed database of public repositories","Copilot queries GitHub search in real time for each suggestion","Copilot uses a local cache of known public code patterns","Copilot runs a hash check against all known open-source licenses"],"a":[0],"e":"The duplication detection filter works by comparing generated suggestions against an indexed snapshot of public GitHub repositories to detect near-verbatim matches.","w":["GitHub maintains an index of public code, and the filter checks suggestions against this index to identify substantial matches (~150+ characters).","Real-time querying of GitHub search for every suggestion would be too slow and inefficient.","There is no local cache on the developer's machine; the check happens server-side.","License checking is a separate concern. The duplication filter focuses on code similarity, not license metadata."]},{"id":"gh-300-c21","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which statement best describes 'role prompting' in the context of GitHub Copilot Chat?","o":["Assigning a specific persona or role to Copilot to guide the style and focus of its responses","Assigning user roles within GitHub organization settings","Restricting Copilot to work only within predefined developer roles","Using role-based access control policies with GitHub Copilot"],"a":[0],"e":"Role prompting means instructing Copilot to take on a persona (e.g., 'Act as a senior security engineer') to influence the tone, depth, and focus of its responses.","w":["Role prompting is a prompt engineering technique where you assign a persona to the AI — e.g., 'You are an expert in AWS infrastructure' — to get more targeted responses.","GitHub organization roles (Owner, Member) are access control settings unrelated to prompting techniques.","Copilot is not restricted based on developer roles in the organization.","RBAC is an identity/access management concept; role prompting is a prompting strategy for AI interactions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c22","d":"github-copilot","x":"What are the effects of enabling content exclusions in GitHub Copilot? (Choose two)","o":["Copilot is disabled entirely for the affected user","Code suggestions become significantly slower","Excluded files are not used as context when generating suggestions","Copilot will not provide completions from within excluded files","All automated tests are skipped for excluded files"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Content exclusions prevent Copilot from using specified files as context and from suggesting completions while a developer has an excluded file open in the editor.","w":["Content exclusions do not disable Copilot entirely — they only affect files matching the exclusion rules.","Content exclusions do not inherently affect suggestion speed.","When a file is excluded, Copilot ignores it as a context source when generating suggestions in other files.","If a developer opens an excluded file, Copilot will not provide inline suggestions within that file.","Content exclusions have no effect on test execution or CI/CD pipelines."]},{"id":"gh-300-c23","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot plan gives developers access to company knowledge bases for context-aware responses?","o":["Individual","Enterprise","Business","Free"],"a":[1],"e":"GitHub Copilot Enterprise allows organizations to create knowledge bases from internal documentation and codebases, which Copilot can then reference to provide organization-specific answers.","w":["The Individual plan is for personal use and does not include enterprise knowledge base features.","Copilot Enterprise includes the ability to build and use knowledge bases (Bing-indexed internal docs, wikis, etc.) to give context-aware, company-specific responses.","Copilot Business focuses on organization-level management and policies but does not include knowledge base creation.","The Free plan has limited features and does not include knowledge base access."]},{"id":"gh-300-c24","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot facilitate learning a new programming language for developers? (Choose two)","o":["By providing embedded video tutorials","By automatically switching the IDE's language mode","By suggesting idiomatic code patterns in the target language","By explaining unfamiliar syntax when asked via Copilot Chat"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Copilot helps language learning by suggesting idiomatic patterns (showing 'how things are done' in a language) and by explaining unfamiliar syntax through Chat when developers ask.","w":["Copilot does not provide video tutorials — it is a code assistant, not a learning management system.","Copilot does not control IDE settings or switch language modes automatically.","As developers write code in a new language, Copilot suggests idiomatic, language-appropriate patterns that help them learn correct conventions.","Developers can ask Copilot Chat 'What does this syntax mean?' or 'How do I write a for loop in Rust?' and receive clear explanations."]},{"id":"gh-300-c25","d":"github-copilot","x":"What should developers consider when using GitHub Copilot to write statistical analysis code?","o":["Copilot fully validates statistical accuracy of all formulas","No additional review is needed for math-heavy suggestions","Developers must verify the mathematical correctness of Copilot's suggestions","Copilot connects to statistical APIs to ensure accurate calculations"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot generates code based on patterns, not mathematical understanding. For statistical analysis, it may generate plausible-looking but mathematically incorrect formulas that must be verified by the developer.","w":["Copilot does not validate statistical accuracy. It predicts likely code patterns, which may look correct but be mathematically wrong.","Math-heavy code requires extra scrutiny precisely because Copilot predicts patterns, not computes results.","Developers must always verify mathematical logic in Copilot suggestions, especially for statistics where subtle errors (wrong formulas, incorrect assumptions) can lead to invalid results.","Copilot does not connect to external APIs at runtime to validate calculations."]},{"id":"gh-300-c26","d":"github-copilot","x":"In which use cases is GitHub Copilot Chat most effective? (Choose two)","o":["Explaining complex code logic to a developer","Managing GitHub repository settings","Scheduling and managing CI/CD pipelines","Generating boilerplate and repetitive code"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Copilot Chat excels at code explanation (making complex logic understandable) and boilerplate generation (automating repetitive tasks), which directly leverage its language model strengths.","w":["Explaining code is one of Copilot Chat's strongest use cases — developers can ask 'What does this code do?' and receive clear natural language explanations.","Repository settings (branch protection, collaborators, webhooks) are managed through the GitHub UI or API, not Copilot Chat.","CI/CD pipeline scheduling is done through GitHub Actions or other CI tools, not Copilot Chat.","Generating repetitive boilerplate (config files, CRUD operations, test suites) is exactly what Copilot is optimized for, saving significant developer time."]},{"id":"gh-300-c27","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which scenario correctly describes the intended use of GitHub Copilot Chat as a developer tool?","o":["Asking Copilot to help debug a function, with an explanation of what went wrong","Using Copilot to automatically deploy the application to production","Letting Copilot manage team member permissions in GitHub","Having Copilot automatically send email notifications to team members"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot Chat is designed to assist with coding tasks like debugging, explaining, and generating code — not for managing deployments, permissions, or external communications.","w":["Asking Copilot to help debug and explain issues is a core, intended use case — it assists the developer while keeping them in control.","Deployment to production is handled by CI/CD tools (GitHub Actions, Jenkins, etc.), not Copilot Chat.","Team permission management is an administrative feature of GitHub, not a Copilot Chat capability.","Copilot Chat cannot send emails or interact with external communication systems."]},{"id":"gh-300-c28","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which types of prompts or code might be flagged by GitHub Copilot's toxicity filter? (Choose two)","o":["Code that could be used to automate harmful or malicious activities","Prompts containing offensive or hateful language","Code with syntax errors in an unsupported language","Requests for generating unit tests"],"a":[0,1],"e":"The toxicity filter screens for harmful content: malicious code (hacking tools, malware patterns) and offensive language in prompts — protecting users and ensuring responsible use.","w":["Prompts asking Copilot to generate code for harmful purposes (e.g., malware, exploits) are flagged by the toxicity filter.","Prompts containing offensive, hateful, or toxic language trigger the filter to refuse the request.","Syntax errors in code are a quality issue, not a toxicity concern — they are not filtered.","Unit test generation is a legitimate, encouraged use case for Copilot and would not be flagged."]},{"id":"gh-300-c29","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub retain prompt and suggestion data for Copilot Business and Enterprise customers? (Choose two)","o":["Prompts are stored indefinitely for audit purposes","Prompts are not retained after the session ends","Suggestions are not used to train the underlying AI model","All user code is stored to improve future suggestions"],"a":[1,2],"e":"For Business and Enterprise plans, GitHub does not persist prompts after the session and explicitly guarantees that code is not used for model training, protecting organizational IP.","w":["Business and Enterprise plans specifically provide protections against long-term data retention of prompts.","Prompts sent to Copilot by Business/Enterprise users are discarded after the suggestion is generated, not stored long-term.","A key selling point of Business/Enterprise is that user code and prompts are never used to train or fine-tune the AI models.","Storing user code for model improvement is explicitly prohibited for Business and Enterprise plans."]},{"id":"gh-300-c30","d":"github-copilot","x":"How is GitHub Copilot Individual billed? (Choose two)","o":["Monthly subscription","Annual subscription","Pay-per-suggestion usage model","Free with any GitHub Pro plan"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot Individual can be purchased as a monthly or annual subscription. There is no pay-per-suggestion model, and it is not automatically included with GitHub Pro.","w":["GitHub Copilot Individual is available as a month-to-month subscription.","An annual subscription option is available, typically at a discounted effective monthly rate.","There is no pay-per-suggestion pricing model for Copilot Individual.","Copilot Individual is a separate paid add-on and is not bundled with GitHub Pro by default."]},{"id":"gh-300-c31","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the best way to share feedback on GitHub Copilot Chat responses in GitHub Mobile?","o":["Email GitHub support directly","Post in the GitHub Community discussion forums","Use the emoji reactions on responses","Submit a GitHub issue in the Copilot repository"],"a":[2],"e":"In GitHub Mobile, the built-in feedback mechanism for Copilot Chat is emoji reactions on responses, providing a quick and contextual way to signal response quality.","w":["Emailing support is for general issues, not the standard in-app feedback mechanism in GitHub Mobile.","Community forums are for general discussion; they are not the official in-app Copilot Chat feedback method.","GitHub Mobile implements emoji reaction feedback on Copilot Chat responses, allowing users to quickly indicate whether a response was helpful.","Filing GitHub issues in a repo is for bug reports, not real-time in-session Copilot Chat feedback in Mobile."]},{"id":"gh-300-c32","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which strategy most effectively increases the performance and quality of GitHub Copilot Chat responses?","o":["Increase the IDE's memory allocation in settings","Disable all other IDE extensions to reduce interference","Use prompt engineering techniques to craft clearer, more specific prompts","Switch to a faster internet connection"],"a":[2],"e":"Prompt engineering — writing clear, specific, and well-structured prompts — is the most impactful way to improve the quality of Copilot Chat responses, regardless of hardware or network.","w":["IDE memory affects editor performance, not the quality of Copilot's AI responses.","Other extensions do not degrade Copilot Chat response quality.","Prompt engineering (being specific, providing context, using examples, defining scope) directly influences how relevant and accurate Copilot's answers are.","While a stable connection is needed, faster internet does not improve the quality of AI-generated responses."]},{"id":"gh-300-c33","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can GitHub Copilot assist during the requirements analysis phase of the SDLC?","o":["By automatically writing user stories from business documents","By running regression tests against existing requirements","By helping translate requirements into technical specifications and pseudocode","By automatically assigning development tasks to team members"],"a":[2],"e":"During requirements analysis, Copilot Chat can help bridge the gap between business requirements and technical implementation by suggesting how to translate them into code structure, APIs, or pseudocode.","w":["Copilot can assist in drafting user stories if given prompts, but it does not automatically parse business documents to generate them.","Running regression tests is a QA/CI activity done after implementation, not during requirements analysis.","Copilot Chat can take requirement descriptions and suggest technical approaches, pseudocode outlines, or API structures, bridging business and engineering.","Task assignment is a project management function handled by tools like Jira or GitHub Issues, not Copilot."]},{"id":"gh-300-c34","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the correct method for an organization to exclude specific files from GitHub Copilot Business suggestions?","o":["Add the files to the repository's .gitignore file","Rename the files with a special prefix recognized by Copilot","Configure content exclusions through the UI settings in GitHub organization settings","Delete the files from the repository permanently"],"a":[2],"e":"Content exclusions in Copilot Business are configured by organization owners or repository admins through the GitHub organization settings UI, specifying file paths or repository patterns.","w":[".gitignore controls which files Git tracks — it has no effect on Copilot suggestions.","There is no Copilot-recognized file naming prefix for exclusion purposes.","Organization owners configure content exclusions via GitHub organization settings → Copilot → Content exclusions, specifying paths using glob patterns.","Deleting files would destroy code permanently — exclusions allow Copilot to ignore files while keeping them in the repository."]},{"id":"gh-300-c35","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which techniques can developers use to improve the quality of prompts for GitHub Copilot? (Choose two)","o":["Be specific and descriptive when writing comments and prompts","Provide example inputs and outputs in the prompt","Use only single-line comments to keep prompts short","Avoid mentioning the programming language in the prompt"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Specificity reduces ambiguity (Copilot knows exactly what you want), while examples show the expected pattern (few-shot style), both significantly improving suggestion quality.","w":["Descriptive, specific prompts give Copilot clear intent and context, leading to more accurate and relevant suggestions.","Providing examples (input/output pairs) guides Copilot to produce suggestions that match the expected pattern — this is few-shot prompting.","Single-line constraints are not a prompting best practice. Detailed multi-line comments often produce better results.","Mentioning the language helps Copilot generate idiomatic code; omitting it can lead to less precise suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c36","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does integrating fairness into AI operations primarily involve?","o":["Using diverse data, fairness metrics, and human oversight throughout the model lifecycle","Collecting the maximum possible amount of training data","Focusing solely on data accuracy to eliminate errors","Improving computational efficiency and processing speed"],"a":[0],"e":"Fairness in AI requires a multi-faceted approach: diverse training data prevents group under-representation, fairness metrics detect biased outcomes, and human oversight catches problems models miss.","w":["Fairness requires diverse and representative data (to prevent bias from imbalance), fairness metrics (to measure outcomes across groups), and human review (to catch harmful patterns).","More data does not guarantee fairness. Large datasets can amplify existing societal biases if they are not carefully curated.","Data accuracy is important but does not address representation fairness or equitable outcomes across demographic groups.","Computational efficiency is a performance concern unrelated to fairness in AI decision-making."]},{"id":"gh-300-c37","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the key benefit of custom models available in GitHub Copilot Enterprise?","o":["They provide faster internet connections for developers","Suggestions are tailored to the organization's specific codebase, conventions, and domain","They automatically reduce licensing costs","They enable automatic code deployment to production"],"a":[1],"e":"Custom models in Copilot Enterprise are fine-tuned on an organization's own codebase, making suggestions more accurate, idiomatic, and aligned with internal standards and proprietary patterns.","w":["Custom models are about AI quality, not network performance.","Fine-tuned models understand the organization's specific libraries, naming conventions, and architectural patterns, dramatically improving suggestion relevance.","Custom models are a premium feature that may add cost, not reduce it.","Deployment automation is handled by CI/CD tools, not AI models."]},{"id":"gh-300-c38","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Chat help developers verify the behavior of a function?","o":["By generating assertions and unit tests for the specific function","By executing the function in a sandboxed environment","By comparing the function to similar functions in online repositories","By running a static type checker against the function"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot Chat can generate targeted unit tests and assertions for a function, allowing developers to verify expected behavior across different inputs and edge cases.","w":["Using the /tests command or a natural language request, Copilot can generate unit tests with assertions that verify function inputs, outputs, and edge cases.","Copilot does not execute code in a sandbox. It generates code suggestions; execution is done by developer-run tests.","Copilot does not search online repositories to compare functions at runtime.","Running type checkers is a separate IDE/toolchain function. Copilot can suggest typed code but does not run type checking itself."]},{"id":"gh-300-c39","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can developers improve the context available to GitHub Copilot when working in the IDE? (Choose two)","o":["Keep relevant related files open in additional editor tabs","Write clear and descriptive comments and docstrings above code","Use only one file per project to centralize context","Disable other IDE plugins to reduce interference"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot uses open tabs and code comments as primary context signals. More relevant open files and descriptive comments directly improve the relevance of suggestions.","w":["Copilot scans open files/tabs for context. Having related files open (e.g., models when writing controllers) provides richer, more relevant suggestions.","Comments and docstrings serve as inline instructions. Detailed comments describing intent help Copilot understand what code needs to do.","Using a single file limits context. In real projects, spreading code across files is better for organization, and having relevant files open helps Copilot understand the broader codebase.","Other IDE plugins generally do not interfere with Copilot's context gathering."]},{"id":"gh-300-c40","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot CLI generate shell command suggestions?","o":["By analyzing system log files on the developer's machine","By interpreting natural language descriptions of what the developer wants to do","By running a local AI model on the developer's machine","By reading shell command history from a configuration file"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot CLI accepts natural language input (e.g., 'find all .log files modified in the last 24 hours') and translates it into the appropriate shell command using the AI model.","w":["Copilot CLI does not analyze system logs to generate commands.","The core function of Copilot CLI is natural language to shell command translation — developers describe what they want in plain English, and Copilot suggests the command.","Copilot CLI is cloud-based; it does not run a local AI model.","Shell command history may provide minor context but is not the mechanism for generating suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c41","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does the /tests slash command do in GitHub Copilot Chat?","o":["Opens the test runner panel in the IDE","Automatically generates unit tests for the selected code","Integrates with testing frameworks and configures them automatically","Executes all existing test files in the project"],"a":[1],"e":"The /tests slash command instructs Copilot Chat to generate unit tests for the currently selected code, helping developers quickly create test coverage for existing functions.","w":["The /tests command does not interact with the IDE test runner panel or UI.","/tests generates unit test code for selected/active code. It helps quickly create test coverage and validate expected behavior.","/tests generates test code only; it does not configure or integrate testing frameworks.","Copilot Chat cannot execute code. /tests generates new test code, it does not run existing tests."]},{"id":"gh-300-c42","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can developers get inline refactoring suggestions from GitHub Copilot in the IDE? (Choose two)","o":["Add descriptive comments above the code describing the desired improvement","Use the /fix command in Copilot Chat","Open the integrated terminal and type a refactoring command","Use gh copilot fix in the GitHub CLI","Highlight the code, right-click, and select 'Refactor with GitHub Copilot'"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Inline refactoring is triggered either by writing comments describing intended improvements (Copilot autocompletes based on them) or by selecting code and using the right-click context menu.","w":["Writing a comment like '// Refactor to use async/await instead of callbacks' above code triggers Copilot to suggest an inline refactoring.","/fix in Copilot Chat is for fixing bugs or errors, not structural refactoring.","The terminal does not provide inline IDE code suggestions.","gh copilot fix is a CLI tool for command-line issues, not for IDE inline refactoring.","In VS Code, highlighting code and right-clicking reveals a 'Refactor with GitHub Copilot' option that triggers Copilot to suggest structural improvements."]},{"id":"gh-300-c43","d":"github-copilot","x":"What are the key limitations of GitHub Copilot Chat that developers should be aware of? (Choose two)","o":["Limited training data may result in knowledge gaps for niche domains","It cannot handle any complex code structures","AI systems have no biases in their outputs","Not all programming languages are fully supported with equal quality"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Copilot Chat is limited by its training data (which may lack coverage of niche domains or newer technologies) and performs inconsistently across languages (stronger with popular ones like Python/JS).","w":["Copilot is trained on a finite dataset. For niche frameworks, proprietary systems, or very recent technologies, its knowledge may be limited or outdated.","Handling complex code is actually a strength of Copilot, not a limitation — though it may not fully understand entire system architectures.","AI models can and do reflect biases from their training data. Claiming 'no biases' is incorrect.","Copilot works best with widely-used languages (Python, JavaScript, TypeScript). Less common languages receive weaker suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c44","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot use chat history within a session?","o":["It retrains the underlying AI model in real time with your chat","It uses previous interactions as context to provide more relevant and continuous suggestions","It shares your chat history with other Copilot users to improve collective knowledge","Chat history has no effect on subsequent suggestions within the same session"],"a":[1],"e":"Within a session, Copilot maintains the previous conversation as context, allowing it to build on prior instructions, maintain conventions established earlier, and provide coherent, continuous assistance.","w":["Copilot does not retrain in real time. The model is static; only the context window changes.","Chat history informs Copilot about what has already been discussed, so it can maintain continuity, respect earlier constraints, and give suggestions that fit the ongoing conversation.","Chat history is private and never shared with other users.","Chat history actively improves suggestion quality by providing accumulated context from the session."]},{"id":"gh-300-c45","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does the GitHub Copilot Usage Metrics API provide to organizations? (Choose two)","o":["Evaluations of individual code quality improvements","Tracks how many Copilot suggestions are accepted across the organization","Automatically refactors low-quality code","Reviews coding style and standards compliance","Provides Copilot Chat-specific acceptance and usage metrics"],"a":[1,4],"e":"The Metrics API is an analytics tool for understanding Copilot adoption. It tracks suggestion acceptance rates (showing usefulness) and Chat-specific metrics (showing conversational AI usage patterns).","w":["The API tracks adoption metrics, not internal code quality assessments.","The API shows how many and what percentage of Copilot suggestions were accepted across the organization — key for measuring ROI.","The Metrics API is read-only analytics; it does not modify code.","Coding style compliance is handled by linters (ESLint, Pylint) — not the Copilot Metrics API.","The API provides Chat-specific data including acceptance rates for chat suggestions, helping organizations understand conversational AI adoption."]},{"id":"gh-300-c46","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is a supported way to interact with GitHub Copilot outside of a local IDE?","o":["Using a properly configured GitHub CLI as the sole interface","Using Copilot Chat capabilities built into Neovim only","Through an IDE debug watch window","Via a web browser at github.com/copilot"],"a":[3],"e":"GitHub Copilot can be accessed through its web interface at github.com, allowing developers to use Copilot Chat and assistance features directly in the browser without a local IDE.","w":["GitHub CLI alone is not a Copilot interface; Copilot CLI is a specific extension — but 'outside of IDE' most directly points to the web interface.","While Copilot does have Neovim support, it is not the primary 'outside IDE' interaction method described here.","IDE debug watch windows are debugging tools, not Copilot interaction points.","GitHub provides a web-based Copilot Chat interface accessible at github.com, usable directly in any web browser without installing an IDE."]},{"id":"gh-300-c47","d":"github-copilot","x":"How long does it typically take for content exclusion changes to propagate and take effect in GitHub Copilot?","o":["Up to 30 minutes","Immediately upon saving","60-90 minutes","Up to 24 hours"],"a":[0],"e":"After configuring or updating content exclusion rules, GitHub Copilot requires up to 30 minutes for the changes to propagate across all systems and users in the organization.","w":["GitHub's documentation states that content exclusion changes take up to 30 minutes to propagate throughout the system.","Changes are not instantaneous — there is a system propagation delay of up to 30 minutes.","60-90 minutes exceeds the documented propagation window.","A 24-hour window is far longer than the actual up-to-30-minute propagation time."]},{"id":"gh-300-c48","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot generate context-aware code suggestions in the IDE?","o":["Through a drag-and-drop interface for code components","By acting autonomously without any developer input","By analyzing the current file, surrounding code, and comments to complete or suggest code","By suggesting only function and variable names based on IntelliSense"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot analyzes the immediate code context — what's in the current file, the cursor position, comments, and nearby code patterns — to generate relevant completions and suggestions.","w":["Copilot is not a drag-and-drop tool. It integrates into the editor as a text-based autocomplete and chat assistant.","Copilot always requires developer input and approval. It suggests; developers decide.","Copilot's core mechanism is analyzing the full context of the open file — including code structure, comments, and cursor position — to generate completions.","While traditional IntelliSense suggests names, Copilot goes far beyond to suggest entire functions, algorithms, and logic blocks."]},{"id":"gh-300-c49","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the primary purpose of GitHub Copilot?","o":["Managing GitHub repositories and branch protection rules","Helping developers write code faster with AI-powered suggestions","Automating software testing and deployment pipelines","Managing collaborative coding sessions and project timelines"],"a":[1],"e":"GitHub Copilot is an AI-powered coding assistant whose primary purpose is to accelerate developer productivity by generating contextually relevant code suggestions.","w":["Repository management (branch protection, settings, collaborators) is a core GitHub platform function, not Copilot's purpose.","Copilot's primary purpose is to augment developer productivity by providing intelligent code suggestions, completions, and chat assistance.","Automated testing and deployment are handled by GitHub Actions and CI/CD pipelines, not Copilot.","Collaborative project management is handled by GitHub Issues, Projects, and similar tools — not Copilot."]},{"id":"gh-300-c50","d":"github-copilot","x":"What are the practical limitations of GitHub Copilot that all developers should understand? (Choose two)","o":["Copilot does not integrate with Visual Studio Code","Suggestions may sometimes be outdated or include security vulnerabilities","Copilot only supports a few programming languages","Copilot requires an active internet connection to generate suggestions"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Developers must know that Copilot's training data may contain outdated/insecure patterns (requiring review) and that the service requires continuous internet access to function.","w":["VS Code is the primary and most featured IDE for GitHub Copilot.","Because Copilot learns from patterns in public code (which may include old or vulnerable code), it may suggest outdated libraries or insecure patterns.","Copilot supports a wide range of programming languages, though it performs best with popular ones.","Copilot's AI model runs in the cloud. Without an internet connection, the IDE cannot connect to the model and suggestions will not appear."]},{"id":"gh-300-c51","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is a key recent improvement in GitHub Copilot's contextual awareness?","o":["Copilot now supports all programming languages with uniform quality","Copilot can now use related files across the project for context, not just the current file","Copilot now includes real-time collaborative editing features","Copilot automatically debugs and fixes all generated code"],"a":[1],"e":"A significant Copilot improvement is its ability to reference multiple open files and project-wide context, allowing it to generate suggestions that are consistent with the broader codebase.","w":["Copilot still performs better with popular languages than with rare or niche ones.","Previously limited to the current file, Copilot can now incorporate context from related open files, making suggestions more aligned with the project's existing conventions and architecture.","Real-time collaborative editing is a feature of tools like Visual Studio Live Share, not Copilot.","Copilot suggests code but does not automatically debug or guarantee the correctness of its own output."]},{"id":"gh-300-c52","d":"github-copilot","x":"How do docstrings improve the quality of GitHub Copilot's code suggestions?","o":["They inform Copilot which programming language to use","They provide structured context about function purpose, inputs, and outputs","They make Copilot generate code fully automatically without prompts","They serve primarily as placeholder stubs for future code"],"a":[1],"e":"Docstrings act as rich, structured context that tells Copilot exactly what a function is supposed to do, what parameters it takes, and what it should return — dramatically improving suggestion accuracy.","w":["The programming language is determined by the file extension and syntax, not by docstrings.","A well-written docstring describing a function's purpose, parameters, and return value gives Copilot the intent it needs to generate an accurate implementation.","Docstrings improve suggestions but do not remove the need for developer prompts, review, and approval.","While stubs can help, docstrings are primarily documentation — and their main Copilot benefit is providing rich contextual guidance."]},{"id":"gh-300-c53","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which Microsoft Responsible AI principle ensures that AI systems treat all people equitably and avoid discrimination?","o":["Privacy and Security","Fairness","Reliability and Safety","Inclusiveness"],"a":[1],"e":"The Fairness principle in Microsoft's Responsible AI framework ensures AI systems treat individuals and groups equitably, avoiding bias, discrimination, and disparate impact.","w":["Privacy and Security focuses on protecting personal data and preventing unauthorized access — not on equitable treatment.","Fairness ensures that AI systems provide similar outcomes for similar individuals and groups, avoiding discrimination based on race, gender, or other characteristics.","Reliability and Safety ensures consistent, predictable performance and avoidance of harmful failures — separate from equitable treatment.","Inclusiveness focuses on empowering all people through AI and ensuring accessibility, which is related to but distinct from the equitable treatment focus of Fairness."]},{"id":"gh-300-c54","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does GitHub Copilot primarily use within the IDE to determine the context for suggestions?","o":["Open files/tabs, cursor position, and selected code","All code visible on screen, read indiscriminately","The entire repository including all submodules and dependencies","The terminal's current folder location"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot gathers context from the IDE's active signals: which files are open, where the cursor is placed, and what code (if any) is selected — these drive the relevance of its suggestions.","w":["Copilot intelligently reads open tabs, cursor position, and selected code to understand what the developer is working on and generate appropriate suggestions.","Copilot does not blindly read all visible text — it uses structured signals from the IDE about open files and cursor context.","Copilot does not automatically ingest the entire repository with all submodules; it uses the files you have open.","Terminal folder location is not a primary input for Copilot's code suggestion logic."]},{"id":"gh-300-c55","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot notify developers when suggestions are blocked due to content exclusion rules?","o":["A dedicated 'Copilot Errors' panel appears in the IDE","GitHub user settings display real-time diagnostics","The suggestions window itself shows an exclusion warning","A notification appears via the Copilot icon in the editor status bar"],"a":[3],"e":"When Copilot suggestions are suppressed due to content exclusion rules, the Copilot icon in the IDE's status bar changes state to indicate that suggestions are limited or blocked.","w":["There is no dedicated 'Copilot Errors' panel in the IDE for content exclusion notifications.","GitHub user settings do not provide real-time, in-IDE diagnostics about blocked suggestions.","The suggestions window simply shows no suggestions — it does not display exclusion-specific warnings.","The Copilot status bar icon in the IDE (shown at the bottom of VS Code, for example) visually indicates when Copilot is active, restricted, or blocked by content exclusion."]},{"id":"gh-300-c56","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Individual use prompt data? (Choose two)","o":["Real-time user input (code, comments) is used to generate context-aware suggestions","Prompt data is used to improve GitHub's repository search engine","Prompt data may be used to improve the machine learning models powering Copilot","Prompt data is stored unencrypted on Microsoft's servers"],"a":[0,2],"e":"For Copilot Individual, prompts are used in real-time for suggestion generation, and (unless the user opts out) may be used to train and improve the AI models — unlike Business/Enterprise where training use is disabled.","w":["The current editing context (what you're typing, nearby comments, open files) is used in real time to generate relevant suggestions.","Copilot prompt data is used for AI model improvement only — not for GitHub's search engine.","For Individual plan users who have not opted out, GitHub may use prompt and suggestion data to improve the underlying AI models.","Data is encrypted in transit and at rest. It is not stored unencrypted."]},{"id":"gh-300-c57","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the purpose of organization audit logs in GitHub Copilot Business?","o":["To track daily code quality metrics for each developer","To assign GitHub Copilot licenses to users","To monitor code conflicts and their resolutions","To monitor administrator activities and configuration changes within the organization"],"a":[3],"e":"Audit logs in Copilot Business are used for governance and security: tracking which admins made what changes to settings, who was assigned licenses, and other administrative actions.","w":["Code quality metrics are surfaced through the Copilot Usage Metrics API and external code analysis tools, not audit logs.","License assignment is an administrative action that is recorded in the audit log, but the audit log itself does not perform assignments.","Code conflicts are managed through Git and version control tools — they do not appear in Copilot Business audit logs.","Audit logs record all significant administrative actions: settings changes, policy updates, license management, and other admin activities, enabling security and compliance reviews."]},{"id":"gh-300-c58","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is few-shot prompting in the context of GitHub Copilot and AI?","o":["Repeating the same prompt multiple times until the AI responds correctly","Providing a small number of examples in the prompt so the model learns the expected pattern","Using as few words as possible in the prompt","Specifying the exact source of information the AI should reference"],"a":[1],"e":"Few-shot prompting means including a small number of input-output examples in the prompt to teach the AI the pattern you want it to follow, resulting in more accurate and consistent responses.","w":["Repeating prompts is not few-shot prompting — it is simply retrying, which may not improve results.","Few-shot prompting provides 2-5 examples of desired behavior directly in the prompt (e.g., example inputs and outputs), helping the model infer the pattern and apply it to new inputs.","Using fewer words describes concise prompting, not few-shot prompting. Few-shot prompts are typically longer because they include examples.","Specifying information sources is part of retrieval-augmented generation or grounding, not few-shot prompting."]},{"id":"gh-300-c59","d":"github-copilot","x":"What should developers be careful about when using GitHub Copilot for math-related programming tasks?","o":["Copilot relies on pattern prediction and may not guarantee mathematical accuracy","Math code always runs faster when generated by Copilot","Copilot automatically refuses all mathematical prompts","Numbers in Copilot's output are always rounded to the nearest integer"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot generates code by pattern matching from training data, not by performing mathematical computation. It may generate plausible-looking but incorrect formulas or algorithms.","w":["Copilot predicts likely code patterns — it does not compute or verify mathematical correctness. A formula may look right syntactically but produce wrong results.","Copilot has no effect on code runtime performance — generated math code runs at the same speed as hand-written code.","Copilot does not refuse mathematical prompts; it attempts to generate relevant code.","Copilot generates code based on patterns; it does not modify numerical values in output."]},{"id":"gh-300-c60","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does the /optimize slash command do in GitHub Copilot within Microsoft Visual Studio?","o":["Converts the code to a more performant programming language","Improves and refines existing code by restructuring and cleaning it for readability and maintainability","Summarizes inline documentation into a condensed format","Analyzes runtime complexity and provides guaranteed performance improvements"],"a":[1],"e":"/optimize focuses on improving code structure, readability, and maintainability by refactoring — it does not guarantee algorithmic performance gains or convert between languages.","w":["Copilot cannot automatically convert codebases to different programming languages for performance gains.","/optimize is used to improve and refine existing code — restructuring it, improving naming, and enhancing readability — primarily style and structural improvements.","Documentation summarization is not the purpose of /optimize; that would be more aligned with /doc or /explain.","Copilot may suggest more efficient algorithms, but it does not formally analyze runtime complexity like a profiler or guarantee performance improvements."]},{"id":"gh-300-c61","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot Chat help developers address security issues in their codebase?","o":["By automatically refactoring the entire codebase to remove all vulnerabilities","By annotating its suggestions with known vulnerability patterns and suggesting safer alternatives","By enforcing organization-wide strict coding standards automatically","By generating detailed security audit reports of the entire codebase"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot acts as a security-aware coding assistant — highlighting potentially insecure patterns in suggestions, warning about common vulnerability types, and suggesting safer alternatives in real time.","w":["Copilot cannot automatically rewrite an entire codebase. It provides suggestions in context only.","Copilot can flag risky patterns (e.g., SQL injection risk, hardcoded credentials) in its suggestions and recommend safer coding alternatives as you write code.","Copilot cannot enforce organization-wide coding standards — that requires linters, code review processes, and branch protection rules.","Full security audit reports require dedicated security scanning tools (e.g., GitHub Advanced Security, Snyk). Copilot is not a security auditing tool."]},{"id":"gh-300-c62","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is a key limitation of GitHub Copilot content exclusions?","o":["They can be worked around because exclusions only fully apply to Git-tracked repositories","Repository administrators and organization owners cannot manage content exclusion settings","Content exclusions are only available in the GitHub Copilot Individual plan","Content exclusions can only be configured by enterprise-level administrators"],"a":[0],"e":"Content exclusions target Git repository paths, but content outside these (local files, pasted snippets, untracked files) can still influence suggestions, meaning exclusions are not absolute.","w":["Since exclusions apply to Git repository paths, content pasted directly into the editor, local untracked files, or snippets from other sources may still influence Copilot's context.","In Copilot Business and Enterprise, both organization owners and repository admins can manage content exclusion settings.","Content exclusions are primarily available in Copilot Business and Enterprise plans — not Individual.","Content exclusion management is available to organization owners and repo admins, not just enterprise-level administrators."]},{"id":"gh-300-c63","d":"github-copilot","x":"What types of information can be retrieved through the GitHub Copilot Business REST API? (Choose two)","o":["View individual code suggestions made to a specific user","List all GitHub Copilot seat assignments for an organization","Get a summary of GitHub Copilot usage statistics for organization members","List all users who are not subscribed to Copilot in the organization"],"a":[1,2],"e":"The Copilot Business REST API enables admins to manage seat assignments and retrieve usage summaries — key tasks for licensing management and ROI assessment.","w":["Individual code suggestions are considered transient and private — they are not accessible via the REST API.","Admins can query the API to see which users have Copilot seats assigned, enabling license management and billing control.","The API provides usage summaries for the organization, showing how actively Copilot is being used and helping assess productivity impact.","The API focuses on assigned seats and usage. A list of non-subscribers would require comparing org members against seat assignments manually."]},{"id":"gh-300-c64","d":"github-copilot","x":"A team using GitHub Copilot Individual cannot control which files Copilot uses as context. What should they do?","o":["Add sensitive files to .gitignore to prevent Copilot from reading them","Upgrade to Copilot Business or Enterprise to access content exclusion features","Manually delete sensitive files from the repository before coding","Use a third-party IDE plugin to restrict Copilot's file access"],"a":[1],"e":"Content exclusions (the ability to specify which files/repositories Copilot should not use as context) are a Copilot Business and Enterprise feature — not available in the Individual plan.","w":[".gitignore controls Git file tracking, not Copilot's context awareness. Copilot can still read .gitignore'd files if they exist in the working directory.","To gain granular control over which files Copilot uses as context, teams must upgrade to Business or Enterprise, which include content exclusion policy management.","Deleting files would destroy code and is not a practical solution for managing Copilot's context.","There is no supported third-party plugin mechanism to restrict Copilot's file access at the Individual plan level."]},{"id":"gh-300-c65","d":"github-copilot","x":"What can GitHub Copilot Chat be effectively used for within a development team? (Choose two)","o":["Writing and improving technical documentation","Replacing the team's version control system","Managing sprint timelines and project plans","Helping onboard new developers to an unfamiliar codebase"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Copilot Chat excels at documentation generation (it can write summaries, README sections, and code comments) and onboarding (answering 'what does X do?' questions about the codebase).","w":["Copilot can draft documentation, write docstrings, create README sections, and improve existing documentation quality.","Version control is handled by Git and GitHub — Copilot has no role in source control management.","Sprint and project management is done via GitHub Projects, Jira, or similar tools — not Copilot Chat.","New developers can use Copilot Chat to ask questions like 'Explain this module' or 'How does authentication work in this project?' to accelerate their understanding."]},{"id":"gh-300-c66","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot help with code refactoring tasks? (Choose two)","o":["Suggesting better naming conventions for variables and functions","Identifying duplicate code blocks and suggesting consolidated versions","Automatically deploying refactored code to the production environment","Replacing the need for human code reviews during refactoring"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot assists refactoring by improving naming (for clarity and convention compliance) and by identifying and helping consolidate repeated code patterns.","w":["Copilot can suggest clearer, more idiomatic variable and function names as part of refactoring for readability.","Copilot can identify duplicate code patterns and suggest ways to extract shared logic into reusable functions or modules.","Copilot has no capability to deploy code — deployment is handled by CI/CD pipelines.","Human code reviews remain essential. Copilot may miss business logic context or introduce new issues that reviewers must catch."]},{"id":"gh-300-c67","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which statements are true about GitHub Copilot code suggestions? (Choose two)","o":["Suggestions are always production-ready and require no further review","Copilot generates entirely original code with no relation to training data","Suggestions are guaranteed to be free of security vulnerabilities","Suggestions may reflect patterns and conventions from public repositories","Developers should always review and test generated code before using it"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Copilot learns from public code patterns (so suggestions reflect common practices in public repos) and all suggestions must be reviewed and tested before production use.","w":["Copilot suggestions may contain bugs, security issues, or outdated patterns — they are never unconditionally production-ready.","Copilot generates code based on patterns learned from training data, which includes public repositories.","Copilot may suggest code with security vulnerabilities. Developers must always review for security issues.","Copilot's training data consists largely of public code, so suggestions naturally reflect the coding patterns, idioms, and conventions found in public repositories.","This is a fundamental best practice: always review, test, and validate Copilot suggestions before committing or deploying them."]},{"id":"gh-300-c68","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which is a TRUE limitation of GitHub Copilot when working with large projects?","o":["Copilot cannot work inside Visual Studio Code","Copilot only supports Python and JavaScript","Copilot requires a dedicated GPU to function","Copilot has limited context awareness in very large codebases"],"a":[3],"e":"Copilot uses a context window that spans open files and nearby code. In very large codebases, it cannot comprehend the entire project structure simultaneously, which can lead to suggestions that don't fit the broader architecture.","w":["VS Code is Copilot's primary and most feature-rich IDE environment.","Copilot supports dozens of programming languages, not just Python and JavaScript.","Copilot runs in the cloud — it does not require a GPU on the developer's machine.","Because Copilot works within a context window based on open files, it cannot 'understand' an entire large codebase at once. This leads to suggestions that may be technically valid but architecturally inconsistent with the broader project."]},{"id":"gh-300-c69","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the purpose of the duplication detection filter in GitHub Copilot?","o":["To prevent developers from committing the same code twice","To block all suggestions that are longer than 10 lines of code","To check for code style consistency across the project","To detect and block suggestions that closely match approximately 150 or more characters of publicly available code"],"a":[3],"e":"The duplication detection filter specifically targets verbatim or near-verbatim reproduction of public code, blocking suggestions that match ~150+ characters against an index of public repositories to reduce IP and licensing risks.","w":["Preventing duplicate commits is a Git workflow concern; the duplication filter is about matching public repository code in suggestions.","There is no line-length threshold for blocking suggestions. The filter is based on similarity to public code, not suggestion length.","Code style consistency is handled by linters and formatters — not the duplication detection filter.","The filter compares Copilot suggestions against indexed public code and blocks those matching approximately 150 or more characters, reducing the risk of copying licensed or copyrighted code."]},{"id":"gh-300-c70","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot reduce the time developers spend writing boilerplate code?","o":["By detecting and deleting all identified boilerplate code blocks automatically","By suggesting reusable code snippets, patterns, and templates based on context","By automatically refactoring the entire codebase to remove repetition","By restricting developers to use only pre-approved templates"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot recognizes when developers are writing boilerplate (e.g., constructors, CRUD operations, test setup) and suggests complete, contextually appropriate snippets, dramatically reducing manual typing.","w":["Copilot does not delete existing code. It only generates suggestions.","When context indicates boilerplate is needed (e.g., a class declaration, a route handler), Copilot suggests the entire pattern, saving developers from typing repetitive code.","Copilot does not automatically refactor codebases. It suggests changes inline, which developers then accept or modify.","Copilot does not restrict developers to templates — it suggests patterns while leaving full control with the developer."]},{"id":"gh-300-c71","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can developers use GitHub Copilot Chat context features to provide better context for queries? (Choose two)","o":["Use @workspace to reference the entire project workspace in the chat","Use /clear to refresh Copilot's understanding of the project","Use @terminal to redirect Copilot responses to the integrated terminal","Use #file or #editor to reference specific files within the chat"],"a":[0,3],"e":"@workspace includes the full project context in the chat conversation, while #file and #editor allow precise targeting of specific source files as context for Copilot's response.","w":["The @workspace participant in Copilot Chat causes Copilot to consider the entire project structure when generating its response, providing broader context.","/clear resets the chat session history — it does not refresh Copilot's project understanding.","@terminal is used to interact with the terminal context (run commands, explain terminal output) — not to redirect responses to the terminal.","Using #file:<filename> or #editor references specific files or the current editor content, allowing developers to ask targeted questions about a specific source file."]},{"id":"gh-300-c72","d":"github-copilot","x":"How is GitHub Copilot Individual available for qualifying open source projects?","o":["Available for free to all GitHub users without any conditions","Available at a 50% discount with a verified open source project","Requires enterprise-level sponsorship to access for free","Free for qualifying open source project maintainers who meet GitHub's criteria"],"a":[3],"e":"GitHub offers Copilot Individual for free to maintainers of popular open source projects that meet specific criteria, as part of GitHub's commitment to supporting the open source community.","w":["Copilot Individual is not free for all GitHub users — it requires a subscription or qualification under the open source program.","There is no 50% discount tier — eligible open source maintainers get it free under the qualifying program.","Enterprise sponsorship is not required for open source maintainers to qualify for the free tier.","GitHub provides free access to Copilot Individual for maintainers of popular open source repositories that meet GitHub's qualifying criteria."]},{"id":"gh-300-c73","d":"github-copilot","x":"In which scenarios might GitHub Copilot content exclusions NOT work as expected? (Choose two)","o":["When the excluded file is stored in a public repository","When a developer manually pastes content from an excluded file into the editor","When code in non-excluded files imports or calls functions from excluded files","When using VS Code as the IDE"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Content exclusions are path-based and apply to file context. They can be bypassed when excluded content is pasted directly (bypassing file path detection) or when non-excluded files reference excluded code (which may bring excluded logic into context indirectly).","w":["Whether the excluded file is public or private does not affect how content exclusions work — they apply based on path, not visibility.","If a developer copies content from an excluded file and pastes it directly into the editor as text, the path-based exclusion no longer applies and Copilot will use that pasted content as context.","If a non-excluded file imports or calls functions from an excluded file, the reference may carry some of the excluded code's context into Copilot's awareness.","Content exclusions work in VS Code and other supported IDEs — the IDE choice does not affect exclusion behavior."]},{"id":"gh-300-c74","d":"github-copilot","x":"When might excluded content still influence GitHub Copilot's suggestions?","o":["When the excluded file was recently modified","When using Copilot Business instead of Individual","When the IDE is restarted after configuring exclusions","When function calls in non-excluded files reference code from excluded files"],"a":[3],"e":"If a file that is not excluded contains imports or function calls pointing to excluded code, Copilot may use those references as context, indirectly incorporating excluded content into its suggestions.","w":["The recency of a file modification does not affect how content exclusions are applied.","Both Copilot Business and Enterprise support content exclusions with equivalent propagation behavior.","Restarting the IDE does not cause previously excluded content to re-enter Copilot's context.","Function calls or imports in non-excluded files that reference excluded code can indirectly expose the excluded logic to Copilot's context, because Copilot reads the non-excluded file's references."]},{"id":"gh-300-c75","d":"github-copilot","x":"What detailed insights does the GitHub Copilot Metrics API provide to organizations? (Choose two)","o":["Individual developer code quality scores","The total number of repositories scanned by Copilot","Language-specific suggestion acceptance rates","Active user counts per day and per week"],"a":[2,3],"e":"The Metrics API provides fine-grained adoption data including per-language acceptance rates (showing where Copilot is most useful) and active user trends over time.","w":["The API provides organizational adoption metrics, not individual developer quality assessments.","Repository scanning is not a metric provided by the Copilot Metrics API.","The API breaks down suggestion acceptance rates by programming language, helping organizations understand where Copilot delivers the most value.","The API provides daily and weekly active user counts, showing adoption trends and engagement patterns over time."]},{"id":"gh-300-c76","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot plans ensure that user prompts and code are NOT used to train the AI model? (Choose two)","o":["Free","Business","Individual","Enterprise"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Copilot Business and Enterprise explicitly guarantee that user prompts, suggestions, and code are never retained for or used in model training — a critical data privacy protection for organizations.","w":["The Free plan does not provide the same enterprise-level data privacy guarantees.","Copilot Business's key privacy commitment is that user code and prompts are not retained or used for training AI models.","Individual plan users can opt out of training data use, but it is not guaranteed by default as it is with Business and Enterprise.","Copilot Enterprise also explicitly guarantees that customer prompts and code are not used for model training."]},{"id":"gh-300-c77","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot use previous chat interactions to improve code completions?","o":["It maintains the conversation context to provide suggestions consistent with earlier instructions","It retrains the underlying model using your conversation history in real time","It shares your conversation history with other Copilot users to improve collective suggestions","Previous chat interactions have no effect on subsequent suggestions"],"a":[0],"e":"Within a chat session, Copilot includes prior conversation turns in its context window, allowing it to maintain continuity, respect previously established constraints, and build on earlier exchanges.","w":["Copilot uses the conversation history as context in its context window, so it can remember preferences, constraints, and code patterns discussed earlier in the session.","The model does not retrain in real time. Conversation context is window-based, not learning-based.","Chat history is completely private and is never shared with other users.","Chat history actively influences suggestion quality by informing Copilot about what has been discussed and decided."]},{"id":"gh-300-c78","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot Enterprise features are consistently available across all supported IDEs? (Choose two)","o":["Organization knowledge base access","GitHub Copilot Chat","Inline code suggestions (autocomplete)","Custom fine-tuned organizational models","Enterprise audit log exports"],"a":[1,2],"e":"GitHub Copilot Chat and inline code suggestions are the core features available across all supported IDEs. Some advanced Enterprise features may have varying IDE support.","w":["Knowledge base access has limited IDE support and is primarily available through the GitHub.com interface.","Copilot Chat is available across VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains, and other supported IDEs.","Inline code suggestions (autocomplete as you type) are the foundational feature available in all Copilot-supported IDEs.","Custom fine-tuned models may have limited or varying availability depending on the IDE.","Audit log exports are a GitHub web-based administrative feature, not an IDE feature."]},{"id":"gh-300-c79","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot help minimize context switching for developers?","o":["By keeping developers in the IDE to get code help, explanations, and suggestions without switching applications","By automatically switching between different applications on behalf of the developer","By sending code-related notifications to a centralized external dashboard","By integrating with external project management tools to update task status automatically"],"a":[0],"e":"Context switching is costly for developers. Copilot reduces it by providing coding help, answers, documentation, and code generation directly within the IDE — eliminating the need to search Stack Overflow, browse docs, or open other tools.","w":["With Copilot Chat available inside the IDE, developers can get answers to coding questions, generate code, and understand unfamiliar code without ever leaving their editor.","Copilot does not control or switch between applications — it is an IDE-embedded assistant.","Copilot does not send notifications to external dashboards.","Copilot has no integration with project management tools for task status updates."]},{"id":"gh-300-c80","d":"github-copilot","x":"What can GitHub Copilot Chat do for developers directly within the IDE? (Choose two)","o":["Explain what a selected piece of code does in natural language","Deploy code changes directly to a cloud environment","Generate unit tests for a selected function or class","Manage pull request merges and branch deletions"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Within the IDE, Copilot Chat can explain code (using the /explain command or natural language) and generate unit tests (using /tests), both without leaving the editor.","w":["Developers can select code and ask Copilot Chat to explain what it does, making it easy to understand unfamiliar code without switching to documentation.","Code deployment is handled by CI/CD tools like GitHub Actions — Copilot Chat has no deployment capability.","Using /tests or requesting test generation in natural language, Copilot Chat produces unit test code for functions, accelerating test coverage.","Pull request management requires the GitHub web interface or GitHub CLI — Copilot Chat does not manage repository operations."]},{"id":"gh-300-c81","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the recommended best practice when prompting GitHub Copilot for higher-quality results?","o":["Provide clear examples of expected input and output in the prompt","Use only single-word prompts to keep things simple","Avoid adding any context or constraints to prompts","Ask multiple unrelated questions in a single prompt"],"a":[0],"e":"Providing concrete examples of expected input/output (few-shot style) is the most effective prompting technique — it shows Copilot the precise pattern you want it to replicate.","w":["Including examples directly shows Copilot the format, style, and content you expect, leading to much more accurate and consistent suggestions.","Single-word prompts are too vague and leave too much ambiguity for Copilot to produce useful results.","Context and constraints are essential for quality suggestions. Stripping them out reduces Copilot's ability to generate relevant code.","Combining multiple unrelated questions confuses context and reduces the relevance of suggestions for any individual question."]},{"id":"gh-300-c82","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the main role of pre-processing in GitHub Copilot Chat before a query is sent to the AI model?","o":["Filtering offensive content before it reaches the model","Sending the data to GitHub's central analytics servers","Compressing the prompt to reduce network bandwidth usage","Adding relevant context from the IDE environment to the prompt"],"a":[3],"e":"Pre-processing enriches the raw prompt with additional context gathered from the IDE — open files, selected code, cursor position, language — before sending it to the AI model to improve response quality.","w":["Content filtering does occur, but it is a separate pipeline step. The primary role of pre-processing is context augmentation, not filtering.","Pre-processing is not about analytics data collection; it is about enriching the AI prompt.","Prompt compression is not a described feature of Copilot's pre-processing pipeline.","Pre-processing is primarily responsible for gathering context signals from the IDE (open tabs, selected code, file type, project structure) and appending them to the user's prompt to help the AI model generate more relevant responses."]},{"id":"gh-300-c83","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which sources can be specified in GitHub Copilot content exclusions? (Choose three)","o":["Individual lines of code within a file","Entire repositories","Specific files within a repository","Individual user accounts","Folders and directory paths within a repository"],"a":[1,2,4],"e":"Content exclusions support repository-level, file-level, and folder/directory-level exclusions using glob patterns. Individual lines and users cannot be specified as exclusion targets.","w":["Content exclusions work at the file, folder, or repository granularity — not at the individual line level.","You can exclude entire repositories by specifying the repository path in the content exclusion settings.","Specific files can be excluded using glob patterns such as **/secrets.yaml or src/config.ts.","Content exclusions apply to code sources (files, folders, repos), not to specific user accounts.","Entire directory paths can be excluded, such as src/private/** to exclude all files under the private directory."]},{"id":"gh-300-c84","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the key limitation when using GitHub Copilot for large-scale code refactoring tasks?","o":["Copilot cannot read files larger than 1MB","Copilot may not understand the full business logic and architectural context behind the code","Copilot can only refactor Python and JavaScript","Refactoring suggestions require a GitHub Enterprise license"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot works within a limited context window and does not have complete knowledge of business rules, historical architectural decisions, or cross-system dependencies — critical factors in large-scale refactoring.","w":["There is no such specific file size limit for Copilot's context reading.","Large-scale refactoring requires understanding the entire system's intent, historical decisions, and business rules — context that Copilot's training data and limited window cannot fully capture.","Copilot supports refactoring across many languages; it is not limited to Python and JavaScript.","Refactoring suggestions are available in all Copilot plans, not just Enterprise."]},{"id":"gh-300-c85","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the most important factor for successfully identifying missing tests using GitHub Copilot?","o":["The total number of existing tests in the project","The programming language being used","Ensuring the correct and relevant context is available to Copilot","The overall size of the codebase"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot identifies missing tests most effectively when it has access to the function being tested, the existing test cases, and any relevant code context — without sufficient context it cannot accurately identify gaps.","w":["The number of existing tests alone does not help Copilot identify what is missing without understanding the function's requirements.","While language affects suggestion quality, the availability of context is the more fundamental factor for test gap identification.","Providing Copilot with the function code, existing test cases, and any relevant documentation or comments gives it the context needed to identify what edge cases or behaviors are not yet tested.","Codebase size is a factor in Copilot's overall performance but is not the primary driver for identifying specific missing tests."]},{"id":"gh-300-c86","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the best way to promote transparency when GitHub Copilot applies AI content filtering?","o":["Hide all AI filtering decisions from users to avoid confusion","Clearly communicate to users when and why content is filtered or blocked","Disable content filtering for advanced or senior developers","Apply content filtering only in enterprise pricing tiers"],"a":[1],"e":"Transparency in AI systems means users should be informed when filtering decisions are made — this builds trust, enables users to adjust their prompts, and upholds Microsoft's Responsible AI principles.","w":["Hiding filtering decisions contradicts the principle of transparency and erodes user trust in the AI system.","Informing users when content is filtered (and ideally why) promotes transparency, helps users understand system behavior, and enables them to reformulate requests appropriately.","Content filtering applies to all users regardless of experience level — bypassing it for certain users would create inconsistency and potential harm.","Content filtering is applied across all Copilot plans as a safety measure, not limited to enterprise tiers."]},{"id":"gh-300-c87","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is Zero-Shot Prompting in the context of AI and GitHub Copilot?","o":["Giving examples of the algorithm and its expected outcome","Asking the AI to perform a task without providing any examples — relying solely on its training","Giving examples of the problem context before stating the question","Providing as little context as possible in the prompt","Telling the AI to show only the correct answer without explanation"],"a":[1],"e":"Zero-shot prompting means giving the AI a direct instruction or question with no examples — the model uses its training knowledge alone to generate a response, without being shown sample input/output pairs.","w":["Providing examples of an algorithm and outcome is few-shot prompting, not zero-shot.","Zero-shot means 'zero examples provided.' The AI must rely entirely on its pre-trained knowledge to understand and complete the task.","Giving examples of the problem context before the question is a form of few-shot prompting.","While zero-shot prompts can be concise, 'providing as little context as possible' is not the definition — clear instructions can still be zero-shot if no examples are given.","Specifying output constraints is not a prompting style — it is an output format instruction."]},{"id":"gh-300-c88","d":"github-copilot","x":"How should developers make GitHub Copilot Chat follow company-specific testing standards when generating tests?","o":["Use a specific slash command to activate company standards mode","Configure standards via a terminal command before using Copilot","Set testing standard preferences in Copilot's configuration settings","Provide examples of the expected test format and style directly in the prompt"],"a":[3],"e":"This is an application of few-shot prompting — by including examples of your company's testing style directly in the prompt, Copilot learns the pattern and replicates it in generated tests.","w":["There is no slash command to activate company-specific testing standards. Slash commands control actions, not stylistic standards.","Terminal commands cannot configure Copilot's stylistic output for test generation.","Copilot's configuration settings control features and access, not writing style or testing conventions.","The most effective approach is to include example tests matching your company's standards in the prompt. Copilot will then mirror those patterns (framework, naming conventions, assertion style) in its generated tests."]},{"id":"gh-300-c89","d":"github-copilot","x":"At which administrative levels can duplicate detection policies for GitHub Copilot be configured? (Choose two)","o":["Individual user settings","Organization settings","Repository settings","Enterprise settings"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Duplicate detection policies are centralized governance controls configured at the Organization level (for all repos within an org) and Enterprise level (for company-wide policy across multiple organizations).","w":["Individual users cannot configure organization-wide policy controls like duplicate detection — these are administrative settings.","Organization owners can configure duplicate detection policies in Organization Settings → Copilot → Policies, applying the control to all repositories in the organization.","Copilot policies like duplicate detection are not configurable per individual repository — they are set at org or enterprise level.","Enterprise administrators can configure duplicate detection policies at the Enterprise Settings → Copilot → Policies level, governing all organizations within the enterprise."]},{"id":"gh-300-c90","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can GitHub Copilot assist developers during the design phase of the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)?","o":["By generating complete UI prototypes automatically without any prompts","By suggesting design patterns and best practices relevant to the project","By independently creating a complete software architecture design","By managing design team collaboration and version control workflows"],"a":[1],"e":"During design, Copilot can recommend appropriate design patterns (MVC, Singleton, Factory, etc.) and architectural best practices based on the project context — helping developers make informed design decisions faster.","w":["Copilot requires developer prompts and cannot autonomously generate full UI prototypes.","Copilot analyzes existing code and context to suggest relevant design patterns, clean code approaches, and architectural styles that fit the project's needs.","Copilot assists humans; it cannot independently create a complete software design. Architects must provide direction and make final decisions.","Design team collaboration and version control are managed by GitHub, Confluence, Jira, and similar platforms — not Copilot."]},{"id":"gh-300-c91","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which checks are applied before GitHub Copilot displays its responses to users? (Choose two)","o":["Compatibility with user-specific and organizational policy settings","Automated code quality analysis and grading","Verification that suggestions do not match publicly available code (when the filter is enabled)","Performance benchmarking of generated code"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Before showing suggestions, Copilot checks: (1) whether the suggestion complies with admin-configured policies (content settings, enabled features), and (2) whether it closely matches public code when the duplication filter is enabled.","w":["Copilot respects organization and user-level policy configurations — if certain features are disabled or content filters are configured, those settings are applied before suggestions are displayed.","Copilot does not run formal code quality analysis (like Sonar or CodeClimate) before displaying suggestions.","When the duplicate detection filter is enabled, Copilot checks suggestions against an index of public code and suppresses matches before showing them.","Performance benchmarking requires code execution, which Copilot cannot perform before displaying suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c92","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot help developers write documentation?","o":["By suggesting summaries, descriptions, and docstrings based on the code's functionality","By only generating documentation in Markdown format","By automatically generating complete, production-ready documentation without human review","Copilot cannot assist with documentation — it only generates executable code"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot analyzes the existing code and generates relevant documentation: function summaries, parameter descriptions, return value notes, README sections, and inline comments — saving significant time.","w":["Copilot can analyze what code does and generate meaningful documentation including function summaries, docstrings, inline comments, and README content.","Copilot can generate documentation in many formats: Markdown, JSDoc, Python docstrings, XML comments, plain text descriptions, etc.","While Copilot generates documentation, human review and refinement are always required. It does not guarantee complete or production-ready documentation.","Documentation generation is one of Copilot's common and explicitly supported use cases."]},{"id":"gh-300-c93","d":"github-copilot","x":"How can developers provide feedback about GitHub Copilot Chat directly from within their IDE?","o":["By emailing the GitHub support team","By using the 'Share Feedback' button in the Copilot Chat panel","By filling out a form on the GitHub website","By posting feedback in GitHub Community discussion forums"],"a":[1],"e":"The 'Share Feedback' button in the Copilot Chat panel provides a direct, in-context feedback mechanism that links feedback to the actual Copilot session, giving GitHub better context about the issue.","w":["Emailing support is for general account or billing issues — not the designated channel for Copilot Chat in-session feedback.","The 'Share Feedback' button directly within the Copilot Chat panel sends feedback to GitHub tied to the current session context, making it the standard and most effective in-IDE feedback method.","The GitHub website form is not the standard workflow for providing feedback during an IDE session.","Community forums are public discussion spaces — not the direct product feedback channel for Copilot Chat sessions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c94","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the recommended best practice when using GitHub Copilot Chat code suggestions?","o":["Rely solely on Copilot's suggestions without further review","Regularly review and refactor the code suggested by Copilot","Disable Copilot's inline suggestions to use only Chat mode","Avoid using Copilot for any complex or critical code tasks"],"a":[1],"e":"Since Copilot is an AI assistant (not a replacement for developer judgment), its suggestions should always be reviewed, tested, and refactored as needed to meet quality, security, and project standards.","w":["Sole reliance without review risks bugs, architectural misalignment, and security vulnerabilities entering the codebase.","Regular review and refactoring of Copilot suggestions ensures code quality, catches errors, improves readability, and maintains consistency with the project's architecture.","Disabling inline suggestions reduces productivity. Both inline and Chat modes should be used together for maximum effectiveness.","Copilot can be valuable for complex tasks when used with appropriate review and testing — avoiding it entirely for complex work negates significant productivity benefits."]},{"id":"gh-300-c95","d":"github-copilot","x":"What are the key limitations of GitHub Copilot Chat? (Choose two)","o":["May struggle with large codebases and deep architectural dependencies","Limited training data means it may lack knowledge of niche frameworks or recent technologies","Extensive support for all programming languages is a notable limitation","Copilot Chat has absolutely no biases in its code suggestions"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Copilot has a finite context window (limiting large codebase comprehension) and a training data cutoff (meaning it may lack knowledge of very new or niche technologies).","w":["Copilot cannot fully understand large, complex systems with deep interdependencies — its context window limits how much of the system it can consider at once.","Copilot's training data has a cutoff date and may under-represent niche frameworks, proprietary libraries, or recently released technologies.","Multi-language support is a strength of Copilot, not a limitation.","AI models can and do reflect biases from their training data. Claiming zero biases is incorrect and dangerous."]},{"id":"gh-300-c96","d":"github-copilot","x":"What happens when GitHub Copilot's training data includes deprecated syntax or outdated code patterns?","o":["Copilot always automatically filters out deprecated code elements","Copilot may suggest the deprecated syntax or patterns if they appear in training data","Copilot rejects all prompts that involve deprecated features","Copilot automatically updates deprecated syntax to the latest standards"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot generates suggestions based on patterns it learned from training data. If deprecated patterns were common in that data, Copilot may reproduce them — requiring developers to verify suggestions are current.","w":["Copilot does not have a comprehensive, constantly-updated filter for all deprecated APIs and patterns across all languages.","If a deprecated API or pattern was widely used in the training data, Copilot may suggest it since it cannot fully verify whether features are current or outdated.","Copilot does not refuse prompts based on whether they involve deprecated features — it attempts to generate helpful code.","Copilot does not reliably update deprecated code to modern standards. This must be done through developer review and explicit refactoring prompts."]},{"id":"gh-300-c97","d":"github-copilot","x":"What type of code does GitHub Copilot most frequently suggest?","o":["Completely novel and innovative project architectures never seen before","Experimental solutions that are not yet widely adopted","Highly uniform, homogeneous solutions regardless of project context","Code snippets that reflect the most common and widely-used practices found in training data"],"a":[3],"e":"Copilot is fundamentally a pattern-matching system trained on large amounts of public code. It naturally gravitates toward the most common, well-established patterns it has seen most frequently.","w":["Copilot recombines patterns from training data — it does not invent entirely new architectural paradigms.","Copilot favors patterns it has seen frequently in training data, which means experimental, rare, or bleeding-edge approaches are less likely to appear.","Copilot's diverse training data leads to varied suggestions based on context, not uniformly homogeneous output.","Since Copilot learns from public code, it produces suggestions that mirror the most commonly-used conventions, frameworks, and patterns found in that training data."]},{"id":"gh-300-c98","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot configuration is needed to protect against intellectual property risks by blocking verbatim public code reproduction?","o":["Disable the license compliance check configuration","Enable the 'Block public code matches' setting","Enable the 'Allow license check configuration' setting","Enable the 'Allow public code matches' setting"],"a":[1],"e":"Enabling 'Block public code matches' prevents Copilot from suggesting code that closely matches content in public repositories, directly reducing the risk of reproducing copyrighted or licensed code.","w":["Disabling license compliance checks would increase IP risk, not reduce it.","The 'Block public code matches' setting enables the duplication detection filter which prevents Copilot from suggesting code that closely matches known public repository content.","Allowing license checks is a compliance feature but does not block suggestions — it provides information rather than prevention.","Allowing public code matches would explicitly permit verbatim reproduction, increasing IP risk."]},{"id":"gh-300-c99","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does GitHub Copilot maintain test consistency across a codebase?","o":["By referencing external testing documentation and standards","By automatically fixing and rewriting all existing tests in the project","By identifying patterns in how you write tests and suggesting similar patterns for future tests","By generating implementation code that matches the existing test structure"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot learns your testing patterns (framework, naming conventions, assertion style, setup/teardown) from existing tests and replicates these patterns when generating new tests, maintaining consistency.","w":["Copilot learns from your existing code patterns, not from external documentation references.","Copilot suggests improvements but does not autonomously rewrite or fix existing test suites.","By analyzing your existing test files, Copilot understands your testing conventions and suggests new tests that follow the same patterns — maintaining a consistent test suite style.","Test consistency is about maintaining test structure and style, not about generating implementation code."]},{"id":"gh-300-c100","d":"github-copilot","x":"Why is ensuring code security particularly important when using Generative AI tools like GitHub Copilot?","o":["Ensuring code security prevents unauthorized access and potential data breaches","It enables handling of significantly larger datasets","It primarily maintains system integrity across multiple servers","It supports the implementation of advanced AI features"],"a":[0],"e":"GenAI tools often process sensitive code, proprietary business logic, and confidential data. Insecure code generated by or submitted to AI tools can expose systems to unauthorized access and data breaches.","w":["The primary security concern with GenAI tools is that insecure code or prompt handling could allow attackers to gain unauthorized access to systems or steal/leak sensitive data.","Dataset handling capacity is a scalability/performance concern, not a security reason.","System integrity is a byproduct of good security practices, but the most direct and primary reason is preventing unauthorized access and data breaches.","Advanced AI features depend on research and engineering investment, not on code security practices."]},{"id":"gh-300-c101","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does the GitHub Copilot Usage Metrics API help organizations assess their Copilot investment? (Choose two)","o":["By generating automated code quality improvement reports","By tracking the number of Copilot suggestions accepted by developers","By checking and enforcing coding style compliance","By providing Copilot Chat suggestion acceptance metrics"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The Metrics API measures adoption and effectiveness: suggestion acceptance shows how often developers find Copilot useful, while Chat metrics show engagement with conversational AI assistance.","w":["The Metrics API tracks usage patterns and adoption — it does not perform code quality analysis.","Acceptance rate metrics directly indicate how valuable developers find Copilot's suggestions, enabling ROI measurement.","Coding style compliance is enforced by linters and code review processes, not the Copilot Metrics API.","Chat-specific acceptance metrics show how effectively the conversational AI model assists developers, providing insight into Chat adoption and usefulness."]},{"id":"gh-300-c102","d":"github-copilot","x":"How should administrators restrict GitHub Copilot Business usage across all repositories in an organization?","o":["Create a copilot-policy.json file in each repository","Create a Copilot policy file in the organization's .github repository","Configure restriction policies through the organization settings in GitHub","Apply policies through GitHub Actions workflow configuration files"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot Business policies are centrally managed through GitHub organization settings, where admins can restrict features, configure content settings, and apply rules to all users and repositories.","w":["Copilot does not use per-repository policy files. Policies are centralized at org or enterprise level.","The .github repository is for community health files, issue templates, and workflow templates — not for Copilot governance.","Organization administrators configure Copilot Business policies in Organization Settings → Copilot → Policies, where rules automatically apply to all repositories and users in the organization.","GitHub Actions automates CI/CD workflows; it has no role in configuring Copilot access or feature policies."]},{"id":"gh-300-c103","d":"github-copilot","x":"A developer is not receiving any code suggestions from GitHub Copilot in their IDE. Which of the following could explain this? (Choose three)","o":["The developer does not have an active internet connection","The programming language of the current file is not supported by Copilot","The current file is listed in the repository's .gitignore file","The developer does not have an active GitHub Copilot license or subscription","A content exclusion rule is active that blocks GitHub Copilot entirely"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"No internet = cannot reach cloud AI model; unsupported language = Copilot won't suggest in that file type; no license = Copilot is disabled. .gitignore only affects Git, and content exclusion limits context but doesn't fully disable Copilot.","w":["Copilot requires an internet connection to send context to its cloud-based AI model and receive suggestions. No connection = no suggestions.","If the current file's language is not in Copilot's supported language list, or is poorly supported, suggestions may not appear.",".gitignore only controls which files Git tracks for version control. It has no effect on Copilot's ability to provide suggestions.","A valid and active Copilot subscription (Individual, Business, or Enterprise) is required. An expired or unassigned license means Copilot is not activated.","Content exclusion rules prevent specific files from being used as context, but they do not fully disable Copilot suggestions in other files. Suggestions may still appear in non-excluded files."]},{"id":"gh-300-c104","d":"github-copilot","x":"What settings can be configured in GitHub Copilot CLI? (Choose two)","o":["Usage analytics and telemetry data sharing preferences","The default code editor used by the system","Whether commands require explicit confirmation before execution","Which GitHub CLI subcommands are available"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Copilot CLI allows configuring telemetry preferences (controlling data sharing) and execution confirmation behavior (whether Copilot asks before running suggested commands for safety).","w":["Users can configure whether Copilot CLI sends usage analytics and telemetry data to GitHub, controlling their data sharing preferences.","The default code editor is a system-level or git-level config setting (e.g., git config --global core.editor), not configurable via Copilot CLI.","Copilot CLI's execution confirmation setting controls whether it asks for user approval before executing a suggested shell command — an important safety feature.","Copilot CLI is a separate assistant tool layer; it does not modify or configure which GitHub CLI subcommands exist or are available."]},{"id":"gh-300-c105","d":"github-copilot","x":"What can be done during AI model development to minimize bias in AI systems?","o":["Collect the maximum possible amount of data for training","Focus exclusively on increasing data accuracy","Use diverse data sources, apply fairness metrics, and include human oversight","Improve computational efficiency and model processing speed"],"a":[2],"e":"Bias in AI requires a three-pronged approach: diverse data prevents under-representation, fairness metrics detect biased outcomes, and human oversight catches harmful patterns that automated systems miss.","w":["More data alone does not eliminate bias. A massive biased dataset still produces a biased model. Quality, diversity, and representation matter more than volume.","Data accuracy is about correctness of labels, not about ensuring equitable representation across groups. Accurate data can still be systematically biased.","The combination of diverse training data (ensuring all groups are fairly represented), fairness metrics (measuring outcomes across groups), and human review (catching subtle biases) is the comprehensive approach to bias mitigation.","Computational efficiency is about system performance and speed — it does not address the ethical dimension of bias in training data or model outputs."]},{"id":"gh-300-c106","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which GitHub Copilot slash command is used to improve and clean up existing code for better readability and structure in Visual Studio?","o":["/explain","/fix","/optimize","/tests"],"a":[2],"e":"/optimize is the slash command used to refactor and improve code structure, readability, and maintainability — cleaning up code without necessarily changing its behavior.","w":["/explain provides a natural language explanation of what selected code does — it does not modify the code.","/fix suggests fixes for code that has errors, bugs, or problems — it is focused on correctness, not structural improvement.","/optimize (in Visual Studio and some other IDEs) is used to improve and refine code quality, readability, and structure — the primary refactoring slash command.","/tests generates unit test code for the selected implementation — it does not improve existing code structure."]},{"id":"gh-300-c107","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which statement best describes the appropriate use of GitHub Copilot in a development workflow?","o":["Copilot should replace all manual coding for maximum efficiency","Copilot should be used as an AI assistant that augments developer productivity, with human review always required","Copilot decisions should be accepted automatically to speed up development pipelines","Copilot should only be used for writing comments and documentation, not code"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot is designed as an AI assistant — it accelerates development by generating suggestions, but human oversight, review, and judgment must always remain in the loop for quality and safety.","w":["Replacing all manual coding with Copilot would eliminate the critical human judgment needed for architecture, security, and correctness decisions.","Copilot is an AI assistant that augments (not replaces) developer productivity. Developers must always review, test, and take responsibility for code they commit.","Automatically accepting all suggestions without review is explicitly against best practices — it introduces bugs and security vulnerabilities.","Copilot is highly effective for code generation, not just documentation. Limiting it to documentation only would greatly underutilize its capabilities."]},{"id":"gh-300-c108","d":"github-copilot","x":"What does GitHub Copilot's pre-processing step add to a user's prompt before sending it to the AI model?","o":["User profile and account information","Content from the selected code, open files, and cursor position context","Network diagnostics and connection speed data","Results from previous chat sessions by other users"],"a":[1],"e":"Pre-processing enriches the prompt by automatically including the developer's current IDE context — selected code, open file content, cursor position, programming language — to help the AI understand the task.","w":["User profile and account information are not included in Copilot prompts to the AI model.","The pre-processing step augments the user's prompt with IDE context signals: the selected code, open tab content, cursor location, file type, and recent code — making the AI response much more relevant.","Network diagnostics are technical infrastructure data; they are not added to AI prompts.","Copilot sessions are completely private. Other users' chat history is never included in your prompts."]},{"id":"gh-300-c109","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which Microsoft Responsible AI principle focuses on protecting personal data and preventing misuse of user information?","o":["Fairness","Transparency","Privacy and Security","Accountability"],"a":[2],"e":"The Privacy and Security principle in Microsoft's Responsible AI framework ensures that AI systems safeguard personal data, protect against misuse, and implement appropriate security controls.","w":["Fairness focuses on equitable treatment and avoiding discrimination — not data protection.","Transparency is about making AI explainable and understandable — not specifically about data privacy.","Privacy and Security focuses on protecting user data, preventing unauthorized access, and ensuring AI systems handle sensitive information responsibly.","Accountability (though a Responsible AI concept) focuses on establishing who is responsible for AI decisions and outcomes — not data privacy specifically."]},{"id":"gh-300-c110","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which scenario would be an appropriate use of GitHub Copilot's /fix slash command?","o":["Generating comprehensive documentation for a well-working function","Creating unit tests for a new feature","Resolving a syntax error or logical bug in selected code","Restructuring working code for better architectural alignment"],"a":[2],"e":"/fix is specifically designed for fixing errors, bugs, and issues in code. It analyzes the selected problematic code and suggests corrections to make it work correctly.","w":["Documentation generation is best handled by /doc or by asking Copilot Chat directly — not /fix.","Unit test generation is the purpose of /tests — not /fix.","/fix is designed for correcting code that has a problem — syntax errors, null pointer issues, incorrect logic — and suggests the appropriate code fix.","Restructuring working code for better architecture is a refactoring task suited to /optimize or /refactor commands."]},{"id":"gh-300-c111","d":"github-copilot","x":"How does the @workspace participant in GitHub Copilot Chat improve the quality of responses?","o":["It restricts Copilot to only answer questions about the current file","It resets the chat session to start fresh with new context","It causes Copilot to consider the entire workspace project structure when generating responses","It enables Copilot to access external API documentation in real time"],"a":[2],"e":"@workspace is a context participant that tells Copilot Chat to search across the entire project workspace, providing broader context that leads to more accurate, project-aware responses.","w":["@workspace expands context to the entire project — the opposite of restricting to a single file.","Resetting the chat session is done with /clear — @workspace adds context, it does not reset it.","Using @workspace allows Copilot to search through and reference the entire project — finding relevant files, functions, and patterns that help answer questions about architecture, cross-file relationships, and overall project structure.","Copilot Chat does not access external APIs or browse the internet in real time, regardless of which participant is used."]},{"id":"gh-300-c112","d":"github-copilot","x":"What is the Fill-In-the-Middle (FIM) technique used by GitHub Copilot?","o":["Improves suggestions by considering both the code before the cursor (prefix) and the code after the cursor (suffix) to predict the missing middle section","Restricts Copilot to use only external databases to generate code suggestions","Allows Copilot to generate suggestions based only on the prefix (code before the cursor), ignoring what comes after","Ignores both prefix and suffix, focusing only on code comments to infer intent"],"a":[0],"e":"The Fill-In-the-Middle (FIM) technique allows GitHub Copilot to consider the code both before (prefix) and after (suffix) the current cursor position when generating suggestions. Unlike traditional left-to-right completion that only looks at what was written before, FIM understands the full surrounding context, resulting in more accurate, consistent inline completions that fit naturally between existing code blocks.","w":["FIM looks at the prefix (code before cursor) and suffix (code after cursor) and predicts the missing middle section. This leads to better variable consistency, correct function structure, and more context-aware completions.","Copilot primarily uses the AI model trained on code plus the current file context. It does not rely on external databases for each suggestion.","This describes traditional left-to-right completion, which is what Copilot used before FIM was introduced. FIM specifically adds the suffix (code after the cursor) to the context.","Comments do help provide intent, but Copilot's FIM technique heavily relies on surrounding code structure, not just comments."]},{"id":"gh-300-c113","d":"github-copilot","x":"What kind of insights can the GitHub Copilot usage metrics API provide to help evaluate the effectiveness of GitHub Copilot? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. (Choose two.)","o":["The API can generate detailed reports on code quality improvements made by GitHub Copilot","The API can track the number of code suggestions accepted and used in the organization","The API can provide feedback on coding style and standards compliance","The API can provide Copilot Chat specific suggestions acceptance metrics","The API can refactor your code to improve productivity"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The GitHub Copilot Usage Metrics API is an analytics tool that measures adoption and usage patterns. It tracks how many code suggestions are accepted and used across the organization (B), and provides Copilot Chat-specific acceptance metrics (D). It does not evaluate code quality, enforce coding standards, or refactor code.","w":["The Metrics API tracks suggestion acceptance rates and usage data. It does not evaluate the quality of code produced or generate code quality improvement reports.","The API tracks how many Copilot suggestions developers accept and use across the organization. This is a core adoption metric showing the value Copilot delivers to the team.","Coding style and standards compliance is handled by linters and static analysis tools such as ESLint or Pylint, not by the Copilot Usage Metrics API.","The API includes Copilot Chat-specific metrics such as how often chat suggestions are accepted. This helps evaluate the effectiveness of the conversational AI assistance feature separately from inline completions.","The Metrics API is a read-only analytics and reporting tool. It cannot modify, refactor, or execute any code. Code refactoring is a separate Copilot feature used inside the IDE."],"ns":1},{"id":"gh-300-c114","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which of the following steps correctly demonstrates how to establish an organization-wide policy for GitHub Copilot Business to restrict its use to certain repositories?","o":["Create a copilot.policy file in each repository","Create a copilot.policy in the .github repository","Configure the policies in the organization settings","Apply policies through the GitHub Actions configuration"],"a":[2],"e":"GitHub Copilot Business policies are managed centrally through the organization settings. Administrators navigate to Organization Settings → Copilot → Policies to allow or block Copilot access for specific repositories and enforce rules across all users in the organization. There is no copilot.policy file format supported, the .github repository is for community health files not Copilot governance, and GitHub Actions automates CI/CD workflows — it does not control Copilot permissions.","w":["GitHub Copilot does not support a per-repository copilot.policy file for access control. Policies are managed centrally at the organization level, not individually per repository.","The .github repository is used for community health files such as issue templates and contributing guides. It does not support a copilot.policy file for governing Copilot Business access.","Organization-wide Copilot Business policies are configured in the organization settings. Admins can allow or block Copilot for specific repositories and apply compliance rules across all users and repos in the organization.","GitHub Actions is a CI/CD automation platform. It handles workflows like build, test, and deploy — it does not manage or enforce GitHub Copilot access permissions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c115","d":"github-copilot","x":"What reasons could apply if code suggestions are not working in your editor? (Choose three.)","o":["You do not have an active internet connection","Your programming language is not supported","You are working in files included in your .gitignore","You do not have a valid GitHub Copilot license","Your content exclusion is active and blocks the use of GitHub Copilot"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"GitHub Copilot suggestions can stop working for three main reasons: no internet connection (Copilot relies on cloud-based AI models and cannot function offline), unsupported programming language (some languages or file types are not supported), or no valid license (Copilot requires an active Individual, Business, or Enterprise subscription). Files in .gitignore are still readable by Copilot — .gitignore only controls Git tracking. Content exclusions limit what Copilot uses as context but do not fully disable suggestions in the editor.","w":["GitHub Copilot sends code context to cloud-hosted AI models and receives suggestions in real time. Without an active internet connection, this communication is impossible and no suggestions will appear.","Copilot performs best with popular languages like Python, JavaScript, and TypeScript. If your file type or programming language is unsupported, Copilot will not generate suggestions for it.","The .gitignore file only tells Git which files to exclude from version control tracking. GitHub Copilot can still read and use those files as context for generating suggestions regardless of .gitignore.","A valid GitHub Copilot license (Individual, Business, or Enterprise) is required. If your subscription has expired, was not assigned, or is inactive, Copilot suggestions will be disabled in the editor.","Content exclusions prevent Copilot from using specific files or repositories as context for suggestions, but they do not completely disable Copilot's suggestion functionality in the editor."]},{"id":"gh-300-c116","d":"github-copilot","x":"GitHub Copilot in the Command Line Interface (CLI) can be used to configure the following settings. Each correct answer presents part of the solution. (Choose two.)","o":["Usage analytics","The default editor","The default execution confirmation","GitHub CLI subcommands"],"a":[0,2],"e":"GitHub Copilot CLI supports configuration of usage analytics (telemetry settings controlling whether usage data is collected and sent) and the default execution confirmation (whether Copilot-suggested commands require a confirmation step before running). The default editor is a system or Git-level setting configured outside of Copilot CLI, and Copilot CLI does not modify or configure GitHub CLI subcommands — it operates as a separate AI assistant tool.","w":["Copilot CLI allows users to configure usage analytics settings, which control whether telemetry and usage data are collected and transmitted to GitHub for product improvement.","The default editor is configured at the system level or through Git configuration (e.g., git config --global core.editor), not through GitHub Copilot CLI settings.","Copilot CLI allows configuration of the default execution confirmation setting, which determines whether suggested shell commands must be explicitly confirmed before they are executed.","GitHub Copilot CLI functions as a separate AI assistant tool and does not have the ability to configure or modify GitHub CLI subcommands."]},{"id":"gh-300-c117","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which action most directly demonstrates responsible use of Copilot output in regulated codebases?","o":["Accept suggestions that compile without warnings","Run unit tests and code review on AI changes before merge","Enable duplicate detection and skip reviews","Pin a single model for all repos"],"a":[1],"e":"Responsible use keeps humans-in-the-loop: testing & review on AI-authored changes.","w":["Compiling without warnings does not validate correctness, security, or regulatory compliance.","Human-in-the-loop testing and code review is the foundation of responsible AI use in regulated code.","Duplicate detection helps with licensing risk but does not replace code review.","Pinning a model improves consistency but has no direct bearing on responsible use practices."]},{"id":"gh-300-c118","d":"github-copilot","x":"Your team wants to limit bias and insecure patterns. Which two safeguards align with guidance? (Choose 2)","o":["Treat AI code as production-ready","Keep linters, SAST, and code review in the loop","Disable all logs and usage analytics","Educate developers on limitations/hallucinations"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Pair Copilot with DevSecOps tools; educate devs about limitations.","w":["AI-generated code must always be reviewed; treating it as production-ready bypasses necessary safeguards.","Pairing Copilot with SAST and code review ensures security and quality issues are caught.","Disabling logs removes the visibility needed to detect misuse or systematic bias.","Educating developers about hallucinations and limitations reduces over-reliance on AI output."]},{"id":"gh-300-c119","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which Responsible AI principle is most tied to auditable AI-assisted dev?","o":["Reliability & Safety","Inclusiveness","Transparency","Fairness"],"a":[2],"e":"Transparency enables auditability and accountability.","w":["Reliability & Safety concerns correctness and harm prevention, not auditability of AI decisions.","Inclusiveness ensures AI serves all people equitably; it is not about audit trails.","Transparency enables understanding and auditing of AI behavior, decisions, and recommendations.","Fairness addresses bias and equitable outcomes; auditability is captured by Transparency."]},{"id":"gh-300-c120","d":"github-copilot","x":"Pasting a dataset with emails into a prompt is...","o":["Always allowed—prompts are not processed","Allowed if the repo is private","Discouraged: minimize personal data in prompts/context","Allowed only in Enterprise"],"a":[2],"e":"Minimize sensitive data in prompts and shared context.","w":["Prompts are processed by the model and may be retained; they are not privacy-neutral.","Privacy risks apply regardless of repo visibility; private repos are not a safe harbour for personal data.","Responsible AI guidance explicitly recommends minimising sensitive and personal data in AI interactions.","This is a universal best practice across all Copilot plans, not an Enterprise-only restriction."]},{"id":"gh-300-c121","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which feature helps reduce resurfacing of public code in suggestions?","o":["MCP allow lists","Duplicate detection filter","Edit mode","PR summaries"],"a":[1],"e":"Duplicate detection reduces close matches to public code.","w":["MCP allow lists govern which external tools an agent may call; they do not check for code similarity.","Duplicate detection flags suggestions that closely match public code, reducing licensing risk.","Edit mode enables multi-file edits; it has no role in code deduplication.","PR summaries help reviewers understand pull-request changes; they are not related to deduplication."]},{"id":"gh-300-c122","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which statement best captures Copilot’s role in security?","o":["Copilot replaces SAST","Copilot eliminates dependency checks","Copilot complements review/scan tools, not replaces","Copilot prevents all insecure code"],"a":[2],"e":"Use Copilot alongside—not instead of—security tooling.","w":["Copilot does not perform static analysis; dedicated SAST tools remain essential.","Dependency scanning (Dependabot, etc.) is a separate, still-required security process.","Copilot works alongside security tooling to complement, not replace, code scanning and reviews.","Copilot can and does suggest insecure patterns; it provides no security guarantee."]},{"id":"gh-300-c123","d":"github-copilot","x":"You’re asked to document how AI helped author code in a PR. Which feature aids maintainers?","o":["Commit signing","Copilot pull‑request summaries",".gitattributes","CODEOWNERS only"],"a":[1],"e":"PR summaries help reviewers understand changes/focus areas.","w":["Commit signing verifies the author identity but does not document AI involvement in authorship.","PR summaries describe AI-assisted changes and highlight focus areas for reviewers.",".gitattributes configures file handling behaviour; it does not document AI contributions.","CODEOWNERS assigns reviewers but does not capture information about AI authorship."]},{"id":"gh-300-c124","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which change most improves reliability & safety when adopting Copilot?","o":["Remove test suites to ship faster","Add test generation and coverage gates","Forbid model updates","Turn off reviews for AI changes"],"a":[1],"e":"Testing and coverage protect quality with AI changes.","w":["Removing test suites increases risk; speed at the expense of quality is counterproductive.","Strong test coverage and coverage gates are the most direct way to protect quality with AI changes.","Blocking model updates prevents improvements without improving reliability.","Reviews are critical for AI-assisted changes; removing them introduces risk."]},{"id":"gh-300-c125","d":"github-copilot","x":"When Copilot suggests license‑sensitive code, you should...","o":["Assume it is public domain","Verify license/attribution before use","Ask model to self‑certify license","Accept automatically if it compiles"],"a":[1],"e":"Developers remain responsible for license compliance.","w":["Code is not public domain by default; license terms vary widely and must be verified.","Developers are legally responsible for the license compliance of code they include, AI-generated or otherwise.","Models cannot reliably certify licensing; always verify through authoritative external sources.","Successful compilation says nothing about whether the code is licensed for your use."]},{"id":"gh-300-c126","d":"github-copilot","x":"Two emphasized areas in Responsible Use for Chat/CLI are: (Choose 2)","o":["Understand feature limitations","Disable all approvals in CLI","Guard secrets/sensitive files","Use only legacy models"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Know limits and avoid exposing sensitive data.","w":["Understanding feature limitations prevents over-reliance and helps catch errors the model may introduce.","Disabling approvals removes a key safety mechanism; approvals should be retained.","Protecting secrets from appearing in prompts is a core responsible-use principle.","Model choice is an operational preference; it does not relate to responsible-use principles."]},{"id":"gh-300-c127","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which plan adds enterprise‑grade capabilities beyond org policies?","o":["Free","Pro/Pro+","Business","Enterprise"],"a":[3],"e":"Enterprise extends Business with additional enterprise features.","w":["Free is for individual use with limited features and no enterprise capabilities.","Pro/Pro+ are enhanced individual plans without org-level management.","Business adds centralised management and SSO, but Enterprise extends further with GHES, audit, and more.","Enterprise extends Business with GHES, SAML, audit streaming, knowledge bases, and additional enterprise features."]},{"id":"gh-300-c128","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which is true about Copilot Free?","o":["Unlimited chat & completions","Limited monthly quotas","Enterprise Cloud required","Includes full code review"],"a":[1],"e":"Free has quotas suitable for light use.","w":["Unlimited access is a paid-plan feature; Free has monthly quotas on chat messages and code completions.","Free tier has monthly usage quotas suitable for light or exploratory use.","Free is available to any GitHub user; Enterprise Cloud is not required.","Advanced code review features require paid plans; they are not available on the Free tier."]},{"id":"gh-300-c129","d":"github-copilot","x":"Need seat management, policy control, SSO. Minimum plan?","o":["Pro","Business","Pro+","Free"],"a":[1],"e":"Business introduces centralized management & SSO.","w":["Pro is an individual plan; it does not include organisational seat management or SSO.","Business introduces centralised seat management, policy controls, and SSO integration.","Pro+ is an enhanced individual plan without org management capabilities.","Free has no organisational management, policy, or SSO features."]},{"id":"gh-300-c130","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which feature enacts multi‑file changes from one prompt in IDE?","o":["Copilot Edits","Web Chat only","Code scanning","Actions"],"a":[0],"e":"Edits supports multi‑file edits with reviewable diffs.","w":["Copilot Edits (Edit mode) supports multi-file changes from a single prompt with reviewable diffs in the IDE.","Multi-file IDE changes are an IDE-native feature, not limited to or requiring the web Chat.","Code scanning is a security analysis feature; it does not make code edits.","GitHub Actions is a CI/CD platform; it does not support interactive multi-file editing."]},{"id":"gh-300-c131","d":"github-copilot","x":"Copilot CLI can do which two? (Choose 2)","o":["Run in terminal, propose/execute commands with approvals","Q&A only, no file changes","Attach files via @ for context","Works only in VS Code terminal"],"a":[0,2],"e":"CLI supports approvals and @mentions for context.","w":["CLI proposes shell commands and executes them with explicit user approval, supporting full file operations.","CLI can make file changes with approval; it is not limited to question-and-answer interactions.","@ references in CLI attach files or images, providing richer context for the conversation.","CLI is terminal-native and works in any compatible shell, not only in the VS Code terminal."]},{"id":"gh-300-c132","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where is Copilot Chat available?","o":["IDEs, GitHub.com, Windows Terminal, Mobile","IDEs only","Terminal only","GitHub.com only"],"a":[0],"e":"Chat spans multiple surfaces.","w":["Copilot Chat is available in IDEs, GitHub.com, Windows Terminal, and mobile applications.","IDEs are just one surface; Chat also runs on GitHub.com, in the terminal, and on mobile.","The terminal is just one of several Chat surfaces.","GitHub.com is just one of several Chat surfaces; IDEs and other environments are also supported."]},{"id":"gh-300-c133","d":"github-copilot","x":"Auto-select a model for Chat/Agent is called...","o":["Model pin","Auto model selection","MCP skills","Duplicate detection"],"a":[1],"e":"The platform can pick models automatically.","w":["Model pin locks to a specific chosen model; auto-select dynamically picks the best model.","Auto model selection allows the platform to automatically choose the optimal model per task.","MCP skills extend Copilot with external tool integrations; they are unrelated to model selection.","Duplicate detection checks for code similarity; it has no role in model selection."]},{"id":"gh-300-c134","d":"github-copilot","x":"What’s unique about the coding agent vs simple chat?","o":["Text only","Implements changes and opens PRs","Mobile only","Disabled for Business"],"a":[1],"e":"Coding agent can implement and raise PRs.","w":["The coding agent does far more than produce text; it implements changes and interacts with the repository.","The coding agent can make file changes, run tests, iterate, and open pull requests autonomously.","The coding agent is not limited to mobile devices.","The coding agent is available on appropriate paid plans; it is not disabled for Business."]},{"id":"gh-300-c135","d":"github-copilot","x":"Third‑party coding agents are...","o":["Not supported","Supported (public preview)","Enterprise‑only","Server‑only"],"a":[1],"e":"Supported alongside Copilot agent in preview.","w":["Third-party coding agents are supported alongside the native Copilot agent.","Third-party agents are supported in public preview, allowing organisations to bring their own agents.","Third-party agent support is not restricted to the Enterprise plan.","Third-party agents are not limited to server-side environments."]},{"id":"gh-300-c136","d":"github-copilot","x":"Plans for individuals include...","o":["Pro & Pro+ (and Free)","Business & Enterprise","Business only","Enterprise only"],"a":[0],"e":"Individuals can use Free/Pro/Pro+.","w":["Free, Pro, and Pro+ are the three individual-tier Copilot plans.","Business and Enterprise are organisational and enterprise-level plans, not individual.","Business is an org plan requiring administrator management, not suitable for individual use.","Enterprise is an org/enterprise plan, not available as an individual subscription."]},{"id":"gh-300-c137","d":"github-copilot","x":"AI code review suggestions come from...","o":["Pull request templates","Copilot code review","Copilot Spaces","Protected branches"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot code review assists reviewers.","w":["PR templates are user-defined forms for structuring pull requests; they are not AI suggestion sources.","Copilot Code Review analyses pull requests and provides AI-generated review comments and suggestions.","Copilot Spaces is for sharing project knowledge as context; it does not generate code review comments.","Protected branches enforce merge rules; they do not generate suggestions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c138","d":"github-copilot","x":"Terminal orchestration with step approvals is...","o":["CLI autopilot/permissions","PR summaries","Edit mode","Duplicate detection"],"a":[0],"e":"CLI can plan/execute with tool approvals.","w":["Copilot CLI supports terminal orchestration with per-step approval prompts before executing tools.","PR summaries describe pull-request changes; they are unrelated to terminal orchestration.","Edit mode handles multi-file edits in IDEs; it is not a terminal orchestration feature.","Duplicate detection identifies code similarity; it has no terminal orchestration role."]},{"id":"gh-300-c139","d":"github-copilot","x":"Product page confirms Copilot spans editor, CLI, GitHub, and agents. Therefore...","o":["Only IDEs","It spans editor, CLI, GitHub, and agents","Cloud editor only","HTTP API only"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot is cross-surface.","w":["IDEs are just one of the surfaces Copilot operates on.","Copilot is intentionally cross-surface, spanning editor, CLI, GitHub.com, and agentic workflows.","Copilot works in local IDEs as well as cloud editors.","HTTP API is one access method; Copilot spans multiple surfaces beyond an API."]},{"id":"gh-300-c140","d":"github-copilot","x":"PR summaries help by...","o":["Generating summaries & focus areas","Running builds","Forcing approvals","Changing branch policy"],"a":[0],"e":"Summaries provide highlights for reviewers.","w":["PR summaries generate a description of changes and highlight focus areas to guide reviewer attention.","Build execution is a CI/CD concern; PR summaries are informational only.","PR summaries are informational and advisory; they do not enforce any approvals.","Branch policies are a separate repository settings feature unrelated to PR summaries."]},{"id":"gh-300-c141","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where to find Chat/Inline shortcuts in VS Code?","o":["Copilot VS Code features cheat sheet","Trust Center","Pricing","Actions Marketplace"],"a":[0],"e":"Cheat sheet lists shortcuts & features.","w":["The official VS Code Copilot cheat sheet lists all available shortcuts and inline/chat features.","Trust Center covers security and privacy documentation, not editor shortcuts.","Pricing pages describe plan features and costs, not keyboard shortcuts.","Actions Marketplace lists CI/CD workflow extensions, not IDE shortcuts."]},{"id":"gh-300-c142","d":"github-copilot","x":"GA features on GitHub.com Chat enable...","o":["Only static thread","Issue/PR ops with previews & attachments","No previews","No attachments"],"a":[1],"e":"Web Chat supports richer interactions.","w":["Static threads are a limited view; GA Chat supports much richer interactions.","GA web Chat includes file attachments, issue/PR integration, search, and previews.","Previews are a supported feature in GitHub.com Chat.","Attachments (files, images) are a supported feature in GitHub.com Chat."]},{"id":"gh-300-c143","d":"github-copilot","x":"Predict the next likely edit feature is...","o":["Next edit suggestions","Duplicate detection","Code scanning","Model pinning"],"a":[0],"e":"Some IDEs show next edit suggestions (preview).","w":["Next edit suggestions predict the developer's next likely change based on recent context.","Duplicate detection identifies similar public code; it does not predict next edits.","Code scanning finds vulnerabilities; it has no next-edit prediction function.","Model pinning locks to a specific model; it is unrelated to edit prediction."]},{"id":"gh-300-c144","d":"github-copilot","x":"Copilot Spaces per Learn modules helps...","o":["Personal notes only","Scale org knowledge as shared context","Device-only feature","Deprecated"],"a":[1],"e":"Spaces centralize & share project knowledge.","w":["Spaces is for organisational knowledge sharing, not just personal notes.","Copilot Spaces centralises project and organisational knowledge for use as shared AI context.","Spaces is a cloud-accessible feature, not limited to a single device.","Copilot Spaces is an active current feature, not deprecated."]},{"id":"gh-300-c145","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which are listed as data used by Copilot? (Choose 3)","o":["Prompts","Suggestions","User engagement data","Password vaults"],"a":[0,1,2],"e":"Trust materials list prompts, suggestions, engagement data.","w":["Prompts (user input) are a primary category of data the Copilot service processes.","Suggestions (model output) are captured as part of the data pipeline.","User engagement signals such as accept/dismiss are tracked pseudonymously for Business/Enterprise.","Password vaults are not a data type used or accessed by the Copilot service."]},{"id":"gh-300-c146","d":"github-copilot","x":"Copilot Business/Enterprise data is used to train models.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Business/Enterprise data is not used to train GitHub models.","w":["GitHub explicitly guarantees that Business and Enterprise customer data is not used to train GitHub's AI models.","Business and Enterprise plans explicitly exclude customer data from model training."]},{"id":"gh-300-c147","d":"github-copilot","x":"IDE sends prompt + context to produce suggestions describes...","o":["Local-only generation","Data pipeline for suggestions","On-prem model","No context used"],"a":[1],"e":"Context is used to generate suggestions.","w":["Suggestion generation happens server-side; the IDE sends context and receives results remotely.","The IDE packages context (open files, cursor position) and sends it to the Copilot service to produce suggestions.","On-premises model deployment is a separate advanced scenario, not the standard pipeline.","Copilot specifically uses context to improve suggestion relevance; context is not ignored."]},{"id":"gh-300-c148","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which limitation is accurate?","o":["LLMs never hallucinate","Copilot can be wrong/insecure; review is required","Copilot enforces policies automatically","Knows private code without context"],"a":[1],"e":"AI output requires human validation.","w":["Hallucination is a widely documented and inherent limitation of large language models.","AI output can be incorrect or insecure; human review is required before accepting suggestions.","Policy enforcement is an admin and governance responsibility; Copilot does not auto-enforce policies.","Copilot only uses code explicitly provided in the context window; it has no access to unsent private code."]},{"id":"gh-300-c149","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where to find official answers for security/privacy?","o":["Trust Center FAQs & docs","Only social media","Third-party forum","None"],"a":[0],"e":"Trust Center centralizes security/privacy details.","w":["GitHub's Trust Center is the authoritative source for security certifications, privacy details, and compliance FAQs.","Social media posts are not authoritative; always use official documentation for security/privacy questions.","Third-party forums may be inaccurate or outdated; official Trust Center documentation is the correct source.","The Trust Center provides comprehensive, regularly updated official information."]},{"id":"gh-300-c150","d":"github-copilot","x":"Auto model selection purpose?","o":["Avoid GPU","Choose model per task/perf constraints","Disable all models","Force o3 only"],"a":[1],"e":"Automatic selection optimizes for task.","w":["GPU usage is an infrastructure concern; model selection is about task suitability, not compute avoidance.","Auto model selection picks the most appropriate model for the current task and performance constraints.","Auto selection enables the best model dynamically; it does not disable models.","Auto selection uses whichever model is optimal; it does not force a single model such as o3."]},{"id":"gh-300-c151","d":"github-copilot","x":"Custom behavior via instructions/skills/agents refers to...","o":["Custom agents & skills","Code Scanning","Dependabot","None"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs describe agents/skills for customization.","w":["Custom instructions, agents, and skills allow tailoring Copilot's behaviour for specialised workflows.","Code scanning is a security analysis feature independent of agent or skill customisation.","Dependabot manages dependency updates and is separate from Copilot customisation.","Custom agents and skills are a well-documented extensibility mechanism in Copilot."]},{"id":"gh-300-c152","d":"github-copilot","x":"Does Copilot \"copy-paste\" from training?","o":["Yes, raw copy","No; models don’t contain code; duplicate detection helps","Yes, for small snippets","Only offline"],"a":[1],"e":"Guidance clarifies how suggestions are generated.","w":["Copilot generates suggestions using an LLM; it does not retrieve or replay stored training code verbatim.","Models generate code stochastically; duplicate detection separately flags close matches to public code.","Even short suggestions are generated, not retrieved from a stored snippet database.","There is no offline mode that changes the generation vs retrieval behaviour."]},{"id":"gh-300-c153","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where to find certifications like SOC/ISO?","o":["Trust Center compliance","Random blog","Marketplace listing","Nowhere"],"a":[0],"e":"Compliance reports listed in Trust Center.","w":["SOC 2, ISO 27001, and other compliance certifications are listed and downloadable from the Trust Center.","Blog posts are not authoritative sources for compliance certifications.","The GitHub Marketplace lists extensions, not compliance and security certification documents.","GitHub Copilot has published compliance certifications that are accessible from the Trust Center."]},{"id":"gh-300-c154","d":"github-copilot","x":"Agentic memory at high level means...","o":["Store repo-specific details for future work","Global memory across all repos","Replaces audits","Unrelated"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs describe repository-scoped memory features.","w":["Agentic memory allows a Copilot agent to store and recall repository-specific details across sessions.","Memory is scoped to individual repositories, not shared globally across all repos.","Memory stores context for agent tasks; audits are a separate governance activity.","Agentic memory is a well-defined feature for persistent context in long-running agent tasks."]},{"id":"gh-300-c155","d":"github-copilot","x":"User engagement data includes...","o":["Only code contents","Pseudonymous identifiers, accept/dismiss, metrics","Only prompts","Only errors"],"a":[1],"e":"Trust Center lists user engagement signals.","w":["Code content is a separate data category; engagement data covers interaction signals, not raw code.","Engagement data includes pseudonymous identifiers and signals such as acceptance, dismissal, and usage metrics.","Prompts are a distinct data category; engagement data captures interaction dynamics.","Error rates may be a metric, but engagement data broadly covers interaction signals."]},{"id":"gh-300-c156","d":"github-copilot","x":"Is Business seat required to run CLI?","o":["Yes","No; CLI available across plans (policy-permitting)"],"a":[1],"e":"CLI availability spans plans; org policy may govern.","w":["A Business seat is not required to use Copilot CLI; it is available across active Copilot plans.","CLI availability spans plans; org policy may govern whether members can use it."]},{"id":"gh-300-c157","d":"github-copilot","x":"Most effective refactor prompt?","o":["Improve this","Refactor processOrder() to pure fn; keep behavior; split into 3 helpers; add JSDoc; don’t change public API","make it better asap!","shorten code"],"a":[1],"e":"Specific constraints make stronger prompts.","w":["Vague prompts like \"Improve this\" produce inconsistent results without clear objectives or constraints.","Specifying function name, target pattern, behavioural constraints, and structure yields precise, reliable refactoring.","Urgency and vagueness do not help the model produce targeted, high-quality output.","\"Shorter code\" is a single weak constraint that may sacrifice readability, correctness, or completeness."]},{"id":"gh-300-c158","d":"github-copilot","x":"Attach files as context in CLI using...","o":["!","/","@","#"],"a":[2],"e":"@references attach files/images.","w":["! is used for shell or escape commands in some CLI contexts, not for file attachment.","/ is used for slash commands such as /help or /clear, not for file references.","@ references attach files, images, or URLs to the conversation context in Copilot CLI.","# is not a recognised file or context reference symbol in Copilot CLI."]},{"id":"gh-300-c159","d":"github-copilot","x":"Multi‑file changes with iterative control in IDE?","o":["Copilot Edits (Edit mode)","Terminal only","Code scanning","Actions only"],"a":[0],"e":"Edits supports multi‑file changes.","w":["Copilot Edits (Edit mode) supports multi-file changes with step-by-step review and an Accept/Discard workflow.","Terminal interactions are single-command; edit mode requires the IDE for multi-file iteration.","Code scanning analyses code for security vulnerabilities; it does not propose interactive edits.","GitHub Actions runs CI/CD workflows; it does not support interactive multi-file IDE editing."]},{"id":"gh-300-c160","d":"github-copilot","x":"Strong unit‑test prompt should specify... (Choose 3)","o":["Framework","Edge cases","AAA pattern","Random seed only"],"a":[0,1,2],"e":"Framework, edges, and structure drive quality.","w":["Specifying the test framework (Jest, pytest, JUnit, etc.) ensures generated tests are compatible with the project.","Explicitly requesting edge cases and invalid inputs improves coverage of boundary conditions.","The Arrange-Act-Assert pattern produces readable, maintainable tests with clear structure.","A random seed alone provides no useful guidance for test generation quality or coverage."]},{"id":"gh-300-c161","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where to store prompt file generate-unit-tests.prompt.md?","o":[".github/prompts","docs/",".vscode/","root only"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs specify .github/prompts folder.","w":["The official location for reusable Copilot prompt files is .github/prompts/ in the repository.","docs/ is for human-readable documentation; it is not the designated location for Copilot prompt files.",".vscode/ stores editor and workspace settings; it is not the target for Copilot project prompts.","Placing prompt files in the repo root is not supported; the designated path is .github/prompts/."]},{"id":"gh-300-c162","d":"github-copilot","x":"Resource listing Chat/Inline shortcuts in VS Code?","o":["Trust Center","VS Code Copilot features cheat sheet","Marketplace badges","Actions docs"],"a":[1],"e":"Cheat sheet summarizes features/shortcuts.","w":["Trust Center covers security certifications and privacy documentation, not IDE shortcuts.","The VS Code Copilot cheat sheet is the canonical reference for shortcuts and inline/chat features.","Marketplace badges indicate extension ratings; they do not list keyboard shortcuts.","GitHub Actions documentation describes CI/CD workflows, not IDE keyboard shortcuts."]},{"id":"gh-300-c163","d":"github-copilot","x":"Best approach to review & improve a file in IDE quickly?","o":["Inline suggestions only","Open Chat, attach the file, request review + plan","PR summaries without PR","CLI only"],"a":[1],"e":"Chat with file context is most effective.","w":["Inline suggestions are reactive and single-location; they cannot provide a full file-level review plan.","Attaching a file in Chat gives the model full context for a structured review and improvement plan.","PR summaries require an open pull request; they cannot be generated for an arbitrary file in the IDE.","CLI is terminal-focused; in-IDE Chat with file attachment is the better approach for file review."]},{"id":"gh-300-c164","d":"github-copilot","x":"GA features on GitHub.com Chat include...","o":["Editing threads & switching models","Only one static thread","No previews","No attachments"],"a":[0],"e":"Web chat added rich features.","w":["GA web Chat supports thread editing, model switching, file attachments, and rich interactions.","Multiple threads and thread editing are supported; a single static thread is a misconception.","Previews (e.g., rendered markdown, file diffs) are a supported feature in web Chat.","File and image attachments are fully supported in GitHub.com Chat."]},{"id":"gh-300-c165","d":"github-copilot","x":"Approve tools per command in terminal via...","o":["CLI permission prompts","Model pin","Agent mode in IDE only","PR templates"],"a":[0],"e":"CLI asks for approvals before using tools.","w":["CLI displays permission prompts before executing tools or making file changes, giving explicit user control.","Model pin selects a specific model; it has no effect on tool execution approvals.","Terminal approval prompts are a CLI feature and apply in any terminal, not only IDE agent mode.","PR templates define the structure of pull requests; they do not govern CLI command approvals."]},{"id":"gh-300-c166","d":"github-copilot","x":"Instruction to avoid brittle outputs?","o":["Do whatever","Provide constraints, examples, acceptance tests","Hide requirements","Use secrets for clarity"],"a":[1],"e":"Structured guidance improves reliability.","w":["Open-ended instructions produce unpredictable and often low-quality output.","Clear constraints, illustrative examples, and acceptance tests guide the model toward reliable, verifiable output.","The model cannot address requirements it is not given; hiding requirements guarantees poor output.","Secrets must never appear in prompts; including them creates a security risk, not clarity."]},{"id":"gh-300-c167","d":"github-copilot","x":"Keep iterating without losing context with...","o":["GitHub.com Chat threaded editing & model reloads","CLI only","Desktop app only","Not supported"],"a":[0],"e":"Threads & model reloads are GA on web.","w":["GitHub.com Chat's threaded conversations preserve context across iterations and support model reload within a thread.","CLI interactions are stateless per invocation; they do not maintain multi-turn conversation context.","Iterative context with threading is fully supported in the web Chat, without requiring a desktop application.","Threaded conversation with persistent context is a core, generally-available feature of GitHub.com Chat."]},{"id":"gh-300-c168","d":"github-copilot","x":"Search repo, propose edits, open PR from web using...","o":["GitHub.com Chat with rich file interactions","VS Code only","CLI only","JetBrains only"],"a":[0],"e":"Web Chat supports file interactions & repo ops.","w":["GitHub.com Chat supports repository search, rich file interactions, and can trigger PR creation via the coding agent.","These repo-level operations are available from the web Chat; VS Code is not required.","CLI is terminal-based and command-oriented; repo-level PR operations are handled in the web Chat interface.","JetBrains integration provides IDE code completions; web-based PR operations are not JetBrains-specific."]},{"id":"gh-300-c169","d":"github-copilot","x":"Metrics cited by GitHub include...","o":["Slower reviews","Faster tasks & reviews; better quality","Fewer commits","None"],"a":[1],"e":"Materials cite improved speed and quality.","w":["GitHub's materials consistently cite faster, not slower, code and review cycles as a Copilot benefit.","GitHub cites faster task completion, accelerated code reviews, and improved code quality as key productivity metrics.","Fewer commits is not a metric GitHub highlights; productivity and quality gains are the documented benefits.","GitHub extensively documents and publishes Copilot productivity impact metrics."]},{"id":"gh-300-c170","d":"github-copilot","x":"Bootstrap tests, fix failures, and generate commit messages in VS Code using... (Choose 2)","o":["Copilot smart actions & Chat","Only git CLI","Only web Chat","Only Xcode"],"a":[0],"e":"VS Code integrations support these tasks.","w":["Copilot smart actions (Generate Tests, Fix) and Chat in VS Code support test bootstrapping, failure fixing, and commit messages.","While the git CLI handles commits, test generation and failure fixing require Copilot IDE integration.","These tasks are primarily IDE-based workflows; web Chat alone does not cover the VS Code integration.","Copilot integrates with VS Code and other IDEs; it does not have an Xcode integration."]},{"id":"gh-300-c171","d":"github-copilot","x":"Create a PR from Chat via...","o":["Coding agent + Chat","Code scanning","Dependabot","Branch protections"],"a":[0],"e":"Coding agent can raise PRs.","w":["The Copilot coding agent can implement changes across files and open pull requests from a Chat conversation.","Code scanning finds security vulnerabilities; it does not create PRs from Chat prompts.","Dependabot automatically creates dependency update PRs; it does not respond to Chat prompts.","Branch protections govern merge access; they do not create pull requests."]},{"id":"gh-300-c172","d":"github-copilot","x":"Terminal‑native AI assistant with MCP integrations is...","o":["Edits","Copilot CLI","PR summaries","Codespaces"],"a":[1],"e":"CLI supports MCP.","w":["Copilot Edits is an IDE multi-file editing feature; it is not a terminal-native tool.","Copilot CLI is the terminal-native AI assistant and supports MCP server integrations.","PR summaries are a GitHub.com feature for reviewing pull requests; they are not a terminal tool.","GitHub Codespaces is a cloud development environment; it is not an AI terminal assistant."]},{"id":"gh-300-c173","d":"github-copilot","x":"Refactor & improve across multiple files with reviewable diffs using...","o":["Edits in IDE","Only inline completions","PR descriptions only","None"],"a":[0],"e":"Edits proposes multi‑file diffs.","w":["Copilot Edits in IDEs proposes multi-file refactoring changes with visual diffs for human review and acceptance.","Inline completions are single-location; they do not support multi-file diffs or iterative refactoring.","PR descriptions describe an existing set of changes; they do not perform refactoring.","Copilot Edits specifically supports multi-file refactoring with reviewable diffs."]},{"id":"gh-300-c174","d":"github-copilot","x":"Using agent mode for a backlog item also requires...","o":["Retain reviews for agent PRs","Disable protections","Turn off CI","Direct commits to main"],"a":[0],"e":"Keep normal governance.","w":["Normal governance including code review, CI, and branch protections should apply to agent-created PRs.","Disabling branch protections removes important safeguards that are especially needed for AI-authored code.","CI should run for agent-created code to catch functional and security issues before merge.","Direct commits to main bypass review processes and should not be used for agent-created changes."]},{"id":"gh-300-c175","d":"github-copilot","x":"Cookbook‑style Copilot examples are in...","o":["Random tweets","GitHub Blog deep dives","Private forums only","None"],"a":[1],"e":"Blog posts showcase patterns.","w":["Twitter/X is not an authoritative source for verified Copilot best practices or patterns.","GitHub Blog publishes in-depth, curated practical examples and cookbook-style pattern guides for Copilot.","Copilot guidance is publicly available and not limited to private forums.","GitHub's blog and official documentation contain extensive, publicly accessible usage examples."]},{"id":"gh-300-c176","d":"github-copilot","x":"Best combo to onboard devs on large codebase?","o":["Chat to explain + Edits to improve","Only code scanning","Only PR templates","No tools needed"],"a":[0],"e":"Chat + Edits speeds comprehension & changes.","w":["Chat explains and documents existing code; Edits makes targeted improvements — together they accelerate onboarding.","Code scanning identifies security issues; it does not explain code to developers or support onboarding.","PR templates structure new contributions; they do not help with understanding an existing codebase.","AI tooling significantly accelerates onboarding to large, complex codebases."]},{"id":"gh-300-c177","d":"github-copilot","x":"Available in Copilot Free (subject to quotas)?","o":["Chat on GitHub/IDEs with limits","Unlimited agent mode","Enterprise knowledge bases","Code review for all repos"],"a":[0],"e":"Free offers limited access.","w":["Copilot Free provides access to Chat on GitHub.com and in IDEs, subject to monthly usage quotas.","Agent mode has usage limits even on paid plans; unlimited agent mode is not offered on Free.","Knowledge bases and Spaces features are Enterprise-tier capabilities not available on the Free plan.","Advanced AI code review is a paid plan feature; it is not available for all repos on Free."]},{"id":"gh-300-c178","d":"github-copilot","x":"Before modifying files, CLI will...","o":["Modify silently","Ask you to trust the folder and approve tools","Require SSO","Deny all edits"],"a":[1],"e":"CLI has trust & approval prompts.","w":["Silent file modification would be unsafe; CLI implements explicit trust and approval prompts.","CLI asks you to trust the working folder and approve tool use before modifying any files.","SSO is an authentication mechanism for org members; folder trust is a separate CLI safety mechanism.","CLI can make file changes; it requires explicit approval, not a blanket denial."]},{"id":"gh-300-c179","d":"github-copilot","x":"Switch models within a chat to compare answers is GA on...","o":["GitHub.com Chat","Only IDEs","CLI only","Not supported"],"a":[0],"e":"Model switching is GA on web.","w":["Model switching within a conversation is a generally available feature of GitHub.com Chat.","Model switching in Chat threads is primarily a web feature; IDE extensions have separate model configuration.","CLI does not support interactive, in-conversation model switching.","Model switching is a documented, generally available feature in GitHub.com Chat."]},{"id":"gh-300-c180","d":"github-copilot","x":"Canonical plans comparison lives at...","o":["Copilot plans page in Docs","Blog comments","Random tutorial","Pricing portal only"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs page holds plan comparison.","w":["The official GitHub Copilot plans comparison page in GitHub Docs is the authoritative source.","Blog comments are user-generated and not an authoritative or reliable source for plan details.","Tutorials may be outdated; always refer to official documentation for current plan information.","Plan details and feature comparisons are documented in GitHub Docs, not only a pricing portal."]},{"id":"gh-300-c181","d":"github-copilot","x":"Recommended quick way to generate tests in VS Code?","o":["Generate Tests action or Chat with file context","Disable tests","PR summaries only","Edit mode only"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs show smart action & chat flows.","w":["The Generate Tests smart action and Chat with file context are the recommended quick paths for test generation in VS Code.","Disabling tests undermines code quality; this is contrary to all best practice guidance.","PR summaries describe pull-request changes; they do not generate test source code.","While Edit mode can be used for tests, the Generate Tests smart action is the quickest and most direct approach."]},{"id":"gh-300-c182","d":"github-copilot","x":"Generate Unit Tests prompt template emphasizes... (Choose 3)","o":["Edge cases & invalid inputs","AAA pattern","Test independence","Skip exceptions"],"a":[0,1,2],"e":"Template calls out coverage & structure.","w":["Edge cases and invalid inputs ensure tests cover boundary conditions and common error paths.","Arrange-Act-Assert is the recommended structure for unit tests for readability and maintainability.","Test independence ensures tests can run in any order without side-effects or coupling.","Exception handling is important; tests should cover expected exceptions, not skip them."]},{"id":"gh-300-c183","d":"github-copilot","x":"Learn modules on testing focus on...","o":["Edge cases + verify runs in VS Code","Only manual QA","Flaky test skipping","Disable test explorers"],"a":[0],"e":"Learn emphasizes edges & validation.","w":["Microsoft Learn modules emphasise covering edge cases and verifying that generated tests run successfully in VS Code.","Learn modules cover AI-assisted automated test generation, not only manual QA.","Skipping flaky tests is an anti-pattern; Learn recommends investigating and fixing them.","Test explorers are recommended tools for managing test runs; disabling them is counterproductive."]},{"id":"gh-300-c184","d":"github-copilot","x":"GitHub Blog shows Copilot can...","o":["Replace TDD","Generate tests across languages with prompt tips","Eliminate assertions","Force green builds"],"a":[1],"e":"Guidance shows multi-language examples.","w":["Copilot assists with testing but does not replace TDD as a methodology; human-driven design is still valuable.","GitHub Blog examples show generating tests across multiple languages using effective prompting techniques.","Assertions are essential test verification logic; Copilot helps write them comprehensively, not eliminate them.","Copilot does not control build status; tests must pass legitimately through the normal CI process."]},{"id":"gh-300-c185","d":"github-copilot","x":"Fix failing tests in VS Code using...","o":["Integrated suggestions with Test Explorer","Disable asserts","Mute failures","Retry infinitely"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot ties into Test Explorer.","w":["Copilot integrates with VS Code's Test Explorer to highlight failing tests and suggest targeted fixes.","Removing assertions eliminates the test's verification logic, making the test meaningless.","Muting failures hides problems rather than addressing their root cause.","Infinite retry masks flaky tests rather than diagnosing and fixing the underlying issue."]},{"id":"gh-300-c186","d":"github-copilot","x":"Prompt file inputs to target function/framework are...","o":["function_name and framework","Only file path","No params","Random seed only"],"a":[0],"e":"Template defines these inputs.","w":["The generate-unit-tests prompt template defines function_name and framework as the key inputs for targeted generation.","File path alone is insufficient; function name and framework direct the model to produce useful tests.","Prompt templates are designed with specific input parameters; they do not work without parameters.","A random seed is irrelevant to test generation quality, coverage, or structure."]},{"id":"gh-300-c187","d":"github-copilot","x":"Realistic expectation from Copilot tests is...","o":["Good starting coverage needing curation","Perfect tests","No reviews","No CI"],"a":[0],"e":"AI-generated tests still need human oversight.","w":["AI-generated tests provide a useful starting baseline, but require human curation for correctness and completeness.","AI-generated tests often miss important edge cases or contain subtle assertion errors; perfection is not realistic.","All AI-generated output, including tests, should be reviewed before being committed to the codebase.","CI is essential for running the test suite and catching regressions; it should always be active."]},{"id":"gh-300-c188","d":"github-copilot","x":"Applied Skills assessment emphasizes...","o":["Explain, document, develop features, tests, refactor/debug","Only docs","Only E2E","Only Python"],"a":[0],"e":"Assessment covers end-to-end dev tasks.","w":["The Applied Skills assessment covers the complete developer workflow: explain, document, develop, test, refactor, and debug.","Documentation is just one task in the broader assessment; the full workflow is tested.","The assessment covers unit, integration, and other levels of testing, not only end-to-end.","The assessment is language-agnostic and includes examples in multiple programming languages."]},{"id":"gh-300-c189","d":"github-copilot","x":"Feature that prevents Copilot from accessing specific paths?","o":["Secret scanning","Content exclusion","PR templates","Branch protections"],"a":[1],"e":"Content exclusions gate Copilot context.","w":["Secret scanning detects exposed credentials and secrets but does not restrict which files Copilot can access.","Content exclusions prevent Copilot from ingesting specific file paths or glob patterns as context.","PR templates define the structure of pull-request descriptions; they do not affect AI file access.","Branch protections govern merge permissions; they do not restrict which files Copilot uses as context."]},{"id":"gh-300-c190","d":"github-copilot","x":"Who can configure org-level exclusions?","o":["Any collaborator","Organization owners","Dependabot maintainers","External users"],"a":[1],"e":"Org owners manage org-level settings.","w":["Collaborators have repository-level permissions; configuring org-level Copilot settings requires owner access.","Only organisation owners have the permissions to configure org-level Copilot content exclusions.","Dependabot maintainers manage dependency automation configuration; they have no role in Copilot settings.","External users have no access to organisation settings of any kind."]},{"id":"gh-300-c191","d":"github-copilot","x":"Where to audit exclusion changes?","o":["Org/repo audit logs","Local logs only","Not available","Git history only"],"a":[0],"e":"Audit events record changes.","w":["GitHub's organisation and repository audit logs record changes to Copilot content exclusion settings.","Content exclusion changes are server-side events recorded in GitHub audit logs, not local system logs.","Audit events for exclusion configuration changes are available in the organisation audit log.","Git history tracks code changes in a repository; policy and configuration changes appear in the audit log."]},{"id":"gh-300-c192","d":"github-copilot","x":"Availability & limitations of exclusions?","o":["Apply to inline, Chat, and code review, but some modes/CLI may not honor","Disable Git","No exceptions","Remove read perms"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs note limitations for some modes and CLI.","w":["Exclusions apply to inline completions, Chat, and code review, but documentation notes limitations for some CLI modes.","Content exclusions restrict what Copilot uses as context; they have no interaction with Git operations.","The official documentation explicitly documents limitations and exceptions to content exclusion coverage.","Content exclusions affect what Copilot accesses as context; they do not modify repository read permissions."]},{"id":"gh-300-c193","d":"github-copilot","x":"Client sends what to fetch policy?","o":["Entire file contents","Repository URL (not logged)","Public file hashes","Nothing"],"a":[1],"e":"Client uses repo URL to retrieve policy.","w":["File contents are not sent to fetch the exclusion policy; only identifying information is used.","The client sends the repository URL to retrieve the applicable content exclusion policy; the request itself is not logged.","File hashes are not used in the policy retrieval process.","The client must send identifying information (the repository URL) to receive the correct policy."]},{"id":"gh-300-c194","d":"github-copilot","x":"Caveat even with exclusions enabled?","o":["Some semantic info may flow from IDE (types/hover)","Total isolation guaranteed","Only public repos affected","JetBrains only"],"a":[0],"e":"Docs warn about indirect semantic information.","w":["IDE language server data such as type information and hover data may still flow into Copilot's context indirectly.","The documentation explicitly warns that total context isolation is not guaranteed even with exclusions enabled.","The indirect semantic information caveat applies across both public and private repositories.","The caveat regarding indirect semantic data applies to all IDEs, not only JetBrains products."]},{"id":"gh-300-c195","d":"github-copilot","x":"API to manage exclusions at scale?","o":["GraphQL code scanning","REST endpoints for Copilot content exclusion","Actions REST","Audit streaming only"],"a":[1],"e":"REST endpoints exist for exclusion management.","w":["The code scanning GraphQL API manages vulnerability alerts; it does not manage Copilot content exclusions.","GitHub provides dedicated REST API endpoints specifically for managing Copilot content exclusions at scale.","The Actions REST API manages CI/CD workflows; it does not control Copilot exclusion policies.","Audit streaming outputs audit events; it does not configure or manage exclusion policies."]},{"id":"gh-300-c196","d":"github-copilot","x":"Which statement about IP indemnification is accurate?","o":["Not offered","Offered for unmodified suggestions (terms apply)","Free tier only","Enterprise Server only"],"a":[1],"e":"Public materials reference IP indemnification.","w":["GitHub does offer IP indemnification for eligible Copilot suggestions under specific plan terms.","IP indemnification is available for eligible unmodified suggestions on qualifying plans, subject to the terms of service.","IP indemnification applies to qualifying paid plans (Business/Enterprise), not the Free tier.","IP indemnification is available across eligible Business and Enterprise plans, not restricted to GHES."]}]},{"id":"ai-103","code":"AI-103","title":"Azure AI Apps and Agents Developer","vendor":"Microsoft","exam":{"count":54,"minutes":90,"passPercent":80},"domains":{"models":"Models","agents":"Agents","evaluation":"Evaluation","operations":"Operations","multimodal":"Multimodal","content-understanding":"Content Understanding","safety":"Safety","rag":"RAG","speech":"Speech","video":"Video","security":"Security","prompt-engineering":"Prompt Engineering","observability":"Observability","development":"Development","tools-data":"Tools Data"},"qs":[{"id":"ai-103-1","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT - Case Study\n\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\n\nTo answer the case study questions, you need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\n\nTo start the case study, select the Next button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the Question button to return to the question.\n\nOverview - Company Information\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\n\nExisting Environment - Identity Environment\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services. Contoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions. The team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement, monitor, and secure AI applications. Contoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before solution deployments.\n\nGenerative Environment\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\n\nProject1\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests. Agent1 uses a base model deployment. A safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled. Tool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled. Conversation memory constraints are NOT configured. Agent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products. Project1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU). Agent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\n\nProject2\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution. Development of the solution is incomplete.\n\nData Environment\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications. The Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products. The product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\n\nProblem Statements\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products. A recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet. Agent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions. The finance department reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\n\nRequirements - Planned Changes\nContoso plans to implement a solution for Project1 that analyzes vendor invoices by evaluating both visual layout and textual content so invoice details can be verified against vendor contract terms. Contoso will update the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses. Agent1 must retrieve and use detailed product information from product sheets stored in storage1. An indexing solution must enable Agent1 to answer customer questions. The video creation solution must be completed.\n\nTechnical Requirements\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity. Product sheets must be processed by an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search. Responses generated using product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate. Agent1 must answer natural-language questions about product details. The model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses. Data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\n\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models. Access to Azure resources must follow least privilege. Developers must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication. Access to Project1 must be assigned to Agent1Dev Team by using security group SC_Agent1_Dev and to Agent1Test Team by using security group SC_Agent1_Test. Agent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document containing customer data is added erroneously to the product-sheet repository. Product sheets might contain images with embedded text, and Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions hidden within images.\n\nBusiness Requirements\nUsers interacting with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including retaining conversation context and recalling relevant information from previous interactions. Agent1 must answer questions only about products sold by Contoso.\n\nYou need to configure the model deployment for Agent1 to meet the technical requirements. What should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Deployment type: Standard; Version update policy: Upgrade once a new default version becomes available","Deployment type: Standard; Version update policy: Opt out of automatic model version upgrades","Deployment type: Global Standard; Version update policy: Once the current version expires","Deployment type: Global Provisioned; Version update policy: Opt out of automatic model version upgrades"],"a":[1],"e":"Standard deployment suits variable demand without reserved throughput. Opting out of automatic version upgrades keeps the model version stable.","t":"Variable load = Standard. Stability = OPT OUT of auto-upgrade.","k":["Standard deployment","version update policy","opt out"]},{"id":"ai-103-2","d":"models","x":"Case Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to configure Agent1 to meet the security and compliance requirements.\nWhat should you use?","o":["self-harm content filtering","Personally identifiable information (PII) Detection","violence content filtering","prompt shields"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Prompt shields block malicious instructions hidden in images or documents. PII Detection prevents customer data from leaking. Self-harm and violence filters only cover specific harm categories.","t":"Hidden attacks = Prompt SHIELDS. Personal data = PII detection.","k":["prompt shields","PII detection","security"]},{"id":"ai-103-3","d":"models","x":"You are planning a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1 that will contain multiple agents. Each agent will access the same Azure AI Search resource.\nYou need to recommend a solution to centrally manage the Azure AI Search credentials within Project1. The solution must be implemented across all the agents.\nWhat should you recommend?","o":["Enable role-based access control (RBAC) for the Azure AI Search resource","Add a connection to the Azure AI Search resource","Disable key-based access control on the Azure AI Search resource","Create a managed private endpoint that connects to the Azure AI Search resource"],"a":[1],"e":"Add a project-level connection to Azure AI Search to store and manage its credentials centrally. All agents in Project1 share that connection instead of configuring credentials separately.","t":"Shared credentials across agents = one project CONNECTION.","k":["project connection","Azure AI Search","credentials"]},{"id":"ai-103-4","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYour company is piloting a customer support agent in a Microsoft Foundry project name Project1. Project1 is connected to an existing Application Insights resource, and the company’s support team reviews runs in the Traces tab.\nThe Foundry Agent Service is configured to perform the following actions:\nRetrieve the Application Insights connection string by calling project_client.telemetry.get_application_insights_connection_string().\nCall configure_azure_monitor(connection_string=...) to enable telemetry.\nA separate LangChain service is configured to use OpenTelemetry and has the following configurations:\nUses AzureAIOpenTelemetryTracer(connection_string=..., enable_content_recording=False)\nPasses the tracer by using config={“callbacks”:[azure_tracer]}\nCompany policy has the following requirements:\nTelemetry from LangChain and OpenTelemetry must be distinguishable within the same Application Insights resource.\nSecrets and credentials must NOT be stored in prompts, tool arguments, or span attributes.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Yes; Yes; No","No; Yes; No","No; No; Yes","Yes; No; Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Statement 1 is No: LangChain must be instrumented with a tracer passed through the callback config; turning on Azure Monitor alone does not trace LangChain. Statement 2 is Yes: OTEL_SERVICE_NAME sits in resource metadata, so different values separate services in one Application Insights resource. Statement 3 is No: enable_content_recording=False stops recording prompt and tool content, which keeps secrets out of spans.","t":"LangChain needs its own tracer. SERVICE_NAME separates. Content recording OFF hides secrets.","k":["OTEL_SERVICE_NAME","LangChain tracer","content recording"]},{"id":"ai-103-5","d":"models","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that processes procurement documents submitted by suppliers.\nYou need to implement two pipelines by using Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nInclude a pipeline named Pipeline1 that supports cost-effective, high-volume processing of standalone PDF invoices.\nInclude a pipeline named Pipeline2 that supports cross-document validation by using multi-step reasoning and reference data.\nHow should you configure each pipeline? To answer, drag the appropriate configurations to the correct pipelines. Each configuration may be used once, more than once, of not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Pipeline1: Single-file task in standard mode; Pipeline2: Multi-file task in pro mode","Pipeline1: Multi-file task in pro mode; Pipeline2: Single-file task in standard mode","Pipeline1: Single-file task in pro mode; Pipeline2: Multi-file task in standard mode","Pipeline1: Multi-file task in standard mode; Pipeline2: Single-file task in pro mode"],"a":[0],"e":"Pipeline1 processes independent invoices in high volume, so single-file standard mode is cheaper. Pipeline2 does cross-document validation with reference data and multi-step reasoning, so it needs a multi-file task in pro mode.","t":"One doc, many times = Standard. Many docs, deep reasoning = Pro.","k":["Content Understanding","standard mode","pro mode","multi-file"]},{"id":"ai-103-6","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Python application named App1 that integrates with a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1.\nYou need to ensure that App1 meets the following requirements:\nAuthenticates by using a Microsoft Entra managed identity\nSends prompts to a deployed model by using the Azure OpenAI Responses API\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["configuration 1: AzureKeyCredential; configuration 2: create","configuration 1: DefaultAzureCredential; configuration 2: create","configuration 1: DefaultAzureCredential; configuration 2: compact","configuration 1: ClientSecretCredential; configuration 2: retrieve"],"a":[1],"e":"DefaultAzureCredential picks up the managed identity token when App1 runs in Azure, with no stored key or secret. The Responses API creates a new reply with responses.create(...). Retrieve fetches an existing response and compact does not send a prompt.","t":"Managed identity = DEFAULT credential. New answer = CREATE.","k":["DefaultAzureCredential","Responses API","create"]},{"id":"ai-103-7","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a workflow for a customer support triage process.\nYou have an Ask a question node that stores user responses in a local variable named Var01.\nYou need to create the following Power Fx expressions:\nAn if/else condition expression that ensures that Var01 contains a value\nA Send message expression that returns the stored user response in uppercase\nHow should you configure the expressions? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["If/else: IsEmpty(Local.Var01); Send message: {Local.Var01}","If/else: Not(IsBlank(Local.Var01)); Send message: {Upper(Var01)}","If/else: Not(IsBlank(Local.Var01)); Send message: {Upper(Local.Var01)}","If/else: IsBlank(Local.Var01); Send message: {Upper(Local.Var01)}"],"a":[2],"e":"Not(IsBlank(Local.Var01)) is true only when Var01 has a value. The message must reference the local scope and apply Upper, so {Upper(Local.Var01)} returns the stored answer in uppercase.","t":"Has value = NOT IsBlank. Always keep the Local. prefix.","k":["Power Fx","IsBlank","Upper","Local scope"]},{"id":"ai-103-8","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a customer support agent built by using the Foundry Agent Service.\nThe agent uploads user-provided screenshots to Azure Storage through a ticketing tool and receives a blob URL for additional reasoning.\nYou need to use image moderation during agent runs and prevent harmful content from being returned during runs. Azure AI Content Safety must access the images by using the blob URL. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhat should you configure for Content Safety? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.\n\nGuardrails options:\nSelect Tool call and set Action to Block.\nSelect User input and Output and set Action to Annotate.\nSelect User input and Tool response and set Action to Annotate.\nSelect User input, Output, Tool response, and Tool call and set Action to Block.\n\nStorage access options:\nStorage account access keys\nA user-assigned identity that is assigned the Storage Queue Data Contributor role\nA system-assigned managed identity that is assigned the Storage Blob Data Reader role\nA system-assigned managed identity that is assigned the Storage Blob Data Contributor role\n\nSelect the correct Guardrails/Storage access configuration combination.","o":["Guardrails: Select Tool call and set Action to Block; Storage access: A user-assigned identity assigned the Storage Queue Data Contributor role","Guardrails: Select User input and Tool response and set Action to Annotate; Storage access: A system-assigned managed identity assigned the Storage Blob Data Contributor role","Guardrails: Select User input, Output, Tool response, and Tool call and set Action to Block; Storage access: A system-assigned managed identity assigned the Storage Blob Data Reader role","Guardrails: Select User input and Output and set Action to Annotate; Storage access: Storage account access keys"],"a":[2],"e":"To moderate harmful image content across the whole agent run and stop it from being returned, cover user input, output, tool response and tool call with the Block action. Content Safety only reads the blob, so a system-assigned managed identity with Storage Blob Data Reader follows least privilege.","t":"Block EVERYTHING, read ONLY (Blob Data Reader).","k":["guardrails","Block","Blob Data Reader","least privilege"]},{"id":"ai-103-9","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains three agents as shown in the following table.\n\nName | Description\nTriageAgent | Classifies incoming customer requests\nPolicyAgent | Answers policy questions by searching internal content\nActionAgent | Creates or updates tickets by calling an HTTP API\n\nYou need to orchestrate the agents to ensure that the customer requests meet the following requirements:\nSupport a deterministic, step-based process that uses conditional branching and shared state across the agents.\nOptionally trigger a ticket action based on the triage result.\nThe solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","o":["threads and runs without a workflow","a workflow","a multi-agent group chat session","separate agent runs coordinated in the application code"],"a":[1],"e":"A workflow gives a deterministic step order, conditional branching and shared state across agent steps with little code. It can run triage first, call the policy agent when needed and trigger the ticket action based on the result. Group chat is not deterministic and app-code orchestration needs more effort.","t":"Deterministic steps + branching = WORKFLOW.","k":["workflow","deterministic","conditional branching","shared state"]},{"id":"ai-103-10","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent uses Azure Speech in Foundry Tools.\nYou fine-tune a baseline speech to text model for the en-us locale and publish the model.\nThe agent calls the Speech to text REST API and returns an error message indicating that the project ID is invalid.\nYou need to set the project property to the correct ID.\nTo what should you set the project property?","o":["the custom speech project ID","the project ID","the custom speech endpoint URL","the project URL"],"a":[0],"e":"The project property in the Speech to text REST API must hold the Custom Speech project ID of the fine-tuned model. URLs are not IDs, and a generic project ID does not identify the Custom Speech project.","t":"Custom model? Use the CUSTOM SPEECH project ID.","k":["Custom Speech project ID","speech to text"]},{"id":"ai-103-11","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent named PaymentAgent.\nPaymentAgent includes a function tool that issues customer refunds by using an external API.\nYou are creating a workflow in YAML.\nYou need to ensure that the workflow pauses for human approval and continues with the refund step only after approval is granted.\nHow should you complete the workflow definition? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["configuration 1: basic_chat; configuration 2: approval == \"approved\"","configuration 1: ask_question; configuration 2: approval == \"approved\"","configuration 1: ask_question; configuration 2: propose_refund.output != null","configuration 1: data_transformation; configuration 2: true"],"a":[1],"e":"The ask_question step pauses the workflow and collects a human approval. The refund step should run only when approval == \"approved\". Checking that a proposal exists does not prove approval, and true skips the gate.","t":"Pause for a human = ASK_QUESTION. Gate = approval == approved.","k":["ask_question","human approval","workflow YAML"]},{"id":"ai-103-12","d":"models","x":"You have an Azure Speech in Foundry Tools resource that hosts a custom speech to text model deployed to a custom endpoint. An agent uses the endpoint to perform real-time speech recognition.\nYou are approaching the expiration date of the custom speech to text model.\nWhat is the expected behavior when the model expires?","o":["Speech recognition requests will continue to use the expired custom model until the model is removed manually.","Speech recognition requests will fall back to the most recent base model for the same locale.","Speech recognition requests will return a 4xx error until a new custom model is deployed.","The custom model will be deleted automatically when the model expires."],"a":[1],"e":"When a deployed custom speech-to-text model expires, requests to the custom endpoint fall back to the latest base model for the same locale. It does not keep serving the expired model, does not return 4xx just for that, and is not deleted automatically.","t":"Expired custom model = FALLS BACK to base model.","k":["model expiration","fallback","base model"]},{"id":"ai-103-13","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a model deployment.\nYou have an application that calls the deployment by using the Azure OpenAI v1 API and DefaultAzureCredential.\nThe developers at your company receive HTTP 403 errors when they send inference requests, even after running az login.\nYou need to ensure that the developers can perform model inference. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich role-based access control (RBAC) role should you assign to the developers?","o":["Cognitive Services Data Reader","Cognitive Services User","Cognitive Services OpenAI User","Contributor"],"a":[2],"e":"Cognitive Services OpenAI User grants permission to call Azure OpenAI inference APIs with Entra ID auth, fixing the 403 with least privilege. Cognitive Services User is not the Azure OpenAI data-plane role, Contributor is too broad, and Data Reader does not allow inference.","t":"403 on OpenAI inference = OpenAI USER role.","k":["Cognitive Services OpenAI User","RBAC","least privilege","403"]},{"id":"ai-103-14","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent has a Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool that queries a knowledge base stored in Azure AI Search.\nSome agent runs return answers from the base model without invoking the knowledge base, which results in responses without grounded citations.\nYou are provided with the following code snippet that runs the agent.\n\nYou need to add the correct tool_choice parameter to the code to deterministically force the agent to invoke the MCP tool on each run.\nWhat should you add?","o":["tool_choice={\"type\": \"mcp\"}","tool_choice={\"auto\"}","tool_choice={\"required\"}","tool_choice={\"type\": \"knowledge_base\"}"],"a":[2],"e":"Setting tool_choice to required forces the agent to call at least one available tool every run, so it cannot answer from the base model alone. auto lets the model skip tools, and mcp or knowledge_base are not valid tool_choice values for this.","t":"Must call a tool every time = REQUIRED.","k":["tool_choice","required","grounded citations"]},{"id":"ai-103-15","d":"models","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a customer support agent grounded in internal documentation.\nAfter a recent update, users report the following issues:\nSome answers are unsupported by retrieved documents.\nA small number of responses are flagged for policy violations.\nYou need to evaluate each issue.\nWhich observability signals should you use for each issue? To answer, drag the appropriate observability signals to the correct issues. Each observability signal may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the spit bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.\n\nSelect the correct issue/observability-signal combination.","o":["Unsupported answers: Latency breakdown; Policy violations: Token usage","Unsupported answers: Token usage; Policy violations: Groundedness evaluator","Unsupported answers: Groundedness evaluator; Policy violations: Safety evaluation signal","Unsupported answers: Safety evaluation signal; Policy violations: Trace timing"],"a":[2],"e":"The groundedness evaluator compares responses with retrieved documents and flags unsupported claims. The safety evaluation signal measures risk and policy violations. Token usage, latency and trace timing explain cost and performance, not grounding or safety.","t":"Made-up claims = GROUNDEDNESS. Rule breaks = SAFETY.","k":["groundedness evaluator","safety evaluation"]},{"id":"ai-103-16","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1 that contains an agent. The agent uses an OpenAPI 3.0 specification to call an external weather service.\nThe weather service requires a key to be passed in an HTTP header. The key value is stored as a connection in Project1.\nYou need to ensure that the key value from the connection is included automatically whenever the OpenAPI tool is invoked.\nWhat should you configure in the OpenAPI specification?","o":["an API key security scheme","a header parameter defined for each operation","a Bearer token security scheme","an Azure Key Vault connection"],"a":[0],"e":"Define an API key security scheme in the OpenAPI spec with the key located in the required HTTP header. When the tool uses the project connection, Foundry automatically applies the stored key. A manual header parameter does not link to the secret, and Bearer is for tokens.","t":"Key from connection in header = API KEY security scheme.","k":["OpenAPI","API key security scheme","connection"]},{"id":"ai-103-17","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that serves a high-volume chat app.\nMost requests are simple FAQs, but some require advanced reasoning.\nYou need to reduce costs and latency for common queries, without degrading the quality of the responses to complex questions.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Use a model cascade that routes the requests to different models.","Route all the requests to a smaller model.","Route all the requests to the most capable model.","Increase the value of the max_tokens parameter for all the requests."],"a":[0],"e":"A model cascade sends simple FAQs to a smaller, faster, cheaper model and routes complex requests to a stronger model. This cuts cost and latency without hurting quality on hard questions.","t":"Easy to small, hard to big = CASCADE.","k":["model cascade","model router","cost","latency"]},{"id":"ai-103-18","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an internal Q&A agent.\nUsers report the following issues when they ask the agent questions:\nAn increase in the following response: “No relevant information found”\nPeriodic HTTP 429 rate limit exceeded errors during peak hours\nYou need to identify whether each issue is caused by model unavailability, resource limits, or inference failures.\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Metrics: Time To Response and Total Tokens; Diagnostic log: trace","Metrics: Only Tokens Cache Match Rate; Diagnostic log: AllMetrics","Metrics: Only Total Requests filtered to status code 200; Diagnostic log: audit","Metrics: Model Availability Rate and Provisioned Utilization; Diagnostic log: RequestResponse"],"a":[3],"e":"Model Availability Rate shows whether the model is available, and Provisioned Utilization shows whether capacity limits cause the 429s. The RequestResponse diagnostic log records request and response details, including errors, to investigate inference failures.","t":"Availability + Utilization metrics, RequestResponse log.","k":["Model Availability Rate","Provisioned Utilization","RequestResponse log","429"]},{"id":"ai-103-19","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a high-traffic agent.\nAfter a recent update, operational costs increase significantly.\nMonitoring confirms that the volume of user traffic to the agent remains unchanged.\nYou suspect that changes to the request or response characteristics are causing the increase. You need to identify whether the additional costs are driven by the model input size, the model output size, or expanded tool usage.\nWhich observability capability should you use?","o":["token usage","evaluation metrics","run success rate","latency"],"a":[0],"e":"Token usage shows input and output token consumption, so you can see bigger prompts, longer responses or tool-related context driving cost while traffic is flat. Latency, evaluation metrics and success rate do not reveal token-based cost sources.","t":"Cost up, traffic flat = check TOKENS.","k":["token usage","cost","input vs output"]},{"id":"ai-103-20","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses tools to retrieve internal content and call external APIs. The agent is configured to let the model decide when to call the tools.\nYou need to publish the agent for a compliance workflow. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nEach workflow run must include a retrieval step before generating a response.\nTool calls must authenticate by using the published agent’s own identity.\nTool access must use an identity isolated from other project resources.\nTool access must use support audit tracing.\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["tool_choice: required; authentication: Using a distinct agent identity bound to the client application","tool_choice: none; authentication: Storing API keys in prompts","tool_choice: required; authentication: Using the shared project agent identity","tool_choice: auto; authentication: Using the shared project agent identity"],"a":[0],"e":"tool_choice required forces a tool call every run, so retrieval always happens first. A distinct agent identity bound to the client application isolates tool access from other project resources, supports keyless auth as the published agent, and is traceable for audits.","t":"Always retrieve = REQUIRED. Isolated identity = DISTINCT agent identity.","k":["tool_choice required","agent identity","audit"]},{"id":"ai-103-21","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1 that contains the following:\nAn OpenAPI tool that calls an external API\nA project connection named Connection1 that stores the API key of the external API\nWhen an agent calls the OpenAPI tool, the API returns a 401 unauthorized error, and traces show that the API key header is NOT being sent.\nYou need to ensure that the OpenAPI tool automatically includes the API key from Connection1 on all requests.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Enable identity passthrough so that the tool uses the Microsoft Entra token of the caller.","Connect the tool to Connection1.","Configure the tool to use the default connection of Project1.","Add the API key header manually to the OpenAPI specification."],"a":[1],"e":"Connecting the OpenAPI tool to Connection1 links it to the stored API key credential so Foundry attaches the key header automatically. Identity passthrough sends an Entra token, and adding the header manually does not fill in the secret safely.","t":"401 + missing key header = CONNECT the tool to the connection.","k":["OpenAPI tool","connection","401","API key"]},{"id":"ai-103-22","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a customer support agent. The agent calls an internal knowledge API tool before generating responses.\nUsers report the following issues:\nSome requests take more than 15 seconds to complete.\nSome responses are incorrect, even when the knowledge API returns the expected data.\nYou need to inspect individual agent runs to view the ordered sequence of large language model (LLM) calls, tool invocations, and timing information.\nWhich observability capability should you use?","o":["safety metrics","monitoring","token usage","tracing"],"a":[3],"e":"Tracing gives an ordered per-run view of LLM calls, tool invocations, inputs, outputs and timing spans, so you can find both slow steps and reasoning problems after the tool returns. Token usage, monitoring and safety metrics are aggregates.","t":"Step-by-step story of one run = TRACING.","k":["tracing","spans","tool invocations","latency"]},{"id":"ai-103-23","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a multimodal AI generative model that accepts image uploads and uses extracted image text to generate responses.\nYou discover that users can upload unsafe images and embed hidden instructions into images to manipulate the model.\nYou need to implement controls to mitigate the risk.\nSolution: You configure a prompt shield for user prompts.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. A prompt shield for user prompts protects against injection in text the user types directly. It does not moderate unsafe images or inspect text extracted from images for hidden instructions.","t":"User-prompt shield misses image text. No.","k":["prompt shield","user prompt","No"]},{"id":"ai-103-24","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a multimodal AI generative model that accepts image uploads and uses extracted image text to generate responses.\nYou discover that users can upload unsafe images and embed hidden instructions into images to manipulate the model.\nYou need to implement controls to mitigate the risk.\nSolution: You configure image moderation to block unsafe content before processing the images.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. Image moderation blocks unsafe images before processing, but it cannot detect hidden injection instructions inside a safe-looking image.","t":"Moderation catches ugly images, not hidden commands. No.","k":["image moderation","indirect prompt injection","No"]},{"id":"ai-103-25","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a multimodal AI generative model that accepts image uploads and uses extracted image text to generate responses.\nYou discover that users can upload unsafe images and embed hidden instructions into images to manipulate the model.\nYou need to implement controls to mitigate the risk.\nSolution: You configure a prompt shield for documents.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Yes. A prompt shield for documents detects indirect prompt injection in user-provided content, including instructions in text extracted from images before the model responds.","t":"Hidden instructions in content = DOCUMENT prompt shield. Yes.","k":["document prompt shield","indirect injection","Yes"]},{"id":"ai-103-26","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a multimodal AI generative model that accepts image uploads and uses extracted image text to generate responses.\nYou discover that users can upload unsafe images and embed hidden instructions into images to manipulate the model.\nYou need to implement controls to mitigate the risk.\nSolution: You configure protected material detection.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[0],"e":"No. Protected material detection finds copyrighted text and code. It does not moderate unsafe images or block hidden manipulation instructions.","t":"Protected material = copyright, not safety. No.","k":["protected material detection","copyright","No"]},{"id":"ai-103-27","d":"models","x":"Question: 27\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to recommend a solution to assess the responses generated by Agent1 when the agent uses the product information stored in storage1. The solution must meet the technical requirements.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","o":["a custom guardrail","a groundedness evaluator","a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) evaluator","model fine-tuning"],"a":[2],"e":"A RAG evaluator assesses the whole retrieval-augmented flow: retrieval quality plus response relevance, completeness and groundedness. A groundedness evaluator is narrower, guardrails control runtime behavior, and fine-tuning is not evaluation.","t":"Judge the whole retrieve+answer chain = RAG evaluator.","k":["RAG evaluator","retrieval quality","evaluation"]},{"id":"ai-103-28","d":"models","x":"Question: 28\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to configure Agent1 to answer customer questions about only the Contoso products. The solution must meet the business requirements.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Add few-shot examples.","Increase the value of the temperature parameter.","Apply top-p sampling.","Modify the system message instructions."],"a":[3],"e":"System message instructions set the agent's fixed behavior and scope, so they can tell Agent1 to answer only about Contoso products and refuse other topics. Few-shot examples are softer, and temperature or top-p only control randomness.","t":"Scope rules live in the SYSTEM message.","k":["system message","scope","instructions"]},{"id":"ai-103-29","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project.\nYou plan to build a customer support solution that contains an agent. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nProvide accurate, context-aware responses grounded in internal product documentation stored in Azure AI Search.\nRequire deep, multi-step reasoning across long contexts.\nGenerate detailed natural language responses.\nWhich type of model should you use to power the agent?","o":["a key phrase extraction model","a multimodal model","a small language model (SLM)","a large language model (LLM)"],"a":[3],"e":"An LLM can synthesize Azure AI Search results into detailed natural-language answers and reason across long contexts. Multimodal is for images or audio, SLMs suit simpler tasks, and key phrase extraction is a narrow NLP task.","t":"Deep + long + detailed = LARGE language model.","k":["LLM","long context","reasoning"]},{"id":"ai-103-30","d":"models","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a deployed ticket-triage agent.\nYou discover that sometimes the agent responds without calling any tools, even when a tool is required.\nYou need to ensure that the agent calls a tool during execution.\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["\"tool_choice\": \"required\"","\"type\": \"tools\"","\"tool_choice\": \"auto\"","\"response_format\": \"required\""],"a":[0],"e":"Set \"tool_choice\": \"required\" in the run payload so the agent must call at least one tool. auto lets the model decide, and response_format controls output shape, not tool calls.","t":"Force a tool call = tool_choice REQUIRED.","k":["tool_choice","required"]},{"id":"ai-103-31","d":"models","x":"You are building a web app named App1 that generates responses by using a model deployed to a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1.\nBefore sending the prompts to the model, App1 must retrieve documents by using Azure AI Search.\nYou need to integrate Project1 and App1. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nMultiple client applications must use the same search configuration.\nA security policy must prevent key-based authentication.\nAdministrative effort must be minimized.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Enable a managed identity for each application and call Azure AI Search directly.","Configure an Azure AI Search connection in Project1 and reference the connection in each application.","Create a custom HTTP connection in Foundry and manually configure Azure AI Search endpoints per application.","Call Azure AI Search directly from each application by using Microsoft Entra authentication."],"a":[1],"e":"An Azure AI Search connection in Project1 centralizes the endpoint and auth so many apps reference one configuration. It supports keyless Entra auth and only needs managing once. Per-app direct calls repeat the setup.","t":"Many apps, one config, no keys = project CONNECTION.","k":["Azure AI Search connection","keyless","shared config"]},{"id":"ai-103-32","d":"models","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent used by the financial analysts at your company.\nYou need to optimize the agent workflow by providing additional data access and processing capabilities. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that the agent can perform calculations during conversations.\nEnsure that the agent can access up-to-date information from public websites.\nEnsure that the agent can retrieve information from documents uploaded directly to the agent.\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate tools to the correct requirements. Each tool may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.\n\nTools:\nCode interpreter\nComputer use\nFile search\nGrounding with Bing Search\nMicrosoft Fabric\n\nSelect the correct public-web-information/calculations/uploaded-documents combination.","o":["Public websites: File search; Calculations: Grounding with Bing Search; Uploaded documents: Code interpreter","Public websites: Grounding with Bing Search; Calculations: Code interpreter; Uploaded documents: File search","Public websites: Computer use; Calculations: Microsoft Fabric; Uploaded documents: Grounding with Bing Search","Public websites: Microsoft Fabric; Calculations: File search; Uploaded documents: Computer use"],"a":[1],"e":"Grounding with Bing Search gets current public web info. Code interpreter does calculations. File search retrieves content from documents uploaded to the agent. Computer use and Fabric serve other needs.","t":"Web = Bing. Math = Code interpreter. Uploads = File search.","k":["Bing grounding","code interpreter","file search"]},{"id":"ai-103-33","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a prompt agent used by a customer support web app.\nThe agent is invoked from a Python service that does NOT run in the Foundry portal.\nYou need to implement end-to-end tracing to capture latency breakdowns and exceptions across agent runs.\nWhich two components can you use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Application Insights","the Azure Monitor Agent","Microsoft Sentinel","a Log Analytics workspace","OpenTelemetry"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Application Insights collects distributed telemetry like request durations, dependencies, exceptions and end-to-end traces. OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard to create and propagate spans across the service and agent runs. Log Analytics only stores logs, Azure Monitor Agent is for machines, Sentinel is SIEM.","t":"Trace code outside the portal = OpenTelemetry + App Insights.","k":["OpenTelemetry","Application Insights","tracing"]},{"id":"ai-103-34","d":"models","x":"You have a customer support agent that uses the Microsoft Foundry Agent Service.\nSometimes, customers return to a session days later to continue the same support case, and the agent must resume with the full historical context. The agent must provide the following:\nMulti-turn continuity within the session\nCross-session continuity for the same case\nAccess to the full interaction history, including user messages, agent messages, tool calls, and tool outputs\nYou need to ensure that the agent automatically reloads the complete history on each new turn.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Enable memory summarization on the agent definition to persist the context automatically.","Persist only the final model response stored in the client application and prepend the response to future prompts.","Create and reuse a conversation by storing the conversation’s ID and supplying the ID on subsequent requests."],"a":[2],"e":"Store the conversation ID for the support case and pass it on later requests. Agent Service then reloads the whole conversation state, including user and agent messages, tool calls and tool outputs. Saving only the final reply loses history, and summarization loses detail.","t":"Full history every turn = reuse the CONVERSATION ID.","k":["conversation ID","history","multi-turn"]},{"id":"ai-103-35","d":"models","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a deployed chat model.\nYou have a Python service that sends API requests to the model. The service is integrated with an automated validation system that compares generated outputs against approved response patterns.\nStakeholders report that small wording differences are causing validation mismatches.\nYou need to update the request parameters to improve output stability. The solution must maximize reasoning quality.\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["temperature: 1; effort: \"medium\"","temperature: 0; effort: \"low\"","temperature: 0; effort: \"high\"","temperature: 2; effort: \"high\""],"a":[2],"e":"Temperature 0 minimizes sampling randomness so wording stays consistent for automated validation. Effort high maximizes reasoning quality.","t":"Stable + smart = temperature 0, effort HIGH.","k":["temperature 0","reasoning effort high"]},{"id":"ai-103-36","d":"models","x":"You have a chat app in a Microsoft Foundry project and an Azure AI Search vectorized index.\nYou need to connect to the index to meet the following requirements:\nComplex questions must retrieve information from multiple chunks.\nMulti-turn conversations must influence retrieval planning.\nRetrievals must run in parallel to reduce latency.\nWhich retrieval approach should you use?","o":["iterative retrieval","agentic Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","chain of thought","classic Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)"],"a":[1],"e":"Agentic RAG lets an agent plan retrieval: it splits complex questions into several retrieval actions, uses multi-turn context in planning, and runs retrievals in parallel to cut latency. Iterative retrieval is sequential and classic RAG is a single simple pass.","t":"Plan + parallel + multi-turn = AGENTIC RAG.","k":["agentic RAG","parallel retrieval","query planning"]},{"id":"ai-103-37","d":"agents","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou need to recommend a plan to create a customer support agent by using the Microsoft Foundry Agent Service. The agent must meet the following requirements:\nRetain user preferences across multiple conversations.\nEnable users to provide contextual grounding by directly uploading documents during a chat.\nWhich Foundry capability should you recommend for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Agent memory that uses persistent storage; Azure AI Search tool","Conversation history; Azure AI Search tool","Agent memory that uses persistent storage; File search tool","Orchestration-managed session context; Code interpreter tool"],"a":[2],"e":"Agent memory with persistent storage keeps user preferences across separate conversations. The File search tool lets users upload documents during chat and use them as grounding context. AI Search is for an existing index and Code interpreter runs code.","t":"Remember across chats = MEMORY. Uploaded docs = FILE SEARCH.","k":["agent memory","persistent storage","file search"]},{"id":"ai-103-38","d":"evaluation","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nYou need to improve response completeness. The solution must be implemented in the logic of the application code before responses are returned.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Switch to Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).","Add a retry evaluation before the responses are returned.","Replace the model with a smaller deployment.","Decrease the value of the max_tokens parameter."],"a":[1],"e":"A retry evaluation in the app logic checks completeness before the response is returned and can regenerate if it falls short. Lower max_tokens truncates, switching to RAG adds no check, and a smaller model does not help.","t":"Check before you send = RETRY evaluation.","k":["retry evaluation","completeness","app logic"]},{"id":"ai-103-39","d":"operations","x":"You have a customer support agent built by using the Microsoft Foundry Agent Service. The agent calls an Azure OpenAI model deployment.\nDuring load testing, calls intermittently fail and return an HTTP 429 rate limit exceeded error.\nYou need to handle throttling to reduce call failures and improve reliability under load. The solution must remain within the service and model limits.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Implement a retry policy that uses exponential backoff and jitter.","Create a new thread and retry the calls immediately.","Spit uploaded content into smaller files.","Reduce the number of registered tools."],"a":[0],"e":"HTTP 429 means throttling. Retrying with exponential backoff and random jitter spaces out retries so they do not collide, improving reliability within limits. Immediate retries make throttling worse.","t":"429? BACK OFF exponentially + add JITTER.","k":["429","exponential backoff","jitter","throttling"]},{"id":"ai-103-40","d":"evaluation","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nYou use a GitHub Actions workflow for CI/CD.\nYou need to configure the workflow to automatically evaluate the agent when a pull request (PR) is created and prevent branches from merging if the evaluation results do NOT meet the defined thresholds.\nHow should you configure the workflow? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["An Azure Login action that uses OpenID Connect (OIDC); Send an alert","A personal access token (PAT); Lock the target branch","An Azure Login action that uses OpenID Connect (OIDC); Fail","A user-assigned managed identity; Send an alert"],"a":[2],"e":"Use the Azure Login action with OpenID Connect to get short-lived Azure credentials without storing long-lived secrets. When evaluation misses thresholds, the job must Fail so the required PR check fails and branch protection blocks the merge. An alert cannot block a merge.","t":"OIDC to log in, FAIL to block the merge.","k":["OIDC","GitHub Actions","fail check","branch protection"]},{"id":"ai-103-41","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nUsers report that some responses omit required regulatory clauses, even when the clauses are present in the retrieved content.\nYou need to improve response completeness.\nSolution: You increase the value of the max_tokens parameter.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"Yes. If clauses that are in the retrieved content are missing, a low output token limit may be cutting the summary short. Raising max_tokens gives the model room to include them.","t":"Content is there but answer is cut = raise MAX_TOKENS. Yes.","k":["max_tokens","truncation","Yes"]},{"id":"ai-103-42","d":"evaluation","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nUsers report that some responses omit required regulatory clauses, even when the clauses are present in the retrieved content.\nYou need to improve response completeness.\nSolution: You add a reflection pass that regenerates the response if the required clauses are missing.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Yes. A reflection pass checks the summary against the required clauses before returning it and regenerates or fixes the answer if something is missing. That directly improves completeness.","t":"Self-check and redo = REFLECTION. Yes.","k":["reflection pass","completeness","Yes"]},{"id":"ai-103-43","d":"models","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nUsers report that some responses omit required regulatory clauses, even when the clauses are present in the retrieved content.\nYou need to improve response completeness.\nSolution: You increase the value of the temperature parameter.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. Higher temperature adds randomness and variation; it does not make required clauses appear and can make coverage less predictable.","t":"Temperature = randomness, not completeness. No.","k":["temperature","No"]},{"id":"ai-103-44","d":"evaluation","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nUsers report that some responses omit required regulatory clauses, even when the clauses are present in the retrieved content.\nYou need to improve response completeness.\nSolution: You run an evaluation flow that scores responses for completeness and blocks responses that fall below a defined threshold.\nDoes this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"Yes. An evaluation flow scores each response for completeness and blocks those below the threshold, so incomplete summaries never reach users. It can also trigger a retry.","t":"Score it, block the weak ones. Yes.","k":["evaluation flow","threshold","Yes"]},{"id":"ai-103-45","d":"multimodal","x":"You are creating an image-editing workflow in a Microsoft Foundry project.\nThe workflow must meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that background objects can be removed by applying a mask-based inpainting edit.\nPreserve the original lighting and style of the edited images.\nUse the built-in image editing controls, NOT a custom model.\nYou need to ensure that image edits apply exclusively inside the masked area.\nHow should you configure the workflow?","o":["Set generation mode to image_variation and provide the original image as a reference.","Enable image_to_image mode and a high-strength value to regenerate the full image based on the prompt.","Enable text_to_image mode and a prompt describing the desired background removal.","Enable mask_inpainting and supply both the input image and a mask indicating which part of the image to modify."],"a":[3],"e":"Mask inpainting edits only the area marked by the mask, keeping lighting, style and all pixels outside it. Variation, text-to-image and high-strength image-to-image change much more of the image.","t":"Edit only one spot = MASK inpainting.","k":["mask inpainting","image editing"]},{"id":"ai-103-46","d":"multimodal","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that generates product marketing images from text prompts.\nAfter publishing several images, the legal team at your company identifies a competitor’s logo on a sign in the background of an image.\nYou need to remove only the logo, while preserving the rest of the image.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Apply a mask-based inpainting edit to the part of the image that contains the logo.","Rerun the prompt by using a different random seed.","Increase the prompt guidance strength.","Modify the original prompt to exclude brand names."],"a":[0],"e":"Mask-based inpainting limits regeneration to the selected logo area, so the logo is removed while the rest of the image stays the same. New seeds, stronger guidance or prompt edits make a new image.","t":"Remove one thing, keep the rest = MASK inpainting.","k":["mask inpainting","logo removal"]},{"id":"ai-103-47","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent ingests scanned PDF vendor invoices that contain tables and embedded QR codes.\nThe agent must preserve the PDF layout in the extracted output to ensure that downstream processing can reference sections and tables.\nYou plan to call Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools.\nYou need to extract content and layout elements and detect QR codes without requiring a language model deployment.\nWhich built-in analyzer should you use?","o":["prebuilt-layout","prebuilt-documentFieldSchema","prebuilt-documentSearch","prebuilt-read"],"a":[0],"e":"The prebuilt-layout analyzer extracts content plus layout elements like sections and tables and detects barcodes and QR codes, with no LLM deployment needed. prebuilt-read only extracts text.","t":"Tables + layout + QR = prebuilt-LAYOUT.","k":["prebuilt-layout","QR code","Content Understanding"]},{"id":"ai-103-48","d":"multimodal","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent and an image generation model deployment.\nThe agent generates original images from user-supplied product photos.\nYou need to ensure that the generated images maintain the product identity and visual characteristics of the provided photo.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Apply a groundedness detection filter.","Include a prompt and input image in the request.","Set the input_fidelity parameter to high.","Decrease the value of the temperature parameter."],"a":[2],"e":"Setting input_fidelity to high tells the model to preserve the identity and visual details of the supplied product photo. Sending an image is required but does not set how closely to match it. Groundedness and temperature are unrelated.","t":"Keep the product looking the same = input_FIDELITY high.","k":["input_fidelity","image generation"]},{"id":"ai-103-49","d":"safety","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent accepts user-uploaded screenshots and uses a multimodal chat model.\nSome screenshots contain potentially malicious embedded text.\nYou need to prevent a prompt injection attack and ensure that third-party content is treated as lower trust.\nHow should you configure prompt shields for document attacks? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Set action to annotate; Create a custom blocklist.","Set action to block; Use optical character recognition (OCR) to extract the text from the images first.","Set action to block; Enable Spotlighting.","Disable the shield; Use optical character recognition (OCR) to extract the text from the images first."],"a":[2],"e":"Set the document prompt shield action to block so detected document attacks cannot affect the agent run. Enable Spotlighting to mark third-party content as untrusted, so embedded text is not treated as instructions. Annotate does not stop attacks and OCR adds no trust boundary.","t":"BLOCK attacks + SPOTLIGHT untrusted content.","k":["prompt shields","block","Spotlighting"]},{"id":"ai-103-50","d":"safety","x":"You are deploying a support agent that enables users to upload photos.\nYou need to automatically classify uploaded images for harmful content. The solution must block content based on severity levels.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Apply keyword scanning to optical character recognition (OCR) output by using Azure Vision in Foundry Tools.","Implement image moderation.","Use blocklists.","Enable prompt shields."],"a":[1],"e":"Image moderation classifies images by harm category and returns severity levels for automatic blocking. OCR keyword scans only see text, prompt shields target injection, and blocklists match terms.","t":"Harmful pictures by severity = IMAGE moderation.","k":["image moderation","severity levels"]},{"id":"ai-103-51","d":"safety","x":"You have an app named App1 that uses a Microsoft Foundry multimodal model deployment.\nApp1 runs optical character recognition (OCR) on uploaded images and appends the OCR output to the prompt as additional context.\nSome uploaded images contain embedded text.\nYou need to prevent potentially malicious instructions from being processed by the model.\nWhat should you use?","o":["prompt shields for documents","image moderation","protected material text","prompt shields for user prompts"],"a":[0],"e":"OCR output from uploads is third-party document content that may contain indirect prompt injection. Prompt shields for documents detect such instructions before the model processes them. Image moderation checks visuals and user-prompt shields cover typed input.","t":"Text pulled from files = DOCUMENT shield.","k":["prompt shields for documents","OCR","indirect injection"]},{"id":"ai-103-52","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nYou need to process mixed-format documents that contain scanned text, tables, and multicolumn layouts. The extracted content must preserve the document structure and be converted into the Markdown format for downstream reasoning.\nWhat should you configure first?","o":["an Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools analyzer","a generative chat completion request","an Azure OpenAI Responses API call that uses a multimodal model","an Azure Language in Foundry Tools text analysis model deployment"],"a":[0],"e":"Configure an Azure Content Understanding analyzer first to extract scanned text and layout, including tables and multi-column content, and produce structured Markdown for later reasoning. Language text analysis does not preserve layout, and chat calls are not a reliable first extraction step.","t":"Messy docs to Markdown = CONTENT UNDERSTANDING first.","k":["Content Understanding","Markdown","layout"]},{"id":"ai-103-53","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have an application that processes scanned PDF invoices. The invoices have varied layouts and include multipage tables.\nYou have a pipeline that uses optical character recognition (OCR) and extracts totals and invoice numbers. The results are often incorrect because the document structure is ignored.\nYou need to implement a solution that provides OCR, layout analysis, and template-generalizing field extraction. The solution must NOT require training a custom model. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","o":["an Azure Machine Learning model","Azure Language in Foundry Tools","Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools"],"a":[2],"e":"Azure Content Understanding provides OCR, layout analysis and field extraction that generalizes across templates, with no custom model training and low admin effort. Language is for text analytics and Azure ML needs building and training.","t":"OCR + layout + fields, no training = CONTENT UNDERSTANDING.","k":["Content Understanding","field extraction","no training"]},{"id":"ai-103-54","d":"rag","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses a knowledge source built from documents stored in Azure Blob Storage. The documents include digitally scanned PDFs that contain multipage tables.\nYou have an ingestion job that extracts only plain text, causing loss of table structure, headings, and page-number metadata.\nUsers frequently ask questions that require the retrieval of specific table rows across the pages.\nYou need to configure an ingestion job for a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline that performs optical character recognition (OCR) on scanned PDFs, preserves tables and headings as structure-aware chunks, and stores page-number metadata with each chunk.\nHow should you configure the ingestion job?","o":["Use OCR and page-level chunking.","Use page-level OCR extraction and store each page as a single chunk.","Use advanced data parsing to reingest the documents.","Use basic parsing and fixed-size chunking."],"a":[2],"e":"Advanced data parsing re-ingests documents with structure-aware processing: OCR for scanned PDFs, preserved headings and tables, and page-level metadata on chunks. Page-level or fixed-size chunking loses table structure.","t":"Structure-aware chunks = ADVANCED parsing.","k":["advanced data parsing","structure-aware chunking","page metadata"]},{"id":"ai-103-55","d":"rag","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses Azure AI Search as the retriever.\nYou plan to ingest PDF into an Azure AI Search index to ensure that the agent can ground responses in texts in both documents and embedded images.\nUsers require citations that link to the source files.\nYou need to ensure that during indexing, the images are extracted into a structure that can be used as input for the built-in optical character recognition (OCR) skill.\nWhich indexing approach should you use?","o":["an indexer to extract image data into a normalized_images collection","the outputFieldMappings parameter to write image data to a searchable field","a skillset to run the OCR skill directly against the content field of the index","a Shaper skill to restructure the OCR input"],"a":[0],"e":"Configure the indexer to extract embedded images into the normalized_images collection. The built-in OCR skill then processes each normalized image during enrichment, and source file metadata is kept for citations.","t":"OCR on images needs NORMALIZED_IMAGES from the indexer.","k":["normalized_images","OCR skill","indexer"]},{"id":"ai-103-56","d":"content-understanding","x":"Question: 56\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to recommend an invoice review solution that resolves the issue reported by the finance department.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","o":["chat completions","Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools","Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools","Image Analysis"],"a":[2],"e":"Azure Content Understanding analyzes both visual layout and text of complex invoices with tables, logos and changing layouts, extracting structured data to compare against vendor contract terms. Chat completions or Image Analysis alone are not a document pipeline.","t":"Multimodal invoice review = CONTENT UNDERSTANDING.","k":["Content Understanding","invoices","multimodal"]},{"id":"ai-103-57","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe knowledge source for the agent is a set of scanned PDF troubleshooting guides stored in Azure Blob Storage. The guide pages contain two-column layouts and tables.\nYou use Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools to process the PDFs.\nYou plan to ingest the processed content into an index for Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) and store extracted fields for downstream automation.\nStakeholders must be able to verify where each extracted field value came from in the original PDF and route low-reliability extractions for manual review.\nYou need to ensure that the Content Understanding document analyzer output includes a per-field confidence score and source grounding to locations within the source document.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Configure the analyzer to use generative extraction for all fields.","Set enableSegment to true.","Enable estimateFieldSourceAndConfidence.","Provide labeled samples."],"a":[2],"e":"Enable estimateFieldSourceAndConfidence so each extracted field includes an estimated confidence score and grounding to its location in the source document. Low-confidence results can then go to manual review.","t":"Field SOURCE + CONFIDENCE = estimateFieldSourceAndConfidence.","k":["estimateFieldSourceAndConfidence","confidence score","grounding"]},{"id":"ai-103-58","d":"speech","x":"You are building a speech processing solution in Microsoft Foundry for a customer support platform.\nThe platform will transcribe live phone calls, so that supervisors at your company can view call transcripts and detect issues while the calls are in progress. The call audio will arrive as a continuous stream from the telephony system.\nYou need to ensure that the call transcripts appear within only a few seconds of the audio stream.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Use real-time speech to text to process streaming audio input.","Use speech translation to generate the transcripts into multiple languages.","Use text to speech by using a custom neural voice.","Run a batch transcription job on recorded audio files."],"a":[0],"e":"Real-time speech to text handles continuous audio streams and returns interim and final results with low latency, so supervisors see transcripts within seconds. Batch transcription works on finished recordings.","t":"Live stream = REAL-TIME STT, not batch.","k":["real-time speech to text","streaming"]},{"id":"ai-103-59","d":"speech","x":"You are creating an agent workflow in a Microsoft Foundry project to support natural voice interactions.\nThe agent must receive continuous audio input, convert the input into text for reasoning, and then return spoken responses to a user. The workflow must meet the following requirements:\nSupport turn-taking dynamics, where the agent begins to generate the speech output before the user finishes speaking.\nOperate with low latency to maintain conversational experience.\nYou need to enable both speech to text and text to speech in a real-time agent interaction.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Use batch transcription to convert the audio input and return text responses from the agent.","Use speech translation to convert the audio into another language and return the translated text.","Use an embeddings model to encode the audio, and then decode the audio into text and speech.","Use real-time speech to text for incoming audio and text to speech for agent responses."],"a":[3],"e":"Real-time speech to text processes continuous input with low latency, and text to speech turns the agent's replies into audio. Together they support a fast voice conversation with turn-taking.","t":"Ears = real-time STT. Mouth = TTS.","k":["speech to text","text to speech","voice agent"]},{"id":"ai-103-60","d":"speech","x":"You have an application named App1 that uses Azure Speech in Foundry Tools to transcribe live calls.\nTranscript segments often contain both English and Spanish. App1 sends each segment to Azure Translator in Foundry Tools to translate to another language.\nSometimes, mixed-language segments result in incomplete or incorrect translations.\nYou need to reduce translation errors. The solution must ensure that the entire transcript is translated successfully.\nWhat should you do before sending the segments to Translator?","o":["Split the mixed-language segments into single-language segments and translate each segment separately.","Use document translation to translate the entire transcript as a single document.","Specify English as the source language in the translation request for all the segments.","Enable automatic language detection for the translation request."],"a":[0],"e":"Split mixed-language segments into single-language pieces before translating, so each request has one consistent source language. Auto-detect picks a dominant language, forcing English mistranslates Spanish, and document translation does not suit live segments.","t":"Mixed languages? SPLIT first, then translate.","k":["split segments","Translator","mixed language"]},{"id":"ai-103-61","d":"rag","x":"Question: 61\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to configure an indexing pipeline for Agent1 to retrieve the relevant product information in storage1. The solution must meet the technical requirement.\nWhich two built-in skills should you use? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Azure OpenAI Embedding","Entity Recognition","Language Detection","key phrase extraction","Text Split","Merge"],"a":[0,4],"e":"The Text Split skill chunks the product sheets and the Azure OpenAI Embedding skill creates vectors for those chunks. Together they build the chunked vector index for semantic and vector search.","t":"Chunk it (Text Split), vector it (Embedding).","k":["Text Split","Azure OpenAI Embedding","vector index"]},{"id":"ai-103-62","d":"rag","x":"Question: 62\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to recommend a solution to support the planned changes and technical requirements for Agent1 to use the product information stored in storage1.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Azure AI Search","Grounding with Bing Search","Azure Translator in Foundry Tools","Azure Document intelligence in Foundry Tools"],"a":[0],"e":"Azure AI Search provides the semantic and vector index needed to retrieve product sheet content from storage1 and ground Agent1's answers. Translator does not retrieve, Bing is public web, and Document Intelligence only extracts.","t":"Private docs retrieval = AZURE AI SEARCH.","k":["Azure AI Search","vector search","grounding"]},{"id":"ai-103-63","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1.\nProject1 contains an application that processes PDF vendor invoices.\nYou need to configure Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools to generate a Markdown output that preserves the sections and table structure of the PDFs. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Configure content=markdown when you analyze the document.","Configure output=figures when you analyze the PDF.","Increase the confidence threshold.","Set the output_content_format=ContentFormat.MARKDOWN value."],"a":[3],"e":"Set output_content_format to ContentFormat.MARKDOWN when analyzing. The built-in Markdown output keeps sections and tables with no extra post-processing.","t":"Markdown out = output_content_format MARKDOWN.","k":["Document Intelligence","Markdown output"]},{"id":"ai-103-64","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that ingests scanned PDF invoices stored in Azure Blob Storage. Each invoice contains printed fine items and has a table-based layout.\nExtracted results are stored as structured JSON and used as grounding data for an agent in a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) solution.\nYou need to create a single analyzer that meets the following requirements:\nExtracts the invoice number, invoice date, vendor name, and total amount across varying templates\nReturns confidence scores so that results with confidence below 0.80 can be routed for supervisor review\nWhat should you use?","o":["the Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools prebuilt-layout analyzer","a custom Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools analyzer that defines the required fields as the extracted fields and the returned confidence scores for routing","the Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools prebuilt-documentSearch analyzer and search.score from the Azure AI Search results for routing","a Foundry agent that has groundedness guardrails enabled to extract invoice fields and confidence scores"],"a":[1],"e":"A custom Content Understanding analyzer lets you define the invoice fields to extract across templates and returns per-field confidence so values below 0.80 can be routed for review. Prebuilt-layout has no business fields and search scores are retrieval relevance.","t":"Your own fields + confidence = CUSTOM analyzer.","k":["custom analyzer","field confidence","invoices"]},{"id":"ai-103-65","d":"evaluation","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that uses Azure AI Search to ground an agent in internal documentation.\nAfter a recent content update, users report that the agent’s answers have become less accurate.\nYou need to identify whether the retrieved content is negatively influencing the model’s generated responses.\nWhich observability signal should you review?","o":["latency breakdown traces","prediction drift metrics","indexer status and failure history","groundedness evaluation metrics"],"a":[3],"e":"Groundedness evaluation metrics show whether answers are supported by the retrieved context, revealing whether updated content leads to unsupported answers. Indexer status, latency traces and prediction drift measure other things.","t":"Is the answer backed by retrieved text? GROUNDEDNESS.","k":["groundedness","retrieved content"]},{"id":"ai-103-66","d":"video","x":"Question: 66\nHOTSPOT - Case Study\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to ensure that the marketing department can generate videos by using the model deployed to Project2.\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["retrieve; create","download_content; list","create; retrieve","list; download_content"],"a":[2],"e":"Call client.videos.create to submit the async video job, then poll client.videos.retrieve(video.id) until the status is completed, failed or cancelled. list returns a collection and download_content is used after completion.","t":"Create, then retrieve (poll).","k":["videos.create","videos.retrieve","async"]},{"id":"ai-103-67","d":"agents","x":"Question: 67\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to configure personalized user interactions for Agent1. The solution must meet the business requirements.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","o":["knowledge","tools","guardrails","memory"],"a":[3],"e":"Memory lets Agent1 keep conversation context and recall relevant details from earlier interactions, giving a personalized experience. Knowledge adds indexed info, guardrails restrict behavior, and tools take actions.","t":"Personalized + recall past = MEMORY.","k":["memory","personalization"]},{"id":"ai-103-68","d":"security","x":"Question: 68\nHOTSPOT - Case Study\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study.\nTo answer the case study questions, you will bed to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study.\nA Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, select the “Next” button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the “Question” button to return to the question.\nOverview -\nCompany Information -\nContoso, Ltd is a multinational retail company that builds, deploys, and manages generative AI and agent-based solutions by using Microsoft Foundry.\nExisting Environment -\nIdentity Environment -\nContoso uses Microsoft Entra ID for identity management, authentication, and authorization capabilities that enable agents to access organizational resources and services.\nContoso recently formed a new AI engineering team named Agent1Dev Team to optimize and maintain existing AI solutions.\nThe team collaborates with solution architects, DevOps engineers, and security engineers to design, implement. monitor, and secure AI applications.\nContoso also has a team named Agent1Test Team that is responsible for validating AI solutions before the solution deployments.\nGenerative Environment -\nContoso has a Microsoft Foundry deployment that contains two projects named Project1 and Project2.\nProject1 -\nProject1 contains a customer support agent named Agent1 that assists customers with product inquiries and troubleshooting requests.\nAgent1 has the following configurations:\nAgent1 uses a base model deployment.\nA safety evaluation pipeline is NOT enabled.\nTool invocation approval workflows are NOT enabled.\nConversation memory constraints are NOT configured.\nAgent1 interacts with customers by using digital support channels and answers general questions about Contoso products.\nProject1 is deployed to an Azure region located in the European Union (EU).\nAgent1Dev Team will use Project1 to optimize and maintain Agent1.\nProject2 -\nProject2 contains a deployed video generation model. The marketing department at Contoso has access to Project2 and plans to use the model to develop a video creation solution.\nDevelopment of the solution is incomplete.\nData Environment -\nContoso stores product-related information in Azure resources that support AI applications.\nThe Azure environment contains an Azure Blob Storage account named storage1 that stores product detail sheets for all the Contoso products.\nThe product sheets include specifications, feature descriptions, and product support information that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions. The product sheets are stored in the PDF format.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso identifies the following issues:\nAgent1 has only general knowledge of the Contoso products.\nA recent chat interaction with Agent1 was analyzed for sentiment. The results of the analysis have NOT been processed yet.\nAgent1 does NOT use the detailed product information in the product sheets stored in storage1 when responding to customer questions.\nThe finance department at Contoso reports that vendor invoices must be reviewed manually to ensure that the invoices match the terms defined in the vendor contracts. The invoices contain tables, logos, and varied layouts that make the documents difficult to process consistently.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso plans to implement the following changes:\nImplement a solution for Project1 that analyzes the vendor invoices by evaluating both the visual layout and the textual content of the invoices, so that the invoice details can be verified against the vendor contract terms.\nUpdate the base model deployment used by Agent1 and standardize the model version to ensure continuity and consistent responses.\nEnable Agent1 to retrieve and use the detailed product information from the product sheets stored in storage1.\nImplement an indexing solution for the product sheets that Agent1 can use to answer customer questions.\nComplete the development of the video creation solution.\nTechnical Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following technical requirements:\nThe model deployment used by Agent1 must support scalable, high-throughput generative AI workloads and dynamically scale to handle variable customer support traffic, without requiring reserved throughput capacity.\nThe product sheets must be processed by using an indexing pipeline that enables semantic and vector search, so that Agent1 can retrieve the relevant product information.\nResponses generated by using the product sheet information must be relevant, complete, and accurate.\nAgent1 must be able to use the product sheets to answer natural language questions about product details.\nThe model version used by Agent1 must remain consistent to ensure stable responses.\nThe data processed by the model must remain within the EU.\nSecurity and Compliance Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security and compliance requirements:\nAPI keys must NOT be used to access Foundry-deployed models.\nAccess to the Azure resources must follow the principle of least privilege.\nThe developers at Contoso must authenticate to Microsoft Foundry resources by using Microsoft Entra authentication.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Dev Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Dev.\nAccess to Project1 must be assigned to the members of Agent1Test Team by using a security group named SC_Agent1_Test.\nAgent1 must never reveal customer information, even if a document that contains customer data is added erroneously to the product sheet repository in storage1.\nThe product sheets might contain images that include embedded text. Agent1 must be protected from malicious instructions potentially hidden within the images.\nBusiness Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following business requirements:\nUsers that interact with Agent1 must have a personalized experience in future interactions, including the ability for Agent1 to retain conversation context and recall relevant information from previous interactions.\nAgent1 must answer questions only about the products sold by Contoso.\nYou need to ensure that Agent1Dev Team can access Agent1. The solution must meet the security and compliance requirements.\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["AzureKeyCredential(); get","None; get_version","DefaultAzureCredential(); get","DefaultAzureCredential(); create_version"],"a":[2],"e":"DefaultAzureCredential uses Microsoft Entra auth, meeting the no-API-key rule once the dev group has the right RBAC role. Use project_client.agents.get(agent_name=...) to fetch the existing agent. create_version makes a new version and get_version targets a specific one.","t":"Entra login + GET existing agent.","k":["DefaultAzureCredential","agents.get","RBAC"]},{"id":"ai-103-69","d":"evaluation","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) chat solution used by customer support agents.\nYou are adding an automated pre-production evaluation step to a CI/CD pipeline named Pipeline1. The evaluation will run against a labeled test dataset that contains support questions and the expected grounding context.\nYou need to ensure that Pipeline1 fails if unsupported content or a retrieval mismatch exceeds a defined threshold:\n•responses include claims not supported by the retrieved source content\n•retrieved source content does not align with the labeled expected context\nWhich two built-in evaluators should you use in Pipeline1? Each correct answer presents pat of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Fluency","Coherence","Groundedness","Retrieval","Response Completeness"],"a":[2,3],"e":"The Groundedness evaluator flags claims not supported by retrieved sources. The Retrieval evaluator checks whether retrieved context matches the labeled expected context. Fluency, coherence and completeness measure other aspects.","t":"Claims backed = GROUNDEDNESS. Right docs fetched = RETRIEVAL.","k":["groundedness","retrieval evaluator"]},{"id":"ai-103-70","d":"agents","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a support-ticket triage agent built by using the Foundry Agent Service.\nThe agent uses tool to classify the ticket type and sot the ticket priority.\nSometimes, the same support case continues across multiple sessions over several days.\nYou need to persist state by using a durable ID to ensure that the agent can automatically reuse the full interaction history. The solution must preserve previous user messages, tool calls and tool outputs across turns and sessions.\nWhich runtime component should you use?","o":["response","conversation","agent","output item"],"a":[1],"e":"A conversation has a durable ID and stores the full history, including user messages, agent responses, tool calls and outputs. Reuse its ID across turns and sessions. An agent is the configured worker, a response is one execution, and an output item is part of it.","t":"Durable history container = CONVERSATION.","k":["conversation","durable ID","history"]},{"id":"ai-103-71","d":"evaluation","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Founcy project that contains a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) solution.\nYou need to run a pre-production evaluation by using labeled CSV dataset that contains the query, context, response and ground truth. The evaluation must measure the following:\n•Whether responses address the user query\n•Whether responses are supported by the provided context\n•Whether responses contain sensitive or proprietary information\nWhich AI quality evaluation metrics should you use? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["GPT similarity and F1 score; Indirect attack","Groundedness and ROUGE score; Violent content","Coherence and Fluency; Hateful and unfair content","Groundedness and Relevance; Protected material"],"a":[3],"e":"Relevance checks whether the response answers the query, groundedness checks support by context, and the protected material evaluator detects proprietary content. The other pairs measure style, similarity or other risks.","t":"Answers it? RELEVANCE. Backed? GROUNDEDNESS. Secret stuff? PROTECTED material.","k":["groundedness","relevance","protected material"]},{"id":"ai-103-72","d":"evaluation","x":"You plan to configure an evaluation in Microsoft Foundry for a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) chat app.\nYou need to provide scores for groundedness, relevance, and harmful content categories.\nWhich two evaluation categories can you use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["AI quality (NLP) metrics","AI quality (AI assisted) metrics","risk and safety metrics","similarity evaluators","fluency evaluator"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Risk and safety metrics score harmful content categories. AI quality (AI assisted) metrics include model-judged groundedness and relevance for RAG responses. NLP metrics, similarity and fluency do not cover these.","t":"Harm = RISK & SAFETY. RAG quality = AI-ASSISTED.","k":["risk and safety","AI-assisted metrics"]},{"id":"ai-103-73","d":"agents","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent uses two tools to perform the following actions:\n•Use Azure AI Search to retrieve answers from a private product documentation index.\n•Use the web search tool to retrieve public information on the internet.\nYou need to ensure that for a specific run, the agent deterministically retrieves information only from the internet.\nTo what should you set tool_choice?","o":["“auto”","{“type”: “bing_grouding”}","“required”","{“type”: “azure_ai-search”}"],"a":[1],"e":"Name the Bing grounding (web search) tool in tool_choice to force that tool for the run. auto lets the model pick, required forces some tool but not which one, and the AI Search type forces private docs.","t":"Force ONE specific tool = name its TYPE.","k":["tool_choice","bing_grounding","specific tool"]},{"id":"ai-103-74","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a customer support agent built on a deployed chat model.\nThe agent responses are validated by using an automated testing system that compares generated answers to stored expected outputs. Identical prompts must return consistent response to prevent automated test failures.\nYou need to reduce response variability, without modifying the prompt or reducing factual accuracy.\nWhat should you do for the model?","o":["Decrease the temperature parameter.","Remove stop sequences from the requests.","Increase the temperature parameter.","Increase the max_tokens parameter."],"a":[0],"e":"Lowering temperature reduces sampling randomness, so identical prompts give more consistent output while using the same facts. max_tokens and stop sequences control length, and raising temperature adds randomness.","t":"Consistent output = LOWER temperature.","k":["temperature","consistency"]},{"id":"ai-103-75","d":"prompt-engineering","x":"You are developing prompts for a Micosoft Foundry project that classifies incoming support tickets by category.\nYou need to improve accuracy by showing the model how correct classifications look, without retaining the model or storing knowledge permanently.\nWhich prompt engineering approach should you use?","o":["few-shot learning","Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","chain of thought","zero-shot learning"],"a":[0],"e":"Few-shot prompting puts correctly classified examples in the prompt to show the desired pattern, without fine-tuning or permanently storing anything. Zero-shot gives no examples, chain of thought guides reasoning, and RAG retrieves outside info.","t":"Show examples in the prompt = FEW-SHOT.","k":["few-shot","prompt engineering","classification"]},{"id":"ai-103-76","d":"agents","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent for a customer support chat app. The agent uses a memory store and a memory search tool.\nYou need to ensure that the conversation history does NOT persist across separate sessions.\nTo what should you set the scope of the memory tool?","o":["{{$conversationId}}","{{$userId}}","session","global"],"a":[2],"e":"Setting the memory tool scope to session isolates history to the current session so it is not reused later. conversationId or userId scopes persist across interactions, and global shares memory broadly.","t":"No carry-over between sessions = scope SESSION.","k":["memory scope","session"]},{"id":"ai-103-77","d":"agents","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nYou need to enable long-term memory to ensure that the agent can recall user preferences across separate conversations. Stored memories must be isolated per authenticated user without the client application manually generating user IDs.\nHow should you complete the Python code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["scope: \"{{$userId}}\"; tools: [memory_tool]","scope: \"session\"; tools: [memory_tool]","scope: \"{{$conversationId}}\"; tools: [mem_store_name]","scope: \"{{$userId}}\"; tools: [mem_store_name]"],"a":[0],"e":"Use {{$userId}} as the memory scope so Foundry uses the authenticated user's identity to isolate their memories across conversations without the client creating IDs. Add the memory_tool object to tools; mem_store_name is just a string.","t":"Per-user memory = {{$userId}} + memory_tool object.","k":["memory scope","$userId","memory_tool"]},{"id":"ai-103-78","d":"observability","x":"You have a web app named App1 that processes user prompts by integrating with a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1. App1 performs the following actions:\n•Sends prompts directly to a model by using the Azure OpenAI Responses API\n•Invokes the Azure AI Content Safety tool by using a Foundry connection within the same request\nYou need to configure end-to-end visibility into each step of the request workflow.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Enable logging by using Foundry Local.","Enable application tracing in Project1.","Route requests through the Azure OpenAI endpoint.","Enable logging by using the client SDK for Content Safety."],"a":[1],"e":"Application tracing in the Foundry project captures the whole request workflow, including the Responses API model call and the Content Safety tool call through the connection, as correlated steps. SDK logging covers only one part and Foundry Local is for local dev.","t":"End-to-end visibility = APPLICATION TRACING.","k":["application tracing","end-to-end","Foundry"]},{"id":"ai-103-79","d":"agents","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains two agents named PolicyWriter and RskReviewer.\nPolicyWriter generates daft updates for customer polices, and RiskReviewer reviews the drafts.\nIn the visual builder, you need to create a workflow that meets the following requirements:\n•Finalizes low-risk updates without manual intervention\n•Ensures predictable execution across the agents\n•Requires user approval for highs updates\nWhat should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each comet selection is worth one point.","o":["The sequential template that passes outputs node-by-node; Add a Conditional statement.","The sequential template that passes outputs node-by-node; Add a Basic chat node.","The group chat template to dynamically route control between the agents; Add an Ask a question node.","The human-in-the-loop template that pauses execution of the workflow for input; Add a Basic chat node."],"a":[0],"e":"The sequential template passes output from PolicyWriter to RiskReviewer in a fixed, predictable order. A Conditional statement after review sends low-risk drafts to auto-finalize and high-risk drafts to approval. Group chat is dynamic and human-in-the-loop would pause everything.","t":"Predictable = SEQUENTIAL. Risk split = CONDITIONAL.","k":["sequential template","conditional","workflow"]},{"id":"ai-103-80","d":"observability","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent named Agent1.\nAgent runs successful, but Foundry Control Plane does NOT display values for error rates, runs, and token usage, and the Traces tab is empty.\nYou need to ensure that Found Control Plane displays the appropriate values for Agent1.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Assign to a Log Analytics workspace to Agent1.","Update Agent1 to a new version.","Restart Agent from Foundry Control Plan","Enable Application Insights for Agent1."],"a":[3],"e":"Enable Application Insights for Agent1 so runs, error rates, token usage and traces are collected and shown in Foundry Control Plane. A new version or restart does not configure telemetry, and linking a Log Analytics workspace alone does not turn on trace collection.","t":"Empty dashboards and traces = turn on APP INSIGHTS.","k":["Application Insights","Foundry Control Plane","traces"]},{"id":"ai-103-81","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Too to process vendor onboarding packets. The packs include digital PDFs that contain tables and hyperlinks.\nThe extracted content is indexed for search and provided to a downstream agent in the Markdown format.\nYou need to generate a Markdown output that has a layout and a semantic structure optimized for Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) workflows.\nWhich built-in analyzer should you use?","o":["prebuilt-layout","prebuilt-documentFieldSchema","prebuilt-read","prebuilt-documentSearch"],"a":[3],"e":"prebuilt-documentSearch produces Markdown designed for search and RAG, keeping layout and adding semantic structure for retrieval and grounding. prebuilt-layout focuses on structural elements, prebuilt-read on text, and documentFieldSchema on fields.","t":"Markdown for RAG/search = prebuilt-DOCUMENTSEARCH.","k":["prebuilt-documentSearch","RAG","Markdown"]},{"id":"ai-103-82","d":"rag","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses Azure AI Search for Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).\nYou plan to ingest and index PDF product manuals.\nYou need to build a solution that supports semantic similarity matching. The solution must ensure that the agent retrieves relevant data when user questions use different wording than the product manuals.\nWhich indexing approach should you use?","o":["suggesters","semantic ranking","vector search","analyzers"],"a":[2],"e":"Vector search uses embeddings to represent manual content and questions by meaning, so retrieval works even when wording differs. Semantic ranking reorders keyword results, suggesters do type-ahead, and analyzers tokenize text.","t":"Different words, same meaning = VECTORS.","k":["vector search","embeddings","semantic similarity"]},{"id":"ai-103-83","d":"rag","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry agent that grounds responses from an Azure Search index that contains the following:\n•Searchable text fields for product names and product codes\n•A vector field that stores embeddings for product descriptions\nYou need to ensure that users can query the index by using the following:\n•Exact product names or codes\n•Natural language descriptions of the products\nWhat should you configure?","o":["hybrid search","keyword search only","vector search only","semantic search only"],"a":[0],"e":"Hybrid search combines keyword search, good for exact names and codes, with vector search, good for natural-language descriptions. Vector-only struggles with exact IDs and keyword-only misses meaning.","t":"Exact codes + natural language = HYBRID.","k":["hybrid search","keyword","vector"]},{"id":"ai-103-84","d":"rag","x":"You have an Azure AI Search indexer that ingests PDF policy manuals.\nClient applications must display page-level citations that have bounding polygons for both text and images.\nYou need to add a single built-in multimodal content extraction skill to the Azure AI Search skillset. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n•Provide text and image location metadata.\n•Extract tables that span multiple pages.\nWhat should you add?","o":["Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools","Document Layout","Document Extraction","GenAI Prompt"],"a":[1],"e":"The Document Layout skill is the built-in multimodal extraction skill that keeps document structure, gives text and image locations with bounding polygons, and handles tables spanning pages. Document Extraction lacks layout-aware output and GenAI Prompt is for enrichment.","t":"Polygons + page-spanning tables = DOCUMENT LAYOUT skill.","k":["Document Layout skill","bounding polygons","skillset"]},{"id":"ai-103-85","d":"rag","x":"You are building an Azure AI Search indexing pipeline named Pipeline1 that ingests invoices stored in Azure Blob Storage. The invoices are stored as scanned images.\nYou need to enable users to search invoice data across the invoice fields.\nWhich built-in skill should you add to the skillset of Pipeline1?","o":["Text Split","Image Analysis","Text Translation","optical character recognition (OCR)"],"a":[3],"e":"Add the OCR skill to pull searchable text, including invoice field values, from scanned images. Text Split needs text first, Translation changes language, and Image Analysis gives visual insights, not text.","t":"Scanned images to searchable text = OCR.","k":["OCR skill","scanned invoices","skillset"]},{"id":"ai-103-86","d":"content-understanding","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that uses Azure Content Understanding in Foundry Tools to analyze marketing videos.\nVideo segmentation is enabled.\nYou need to configure an analyzer to output a generated JSON field that describes the color scheme of each video segment.\nHow should you configure the analyzer? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Field value type: string; Field method: classify","Field value type: string; Field method: generate","Field value type: group; Field method: generate","Field value type: table; Field method: classify"],"a":[1],"e":"A color-scheme description is free-form generated text, so use field type string with method generate. classify picks from fixed categories, table is tabular and group is a structure.","t":"Describe in your own words = STRING + GENERATE.","k":["field method generate","string","video analyzer"]},{"id":"ai-103-87","d":"content-understanding","x":"You have an invoice-processing application named App1 that uses Azure Constant Understanding in Foundry Tools.\nYou are building a new Content Understanding pipeline named Pipeline1 that must meet the following requirements:\n•Compare an invoice to its related purchase order\n•Validate the voice against static vendor contact documents\n•Return a single structured output that includes discrepancy findings\nYou need to configure Pipeline1 and expose the pipeline as a single analyzer endpoint. What should you configure?","o":["a single-file task in standard mode that uses confidence scores enabled for the extracted fields.","a multiple-file task in pro mode that uses the vendor contract files as reference data","a single-file task in standard mode that uses the vendor contract provided as an additional document during analysis.","a multi-file task in standard mode that uses the invoice and purchase order as input to the analyzer"],"a":[1],"e":"Use a multiple-file task in pro mode with vendor contract files as reference data. Pro mode supports multi-step, cross-document reasoning to compare the invoice with the purchase order and check against the vendor documents in one structured output.","t":"Cross-check many docs + reference = MULTI-FILE PRO mode.","k":["pro mode","multi-file","reference data"]},{"id":"ai-103-88","d":"video","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that generates short promotional product videos.\nAfter several clips are approved, reviewers notice a small watermark in the top-right corner of some videos.\nYou need to remove the watermark without regenerating the videos.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Modify the original prompt to exclude watermarks.","Increase the guidance scale.","Crop the video by using the size parameter.","Apply a mask-based inpainting edit to the affected part of the video."],"a":[3],"e":"A mask-based inpainting edit targets only the marked watermark area of the existing video, removing it without regenerating the clip. Prompt or guidance changes need a new generation, and cropping changes the frame.","t":"Remove one spot without redo = MASK inpainting (again).","k":["mask inpainting","video edit","watermark"]},{"id":"ai-103-89","d":"multimodal","x":"You have a web app named App1 that sends requests to a multimodal chat model deployment in a Microsoft Foundry project.\nUser messages can contain both text and images.\nCurrently, App1 includes image URL: as plain text inside the message content so the model cannot recognize them as images.\nTraces show that the requests contain a single text message instead of a multimodal content array.\nYou need to send the message as a structured array that includes both the text portion and the image reference to ensure that the model can process the image correctly.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Add the image URL to the request metadata section, so the model can resolve the processing issue automatically.","Place the image URL inside the System Message and set type to image_url so the model loads the image at initialization.","Set the user message content array to include items that have type: text and type: image_url.","Encode the image to base64 and include the encoded data inside the content string of the user message."],"a":[2],"e":"Send user content as a structured array with a type text item and a type image_url item, so the model sees the image as image input. URLs or base64 inside a text string stay text, metadata is not model input, and system messages hold instructions.","t":"Multimodal = array with TEXT + IMAGE_URL items.","k":["multimodal","content array","image_url"]},{"id":"ai-103-90","d":"speech","x":"You are building a customer support web app named App1 in Microsoft Foundry that uses a GPT realtime model.\nApp1 must support:\n•Live, low-latency voice conversations that use Azure OpenAI\n•Streaming audio input from users and playback audio responses\nYou need to configure a connection method that supports real-time audio streaming in client application and targets approximately 100 ms latency.\nWhich connection method should you use?","o":["SIP","RTMP","WebRTC","WebSocket"],"a":[2],"e":"WebRTC is built for real-time, two-way media streaming in browsers and clients with very low latency, around 100 ms. RTMP is for broadcasting, SIP is telephony signaling, and WebSocket streams events but is not a media transport.","t":"~100 ms live voice in the client = WebRTC.","k":["WebRTC","realtime","low latency"]},{"id":"ai-103-91","d":"evaluation","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent and uses a GitHub repository. The repository contains a YAM file named File1 that defines the evaluation settings of the agent.\nYou need to create a GitHub Actions workflow that runs the evaluation defined in File1 when a pull request (PR) is opened. How should you configure the workflow?","o":["Set tenant-id to the Microsoft Entra tenant ID","Set model-deployment-name to the deployed model.","Set project-endpoint to the endpoint of the project.","Set evaluation-config to the path of the YAML file."],"a":[3],"e":"Set evaluation-config to the path of File1 so the GitHub Actions evaluation step loads and runs the YAML settings on pull requests. Endpoint, deployment name and tenant ID are environment or auth settings.","t":"Which evaluation to run = EVALUATION-CONFIG path.","k":["evaluation-config","GitHub Actions","YAML"]},{"id":"ai-103-92","d":"security","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent.\nThe agent uses a stored access key to retrieve secrets from an Azure key vault, which violates a keyless-credentials requirement.\nYou need to ensure that the agent can retrieve the secrets. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Enable a system-assigned managed identity at the Foundry level; Add a Key Vault access policy for the secrets.","Enable a system-assigned managed identity at the project level; Add an API key to application settings.","Enable a system-assigned managed identity at the project level; Assign the Key Vault Secrets User role to the managed identity.","Create a service principal and store the principal’s client secret; Assign the Key Vault Secrets User role to the managed identity."],"a":[2],"e":"Enable a system-assigned managed identity at the project level to give the agent a keyless identity scoped to the project, then assign only the Key Vault Secrets User role to read secrets. Foundry-level identity is broader, and client secrets or API keys are stored credentials.","t":"Keyless + least privilege = project MI + Secrets USER.","k":["managed identity","Key Vault Secrets User","least privilege"]},{"id":"ai-103-93","d":"evaluation","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a customer support application.\nYou create an evaluation named Run1 that has the following configurations:\n•Includes risk and safety metrics\n•Includes the protected material evaluation\n•Includes harmful content metrics that use a medium severity threshold\nYou create an evaluation named Run2 that has the following configurations:\n•Includes risk and safety metrics\n•Includes the protected material evaluation\n•Includes harmful content metrics that use a high severity threshold\nYou run both evaluations against a dataset named DB1 and receive the following results:\n•Content harm defect rate of Run1: 12%\n•Content harm defect rate of Run2: 4%\n•Protected material evaluation of Run1: 6%\n•Protected material evaluation of Run1: 6%\nYou start a fine-tuning job by using DB1. The job fails during automatic RAI checks for multiple content harm types.\nYou discover that the content filtering configuration is set to high severity.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["1: Yes; 2: No; 3: No","1: No; 2: No; 3: Yes","1: No; 2: Yes; 3: Yes","1: Yes; 2: Yes; 3: No"],"a":[2],"e":"1 No: lowering filter severity does not fix a fine-tuning job that failed RAI checks; the training data must be fixed. 2 Yes: a medium threshold flags a wider range of harm than high, so Run1's higher defect rate fits. 3 Yes: protected material is evaluated separately from harm severity, so the equal 6% shows those thresholds do not affect it.","t":"Fix the DATA, not the filter. Medium catches more. Protected material is separate.","k":["RAI checks","severity threshold","protected material"]},{"id":"ai-103-94","d":"observability","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains a multi-agent solution. The agents use tool calling to query internal systems.\nYou need to implement responsible AI auditing to meet the following requirements:\n•Capture all the nested operations across the entire agent run.\n•Record tool invocation arguments and retuned results as metadata.\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct targets Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need o dag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Hierarchical spans; Tool call attributes","Hierarchical spans; A KQL query filter","Sampling; Tool call attributes","A KQL query filter; Trace sampling policy"],"a":[0],"e":"Hierarchical spans form a parent-child trace that captures nested agent, model and tool operations across the run. Tool call attributes store tool arguments and returned results. Sampling and KQL filters decide what is kept or queried.","t":"Nested = HIERARCHICAL spans. Tool details = ATTRIBUTES.","k":["hierarchical spans","tool call attributes","tracing"]},{"id":"ai-103-95","d":"operations","x":"DRAG DROP -\n\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent uses threads and file uploads and calls an Azure OpenAI model deployment.\nDuring load testing, calls intermittently fall and return an HTTP 429 rate limit exceeded error. Some user uploads fail and generate an HTTP 400 file size exceeded error.\nYou need to mitigate the errors and reduce call failures. The solution must remain within the service and model limits.\nWhat should you do to resolve each error? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct errors. Each action may be used once, more than once or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each comet selection is worth one point.","o":["HTTP 429: Split content into smaller files before uploading the files; HTTP 400: Increase tenant-wide quotas.","HTTP 429: Implement exponential backoff and jitter in the retry logic; HTTP 400: Split content into smaller files before uploading the files.","HTTP 429: Use additional agent tools to reduce the message size; HTTP 400: Implement exponential backoff and jitter in the retry logic.","HTTP 429: Increase tenant-wide quotas; HTTP 400: Move large content to files and use file search."],"a":[1],"e":"HTTP 429 is throttling, so retry with exponential backoff and jitter. HTTP 400 file size exceeded breaks a size limit, so split content into smaller files before upload. Raising tenant quotas does not fit within current limits.","t":"429 = WAIT smartly. 400 too big = SPLIT files.","k":["429","400","backoff","file size"]},{"id":"ai-103-96","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project.\nYou need to deploy a model from the model catalog to support a search solution for internal policy documents. The model must generate vector representations of the text in the documents and of user queries.\nWhich type of model should you use?","o":["a small language model (SLM)","an image generation model","a large language model (LLM)","an embedding model"],"a":[3],"e":"An embedding model turns document text and queries into numeric vectors for semantic similarity search. LLMs and SLMs generate text and image models make images.","t":"Text to vectors = EMBEDDING model.","k":["embedding model","vector search"]},{"id":"ai-103-97","d":"models","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project.\nYou need to deploy a model from the model catalog to support real-time inference. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n•Use key-based authentication.\n•Support real-time REST API access.\n•NOT consume the vCPU quota of the virtual machines in the Azure subscription.\nWhich type of deployment should you use?","o":["batch","standard","self-hosted container","serverless API"],"a":[3],"e":"A serverless API deployment gives real-time REST inference with key auth while Microsoft manages compute, so it does not use your subscription's VM vCPU quota. Batch is async and self-hosted containers use your compute.","t":"No vCPU quota used = SERVERLESS API.","k":["serverless API","vCPU quota","real-time"]},{"id":"ai-103-98","d":"security","x":"HOTSPOT -\n\nYou plan to create a Microsoft Foundry project named Project1 that will contain an agent and use an Azure key vault named KV1.\nYou need to configure a connection from Project1 to KV1.\nHow should you complete the Bicep code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area?\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["category: 'AzureAIService'; authType: 'AccountKey'","category: 'AzureKeyVault'; authType: 'ApiKey'","category: 'AzureKeyVault'; authType: 'AccountManagedIdentity'","category: 'AzureOpenAI'; authType: 'ApiKey'"],"a":[2],"e":"The target is Key Vault, so category is AzureKeyVault. authType AccountManagedIdentity lets the Foundry account use its managed identity for keyless access. AccountKey and ApiKey are key-based.","t":"Key Vault + keyless = AzureKeyVault + AccountManagedIdentity.","k":["Bicep","AzureKeyVault","managed identity"]},{"id":"ai-103-99","d":"agents","x":"HOTSPOT -\nYou have a Microsoft Foundry project.\nYou need to create a customer support agent that meets the following requirements:\n•Grounds responses only in company policy documents stored in curated repositories\n•Retains customer preferences across separate chat sessions\nHow should you configure the agent? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Knowledge grounding: Embed the policy documents directly into the agent instructions. Memory: Retain user preferences in the state of the client application.","Knowledge grounding: Configure retrieval from approved data sources. Memory: Enable agent memory that uses persistent storage.","Knowledge grounding: Configure retrieval from approved data sources. Memory: Use orchestration-managed session context.","Knowledge grounding: Upload the policy documents directly to the agent. Memory: Use orchestration-managed session context."],"a":[1],"e":"Retrieval from approved data sources limits grounding to curated policy repositories. Agent memory with persistent storage keeps preferences across sessions. Session context does not persist, client state is not agent memory, and embedding docs in instructions does not scale.","t":"Curated retrieval + PERSISTENT memory.","k":["approved data sources","persistent memory"]},{"id":"ai-103-100","d":"development","x":"You have a Microsoft Foundry project that contains an agent. The agent generates summaries from retrieved policy documents.\nYou need to improve response completeness. The solution must be implemented in the logic of the application code before responses are returned.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Add a retry evaluation before the responses are returned.","Increase the value of the presence_penalty parameter","Decrease the value of the temperature parameter.","Replace the model with a smaller deployment."],"a":[0],"e":"Add an evaluation or reflection step in the app to check that the summary covers the required information, and retry if it does not, before returning. Temperature and presence_penalty change generation style, and a smaller model does not help.","t":"Completeness in app code = RETRY evaluation.","k":["retry evaluation","completeness"]},{"id":"ai-103-101","d":"tools-data","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou develop a test method to verify the results retrieved from a call to the Azure Vision in Foundry Tools API. The call is used to analyze the existence of company logos in images. The call returns a collection of brands named brands.\nYou have the following code segment:\n\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Statement 1: No. Statement 2: Yes. Statement 3: Yes.","Statement 1: Yes. Statement 2: Yes. Statement 3: No.","Statement 1: No. Statement 2: No. Statement 3: No.","Statement 1: Yes. Statement 2: No. Statement 3: Yes."],"a":[1],"e":"1 Yes: confidence >= 0.75 shows brands at 75% or more. 2 Yes: rectangle.x and y are the top-left corner. 3 No: w and h are width and height, not the bottom-right corner; that is x+w and y+h.","t":"x,y = top-left. w,h = size, not a corner.","k":["bounding box","confidence","Vision"]},{"id":"ai-103-102","d":"models","x":"You have an Azure subscription.\nYou plan to build an app that will use the Azure AI DALL-E model.\nYou need to deploy the model.\nWhat should you use?","o":["the Azure SDK for Python and PowerShell cmdlets.","the Azure portal and Microsoft Graph API","the Azure SDK for JavaScript and Azure Machine Learning Studio.","Microsoft Foundry and the Azure Command Line Interface (CLI)"],"a":[3],"e":"Deploy the DALL-E model through Microsoft Foundry, or automate it with the Azure CLI. Graph API and Azure ML Studio are not deployment surfaces for this.","t":"Deploy models = FOUNDRY (or CLI).","k":["DALL-E","Foundry","Azure CLI"]},{"id":"ai-103-103","d":"security","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou have an Azure subscription.\nYou need to create a new resource that will generate fictional stores in response to user prompts. The solution must ensure that the resource uses a customer-managed key to protect data.\nHow should you complete the script? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["--kind LanguageAuthoring; --assign-identity","--kind AIServices; --api-properties","--kind OpenAI; --api-properties","--kind OpenAI; --encryption"],"a":[3],"e":"Generating stories needs an Azure OpenAI resource, so use --kind OpenAI. The customer-managed key settings (keySource Microsoft.KeyVault) are passed with --encryption. --api-properties does not set encryption.","t":"Generate text = OpenAI kind. CMK = --ENCRYPTION.","k":["--kind OpenAI","--encryption","customer-managed key"]},{"id":"ai-103-104","d":"security","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou have a Python application collects customer comments before posting them to a public forum.\nYou need to send a text comment to Azure AI Content Safety and return the self-harm severity from the response.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["request = AnalyzeTextOptions(text=comment); response = client.analyze_image(request)","request = TextCategory.SELF_HARM(comment); response = client.moderate_text(request)","request = AnalyzeTextOptions(text=comment); response = client.analyze_text(request)","request = AnalyzeTextOptions(categories=comment); response = client.analyze_text(request)"],"a":[2],"e":"AnalyzeTextOptions takes the comment through the text argument, and client.analyze_text sends it to Content Safety. Then read the self-harm severity from categories_analysis. analyze_image is for images.","t":"Text in, analyze_TEXT out.","k":["Content Safety","analyze_text","self-harm"]},{"id":"ai-103-105","d":"tools-data","x":"You have a custom named entity recognition (NER) project in Azure Language in Foundry Tools for support tickets. The schema for the project contains an entity type named ContactInfo.\nIn tagged training files, ContactInfo is used for phone numbers, email addresses, and social media handles.\nModel evaluation shows low precision for ContactInfo, including false positives in which nearby text is extracted as ContactInfo.\nYou need to improve the precision of the project.\nWhat should you do before retraining the model?","o":["Trigger an auto-labeling job.","Add more support tickets as training data and label more ContactInfo entities.","Replace ContactInfo by using Phone, Email, and SocialMedia entities. Relabel every matching span.","Lower the confidence threshold for ContactInfo."],"a":[2],"e":"ContactInfo mixes phone numbers, emails and social handles with different patterns. Splitting it into Phone, Email and SocialMedia entities and relabeling gives clearer training signals and fewer false positives. Lower thresholds raise recall, not precision.","t":"Low precision from a broad label? SPLIT the entity.","k":["entity schema","precision","relabel"]},{"id":"ai-103-106","d":"tools-data","x":"You are building a text-to-speech solution that uses Azure Speech in Foundry Tools to read instructions from the script in a text file.\nYou discover that the solution often pronounces technical terms incorrectly.\nYou need to prevent the incorrect pronunciations. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?","o":["Use Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML) to specify phonemes.","Use Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML) to apply say as rules.","Use Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML) to adjust the prosody of the voice.","From Speech Studio, train a custom neural voice","From Azure OpenAI use the Whisper model."],"a":[0],"e":"SSML phonemes specify exactly how a word or technical term should be pronounced, fixing mispronunciation with little effort. say-as changes how text like dates is read, prosody changes speed and pitch, custom voice is heavy, and Whisper is speech to text.","t":"Wrong pronunciation = SSML PHONEME.","k":["SSML","phoneme","pronunciation"]},{"id":"ai-103-107","d":"security","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou have a Python application that redacts sensitive information before sending prompt text to a language model. The application has the following code:\n\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Statement 1: Yes. Statement 2: Yes. Statement 3: No.","Statement 1: Yes. Statement 2: No. Statement 3: No.","Statement 1: No. Statement 2: Yes. Statement 3: Yes.","Statement 1: No. Statement 2: No. Statement 3: Yes."],"a":[2],"e":"1 No: the PII categories are only Person and PhoneNumber, so there are no records for contact email or SSN. 2 Yes: email is not a configured category, so the address stays in text_for_model. 3 Yes: phone numbers are detected and masked by entity type.","t":"Only listed PII categories get redacted.","k":["PII redaction","categories","entityMask"]},{"id":"ai-103-108","d":"tools-data","x":"DRAG DROP\n-\nYou are developing an application that will detect faulty components produced on a factory production line. The components are specific to your business.\nYou need to use the Azure Custom Vision API to help detect common faults.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.","o":["1. Create a project. 2. Train the object detection model. 3. Upload and tag images.","1. Create a project. 2. Upload and tag images. 3. Train the classifier model.","1. Initialize the training dataset. 2. Upload and tag images. 3. Train the object detection model.","1. Upload and tag images. 2. Create a project. 3. Train the classifier model."],"a":[1],"e":"In Custom Vision, create the project, upload and tag images of components, then train the classifier. Training comes after tagging, and a classifier fits detecting common faults.","t":"Create, Tag, Train (in that order).","k":["Custom Vision","classifier","tag images"]},{"id":"ai-103-109","d":"security","x":"You are developing a new sales system that will process user-generated video and text from a public-facing website.\nYou plan to notify users that their data has been processed by the sales system.\nWhich responsible AI principle does this help meet?","o":["inclusiveness","transparency","reliability and safety","fairness"],"a":[1],"e":"Telling users their data was processed by the AI system shows them how it handles their data, which is transparency.","t":"Telling users = TRANSPARENCY.","k":["transparency","responsible AI"]},{"id":"ai-103-110","d":"tools-data","x":"You are designing a content management system.\nYou need to ensure that the reading experience is optimized for users who have reduced comprehension and learning differences, such as dyslexia. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhich Azure service should you include in the solution?","o":["Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools","Azure AI Immersive Reader","Azure Language in Foundry Tools","Azure Translator in Foundry Tools"],"a":[1],"e":"Azure AI Immersive Reader has built-in reading aids like text formatting, read aloud and line focus for readers with learning differences such as dyslexia, with minimal development.","t":"Reading help for dyslexia = IMMERSIVE READER.","k":["Immersive Reader","accessibility","dyslexia"]},{"id":"ai-103-111","d":"security","x":"You have an Azure subscription that contains an Azure App Service app named App1.\nYou provision a Microsoft Foundry Service resource named CSAccount1.\nYou need to configure App1 to access CSAccount1. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you use to configure App1?","o":["the endpoint URI and a shared access signature (SAS) token","the endpoint URI and subscription key","the endpoint URI and an OAuth token","a system assigned managed identity and an X.509 certificate"],"a":[1],"e":"The simplest setup is the endpoint URI and a subscription key. OAuth adds token setup, SAS tokens are for Storage, and managed identity does not need an X.509 certificate.","t":"Minimal effort = ENDPOINT + KEY.","k":["endpoint","subscription key"]},{"id":"ai-103-112","d":"security","x":"DRAG DROP\n-\nYou have a web app that uses Azure AI Search.\nWhen reviewing activity you see greater than expected search query volumes. You suspect that the query key is compromised.\nYou need to prevent unauthorized access to the search endpoint and ensure that users only have read only access to the documents collection. The solution must minimize app downtime.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.","o":["1. Delete the compromised key. 2. Add a new query key. 3. Change the app to use the new key.","1. Regenerate the primary admin key. 2. Change the app to use the secondary admin key. 3. Delete the compromised key.","1. Add a new query key. 2. Change the app to use the new key. 3. Delete the compromised key.","1. Change the app to use the secondary admin key. 2. Regenerate the secondary admin key. 3. Add a new query key."],"a":[2],"e":"Add a new query key, switch the app to it, then delete the compromised key. This keeps the app running and gives read-only access. Admin keys are too powerful, and deleting first causes downtime.","t":"ADD new, SWITCH app, DELETE old.","k":["query key","key rotation","read-only"]},{"id":"ai-103-113","d":"tools-data","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou need to create a new resource that will be used to perform sentiment analysis and optical character recognition (OCR). The solution must meet the following requirements:\n•Use a single key and endpoint to access multiple services.\n•Consolidate billing for future services that you might use.\n•Support the use of Azure Vision in Foundry Tools in the future.\nHow should you complete the HTTP request to create the new resource? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["POST; TextAnalytics","PATCH; ComputerVision","PUT; CognitiveServices","PUT; TextAnalytics"],"a":[2],"e":"Use PUT to create a named ARM resource, with kind CognitiveServices for a multi-service resource that has one key and endpoint and consolidated billing, including future Vision use. TextAnalytics and ComputerVision are single-service kinds.","t":"One key for many = PUT + COGNITIVESERVICES.","k":["PUT","CognitiveServices","multi-service"]},{"id":"ai-103-114","d":"tools-data","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou are building a model to detect objects in images.\nThe performance of the model based on training data is shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["The percentage of false positives is: 25. The value for the number of true positives divided by the total number of true positives and false negatives is: 100.","The percentage of false positives is: 100. The value for the number of true positives divided by the total number of true positives and false negatives is: 25.","The percentage of false positives is: 0. The value for the number of true positives divided by the total number of true positives and false negatives is: 25.","The percentage of false positives is: 0. The value for the number of true positives divided by the total number of true positives and false negatives is: 77.2."],"a":[2],"e":"Precision is 100%, and precision = TP/(TP+FP), so false positives are 0%. TP/(TP+FN) is recall, shown as 25%. 77.2% is mAP.","t":"Precision 100 = 0 FP. TP/(TP+FN) = RECALL.","k":["precision","recall","mAP"]},{"id":"ai-103-115","d":"security","x":"You are developing an application that will use Azure AI Search for internal documents.\nYou need to implement document-level filtering for Azure AI Search.\nWhich three actions should you include in the solution? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Retrieve the group memberships of the user","Create one index per group.","Send access tokens from Microsoft Entra ID, with the search request.","Retrieve all the groups.","Supply the groups as a filter for the search requests","Add allowed groups to each index entry"],"a":[0,4,5],"e":"For security trimming, store allowed group IDs on each document, get the current user's group memberships, and pass those groups as an OData filter on the query. One index per group does not scale, and a token alone does not filter documents.","t":"Tag docs with groups, get user groups, FILTER by them.","k":["security trimming","group filter","document-level security"]},{"id":"ai-103-116","d":"tools-data","x":"You have an Azure AI Search indexer that ingest PDF policy manuals.\nClient applications must display page-level citations that have bounding polygons for both text and images.\nYou need to add a single built-in multimodal content extraction skill to the Azure AI Search skillset. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n•Provide text and image location metadata.\n•Extract tables that span multiple pages.\nWhat should you add?","o":["Azure Content Understanding","GenAI Prompt","Document Layout","Document Extraction"],"a":[2],"e":"Document Layout is the built-in Azure AI Search multimodal skill that gives text and image location metadata with bounding polygons and extracts tables across pages. Content Understanding is a separate Foundry capability.","t":"Location + spanning tables = DOCUMENT LAYOUT skill.","k":["Document Layout skill","bounding polygons"]},{"id":"ai-103-117","d":"tools-data","x":"HOTSPOT\n-\nYou are creating an enrichment pipeline that will use Azure AI Search. The knowledge store contains unstructured JSON data and the text from scanned PDF documents.\nWhich projection type should you use for each data type? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["JSON data: Table projection. Extracted text data: Object projection.","JSON data: Table projection. Extracted text data: File projection.","JSON data: File projection. Extracted text data: Table projection.","JSON data: Object projection. Extracted text data: Object projection."],"a":[3],"e":"Object projections store enriched content as JSON objects in Azure Storage, which fits unstructured JSON and extracted text. Table projections are for relational data and file projections for binaries like images.","t":"JSON or text = OBJECT. Rows = TABLE. Images = FILE.","k":["knowledge store","object projection"]}],"desc":"Azure AI Foundry, prompt shields, custom agents, RAG pipelines, multimodal models and AI safety evaluation."},{"id":"ab-100","code":"AB-100","title":"Agentic AI Business Solutions Architect","vendor":"Microsoft","exam":{"count":54,"minutes":90,"passPercent":80},"domains":{"agentic-ai":"Agentic AI"},"qs":[{"id":"ab-100-1","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Overview - Contoso, Ltd. is a high-tech manufacturing company that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance. Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and Dynamics 365 Commerce for its North American operations. The company designs and develops innovative products that have many patents and proprietary technologies. The patents and engineering designs are closely guarded secrets. Contoso executives want to integrate and adopt AI solutions to help scale the company in preparation for an anticipated period of rapid growth. The company has multiple legal entities and Azure subscriptions that will be used in the adopted AI solutions. Requirements - AI Adoption - The following executives will have specific responsibilities in the overall AI adoption: Chief Technology Officer (CTO): Select one Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management or Dynamics 365 Commerce prebuilt AI agent and one custom Microsoft Copilot Studio AI agent to prioritize and deploy during the initial AI adoption phase. Chief Information Officer (CIO): Ensure that appropriate security labels are assigned to the data used by the AI agents. Chief Financial Officer (CFO): Analyze the return on investment (ROI) for the AI agents being deployed. Chief Information Security Officer (CISO): Discover and inventory AI resources for auditing. Chief Executive Officer (CEO): Ensure that all solutions adhere to industry-standard responsible AI practices. All AI initiatives and agents will have a detailed business use case, a defined audience profile, and an estimated ROI that will compare the cost savings of the current process against the estimated costs of using the new AI solutions.The company's research and development (R&D) department already has a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that contains comprehensive product specifications and compliance data. Prebuilt AI Agent - The CTO has NOT yet selected which prebuilt AI agent to use in Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. The CTO wants to view available agent templates to identify which agent will add the most business value. Depending on which high-priority AI agents are identified, its agent capabilities must be previewed in a discovery meeting with the relevant business operation stakeholders. Custom AI Agent - Contoso has identified the following custom AI agent requirements: The custom AI agent will use data from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management to answer questions for the manufacturing team as a low-code solution. The custom AI agent will be accessible from within Microsoft Teams. The custom AI agent must be designed to eventually connect to other agents that can be selected based on their description. The topics used in the custom AI agent will be selected based NOT on a trigger phrase, but on a description of the purpose of the query, to make the interactions more conversational. The custom AI agent must be able to answer questions about product specifications by using existing technologies. The product specifications are maintained by the R&D department. The custom AI agent must be integrated with and accessible from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. The custom AI agent must be able to use Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management business logic that is stored outside of the application. Analysis, Reporting, and Troubleshooting Contoso has identified the following analysis, reporting, and troubleshooting requirements: The CISO will audit all the AI solutions monthly for compliance and security. The CFO will analyze all the AI solutions quarterly to compare the estimated ROI against actual measured efficiencies and adoption. The CFO will use the Copilot Studio agent usage estimator to perform this analysis. The CISO wants to identify how much sensitive data was accessed for a given AI agent run and who accessed the data. Too much sensitive data accessed by a single user might indicate a high security risk. The CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of the AI agent responses during user interactions with the agents. Consistently poor feedback will trigger an escalated reengineering discussion. The CEO wants a quarterly assessment of all the required metrics for their specific responsibilities. The tools used for the assessments must be Microsoft-recommended and must verify reliability, interpretability, fairness, and compliance. The CFO wants to identify how many interactions with the AI agents are abandoned on a given day as compared to resolved conversations. Too many abandoned sessions might indicate that Copilot Studio credits are being used inefficiently by end users. Which two components in the custom AI agent design should the CFO evaluate in the quarterly agent analysis? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["The average characters in a chat message","The average session time per agent","The GPT models used for the agent","The agent orchestration method"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The CFO cares about cost and business value. The orchestration method decides how the agent picks knowledge, tools and topics, so it drives Copilot credit use. Average session time shows how long an interaction takes and supports ROI tracking. GPT model choice and message length are not operational ROI metrics.","t":"CFO = money. Money leaks through HOW it orchestrates and HOW LONG sessions run.","k":["orchestration","session time","credits","ROI"]},{"id":"ab-100-2","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which template should you use for the AI agent to meet the requirements for the sales executives?","o":["AI agents in Microsoft Foundry","Voice in Microsoft Copilot Studio","AI chat in Microsoft Foundry","IT Helpdesk in Microsoft Copilot Studio"],"a":[1],"e":"Sales executives need hands-free voice interaction through headsets with a low-code approach. The Voice template in Copilot Studio is built for speech-enabled agents. Foundry templates are pro-code and IT Helpdesk is the wrong scenario.","t":"Headset + hands-free = Voice template in Copilot Studio.","k":["Voice template","hands-free","low-code","Copilot Studio"]},{"id":"ab-100-3","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that will analyze historical business data to predict customer behavior. The data is currently stored in an Azure SQL database, flat files, APIs, and logs. You need to organize the data into a format that can be used as a knowledge source in Copilot Studio. What should you include in the solution?","o":["Azure Translator in Foundry Tools","Azure AI Search","Azure Cosmos DB","Azure Data Lake Storage"],"a":[1],"e":"The data is scattered across SQL, flat files, APIs and logs. Azure AI Search connects to many sources, indexes them, and supports keyword and vector search, giving Copilot Studio one RAG-ready knowledge source. Data Lake only stores raw data, Cosmos DB is a NoSQL database, and Translator translates text.","t":"Scattered data + knowledge source = INDEX it = Azure AI Search.","k":["Azure AI Search","indexing","RAG","multiple sources"]},{"id":"ab-100-4","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support. The agent must securely retrieve product warranty data from a REST API. The solution must minimize development effort. What should you include in the design?","o":["Create a custom connector in Copilot Studio and use the connector to call the API.","Use a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to screen scrape the warranty data.","Export the agent as a managed solution and customize the agent in Power Apps.","Add the warranty data to the Fallback topic."],"a":[0],"e":"A custom connector wraps a REST API as a configurable action in Copilot Studio, supports OAuth 2.0 or API keys, and needs little development. Screen scraping is fragile, managed solution export does not call APIs, and the Fallback topic only handles unrecognized intent.","t":"REST API + Copilot Studio + low effort = Custom Connector.","k":["custom connector","REST API","OAuth","low effort"]},{"id":"ab-100-5","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled. You need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are presented to users. Solution: You configure AI Builder lead scoring models to influence opportunity summaries. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[0],"e":"No. AI Builder lead scoring predicts and ranks lead conversion. It does not change how Copilot generates or shows opportunity summaries. That is done by configuring the summary fields and form settings in Dynamics 365 Sales.","t":"Lead scoring scores LEADS, not SUMMARIES.","k":["lead scoring","opportunity summary","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-6","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing an AI strategy for Microsoft Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps. You are evaluating the use of Microsoft Copilot Studio to provide in-app help and guidance based on generative AI general knowledge. You need to recommend which knowledge sources to include in the generative help and guidance agent. The solution must minimize the risk of generating inaccurate responses. What should you recommend?","o":["Are not supported - Must be enabled for the agent","Must be excluded from the agent - Is not supported","Must be uploaded to the agent - Is not supported","Must be uploaded to the agent - Must be disabled for the agent"],"a":[3],"e":"To reduce inaccurate answers, upload trusted custom knowledge (official docs, company data) to the agent and disable generative AI general knowledge, which may bring in outside or outdated information.","t":"Own docs IN, general knowledge OFF.","k":["custom knowledge","disable general knowledge","accuracy"]},{"id":"ab-100-7","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company is designing a Microsoft Power Platform solution to reduce the manual steps of a business process by deploying an existing AI model. You need to calculate the return on AI investment (ROAI) by identifying the metadata and telemetry of the solution. What should you use?","o":["Microsoft Power Platform admin center","The Business value toolkit","Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure","Success by Design"],"a":[1],"e":"The Business value toolkit identifies the metadata, telemetry and value metrics needed to quantify and track ROAI for a Power Platform solution. The admin center manages environments, Success by Design guides implementations, and CAF is for Azure cloud adoption.","t":"ROAI + Power Platform telemetry = Business VALUE toolkit.","k":["Business value toolkit","ROAI","telemetry"]},{"id":"ab-100-8","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. You are designing an AI supply chain process that meets the following requirements: provide managers with AI-driven insights that surface key information from customer orders; help planners use AI to anticipate future product needs more accurately. You need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design. What should you recommend for each requirement?","o":["Workload insights with Copilot - The Supplier Communications Agent","AI Summaries with Copilot - Generative insights for Demand planning","AI Summaries with Copilot - Product information management","Workload insights with Copilot - Generative insights for Demand planning"],"a":[1],"e":"AI Summaries with Copilot highlight key information from customer orders for managers. Generative insights for Demand planning help planners forecast future product demand.","t":"Managers SUMMARIZE, planners FORECAST demand.","k":["AI Summaries","Demand planning","Supply Chain"]},{"id":"ab-100-9","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft 365 E5 subscription and uses Microsoft Copilot Studio. The company has a Microsoft SharePoint Online library that contains 10,000 policy PDFs from various departments. The library contains a populated column named Department for each PDF. You need to design a Copilot Studio agent that will use the SharePoint library as a knowledge source. The solution must meet the following requirements: Enable the agent to answer user questions about company policies. Ensure that the agent can identify which departments and policies are connected. What should you include in the design for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Import the PDFs into Microsoft Dataverse. - From Copilot Studio, configure the SharePoint tool.","From Copilot Studio, add SharePoint as a knowledge source. - From Copilot Studio, configure the SharePoint tool.","Use AI Builder to process and feed SharePoint content. - Upgrade to SharePoint Premium.","From Copilot Studio, add SharePoint as a knowledge source. - Create a Microsoft Dataverse table for the departments."],"a":[1],"e":"Add the SharePoint library directly as a knowledge source so the agent can answer policy questions. Configure the SharePoint tool so the agent can use the existing Department column metadata to link departments and policies. No Dataverse import or SharePoint Premium is needed.","t":"Answer = knowledge source. Relationships via metadata = SharePoint tool.","k":["SharePoint knowledge source","SharePoint tool","metadata"]},{"id":"ab-100-10","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled. You need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are presented to users. Solution: You add fields to the opportunity summary. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Yes. Copilot opportunity summaries in Dynamics 365 Sales are driven by form configuration. Adding fields to the opportunity summary directly changes what is extracted and shown.","t":"Want a different summary? Change its FIELDS. Yes.","k":["opportunity summary","add fields","Yes"]},{"id":"ab-100-11","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry project that uses a single agent and a single prompt to complete a series of tasks. The agent encounters the following issues: It frequently produces incomplete results. It struggles with domain-specific reasoning. Agent response times are remarkably slow. You need to recommend a solution to improve the overall performance and accuracy of the agent. What should you include in the recommendation? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Move to a multi-agent architecture. - Add a prebuilt connector.","Move to a multi-agent architecture. - Add a grounding data source.","Upgrade to a larger generative AI model. - Add a prebuilt connector.","Upgrade to a larger generative AI model. - Add a grounding data source."],"a":[1],"e":"For performance, a multi-agent architecture splits a long chain of tasks across specialized agents, so prompts are lighter and responses faster. For accuracy, a grounding data source adds RAG with trusted domain knowledge. A bigger model is usually slower, and a prebuilt connector only adds connectivity.","t":"Slow = split the work (multi-agent). Wrong = ground it (RAG).","k":["multi-agent","grounding","RAG","performance vs accuracy"]},{"id":"ab-100-12","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have an Azure subscription named Subscription1. Subscription1 contains a resource group named RG1. RG1 contains resources that were deployed by using templates. You need to view the date and time when the resources were created in RG1. Solution: From the RG1 blade, you click Deployments. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Yes. Resources deployed by templates (ARM or Bicep) are recorded in the resource group's Deployments history, which shows start time and timestamp. For manually created resources, the Activity Log is the better place.","t":"Template-deployed? Check DEPLOYMENTS for the timestamp.","k":["Deployments","ARM template","resource group","Yes"]},{"id":"ab-100-13","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an ecommerce support portal that uses Microsoft Dataverse. You are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for the portal. The agent must respond with a default help message when the user input is unclear and initiate external processes, such as retrieving the order status, when users make specific requests. Generative orchestration will be enabled for the solution. What should you recommend?","o":["The Fallback topic - The Escalate topic","A trigger phrase - A skill","A tool (connector) - A trigger phrase","The Fallback topic - A tool (connector)"],"a":[3],"e":"The Fallback topic runs when the agent cannot match the user's request, so it returns a default help message. With generative orchestration, the agent picks a tool or connector on its own to call APIs or flows, such as getting order status.","t":"Unclear = Fallback. Do something = Tool.","k":["Fallback topic","tool","connector","generative orchestration"]},{"id":"ab-100-14","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support. You are reviewing and validating the following prompts: A prompt that has instructions to \"help the customer as best you can\". A prompt that helps retrieve product information from a knowledge base. You need to ensure that the agent delivers consistent and accurate responses. What should you do for each prompt? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["(1) Add filler words to make the prompt sound more natural - (2) Remove the knowledge source so that the model responds freely with general product information.","(1) Rewrite the prompt with clear and task-specific instructions - (2) Use responses with only reference sources and limit the response scope.","(1) Rewrite the prompt to limit the response length to 50 words - (2) Enable automatic multi-language translation for the knowledge base.","(1) Keep the prompt vague to enable model flexibility - (2) Add several open-ended questions to give the model broader context."],"a":[1],"e":"The prompt 'help the customer as best you can' is vague, so rewrite it with clear, task-specific instructions. For knowledge base retrieval, answer only from reference sources and limit scope to improve accuracy and reduce hallucination.","t":"Vague prompt? Make it specific. Knowledge prompt? Sources only.","k":["specific instructions","reference sources","hallucination"]},{"id":"ab-100-15","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a customer order system that creates sales orders manually. You need to design an AI solution to automate the following tasks as part of the system: Save the order details to a database. Update the order status in the database. Extract the order details from an order file. Prepare and send a confirmation email to customers. The solution must minimize development effort and support intelligent automation and solution integration. What should you include in the design?","o":["A multi-agent solution that uses the Semantic Kernel SDK","A multi-agent solution that uses Microsoft Foundry Agent Service","A workflow in Azure Logic Apps","A Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses Microsoft Power Automate workflows"],"a":[3],"e":"Copilot Studio with Power Automate is low-code. Power Automate connectors extract file data, save and update the database, and send emails, while Copilot Studio adds intelligent orchestration. Semantic Kernel and Foundry Agent Service are pro-code, and Logic Apps lacks the low-code agent experience.","t":"Minimize dev effort + intelligent automation = Copilot Studio + Power Automate.","k":["Copilot Studio","Power Automate","low-code"]},{"id":"ab-100-16","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI business solution. You need to extend the solution so that Microsoft 365 Copilot can invoke external logic hosted in Azure services. What should you include in the solution?","o":["Microsoft Power Platform connectors","Microsoft Copilot Studio skills","Custom engine agents"],"a":[1],"e":"Microsoft 365 Copilot calls external logic like Azure Functions, Logic Apps or REST APIs through Copilot Studio skills or actions. A skill describes the capability so Copilot can choose and invoke it. Connectors are used inside actions, and custom engine agents are a separate agent, not an extension.","t":"Extend M365 Copilot with Azure logic = SKILL.","k":["Copilot Studio skills","M365 Copilot","Azure logic"]},{"id":"ab-100-17","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform solution that contains the following components: Microsoft Dataverse tables, a Microsoft Power BI workspace named WS1, a canvas app named App1 that uses Dataverse, and a Power BI semantic model that connects to Dataverse by using DirectQuery. You plan to use generative AI to provide answers to queries based on a subset of corporate data. You need to ensure that the data is available as a grounding data source for AI systems. What should you do?","o":["Share WS1.","Endorse the semantic model.","Export the semantic model.","Populate a Dataverse table."],"a":[3],"e":"To make a subset of corporate data a grounding source, store it where AI features can query it directly. Dataverse is deeply integrated with Power Platform and Copilot. Sharing, endorsing or exporting a Power BI model serves reporting, not grounding.","t":"Grounding for Power Platform AI = put it in DATAVERSE.","k":["Dataverse","grounding","Power BI"]},{"id":"ab-100-18","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A retail company plans to deploy Microsoft Copilot Studio agents to support: Microsoft Dynamics 365 Commerce scenarios. A Microsoft Power Apps inventory management solution. You need to recommend a solution to organize product catalog data as a consistent source for multiple AI systems. What should you recommend?","o":["Centralize the product catalog data in Microsoft Dataverse and expose the data to both agents.","Let each agent scrape product details from Microsoft SharePoint Online libraries.","Configure prompts to pull product details from the PDFs of external vendors.","Store the product catalog data in a separate custom table for each agent."],"a":[0],"e":"Centralizing the product catalog in Dataverse gives one consistent source that both the Commerce agent and the Power Apps solution can use. Scraping SharePoint or vendor PDFs is unstructured, and separate tables per agent duplicate data.","t":"Many AIs, one truth = central Dataverse.","k":["single source of truth","Dataverse","consistency"]},{"id":"ab-100-19","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that meets the following requirements: Supports interactive speech responses and optimizes decision-making and the accuracy of responses. What should you include in the design for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct requirements. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.","o":["Azure Language in Foundry Tools — Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML)","Copilot Studio voice features — A deep reasoning model","Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML) — A deep reasoning model","A deep reasoning model — Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML)"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot Studio voice features provide built-in interactive speech. A deep reasoning model improves decision-making and accuracy for complex requests. SSML only formats pronunciation and tone.","t":"Talk = Voice features. Think = Deep reasoning.","k":["voice features","deep reasoning model","SSML"]},{"id":"ab-100-20","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing end-to-end test scenarios for a business solution that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales and Dynamics 365 Finance. You need to ensure that the business solution properly exchanges data between the Dynamics 365 apps and aligns with defined user workflows and business processes. Which testing types should you use?","o":["Drift - User acceptance","Integration - Performance","Integration - User acceptance","Exploratory - Performance"],"a":[2],"e":"Integration testing checks data exchange between Dynamics 365 Sales and Finance. User acceptance testing checks that the solution fits real user workflows and business processes.","t":"Apps talking = Integration. Users happy = UAT.","k":["integration testing","UAT"]},{"id":"ab-100-21","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to implement an AI business solution for a consumer goods company. You need to orchestrate sales order fulfillment and shipping, and analyze historical data and trends to replenish stock. Which agent types should you use?","o":["Task - Prompt-and-response","Autonomous - Prompt-and-response","Autonomous - Task","Prompt-and-response - Autonomous"],"a":[1],"e":"An autonomous agent fits order fulfillment and shipping, which is a multi-step process that runs independently. A prompt-and-response agent fits analysis of historical data, where a manager asks and gets results.","t":"Runs by itself = Autonomous. Ask and answer = Prompt-and-response.","k":["autonomous agent","prompt-and-response","orchestration"]},{"id":"ab-100-22","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has two Microsoft Power Platform environments named Dev1 and Prod1. A Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 is built into a solution in the Dev1 environment. You plan to deploy Agent1 to Prod1. You need to make Agent1 available to the users in Prod1. The solution must minimize administrative effort. What should you do?","o":["Export the solution as an unmanaged solution and import the solution into Prod1.","Share Agent1 with the users in Prod1.","Create a new Copilot Studio agent in Prod1 by replicating the configuration of Agent1.","Export the solution as a managed solution and import the solution into Prod1."],"a":[3],"e":"In Power Platform ALM, managed solutions move components from Dev to Test or Prod. Exporting as managed and importing into Prod1 keeps deployment consistent with little admin effort and prevents unintended edits. Unmanaged is for Dev.","t":"Going to PROD? Go MANAGED.","k":["managed solution","ALM","Dev to Prod"]},{"id":"ab-100-23","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY — This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which framework should you use for the infrastructure migration?","o":["Microsoft Power Platform Project Setup Wizard","Microsoft Power Platform Center of Excellence (CoE)","Success by Design","Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure"],"a":[3],"e":"The requirement is to move all infrastructure to Azure using Microsoft's recommended method. The Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure is that official guidance. Success by Design is for Dynamics 365 projects, CoE is Power Platform governance, and Project Setup Wizard sets up one project.","t":"Infrastructure to Azure = CAF (Cloud Adoption Framework).","k":["Cloud Adoption Framework","migration","Azure"]},{"id":"ab-100-24","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment that contains Microsoft Dataverse data. You create a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 that processes the Dataverse data. You discover that Agent1 fails to return relevant or accurate results. You need to improve the quality and reliability of data grounding. What should you do?","o":["Verify and cleanse the Dataverse data.","Retrain Agent1.","Add example user inputs to the training data of Agent1.","Use an adaptive card in Agent1."],"a":[0],"e":"Grounding quality depends on source data. If Dataverse data is wrong, missing or duplicated, the agent returns poor results, so verify and cleanse it. Retraining or adding examples mainly helps intent understanding, and adaptive cards are UI.","t":"Garbage in, garbage out. Clean the data.","k":["data quality","cleanse data","grounding"]},{"id":"ab-100-25","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Case study - This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which components should you use to meet the sales cycle enablement requirements? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["the Power Platform admin center - an event trigger","Dynamics 365 Sales - a REST API","Microsoft Copilot Studio - a Fallback topic","Microsoft Foundry - a custom connector"],"a":[2],"e":"Copilot Studio is the low-code platform for a custom agent built on Dataverse. A Fallback topic handles cases the agent cannot answer and can hand the conversation to a live representative.","t":"Low-code agent = Studio. Can't answer = Fallback.","k":["Copilot Studio","Fallback topic","escalation"]},{"id":"ab-100-26","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to automate tasks in a web app. During testing, you discover that the automation sometimes fails because of frequent changes to the app's user interface. You need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent successfully automates the tasks. The solution must minimize changes to the agent. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Custom models in Azure AI Studio","Conversation topics in Copilot Studio","An agent flow in Copilot Studio","Computer Use in Copilot Studio"],"a":[3],"e":"When the web UI changes often, fixed locators break. Computer Use in Copilot Studio works from what is visible on screen, so automation adapts to UI changes without much change to the agent.","t":"UI keeps changing? Let it SEE the screen = Computer Use.","k":["Computer Use","UI changes","automation"]},{"id":"ab-100-27","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Foundry agents. You need to ensure that an agent can dynamically use external tools at runtime without updating the agent. What should you include in the solution?","o":["A Model Context Protocol (MCP) server","Microsoft Copilot Studio","Azure AI Search","A Microsoft Foundry hub"],"a":[0],"e":"An MCP server lets an agent discover and use external tools, APIs or data dynamically at runtime without updating the agent. A Foundry hub manages resources, AI Search handles retrieval, and Copilot Studio is a build platform.","t":"Dynamic tools at runtime = MCP (USB-C for tools).","k":["MCP","runtime tools","dynamic discovery"]},{"id":"ab-100-28","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY — This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which framework should you use to meet the AI agent requirements for the sales cycle enablement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Success by Design — Success by Design","Microsoft Power Platform Well-Architected framework — Success by Design","The ALM Accelerator for Microsoft Power Platform — Microsoft Power Platform Well-Architected framework","Success by Design — Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure"],"a":[0],"e":"Success by Design fits both Copilot Studio practices and the conversational experience because it gives phased guidance, checklists and checkpoints for Dynamics 365 and Power Platform solutions. CAF is for Azure migration, ALM Accelerator is for deployment, Well-Architected is for architecture quality.","t":"D365 or Power Platform project guidance = Success by Design (both boxes).","k":["Success by Design","Dynamics 365","implementation"]},{"id":"ab-100-29","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance to manage accounts payable. You are designing an AI invoice processing solution. You need to recommend the prerequisites to configure a prebuilt copilot for accounts payable. What should you recommend?","o":["Build an AI tool in Microsoft Foundry.","From Microsoft Copilot Studio, create an accounts payable agent.","Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales to an accounts payable agent.","From the Power Platform admin center, assign the Finance and Operations AI security role to users."],"a":[3],"e":"The accounts payable copilot is prebuilt in Dynamics 365 Finance. The prerequisite is assigning the Finance and Operations AI security role to users in the Power Platform admin center. Building custom agents is not needed.","t":"Prebuilt copilot? Don't build. Just assign the ROLE.","k":["prebuilt copilot","security role","accounts payable"]},{"id":"ab-100-30","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform solution for customer support. The solution must include AI capabilities in Microsoft Power Automate and must meet the following requirements: Use a centralized workspace for AI models. Generate short overviews from large amounts of unstructured text, such as case notes or transcripts, without requiring additional training or coding. What should you include in the recommendation for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Microsoft Dataverse - An AI Builder prebuilt prompt","Azure OpenAI Foundry - An AI Builder prebuilt model","Microsoft Copilot Studio - Azure OpenAI","Azure OpenAI Foundry - GitHub Copilot"],"a":[1],"e":"Azure OpenAI Foundry is the centralized workspace to manage and test AI models. To summarize case notes or transcripts in Power Automate with no training or code, use an AI Builder prebuilt model.","t":"Central model workspace = Foundry. No-code summary = AI Builder prebuilt.","k":["Foundry","AI Builder prebuilt","summarization"]},{"id":"ab-100-31","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You have an Azure subscription named Subscription1. In Subscription1, you create an Azure file share named share1. You create a shared access signature (SAS) named SAS1 as shown in the following exhibit.","o":["Scenario 1: Will be prompted for credentials / Scenario 2: Will have no access.","Scenario 1: Will have read, write, and list access / Scenario 2: Will have read, write, and list access.","Scenario 1: Will have no access / Scenario 2: Will have no access.","Scenario 1: Will have no access / Scenario 2: Will have read-only access."],"a":[3],"e":"Both scenarios get no access. Scenario 1 uses an IP outside the allowed range. Scenario 2 uses an allowed IP, but net use connects over SMB while the SAS allows HTTPS only, so it fails.","t":"SAS checks IP AND protocol. net use = SMB, not HTTPS.","k":["SAS","IP range","HTTPS only","SMB"]},{"id":"ab-100-32","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A financial services company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance. Currently, the company's support staff manually reviews customer transaction histories to detect potential fraud cases before escalating the cases. You need to recommend an automation solution for the review process. The solution must ensure that escalations reach a human analyst for final decision making. What should you recommend?","o":["Export the data to a data lake for analysis in Microsoft Power BI.","Configure a task agent to generate fraud risk scores for the human analyst to review.","Use Microsoft 365 Copilot in Word to automatically finalize fraud detection policies.","Deploy an autonomous agent that closes non-fraud cases automatically."],"a":[1],"e":"A task agent can analyze transaction history and generate fraud risk scores, then hand results to a human analyst for the final decision. That is human-in-the-loop. An autonomous agent closing cases removes human approval.","t":"Human must decide = Task agent scores, human approves.","k":["task agent","human-in-the-loop","fraud"]},{"id":"ab-100-33","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units. The company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product reviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis. What should you use?","o":["Cost Management + Billing","Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator","Azure Reservations","Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator"],"a":[0],"e":"Cost Management + Billing tracks, allocates and forecasts Azure costs across business units, which supports ROAI cost evaluation. The Fabric estimator is Fabric-only, TCO compares on-prem with Azure, and Reservations are a discount mechanism.","t":"Running Azure costs across units = Cost Management + Billing.","k":["Cost Management","ROAI","business units"]},{"id":"ab-100-34","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Case study This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Overview - Contoso, Ltd. is a high-tech manufacturing company that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance. Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and Dynamics 365 Commerce for its North American operations. The company designs and develops innovative products that have many patents and proprietary technologies. The patents and engineering designs are closely guarded secrets. Contoso executives want to integrate and adopt AI solutions to help scale the company in preparation for an anticipated period of rapid growth. The company has multiple legal entities and Azure subscriptions that will be used in the adopted AI solutions. Requirements - AI Adoption - The following executives will have specific responsibilities in the overall AI adoption: Chief Technology Officer (CTO): Select one Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management or Dynamics 365 Commerce prebuilt AI agent and one custom Microsoft Copilot Studio AI agent to prioritize and deploy during the initial AI adoption phase. Chief Information Officer (CIO): Ensure that appropriate security labels are assigned to the data used by the AI agents. Chief Financial Officer (CFO): Analyze the return on investment (ROI) for the AI agents being deployed. Chief Information Security Officer (CISO): Discover and inventory AI resources for auditing. Chief Executive Officer (CEO): Ensure that all solutions adhere to industry-standard responsible AI practices. All AI initiatives and agents will have a detailed business use case, a defined audience profile, and an estimated ROI that will compare the cost savings of the current process against the estimated costs of using the new AI solutions. The company's research and development (R&D) department already has a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that contains comprehensive product specifications and compliance data. Prebuilt AI Agent - The CTO has NOT yet selected which prebuilt AI agent to use in Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. The CTO wants to view available agent templates to identify which agent will add the most business value. Depending on which high-priority AI agents are identified, its agent capabilities must be previewed in a discovery meeting with the relevant business operation stakeholders. Custom AI Agent - Contoso has identified the following custom AI agent requirements: The custom AI agent will use data from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management to answer questions for the manufacturing team as a low-code solution. The custom AI agent will be accessible from within Microsoft Teams. The custom AI agent must be designed to eventually connect to other agents that can be selected based on their description. The topics used in the custom AI agent will be selected based NOT on a trigger phrase, but on a description of the purpose of the query, to make the interactions more conversational. The custom AI agent must be able to answer questions about product specifications by using existing technologies. The product specifications are maintained by the R&D department. The custom AI agent must be integrated with and accessible from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management. The custom AI agent must be able to use Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management business logic that is stored outside of the application. Analysis, Reporting, and Troubleshooting Contoso has identified the following analysis, reporting, and troubleshooting requirements: The CISO will audit all the AI solutions monthly for compliance and security. The CFO will analyze all the AI solutions quarterly to compare the estimated ROI against actual measured efficiencies and adoption. The CFO will use the Copilot Studio agent usage estimator to perform this analysis. The CISO wants to identify how much sensitive data was accessed for a given AI agent run and who accessed the data. Too much sensitive data accessed by a single user might indicate a high security risk. The CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of the AI agent responses during user interactions with the agents. Consistently poor feedback will trigger an escalated reengineering discussion. The CEO wants a quarterly assessment of all the required metrics for their specific responsibilities. The tools used for the assessments must be Microsoft-recommended and must verify reliability, interpretability, fairness, and compliance. The CFO wants to identify how many interactions with the AI agents are abandoned on a given day as compared to resolved conversations. Too many abandoned sessions might indicate that Copilot Studio credits are being used inefficiently by end users. What should you recommend to assist the CTO with the prebuilt agent selection process?","o":["Immersive Home","Agent management","Lifecycle Services (LCS)","Copilot Studio"],"a":[1],"e":"Agent management is the hub in Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps to discover, manage and preview prebuilt AI agents, which matches the CTO's task. Copilot Studio builds custom agents and LCS manages deployment lifecycle.","t":"Pick a PREBUILT agent = Agent management.","k":["Agent management","prebuilt agents","CTO"]},{"id":"ab-100-35","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to design a multi-agent solution that includes a custom agent. The solution must define the rules and constraints that the agent must follow and automate a backend process involving data movement between services that runs independently of the agent's reasoning steps. What should you include for each requirement?","o":["Conversation topics - Microsoft Power Automate cloud flow","Agent flows - Microsoft Power Pages","Microsoft Power Automate cloud flow - Microsoft Power Pages","Agent flows - Microsoft Power Automate cloud flow"],"a":[3],"e":"Agent flows define the rules, constraints and sequence the agent must follow. A Power Automate cloud flow automates backend data movement between services, independent of the agent's reasoning.","t":"Agent's rules = Agent flows. Backend plumbing = Cloud flow.","k":["agent flows","cloud flow","backend automation"]},{"id":"ab-100-36","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to deploy an AI-based customer service app that will autonomously manage interactions, escalate complex cases, and learn from historical ticket data. You need to perform a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis of the app deployment. The solution must ensure that the analysis is accurate. What should you do first?","o":["Conduct an AI market benchmarking study.","Identify and quantify all the development, deployment, and operating costs.","Establish the AI performance metrics.","Model the customer experience."],"a":[1],"e":"An accurate ROAI analysis starts by identifying and quantifying all development, deployment and operating costs. Those costs are the baseline for comparing benefits. Metrics and customer experience come after.","t":"ROI first step = count the COSTS.","k":["ROAI","costs first"]},{"id":"ab-100-37","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365. You need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in Microsoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship management (CRM) data. Solution: You recommend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales. Does this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"Yes. Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales works in Outlook and Teams with a CRM like Dynamics 365. It summarizes email threads, suggests replies and gives CRM context for meeting prep.","t":"Outlook + CRM + Sales = Copilot for Sales. Yes.","k":["Copilot for Sales","Outlook","CRM","Yes"]},{"id":"ab-100-38","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled. You need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are presented to users. Solution: You add the opportunity summary widget to the Opportunity form. Does this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. Adding the opportunity summary widget only shows an existing UI component. It does not change how Copilot generates the summary or what it contains.","t":"Widget = display only. Doesn't customize. No.","k":["widget","opportunity summary","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-39","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to enhance customer support. The company stores customer data across ServiceNow, Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and Excel files in SharePoint Online. You need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent can deliver accurate and timely responses. What should you recommend?","o":["Enable incremental indexing in Azure AI Search.","Create custom prompts.","Implement Microsoft Power Platform connectors.","Implement a model router for query handling."],"a":[2],"e":"Power Platform connectors give ready-made, secure API connections so Copilot Studio can reach ServiceNow, Dynamics 365 and SharePoint Excel files in real time. A model router only routes between models, custom prompts give no data access, and AI Search is not the most direct option here.","t":"Many business systems + live data = CONNECTORS.","k":["Power Platform connectors","ServiceNow","real-time data"]},{"id":"ab-100-40","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company processes invoices stored across multiple systems in multiple formats. You need to implement an AI solution to automate the invoice processing. The solution must meet the following requirements: Automate multi-step invoice processing tasks, including document analysis, data validation, and approval routing. Enable users to interact directly via Microsoft Teams to review and approve invoices. Minimize development efforts to define and customize approval workflows. What should you include in the solution?","o":["Azure OpenAI and Azure Functions","Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder","Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools and Azure Logic Apps","A SharePoint agent"],"a":[1],"e":"AI Builder has a prebuilt invoice processing model to extract and analyze invoice data. Copilot Studio lets users review and approve in Teams and builds low-code workflows. Logic Apps, Functions and Azure OpenAI need more pro-code work.","t":"Invoice + Teams + low-code = Copilot Studio + AI Builder.","k":["AI Builder","invoice processing","Teams","low-code"]},{"id":"ab-100-41","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales to manage leads that are stored in a Microsoft Dataverse table named Lead and use non-standard terminology and custom columns. You need to configure business terms in the Lead table so that Microsoft Copilot controls can summarize the leads efficiently. The solution must minimize administrative effort. How should you configure the business terms?","o":["Map the field display names as business terms.","Add the schema names as business terms.","Create new business terms for each field.","Combine all the fields into one custom field."],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot uses Dataverse metadata to understand data. Display names are the friendly business-language labels, so mapping them as business terms helps Copilot understand custom columns with minimal admin effort.","t":"Business terms = DISPLAY names (human words), not schema names.","k":["business terms","display names","Dataverse metadata"]},{"id":"ab-100-42","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a portfolio of AI initiatives at different stages of development. You need to recommend a structured approach to evaluating the return on AI investment (ROAI) across all the initiatives. The solution must balance immediate results with long-term values and strategic innovations. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["The internal rate of return (IRR) function","A simple cost and benefit analysis","A horizon-based framework","A prioritization grid"],"a":[2],"e":"A horizon-based framework groups initiatives by time horizon: H1 optimizes current results, H2 builds new opportunities, H3 explores strategic innovation. It balances short-term and long-term value. IRR or cost-benefit only look at finance, and a grid only ranks.","t":"Now + later + future innovation = HORIZONS (H1, H2, H3).","k":["horizon-based framework","ROAI","portfolio"]},{"id":"ab-100-43","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing a low-code AI business solution by using Microsoft Copilot Studio. The solution must include an agent that automates tasks by simulating user interactions across third-party apps and websites, such as clicking buttons, entering text, and extracting information from screens. You need to recommend what to include in the agent. What should you recommend?","o":["Copilot skills","Model Context Protocol (MCP)","A natural language understanding + (NLU+) model in Copilot Studio","Computer Use in Copilot Studio"],"a":[3],"e":"Computer Use lets the agent mimic a user on third-party apps and websites: clicking buttons, typing text and reading screens. MCP connects tools and data, NLU+ understands language, and skills call configured flows or APIs.","t":"Click + type + read screen = Computer Use.","k":["Computer Use","UI automation","third-party apps"]},{"id":"ab-100-44","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which tool should you use for the prospect communication requirements in Dynamics 365 Sales?","o":["Copilot email assist","Deep Research in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service","Azure AI Search","The Voice template in Microsoft Copilot Studio"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot email assist in Dynamics 365 Sales helps sellers draft, summarize and reply to prospect emails faster. AI Search is for search, the Voice template is for speech, and Deep Research is not a daily email tool.","t":"Talk to prospects faster in Sales = EMAIL assist.","k":["Copilot email assist","Dynamics 365 Sales","prospects"]},{"id":"ab-100-45","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center agent. You need to ensure that the agent can transfer the conversation to a live customer service representative. Which two components should you include in the solution? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["An Azure AI Bot Service skill","Customer engagement hub","Microsoft Copilot Studio","Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit","Microsoft Foundry"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Copilot Studio builds and configures the agent, including escalation to a human. The Customer engagement hub receives the transferred conversation, queues it and routes it to the right customer service representative.","t":"Build the bot (Studio) + hand-off desk (Customer engagement hub).","k":["Copilot Studio","Customer engagement hub","live agent transfer"]},{"id":"ab-100-46","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You plan to use a Data Science Virtual Machine (DSVM) with the open source deep learning frameworks Caffe2 and PyTorch. You need to select a pre-configured DSVM to support the frameworks. What should you create?","o":["Data Science Virtual Machine for Windows 2012","Data Science Virtual Machine for Windows 2016","Data Science Virtual Machine for Linux (CentOS)","Data Science Virtual Machine for Linux (Ubuntu)","Geo AI Data Science Virtual Machine with ArcGIS"],"a":[3],"e":"The Ubuntu Linux DSVM comes preconfigured with open source deep learning frameworks like Caffe2 and PyTorch plus the needed drivers. Windows, CentOS and Geo AI DSVMs serve other purposes.","t":"Deep learning open source = Ubuntu DSVM.","k":["DSVM","Ubuntu","PyTorch","Caffe2"]},{"id":"ab-100-47","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment. You need to build two agents named Agent1 and Agent2. Agent1 must be extendable by using the Semantic Kernel and must connect to multiple business apps and APIs. Agent2 must connect directly to data stored in Microsoft Dataverse and must be embeddable in a Microsoft Power Apps canvas app. What should you use to build each agent?","o":["Microsoft Foundry - Azure Logic Apps","Microsoft Copilot Studio - Copilot in Power Apps","Microsoft Copilot Studio - Azure Logic Apps","Copilot in Power Apps - Microsoft Copilot Studio"],"a":[1],"e":"Agent1 needs Semantic Kernel extensibility and many app and API connections, which Copilot Studio supports. Agent2 must work with Dataverse and be embedded in a canvas app, which is what Copilot in Power Apps does.","t":"Extend + many APIs = Studio. Inside a canvas app = Copilot in Power Apps.","k":["Copilot Studio","Copilot in Power Apps","Semantic Kernel","canvas app"]},{"id":"ab-100-48","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI agent that automates the review of customer feedback stored in a cloud database. You plan to generate monthly reports from the agent's output to provide insights into customer sentiment and guide product development and marketing. You need to ensure that the data ingested by the agent is clean and suitable for the intended use. What should you do to prepare the data?","o":["Create a workflow in Microsoft Power Automate.","Identify and address biased data.","Sort the database by customer last name.","Create an agent flow in Microsoft Copilot Studio."],"a":[1],"e":"Customer feedback data can be biased, for example only very happy or very angry customers respond. Identify and fix bias so sentiment reports are reliable. Automation flows or sorting by last name do not improve data quality.","t":"Clean data for insights = remove BIAS.","k":["biased data","data preparation","sentiment"]},{"id":"ab-100-49","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to design a Microsoft 365 Copilot solution to optimize employee productivity. The solution must meet the following requirements: Ensure that the employees can query content stored in a subset of Microsoft SharePoint Online sites and in Teams by using natural language-based prompt actions. Ensure that employees receive contextually relevant responses in Microsoft 365 Copilot. What should you include in the design?","o":["Build a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to read the SharePoint content and post the responses to Teams.","Create a custom REST API that crawls the SharePoint content.","Modify SharePoint settings.","Configure Microsoft Graph access."],"a":[3],"e":"Microsoft Graph is the main access layer that lets Microsoft 365 Copilot query SharePoint and Teams content in context while respecting user permissions. No custom crawler or desktop flow is needed.","t":"M365 content for Copilot = Microsoft GRAPH.","k":["Microsoft Graph","M365 Copilot","SharePoint","Teams"]},{"id":"ab-100-50","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing a testing solution for Microsoft Copilot Studio agents. You need to validate prompt engineering best practices to ensure that the agents generate accurate and contextually relevant responses. Which prompt validation techniques and metrics should you include in the solution? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point","o":["Use prompts that have varied phrasing - Response relevance and accuracy.","Exclude domain-specific terminology from the prompts - The response generation time.","Keep the prompt vague to enable model flexibility - The number of active user sessions.","Use only simple, one-word prompts - The number of words generated per response."],"a":[0],"e":"Test with prompts that use varied phrasing to check that the agent understands the same intent in real situations. Measure response relevance and accuracy. Word count, generation time and session counts do not measure content quality.","t":"Test many phrasings, score relevance + accuracy.","k":["varied phrasing","relevance","accuracy","prompt testing"]},{"id":"ab-100-51","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft 365 tenant in Canada and multiple Microsoft Power Platform environments in Canada and the United States. The company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to the Canadian environment that will use: Microsoft Dataverse data stored in Canada. A connector that connects to an Azure OpenAI instance in the United States. You need to ensure that the agent adheres to data residency and data movement policies before being deployed. What should you do?","o":["Migrate the tenant to the United States.","Ensure that the data processed by Azure OpenAI is stored in the United States.","From the Microsoft Purview portal, validate the Data loss prevention settings.","Ensure that cross-region data movement is enabled for the Canadian environment and connector dependencies."],"a":[3],"e":"The Canadian agent calls Azure OpenAI in the US, so data crosses regions. Enable cross-region data movement for the Canadian environment and the connector dependencies before deployment. Moving the tenant or only checking DLP does not solve this.","t":"Data crossing borders? Allow CROSS-REGION movement.","k":["cross-region data movement","data residency","connector"]},{"id":"ab-100-52","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform business solution that consolidates data from multiple internal and external data sources. The solution must meet the following requirements: Provide the data as a centralized source for multiple AI systems, including Microsoft Copilot Studio agents, Dynamics 365 applications, and external AI models. Support built-in data classification and protection policies. Provide data for grounding and analytics. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Azure Data Lake Storage","Azure Cosmos DB","Microsoft Dataverse","A Microsoft Power BI semantic model"],"a":[2],"e":"Dataverse is the central data platform for Power Platform and Dynamics 365. It serves Copilot Studio, Dynamics apps and other AI systems, and has built-in security, classification and protection. Data Lake and Cosmos DB are general Azure services, and Power BI models are for analytics.","t":"Central data for Power Platform + D365 + protection = DATAVERSE.","k":["Dataverse","centralized source","data protection"]},{"id":"ab-100-53","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units. The company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product reviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis. What should you use?","o":["Azure pricing calculator","Anomaly Detection in Azure Cost Management","Azure Reservations","Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator"],"a":[0],"e":"The Azure pricing calculator estimates expected costs of Azure services before deployment, such as Azure OpenAI or AI Language for sentiment analysis, which feeds the ROAI analysis. Reservations are discounts, and Anomaly Detection tracks costs already incurred.","t":"POTENTIAL (future) cost = Pricing calculator.","k":["Azure pricing calculator","ROAI","cost estimate"]},{"id":"ab-100-54","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance for accounts payable and customer debt recovery. You are designing an AI finance process that meets the following requirements: Provides AI-driven details to help staff identify overdue vendor invoices and outstanding balances. Helps staff reduce how long it takes to review overdue invoices and payment history. You need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design. What should you recommend for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["AI Summaries with Copilot - The Account Reconciliation agent","Agent management - Collections coordinator summary","The Supplier Communication agent - Analyze demand plans with Copilot","AI Summaries with Copilot - Collections coordinator summary"],"a":[3],"e":"AI Summaries with Copilot help staff spot overdue vendor invoices and balances. The Collections coordinator summary pulls together the data that speeds up review of overdue invoices and payment history.","t":"Spot overdue = AI Summaries. Faster collections review = Collections coordinator summary.","k":["AI Summaries","Collections coordinator summary","overdue invoices"]},{"id":"ab-100-55","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing two Microsoft Copilot Studio agents named Agent1 and Agent2. Each agent must meet the following requirements: Each agent must use a standard model. Each agent must NOT use generative orchestration. Agent1 must support simple and short phrases for a given topic. Agent2 must integrate with Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice channel. You need to recommend language models for the agents. What should you recommend for each agent? To answer, drag the appropriate language models to the correct agents. Each language model may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Agent1: Natural language understanding + (NLU+) - Agent2: Conversational language understanding (CLU)","Agent1: Natural language understanding (NLU) - Agent2: Natural language understanding + (NLU+)","Agent1: Conversational language understanding (CLU) - Agent2: Natural language understanding (NLU)","Agent1: Azure OpenAI - Agent2: Natural language understanding + (NLU+)"],"a":[1],"e":"Agent1 handles short, simple phrases for one topic without generative orchestration, so NLU is enough. Agent2 runs on the Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice channel, which needs the more advanced NLU+.","t":"Simple text = NLU. Voice in Contact Center = NLU PLUS.","k":["NLU","NLU+","voice channel","Contact Center"]},{"id":"ab-100-56","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365. You need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in Microsoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship management (CRM) data. Solution: You recommend a Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[0],"e":"No. A generic Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template does not guarantee the dedicated Dynamics 365 CRM integration for meeting prep and sales support. Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales is the right fit.","t":"Sales + CRM + Outlook needs Copilot for SALES, not a generic template.","k":["Copilot for Sales","agent template","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-57","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A manufacturing company wants to deploy an agent that will automate supplier invoice processing. You are designing a solution to evaluate the financial implications of the deployment. The company is especially concerned about budget overruns. You need to ensure that the solution considers the total cost of ownership (TCO), the expected savings from using automation, and whether to extend the existing AI capabilities. What should you include in the design?","o":["A return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis","A break-even analysis only","Training a custom model","Adopting prebuilt agents to reduce the deployment time"],"a":[0],"e":"An ROAI analysis covers total cost of ownership, expected savings from automation and whether to extend existing AI capabilities. Break-even analysis only finds the breakeven point, and the other options are technical choices, not financial evaluations.","t":"TCO + savings + reuse = full ROAI analysis.","k":["ROAI","TCO","savings"]},{"id":"ab-100-58","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365. You need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in Microsoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship management (CRM) data. Solution: You recommend a classic Microsoft Dataverse workflow. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[0],"e":"No. A classic Dataverse workflow is rule-based automation with no generative AI, so it cannot summarize email threads or draft replies. Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales is the right fit.","t":"Classic workflow = no AI. No.","k":["classic workflow","Copilot for Sales","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-59","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 to manage service operations. Dispatchers coordinate service requests, and technicians perform scheduled on-site work. You need to design a solution that will use Microsoft Copilot to improve the efficiency of the service operations. The solution must meet the following requirements: Provide AI-driven assistance to help staff organize and resolve work orders. Deliver contextual AI support to frontline workers as they prepare for and complete customer appointments. Which two components should you include in the design? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["The Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app","Copilot in Customer Service","Copilot in Outlook","Copilot Service workspace","Dynamics 365 Customer Service","Copilot in Field Service"],"a":[0,5],"e":"Copilot in Field Service helps dispatchers organize, summarize and resolve work orders. The Field Service mobile app gives frontline technicians contextual AI help as they prepare for and complete appointments.","t":"Work orders + frontline = everything FIELD Service.","k":["Copilot in Field Service","Field Service mobile app","work orders"]},{"id":"ab-100-60","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to design a shared prompt library that will be used across multiple business units. The solution must meet the following requirements: Ensure consistent AI responses with reusable formats. Support governance and version control. Minimize administrative effort. Minimize ongoing costs. What should you recommend for each requirement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Maintain a prompt history - Define standardized prompt templates.","Delegate department-specific prompt templates - Store prompts in a Git repository.","Define standardized prompt templates - Store prompts in a Git repository.","Define standardized prompt templates - Categorize prompts by business function."],"a":[2],"e":"Standardized prompt templates give consistent responses with reusable formats. A Git repository gives governance and version control at low admin effort and cost, compared with building a custom system.","t":"Consistency = templates. Versioning = Git.","k":["prompt templates","Git","version control"]},{"id":"ab-100-61","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company extends Copilot in Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service. You need to recommend an automated application lifecycle management (ALM) process so that the Copilot components can be safely developed, tested, and promoted to production. Which two actions should you include in the ALM process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Use Microsoft Power Platform pipelines.","Include the components in a solution.","Store the agent transcripts in source control.","Use an unmanaged solution in production.","Rebuild the agents in each environment."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Power Platform pipelines automate safe deployment between Dev, Test and Production. Copilot components must be packaged in a solution to move through ALM. Never use unmanaged in production or rebuild agents by hand.","t":"ALM = put it in a SOLUTION, ship with PIPELINES.","k":["Power Platform pipelines","solution","ALM"]},{"id":"ab-100-62","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a team that analyzes its customers by using a manual process. You are designing an AI-based agent to automate and improve the process. You need to recommend on which platform to build the agent. The solution must use generative AI to answer common questions, provide analytics to review AI performance, identify customer demographics, and minimize custom development. Solution: You recommend Microsoft Copilot Studio. Does this meet the goal?","o":["No","Yes"],"a":[1],"e":"Yes. Copilot Studio supports generative answers, built-in analytics for agent performance, CRM data connections for demographics, and is low-code to minimize custom development.","t":"Gen AI + analytics + low-code = Copilot Studio. Yes.","k":["Copilot Studio","analytics","low-code","Yes"]},{"id":"ab-100-63","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has Microsoft Power Platform development, staging, and production environments. Each environment has its own Microsoft Dataverse tables and Azure AI Search index. You are designing an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1. Agent1 uses a Dataverse table named CustomerOrders and an Azure AI Search index named customer-knowledge. You need to deploy Agent1 to production while using production grounding data sources, minimizing downtime, and handling credentials and endpoints securely. What should you include in the deployment package solution, and what should you reconfigure after deployment?","o":["The data sources only - The Agent1 configuration","Agent1, the data sources, and the data source connections - The environment variables","Agent1 and references to the data sources - The environment variables","Agent1 only - The Dataverse connection only"],"a":[2],"e":"Package Agent1 with references to the data sources, not the physical production tables or indexes. After deployment, set environment variables to point at production endpoints and connections without hard-coding credentials.","t":"Ship REFERENCES, then set ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES.","k":["environment variables","references","deployment"]},{"id":"ab-100-64","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Foundry agent. You need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that the agent evaluates against baseline accuracy metrics before being deployed. What should you recommend?","o":["Enable Application Insights and use Azure Monitor.","Use Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring.","Configure GitHub Actions for new agent versions.","Deploy each new agent version directly to production."],"a":[1],"e":"Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring checks the agent against baseline metrics before deployment and tracks quality drift over time. GitHub Actions is generic CI/CD, and App Insights monitors runtime.","t":"Evaluate before deploy = Foundry Observability + evaluation.","k":["Foundry Control Plane","evaluation","drift monitoring"]},{"id":"ab-100-65","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI business solution that uses Microsoft Copilot Studio agents. You need to recommend prompt best practices to improve the effectiveness of agent interactions. Which two actions should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Measure system resource usage during prompt processing.","Track the duration of the average user session.","Analyze the prompt length distribution.","Use clear and specific instructions in the prompts.","Regularly test and refine the prompts based on user input."],"a":[3,4],"e":"Prompts should give clear, specific instructions to reduce ambiguity. Test and refine prompts regularly based on user input in a continuous improvement loop. The other options are operational metrics, not prompt practices.","t":"Be specific + keep refining.","k":["clear instructions","iterate","prompt best practices"]},{"id":"ab-100-66","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to recommend a security solution for agents in a Microsoft Power Platform environment. The agents must use only approved connectors and services. The solution must prevent the agents from accessing sensitive data. What should you recommend?","o":["Configure Azure Monitor to capture connector activity logs.","Enable a Microsoft Dataverse audit.","Deploy data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform.","Enable customer-managed keys in Microsoft Dataverse."],"a":[2],"e":"Power Platform DLP policies let admins classify, allow or block connectors, so agents only use approved services and cannot reach sensitive data. Azure Monitor and audits only log, and customer-managed keys only encrypt.","t":"Approved connectors only = DLP policy.","k":["DLP","connectors","Power Platform"]},{"id":"ab-100-67","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry agent that summarizes customer feedback and recommends products to customers. The agent references data from multiple knowledge sources. Users report that the agent response time is slow, and telemetry shows that the agent frequently reaches its token usage limit. You need to reduce token usage without degrading the quality of generated responses. What should you recommend?","o":["Chunk documents during indexing.","Reduce the number of knowledge sources used by the agent.","Lower the maximum token usage limit for the responses.","Reconfigure the prompts to limit the amount of retrieved content from the knowledge sources."],"a":[3],"e":"Limiting how much content is retrieved from knowledge sources through the prompt reduces input tokens and speeds up responses while keeping needed sources. Fewer sources may hurt quality and a lower token limit makes things worse.","t":"Token limit hit? Retrieve LESS, not fewer sources.","k":["token usage","retrieved content","prompt"]},{"id":"ab-100-68","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has Microsoft 365 Copilot agents. You need to design a security solution for the agents. The solution must identify and mitigate potential risks related to AI use, protect AI apps and sensitive data processed or generated by the agents, and support responsible AI governance by retaining and logging interactions, detecting policy violations, and investigating incidents. Which two components should you include in the design? NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Microsoft Purview","Azure AI Content Safety","Role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry","Microsoft Defender"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Microsoft Purview handles AI governance, sensitive data protection, retention and logging of interactions, policy violation detection and investigations. Microsoft Defender protects AI apps and detects and mitigates AI risks. Content Safety only filters harmful content.","t":"Govern + log = Purview. Protect + detect threats = Defender.","k":["Microsoft Purview","Microsoft Defender","AI governance"]},{"id":"ab-100-69","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled. You need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are presented to users. Solution: You build Microsoft Power Automate flows to trigger customized Copilot summaries. Does this meet the goal?","o":["NO","YES"],"a":[0],"e":"No. Power Automate automates workflows like notifications or data updates, but it does not change Copilot's prompt, summary logic or presentation in Dynamics 365 Sales.","t":"Flows automate tasks, they don't reshape Copilot summaries. No.","k":["Power Automate","opportunity summary","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-70","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI solution that uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent. You need to monitor the agent's performance. The solution must monitor the agent's telemetry in near-real-time (NRT), download transcripts of full conversations, and monitor the agent's usage and performance. What should you use for each requirement? You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Monitor telemetry in NRT: Application Insights; Download full conversation transcripts: Microsoft Power Apps; Monitor usage and performance: Copilot Studio","Monitor telemetry in NRT: Application Insights; Download full conversation transcripts: Copilot Studio; Monitor usage and performance: Microsoft Power Apps","Monitor telemetry in NRT: Log Analytics; Download full conversation transcripts: Microsoft Power Apps; Monitor usage and performance: Copilot Studio","Monitor telemetry in NRT: Log Analytics; Download full conversation transcripts: Copilot Studio; Monitor usage and performance: Application Insights"],"a":[0],"e":"Application Insights gives near-real-time telemetry. Power Apps (or the admin center) can export the ConversationTranscript table with full conversations. Copilot Studio Analytics shows usage and performance.","t":"Live = App Insights. Transcripts = Power Apps table. Usage = Studio.","k":["Application Insights","ConversationTranscript","Copilot Studio analytics"]},{"id":"ab-100-71","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Contoso requires the CEO to perform a quarterly assessment of AI solutions. The assessment must use Microsoft-recommended tools and verify reliability, interpretability, fairness, and compliance. What should you recommend to assist the CEO with these responsibilities?","o":["The Microsoft Service Trust Portal","Compliance Center","Microsoft Purview","The Responsible AI dashboard","Microsoft Foundry Tools"],"a":[3],"e":"The Responsible AI dashboard assesses reliability, interpretability, fairness and compliance. Service Trust Portal shows Microsoft certifications, Purview handles data governance, and Compliance Center is broad enterprise compliance.","t":"Fairness + interpretability = Responsible AI dashboard.","k":["Responsible AI dashboard","fairness","interpretability"]},{"id":"ab-100-72","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels. You need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance insights for the agents. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Microsoft Purview","Application Insights","Log Analytics","Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice"],"a":[1],"e":"Copilot Studio integrates with Application Insights to capture detailed telemetry such as messages, triggered topics, errors and performance across channels. Log Analytics stores and queries logs, Customer Voice is surveys, and Purview is governance.","t":"Telemetry + performance = Application Insights.","k":["Application Insights","telemetry","multi-channel"]},{"id":"ab-100-73","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses generative AI to assist Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service representatives. The agent currently exhibits a low resolution rate and a high escalation rate. You need to identify the issue. What should you use?","o":["The Insights tab from the Search & intelligence settings of the Microsoft 365 admin center","The Agent dashboard of Dynamics 365 Customer Service historical analytics","The Analytics tab in Copilot Studio","The Copilot hub in the Power Platform admin center"],"a":[2],"e":"The Analytics tab in Copilot Studio shows resolution rate and escalation rate drivers, so you can find which topics, prompts or knowledge sources cause failures. Other dashboards do not analyze Copilot Studio conversations in depth.","t":"Studio agent problem? Check Studio ANALYTICS.","k":["Analytics tab","resolution rate","escalation rate"]},{"id":"ab-100-74","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI agent that automates the review of customer feedback stored in a cloud database. You plan to generate monthly reports from the agent's output to provide insights into customer sentiment and guide product development and marketing. You need to ensure that the data ingested by the agent is clean and suitable for the intended use. What should you do to prepare the data?","o":["Translate the data into a single language.","Identify and address biased data.","Sort the database by customer last name.","Ensure that the size of the database does not exceed 100 GB."],"a":[1],"e":"Check customer feedback data for bias so the dataset is representative and sentiment results are trustworthy. Size limits, translation or sorting by name do not fix data quality or fairness.","t":"Clean for insights = fix BIAS (again!).","k":["biased data","data quality"]},{"id":"ab-100-75","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a canvas app named App1 in a Microsoft Power Platform environment named Env1. Env1 uses a customer-managed key for data encryption. App1 connects to multiple data sources to retrieve and update customer and order information. You need to recommend a solution to add Microsoft Copilot components to App1 without modifying the current security or encryption configurations of Env1. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Move App1 to a new environment that uses Microsoft-managed keys.","Modify the data sources of App1 to make them compatible with Copilot.","Enable Copilot features for Env1.","Duplicate App1 and republish the app in Env1."],"a":[2],"e":"Enable Copilot features for Env1. This is the prerequisite for adding Copilot components to a canvas app and does not change the customer-managed key or security setup.","t":"Don't move, don't copy. Just ENABLE Copilot.","k":["enable Copilot","customer-managed key","canvas app"]},{"id":"ab-100-76","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Contoso has identified analysis, reporting, and troubleshooting requirements for its Copilot Studio agents. The CFO wants to identify how many interactions are abandoned on a given day compared to resolved conversations, to assess Copilot Studio credit usage. The CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of AI agent responses; consistently poor feedback will trigger reengineering. Which Copilot Studio analytics metrics should you recommend for each executive?","o":["Use - Effectiveness","Effectiveness - Tool use","Use - Satisfaction","Satisfaction - Effectiveness"],"a":[2],"e":"The Use metrics show session volume and outcomes like abandoned versus resolved, which helps the CFO assess credit usage. Satisfaction metrics reflect user feedback on answer quality, which the CTO needs.","t":"CFO counts USE. CTO cares about SATISFACTION.","k":["Use","Satisfaction","Copilot Studio analytics"]},{"id":"ab-100-77","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You need to recommend a solution for integrating a Microsoft Copilot agent with a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center chat channel. The agent must answer customer inquiries and seamlessly transfer the conversation to a live customer service representative whenever the customer requests escalation. What should you recommend?","o":["Configure the Escalate topic","Build an agent flow","Configure the Conversation Start topic","Configure a skill","Call a Microsoft Power Automate connector"],"a":[0],"e":"The Escalate topic hands the conversation from the Copilot agent to a live representative and connects with Dynamics 365 Customer Service or Contact Center, passing the conversation context along.","t":"Customer wants a human = ESCALATE topic.","k":["Escalate topic","live agent","Contact Center"]},{"id":"ab-100-78","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are evaluating a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that supports Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service representatives. You need a testing solution that evaluates agent effectiveness during active sessions, validates accurate and helpful responses, and provides measurable, actionable insights for continuous improvement. What should you recommend?","o":["Measure uptime and page load times.","Perform load testing to validate how the agent scales under a high chat volume.","Track resolution, deflection, and accuracy by using dashboards and use scripts to ensure consistent responses.","Review historical tickets to find agents that have the shortest resolution times."],"a":[2],"e":"Dashboards that track resolution, deflection and accuracy measure effectiveness and answer quality, and test scripts check consistent responses across scenarios. Load tests, uptime and historical ticket times measure infrastructure, not answer quality.","t":"Measure quality: resolution + deflection + accuracy.","k":["resolution","deflection","accuracy","dashboards"]},{"id":"ab-100-79","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a team that analyzes its customers by using a manual process. You are designing an AI-based agent to automate and improve the process. The solution must use generative AI to answer common questions, provide analytics to review AI performance, identify customer demographics, and minimize custom development. Solution: You recommend Microsoft Security Copilot. Does this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. Security Copilot is built for cybersecurity work like incident investigation and threat analysis. It is not a low-code platform for customer analysis agents.","t":"Security Copilot = security only. No.","k":["Security Copilot","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-80","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are creating validation criteria for a custom generative AI model that produces business reports based on internal enterprise data. You need to assess whether the model's outputs are appropriate and meaningful for the business reports. Which metric should you use?","o":["Alignment of the output to domain-specific tasks","The number of active users interacting with the model","The average system resource usage during inference","The model training duration"],"a":[0],"e":"Alignment of outputs to domain-specific tasks tells you whether AI-generated reports are relevant, accurate and meaningful for real business work. User counts, resource use and training time are usage or infrastructure metrics.","t":"Meaningful for business = ALIGNED to domain tasks.","k":["domain alignment","output quality"]},{"id":"ab-100-81","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that provides answers based on a knowledge base for customer support. Users report that, occasionally, the agent provides inaccurate answers. You need to use metrics from the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio to identify the cause of the inaccuracies. Which two options should you use?","o":["Survey results","Engagement, resolution, and escalation rates","Session information and session outcomes","Quality of generated answers","Topic usage and topics with low resolution"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Topic usage and topics with low resolution show which topics trigger but fail to resolve, pointing to where answers go wrong. Quality of generated answers directly rates answers built from the knowledge base. Surveys and overall rates do not show the cause.","t":"Find inaccuracy: WHICH topics fail + HOW GOOD answers are.","k":["quality of generated answers","topics with low resolution"]},{"id":"ab-100-82","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company deploys a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow. You need to recommend a testing solution that meets the following requirements: test cases must validate the most recent changes to the agent before the agent is released, and the flow must be validated as part of the agent's orchestration. What should you recommend for each requirement?","o":["Run tests against the production version of the agent. Add the flow to the agent as a tool.","Publish the agent to a channel and test the agent on live users. Use the Power Automate for desktop console.","Run tests against the latest unpublished version of the agent. Add the flow to a canvas app.","Run tests against the latest unpublished version of the agent. Add the flow to the agent as a tool"],"a":[3],"e":"Test the latest unpublished version so the newest changes are validated before release. Add the desktop flow to the agent as a tool so it is tested as part of the agent's orchestration.","t":"Test UNPUBLISHED latest + flow as a TOOL.","k":["unpublished version","flow as tool","testing"]},{"id":"ab-100-83","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has Microsoft Copilot Studio agents and plans to deploy custom connectors across development, test, and production environments. You need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure consistency and prevent direct editing in production. Which two actions should you include in the design? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Deploy managed solutions to production.","Include agents and connectors in a solution.","Deploy unmanaged solutions to production.","Move the agents between the environments by using data export and import.","Manually rebuild the agents in each environment."],"a":[0,1],"e":"Put agents and custom connectors in one solution to manage and deploy them consistently. Deploy managed solutions to production to lock components and prevent direct edits.","t":"Solution + Managed = consistent and locked.","k":["solution","managed solution","ALM"]},{"id":"ab-100-84","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has multiple AI models that support generation of sales transactions. Each release of the models must be reviewed by a security and compliance team before being deployed to the production environment. The security and compliance team must have access to prior versions to properly determine potential exposures introduced. You need to recommend a solution to evaluate the impact of each deployment to production. The solution must enhance business continuity. What should you recommend?","o":["Establish a promotion process by using a quality gate.","Implement version control for all the AI system components.","Create a central model registry that uses version history.","Track model retirement schedules to prevent service disruptions."],"a":[2],"e":"A central model registry with version history keeps every model version, so the security team can compare older versions and assess new exposures before production. Quality gates, generic version control and retirement schedules do not give this.","t":"Need old model versions for review = Model REGISTRY with history.","k":["model registry","version history","business continuity"]},{"id":"ab-100-85","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI solution named Solution1 deployed to production. Solution1 uses an Azure OpenAI model to generate marketing emails for existing customers. During an internal review, you identify that Solution1 creates different emails depending on customers' traits. You need to recommend a strategy to mitigate the bias. The strategy must adhere to Microsoft responsible AI principles. What should you recommend?","o":["Retrain the model by using a larger dataset.","Modify the system instructions of Solution1.","Modify the contents of the training dataset.","Modify Solution1 to randomly generate emails for different traits."],"a":[2],"e":"Bias usually comes from unbalanced or prejudiced training data. Fix the root cause by reviewing, cleaning and balancing the training dataset. Changing the system prompt or randomizing output only treats symptoms, and more data without better quality does not help.","t":"Bias lives in the DATA. Fix the dataset.","k":["bias","training dataset","responsible AI"]},{"id":"ab-100-86","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels. You need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance insights for the agents. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Application Insights","Azure DevOps","Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice","Azure Advisor"],"a":[0],"e":"Copilot Studio integrates with Application Insights to collect detailed telemetry such as messages, triggered topics and errors, with performance analysis across channels. Azure Advisor optimizes infrastructure, DevOps handles CI/CD, and Customer Voice is surveys.","t":"Telemetry for Studio agents = Application Insights.","k":["Application Insights","telemetry"]},{"id":"ab-100-88","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY — This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. You must reference the information provided in the case, and you cannot return to the case study after leaving it. Overview — Contoso, Ltd. is a high-tech manufacturing company that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and Dynamics 365 Commerce for its North American operations. The company designs and develops innovative products with patents and proprietary technologies that are closely guarded secrets. Contoso wants to adopt AI solutions to scale in preparation for rapid growth and has multiple legal entities and Azure subscriptions. AI Adoption — The CTO must select one Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management or Dynamics 365 Commerce prebuilt AI agent and one custom Microsoft Copilot Studio AI agent to prioritize and deploy. The CIO must ensure that appropriate security labels are assigned to data used by the AI agents. The CFO analyzes ROI, the CISO discovers and inventories AI resources for auditing, and the CEO ensures that all solutions adhere to industry-standard responsible AI practices. All AI initiatives and agents must have a detailed business use case, a defined audience profile, and an estimated ROI. The R&D department already has a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server containing comprehensive product specifications and compliance data. Prebuilt AI Agent — The CTO has not selected which prebuilt AI agent to use in Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management and wants to view available agent templates to identify which agent will add the most business value. Custom AI Agent — The custom AI agent must use data from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management to answer questions for the manufacturing team as a low-code solution, be accessible from Microsoft Teams, eventually connect to other agents selected by description, select topics based on a description of the query rather than a trigger phrase, answer questions about R&D product specifications using existing technologies, integrate with and be accessible from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and use Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management business logic stored outside the application. Analysis, Reporting, and Troubleshooting — The CISO will audit all AI solutions monthly for compliance and security. The CFO will analyze estimated ROI against actual measured efficiencies and adoption quarterly using the Copilot Studio agent usage estimator. The CISO wants to identify how much sensitive data was accessed for a given AI agent run and who accessed it. The CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of AI agent responses. The CEO wants a quarterly assessment using Microsoft-recommended tools that verify reliability, interpretability, fairness, and compliance. The CFO wants to compare abandoned interactions with resolved conversations to assess Copilot Studio credit usage. What should you include in the custom AI agent design to meet the R&D product specifications and the compliance information requirements? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["An Azure AI Bot Service channel - A REST API","The Semantic Kernel - A tool","A custom OData entity - An event trigger","A custom connector - The MCP server"],"a":[3],"e":"A custom connector bridges the low-code agent to external systems such as an MCP server. Then add the MCP server to the agent so it can reach R&D product specs and compliance data.","t":"Bridge = custom connector. Data hub = MCP server.","k":["custom connector","MCP server"]},{"id":"ab-100-89","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI solution built by using Microsoft Copilot Studio and Power Platform. The solution is used by the company's sales, marketing, and customer service teams. You are performing a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis to evaluate the impact of the solution. You need to identify which measurable business drivers to include in the analysis. Which two business drivers should you identify? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Economic market predictability","Increased employee productivity","The reduced average case resolution time","Brand awareness","Market capitalization"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Reduced average case resolution time measures how Copilot speeds up customer requests. Increased employee productivity measures time saved and more work completed. Market cap, brand awareness and market predictability cannot be tied directly to the AI solution.","t":"Measurable drivers: FASTER cases + MORE productive staff.","k":["resolution time","productivity","ROAI drivers"]},{"id":"ab-100-90","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company plans to implement an AI solution containing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent and a Microsoft Foundry agent. The solution is stored in a source code repository. A test environment must be used before production, production must be isolated from development and testing, and deployment must be repeatable, fully automated, and require no manual intervention. Which deployment method should you recommend for each agent? NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Export from the source code repository and import to the target environment. - Use an Azure DevOps pipeline.","Use a Bicep file. - Use a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline.","Use a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline. - Use a Bicep file","Use a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline. - Use an Azure DevOps pipeline."],"a":[3],"e":"Copilot Studio belongs to Power Platform, so use a Power Platform deployment pipeline across Dev, Test and Prod. The Foundry agent fits an Azure DevOps pipeline for automated CI/CD from the source repo. Bicep and manual export/import do not fit.","t":"Studio rides Power Platform pipeline. Foundry rides Azure DevOps.","k":["Power Platform pipeline","Azure DevOps","CI/CD"]},{"id":"ab-100-91","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses a fine-tuned Microsoft Foundry model that requires frequent updates as new customer feedback becomes available. You need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process that meets the following requirements: Data changes must be tracked and versioned. The model must be retrained consistently by using approved training data. Which two actions should you include in the design? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Create a content filter.","Store the training data in Azure Blob Storage that has version control enabled.","Store the training data in Azure Files.","Associate the storage location to the fine-tuning job.","Upload the training data to Microsoft Foundry data files."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Uploading to Foundry data files keeps fine-tuning on approved data. Azure Blob Storage with versioning tracks changes, restores old versions and audits the data used in each fine-tune. Content filters and Azure Files do not meet these ALM needs.","t":"Versioned data = Blob versioning. Approved training = Foundry data files.","k":["Blob versioning","Foundry data files","fine-tuning"]},{"id":"ab-100-92","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an AI solution that uses Azure OpenAI models. You need to recommend a governance solution that monitors and audits changes to model configurations and data usage. The solution must minimize administrative effort. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Azure Policy","Azure Stream Analytics","Azure API Management","Azure Monitor","Microsoft Purview"],"a":[4],"e":"Microsoft Purview gives governance, data management, data usage tracking and audits for AI resources with little admin effort. Azure Monitor tracks performance, Policy enforces config rules, API Management manages APIs, and Stream Analytics processes streams.","t":"Audit data usage + governance = PURVIEW.","k":["Microsoft Purview","audit","governance"]},{"id":"ab-100-93","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that has been in production for three months. The agent has received positive feedback from users. You need to identify the number of questions unanswered by the agent and the number of abandoned sessions between the users and the agent. Which Copilot Studio insights should you use? To answer, drag the appropriate insights to the correct requirements. Each insight may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Generated answer rate and quality — Conversation outcomes","Reactions — Survey results","Conversation outcomes — Generated answer rate and quality","Survey results — Generated answer rate and quality"],"a":[0],"e":"Unanswered questions show up in Generated answer rate and quality. Abandoned sessions show up in Conversation outcomes, which classifies sessions as Resolved, Escalated or Abandoned.","t":"Unanswered = answer RATE. Abandoned = OUTCOMES.","k":["generated answer rate","conversation outcomes"]},{"id":"ab-100-94","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses Azure OpenAI models that use grounding data from Microsoft Fabric for agents. The models are fine-tuned by using proprietary datasets. You need to design a governance solution that restricts access to the grounding data to assigned roles and restricts model fine-tuning to only the AI engineering team. What should you include in the design?","o":["Role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry - Microsoft Purview access policies","Azure Monitor alerts - Microsoft Entra Conditional Access","Azure Policy compliance rules - Azure Resource Manager (ARM) resource locks","Microsoft Purview access policies - Role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry"],"a":[3],"e":"Purview access policies control role-based access to grounding data in Fabric. RBAC in Microsoft Foundry limits fine-tuning to the AI engineering team. Monitor, Policy, locks and Conditional Access do other jobs.","t":"Data access = Purview. Who can fine-tune = Foundry RBAC.","k":["Purview access policies","RBAC","fine-tuning"]},{"id":"ab-100-95","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing a testing solution for a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service and Dynamics 365 Sales. You need to design end-to-end scenarios to test the agent's ability to coordinate tasks and data interactions across both apps, interpret user input, and provide contextually relevant outputs. Which test scenario and metric should you include in the design?","o":["Test scenario: Run task-based scenarios that involve both apps; Metric: Track the successful completion of cross-app tasks","Test scenario: Run task-based scenarios that involve both apps; Metric: Track the average click rate across both apps","Test scenario: In each app, test isolated tasks without using workflows; Metric: Measure the initial prompt response time for each app","Test scenario: Test visual consistency across both apps; Metric: Track the successful completion of cross-app tasks"],"a":[0],"e":"To test coordination across Customer Service and Sales, run task-based scenarios that involve both apps and track the successful completion of cross-app tasks. Isolated tests, click rates and visual checks do not measure this.","t":"Cross-app test = cross-app TASKS completed.","k":["end-to-end testing","cross-app tasks"]},{"id":"ab-100-96","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio prompt-and-response agent. You need to ensure that the agent provides effective and relevant responses and provides conversational outcomes. Which metric should you use for each requirement?To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Reactions - Satisfaction","Tool use - Topics by outcome","Generated answer rate and quality - Topics by outcome","Generated answer rate and quality - Tool use"],"a":[2],"e":"Generated answer rate and quality shows whether responses are effective and relevant. Topics by outcome tracks conversation results like Resolved, Escalated or Abandoned.","t":"Good answers = answer QUALITY. Results = topics by OUTCOME.","k":["generated answer quality","topics by outcome"]},{"id":"ab-100-97","d":"agentic-ai","x":"ASE STUDY This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. However, there might be additional case studies or other exam sections. Manage your time to ensure that you can complete all the exam sections in the time provided. Pay attention to the Exam Progress at the top of the screen so you have sufficient time to complete any exam sections that follow this case study. To answer the case study questions, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case. Case studies and associated questions might contain exhibits or other resources that provide more information about the scenario described in the case. Information provided in an individual question does not apply to the other questions in the case study. A Review Screen will appear at the end of this case study. From the Review Screen, you can review and change your answers before you move to the next exam section. After you leave this case study, you will NOT be able to return to it. To start the case study - To display the first question in this case study, select the \"Next\" button. To the left of the question, a menu provides links to information such as business requirements, the existing environment, and problem statements. Please read through all this information before answering any questions. When you are ready to answer a question, select the \"Question\" button to return to the question. Background - Fabrikam, Inc., is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to migrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company will implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary technologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team. As part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and implement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations. Problem Statements - Fabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to use corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer interactions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input. As a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate systems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience. Fabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT adopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on demonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will be the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority. Planned Initiatives - General - Fabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and responsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the cloud to support the adoption of these technologies. Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology; this will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by end users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common data model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales team. Sales Cycle Enablement - To achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the sales cycle: Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component. Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene as appropriate. Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and customer interactions. Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and send follow-up communications to prospects. Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have questions about internal policies or customer data. Requirements - Infrastructure Migration - Fabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements: Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads. The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the cloud. Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the solution will save the company money. Sales Cycle Enablement - Fabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement: The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience. A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the agent during the initial AI agent adoption process. Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales executive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed attempts. The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is required for stakeholder sign off. The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and efficiently than currently. Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a monthly basis. Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for future auditing. Which tool should you recommend to address the sensitive information concerns in the sales process?","o":["Monitoring in Microsoft Foundry","Model Context Protocol (MCP)","The Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio","Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI","Application Insights"],"a":[4],"e":"Application Insights gives detailed telemetry and logs for data exchanged through the agent, including user info and IDs, for querying, troubleshooting and auditing. Copilot Studio Analytics is an overview dashboard and MCP is a protocol.","t":"Track who said what for audits = Application Insights logs.","k":["Application Insights","audit","sensitive info"]},{"id":"ab-100-98","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units. The company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product reviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis. What should you use?","o":["Azure Monitor","Azure Reservations","Azure pricing calculator","Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator"],"a":[2],"e":"The Azure pricing calculator estimates the cost of planned Azure services, like Azure OpenAI or AI Language, before deployment. TCO compares on-prem with cloud, Reservations optimize cost later, and Monitor watches running systems.","t":"Potential cost before building = Pricing calculator.","k":["pricing calculator","ROAI"]},{"id":"ab-100-99","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has multiple Dynamics 365 and Azure resources and is implementing a custom Microsoft Copilot Studio AI agent. Which two components for the custom AI agent should you include in the application lifecycle management (ALM) process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.","o":["An X++ model","A Cloud Scale Unit (CSU) package","A ZIP package","An Azure package","A Microsoft Power Platform solution"],"a":[2,4],"e":"A Power Platform solution packages the Copilot Studio agent and related parts for ALM across Dev, Test and Prod. The ZIP package is the exported file format used to move the solution. X++, CSU and Azure packages are for other systems.","t":"Solution inside a ZIP.","k":["Power Platform solution","ZIP package","ALM"]},{"id":"ab-100-100","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units. The company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product reviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis. What should you use?","o":["Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator","Cost Management + Billing","Azure savings plans","Azure Monitor"],"a":[1],"e":"With many business units, Cost Management + Billing analyzes and forecasts spend, allocates costs per unit and sets budgets and alerts for ROAI. Savings plans optimize price, the Fabric estimator is Fabric-only, and Monitor is technical.","t":"Multiple business units + cost = Cost Management + Billing.","k":["Cost Management","business units","ROAI"]},{"id":"ab-100-102","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing an AI business solution that contains a Microsoft Power Automate workflow, a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent, a Microsoft Dataverse database, and a Microsoft Power Apps app. As part of the application lifecycle management (ALM) process, you plan to package the components so that they can be deployed to other environments as a group. You need to recommend a solution that supports versioning, dependencies, and deployments. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Azure DevOps","Microsoft Power Platform solutions","GitHub Actions"],"a":[1],"e":"Power Platform solutions package Power Apps, Power Automate, Dataverse and Copilot Studio into one deployable unit with versioning and dependencies. GitHub Actions and Azure DevOps are CI/CD tools that still work on solutions.","t":"Package as a group = SOLUTION.","k":["Power Platform solutions","versioning","dependencies"]},{"id":"ab-100-103","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company deploys agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources. The agents are deployed to both the United States and Europe. You need to recommend a governance solution that enforces deployment only to approved Azure regions and provides continuous compliance verification of the resources. What should you include in the recommendation for each requirement?","o":["Azure Policy — Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Azure Monitor — Microsoft Defender for Cloud"],"a":[0],"e":"Azure Policy's Allowed locations policy enforces deployment only to approved regions. Microsoft Defender for Cloud gives continuous compliance assessment, scores and dashboards. Azure Monitor is telemetry.","t":"Enforce = POLICY. Keep checking = DEFENDER for Cloud.","k":["Azure Policy","Defender for Cloud","allowed locations"]},{"id":"ab-100-104","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Which existing tool and data should you use to gather the required metrics for stakeholder signoff for the AI agents? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["Microsoft Copilot Studio — the current time to complete the task today per instance","Azure Resource Monitor (ARM) — the current cost to complete the tasks per instance","Microsoft Foundry — the current cost to complete the tasks per instance","Dynamics 365 Sales — the current cost to complete the tasks per instance"],"a":[0],"e":"Use Copilot Studio and the current time to complete each task today. That baseline lets you compare the current process with the agent process to show savings and ROI for stakeholder signoff.","t":"Signoff needs a BASELINE: today's time per task.","k":["baseline","time per task","stakeholder signoff"]},{"id":"ab-100-105","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry generative AI model. You need to evaluate the model's output to measure the overall quality and coherence of generated responses. The evaluation must use GPT-4o as a judge and return a numeric score for each output. Which type of metric should you use?","o":["risk and safety","AI quality (NLP)","AI quality (AI assisted)","Groundedness"],"a":[2],"e":"AI quality (AI assisted) metrics use another model as the judge. Here GPT-4o rates quality and coherence and returns a score for each output. NLP metrics use traditional measures, risk and safety checks harmful content, and groundedness checks source fidelity.","t":"LLM as judge = AI-ASSISTED quality.","k":["AI-assisted metrics","LLM judge","coherence"]},{"id":"ab-100-106","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses custom connectors to interact with enterprise APIs. You need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that the connectors are deployed consistently across development, test, and production environments and meet governance and traceability requirements. What should you recommend?","o":["Maintain connector definitions in environment variables.","Manage the connectors as solution components and deploy the components by using ALM pipelines.","Export and import the connectors between the environments as unmanaged solutions.","Deploy the APIs as Azure Functions."],"a":[1],"e":"Manage custom connectors as Power Platform solution components and deploy them through ALM pipelines across Dev, Test and Prod for consistency, governance and traceability. Unmanaged imports do not fit Test or Prod.","t":"Connectors too: solution + pipeline.","k":["solution components","ALM pipelines","custom connector"]},{"id":"ab-100-107","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Which tool should you recommend to help secure funding for future AI agent development?","o":["Evaluations in Microsoft Foundry","the Azure Cost Optimization workbook","Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)","Azure Operator Insights","the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio"],"a":[4],"e":"The case requires showing that AI agent adoption grows month by month to win funding. The Analytics tab in Copilot Studio gives adoption metrics and reports over time for stakeholders.","t":"Funding needs ADOPTION proof = Studio Analytics.","k":["Analytics tab","adoption","funding"]},{"id":"ab-100-108","d":"agentic-ai","x":"You are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses a custom Microsoft Foundry model to generate responses. You need to ensure that the agent can securely connect to and invoke the custom model during user interactions. What should you include in the design?","o":["Create a custom engine agent.","Configure the agent to use classic orchestration.","Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent.","Add the Microsoft Foundry model as a Copilot Studio skill."],"a":[2],"e":"Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent. The connection handles secure authentication and authorization so Copilot Studio can call the custom Foundry model during conversations.","t":"Securely reach Foundry model = CONNECTION.","k":["Foundry connection","custom model","authentication"]},{"id":"ab-100-109","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels. You need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance insights for the agents. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio","Log Analytics","Microsoft Power BI","Azure Advisor"],"a":[1],"e":"Log Analytics centralizes, queries and analyzes logs from many agents using KQL, often together with Application Insights. The Analytics tab is basic per-agent reporting, Power BI visualizes, and Advisor gives infrastructure tips.","t":"No App Insights option? Pick LOG ANALYTICS.","k":["Log Analytics","KQL","telemetry"]},{"id":"ab-100-110","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company is using Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations apps. The company wants to leverage Microsoft Copilot in-app help and guidance to generate responses for its internal business processes. You need to add an additional knowledge source for these business processes. The solution must allow Copilot to access the required information without creating any new topics in the Copilot agent for Finance and Operations. Which knowledge source should you add?","o":["A file upload","Microsoft Dataverse","A public website","Azure AI Search"],"a":[0],"e":"Upload internal process documents (PDF or Word) as a file so Copilot's generative answers can use them without creating new topics.","t":"No new topics + internal docs = just UPLOAD the file.","k":["file upload","generative answers","knowledge source"]},{"id":"ab-100-111","d":"agentic-ai","x":"Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen. A company has a team that analyzes its customers by using a manual process. You are designing an AI-based agent to automate and improve the process. The solution must use generative AI to answer common questions, provide analytics to review AI performance, identify customer demographics, and minimize custom development. Solution: You recommend GitHub Copilot. Does this meet the goal?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"No. GitHub Copilot is a coding assistant in the IDE, not a platform for customer-facing agents with analytics. Copilot Studio is the right fit.","t":"GitHub Copilot writes code, not customer agents. No.","k":["GitHub Copilot","No"]},{"id":"ab-100-113","d":"agentic-ai","x":"A company has Microsoft Foundry agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources. The agents are deployed to both the United States and Europe. A company mandate states that the agents and their grounding data must adhere to data residency and movement regulations. You need to recommend a governance solution for the agents. What should you include in the recommendation?","o":["Azure Monitor","Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Microsoft Purview","Azure Policy"],"a":[2],"e":"Microsoft Purview provides data governance and compliance: classification, lifecycle tracking, and auditing of how sensitive data, including AI grounding data, is used or moved. Defender is security posture, Policy is resource rules, Monitor is telemetry.","t":"Data residency + data movement rules = PURVIEW.","k":["Microsoft Purview","data residency","grounding data"]},{"id":"ab-100-114","d":"agentic-ai","x":"CASE STUDY — This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately from other exam sections. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case study. You must reference the information provided in the case, and you cannot return to the case study after leaving it. Overview — Contoso, Ltd. is a high-tech manufacturing company using Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and Dynamics 365 Commerce. The company has multiple legal entities and Azure subscriptions, and its R&D department has a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server containing product specifications and compliance data. Requirements — The CTO must select one prebuilt Dynamics 365 AI agent and one custom Microsoft Copilot Studio agent. The CIO is responsible for security labels, the CFO analyzes ROI, the CISO audits and inventories AI resources, and the CEO ensures responsible AI practices. All AI initiatives must have a business use case, audience profile, and estimated ROI. The custom agent must be low-code, use Supply Chain Management data, be accessible in Microsoft Teams and Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, support agent-to-agent connections selected by description, use conversational topic selection instead of trigger phrases, access R&D product specifications through existing technologies, and use external Supply Chain Management business logic. Analysis, reporting, and troubleshooting requirements — The CISO audits compliance and security monthly. The CFO compares estimated ROI with measured efficiencies and adoption quarterly using the Copilot Studio agent usage estimator. The CISO needs to identify sensitive data accessed during an agent run and who accessed it. The CTO tracks user feedback about agent response quality. The CEO performs a quarterly assessment using Microsoft-recommended tools that verify reliability, interpretability, fairness, and compliance. The CFO compares abandoned interactions with resolved conversations to assess Copilot Studio credit usage. Which Copilot Studio analytics metrics should you recommend to assist the executives with their specific responsibilities? To answer, drag the appropriate metrics to the correct executives. Each metric may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.","o":["CFO: Use; CTO: Satisfaction","CFO: Use; CTO: Effectiveness","CFO: Satisfaction; CTO: Effectiveness","CFO: Effectiveness; CTO: Tool use"],"a":[0],"e":"The CFO needs abandoned versus resolved interactions to assess credit usage, which is the Use metric. The CTO tracks user feedback on answer quality, which is Satisfaction.","t":"CFO counts USE. CTO feels SATISFACTION.","k":["Use","Satisfaction"]}],"desc":"Copilot Studio agents, Dynamics 365 AI, Model Context Protocol, agent orchestration and AI ROI governance."}]};
